INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter1-satavahanas', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p1", "partName": "Pre-Satavahana Age", "bodyMd": "*   **Telangana** has one of the earliest histories in India, sitting at the meeting point of northern and southern kingdoms.\n*   The name \"Telangana\" itself is comparatively recent — it came into use only from the time of the **Delhi Sultans**, first mentioned by the poet **Amir Khusrau**. At that point it referred to a much wider Telugu-speaking belt, sometimes stretching all the way to Tamil Nadu.\n*   Under **Golconda Qutub Shahi** rule, \"Telangana\" also covered Coastal Andhra. It was only later, once the **Nizam** handed Northern Circars (Coastal Andhra) and Ceded Districts (Rayalaseema) to the East India Company, that \"Telangana\" narrowed down to specifically mean the Telugu-speaking parts of **Hyderabad State**.\n*   Telugu speakers have historically been called **Andhras**, their land **Andhra Desa**. The **Buddhist Jatakas (600–400 B.C.E.)** already refer to the land between the Godavari and Krishna rivers as **Andhapatham/Andhakaratham**.\n*   Before the Satavahanas, the region was made up entirely of **republics (gana rajyas)** — one of these was ruled by a king named **Shalivahanudu**.\n\n### Earliest References\n\n*   The **earliest reference to the Andhras** appears in the **Aitareya Brahmana of the Rigveda (1000 B.C.E.)**: sage Vishwamitra curses his fifty sons to live south of the Vindhyas among the Andhra, Pundra, Pulinda, Sabara and Mutiba tribes — all described as non-Aryan stock.\n*   **Sage Agastya** is credited (in the Puranas, Mahabharata, and Sangam literature) with bringing Aryan culture into the south — the Mahabharata even has him crushing Vindhya''s pride and drinking the ocean dry, symbolizing this southward push.\n*   Among the 16 Mahajanapadas of the 6th century B.C.E., **Asmaka** (Nizamabad, Karimnagar, Adilabad districts) was the only one located in South India, with its capital at **Podana** (present-day Bodhan). The kingdoms of **Alaka (Asmaka)** and **Mulaka** (Nanded, Aurangabad districts) lay on either side of the Godavari and were together known as \"**Andhakarattas**\"; Mulaka''s capital was **Prathistanapura (Paithan)**.\n*   **Megasthenes**, the Greek ambassador who visited India in the 4th century B.C.E., recorded that the Andhras commanded **30 fortified cities, 1 lakh infantry, 2,000 cavalry, and 1,000 elephants** — a claim **Pliny** later confirmed. The forts he described have been identified at Bodhan, Kotilingala, Dhulikatta, Peddabankur, Kondapur, Phanigiri, Gajulabanda, Indrapuri (Telangana) and Sathanikota, Veerapuram, Bhattiprolu, Dhanyakatakam, Vijayapuri, Suvarnagiri, Vengi, Narasala (Andhra Pradesh).\n*   Ashoka''s **13th Rock Edict** places the kingdoms of Kalinga, Andhra, Bojaka and Rattika just south of his empire, following his dharma.\n*   After Mauryan power collapsed, **Pushyamitra Sunga** — commander-in-chief — killed the last Mauryan emperor **Brihadradha** and seized the throne in **187 B.C.E.**; the Andhras rose in rebellion against this but were defeated by the Sungas (recorded in Kalidasa''s _Malavikagnimitra_).\n\n### Pre-Satavahana Petty Kingdoms (2nd–1st century B.C.E.)\n\n*   With the Mauryas gone and the Satavahanas yet to rise, the Deccan was ruled by scattered local kings.\n*   The **Datukaranda inscriptions** (Bhattiprolu Stupa, 3rd century B.C.E.) name a king **Kubiraka**, who ruled with the help of a Nigamasabha and Gostis. The **Vaddemanu inscriptions** add names like Rajasomaka, Guntupalli, Velpuru, and Sarisada, Mahasada, Ashokasada, Shivasada, Shivamahasada.\n*   Coins from this era — of Maharathi and Mahatalavara rulers (Kondapur, Hyderabad, Kotilingala) and of Gobada, Samagopa, Narana, Kamvayasiri (Kotilingala) — along with Sivamahastin and Sivaskandahastin coins at Veerapuram, fill out the picture of these small kingdoms.\n*   Excavated pre-Satavahana habitations at Kondapur, Peddabankur and Kotilingala show red and red-black pottery, and evidence that iron was already being melted and worked into plough rods and tools — a blacksmith''s furnace has been found at **Peddabankur** (Karimnagar district).\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p2", "partName": "Sources of Information", "bodyMd": "*   Reconstructing Satavahana history relies on two kinds of evidence: **literary sources** and **archaeological sources** (inscriptions, coins, monuments, paintings, sculpture).\n\n### Inscriptions\n\n*   All known Satavahana inscriptions are in **Prakrit, written in Brahmi script**, and only **24** survive from the dynasty''s entire long rule — 8 from Nasik, 5 from Kanheri, 3 from Karle, 1 from Bilsa, 2 from Naneghat, 1 from Mykadoni (Kurnool district), 1 from Chinaganjam, 2 from Amaravati, 1 from Kodavali. These **24 inscriptions** cover only the early and end rulers of the dynasty — no inscriptions have been found for any of the rulers who reigned in between.\n*   The **Naneghat inscription**, issued by **Naganika** (wife of Satakarni I), is a decorative record carved with figures of the early Satavahanas. \n*   The **Kanheri inscription** was issued by **Krishna (Kanha)**. \n*   The **Nasik inscription of Gautami Balasri** — issued in the 19th regnal year of her grandson Vashistiputra Sri Pulomavi — recounts the conquests of her son **Gautamiputra Satakarni**. And the **Hathigumpha inscription** of the Kalinga king **Kharavela** (Maha Meghavahana dynasty), though not a Satavahana record itself, is one of the most valuable sources for Satavahana chronology, since it describes his contemporary, Satakarni.\n\n### Coins\n\n*   The Satavahanas minted mostly **lead and copper** coins, along with **Potin** coins (an alloy of copper, tin and other metals); **silver coins** appear only from the time of **Gautamiputra Satakarni** onward. Coins typically carried the king''s name and titles, sometimes the father''s name too, and were deciphered through epigraphic script analysis. Common symbols include the **ox, elephant, lion, hill, Ujjain symbol, ship, sun, moon, lotus and couch**.\n*   The single strongest piece of evidence for the dynasty''s Telangana origins comes from coinage: coins of **Chimuka Satavahana** and **Satakarni I** are found at Kotilingala, and since **Chimuka''s coins turn up in greatest numbers only at Kotilingala**, this points to Telangana as the Satavahanas'' first home. Many of these ruler names — previously known only from Puranas or scattered inscriptions — came to light after **Sanganabatla Narahari Sarma**, a Postal Department employee, collected Kotilingala coins and handed them to epigraphist **Dr. P.V. Parabrahma Sastry** in 1970.\n*   Coins also surface at Nevasa, Maski (Karnataka), Tripuri (Madhya Pradesh), Nagarjunakonda, Kondapur and Peddabankur. A distinctive category, **restruck coins** — where one ruler''s symbols are stamped over another''s — arises between contemporaries, through inheritance, rebellion, or conquest. \n*   The most famous example: **9,270 silver coins** of the Western Kshatrapa ruler **Nahapana**, found at Tarahala and Jogalthembi, restruck by **Gautamiputra Satakarni** — direct numismatic proof of his victory over Nahapana. \n*   Coins of **Rudrasena II** found at the Buddhist site of **Phanigiri** (Nalgonda district) point to ongoing Satavahana–Kshatrapa contact, while a **ship-figure coin of Yagnasri Satakarni** at Nagarjunakonda is evidence of sea trade. \n*   Higher officials — Mahatalavara, Mahasenapati, Maharathi, Mahagramika — also had the right to mint their own coins, as Maharathi **Sadakana (Satakarni) Kalalaya** did. **Roman coins**, found at several South Indian sites, arrived through India''s trade relations with Rome.\n\n### Excavations & Monuments\n\n*   Chronology at these sites has largely been fixed through **carbon dating**. Key Telangana excavation sites include **Phanigiri, Gajulabanda, Kotilingala, Peddabankur, Kadambapur, Dhulikatta**; in Andhra, Amaravati, Bhattiprolu, Gudivada, Jaggayyapeta, Guntupalli, Ramateertham and Salihundam.\n\n#### Kondapur\n\n*   Located in **Medak district**, Kondapur was already a town by the 3rd century B.C.E. and, before the Satavahanas, served as the capital of the **Asika kingdom** (Rangareddy, Hyderabad, Medak districts) — one of the 30 forts Megasthenes described. \n*   It functioned as a major Roman trade hub; a necklace of Roman sestertii bearing the **Tiberius Caesar** symbol has been found here, along with **4,000 Satavahana coins** naming rulers such as Sadavahana, Gauthamiputra Satakarni, Vasishtiputra Satakarni, Vasishtiputra Pulumavi, Shivasri Pulumavi III and Yagnasri Satakarni. **Mallampalli Somasekhara Sarma** believed Kondapur served as the Satavahanas'' **mint center**.\n\n#### Kotilingala\n\n*   Sitting at the confluence of the Peddavagu and Godavari in Karimnagar district, Kotilingala was excavated between 1980–1983, uncovering the walls of a Satavahana **fort and town**, along with coins of **Simuka** and pre-Satavahana rulers. \n*   This site was already prominent by the 4th century B.C.E., and everything points to it being the **first capital of the Satavahana Empire** — a sandstone pillar found here bears \"**Naga gopi nikaya**\" in Brahmi script. Nearby, at **Munula Gutta**, Jain stone shades have also been found.\n\n#### Peddabankur\n\n*   On the bank of the Hussaimiya rivulet in Karimnagar district, a farmer''s field turned up a pot holding over **22,000 Satavahana coins**. Excavations between 1968–74 revealed three brick forts, 22 hand-dug wells (brick-lined), and one well built from earthen bangles, along with iron axes, pins, swords, spears, sickles, chisels, beads, bangles and terracotta seals. Coins of Satavahana, Satakarni, Pulumavi and Sivasri Pulumavi turned up alongside **Roman coins of Augustus Caesar and Tiberius**.\n\n#### Dhulikatta\n\n*   Dhulikatta, also on the Hussaimiya rivulet in Karimnagar district, was excavated between 1972–75 by archaeologist **V.V. Krishna Sastry**, bringing to light a Satavahana-period Buddhist Stupa with Brahmi inscriptions on the surrounding stone slabs. \n*   The name \"Dhulikatta\" itself means \"mud fort\" — inside it, palaces, walls, granaries and circular fortifications have been found, along with iron weapons, terracotta dolls, an ivory comb made from elephant tusk, earthen pots, and even Mauryan-era punch-marked coins. \n*   Buddhist monuments of this kind broadly fall into Stupas, Chaityas and Sangharamas; the sculptural style associated with the period is known as Amaravati sculpture, and it remains our best window into contemporary dress, hairstyle and jewellery.\n\n### Literary Sources\n\n*   The **Puranas** (Vishnu, Bhagavata, Matsya, and the _Kaliyuga Rajavrithantam_ portion of the Bhavishya Purana) preserve dynastic genealogies, but since they were compiled centuries after Satavahana rule ended, **inscriptions and coins remain the more reliable sources** — of the 30 rulers the Puranas list, almost nothing is known about rulers from 4 to 22. **Pargitar** translated these Puranic king-lists into English as _Dynasties of the Kali Age_.\n*   Works like the **Gathasaptasati**, **Brihatkatha**, and **Lilavati** — all associated with the time of **Hala** — and Vatsayana''s **Kamasutra** shed light on administration and everyday social life.\n*   Among foreign sources, Megasthenes'' _Indica_ (4th century B.C.E.), the Roman historian **Pliny''s** (28–79 C.E.) _Historia Naturalis_, and the anonymous sailor''s account _Periplus of the Erythrean Sea_ (~60 C.E.) together describe India''s eastern trade, ports and commercial towns during Satavahana times.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p3", "partName": "The Original Home of the Satavahanas — Historians'' Debate", "bodyMd": "_(A genuine, unresolved dispute among historians.)_\n\n**1\\. Coastal Andhra Argument** — R.G. Bhandarkar, V.A. Smith, Barnet and J. Rapson placed the homeland between the Godavari and Krishna rivers, with **Dhanyakatakam (Amaravati)** as the first capital.\n\n*   V.A. Smith and Dr. Burgess instead favoured **Srikakulam** as the first capital.\n*   D.R. Bhandarkar argued for **\"Andhranagari\"** on the river Telivaha.\n*   Gurti Venkat Rao held that the dynasty was born in Coastal Andhra and spread east to west, with the capital shifting to Dhanyakatakam only later.\n\n**2\\. Maharashtra Argument** — P.T. Srinivasa Ayangar, K. Gopalachary, D.C. Sircar and Bhandary argued for Maharashtra instead, with **Prathishtanapuram (Paithan)** as the first capital, holding that Vashistiputra Pulumavi was the one who conquered Andhra Desa. Their case rests on four points:\n\n*   The earliest inscriptions are found only at Naneghat and Nasik.\n*   Kharavela''s Hathigumpha inscription records his invasion of Satakarni''s kingdom.\n*   Gautami Balasri''s Nasik inscription doesn''t mention Andhra areas within Gautamiputra''s empire.\n*   The Amaravati inscription — issued by Vashistiputra Pulumavi — is the first Satavahana record found in Andhra at all, with none of the earlier rulers represented there.\n\n**3\\. Vidarbha Argument** — V.V. Mirasi proposed Vidarbha instead, arguing the Satavahanas were \"Andhrabhrityas\" rather than Andhras proper.\n\n*   He pointed to Gautamiputra Satakarni calling himself \"benakatasami\" in a Nasik inscription, identifying \"Katakam on the Bena river\" with the Kanhan (a Wainganga tributary) as the likely capital.\n*   This view found little support beyond suggesting Vidarbha was simply one part of the empire under Satakarni I and Gautamiputra.\n\n**4\\. Karnataka Argument** — Dr. V.S. Suktankar argued for Ballari (Karnataka).\n\n*   He cited the Mykadoni inscription of Pulumavi (the last Satavahana ruler, found in Kurnool district, referring to a place called \"Satavahanahara\") and the Hirahadagalli copper-plate inscription of the Pallava king Shivaskanda Varma IV (referring to \"Satavahana Ratta\").\n*   Suktankar was also the first to propose that the Satavahanas were a sub-dynasty among the Andhras, and that \"Andhra\" (of the Puranas) and \"Satavahana\" (of the inscriptions) referred to two different ruling groups — Andhras in the Andhra region, Satavahanas in Maharashtra.\n\n**5\\. Telangana Argument** — Ajaymitra Sastry, Deme Raji Reddy, Thakur Rajaram Singh, Krishna Sastry and archaeologist P.V. Parabrahma Sastry built their case on coin distribution.\n\n*   Chimuka Satavahana and Satakarni coins turn up at Kotilingala, Peddabankur, Dhulikatta, Kadambapur and Kondapur — all in Telangana.\n*   Since Srimuka''s coins are found only at Kotilingala, he must have founded the dynasty there, making Kotilingala the first capital.\n*   Kharavela''s claim (in the Hathigumpha inscription) of invading the Satavahana kingdom from the west lends further support.\n*   On the whole, the coin and inscription evidence points fairly clearly to the Satavahanas establishing their kingdom at Kotilingala and extending first into southern Maharashtra — though nothing confirms it reached Coastal Andhra first from Telangana.\n\n### Are the Satavahanas and the Andhras One?\n\n**1.** E.J. Rapson, R.G. Bhandarkar, L.D. Barnett, Maramanda Rama Rao hold that the Satavahanas of the inscriptions and the Andhras of the Puranas are simply the same people under two names: \"Andhra\" denoting nationality, \"Satavahana\" the ruling family.\n\n*   The genealogies in the Matsya and Vayu Puranas line up with those in the inscriptions.\n*   The dynasty went by several names in different sources — Salivahanas, Satakarnis, Andhras, Andhrabhrityas.\n*   Hala himself used Salivahana, Satavahana and Satakarni interchangeably in the Gathasaptasati.\n*   The grammarian Hemachandra considered \"Salivahana\" simply a corrupted form of \"Satavahana.\"\n*   The Jain writer Jinaprabha Suri offered a folk etymology: the name arose because \"hundreds of vehicles\" (satavahana) were donated by the dynasty''s founder.\n\n**2.** H.C. Roychowdary and Srinivas Sastry, building on P.V. Parabrahma Sastry''s Telangana-origin theory, read the evidence differently.\n\n*   Under Samagopa, who ruled from Kotilingala, a commander named Sirisatavahana served him before declaring independence — which is why the Puranas remember the dynasty as \"Andhrabhrityas,\" former servants of the Andhras.\n*   This camp also points out that the Satavahanas used Prakrit while Telugu was the Andhras'' language, treating this as evidence the two were genuinely distinct peoples.\n\n**3.** A traditional story, recorded in the Kathasaritsagaram (based on Gunadhya''s Brihatkatha), explains where the name \"Satavahana\" came from — not a historical argument like points 1 and 2, but a passed-down tale.\n\n*   A Yaksha (semi-divine being) named Sata existed, in the shape of a lion.\n*   King Deepakarni killed this lion-shaped Yaksha.\n*   Inside it, a boy was found, whom Deepakarni raised as his own.\n*   Because the boy was discovered inside the Yaksha \"Sata,\" he was named Satavahana — the story behind the dynasty''s name.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p4", "partName": "Chronology", "bodyMd": "*   Satavahana chronology is a genuinely difficult problem. Historians disagree sharply on when the dynasty **began**, though there''s broad agreement it **ended around 225–230 C.E.**\n*   The Puranic accounts themselves conflict: \n\n*   The **Matsya Purana** lists 30 Andhra kings ruling 456 years, while the **Vayu Purana** counts only 17 kings over 272 years — and several named rulers have no surviving inscriptions or coins to confirm them.\n*   Working from **Pargitar''s** Puranic king-list, scholars like V.V. Mirasi, Dr. K. Gopalachary, Gurti Venkat Rao and Maramanda Rama Rao support a **\"long chronology\"** of roughly 450 years, running from 220 B.C.E. to 225 C.E. — Gurti Venkat Rao ties this to the succession war after Bindusara''s death in 271 B.C.E., during which **Simuka** seized the opportunity to declare independence.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p5", "partName": "Caste of the Satavahanas", "bodyMd": "*   Scholars disagree on the Satavahanas'' caste — the evidence points in several different directions.\n*   The **Brahmin case**: Gautamiputra Satakarni is called \"**Eka Brahmana**\" (unparalleled Brahmin), \"Kshatriya Darpamana Mardana\" and \"Dvijavara Kutumba Vivardhana\" in the Nasik inscription, and Satavahana kings took their **mother''s gotra**, which some read as an official declaration of Brahmin descent.\n*   **Kshatriya case**: Governance was seen as a Kshatriya''s duty. Gautami Balasri is called \"Rajarshi Vadhuvu\" and \"Kshatriya Patni\" in her Nasik inscription, and the dynasty performed **Asvamedha and Rajasuya** sacrifices — rites traditionally reserved for Kshatriyas.\n*   A **Naga-race theory** draws on the _Dvatrimsat Puttalika_, which claims the dynasty''s founder was born to a Naga king of the Godavari coast and a Brahmin woman.\n*   **Sudra**: The _Bhagavata Purana_ calls Simuka \"**Vrishaludu**,\" implying mixed parentage; in Kannada, \"Satavahana\" means \"**ryot**\" (farmer) — taken as pointing to Sudra origins.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p6", "partName": "Satavahana Rule — Political Developments", "bodyMd": "Historians split the dynasty into **Early Satavahanas** (from the founding up to Gautamiputra Satakarni''s accession) and **Later Satavahanas** (from Gautamiputra Satakarni to the last king).\n\n### Early Satavahanas\n\n**Simuka**\n\n*   Coins bearing the names \"Sadavahana,\" \"Satavahana\" and \"Chimuka\" have surfaced at Kondapur, Kotilingala, near Warangal, Aurangabad, Junnar and Nevasa. Based on the script on these coins, Ajayamitra Sastry and Dr. Deme Raji Reddy identify Sadavahana with Srimuka, and hold that **Simuka was the only ruler who actually bore the name \"Satavahana.\"**\n*   The Puranas describe **Srimuka**, of the Andhra race, killing **Susarma** of the Kanva dynasty, ending Sunga authority, and founding the kingdom (they also name Sindhaka, Balapuchchaka and Bali). His coins are found only at **Kotilingala** — clearly his capital — and appear under three names: \"Srichimuka,\" \"Sirisatavahana\" and \"Siri Chimuka Satavahana.\" The Naneghat inscription records him as \"Raya Simuka Satavahana Sirimato.\"\n*   Jain accounts say he embraced Jainism and built several temples, and that he ruled **23 years**; but they also describe him turning cruel and anti-people in his final years, leading to his deposition and murder — a portrayal likely coloured by the fact that Simuka reverted to Vedic dharma toward the end of his reign.\n\n**Krishna (Kanha)**\n\n*   Simuka''s brother and successor; the Puranas credit him with an **18-year** reign. He was the **first Satavahana ruler to issue an inscription** — his Nasik record mentions a Mahamatra constructing a cave for Buddhist monks, still using Mauryan administrative terminology. Under him the kingdom''s western boundary extended as far as **Nasik**.\n\n**Satakarni I**\n\n*   Third in the Puranic list and son of Srimuka, Satakarni I ruled **18 years** and is the ruler named in the Naneghat inscription. His wife **Nayanika (Naganika)**, daughter of Maharathi Tranakayiro, is shown there alongside their sons — **Kumara Hakusri Sati Srimat**, **Kumara Satavahana**, and the elder son **Vedisri**. After Satakarni''s death, Naganika ran the administration herself until Vedisri came of age. Satakarni performed **two Asvamedha sacrifices and one Rajasuya**, plus 20 other rites, donating thousands of Karshapanas along with cows, elephants and horses — earning him the titles \"Apratihata Chakra,\" \"Veera,\" \"Sura\" and \"Dakshinapathipati\" from Naganika.\n*   The Hathigumpha inscription records that Kharavela, in his second regnal year, sent his army west — \"not caring for Satakarni\" — reaching the Kanna-Bena river and attacking Mushika Nagaram. Dr. R. Subrahmanyam identifies Mushika Nagaram with Kondapur, though the _Chulla Kalinga Jatakam_ instead credits Asmaka king Aruna with conquering Kalinga. \n*   The Matsya Purana lists a further **18 kings** between Satakarni I and Gautamiputra Satakarni (whom it numbers as the 23rd king), but not one of them is confirmed by an inscription or coin.\n\n**Satakarni II**\n\n*   The sixth ruler in the Puranic list, credited with a **56-year** reign. Some scholars attribute the Sanchi Stupa inscription to him, and he is said to have taken **Malwa** from the Sakas.\n\n**The rulers after Satakarni II**\n\n*   Eight kings followed in succession — Lambodara, Apeelaka, Meghaswati, Swati, Skanda Swati, Mrigendra Swatikarni, Kuntala Satakarni and Swati Karni. A large copper coin found in Chanda district is thought to belong to **Apeelaka**, the eighth of these, who ruled **12 years**. During the reign of **Pulumavi I**, raids by the **Sakas and Pahlavas** in the northwest created serious instability, and efforts to bring his authority to an end failed. After his death, **Saka king Nahapana** attacked, occupied part of Maharashtra, and took the title **Mahakshatrapa**, defeating **Sundara Satakarni, Chakora Satakarni and Sivaswati** in the process.\n\n**Hala**\n\n*   The **17th king** of the dynasty, and a genuine literary figure in his own right — earning the title \"**Kavivatsala**\" and composing the **Gathasaptasati (Sattasayi)**, according to Bana''s _Harsha Charitra_. He married the Simhala princess **Lilavati**, a union the _Lilavati Kavya_ (by Kutuhala) places at Saptama Godavari before the god Bheemeshwara — a site **Dr. Sanganabhatla Narsaiah** identifies as present-day Vempalli Venkatrao pet in Karimnagar district.\n\n### Later Satavahanas\n\n**Gautamiputra Satakarni (106–130 C.E.)**\n\nYou''re right — the sub-heading treatment for Gautamiputra makes the plain paragraphs for the other rulers stick out and feel inconsistent. Here''s the full \"Later Satavahanas\" section with matching sub-bullet breakdowns for all of them:", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p7", "partName": "Later Satavahanas", "bodyMd": "### Gautamiputra Satakarni (106–130 C.E.)\n\n*   Widely regarded as the **greatest** of all Satavahana rulers, and 23rd in the Puranic list, he ruled effectively for **24 years**. Everything known about his conquests comes from a single source: the **Nasik inscription** his mother **Gautami Balasri** issued after his death, in the 18th regnal year of his son Vasishtiputra Pulumavi.\n\n**Conquests and empire**\n\n*   Defeated the **Sakas, Yavanas and Pallavas**, and **uprooted the Kshatrapa dynasty**.\n*   Conquered **Anupa, Aparanta, Saurashtra, Kukura and Avanti**.\n*   Empire stretched across: Asika (Hyderabad, Medak), Asmika (Nizamabad, Aurangabad), Mulaka (Paithan area), Aparanta (Bombay, Konkan, Puna), Anoopa (upper Mahishmati), Vidarbha (Nagapur, Berar), Akara (Vidisa, eastern Malwa), Avanti (Ujjain, western Malwa), Saurashtra (South Kathiawad) and Kukura (South Rajasthan).\n\n**Titles**\n\n*   Titled \"lord of the mountain regions\" — Vindhya, Achavata, Pariyata, Vahya, Kanvagiri, Siritana, Malaya, Mahendra, Sita, Chekora.\n*   The Nasik inscription adds further epithets: \"Satavahanakula Yasah Pratistapana Kara,\" \"Kshatriya Darpamana Mardana,\" \"Ksharahata Vamsa Niravasheshakara,\" \"Saka, Yavana, Pahlava Nishudana,\" \"Eka Brahmana,\" \"Eka Sura,\" \"Eka Dhanurdhana,\" \"Agama Nilaya,\" and \"Trisamudratoyapitavahana\" — one whose horses drank the water of three seas.\n\n**Evidence**\n\n*   Victory over Nahapana confirmed materially: **9,270** of Nahapana''s silver coins, restruck by Gautamiputra, found at **Jogalthembi**.\n*   Issued two inscriptions in the Nasik caves, in his **18th and 24th** regnal years.\n*   Coins turn up in the hundreds at **Kondapur and Peddabankur**.\n\n### Vashishtiputra Pulumavi (Pulumavi II, 130–154 C.E.)\n\n*   Inscriptions spread across **Nasik, Karle, Amaravati, Myakadoni and Banavasi**.\n*   Ruled from **Dhanyakatakam**.\n*   Called \"**Dakshinapatiswara**\" in Gautami Balasri''s Nasik inscription.\n\n### Vashishtiputra Satakarni (154–165 C.E.)\n\n*   The Kshatrapa **Rudradaman**, in his **Junagadh inscription**, claims to have twice defeated a \"Satakarni, lord of Dakshinapatha\" — very likely this ruler.\n*   His silver and lead coins turn up across the Godavari and Krishna regions.\n*   Mentioned in a **Kanheri inscription** issued by his queen, who identifies herself as the daughter of Mahakshatrapa Rudradaman.\n*   By this point — or by the time of his predecessor Vashishtiputra Pulumavi — the Satavahanas appear to have already lost their Maharashtrian territory to the Kshatrapas.\n\n### Yagnasri Satakarni (165–194 C.E.)\n\n*   The **last great Satavahana ruler**, 26th in the Puranic list, with a reign the Puranas put at **29 years**.\n*   Inscriptions: two survive at **Kanheri**, with others at **Nasik and Chinaganjam** (Krishna district).\n*   Coins bearing a **ship with two sails** attest to continued maritime activity.\n*   Patronized **Acharya Nagarjuna** and Buddhism; praised as \"**Trisamudradhiswara**\" in Bana''s _Harsha Charitra_.\n*   The _Katha Saritsagara_ records that Nagarjuna was killed by the Satavahana crown prince — plunging the kingdom into chaos.\n*   Yagnasri was ultimately **defeated by Rudradaman**, losing the western part of the empire.\n\n### Decline\n\n*   After Yagnasri, three kings — **Vijaya Satakarni, Chandasri and Pulumavi IV** — ruled a combined **17 years**.\n*   **Pulumavi IV** was the last of the line (a Myakadoni inscription of his, from Kurnool district, survives).\n*   The empire unravelled after him, as the **Abhiras, Ikshavakus, Chutus and Pallavas** broke away, establishing their own independent kingdoms and bringing the Satavahana age to a close.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p8", "partName": "Administrative Organization", "bodyMd": "The Satavahanas began by **following Mauryan administrative traditions**, and in turn became the model later dynasties copied. Most of what is known comes from the **Nasik and Karle cave inscriptions**.\n\n### Powers of the Emperor\n\n*   The king headed the state as supreme authority and supreme commander-in-chief, ruling as a **hereditary monarch**. Some kings took their mother''s name, but royal power itself remained strictly with the males, who bore titles like Raja and Maharaja. Kings performed **Asvamedha and Rajasuya** sacrifices, and over time the idea of **divine right monarchy** took hold — kings were seen as embodying the qualities of Puranic figures like Rama and Vishnu, expected to be bound by the Dharmasastras and to protect Dharma.\n\n### Administrative Staff\n\n*   The king was assisted by **Amatyas, Mahamatras and Visvasamatyas**, and a wider body of officials: **Maharathis** (provincial governors), **Mahabhojakas** (governor-equivalent status), **Mahasenapati** (army commander), **Bhandarika** (keeper of income-in-kind), **Heranika/Hiranyaka** (treasurer), **Dutakas** (spies), **Lekhaka** (the emperor''s security), **Nibandhakara** (registered documents), **Mahataraka** (bodyguard), a second sense of **Mahamatras** (overseeing Buddhist-monk affairs), and **Gramaka** (village officials).\n\n### Administrative Units\n\n*   The kingdom was organized into three tiers: the **Rajakamkheta** region under the king''s direct rule, the **feudatory areas**, and the **border areas**. \n*   Feudatories included the Chutus (Mysore), Kurus (Kolhapur), Ikshavakus (Vijayapuri), and the Maharathis/Mahabhojas of Maharashtra, with the crown maintaining marriage alliances with several of them; toward the end of the dynasty, border defence fell to army commanders, who gradually turned into landed feudal lords in their own right.\n*   Below that, the kingdom was divided into **Aharas** (provinces) governed by Amatyas — Soparahara, Govardhanahara (Nasik), Mamalahara and Satavahana among them, often named after their capitals. Cities were run by **Nigama Sabhas**, which doubled as trade associations, with caste elders called **Gahapatis** serving as members — the Bhattiprolu inscription references one such Sabha. \n*   Major cities of the era included Kotilingala, Dhulikatta, Yeleswaram (Nalgonda) and Kondapur in Telangana; Dhanyakatakam and Vijayapuri (Nagarjunakonda) in Andhra Pradesh; Govardhana, Sopara and Pratistanapura (Paithan) in Maharashtra; and Brahmagiri in Karnataka.\n\n### The Rise of Feudatory Power\n\n*   Feudalism crept gradually into the administrative system as regional government passed into the hands of feudatory chiefs, who paid annual tribute and supplied troops in exchange for holding the semi-independent posts of **Mahabhojas and Maharathis** — many of whom became fully independent once the Satavahanas declined. \n*   **D.D. Kosambi** points to the **Myakadoni inscription** (from the 8th regnal year of Sri Pulumavi) as the earliest concrete evidence of this process: a **Mahasenapati**, in exchange for maintaining his post, was granted rights over an entire **Ahara (district)** for army upkeep, and in turn leased one village to a subordinate official. Village administration itself sat with **Gaulmukas (Gulmika)** — who were, again, army officers.\n\n### Village Administration\n\n*   The village was the administrative unit below the Ahara, run by a \"**Gramika**\" (also Gumika/Gulmika/Galmika), who sometimes oversaw several villages at once. Only army officers held this post, whose core duties were **collecting taxes** and **keeping law and order**; because the position became **hereditary**, village officers steadily accumulated political strength — a dynamic that itself fed the rise of feudalism. Villagers, meanwhile, were subject to **vetti** (forced labour) for household and field work; the _Kamasutra_ even describes the specific kinds of vetti expected of women.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p9", "partName": "Economic Development", "bodyMd": "### Agriculture\n\n*   Agriculture was the dominant occupation, built around **rice, jowar, jute, gingelly and sugarcane** as the major crops, with **wheat, red gram, black gram, castor, coconut and cotton** grown alongside. Oxen, asses and he-buffaloes did the tilling; wells were the main water source, supplemented by canals and embankments, with a device called the \"**Persian Wheel**\" — buckets arranged in a circle and turned by animal power — used to lift water from wells and canals. Villagers cultivating wasteland (banjar) did so as **vetti**.\n*   **Land and village grants**: Rulers didn''t simply seize land — they bought it from the ryots first, then granted it for religious institutions, agriculture, or tree-planting.\n    \n    *   **Mugudasa** donated land to clothe an ascetic.\n    *   **Ushavadatta** (Kshatrapa Nahapana''s son-in-law) bought land for **4,000 Karshapanas** from a Brahmin named Asvabhooti, then donated it.\n    *   **Gautamiputra Satakarni** was the first to grant land to Buddhist monks with full administrative and financial rights, tax-free — marking the start of land grants to Brahmins and Buddhist monks in the region.\n\n### Land Tax & Revenue\n\n*   Land tax formed the government''s main income, generally set at **one-sixth of the crop yield** and collected as either **Rajabhoga** or **Devameya**. The Nasik inscription notes that Gautamiputra Satakarni levied taxes in line with Dharma and justice. Beyond land tax, the state also drew duties on highways, mines, ports and passages, a profession tax called **Karukara**, a government monopoly on **salt manufacture**, and income from penalties — payable in either cash or kind. **Brahmins and ascetics were exempt from all taxation.**\n\n### Crafts\n\n*   Cottage industries thrived on the iron ore deposits of Medak, Nizamabad, Karimnagar and Warangal. At **Peddabankur** (200 B.C.E.–200 C.E.), burnt bricks — including roof tiles — were in wide use, alongside 22 brick-lined wells and constructed drainage canals.\n*   Inscriptions name **18 distinct crafts**: Kolikas (weavers), Tilapasikas (oil extractors), Kasakaras (brass workers), Kularikas (potters), Tasakaras (shining-layer creators), Kamaras (blacksmiths), Malakaras (florists), Odayantrikas (water-machine makers), Suvarnakaras (goldsmiths), Vadhakas (carpenters), Selavadhakas (stone workers), Avasenis (handicrafters), Lekhakas (writers), Charmakara (leather workers), Pesakaras (basket makers), Mithikas (stone polishers), Gadhikas (perfume makers) and Manikaras (diamond workers). \n*   Craftsmen organized themselves into **guilds**, each headed by a \"**Sreshti**\" — Junnar inscriptions mention the Dhannuka (grain), Kasakara and Tesakara guilds, while Nasik records the Kularika, Tilapashika and Odayantrika guilds. These guilds effectively functioned as banks, taking deposits and issuing loans: a Junnar inscription records a Buddhist depositor''s income being set aside for transplantation, and a grain merchants'' guild donating a seven-room cave with its own water source.\n*   A **Nasik inscription of Ushavadatta** records a handloom weavers'' guild at Govardhana holding two deposits:\n\n*   **2,000 Karshapanas at 1% per month** — interest used to clothe Buddhist monks who stayed at Nasik during the rainy season (12 Karshapanas per monk).\n*   **1,000 Karshapanas at 3/4% per month** — interest used for the monks'' other needs.\n\n### Trade — Internal & External\n\n*   Merchants engaged in foreign trade were called **Sarthavahas**, themselves organized into guilds under a **Sreshti (Setti)**. Major trade centers included Pratistanapuram, Tagara, Junnar, Nasik and Vaijayanti in Maharashtra; Dhanyakatakam, Vijayapuri, Gudur (Krishna district) and Kantakasila (Ghantasala) in Andhra Pradesh; and **Kotilingala, Dhulikatta and Kondapur** in Telangana — with the **bullock cart** as the main mode of transport.\n*   India–Rome trade is documented in the _Periplus of the Erythrean Sea_, Ptolemy''s _Geography_, and Pliny''s _Natural History_. Roman coins have surfaced at Nellore, Attirala (Kadapa), Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda, Vinukonda, Chebrolu, Ongole and Nandyal in Andhra Pradesh, and at Gootiparti, Akkinapalli, Yeleswaram (Nalgonda), Nustulapur, Peddabankur (Karimnagar) and Nagavarappadu (Khammam) in Telangana; gold coins turn up at **Arikamedu** (Tamil Nadu, near Pondicherry), a major Roman trading post. Pliny records that **1.25 crore dinars** flowed from Rome to India every year.\n*   India''s exports to Rome ranged widely: slave women, animals, ghee, gingelly oil, silk and silk cloth, cotton cloth, pepper, herbs, betel leaves, jaggery, sandalwood, teakwood, coconuts and coconut oil, garlic, tamarind, spices, steel and spun cotton, along with pearls from Sri Lanka and Indian diamonds — the _Periplus_ specifically notes bulk pepper exports from **Mujiris (Cranganore, Kerala)**. In return, India imported silver, copper, glass articles, cosmetics and intoxicants. Because the balance of trade consistently favoured India, **Roman gold flowed steadily into the country**. Trade moved through western ports — Sopar (Sopara), Kalyan (Kalyani), Bharukachham (Broach) — and eastern ones, including Kantakasela (Ghantasala), Koddura (Gudur), Mysolia (Machilipatnam) and Allosignelu (Adurru), as Ptolemy records.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p10", "partName": "Social Organization", "bodyMd": "The picture of Satavahana society is pieced together from the _Katha Saritsagaram_, Kutuhala''s _Lilavati_, Hala''s _Gathasaptasati_, Gunadhya''s _Brihatkatha_, and the sculptures at Amaravati, Bhattiprolu and Nagarjunakonda.\n\n*   The **four-fold varna system** — Brahmins, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas, Sudras — took firm root in Telangana during this period, as Aryan cultural influence spread through the region. Brahmins studied the Vedas and Vedangas and enjoyed both respect and tax exemption; Kshatriyas learned governance and warfare; Vaishyas ran trade and business; Sudras took up agriculture. \n*   **Foreigners** — Greeks, Sakas, Pahlavas, Kushans — assimilated readily into this framework, gaining recognition as Kshatriyas and adopting Hinduism or Buddhism: Nahapana''s daughter Dakshamitra, his son-in-law Ushavadatta, Chastana''s son Jayadamana, and Saka Rudradaman all took Hindu names. The caste system itself was far from rigid — inter-caste marriage was recorded, most notably Hala''s marriage to a Sri Lankan princess (per the _Lilavati Kavya_), Satakarni I''s marriage to the daughter of Maharathi Tranakayiro, and another Satavahana ruler''s marriage to the daughter of Saka Rudradaman. **Gautamiputra Satakarni**, by contrast, took a more conservative line, proudly declaring that he worked to root out varna-violation and restore the purity of the system — a striking tension with the intermarriage evident elsewhere.\n*   The **joint family** was the norm — donation records routinely list every family member. Women enjoyed real respect and freedom: upper-class women carried titles reflecting their husbands'' rank (Mahabhoja, Mahasenapati, Mahatalavarini), and several Satavahana rulers took their **mother''s name** before their own — Gautamiputra Satakarni, Gautamiputra Yagnasri Satakarni, Vasishtiputra Pulumavi and Kausakiputra Satakarni among them. \n*   Some women went further and took on administrative roles directly: **Naganika**, Satakarni I''s widow, governed as guardian to her son Vedisri, and **Gautami Balasri**''s Nasik inscription records donations she made independently. Women also invested in trading guilds in their own right.\n*   **Royal women** in particular were generous **patrons of Buddhist monks.**\n*   Craft-based castes were also forming during this period — Halika (agriculturist), Gadhika (perfumer), Vadhika (carpenter), Kolikas (weavers), Tilapisaka (gingelly-oil extraction), Kamara (blacksmith), Kularika (potter), Udayantrika (irrigation specialist), Dhannuka (grain merchant), Vasakara (bamboo worker).\n*   What is known of dress and ornament — clay bangles, anklets, beads, earrings, shoulder and leg bands — comes largely from excavations at Kotilingala, Dhulikatta, Peddabankur, Kandambapur and Kondapur, backed up visually by the Amaravati sculptures and paintings; both men and women wore jewellery. Music, dance, and pastimes like bullock and cock competitions filled people''s leisure time. \n*   **Slavery** was also present — slaves worked in wealthy households, factories, fields and businesses, and Gunadhya''s _Brihatkatha_ references an active slave trade in which slaves were, by its account, treated poorly.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p11", "partName": "Developments in the Religious Field", "bodyMd": "Vedic religion, Buddhism and Jainism all flourished side by side in the Deccan under the Satavahanas.\n\n### Vedic Religion\n\n*   A number of Satavahana rulers were themselves committed to Vedic practice.\n\n*   **Satakarni I** performed extensive Vedic rites, donating thousands of Karshapanas and cows to Brahmins (as per the Naneghat inscription), and named one of his sons \"**Vedasri**.\" **Gautamiputra Satakarni**, styled \"Eka Brahmana,\" went further still, holding up Puranic figures like Rama, Kesava, Arjun and Bhimsen as ideals alongside traditional Sastric learning (Nasik inscription). \n*   The Naneghat inscription itself records prayers to Sankarshana, Vasudeva, Indra, the Sun and Moon, and the Dikpalakas — Vasava, Kubera, Varun, Yama.\n\n*   Over this period, religion visibly shifted from rite-heavy **Vedic practice** toward a more devotional **Puranic Hinduism**. The recurring names Sankarshana, Vasudeva, Kesava, Govardhana, Krishna and Gopala mark the emergence of **Vaishnavism** — the _Gathasaptasati_ praises Krishna as \"Madhusudhana\" and \"Damodara\" and references the gopikas and Yasoda, with Krishna–Vasudeva worship forming the root from which Bhagavata philosophy, and later full Vaishnava faith, grew. \n*   The **Gathasaptasati** also shows worship of **Pasupati and Gouri**. By the 2nd century B.C.E., **Siva worship** had begun in earnest — the oldest and largest surviving **Sivalinga** stands at **Gudimallam** (Chittoor district).\n    \n    *   **Pasupata Saivism** was formally established in the 1st century C.E. by **Lakulisa Sivacharya** and spread south during Satavahana rule.\n    *   **Shakti philosophy** also began during this period.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p12", "partName": "Jainism", "bodyMd": "*   Broadly, **Buddhism dominated Coastal Andhra** while **Jainism took stronger hold in Telangana**.\n\n**Traditional/legendary roots**\n\n*   **Rishabhanatha**, the first Tirthankara, is said to have ruled Varanasi.\n*   His eldest son **Bahubali** is said to have ruled Asmaka (Bodhan).\n*   The tenth Tirthankara, **Seetalanatha**, is said to have been born at **Bhadrachalam (Badalapuri)**, Khammam district.\n\n**Spread into the Deccan**\n\n*   Linked to a 4th-century-B.C.E. famine in Magadha, which drove Jains under **Bhadrabahu** to migrate to **Shravana Belagola** for twelve years.\n*   On their return, they carried Jainism into Karnataka, Andhra and Telangana.\n*   **Samprati**, Ashoka''s grandson, later converted to Jainism and sent Jain preachers into the Andhra region.\n\n**Satavahana-era evidence**\n\n*   The dynasty''s founder, **Srimuka**, was himself a follower of Jainism.\n*   Four stone shades for Jain monks survive at **Munulagutta**, near his capital Kotilingala — likely used for \"**Sallekhana**\" (ritual fasting unto death).\n*   The _Kalakasuri Prabhandha_ mentions an unnamed Satavahana king patronizing a Jain monk at Pratistanapura.\n*   A Kharavela inscription found at **Guntupalli** (West Godavari district) is thought by some historians to belong to a Jain cave complex.\n\n**Key Jain thinkers**\n\n*   **Acharya Kondakunda** (also Padmanandi) lived in the 1st century C.E. at **Konakondla** (Kurnool district), earning the name \"Kondakundacharya.\" He authored _Samayasara, Mulacara, Panchashaktiya_ and _Pravachanasara_, and propagated the philosophy of \"**Anekantavada**.\"\n*   Other notable acharyas: **Simhanandi** and **Arhabali**.\n*   The period also saw the **council of Venakatatipuri** (identified with present-day Vijayawada), where Jains made their first attempt at compiling a formal canon.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p13", "partName": "Buddhism", "bodyMd": "*   Founded by **Buddha** in the 6th century B.C.E., Buddhism reached Telangana and Andhra within his own lifetime.\n*   Tibetan Buddhist tradition holds that Buddha visited **Amaravati** at the request of Suchendra, king of Shambala, and taught the \"Kalachakramoola Tantropadesam\" there — a Tantric method from the highest yoga tantra.\n\n**Even earlier roots — before Ashoka**\n\n*   The _Suttanipada_''s **Bavari story** tells of Bavari, living on Kavitavana island on the Godavari (in Asmaka — Karimnagar, Nizamabad, Adilabad), who sent 16 disciples to meet Buddha at Rajagriha.\n*   They returned having accepted Buddhism, and Bavari himself followed.\n*   Some scholars place Bavari''s habitation at **Bavanakurti**, Karimnagar district.\n\n**Councils and early spread**\n\n*   A century after Buddha''s Mahaparinirvana, the **Second Buddhist Council** at Vaisali saw young Andhra monks, led by the monk **Mahadeva**, push for changes to Sangha regulations.\n*   Orthodox monks rejected this, splitting the Sangha into **Theravadins** and **Mahasanghikas**.\n*   The Mahasanghika branch may well have originated at **Dhanyakatakam** itself.\n*   Ashoka''s 13th rock edict confirms Andhras were already following Buddhism by his time.\n*   Andhra monks (\"Andhakas\") took part in the **Third Buddhist Council** at Pataliputra, where the \"Kathavathu\" debates were held.\n*   By the 2nd century B.C.E., Buddhism had put down deep roots in Telangana — the stupas at **Dhulikatta and Pasegam** stand as evidence. The Pasegam stupa, per B.N. Sastry, was built by the Buddhist scholar **Chennapoosa** (the site''s older name, \"Chennupoosagam,\" derives from his name, \"Poosa\" meaning \"Acharya\").\n\n**Patronage**\n\n*   Satavahana **kings**, though personally Vedic, extended patronage to Buddhism; the **queens patronized it even more visibly** — Gautamiputra Satakarni, honouring his mother Gautami Balasri, donated an entire village at **Trirasmi** to Buddhist monks.\n*   Ceylon''s _Mahavamsa_ records **4,60,000 monks** attending a stupa ceremony at Anuradhapura, built by the Sri Lankan king **Duppigamini** (1st century B.C.E.), a contemporary of the Satavahanas.\n*   Patronage extended well beyond royalty — officials, merchants, craftsmen and wealthy women competed to donate villages, land and wealth to monks; Nasik, Junnar and Kanheri inscriptions record guilds depositing funds whose interest went to monastic upkeep.\n*   Given this breadth of support, it is generally argued that **merchant patronage, not royal favour, was the real engine behind Buddhism''s growth** in the region.\n\n**Monuments built**\n\n*   Maharashtra: Bhaja, Karle, Nasik, Kanheri, Kondane, Bhedsa.\n*   Andhra Pradesh: Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda, Bhattiprolu, Salihundam, Allur, Ghantasala, Jaggayya Peta, Guntupalli, Ramatirtham, Bojjannakonda, Gummadidurru, Gudivada, Goli.\n*   Telangana: Kondapur, Phanigiri, Tirumalagiri (Nalgonda), Dhulikatta, Pasegam, Meerjampet (Karimnagar).\n\n**Mahayana vs. Hinayana**\n\n*   The 1st century C.E. saw **Mahayana Buddhism** emerge in Andhra, with the Mahasanghikas as its adherents — dubbed \"Andhaka Sakhas\" (\"blind branches\") by the critic Asvaghosha.\n*   **Hinayana** (\"small cart\"): each person must reach nirvana individually; Buddha worshipped only as a symbol.\n*   **Mahayana** (\"big cart\"): aimed to carry as many people as possible toward nirvana; treated solitary nirvana-seeking as selfish and against Buddha''s own teaching; carved and worshipped actual Buddha statues.\n*   Theravadin branches: **Bhadrayanis** (Nasik, Karle inscriptions); **Dharmottareeyulu** (Sopara).\n*   Mahasanghika branches: **Purvasaileeyas** (Amaravati); **Aparasailiyas** (Nagarjunakonda); plus **Rajagirikulu, Mahisasakas, Siddhantikulu**.\n*   In total, **18 branches** of Buddhism took shape — **9** of them in the Andhra region alone.\n\n**Chaityakas and iconography**\n\n*   One further Andhra branch, the **Chaityakas**, ascribed Buddha supreme qualities and laid the groundwork for idol worship.\n*   They introduced symbolic milestones from Buddha''s life for veneration: the lotus (birth), the horse (the Great Departure), the Bodhi Tree (enlightenment), the Dharma Chakra (first sermon), the stupa (nirvana), the trisula (the three jewels), and footprints (his continued presence).\n*   Since depicting Buddha in human form had earlier been prohibited, his **footprints** were carved instead — as at the Dhulikatta stupa, alongside a small Brahmi inscription.\n*   **Naga (serpent) worship** was woven into the same iconography: serpents curl around stupas in relief; a five-headed **nagamuchilinda** appears at Dhulikatta; at Amaravati and Nagarjunakonda, cobras raise their hoods over the seated Buddha, with serpent kings and queens shown in prayer.\n\n**Acharya Nagarjuna**\n\n*   Contemporary of Yagnasri Satakarni; remembered as a genuine polymath, often called the \"second Buddha.\"\n*   The _Lankavatara Sutram_ places his birth in Vedali village, which scholars generally locate in Telangana (near South Kosala, per the Chinese pilgrim Hiuen Tsang).\n*   Founded universities at **Nagarjunakonda and Amaravati**.\n*   Developed the philosophy of \"**Madhyamikavadam**,\" rejecting soul-philosophy and terming this position \"**Sunyavadam**\" (nothingness) — extended to Mahayana thought as a whole in his work _Madhyamika Karika_.\n*   Chinese sources credit him with 20 works; the traveller **Itsing** (~700 C.E.) found some memorized by children and read throughout life by adults, and records his letter collection \"**Suhrullekha**,\" addressed to Yagnasri Satakarni, was similarly memorized by children.\n*   Surviving Sanskrit works: _Pragnaparamita, Ashta Sahasrika, Mula Madhyamikarikavali, Vigraha Vyaptini, Dvadaya Nikayam, Sunyasaptati_.\n*   Under Mahayana influence, **Sanskrit** (rather than Prakrit) became the preferred medium for Buddhist teaching and writing.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p14", "partName": "Educational and Literary Progress", "bodyMd": "*   The period produced a substantial body of literature in **Prakrit, Pali and Sanskrit**, with **Maharastri Prakrit** serving as the regional language and leaving a lasting imprint on Telugu itself.\n*   **Hala**, the poet-king, earned the title \"**Kavi Vatsala**\" and compiled the **Gathasaptasati (Sattasayi)** — 700 Prakrit verses of considerable literary merit, containing numerous Telugu-origin words (Attam, Ammi, Andam, Addam, Potti, Padi among them) and beautiful depictions of rural, native life. He honoured poets **Kumarila and Sreepalita**, and women poets — **Anulakshmi, Anupalabda, Deva, Madhavi** — contributed verses of their own.\n*   His minister **Gunadhya** composed the **Brihatkatha** — 7,000 slokas across seven stories — in **Paisachi**, a regional Prakrit variety. **Durnita**, of the Ganga dynasty, later translated this into Sanskrit — the moral stories of the _Panchatantram_ draw on it — and the Brihatkatha in turn inspired **Somadevasuri''s** _Kathasaritsagara_ and **Kshemendra''s** _Brihatkathamanjari_ in Sanskrit, while **Sarvavarma**, a contemporary of Hala, wrote the Sanskrit grammar _Katantra Vyakaranam_. **Krishna Sastry** suggests Kondapur may have been Gunadhya''s home region.\n*   **Vatsayana''s** _Kama Sutra_, composed either in the Later Satavahana period or shortly after, treats sex and sexual life. \n*   **Kutuhala''s** _Lilavati_, written in Maharashtrian Prakrit, narrates Hala''s marriage to the Simhala princess before the god Bheemeswara — a site some identify with Vempalli Venkatrao pet, Karimnagar district.\n*   Alongside Prakrit and Sanskrit, a language called \"**desi**\" was also spoken at the time; **D.C. Sarcar** considered it the shared base of Telugu and Kannad\n*   **Universities at Dhanyakatakam and Nagarjunakonda** taught grammar, statecraft, economics, logic, mathematics, legal science, astrology and astronomy, drawing students from as far as abroad.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 15, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p15", "partName": "Progress of Architecture, Sculpture and Painting", "bodyMd": "### Architecture\n\n*   Buddhism''s growth filled the Deccan with **stupas, viharas and chaityas**. \n\n*   A **stupa** typically enshrined the remains of Buddha or Buddhist monks at its base, encircled by a cylindrical \"methi\" and capped with a half-dome; a **Pradakshina Patham** (circumambulation path) ran around it, with **Ayaka pillars, Prakaras and Toranas** at the entrances, and an umbrella-like **Harmika** crowning the whole structure. \n*   A **vihara** — the residence of monks and nuns, also called a Sangharama — was typically rectangular or square with a verandah and rooms on three sides fitted with stone sleeping benches; those with gardens for prayer and exercise were called \"**Aramas**,\" and those used only during the rainy season, closer to towns, \"**Varsharamas**.\" \n*   A **chaitya** served as the actual prayer hall — a spacious, pillared room.\n\n*   The major stupas are at **Amaravati and Nagarjunakonda**. Others are found:\n    \n    *   In Andhra: Bhattiprolu, Ghantasala, Jaggayyapeta, Salihundam, Bavikonda, Totlakonda, Sankaram (Bojjannakonda), Goli, Anupu.\n    *   In Telangana: Dhulikatta, Pasegam, Meerjampet (Karimnagar), Phanigiri (Nalgonda), Tirumalagiri.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 16, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p16", "partName": "Sculpture", "bodyMd": "*   The distinctive sculptural style of this period is known as **Amaravati art**, flourishing from the **2nd century B.C.E. to the 2nd century C.E.**, centered on Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda and Jaggayyapeta.\n*   A recurring motif shows five pillars on each side of a platform, representing the **five stages of Buddha''s life**:\n    *   Lotus — birth\n    *   Horse — Mahabhiniskramana (the Great Departure)\n    *   Bodhi Tree — enlightenment\n    *   Dharma Chakra — first sermon\n    *   Stupa — parinirvana\n*   Amaravati''s influence travelled as far as **Southeast Asia** — Annam, Thailand, Java, Sumatra, Borneo.\n*   **James Fergusson**, in his work _Tree and Serpent Worship_, ranked it as the highest achievement of the style.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 17, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p17", "partName": "Painting", "bodyMd": "*   Art critics attribute the colour paintings in **Ajanta cave numbers 9 and 10** (Aurangabad district, Maharashtra) to the Satavahana period, based on their stylistic closeness to Amaravati sculpture.\n*   These depict scenes from **Mahayana Jataka stories**:\n    *   Worship of the Bodhi Tree\n    *   A dance by a group of fifteen figures\n*   Similar period paintings survive in the cave temples of **Bedsa, Pithalkhora and Junnar**.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 18, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch1-satavahanas-p18", "partName": "Summary", "bodyMd": "Once the Mauryan empire declined, the Satavahanas rose to independence and built a vast empire that endured for roughly **two and a half centuries**, from the 1st century B.C.E. to the 3rd century C.E. Starting from **Kotilingala**, their capital later moved to **Pratistanapura** and finally **Dhanyakatakam**, and the dynasty''s most notable rulers — **Satakarni I, Gautamiputra Satakarni, Satakarni II, Vasishtiputra Pulumavi and Yagnasri Satakarni** — presided over a politically united and largely peaceful **Dakshinapatha**. \n\nTrade flourished, both internally and with Rome; towns and craft guilds prospered; and the Satavahanas proved themselves generous patrons of **Prakrit literature**. Religiously, the age saw Vedic tradition and **Buddhism** flourish side by side, Buddhism itself branching into numerous schools including Mahayana and Hinayana, and its influence shaping an entire school of art — Stupas, Chaityas and Viharas built on a scale unmatched before.\n\nAs the first dynasty to establish a truly extensive, stable, long-lasting empire in the Deccan — one that drove real economic and social development and left behind a lasting body of Prakrit and Sanskrit literature, architecture, sculpture and painting — the Satavahana age remains a genuinely **memorable chapter** in Indian history.", "free": true}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p1", "partName": "Founding and Rulers of the Bahamani Dynasty", "bodyMd": "**A.D. 1347** is a landmark year in South Indian history — Allauddin Hassan Bahaman Shah established an independent Muslim kingdom in the Deccan in this year, ruling from Gulbarga as his capital. This was the first Muslim rule established in South India, and under successive Muslim rulers the Deccan made steady progress. The Bahamani kingdom lasted from **A.D. 1347 to 1526**, and the dynasty served the Deccan by establishing a stable and extensive empire, introducing many administrative reforms along the way.\n\nThe important rulers of the Bahamani dynasty, in order:\n\n| Ruler | Reign |\n|---|---|\n| Allauddin Hassan Bahaman Shah | A.D. 1347 – 1358 |\n| Muhammad Shah Bahamani | A.D. 1358 – 1375 |\n| Mujahid Shah Bahamani | A.D. 1375 – 1378 |\n| Mohammad Shah II Bahamani | A.D. 1375 – 1397 |\n| Feroz Shah Bahamani | A.D. 1397 – 1422 |\n| Ahammada Shah Vali Bahamani | A.D. 1422 – 1436 |\n| Allauddin Shah II Bahamani | A.D. 1436 – 1457 |\n| Nizam Shah Bahamani | A.D. 1461 – 1463 |\n| Mohammad Shah III Bahamani | A.D. 1463 – 1482 |\n| Mohammad Shah Bahamani | A.D. 1482 – 1518 |\n| Kareemulla (Kalimulla) | Nominal ruler for 3 years; died **A.D. 1527** |\n\nIn all, the Bahamani kingdom was ruled by **17 sultans over 182 years**. Of these, five were murdered, two died amid disputes/troubles, three were deposed, and two were blinded — a record that reflects how turbulent the dynasty''s internal politics were even as it fought its long external contest with Vijayanagara.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p2", "partName": "Political History and Succession", "bodyMd": "### Background — Two Kingdoms Rise Against Delhi\n\nThe Bahamani kingdom and the Vijayanagara kingdom were the two independent states that emerged in opposition to the imperial power of the Delhi Sultanate. Vijayanagara was founded at Hampi in **1336**, and the Bahamani kingdom was founded at Gulbarga in **1347** (Gulbarga was, at that later time, a district of the Nizam''s state).\n\nWith the Delhi Sultanate weakening, the Amirs of the Deccan organised themselves and revolted against Muhammad bin Tughlaq, electing an elderly leader, Ismail Mukh, as their chief. Unable to bear the burden of rule, this elderly Amir passed authority to a younger man, **Hasan Ali**. On **3 August 1347**, this Hasan established the Bahamani dynasty under the title **Muzaffar Allauddin Ahmad Hasan Gangu**. The entire Deccan became independent within Muhammad bin Tughlaq''s own lifetime.\n\n### Allauddin Hasan Gangu (1347–58)\n\nAs soon as he gained power, Allauddin Ahmad Shah made Gulbarga his capital and renamed it **Hasanabad**. The Andhra ruler **Musunuri Kapaya Nayaka** had helped in the establishment of his kingdom — though it was Kapaya Nayaka who was the recognised power in the region before Hasan''s rise.\n\nHasan Gangu began claiming, as heir to the Delhi Sultanate''s authority over the south, rights over the whole Deccan. His ill conduct caused Kapaya Nayaka — already caught between the Reddy kings on one side and the Padma Nayakas on the other — great difficulty, and hostility between Hasan and the Hindu kings grew steadily. Hasan attacked Warangal and defeated Kapaya Nayaka, who paid tribute in elephants and wealth to make peace. Not satisfied with this, Hasan attacked Warangal twice more, plundering the villages along the way and destroying temples.\n\nUnable to conquer coastal Andhra, Hasan instead occupied **Bidar and Malkhed**. He died in **1358**. By then his kingdom extended north from the Ganga to the Tungabhadra in the south, and from Daulatabad in the west to Bhuvanagiri in the east. He divided his kingdom into four Tarafs — **Gulbarga, Daulatabad, Bidar, and Berar** — and appointed capable governors to administer them.\n\n### Muhammad Shah I\n\nAfter Allauddin Hasan, his elder son **Muhammad Shah** succeeded to the kingdom. He was a courageous and formidable — indeed a very cruel — ruler. He attacked Warangal and forced a harsh treaty upon Kapaya Nayaka. Breaking his own word, Muhammad Shah had Kapaya Nayaka''s son, **Vinayaka Deva**, killed by having his tongue thrown into burning coals — a brutal act that plunged Kapaya Nayaka into grief.\n\nKapaya Nayaka sought justice from the Delhi Sultan, Firoz Tughlaq, for the wrong done to him, but the self-interested Tughlaq gave him no redress; nor did Vijayanagara offer any help. Left entirely without support, the helpless Kapaya Nayaka was finally forced to make peace with the Bahamani Sultan by paying **33 lakh in cash, 300 elephants, and 200 horses** — after which only the name of the kingdom remained to him.\n\nMuhammad Shah was an extremely cruel ruler. He invaded Vijayanagara territory and devastated the region between the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers by every possible means. Professor Sherwani recorded that his court was home to renowned Ulemas. Muhammad Shah died in **1375**.\n\n### Mujahid Shah and Dawood''s Brief Usurpation\n\nAfter Muhammad Shah I, **Mujahid Shah** came to the throne. He fought wars with Bukka Raya over the Krishna–Tungabhadra doab and was defeated twice. Disgraced by this, Mujahid Shah was murdered by a man named **Dawood**, who then seized the kingdom; Mujahid Shah left no heir.\n\nTwo factions then contended for the kingdom — one backing Dawood, the other backing Allauddin''s younger son. Dawood was defeated in this struggle, and while he was reciting his Namaz, a slave killed him. Dawood''s rule lasted only **35 days**.\n\n### Muhammad Shah II (1378–1397)\n\nIt was the second Muhammad Shah who safeguarded the prestige of the Bahamani kingdom. Peace-loving, devout, and sympathetic, he regarded the protection of his subjects as his duty. During his reign the Deccan suffered a terrible famine, and he arranged for grain to be brought in from the Malwa and Gujarat regions on ten thousand bullocks — though even this proved insufficient. He did not neglect his Hindu subjects either.\n\nMuch of the credit for his victories belongs to his minister **Saif Uddin**, who served as minister across the reigns of five sultans and earned a reputation as a capable administrator. Muhammad Shah II established free schools (paathshalas) in the leading towns of Gulbarga, Bidar, Elichpur, Kandahar, and Daulatabad — though these were not secular schools, as entry was reserved for Muslims only, and famine relief from the state likewise reached only Muslim subjects. He gave special donations to the poor, and — taking advantage of this generosity — many people falsely feigned blindness or poverty to obtain government benefits.\n\nMuhammad Shah II ruled for **19 years, 5 months, and 21 days**. After him his eldest son **Ghiyasuddin** ruled for just 1 month and 22 days, followed by Muhammad Shah''s younger son **Shamsuddin**, who ruled for only 7 days. The kingdom then passed to Allauddin Hasan''s grandson, **Firoz Shah**.\n\n### Firoz Shah\n\nFiroz Shah was a capable ruler at first, but before long he turned to self-indulgence, keeping as many as eight hundred women in his harem each day. In **1389**, the grandson of the second Bukka Raya attacked Raichur in an attempt to win independence. Unable to win by fair means, Firoz Shah resorted to treachery and had the Raya''s son killed. In retaliation, the returning Vijayanagara army massacred the Bahamani kingdom''s Hindu subjects.\n\nIn a war fought in **1420**, Firoz Shah suffered defeat, and the humiliation left him bedridden. Having ruled for 25 years, Firoz Shah died in **1422**. The rulers who came after him were not his equal in capability.\n\n### Ahmad Shah (1422–1433)\n\nAhmad Shah, who succeeded Firoz Shah, ruled from 1422 to 1433. He fought a war with the Vijayanagara kingdom and won Kolhapur as part of a territorial settlement; in this fighting some 20,000 Hindu and Muslim soldiers alike were massacred. Ahmad Shah moved the capital city from Gulbarga to **Bidar** — both cities were, at a later time, part of the Nizam''s dominion.\n\n### Allauddin Bahaman Shah II (from 1436)\n\nTaking power in **1436**, the second Allauddin Bahaman Shah ruled for 23 years. He conquered the Konkan region, defeating the Hindu ruler of Sangameshwar and marrying his daughter. Though a patron of Islam, he built many schools and hospitals in Bidar, and issued prohibition orders against intoxicants. He arranged allowances for blind beggars and put them to work cleaning the streets, decreeing banishment from the kingdom for those who refused to work. He fought once with Vijayanagara and won, and fought again and lost; unable to bear the grief of that defeat, he became given to excessive drinking and died of it.\n\n### Humayun Shah \"Zalim\" (the Cruel)\n\n**Humayun Shah**, eldest son of Allauddin Ahmad Shah, committed many cruel acts even while he was crown prince, striking fear into the Amirs. Their efforts to keep him from the throne failed, and once in power he had those Amirs executed.\n\nNo ruler in Deccan history matched Humayun Shah''s cruelty. Those who opposed him were tied by their feet to elephants and dragged mercilessly through the streets; some were thrown alive into wells; some were fed to hungry tigers — and he would laugh with demonic delight while watching such acts. On the night after a wedding, every new bride in the kingdom was made to be presented to satisfy his lust, and the anguished cries of the people rose to the sky.\n\nOne day, while he lay in a drunken, deep sleep, his own mistress struck him repeatedly on the head and killed this menace to the world. Humayun Shah''s reign lasted **3 years and 6 months**.\n\n### Nizam Shah and Muhammad Shah III\n\nAfter Humayun Shah, **Nizam Shah Bahamani** took the throne as an eight-year-old boy. After some time, his brother, known as \"Mudo\" **Muhammad Shah (III)**, ascended the throne in **1463**.\n\n### The End of the Dynasty\n\nAfter Mahmud Gavan''s murder (see below), the kingdom''s foundations were shaken; self-interested Amirs seized power for themselves, and the Sultan, on learning the truth, gradually died of grief. The rulers who followed Muhammad Shah III were incapable and mired in disputes. The last ruler of the Bahamani kingdom was **Kareemulla (Kalimulla)**, who reigned only in name for three years and died in **1527**, at which point the Bahamani kingdom came to an end.\n\nWith the fall of the Bahamani kingdom, its regional governors became independent, giving rise to five successor kingdoms: the **Barid Shahi** at Bidar, the **Qutub Shahi** at Golconda, the **Adil Shahi** at Bijapur, the **Nizam Shahi** at Ahmadnagar, and the **Imad Shahi** at Berar.\n\n### The Bahamani–Vijayanagara Wars\n\nWarfare with Vijayanagara was, effectively, a standing duty of the Bahamani kings across the dynasty''s history. These wars arose largely from long-standing economic and political causes, though both sides invoked religion to rouse their soldiers. Despite committing their full strength, the Bahamani sultans could never conquer Vijayanagara; the Vijayanagara kings repelled every invasion the Bahamani sultans led against them, preserving their self-respect and independence throughout.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p3", "partName": "Sources of Bahamani History", "bodyMd": "The chief sources for reconstructing Bahamani rule fall into three broad categories: literary works, contemporary foreign accounts, and monuments.\n\n**Persian and Sanskrit literary works**\n\n*   **Fatwat-ut-Salatin**, written by Isami\n*   **Bahaman Namah**, written by Adam\n*   **Ishkanama** and **Hazairule Khuddus**, written by Gesudaraj\n*   **Tariq-I-Faristha**, written by Faristha — explains the socio-religious and cultural conditions of the period\n*   **Burhan-i-Masir**, written by Tabtaba — another chief source of Bahamani history\n\n**Contemporary foreign accounts**\n\n*   Writings of Niketin, Barbarossa, and Domingo Peas\n*   Iban Batuta''s travelogue **Rehalla**, which gives details of Bahamani rule\n*   Letters and writings of Prime Minister Mohammad Gavan, which are also a main source\n\n**Monuments and heritage**\n\n*   Jami Masjid and Gesudaraj Darga at Gulbarga\n*   Bidar fort and mosque\n*   Rangin Mahal and Turkish Mahal at Bidar\n*   The entrance gateways (darwazas) of these forts", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p4", "partName": "Administrative System", "bodyMd": "### Concept of Kingship\n\nIn the medieval period, across India, Europe, and Asia, the king was the government and possessed divine rights. According to Amir Khusru, the Khalifa was considered the head of both the worldly and the divine world. In Persia, the Sassanid rulers built their court buildings like temples, and their subjects regarded the ruler as God and themselves as his slaves. Under pressure from the Ravindiya branch, some wanted the Khalifa to declare himself God, but the Khalifa resisted this and instead declared himself the divine ambassador (**Niyabutu-l-Khudavandi**). In this spirit, Udbi wrote that the emperor or Khalifa is God''s shadow on earth (**Sultan Zill Allah-Fil-Arj**).\n\nThe Delhi Sultanate, established in A.D. 1206, carried this theory forward — rulers such as Mubarak Shah, Iltutmish, and Balban declared themselves divine slaves, divine shadows, and \"the blessed one\" (**Manjur-i-Rabbani**). Many later sultans, including the Deccan rulers and especially the Bahamani rulers, followed this same tradition of ruling as divine slaves.\n\n### The Sultan''s Authority\n\nAllauddin Hassan Bahaman Shah, founder of the kingdom, ruled from Gulbarga and established a strong administrative system built on the principle that the word of the king is the law. All authority lay in him: he was head of the army, held the highest seat of Judiciary, and also acted as Chief Justice of the kingdom.\n\n**Administrative divisions**\n\n*   For administrative convenience the kingdom was divided into **Tarafs** (provinces), placed under the control of Amirs.\n*   **Jagirs** were created within the kingdom, developing along lines similar to the feudal system.\n*   The first Prime Minister of the Bahamani kingdom, holding the title **Vakil-i-Sultanate**, was **Malik Saifuddin Ghori**. Under his administration the kingdom extended from Gulbarga (the capital) to Daulatabad in the west, and in the south up to Raichur and Mudgal.\n*   Chaul lay between the coastal area and Daulatabad. Bheed and Paithan were under the control of Mohammad Khan. In the north, Mahor, Ramgir, and parts of Berar were under the control of Sisthani. Bidar, Kaulas, and the Telangana districts were under the control of Azam Humayun.\n\n### Seven Units of Administration (under Allauddin Hassan, per Burhan-i-Masir by Tabtaba)\n\n| Officer | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Sahib-e-Arj | Army supervisor |\n| Khurbegi Mesarsh | Left wing commander |\n| Khurbegi Maimanath | Right wing commander |\n| Dabir | Secretary |\n| Diwan | Finance Minister |\n| Sayyad-Ul-Khasba | In charge of divine affairs |\n| Hajib-Ul-Khasba | Commissioner of the City |\n\n### The Seven-Member Advisory Council\n\nHassan''s successors modified the administrative system to suit changing needs, and an advisory council of seven officers was established to give advice and aid to the Sultan.\n\n| Officer | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Vakil-us-Sultanate | Prime Minister — head of the council |\n| Amir-I-Jumla | Finance Minister |\n| Vazir-i-Ahsraf | Foreign Affairs Minister |\n| Nazir | Finance Secretary |\n| Kotwal | Police Officer |\n| Vazir-I-Kul | Auditor |\n| Sadar Jahan | Member of the Judiciary |\n\nThe head of the council was the Vakil-us-Sultanate, who had one secretary. Whenever the Sultan was out of the state, the Vakil acted as Vice-Sultan or Naib-Badshah. Amir Jumla looked after revenue collection — land tax, irrigation tax, forest tax, import and export duties, and other income — and reported to the King on income and expenditure. Vazir-I-Ashraf looked after foreign affairs, sent ambassadors to various countries, and discussed socio-religious and cultural affairs with the Sultan. \n\nThe finance secretary maintained records of income, expenditure, taxes, and duties, and reported to Amir-I-Jumla on new taxes to be introduced, including on newly cultivated lands. Kotwal discharged police duties, maintained peace, law, and order, sometimes acted as a special judge, and supervised the jails. Sadar Jahan (also called Khaji-Ul-Khajat) was a member of the Judiciary who inquired into cases and ensured criminals were punished, and also looked after religious affairs.\n\nThe Sultan took special care in appointing ministers, discussing candidates personally and selecting efficient, honest, and highly talented persons. Royal family members were not appointed as ministers.\n\n### The Royal Court and Durbar\n\nAll government affairs were conducted in the royal court, attended by ministers, officers of various departments, lords, and Ulemas. General issues were discussed in the Durbar and decisions taken there; the Durbar also received complaints from regional governments and provided solutions. The kings'' orders and acts were read out in the durbar.\n\n*   Mohammad Shah I enhanced the value of the Durbar by giving a place to the **Takt-I-Feroz** (throne).\n*   The durbar was decorated with rare carpets and different colored curtains, and was filled with many bodyguards.\n*   The durbar began every Friday morning and concluded before noon.\n*   From the period of Mohammad Shah I, the honour of the durbar increased further — the Sultan received advice and instructions, and resolved controversies and treacheries through deep discussion with all present before taking a decision.\n\n### Royal Palace Administration\n\nDuring the \"Bahaman Shah\" period, administrative reforms relating to the Royal Palace were introduced, modelled on the Delhi Sultanate''s system. According to Isami, the following jobs were created for the Royal Palace:\n\n| Officer | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Vakil-I-Daar | Chief Supervisor of the Royal Palace |\n| Barbuk | Teacher of the court festivals |\n| Hajib | Welcomed and introduced guests; read the King''s messages in court |\n| Sa-Jandaz | Leader of the personal bodyguards of the Palace |\n| Sarfdadar | Special bodyguard in the Royal Palace |\n| Khurbak | Supervisor of Royal weaponry |\n| Shahnai-I-Barag | Superintendent of the Court (Durbar) |\n| Akurbuk | Supervisor of the Royal Horses |\n| Shahna-I-Phil | Supervisor of the Elephants |\n| Shahna-I-Khavan | Supervisor of the Royal Kitchen |\n| Sarabdar | Supervisor of water supply |\n| Khertadar | Additional officer of the troops |\n| Sar-Dawat-dar | Additional officer of the Royal Stationery |\n| Chesnigir | Officer who tasted the royal food |\n\nIban Batuta wrote in detail about many jobs within the Bahamani administration, and Isami similarly described many posts and the methods of recruitment used during the Bahamani period.\n\n### Provincial Administration — Tarafs, Sarkars, and Paraganas\n\nBahamani rulers divided their kingdom into four regional states known as **Tarafs**; the officer of a Taraf was called a **Tarafdar**. The four Tarafs of the Allauddin Hassan period were:\n\n| Taraf | Area Covered |\n|---|---|\n| Ahasanabad | Right side of Gulbarga to Dabhol, including the Tungabhadra and Krishna region |\n| Daulatabad | Included Junnar and Paithan |\n| Berar | Covered Mahor |\n| Bidar | Covered Kandar, Induru, Kaulas, and other occupied areas of Telangana |\n\nThese provinces were also known as **Atrafs**, and their ruler was the Tarafdar, under whom many forts were maintained. The Tarafdar looked after peace, law, and order in his area, was personally appointed by the Sultan, and on the Sultan''s advice appointed his own assistants. Civil and military affairs were looked after by the **Sar-I-Lashkar**.\n\nAllauddin Hassan gave various titles to the Tarafdars: the Daulatabad Tarafdar received the title of **Masdi Ali**, the Berar Tarafdar received **Majlis Ali**, the Telangana Tarafdar received **Azam Humayun**, and the Gulbarga Tarafdar received **Vakil-Us-Sultanate**. Under the Tarafdars there were many **Thanedars**.\n\nAdministrative hierarchy below the Taraf, in descending order:\n\n1.  **Taraf** (province) — under a Tarafdar\n2.  **Sarkar** (like a modern district) — Tarafs were sub-divided into Sarkars\n3.  **Paragana** (Tahasil) — Sarkars were further sub-divided into Paraganas\n4.  **Village** — Paraganas were divided into villages, the most important and key unit of administration\n\nForts were under the control of an officer known as **Thanedar**, responsible for maintaining peace, law, and order in the fort''s area.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p5", "partName": "Mohammad Gavan (A.D. 1466–1481)", "bodyMd": "Mohammad Gavan was the greatest Prime Minister among all the Prime Ministers of the Bahamani kingdom — indeed, among the most administratively capable Prime Ministers in the whole of Indian history. His father and grandfather had served as Vazirs under the ruler of Gilan in Iran. \n\nHe belonged to the Gavan region, near \"Refta\" in northern Iran, and reached Mustafabad-Dhabol in **A.D. 1453**, coming to the Deccan from Iran at the age of 45. He first came to India as a trader, met Shah Muhibulla (grandson of Shah Niyamutulla Kirmani), and met the Bahamani Sultan Ahammad II before settling in Bidar, from where he rose to a higher position.\n\nHe was first appointed a **Mansabdar of 1000 soldiers**, and the second Muhammad Shah honoured him further with the title of **Amir**. During Humayun Shah''s reign he earned the title **Malik-ul-Tujjar**. In **A.D. 1466**, at the request of his mother Muqduma-I-Jahan, Muhammad Shah III appointed Gavan as Prime Minister — from that day, effective administrative power passed into Gavan''s hands even though the nominal rulers continued to hold the throne. Despite wielding this power, Gavan remained loyal throughout his life and served the state faithfully.\n\n### Curbing the Power of the Foreign Amirs\n\nDuring the Bahamani sultans'' time, foreign Amirs had grown extremely powerful — for instance, **Malik Tuchar**, an Amir belonging to a Caucasus/Anatolian community, commanded two lakh soldiers, and there were other Amirs like him. Such Amirs, if they chose, were strong enough to depose a king. \n\nAs Prime Minister, Gavan reduced the strength of the Amirs who posed a danger to the kingdom, brought the armies under their control into direct state control, and reorganised them into divisions, taking firm measures to train the soldiers systematically. Through this, he turned the army into a formidable, near-invincible force.\n\n### Administrative Appointments and Reforms\n\n**Appointments made by Gavan**\n\n*   Yusuf Adil — appointed Sar-Lashkar of Daulatabad\n*   Malika Hassan — appointed Sar Lashkar of Telangana\n*   Fatullah — appointed Sar Lashkar of Berar\n*   Dariyakhan, Khasimbeg, and Shah Quli — appointed as his personal servants\n\n**Reforms introduced by Gavan**\n\n*   Introduced exact land tax assessment, classification of lands, and evaluation of soil fertility and crop yields\n*   Surveyed the lands of the kingdom\n*   Took strict measures to root out bribery and corruption\n*   Successfully reformed both the legal and military spheres\n*   Established many schools in Bidar city and built one of the biggest Madarsas there, to promote education\n*   Captured the forts of **Rajamahendravaram (Rajamundry) and Kondavidu**\n\nIn **1475**, a terrible famine struck the Deccan. Taking advantage of this, the king of Orissa attacked Telangana; Muhammad Shah repelled the attack, and wrote to the Orissa kings declaring that they had no rights whatsoever over Telangana. Muslim rule continued in Telangana without interruption for about **600 years**, from the Bahamani period down to the Nizam''s time.\n\n### Character and Personal Life\n\nGavan was an extraordinarily high-minded person who upheld many ideals. Despite holding great power in his hands, he never boasted of it. He slept on a simple leather mat and ate from earthen vessels. He performed many acts of charity, and established a great library in Bidar city, gathering together some three thousand rare manuscripts and books. Yet even such a virtuous and accomplished person could not escape the era''s religious intolerance — Gavan, too, destroyed a number of temples that lay along the routes of his military campaigns.\n\nScholars such as Maulana Nooruddin Jami (a great Persian researcher), Sheik Sadruddin, and Abdur Rehaman Ravasi came to the Deccan and settled there, contributing greatly to its literature during this period.\n\n### Sectarian Rivalry and Gavan''s Murder\n\nDuring the Bahamani kings'' time, large numbers of people who came from Persia and settled in the Deccan came to be known as **Afaqis** (or Apaki chiefs); they belonged to the Shia sect, and the Sultans showed them particular favour, giving them high posts. \n\nDeccan-settled Sunnis, who traced their standing back to representing the Delhi Sultanate in the region, formed the majority of the faithful. Rivalry between the Shia and Sunni factions in the Bahamani kingdom grew intense. Gavan himself stayed away from this religious hostility, but the local Deccan Amirs resented his growing prominence and plotted against him.\n\nConspirators spread a rumour, in league with the Orissa kings, that Gavan was secretly writing letters to defame the Sultan. Believing this rumour, the Sultan — who had for so long guarded his kingdom as carefully as the eyelid guards the eye — had Mahmud Gavan murdered in **A.D. 1481**. With Gavan''s murder, the very foundations of the Bahamani kingdom were shaken.\n\nHe earned a permanent name in Bahamani history as a great soldier, administrator, scholar, and legal exponent, and wrote two books — **Riyajul Insha** and **Manjirul Insha**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p6", "partName": "Military Administration", "bodyMd": "Due to frequent wars with Vijayanagara and Malwa, and internal conflicts, the Bahamani rulers developed a very strong military system, organised into two types:\n\n1.  **Central military system**\n2.  **Regional military system**\n\n### Central Military System\n\nThe head of the central military system was the **Amir-Ul-Umra**, the highest official next to the Sultan. He commanded a contingent of 1,500 soldiers; below him, officials held contingents of 1,000, 500, 300, and 100 soldiers.\n\nFor the maintenance of the army, the Sultan provided land grants or fixed income:\n\n*   An officer maintaining 500 soldiers was given one lakh, or **Iqta (Jagir)** land.\n*   An officer maintaining 1,000 soldiers was given two lakh, or land equal to that income, as Iqta (Jagir) land.\n\nDuring Allauddin Hassan''s period, the central government army had **50,000 horse riders** and **25,000 infantry**. Later, during Muhammad Shah''s period and after, the central army strength increased to **one lakh and nine thousand**. Along with this growth in numbers came changes in training and strategy.\n\n### Regional Military System\n\nIn the regional army, the **Tarafdar** was the head, discharging both civil and military duties; in this military capacity he was called **Sar Lashkar**. Many forts existed under regional control, each typically with 1,000 soldiers (sometimes increasing to 10,000). Whenever the Sultan desired, the Tarafdar also discharged military duties directly.\n\n### Composition and Equipment\n\nThe Bahamani army included both Hindu and Muslim soldiers, and was organised into many **Lashkars**. Every Lashkar included elephants and guns. Important weapons of the time were maces, axes, swords, arrows, and guns.\n\nFaristha recorded that the Bahamanis used guns; the place where guns were stored was called **Atish Khana**, headed by an officer called **Mir-i-Atish**. According to Faristha, Mahammad Shah defeated the Vijayanagara king due to the use of guns. Barbosa gave a detailed account of Muhammad Shah''s wars during A.D. 1482–1518, describing the strategies, attacks, army contingents, and weapons used.\n\nOverall, the Bahamani rulers relied on fast-moving cavalry and modern weaponry with large army contingents. Tents and war camps were set up near the battlefield, and soldiers took their wives with them to the battlefield. The secret of their victories in war lay largely in their horse troops.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p7", "partName": "Judicial System", "bodyMd": "The highest office of the Judiciary was the Sultan himself, whose seat was the Supreme Court. The Bahamanis belonged to the Sunni sect; the Imad Shahis (Berar) and the Barid Shahi (Bidar) were also Sunni Muslims, while the Bijapur, Ahemadnagar, and Golconda rulers were Shias.\n\n**Principles of the legal system**\n\n*   All rulers executed the legal system as per the Shariat — the order of the Bahamani Sultan was the law.\n*   Under Islamic legal tradition, people were expected first to worship Prophet Mohammad, and then to respect and obey the Sultan.\n*   Drinking alcohol in open places was prohibited.\n*   Immorality and corruption were prohibited.\n*   Employees were severely punished for neglecting their duties.\n\n**Instances of punishment**\n\n*   During the period of Allauddin Ahamad Shah II, his secretary (Sarmad-i-Kar-i-mulki) Mustahfa Khan Sayyad sent letters without the Sultan''s prior permission; he was punished by beheading and his body was exhibited on the main road.\n*   Allauddin Ahmad Shah II punished the grandson of Hazrat Sayyad Gesudaraj for drinking alcohol — he was publicly beaten 200 times and made to take an oath never to drink again.\n*   Anyone who rebelled against the king was severely punished, sometimes by beheading; rebellion for the throne invited especially strong punishment, sometimes by plucking out the offender''s eyes. Officers who failed to suppress rebellion were also punished severely.\n    *   Saif Khan, who opposed Humayun Shah Bahamani''s accession, was punished by being tied to an elephant''s legs and paraded through the town.\n    *   Prince Hassan Khan, who rebelled against the Sultan, was punished by having his eyes plucked out and was imprisoned.\n*   During the period of Mohammad Shah I Bahamani, an effort was made to eliminate theft: within six months, 20,000 thieves were severely punished and theft was brought under control.\n\n### Judicial Hierarchy\n\n*   **Sadar-I-Sharia** — head of the judicial system next to the Sultan, supervised judicial enquiry and the severity of punishment for crimes, and worked to create a crime-free kingdom.\n*   In the **Tarafs** (provincial states), the head of Judiciary was the **Sadar**, who enquired into and punished crimes occurring in the provinces. Promiscuity was prohibited, though in practice this could not always be stopped, along with looting, thefts, violence, illegal activities, and rebellions.\n*   In the cities, the **Khaji** was the Judge; in big cities, a **Naib-Khaji** was also appointed to assist the Khaji.\n*   One **Mufti** was appointed to every court, to explain legal matters and preserve judgments in writing.\n*   To every city and fort, one **Kotwal** was appointed, who maintained peace and law and order, brought criminals to the courts, and held other protective responsibilities.\n*   **Mahatashibs** were appointed to check whether people were genuinely following the Shariat, and to create religious awareness among the people.\n*   To solve the problems of soldiers, an advocate called **Khaji Asker** was appointed. During Allauddin Hassan Bahamani''s period, one Munajim Badakshi held this post — soldiers who committed crimes, neglected duty, or leaked secret information to enemies were investigated and punished under this office.\n*   In the **Paragana**, the judge was the **Khaji**, assisted by the **Havaldar** and a **Majlis** (which handled only civil cases). The head of the paragana was the Havaldar, also known as **Maksadar**.\n*   After the conquest of Warangal, Ahemad Shah established **Thanas**, headed by a **Thanedar**, who looked after law and order in his area, assisted by the Kotwal.\n*   In the villages, **Mukhaddam** and **Gotas** conducted the administration, resolving disputes, holding judicial enquiries, and punishing criminals.\n\nSelection of judges was done cautiously: they were required to be highly moral and ethical, well-versed in customs and traditions, thorough practitioners of religious rituals, educationally accomplished scholars, and experts in both Hindu and Islamic principles. Only such meritorious persons were appointed as Judges.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p8", "partName": "Police System", "bodyMd": "Bahamani sultans and their successors established a permanent police system for maintaining peace and law and order. In the capital city, every city in the kingdom, and every fort, a police officer called **Kotwal** was appointed, who acted as both police commissioner and municipal commissioner in the city; the Kotwal of a fort also acted as a military officer there. In the cities, an officer called **Samaubatan** was appointed to assist the Kotwal.\n\n*   **Siladar**: an office first appointed by Muhammad Shah Bahamani (following the practice of the Delhi Sultans), responsible for protecting the kingdom, organising guards, and appointing guards to carry weapons from place to place.\n*   **Samaubat**: an officer belonging to the upper strata, who commanded four thousand bodyguards. For protection of the Royal palace, five Siladars and one thousand bodyguards were appointed, on duty the whole day. Only a person fully trustworthy to the Sultan was appointed as Samaubat.\n\nKotwal was directly answerable to the Sultan and was the key person controlling crime in his jurisdiction. He was in charge of jails and jailors; his responsibilities included enquiring into and producing the accused before judges, and — once charges were proved — taking the person into custody and implementing the court''s punishment. In big cities, the Kotwal was assisted by assistants called **Naib-Kotwal**. Police administration acted severely to control crime in the cities, forts, and other important places of the kingdom.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p9", "partName": "Social Conditions", "bodyMd": "After the establishment of the Bahamani kingdom in the Deccan, the influence of the upper-class Hindu society gradually decreased, including in administration, since the Bahamani kingdom was a Muslim state — in the king''s court and Durbar, the role of upper-class Hindus became negligible, and the ministers were mostly Muslims. \n\nThis was encouraged especially under the Tughluq rule in Delhi and continued particularly during the Allauddin Hassan period. Families such as the Gorpajis, Nimbalkars of Paithan, Manes of Masvad, and Godjes of Malawadi could exert some influence on administration but could not obtain ministerial posts; they instead became army officers in the Jagirs due to their close relations with Hassan.\n\n**Dakhanis and Garibhus**: the upper-class people of the time were divided into two groups — the **Dakhanis** (local/old settlers) and the **Garibhus** (newcomers). There was strong confrontation and competition between the two for status and authority, though the powerful Sultans kept this in check. \n\nPrime Minister Mohammad Gavan managed to control this rivalry, but afterward the conflict could not be contained, and this local-versus-non-local issue eventually contributed to the downfall of the kingdom. This local–newcomer rivalry ran alongside a religious dimension as well: newcomers from Persia, known as **Afaqis**, mostly followed the Shia sect and were favoured with high posts by the Sultans, while the older, Deccan-settled community was predominantly Sunni — and rivalry between the two sects grew intense in the kingdom''s later years.\n\nAlthough the Bahamani rulers and higher officials were Muslims, the majority of the population remained Hindu; social formation in the Deccan was similar to that of North India, and caste divisions were based on occupation and profession.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p10", "partName": "Society and Daily Life", "bodyMd": "### Social Classes\n\nSimilar to North India, Deccan society in the 14th century was divided into four classes: **Brahmins** (upper class), followed by **Kshatriyas**, **Vaisyas**, and **Sudras**. After the Kshatriya class, the **Kayasthas** held an important position in society at that time.\n\nIn the Hindu kingdom of Vijayanagara, Brahmins, traders, handicraft workers, weavers, and barbers occupied the social hierarchy, while the **Dombaras (Dommera, or soothsayers)**, **Jogis**, and **Marwars** were considered low-class people.\n\n*   Upper-class Brahmins served as ministers, army generals, army chiefs, and religious advisers, occupying an important position in society.\n*   Kshatriyas looked after administrative affairs.\n*   Other services were provided by traders and Vaisyas.\n*   Sudras provided all types of services.\n\n### Marriage System\n\nBrahmins married within the same caste but not within the same **Gotra** — marriage within the same Gotra was prohibited among Brahmins. Marriage with a niece (sister''s daughter) was in vogue among Lingayats. Among Reddy, Lingayat, and other caste communities, marriage within the same caste and same Gotra was common practice.\n\n**Divorce**: divorce was very difficult in this period, but was permitted if the husband was handicapped, a leper, of unfit mental state, unfit for sexual acts, or suffering from an incurable disease — in such cases the wife was eligible for divorce. Similarly, if the wife was barren, did not cooperate with the husband in sexual acts, or failed to bear a male child, the husband could divorce her and remarry.\n\n**Widow remarriage and Sati**: widow remarriage was against the rules of the period. According to the **Brihaspati Smriti**, a wife was expected to die immediately after her husband''s death — this practice was called **Sati** — and it was followed by the upper classes. When the Bahamanis defeated the Reddy kings, queens of the defeated Reddy kings performed Sati, as recorded by Faristha. Sati was not practiced among the Sudras. Iban Batuta, Nicole Conti, and Barbosa all recorded that Sati was prohibited in the Deccan for pregnant women, women carrying children, and Sudra women.\n\nThe book **Guru Charitra** greatly praised the practice of Sati; Yadava Ramadeva''s queen Kamaisa is recorded as having become Sati. Lower castes and classes did not follow Sati and instead practiced widow remarriage; among upper castes and classes, widow remarriage was prohibited. Women of the lower classes and castes had greater freedom and equal participation in fairs and festivals.\n\n**Dowry**: dowry was compulsory at the time of marriage. There existed a concept called **\"Sthree Dhan\"**, which was in vogue among the upper classes and common across the Deccan. If Sthree Dhan was not given as part of the dowry, the husband was required to give the wife an equal share in his property on par with his sons.\n\n### Family Life\n\nThe joint family system was common, with the eldest member looking after household affairs; even when brothers lived separately, they participated together in religious performances, festivals, and fairs. Polygamy and polyandry had religious acceptance among Muslims but were not practiced much in reality. Faristha recorded that child marriage was common in this period, and works such as **Jnaneshwari** and **Leela Charitra** support the existence of child marriages.\n\nBrahmins rode on horseback during marriage ceremonies; contemporary writings show that Dehsmukhs, Kumbhi, Sutar, Lohar, Gurav, Sonar, Daangar, Jangam, Mahar, and Bheel communities also used horses in marriage processions, and horses were used in Muslim marriages too. \n\nThe Mang community''s bride rode on a buffalo, while the Kaikaris and Burudus did not use any animal — the bride simply walked. Other communities, especially artisans, rode a bull for the procession; Teli, Chamar, Navi, Kumbhar, and Gopal communities used the bull during the bride''s marriage procession. Marriage ceremonies were celebrated over many days.\n\nWomen of the upper class followed the Sati practice, with an exception made for women with children and pregnant women. Yadava Ramadeva''s queen Kamaisa became Sati, and the book **Guru Charitra** greatly praised the Sati practice. People of the lower castes and classes did not follow Sati and instead practiced widow remarriage; for upper castes and classes, widow remarriage was prohibited. Women of the lower classes and castes enjoyed more freedom and equal participation in fairs and festivals.\n\n### Houses\n\nThe Sultan lived in the Royal Palace, which was surrounded by a variety of fruit gardens and was situated at the centre. The Sultan, ministers, officers, and royal family members lived in large, highly decorated palaces filled with different types of household items, furniture, chairs, and curtains. Niketan recorded that the lords led a lavish life, reflecting their social status.\n\nHouses were built on strong foundations, with walls constructed of mud and foundations laid with hard stones — this was confirmed by excavations at Navesa (Ahemadnagar district of Maharashtra). A typical house consisted of three or four rooms, one of which was a kitchen; the upper ceiling was made of mud and grass. Use of mud in house construction was common across all house types in this period. Rich people''s houses typically had one verandah, a drawing room, and four rooms. Excavations at Nevasa uncovered glasses, big jars, bathing tubs, big globes, big plates, and cutlery items commonly used at the time.\n\n### Food\n\nDifferent varieties of rice were used, with good-quality rice consumed by the higher classes, generally eaten with curd, buttermilk, and various leafy vegetables. On medical advice, patients ate limited food — less sugar, rice, and milk. Wheat, millets (jawar), and barley were also eaten; green gram was used in food, and bread made from jawar powder was the staple menu of the common people. \n\nWheat bread mixed with ghee and milk was taken by the high classes; sunflower oil was widely consumed. On special occasions, sugar and jaggery were also consumed, along with different types of pickles. Laddu, pancake, khaddu, and kheer (prepared with milk) were also part of the diet.\n\nNiketan recorded that Hindus of different faiths did not dine together, reflecting the caste system of the period. Some communities ate meat, pigeons, fish, and eggs; Hindu kings mostly belonged to the Maratha or Reddy communities. Higher-class ladies remained behind the veil, or \"Pardha.\" Niketan noted that in Brahmin localities, people did not eat meat, pigeons, fish, or pork, suggesting Brahmins largely resided in such areas. \n\nLingayats and some other castes did not take non-vegetarian food, and Brahmins ate only twice a day; ghee and milk formed part of their diet. Common people ate a variety of foods. According to Isami, both vegetarian and non-vegetarian food was prepared in the Sultan''s palace, betel leaves were chewed after meals, and imported Chinese tableware was used for dining in the palace.\n\n### Dress\n\nPeople generally wore the **Dhoti** and **Uttariam**, along with a tied head-dress. Scholars and poets frequently wore two-piece clothes, and wearing footwear was common. Ladies wore sarees and cholis (blouses). During fairs and festivals, silk and costly dress was worn by people of the higher class. \n\nWoolen blankets were used in winter, similar to those used by Muslims in Delhi. Two-piece cloths were used as head-dress; silk dress was worn beyond the elbow, and another dress beyond the knee was used, along with trousers, petticoats, and dupattas that covered the body fully. Both Hindus and Muslims wore different ornaments, including neck ornaments and rings studded with colored stones. During summer, the Uttariam was not worn by common people, and farmers and craftsmen never wore the Uttariam.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p11", "partName": "Economic Conditions", "bodyMd": "During the Bahamani period, society was divided into three economic classes:\n\n1.  **Upper class** — high officers, Iqtadars, Ulemas, and court employees, who received sufficient income from the government, led a lavish life, and kept servants and soldiers.\n2.  **Middle class** — religious heads, Brahmins, Muslims, small land lords, Tarafdars, local traders, and other employees; they possessed some horses, some servants, and owned houses, acquiring their daily needs without obstruction and leading a somewhat lavish life. Barbosa described that at Calicut, traders possessed servants and horses, dressed well, and led a happy life — a similar happy life was led by traders of the Deccan. In trade and commerce, some individuals rose to high status: Malik-Ul-Tujjar, for example, was appointed Prime Minister.\n3.  **Lower class** — farmers, craftsmen, and daily wagers, who, as recorded by Moreland, led a difficult life. Farmers in particular faced hardship due to low rainfall, small land holdings, and indebtedness. According to Niketan, \"professionals and daily wagers\" fared better in life than farmers, who faced many difficulties.\n\nBahamani rulers and Amirs encouraged professional workers, and different types of cottage industries were promoted, with their production exported to foreign countries. **Bidar, Gulbarga, Warangal, Raichur, Nanded, and Bhuvanagiri** became famous for cottage industries, providing employment and benefit to artisans.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p12", "partName": "Economy, Trade, and Taxation", "bodyMd": "### Revenue System\n\nAgriculture was the main occupation of the people, depending mostly on rainfall, though artificial tanks and canals also provided irrigation. Crop land was divided into three types:\n\n1.  **Metta** (dry) land\n2.  **Magani** (wet) land\n3.  **Gaddi** (grass) land\n\nOne-sixth of the produce was collected as land tax. During the rule of Mohammad Shah, a land survey was conducted under the supervision of Gavan, and land tax was decided based on fertility and irrigation facility available. Wet lands, cash crops, and gardens were taxed more heavily, while dry lands attracted less tax. Land under active cultivation was taxed, while fallow land was given tax exemption. Land donated to temples and mosques was exempted from land tax, and some land was granted for providing services in the villages.\n\n### Trade and Commerce\n\nThe Bahamanis maintained commercial relations with Persia, Africa, Europe, and other western countries. Exports mainly took place from the ports of **Goa, Dabhol, Mahim, and Machilipatnam**. Niketan recorded that these were first-grade ports, and Dabhol was a particularly important port. Faristha noted that many ships were used for commercial purposes, and textiles, Bidri wares, and Warangal carpets were among the goods exported from these ports.\n\n**Imports** included gold, spices, China porcelain and scent items, drugs, high-quality horses, pearls, and slaves. According to Barbosa, sugar was exported in large quantities to Sri Lanka, Arabia, and Persia.\n\n### Taxes\n\nBahamanis collected many taxes through a dedicated tax-collecting department. A contemporary work, **Mahaboob-Ul-Vatan**, records the different taxes imposed on various items during Bahamani rule:\n\n| Item | Tax |\n|---|---|\n| Horses | One Dinar |\n| Cow and buffalo | One Phanam |\n| Goat | Five Jital |\n| Camel | Half Jelool |\n| Elephant | One Dinar |\n\nIn addition, import duty was collected on silk and cotton textiles (three rupees and eight annas per hundred rupees) and on gold and silver ornaments (seven rupees and eight annas per hundred rupees). Salt tax was also collected. There was no tax on wood and grass.\n\n### Coins\n\nCurrency was minted at the Gulbarga and Bidar mints, with some Bahamani coins also minted at Fatehbad. Bahamani coins were made of gold, silver, and copper. During the period of Allauddin Bahaman Shah, coins existed in three types of measurements; during Muhammad Shah I''s period, coins of a single measurement were used. Big-measurement coins were introduced by Muhammad Shah II. The Sultans paid close attention to coin-making and the functioning of the mints, and circulation of fake coins was strictly prohibited.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p13", "partName": "Industries and Handicrafts", "bodyMd": "Bahamani rulers actively encouraged industries and handicrafts, and their production was exported to foreign countries.\n\n*   **Cotton and silk mills** were established in many places.\n*   **Iron and steel works** were carried out at Paithan.\n*   **Silk clothes** were manufactured at Daulatabad, Gulbarga, and Khidki, and were available at cheaper rates; these were exported and also supplied to local Amirs.\n*   **Mashur, Heemru, Sarees, Dhotis, Dorias, and Mulmal cloths** were manufactured across different regions.\n*   Amirs purchased **\"Pagadis\"** (a type of head-dress), manufactured at Raichur and Nanded.\n*   **Bidar** was popular for pot-making — the **Bidri** craft became popular not only within India but also internationally, and continues to be practiced in many places today.\n*   **Warangal** became famous for **carpet making**. Percy Brown recorded that very large beams were manufactured in the Deccan, made of iron.\n*   **War equipment** — swords, water cans, bows, knives, and arrows — was also manufactured in the Deccan. **Nalgonda, Bhuvanagiri, and Adilabad, Nirmal** were popular centres for these.\n*   **Copper** was mined at Chota Nagapur in the Deccan and exported to various places; Marco Polo also discussed this. Niketan recorded Raichur''s diamonds.\n*   **Leather industry** was an important industry of the Bahamani kingdom, especially goat leather; leather was exported to Arabia and Persia from the **Thana port**.\n*   **Ship building**: Allauddin Bahaman II possessed boats bigger than those of the Italians. Contemporary literary works mention shipbuilding during this period, and the Bahamani Shahs used small ships; crossing the Tungabhadra river, the Bahamanis attacked the Vijayanagara kings twice. The Bahamanis made great progress in shipbuilding overall — their ships were noted as more durable and larger in build than those of Italy.\n*   **Bijapur** was very famous for its **textile industry**; textiles were exported to Persia, Arabia, and East Africa, with **Calico and Muslin** being important cloth varieties. Barbosa recorded that Portuguese traders purchased and exported these daily.\n*   **Velvet, Scarlet, Satin, and Damakas**, and woolen clothes, were imported **from Goa**.\n\nMany of these industries were encouraged by the Bahamani rulers, Amirs, Jagirdars, and Izaradars.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p14", "partName": "Religious System", "bodyMd": "### Hindu Reform Movements Before the Bahamani Period\n\nPrior to the establishment of Bahamani rule, a social reform movement had already begun in South India. In the seventh century, the Pallavas and Chalukyas patronized Hinduism, and as a result Buddhism and Jainism weakened while Saivism and Vaisnavism gained ground; Hinduism thereby re-established itself.\n\nDue to the wider Bhakti movement, Saivism rose to new heights under the leadership of **Tirugnana Sambandhar, Tirunavakkarasu, Sundara Murthy, and Manikavashar**; the **Alvars** — including Andal, Kulashekar, Tirumangai, and Nammalvar, some of whom belonged to low castes — inspired the Vaisnava philosophy. These two movements taught equality before God, idol worship, suppression of unwanted desires, divine devotion, and love towards animals.\n\n**Shankaracharya** led the Bhakti movement on the Advaita side; along with **Nadamuni, Ramanuja, and Nimbarka**, he was a key figure of this movement, aimed at the renaissance of Hinduism and the weakening of Buddhism and Jainism through new methods of religious practice.\n\nAt the same time, a religious reform movement was underway in Karnataka: the **Lingayat movement**, founded by **Basava and Chenna Basava** in the twelfth century. This movement spread further during Bahamani rule and taught the ideals of brotherhood, criticizing the caste system.\n\n### Hindu Pilgrimage Centres\n\nNasik and Paithan were pious centres for Hindus; many people took a dip in the River Godavari for the absolving of sins. The Vithoba temple at Pandaripur was a pious place for both Kannadigas and Marathis. Dattatreya was the chief deity at the pious place of Ganagapur. \n\nThe Bhavani Temple at Tulzapur was famous — according to an inscription of A.D. 1398, a fair was held there every year, started during the Feroz Shah period. Tulzapur, Kolhapur, Matapur, and Saptashrungi were the four important Bhavani places. Sirpur, near Akola, was a Jaina worshipping place, and Riddipur, near Buldana, was famous for the **Mahanubhavas**.\n\n### Islam and Sufism\n\nAlongside Hinduism, Islam — established by Prophet Mohammad — played a very important role in Bahamani society. During this period, Muslims were divided into four classes: (1) lords of the army and civil officers, (2) traders, (3) slaves, and (4) poets, scholars, and religious heads. \n\nSlavery was common in Islamic society, and slaves were sometimes raised to the level of Sultan under Bahamani patronage — among the slaves, **Yusuf, Adil Khan, Malik Hassan, and Khasim Barida** rose to high status. Ulemas, Khajis, Amirs, Muftis, Khatibus, and Imams occupied important administrative positions and were patronized by the state.\n\nFor the strengthening of Islam, the **Sufi movement** developed in the Deccan.\n\n*   **Khaja Bande Nawaz Gesudaraj** (of Gulbarga) was the chief exponent of Sufism in the Deccan; his durbar was visited by both Hindus and Muslims. Born in Delhi in **A.D. 1321**, he reached Gulbarga at the age of 90, in **1402–03**; his father, Syed Yousuf, had come to Daulatabad during Mohammad Bin Tughluq''s period.\n\n*   Gesudaraj lived in the Deccan from 1324 to 1331, constructed a **KhanKa** at Gulbarga for his followers and disciples, and preached the **Chisti order** of Sufism. He died in **1422**. Even today, people from Telangana, Maharashtra, Karnataka, and Andhra attend the **Urs** festivals held in his memory.\n\n*   **Burhanuddin** explained the Chisti principles at Daulatabad.\n*   The Sufi orders active in the Deccan included **Chisti, Suhravardhi, Sattari, and Khadri**.\n*   **Shiek Ainuddin** of Bijapur and **Shiek Shirajuddin Junaidi** were important Sufis during Allauddin Hassan Bahaman Shah''s period.\n*   **Shah Niyamatulla Khadri** settled in the Deccan, preaching and expanding the Khadri branch of Sufism, attracting many followers through his teaching and made Islam a stable religion in the Deccan.\n\n### Sufi Philosophy and Hindu–Muslim Syncretism\n\nIn the twelfth century, some of the Muhammadans who arrived in the Deccan from Persia and the Middle East were Sufis. They showed a spirit of brotherhood and thereby attracted Hindus toward them. Central to Sufi philosophy was cultivating true love (prema) for God — those who showed genuine love for God were held to draw close to God, and this was the essence of what Sufi teaching preached. \n\nFormal prayer, fasting, and vows were not, in the Sufis'' own view, the most essential things. Because their teaching was rooted in love for God, Sufi philosophy did not carry hostility toward other faiths; Sufis held that all religions were different paths leading toward the same God, with the upliftment of humanity as their chief goal.\n\nIn the Deccan, Hindu–Muslim unity of this kind began during the reign of **Firoz Shah Bahamani**, and spread widely, taking deep root during the reign of his brother **Ahmad Shah**. Hindus of the time observed Ahmad Shah''s Urs according to the Hindu calendar (Panchangam); they regarded him almost as a saint, worshipping him with devotion and offerings of flowers. The priest who conducted this Urs festival at Gulbarga was, notably, a **Lingayat Jangam**.\n\nDuring the rule of the Deccan sultans, the **Pandharipur tradition** rose to prominence. Being a monotheistic (Ekeshwaravada) tradition, it mixed with unorthodox local practices and, in doing so, helped this tradition take hold. The founder of this tradition was the great devotee **Jnaneshwar**, whose teachings created a new spiritual consciousness and vitality; he taught that a person is not made great by caste, and that in God''s eyes all people are equal.\n\nIn Kurnool district, at **Srisailam**, the **Nada** tradition flourished, with Yogis who were its prophets spreading its teaching widely. By the 15th century, the **Dattatreya tradition** had become strong in the Deccan; among Dattatreya''s devotees were a number of Muslims, who became well known as **Shahi Fakirs**.\n\nThe Bahamani sultans patronised the **Urdu language**, which in that period was known as **Hindavi**. No sustained effort to forcibly convert Hindus to Islam took place under Bahamani rule, in contrast to the practice under the Delhi Empire. Musunuri Kapaya Nayaka, who ruled well, had believed the emergence of an independent Bahamani kingdom would work in his favour, and hoped that the Gulbarga and Warangal kingdoms could coexist peacefully side by side — a hope that was dashed within a short time, after which the Warangal kingdom fell under Bahamani control.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 15, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p15", "partName": "Educational System", "bodyMd": "Nasik and Paithan were the centres of Hindu scholarship. In **A.D. 1290**, the **Jnaneshwari** was compiled at Nevasa. Gangapur was the Vedic centre. Students commonly began their education with texts such as **Amarakosha, Roopavali, Samaschakra, Panchatantra, and Hitopadesha**. **Trivikram Bharati of Kumashi** was a great Vedic researcher of the 14th century. Students received education by going to their teachers directly.\n\n**Islamic education** flourished at Gulbarga, Bidar, Kandahar, Elichpur, Daulatabad, Junnar, Chaut, and Dhabol, which continued as Islamic centres. Recitation of the **Quran** was carried out in an elaborate way, and the reading of **Hadis** was also important. In **A.D. 1472**, **Mohammad Gavan built a great college at Bidar**, which became the great centre of Islamic education in the Deccan; scholars such as **Jalaluddin Davvani and Sadruddin Ravvasi** visited this centre. \n\nGavan also developed the Madarsa library extensively; **calligraphy** developed into a prominent art form, and education continued in Arabic and Persian. At **Elichpur**, the **Darul-Uloom** (educational school) was established, which collected 3000 Dinars in yearly fees; alongside religious education, literature was also taught in the Madarsas.\n\nTeachers gave special attention to their students, and discipline and character were given great importance in society. The Bahamani rulers took special care for the development of education.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 16, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p16", "partName": "Literature", "bodyMd": "Many scholarly works were compiled during the Bahamani period, chiefly by leaders of the Bhakti and Sufi traditions, who enlightened society through their writing.\n\n**Bhakti/Marathi and regional literature**\n\n*   **Jnanadev**, a Maharashtra ascetic, wrote **Jnaneshwari**.\n*   **Namadev** led the Bhagavata sect.\n*   The **Mahanubhava sect** was established by **Chakradhar**, who wrote **Laila Charitra** in Marathi; this work was divided into Ekanka, Purvardha, and Uttarardha.\n*   **Narendra and Parushurama** wrote a book called **Smriti Sthal**.\n*   **Vishwanatha**, native of Balapur, wrote **Jnaan Probodh** in **1331**.\n*   **Ravalo Bhasa** wrote **Guhyadri Varnana** in **A.D. 1333**.\n*   **Narayanabasa** wrote **Raddipurva Varnana** in **1331**.\n*   **Meru Swamy** wrote two great works, **Bheemopadesham** and **Ramasohalalu**.\n*   **Vamana Pandita** wrote **Yadhartha Deepika**.\n*   **Madhavcharya** compiled **Jaiminiya Nyayamala Vistari**.\n*   **Ramanujacharya** wrote **Tantra Rahashya**.\n*   **Vidyaranya** wrote **Panchadashi**, **Jeevanmukti Viveka**, and another work titled **Sarvadarshana Samgraha**.\n*   **Dharmaraja dwari** wrote **Vedanta Paribhasha**.\n*   **Madhava Saraswati** of Karnataka wrote **Sarvadarshana Kaumudi**.\n*   **Sripati** wrote **Srikarabhashya**.\n*   **Madhavacharya** preached the **Dvaita philosophy**; **Shankaracharya** preached **Advaita philosophy**.\n*   **Jayateertha** wrote a commentary on **Nyayasudha**.\n*   **Keshava Bhattu** wrote **Tarka Deepika**; **Varadaraju** wrote **Vevahara Nirnaya**; scholar **Mahadeva** wrote **Kamadhenu**.\n\n**Persian literature and Sufi/court poets** Many books were also written in Persian during this period, by writers across Gulbarga, Raichur, Ahamadnagar, Aurangabad, Daulatabad, and Golconda.\n\n*   **Isami** was the greatest writer of the Bahamani period, and wrote **Putawat Salaatin**; he was the first great poet under Bahamani rule.\n*   **Khaja Bande Nawaz Gesudaraj**, besides being a great Sufi scholar and teacher, was also a great poet — he wrote **Anishul-Ushak**, compiled in Arabic and Persian. He also wrote **Mahirikul Anwar** (on the systems and traditions of Prophet Mohammad''s period), commentaries on **Avaripool Mariff** (originally by Shiek Shihbuddin Suhra), **Taruf** (by Shiek Abubaker Mohammad bin Bukhari), and **Adabul Mureedin** (by Shiek Nazeebuddin Abdul Basheer Suhravardhi), as well as **Khaddoos, Ishka Nama, Asammarul Asrar,** and **Khatima**. He preached brotherhood, symbolic of religious harmony.\n*   **Adhari** (full name Nooruddin Hanji) wrote many works, including **Javahirull Asra, Meerathul Asral (Matnavi)**, and **Si-I-Safa**; the poet **Deewan Adhari** died before completing the writing of **Bahaman Nama**, which was thereafter completed by **Nazeeri and Sami**.\n*   **Nazeeri** earned great fame during the rule of Allauddin Ahamad; **Nazeeri Mulla**, along with Sami, completed the **Bahaman Nama**.\n*   **Faristha** was born in **1552** at Astrabad, was later brought to Ahemadnagar, and subsequently joined the court of the second Ibrahim Adil Shah; he died in **1623** at Bijapur. He wrote **Gulshan-I-Ibrahimi**, also known as **Tariq-I-Faristha**, an important work analyzing the socio-economic and religious conditions of the period.\n*   **Sirajat-Tawarik** was written by Mulla Mohammad Lari; **Tahufatoon-Salatin** was written by Mulla Dawood Bidri; Mulla Abdul Kareem Handani''s **\"Life of Mohammad Gavan\"** also helps in understanding the contemporary conditions of the period.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 17, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p17", "partName": "Architecture", "bodyMd": "Bahamani rulers undertook extensive construction across the Deccan, beginning first at Gulbarga, where they built mosques and tombs.\n\n*   **Muhammad Shah** built a great mosque at Gulbarga in **A.D. 1367**, constructed under the supervision of Rafi-Bin-Shams-bin-Mansur Khajvini; it measured 216 feet from east to west and 176 feet from north to south.\n*   **Gesudaraj''s Darga** was constructed two years after his death, taking seven years to complete; it was built during the rule of Ahammada Vali, with a large dome.\n*   During the period of **Ahammada Vali**, the capital was shifted from Gulbarga to **Bidar**.\n*   During the **Feroz Shah** period, a new town, **Firozabad**, was constructed 12 miles from Gulbarga.\n*   During Ahammada Vali''s period, architects from **Turkey, Baghdad, Iran, Arabia, Rome, and North India** came to Bidar and contributed to its construction and design.\n*   At **Bidar**, the following were constructed: the Bidar fort, the Royal Palace, the sixteen-pillared mosque (**Sola Khambha Masjid**), tombs, and Madarsas. Within the Bidar fort, the **Rangeen Mahal** was beautifully constructed. **Gagan Mahal, Tarkash Mahal, Cheeni Mahal, Nageen Mahal**, and the Royal bathrooms were built during the rule of Mohammad Shah Bahamani.\n*   At **Ashtura** village, the tombs of Bahamani rulers were built; among them, the tomb of Ahammada Shah Vali is especially large.\n*   The Madarsa built by Gavan became the most prominent one of its kind.\n*   The construction of Bidar town was carried out under Ahamad Shah Vali.\n*   **Rauz-I-Shiek** and the **Feroz Shah Tombs** were built as part of the **Hafta Gumbaz** complex. Many other tombs and mosques were constructed at various places across the kingdom.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 18, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter10-bahamani-sultans-p18", "partName": "Summary", "bodyMd": "Bahamani rule spanned roughly **A.D. 1347 to A.D. 1527**, running through 17 sultans over 182 years — a dynasty founded by Allauddin Hasan Gangu at Gulbarga in defiance of a weakening Delhi Sultanate, and one that ended, after Kareemulla''s nominal reign, in the rise of five independent successor kingdoms: Barid Shahi (Bidar), Qutub Shahi (Golconda), Adil Shahi (Bijapur), Nizam Shahi (Ahmadnagar), and Imad Shahi (Berar). \n\nIts internal politics were often turbulent — several sultans were murdered, deposed, or blinded — even as the dynasty fought its long, unresolved contest with Vijayanagara across the Krishna–Tungabhadra doab. Prime Ministers like Mahmud Gavan strengthened administration and reformed the army and revenue system, while the Sufi philosophy, spread through figures such as Gesudaraj and reflected in the Deccan''s wider Hindu–Muslim syncretism, carried Sufi teaching outward across the region. \n\nUnder Bahamani rule, trade and commerce with Arabia, Persia, and East Africa expanded, and the rulers developed towns such as Firozabad while advancing regions like Bidar. Towns including Bidar, Raichur, Gulbarga, Daulatabad, Aurangabad, and Ahamadnagar became centres of Muslim development. \n\nIn society, the division based on caste and class remained clearly visible, and the influence of Islam on Hindu religious life became increasingly predominant — overall, the Deccan became the stage for Islamic rule, culture, and architecture, and it was Bahamani rule that marked the beginning of the era of Muslim kingdoms in the Deccan.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p1", "partName": "Origins and Foundation of the Qutb Shahi Kingdom", "bodyMd": "The Qutb Shahi kingdom emerged on the ruins of the Bahamani Kingdom, with Golconda as its capital. The dynasty''s ancestral home was Hamdam in Iraq (Persia), and the ancestors of Sultan Quli, the founder, migrated to India after losing power in Persia.\n\n**Founder''s early career:**\n\n*   Sultan Quli was appointed ''Tarfdar'' of Telangana by the Bahamani Sultan in 1492 CE.\n*   He declared independence in 1518 CE.\n*   He and his successors ruled most of present Telangana–Andhra areas for a period of more than 175 years.\n\nUnder Qutb Shahi rule, people enjoyed complete religious freedom, and the rulers respected local culture and treated local people like their own children. The region saw all-round development — beautiful mosques, palaces, sarais, bridges, and forts were constructed, and Telugu language also made great progress during this age.\n\n**Decline:** The kingdom witnessed regular attacks from Mughal emperors after Akbar''s death, and it was finally captured by Aurangzeb in 1687 CE.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p2", "partName": "Sources for the Study of Qutb Shahi History", "bodyMd": "A large number of archaeological and literary sources helps in studying Qutb Shahi history. Archaeological sources include inscriptions, coins, and monuments. Books written in Persian, Arabic, Urdu, and Telugu by poets, historians, and scholars also provide useful information, as do foreign travel accounts left by contemporary visitors, traders, and Farmans issued by Qutb Shahi Sultans.\n\n### Archaeological Sources — Inscriptions\n\n| Ruler / Officer Issued | Name of Inscription | Period | Remarks |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shitabkham | Warangal | 1504–1506 CE | Restoration of Hindu temples in this region |\n| Ibrahim-Quli Qutb Shah | Nalgonda (District) Ghazinagar | 1576–1577 CE | — |\n| Muhammad-Quli Qutb Shah | Badshah-e-Ashur Khana | 1592–1596 CE | — |\n| Muhammad-Quli Qutb Shah | Daru-Shifa | 1595 CE | — |\n| Abul Hasan Tanashah | Miyam-Misq | 1681 CE | — |\n| Musunuri Prolayanayaka | Vilasa Copper Plate | 1323–1324 CE | Gives a clear picture of oppressive Muslim rule in the Telugu country |\n\n### Literary Sources\n\n| Book / Work | Author | Remarks / Useful Information |\n|---|---|---|\n| Khazain-ul-Futuh and Nuh Sipihr | Amir Khusrav | Describes the general conditions of the Deccan during the 14th century |\n| Tariq-e-Ferishta (or Gulshan-e-Ibrahimi), Persian | Muhammad Qasim Ferishta | Describes the political condition of the Deccan; used the term ''Telang'' for modern Telangana. Ferishta was born in 1552 CE at Astrabadi, Iran; joined the court of Ibrahim Adilshah of Bijapur and dedicated his work to him. Five pages of this work contain useful information |\n\nForeign travel accounts (Tavernier, Bernier, Thevenot, Methwold and others) and the Farmans issued by the Sultans supplement these inscriptional and literary sources for the study of Qutb Shahi political, social, and cultural life.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p3", "partName": "Qutb Shahi Sultans (1518–1687 CE)", "bodyMd": "Qutb Shahi Sultans ruled Telugu-speaking areas of present Telangana, Andhra, and Rayalaseema for more than one hundred and seventy-five years.\n\n| Sultan | Period of Rule | Key Events |\n|---|---|---|\n| Quli-Qutb-ul-Mulk | 1518–1543 CE | Founder of the dynasty; capital Golconda; contemporary of Srikrishnadevaraya and Achyutadevaraya of Vijayanagara, Babur (founder of Mughal rule), and Shershah-Sur |\n| Jamsheed-Quli | 02.09.1543 – 27.07.1550 CE | Greedy for power; murdered his aged father and ascended the throne; died of cancer |\n| Subhan-Quli | Few months from July 1550 | Son of Jamsheed; ascended the throne at the young age of 7 |\n| Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah | 1550–1580 CE | Sixth son of the founder; escaped to Vijayanagara court fearing his life from elder brother Jamsheed; sheltered by Aliya Ramaraya |\n| Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah | 1580–1612 CE | Third son of Ibrahim-Quli; ascended the throne at age 14; built Hyderabad and Charminar; good friend of Akbar; developed diplomatic relations with Iran |\n| Muhammad-Qutb Shah | 1612–1626 CE | Mughal invasions on Golconda started during this period |\n| Abdullah-Qutb Shah | 1626–1676 CE | Contemporary of Mughal emperors Jahangir and Shahjahan; Golconda faced serious Mughal invasions during his reign |\n| Abul Hassan Tanashah | 1676–1687 CE | Last Qutb Shahi Sultan; defeated in 1687 CE by Aurangzeb; imprisoned at Daulatabad fortress, where he died in 1699 CE |", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p4", "partName": "Qutb Shahi Sultans", "bodyMd": "### Sultan-Quli-Qutb-ul-Mulk (1518–1543 CE)\n\nSultan-Quli-Qutb-ul-Mulk was the founder of the independent Golconda kingdom in 1518 CE. He was a contemporary of the last Delhi Sultan Ibrahim Lodi, Krishnadevaraya of Vijayanagara, Yousuf Adilshah of Bijapur, and the last Bahamani Sultan Muhammad Shah.\n\n**Early life and rise:**\n\n*   His ancestors hailed from Hamdam in Southern Iran; he was born there in 1451 CE to Sher-Quli and Malik Swalch, of the ''Qara Quyunlu'' tribe.\n*   He migrated to India along with his uncle Ali-Quli after they lost power to the ''Akunev'' tribe, reaching Gulbarga, the Bahamani headquarters, during the reign of Muhammad-Shah III (1463–1482 CE).\n*   The execution of Muhammad-Gawan, the Bahamani Prime Minister, in 1481 pushed the kingdom into political confusion; the new Sultan, Shihabuddin Mahmood, was not capable, and a plot by the Sultan''s rivals to kill him was cleverly foiled by Quli — impressing the Sultan.\n*   He was appointed ''Tarfdar'' of Telangana in 1492 CE, crushed the revolts there, and established peace.\n\n**Founding of the kingdom:**\n\n*   By 1518 CE, Quli had also seized Golconda Fort. After the death of the last Bahamani Sultan, Quli declared his independence in 1518 CE, at the age of sixty-seven.\n*   He and his successors ruled Telugu-speaking areas of present Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, and some parts of Karnataka; he ruled as an independent ruler till 1543 CE.\n*   He was a contemporary of Vijayanagara rulers Saluva Narasimharaya, Srikrishnadevaraya, and Achyutadevaraya.\n\n**Conquests:** He attacked the territories of the Reddy, Vijayanagara, and Gajapati rulers, capturing some portions; the Gajapati officer at Warangal, Sitapati (Shitabkham), was defeated in the Battle of Khammammet.\n\n**Building activity:** He strengthened and improved Golconda fort, constructing many buildings, mosques, and palaces, and built a new city, ''Muhammad Nagar'', near Golconda fort. He also attacked Vijayanagara territories after Srikrishnadevaraya''s death, when internal succession struggles broke out, extending his authority from Kohir, Warangal, and Nalgonda up to Masulipatnam.\n\nHe was murdered by his own son Jamsheed inside a mosque in Golconda fort, at the age of 99. According to tradition, Jamsheed engineered the killing through his trusted agent Mir Mahmood, who struck down the aged Sultan-Quli with a sword while he was consulting with masons and architects about mosque construction after Friday prayers.\n\n**Sons and the succession struggle:** Sultan-Quli''s six sons were Hyder-Quli, Qutbuddin, Jamsheed-Quli, Abdul-Karim, Dowlat-Quli, and Ibrahim. In the struggle that followed, Qutbuddin was blinded and imprisoned; Abdul-Karim conspired for the throne with Bijapur''s help while his father was still alive and died at Bijapur; Dowlat-Quli was imprisoned by Jamsheed at Bhongir fort; and Ibrahim, the youngest, fled to Vijayanagara.\n\n### Jamsheed-Quli-Qutb Shah (1543–1550 CE)\n\nSultan-Quli''s last days were not peaceful — his six sons (Hyder-Quli, Qutbuddin, Jamsheed-Quli, Abdul-Karim, Dowlat-Quli, and Ibrahim) fought over the throne. Jamsheed-Quli, the third son, murdered his aged father and ascended the throne.\n\nHis seven-year reign was filled with wars, revolts, and confusion; the people and most officials disliked him. The contemporary Deccani Sultans of Ahmednagar and Bijapur, along with the Vijayanagara king, attacked Golconda territories and captured some portions.\n\n**Administrative significance:** Despite the turmoil, Jamsheed-Quli attempted to introduce a strong administrative system — Kamaluddin-Ardistani was his most capable minister; the Sultan held the highest position in central administration, with Vakil, Peswa, and Mir-Jumla as other important ministers.\n\nHe died of cancer in 1550 CE.\n\n### Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah (1550–1580 CE)\n\nAfter Jamsheed-Quli''s death, Subhan-Quli, his seven-year-old son, was declared Sultan by his mother and some self-serving nobles, with administrator Saif Khan running the actual affairs of the kingdom. \n\nSaif Khan''s misrule and revolt against the young Sultan and queen mother is said to have triggered the recall of Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah, who had been living in exile at the Vijayanagara court. Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah — the youngest son of the founder, Sultan-Quli — returned to Golconda with the help of Aliya Ramaraya, the de facto ruler of Vijayanagara, and seized the throne in 1550 CE, ruling till 1580 CE.\n\nHistorian Haroon-Khan-Sherwani, in his classical work _History of the Qutb Shahi Dynasty_ (1974), described this reign as **\"the kingdom at its height.\"** He was a contemporary of the Mughal emperor Akbar, and friendly diplomatic relations existed between Golconda and the Mughals.\n\n**Military affairs and the Battle of Raksasi-Tangadi:**\n\n*   Ibrahim-Quli made elaborate arrangements to expand his kingdom, fighting Deccan Sultans and the Vijayanagara ruler.\n*   Aliya Ramaraya''s divide-and-rule policy against the Deccani Sultans caused them great loss; by 1564 CE, the Sultans realized their mistake of disunity.\n*   Ibrahim-Quli united the Deccan Sultans through matrimonial alliances between Golconda, Ahmednagar, and Bijapur royal families — Ibrahim-Quli himself married Bibi Jamal, sister of the Ahmednagar ruler; Chand Bibi of Ahmednagar was married to Ali Adilshah of Bijapur, while her brother, the Nizamshah''s son, married Ali Adilshah''s sister; and the Bidar ruler was also linked into this network of alliances, binding the coalition together as one extended family opposed to Aliya Ramaraya.\n*   The combined armies of Golconda, Ahmednagar, and Bijapur, led by Ibrahim-Quli, met the Vijayanagara forces led by Aliya Ramaraya and his brothers in the historic **Battle of Raksasi-Tangadi in January 1565 CE**.\n*   The Deccani Sultans defeated and killed Aliya Ramaraya in the battlefield, plundering the Vijayanagara capital; the victory enhanced the glory of Golconda and Ibrahim-Quli.\n\n**Ministers and officials:** Mustafa-Khan was his most experienced minister of military affairs; Amir-Shah, Muhammad-Anju, and Hussain Baig were other important officials who helped consolidate the Golconda army.\n\n**Fortification and irrigation:** He strengthened Golconda fort with high walls and constructed many tanks to supply water for agriculture, including Hussain Sagar and the Ibrahimpatnam tank. Agriculture, trade, and commerce flourished during his age. He also established friendly, diplomatic relations with the Safavid rulers of Persia.\n\n**Patronage of learning:** Ibrahim-Quli was a great scholar and patron of poets and scholars. Telugu poets Addanki Gangadhara Kavi (author of _Thapathi-Samvaranophakhyanam_) and Kandukuri Rudra Kavi (author of _Nirankushophkhyanam_) received his patronage, and he was praised by scholars as ''Malkibhirama.''\n\nHe died in 1580 CE; the Golconda kingdom made all-round progress during his reign.\n\n### Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah (1580–1612 CE)\n\nMuhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah succeeded his father Ibrahim-Quli, who had trained him in state affairs. He was remembered in medieval Deccan history for his grand works and great abilities — a great scholar in Deccani Urdu with knowledge of Telugu as well. \n\n**Relations with contemporary rulers:**\n\n| Ruler | Dynasty | Remarks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Akbar | Mughal | Friendly diplomatic relations existed |\n| Jahangir | Mughal | Mughal–Golconda relations |\n| Ibrahim Adilshah-II | Adilshahi (1580–1627 CE) | Wars between them |\n| Venkatapatiraya-II | Aravidu | Vijayanagara wars between them |\n| Chand Bibi | Ahmednagar | — |\n| Shah Abbas | Safavid | Diplomatic and cultural contacts existed |\n| Queen Elizabeth | England | Granted permission to London traders to found the East India Company |\n\n**Founding of Hyderabad:** He built Hyderabad city; his peshwa Mir-Momin-Astrabadi designed the plan for the city and Charminar. The new city became the centre of composite culture, attracting scholars, artists, traders, and Afaquis who migrated there during his reign.\n\n**Wars with Bijapur and Vijayanagara:**\n\n*   He waged war with the Bijapur Sultan; a peace treaty was concluded in 1581 CE.\n*   Alikhan-loor, a Golconda commander, revolted against the Sultan with the support of Venkatapathiraya-II of Vijayanagara; Golconda''s first attempt to recapture territory failed due to heavy floods on the river Krishna.\n*   A second attempt after 1585 CE, led by Amin-ul-Mulk, succeeded in capturing Kurnool, Gutti, Udayagiri, and Penugonda; Venkatapathiraya-II then concluded a peace treaty with Golconda.\n\n**Suppression of revolts:** Commander Ain-ul-Mulk suppressed revolts of Bhalevar, Alam Khan (Kondavidu), and Mukundaraja Bahubalendra (Kasimkota), and also suppressed revolts of Elur and Rajahmundry Sardars. The Golconda army invaded Ahmednagar in 1597 CE, defeating Chandbibi.\n\n**Mughal relations:** He received Akbar''s envoy Masood Baig with great respect and sent costly gifts to the Mughal emperor. Friendship continued till Akbar''s death in 1605 CE; Jahangir''s attacks on Golconda thereafter damaged this friendship.\n\nMuhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah died in 1612 CE.\n\n### Sultan-Muhammad-Qutb Shah (1612–1626 CE)\n\nSultan-Muhammad-Qutb Shah, son of Mirza-Muhammad-Amin and nephew of Muhammad-Quli, became Sultan of Golconda in November 1612 CE. He married Hayat-Baksh-Begum, daughter of Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah, and ruled for fourteen years. His mother was ''Khanam Agha,'' belonging to the family of Imam Musakhajim, head of the Shia sect. Diplomatic relations between the Qutb Shahis and Mughals strained during this period.\n\nHe was a peace-loving Sultan and a great believer in Islamic principles, avoiding wars and plots. His peshwa Meer-Momin-Astrabadi, and successor Allam-Ibn-i-Khatan-Amooli, managed state affairs between 1612 and 1626.\n\n**Mughal pressure and the peace treaty:**\n\n*   Malik Amber, Prime Minister of Ahmednagar, influenced the course of Deccan politics; internal developments in Bijapur and Ahmednagar worried the security of the Qutb Shahi kingdom.\n*   Malik Amber''s defeat at the hands of Mughal prince Khurram terrified the rulers of Bijapur and Golconda; Mughal forces defeated Qutb Shahi forces, and a peace treaty was concluded between the defeated Sultan and emperor Jahangir.\n*   Sultan-Muhammad gave gifts worth fifteen lakhs to the Mughal emperor.\n*   According to _Iqbal-Nama_, when Mughal prince Khurram revolted against Noorjahan (Jahangir), Sultan-Muhammad refused to help Khurram but permitted him to reach Bengal via Musulipatnam.\n\n**Building and cultural activity:** Only the Masab-Tank and Khairatabad Mosque were constructed during his period, and he laid the foundation for the Mecca Masjid. He was a good scholar of Persian. Mir-Momin-Astrabadi wrote _Risala-e-Mikdaria_, a Persian work on weights and measurements.\n\n**Foreign relations and trade:** He was a good friend of Shah Abbas of Persia; the Persian ambassador Hassan-Baig-Khifaqui spent two years at his court. European trading companies established trading centres at Masulipatnam, Pulicat, Nagapatnam, and Nizampatnam during his reign.\n\nHe died at the age of 34.\n\n### Abdullah-Qutb Shah (1626–1672 CE)\n\nAbdullah-Qutb Shah ascended the Golconda throne at the age of fifteen. His mother, Hayat-Baksh-Begum, managed state affairs during his early years. The Mughal rulers Shahjahan and Aurangzeb made regular attacks on Golconda during his period. He suffered a defeat at Mughal hands in 1636 CE and accepted a subordinate position.\n\n**Court factionalism:**\n\n*   Nobles and officials of the court divided into groups, threatening the integrity of the Golconda State.\n*   The Sultan entrusted state affairs to his Prime Minister Allama-Ibn-Khatun, a Persian Afaqui; the Deccani Sardars disliked his activities and policies, creating differences between the two sections.\n*   The removal of Muhammad-Raza, a native Deccani, from the post of Dabir by Ibn-Khatun, and the appointment of Persians and Afaquis (Moulana-Bawain, Meer-Qasim, Hakim Nizamuddin Ahmed, Iklaskhan, and others) to higher positions, forced the Deccani Sardars to revolt against Ibn-Khatun.\n*   After Ibn-Khatun''s dismissal, Muhammad-Syed-Arbastani, Governor of the Karnataka region, gathered a huge army and revolted against the centre; he later became Mir-Jumla, with Gandikota as his headquarters.\n\n**Diplomatic honours:** During his reign, the Council of Ministers (Majlis-i-Diwandari) attained complete perfection, meeting daily. Abdullah-Qutb Shah promoted his minister Ain-ul-Mulk ''Yusufshah'' as the Qutb Shahi ambassador at the Court of Jahangir in 1630 CE.\n\n### Abul-Hasan-Tanashah (1672–1687 CE)\n\nAbul-Hasan-Tanashah was the last Qutb Shahi ruler — a contemporary of Aurangzeb and Shivaji, and son-in-law of Abdullah-Qutb Shah. He faced the attacks of Mughal forces with firm determination and was known for his religious tolerance, appointing Akkanna, Madanna, and Kancharla Gopanna to responsible positions, all of whom worked for the welfare of the people.\n\n**The fall of Golconda:**\n\n*   Akkanna and Madanna were killed by their opponents in 1686 CE.\n*   Aurangzeb''s forces captured Golconda in 1687 CE; all the brave efforts of Abul-Hasan-Tanashah''s army failed due to Abdullah-Phani''s greed and Aurangzeb''s tactics.\n*   The gates of Golconda were opened to Mughal forces by the disloyal Qutb Shahi officer Abdullah-Phani.\n*   The Sultan was arrested and first imprisoned at Bidar fort, then shifted to Daulatabad fortress, where he died in 1699 CE. Golconda became part of the Mughal empire.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p5", "partName": "Administration of Qutb Shahis", "bodyMd": "The administrative system introduced by the Qutb Shahi Sultans was unique in nature and structure, providing sound and popular administration to their subjects.\n\n### Sources for Administrative History\n\nMirza-Ibrahim-Zubari''s work _Basatin-us-Salatin_ (_Dastur-ul-Amal_) provides very useful, authentic information about Qutb Shahi polity, its nature, and structure.\n\nZubari was a contemporary of Abdullah-Qutb Shah (1626–1672). The book was written on the model of _Siasat Nama_, written by Nizam-ul-Mulk-Tusi, court scholar of Sultan-Malik-Shah-Seljuki, ruler of Central Asia — a work that discussed the duties and objectives of a Sultan. The Qutb Shahi Sultans followed the guidelines laid out in _Siasat Nama_, while also honouring local peoples'' desires and local laws in running the administration.\n\n**Key advice recorded in Basatin-us-Salatin:**\n\n1.  **On kingship and justice:** In the first part, the author advised the king to protect the law and follow it without fear or favour, saying such a spirit would bring good name to the ruler and prosperity to the kingdom.\n    *   If the ruler treats all subjects with love and takes care of the needy in a just way, he becomes popular.\n    *   The Sultan should appoint Quzis, Muftis, and Judges and pay their salaries in cash.\n    *   Donation of some villages to these officials was also supported.\n2.  **On ministers:** In the second section, the author advised the Sultan to appoint ministers based on loyalty, experience, and dedication.\n    *   Jagirs should be granted to ministers for a period of three years.\n    *   Strict discipline should be maintained with ministers.\n    *   The Sultan should conduct himself in council meetings with high dignity.\n3.  **On public welfare works:** The author advised the Sultan to undertake:\n    *   Construction of trunk roads\n    *   Keeping feeder roads clean\n    *   Making arrangements for the public to purchase required articles during festival days\n    *   Planting shady trees on both sides of roads\n    *   Construction of Sarais, with arrangement of water and free feeding facility funded by the government\n\nAll these suggestions were followed by Qutb Shahi Sultans with true spirit, which made them very popular.\n\n### Central Government\n\n**A) Sultan** — The Sultan was head of the Central Government, enjoying extensive powers. He claimed divine right, calling himself ''Zillullah'' (the image of God). \n\nAs Head of State, he was chief of all organs of power — legislature, judiciary, and executive — the only law-giver and Supreme Commander of the armed forces. He lived in grand style and rarely came out of his palace except to receive very important guests. \n\nAccording to an envoy of Shah Abbas of Persia who visited Abdullah-Qutb Shah''s court in 1636–37 CE, the envoy was very much surprised to see the Sultan''s palace, dress, and headgear studded with costly diamonds and gems — even the ministers and high officials lived in grand buildings. Events like birthdays, marriages, and victory celebrations were held grandly, with costly presents received from foreign rulers on such occasions.\n\n**B) Council of Ministers / Privy Council (Majlis-i-Diwandari)** — This body, after the Sultan, played a key role in administration and consisted of powerful, high officials whose laws were respected by all members.\n\n*   Under Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah, the Council was popularly known as ''Majlis-i-Kingash'' and met only for emergency issues.\n*   Under Abdullah-Qutb Shah, the Majlis-i-Diwandari attained complete perfection and met every day.\n*   The Sultan also promoted some Majlis members to the position of ambassadors — e.g., Abdullah-Qutb Shah promoted his minister Ain-ul-Mulk ''Yusufshah'' as Qutb Shahi ambassador at the court of Jahangir in 1630 CE.\n\n**Key Central Offices:**\n\n| Office | Role | Notable Holders |\n|---|---|---|\n| Peshawa / Vakil / Prime Minister | Second in command after the Sultan; later renamed ''Diwan'' | Mustafa Khan-Arbistani; Sheik-Muhammad-Ibnkhatoon; Madanna |\n| Meer-Jumla | Finance Minister; collected taxes and customs | Mir-Momin; Mansur-Khan-Habashi; Muhammad-Said-Ardistani |\n| Ain-ul-Mulk | Minister of Military Affairs | Saif-Khan; Mansoor-Khan |\n| Nazir | Upheld law, order and ethics | Abu-Talib; Meer-Qasim |\n| Majumdar | Chief Auditor-General | Narayan Rao |\n| Dabir | Head of Diwan-e-Insha; translated Farmans | Qazi-Muzaffar Ali; Moulana-Owaisi |\n| Kotwal | Commissioner of Police | — |\n| Sarkhel | Chief Revenue Officer, capital city | Muhammad-Taqi-Shafirul-Mulk; Mirza-Roz Bihan; Sayyed Muzaffar |\n| Hawaldar | In-charge of go-downs and royal stables | — |\n\nThe Peshwa was paid a salary of twelve thousand Hons and, by the period of Abul Hassan-Tanashah, the post was converted into the office of Diwan; Madanna was the last Diwan of Golconda. The Meer-Jumla, also called Jumla-Tul-Mulk, allocated funds to each department and supervised all departmental accounts — Muhammad-Said-Ardistani rose to this post from the lowest position of Royal Records Keeper through hard work and wisdom, becoming very popular in the kingdom. \n\nThe Ain-ul-Mulk also appointed Taraf-level military officers and prepared war strategies, since the Sultan''s safety depended on this department''s efficiency. The Majumdar''s staff checked the accounts of all departments, and the Qutb Shahi Sultans preferred Hindus for this post. The Dabir also stamped Farmans approved by the Sultan and Council. The Kotwal''s systematic functioning was praised by travellers Tavernier, Thevenot, and Bernier, and his office checked every person entering the capital city.\n\nThe Sarkhel also exercised powers over provincial officers and supervised the activities of foreign traders on the East Coast. The Hawaldar was appointed from among trusted men of the Sultan.\n\n### Provincial and Local Administration\n\nLike other medieval rulers, the Qutb Shahi Sultans divided their kingdom into a number of Tarafs or Smits.\n\n*   Under Abdullah-Qutb Shah there were six Tarafs; according to Dutch Company records, the Tarfdar of Masulipatnam once disobeyed the Sultan''s orders and returned gifts sent to him.\n*   Important Smits of the kingdom included Vinkonda, Kondapalli, Masulipatnam, Eluru, Rajahmundry, Visakhapatnam, and Bellamkonda.\n*   Under the last Sultan, Abul-Hasan-Tanashah, the kingdom was divided into **37 Sarkars and 517 Parganas**. The head of a port-town was known as Shah Bandhar. Land revenue collection rights were auctioned to the highest bidders, known as ''Mustagirs.'' Machilipatnam alone fetched more than one lakh eighty thousand pagodas yearly.\n\n**Local Government:** Provinces and Smits were divided into small units, with villages as the foundation of the entire administration. Farmans mention offices such as Deshpande, Deshmukh, Thanedar, and Sthalakarni. The head of the village was called ''Maqadum''; Kulkarni was the Village Accountant, and Deshpande was the Pargana-level Accounts Officer.\n\n### Military System\n\nThe Qutb Shahi Sultans organized their military system on the model of the Bahamani Sultans with minor changes.\n\n*   A strong, efficient standing army was maintained under the direct control of the Sultan and stationed at the capital city.\n*   Golconda''s armies faced the strong challenge of contemporary Deccani Sultans, Vijayanagara, and Mughal rulers.\n*   The military department was headed by Ain-ul-Mulk. The Sultan granted Jagirs to Tarafdars to maintain a fixed number of armed forces.\n*   The army consisted of Hindu, Muslim, and Irani soldiers. A huge amount from the treasury/revenue was allocated to maintain the army — according to one version, during Abul-Hassan-Tanashah''s period, eight lakhs, eighty-four thousand, four hundred and seventy-seven Hons were spent yearly on army salaries.\n*   According to French traveller Thevenot, the monthly salary of a Muslim soldier was ten Hons.\n*   All forts were well built and stationed under experienced Fort Commanders. The uninterrupted wars with Mughals, Marathas, Deccani Shia Sultans, and Vijayanagara kept the Qutb Shahi army alert. Sirlaskhar led the army during campaigns.\n\n### Land Revenue System\n\nAgriculture remained the primary occupation of the majority, and land revenue was the chief source of income for the imperial treasury. Like the Kakatiyas, the Qutb Shahi Sultans made sincere attempts to encourage agriculture by constructing tanks and canals; the tax burden on peasants was normal. The tiller of the land was described as ''Royat'' in contemporary sources.\n\nAccording to contemporary scholar Girdharilal Asqur, in his work _Tariq-e-Zafrah_, the total revenue of the State in 1685–86, from 21 Sarkars, was **82,95,196 Hons**.\n\n**Revenue Divisions and Receipts (from H.K. Sherwani, _History of the Qutb Shahi Dynasty_, 1974, p. 655):**\n\n| Sarkar | Parganas | Total Revenue (Hons) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Muhammad Nagar | 22 | 15,37,739-13 Annas |\n| Medak | 16 | 35,16,089-6 Annas |\n| Kaulas | 05 | 7,42,480-8 Annas |\n| Melangur | 03 | 67,987-0 Annas |\n| Elgandal | 21 | 12,19,507-4 Annas |\n| Warangal | 16 | 7,48,491 |\n| Khammamet | 11 | 6,29,323-13 |\n| Devarakonda | 13 | 21,19,616-4 |\n| Penagal | 05 | 4,55,215-14 |\n| Bhongir | 11 | 1,47,836-9 |\n| Akar-Kara | 06 | 5,18,228-12 |\n| Kovilkonda | 13 | 10,510,517-14 |\n| Ghanpura | 08 | 5,13,484-4 |\n| Murtuzanagar (Guntur) | 39 | 17,07,705-4 |\n| Machilipatnam | 08 | 2,87,000-0 |\n| Nizampatnam | – | 87,000-0 |\n| Eluru | 12 | 6,90,448-15 |\n| Mustafanagar | 24 | 12,00,160-15 |\n| Rajahmundry | 24 | 6,27,853-0 |\n| Sikkol | 115 | 9,09,242-11 |\n| Kanat-rent | – | 2,55,001-0 |\n\nSikkol Sarkar, with 115 Parganas, was the largest in the entire kingdom; the smallest was Melangur, with three Parganas. The highest revenue to the treasury came from Kovilkonda — 10,510,517-14 Annas.\n\n### Judicial System\n\nThe Qutb Shahi Sultans managed the legal system honouring both Hindu (local) and Islamic laws, making arrangements from village level to deal with civil and criminal cases. Qazis, Moulvis, and Hindu Pandits played key roles in solving cases.\n\nSpecial Courts were established — Dadmahal, founded by Sultan-Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah, and Aman-Mahal, founded by Abdullah Qutb Shah. The Sultan attended these special courts once a week, listened to cases, and delivered judgments with the help of Qazis and Pandits. French travellers Tavernier and Bernier appreciated the Qutb Shahi judicial system, noting that judges and the Sultan delivered judgments without fear or favour.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p6", "partName": "Socio-Religious Life of the People of Qutb Shahi Age", "bodyMd": "Glimpses of socio-religious life come from contemporary Telugu, Persian, and Arabic works and from foreign travellers like Tavernier, Bernier, and Thevenot.\n\nThe Qutb Shahi Sultans tried to maintain Hindu-Muslim unity through liberal policies. Works such as _Tariq-e-Ferishta_, _Tariq-e-Qutb Shah_, _Burhan-e-Masir_, and Telugu works like _Thapathi Samvaranophakhyanam_, _Yayathi Charita_, _Vaijayanthi Vilasam_, _Sukasapthati_, and _Hamsavimsathi_ give details about the social life of the people.\n\n**Society and daily life:** Palavekiri Kadiripathi, in his work _Sukasapthati_, described in detail the socio-religious life of Telangana people — dress, food habits, houses, and marriages of Brahmins, Vaishyas, and Sudras.\n\n*   Brahmins were engaged in ritual work, trade, and agriculture.\n*   Hindus employed in service of the Sultan wore long-size silk pyzamas and kurthas, and some wore long cloth caps.\n*   Reddys lived in grand houses; Reddy women wore many types of gold ornaments and costly sarees. Vaishyas were engaged in trade.\n*   A large number of sub-castes emerged during this age: Kapu, Reddy, Velama, Balija, carpenters, weavers, blacksmiths, boya, potter, tailor, fishermen, washermen, barbers, and others played significant roles in society.\n*   **Social evils:** Child marriages, dowry system, Sati, and prostitution prevailed. The condition of Sudras was miserable — they worked in fields and in the houses of rich people.\n*   Hindus and Muslims celebrated many festivals in a true spirit, both believing in orthodox practices — Holi, Ramzan, Muharram, Nauroz, Bakrid, Dasserah, and Deepavali were the main festivals.\n\n**Position of women:** The contemporary Telugu writer Ponnaganti Telaganarya, in his work _Yayathi Charita_, described the condition of women as not so good in general.\n\n*   A woman was respected only in domestic life; outside, she was given no freedom, living under the control of husband or mother-in-law. The position of widows was very pathetic.\n*   Purdah system was followed by both Hindu and Muslim women.\n*   Foreign travellers Tavernier, Methwold, and Frair wrote that women were considered a means of enjoyment by Sultans and their officials; child marriages, Sati, prostitution, and the Devadasi system were common evils.\n*   Women of royal families like Hayat Bakshi Begum and Chand Bibi excelled in fine arts and politics. Rural women spent most of their time in agricultural labour; some engaged in caste-based professions like weaving. Girls were generally trained in dance, music, and painting.\n*   Polygamy was common among both Hindus and Muslims. Courtesans were patronized by the ruling class — according to Tavernier, there were twenty thousand prostitutes in Hyderabad and Golconda fort, granted licenses by the government to carry on their profession.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p7", "partName": "Economy under Qutb Shahis", "bodyMd": "The Golconda Kingdom was one of the richest medieval kingdoms in the Deccan, owing to two main factors: the natural wealth of the kingdom, especially its rich diamond mines, and its fertile coastal land, water sources, and brisk foreign trade carried through Masulipatnam port with Central Asia and European countries.\n\n### Agriculture and Irrigational Facilities\n\nAgriculture was the chief occupation of the majority. Agricultural land was divided into two categories — Zamindari Lands and Haveli Lands. Agricultural work was carried out between June and October. Commercial crops like cotton, jute, pulses, fruits, and flowers were grown on a large scale; fruits and flowers flourished especially in Telangana, and Eluru in coastal Andhra was famous for indigo cultivation. Thevenot''s account described the different varieties of grapes cultivated in Telangana by the peasants.\n\n**Irrigation works:** Like the earlier Kakatiya rulers, the Qutb Shahi Sultans realized the importance of construction and maintenance of irrigational works, and many Kakatiya-era tanks were repaired.\n\n*   Under Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah, Hussain-Sagar tank, Budwel tank, and Ibrahimpatnam tank were constructed.\n*   A tank was built at ''Durg,'' five kilometres from Golconda fort, supplying water to the royal family and officials through hydrological engineering skill.\n*   Ma-Saheb Tank was built by Khanam-Aga (popularly known as _Maseha_) near Saifabad to supply water to the local public.\n*   An inscription dated 1551 CE states that Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah repaired the ''Pangal'' tank and ''Udaya Samudram'' tank; from the total product under these two tanks, the king was given a 1% share, peasants a 1½% share, Brahmins 1%, and soldiers 1%.\n*   Officials like Rahmatullah and Anthoji (Hawaldar) also encouraged repairing tanks and canals in the Nellore region; Nellore District inscriptions record that rich people, temple trustees, masjid boards, and village assemblies also extended liberal support for construction and repair, increasing the area of agricultural land and production.\n\n### Trade and Commerce\n\nInternal and external trade flourished during this period. Vaishyas (Komatis) played an active role in both. Arab and Portuguese traders supplied the best-breed horses to the Qutb Shahis. Telugu works _Sukasapthati_ and _Hamsavimsati_ provide details of the external trade of this age.\n\n*   After the advent of European trading companies, Masulipatnam, Kondapalli, Narasapuram, Madras, and Golconda emerged as famous trading centres.\n*   Internal trade was carried on bullock-carts, oxen, donkeys, and tangas; external trade was known as _Odaberamu_ (maritime trade).\n*   Masulipatnam, Motupalli, and Narasapuram were the chief ports; best-quality textiles were exported from Masulipatnam to European countries.\n*   Vasumata and Visnudasa were two rich traders of this period. Pilgrimage centres like Tirupati, Srisailam, and Srikalahasti also became famous trading centres.\n*   Golconda, Hyderabad, Warangal, Masulipatnam, Kondapalli, and Penugonda were the main urban centres; rural economy was in good condition, with weekly fairs (angadis) held in villages.\n*   Trade links flourished between Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, and Bengal. ''Perikas'' carried goods and grain from place to place in jute sacks on their heads or on oxen. Golconda''s traders maintained trade relations with the tradesmen of Gulbarga, Aurangabad, Bijapur, and Madras.\n*   Ship-building industry flourished at Narsapur and Masulipatnam. Nirmal and Indur were famous for the iron industry, producing best-quality swords.\n*   The Sultans encouraged trade through tax concessions and by appointing special officers to check weights and measurements, protecting the public from cheating by traders.\n*   The chief currency was Honnu, a gold coin foreigners described as ''Pagoda''; Panam, Thaar, and Kasu were other currency units. Kharkhanas were established by the Sultans to produce clothes and other articles for the royal family.\n*   Chief exports of the period included dress, cloth, diamonds, carpets, indigo, and best-quality swords; chief imports from Arabia, Central Asia, and Europe included horses, spices, gold, lead, dates, and crockery.\n\n### Industries of the Golconda Kingdom\n\nContemporary Telugu literary works, Farmans of the Sultans, and travel accounts of foreign traders provide valuable information about the flourishing industries of the period — diamond mining, diamond cutting and polishing, textile industry, carpet industry, ship-building industry, saltpetre industry, iron and steel industry, and Kalamkari industry. Skilled and trained persons alone were engaged in these industries, some of which depended on agricultural raw material and some on mineral resources; in some industries, work was done on a hereditary basis.\n\n**Diamonds and the diamond industry:** The Golconda Kingdom became very famous for its best-quality diamonds, which enjoyed great demand in the European market.\n\n*   ''Kollur'' near the Krishna River and Ramalla-Kota (Kurnool) were famous for the best-quality diamonds, and these mines existed since the pre-Qutb Shahi age — French traveller Tavernier noted the mines were in operation even during Vijayanagara times.\n*   In 1640 CE, when Tavernier visited the famous Kollur mine, nearly thirty thousand workers — men, women, and children — were engaged in digging, cleaning, and drying diamonds.\n*   World-famous diamonds like Kohinoor, Hope, Pitt, Pigot, and Nizam were found in the Golconda mines. Foreign travellers Francis-Co-Feria (1548 CE), Jaqude-Cotre (Spain), Nicolo-de-Conti (Italy), Tavernier, Bernier, Thevenot (all French), Garcia-de-Ortha, and William-Methwold also mentioned the Golconda diamond mines in their accounts. \n*   According to Tavernier, when he visited Golconda in 1645 CE, he saw the polishing of diamonds near the ''Carvan'' area, west of Charminar.\n*   The world-famous **Kohinoor Diamond** was found at Kollur mine in 1656 CE, during the reign of Abdullah-Qutb Shah; Tavernier also mentioned seeing the Kohinoor in the treasury of Aurangzeb. It weighed 106 carats, passed through many rulers, and was presented to Queen Victoria by Lord Laurence in 1849 after the conquest of Punjab; it is now preserved in the Imperial Rachegali Museum, England.\n*   The Sultans encouraged diamond trade by granting concessions to traders and leasing mining rights to contractors — according to Moorland, the Sultan received an income of nearly three lakh Pagodas from the Ramallakota mine every year, and good income from Kollur and other mines, while contractors also earned huge profits.\n\n**Textile industry:** Golconda textiles enjoyed great demand in the European market, produced in various qualities and colours (red, blue, white) by Golconda''s weavers; contractors paid weavers advances to secure products on time. Many types of flowers and leaves were printed on textiles and sarees; Chintz cloth was very popular. Kalankari cloth was produced by expert weavers at Masulipatnam, Palakollu, and Nizampatnam; best-quality calico cloth was produced at Narasapuram. Contemporary literature names varieties like Thanugudda, Lungi, Salempuri, and Palampur.\n\n**Carpet industry:** The Deccan was famous for best-quality carpets, and Warangal carpets earned a special place in the market. Mettawada and Girmajipeta areas of Warangal were inhabited by many Muslim carpet-weaving families; raw material for carpet-making was available in large quantity in Telangana. Sir George Watt, in his book _Indian Art at Delhi, Calcutta_, praised the great quality of Warangal carpets and the excellent colours used by carpet weavers.\n\n**Ship-building industry:** External trade with Persia and Europe flourished, with big vessels/ships used in sea voyages. Narsapuram, Bheemunipatnam, and Machilipatnam were important shipbuilding centres. Foreign travel accounts of Methwold and Shorer refer to the big and small ships prepared by experienced workers at Narsapur, made using best-quality teak wood and iron.\n\n**Saltpetre industry:** This industry, producing powder used in guns and firangis, flourished under the Qutb Shahis; Masulipatnam, Narsapuram, and Pulicat were famous for saltpetre products.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p8", "partName": "Progress of Telugu Literature under Qutb Shahis", "bodyMd": "The Qutb Shahi Sultans and their officials were great patrons and lovers of the Telugu language; some of them personally learned Telugu. The age of Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah especially became famous for literary activity.\n\n*   Telugu poet Addanki Gangadhara was patronized by Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah, dedicating his work _Thapathi Samvarnopakhyanam_ to the Sultan, who was praised by court poets as ''Malikiba-Rama.''\n*   Kandukuri Rudrakavi, author of _Niramkusopakhyanam_, was also honoured by Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah — impressed by his scholarship, the Sultan granted him the village of ''Chintapalem'' as a gift.\n*   Aminkhan, the in-charge officer of Patancheru under Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah, patronized another famous Telugu poet, Ponneganti Telaganarya, who dedicated his work _Yayati Charitam_ to Amin-Khan. This was the first Telugu work written in ''Achha Telugu.''\n*   Many Telugu scholars and poets wrote Chatu-verses praising Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah''s great qualities.\n\n**Under Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah**, Telugu scholars and poets continued to receive liberal patronage:\n\n*   Ganesha Pandita was the court poet.\n*   Saranga Thammayya, another great Telugu poet at the court — originally the in-charge Revenue Officer (Karanam) of Golconda — wrote the popular Telugu work _Vaijayanthi Vilasam_.\n*   Kshetrayya, the famous Telugu ''Padakartha,'' was a contemporary of Abdullah-Qutb Shah; he visited Abdullah-Qutb Shah''s court and sang more than one thousand Padakeerthanas praising the Sultan. A native of ''Movva'' village in Krishna District, his Padakeerthanas became very popular throughout the Qutb Shahi kingdom.\n\n**Under the last Sultan, Abul Hassan Tanashah**, Telugu scholars continued to receive liberal support, including from the rulers of Jataprole and Gadwal Samsthans.\n\n*   Kancharla Gopanna (Bhakta Ramadas), the Revenue Officer (Tahsildar) of Khammam under Abul Hassan-Tanashah, constructed a temple to Lord Sri Rama at Bhadrachalam — he was later imprisoned for using public money for this construction. Tradition holds that while Gopanna languished in jail for the diverted revenue, Lord Rama and Lakshmana themselves appeared before Tanashah''s treasury and repaid the amount on his behalf, moving the Sultan to release him. He sang many keerthanas on Lord Sri Rama that became very popular throughout Andhradesa, becoming popularly known as Bhakta Ramadas; his work _Dasarathi Satakam_ is a classical work that received great appreciation from scholars.\n*   According to scholars, Prajakavi Yogivemana also belongs to this age — in his poetry, Vemana, in simple language, condemned the social evils and orthodox religious practices of the age.\n\nThus, Telugu literature under the Qutb Shahis continued to enjoy royal and popular support as in the Vijayanagara age; the Qutb Shahi Sultans and their officials did great service to the Telugu language.\n\n**Patronage of Hindu officials:** The Qutb Shahi Sultans honoured local customs and employed Hindus in positions of trust. Mahmud-Quli-Qutb Shah appointed a Hindu, Dharmarao, as his Commander-in-Chief, and another Hindu, Ganapathirao, as governor in charge of festivals and local administration. Fort commanders at key defensive strongholds such as Kondapalli, Koyilakonda, Kondaveedu, Ghanapuram, and Panagal were largely drawn from the Nayaka community.\n\nMuhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah himself composed poetry in Urdu, Persian, and Telugu, though his Telugu verses have not survived; his surviving Divans (poetry collections) in Persian and Urdu together run to about 1800 pages. Abdullah-Qutb Shah was also a devoted patron of learning and had several Sanskrit and Persian romantic tales translated into Urdu, including the tale of Yusuf and Zulaikha.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter11-qutbshahi-rule-p9", "partName": "Art and Architecture", "bodyMd": "The Qutb Shahi Sultans were great lovers of fine arts, constructing many grand buildings — palaces, mosques, forts, asrukhanas, bridges, cities, hospitals, tombs, tanks, and sarais across their kingdom. Among the significant buildings of the Qutb Shahi age, Hyderabad city, Charminar, Mecca Masjid, Toli Masjid, Taramati Baradari, Puranapul, and Hussain Sagar are known even today for their beauty.\n\n### Charminar\n\nCharminar, the edifice of the four minarets, is the legendary masterpiece of the Qutb Shahis. This grand rectangular structure was built upon four grand arches by Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah in **1590–91 CE**, standing as an example of the artistic skill of Qutb Shahi masons and other workers.\n\n### Hyderabad (1590–91 CE)\n\nMuhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah (1580–1612 CE) was a great lover of architecture, and the city of Hyderabad was his own brainchild. It was constructed on the southern bank of the river Musi to accommodate the growing population of the Golconda Kingdom. There is no uniform opinion among historians regarding the real reason for the construction and naming of the city.\n\n**Planning and design:** In the mission of constructing Hyderabad, the Sultan received admirable support from his Peshwa, Mir-Momin Astrabadi, who hailed from a highly respected ''Moosavi'' Syed family of Astrabadi in the Gillon province of Iran — a Scholar, Administrator, Hakim, Engineer, and Alim. He reached Golconda in 1581 CE, won the Sultan''s favour, and rose to the position of ''Peshwa'' by 1585 CE.\n\n*   Mir-Momin Astrabadi planned Hyderabad on the model of ''Isfahan'' of Iran; he designed the first outline map of the city.\n*   The plan was a triangle marking the location of essential buildings, with Charminar as the centre, enclosed between the four arches with a plaza at the centre — replacing ''Maidan-i-Naqsh Jahan'' of Safawid Isfahan.\n*   Charminar was built where the four roads intersected: the northern road extended as far as the Musi river, the southern road went straight to Koh-i-Tur, the eastern road led to the coastal region of the Bay of Bengal, and the western road led to Golconda and beyond.\n*   Charkaman (Julukhana) was built eighty yards north of Charminar. The city extended on the northern side up to a hillock called Naubat Pahad (Ghat), beside which was Hussain Sagar.\n*   During Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah''s period, many beautiful gardens, mosques, palaces, sarais, buildings, and asrukhanas were constructed. Hyderabad attracted many scholars, artists, and Alims from Persia, becoming home to all peace-loving people and a centre of composite culture.\n\nThe poet-king Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah, on the eve of laying the city''s foundation, prayed that the city would bring peace and prosperity, and that people of all castes, creeds, and religions would make it their home like fishes in the ocean.\n\n**Contemporary praise:** An anonymous historian, writing in _Tarikh-e-Qutb Shahi_ (1616 CE) — twenty-five years after the city''s construction — described its beauty and pleasant atmosphere, noting that it lacked nothing and that even an old man hurrying to the city would recover his youth there.\n\nAnother historian, Muhammad Saqui, Court Chancellor of Aurangzeb, who accompanied the emperor during the 1687 siege of Golconda, described Hyderabad as the most pleasant place on earth, whose air and water purify the hearts of people and give them long life. Foreign travellers Tavernier, Bernier, and William Methwold also praised the climate, air, water, trees, and mountains of Hyderabad, calling it heaven on earth.\n\n### Darush-Shifa or House of Cure (1595 CE)\n\nMuhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah constructed Darush-Shifa, a Unani hospital, in 1595 CE for the treatment of poor people. Located close to Purani Haveli, it was a double-storied hospital-cum-residential college; doctors from various countries treated patients there, who were given free medicine and food.\n\n### Golconda Fort\n\nAccording to historians, Golconda fort was built during the Kakatiya period; the grand palaces, mosques, and buildings constructed by different Qutb Shahi Sultans stand as symbols of their imperial power and royalty. It was also known as Muhammadnagar. A traditional account holds that the name ''Golconda'' derives from ''Golla Konda'' (shepherd''s hill) in Telugu, from a legend of a shepherd boy who found an idol on the hill, prompting the Kakatiyas to build a mud fort at the spot.\n\n*   The founder, Sultan Quli, constructed a rock fort in place of the old mud fort.\n*   Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah prepared a grand plan to strengthen the fort, constructing a 7-kilometre-long strong rock boundary with deep trenches, 87 semi-circular bastions (Burjus), and 8 Darwazas. Pettaburj, Moosaburj, and Kagaziburj are famous bastions located in the fort; Fateh Darwaja, Mecca Darwaza, Banjara Darwaza, Patanchru Darwaza, and Moti Darwaza are important gates.\n*   The most famous Fateh Darwaza (Fateh Gate) is 13 feet wide and 25 feet high; best-quality thick teak shutters and iron sheets with spikes were used to prevent elephants and enemies from battering the gate.\n*   Important buildings within the fort — Diwan-Mahal, Jami-Masjid, Bala Hissar, Nagina Bagh, Silah Khana, Ashurkhana, and Katora House — are known for their beauty.\n\n**Defense engineering:** The fort''s builders incorporated remarkable defensive features for its time.\n\n*   Molten lead and boiling oil could be poured onto attackers from the ramparts through openings above the gates, to prevent enemy soldiers and elephants from breaking through.\n*   A ''Kartalai wall'' (clapping wall) was built to allow guards to detect enemy movement by sound.\n*   The fort''s acoustic design allowed a hand-clap or particular sound made at the entrance dome (Fateh Darwaza) to be heard clearly at the Bala Hissar pavilion, nearly a kilometre away at the highest point of the fort — an ingenious early-warning system that is a striking example of the technical knowledge of the age.\n\nGolconda fort stands as a symbol of Qutb Shahi glory and imperial power; it was captured on **September 21, 1687**, by Aurangzeb''s forces.\n\n### Puranapul (1578 CE)\n\nPuranapul, an old bridge over the river Musi, was constructed by Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah in 1578 CE. It has 22 arches, is 600 feet long, 35 feet broad, and 54 feet above the river bed. French traveller Tavernier, when he visited Hyderabad in 1676 CE, was very much impressed by its construction, describing it as the bridge of love and as beautiful as the ''Pont Neuf of Paris.''\n\n### Hussain Sagar (1562 CE)\n\nHussain-Shah-Wali, a famous Sufi saint and son-in-law of Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah, constructed Hussain Sagar in 1562 CE, linking the twin cities of Hyderabad and Secunderabad. It is one and a half miles long and covers an area of eight square miles; the dam was fed by branches of the Balakpur river from the Musi, about 32 miles from Hyderabad.\n\n### Mecca Masjid\n\nSultan Muhammad Qutb Shah (1612–1626 CE) laid the foundation for this grand mosque under the guidance of Darga Mir Faizullah Baig and Chowdary Rajaiah. According to one version, construction started in 1614 CE; due to various reasons, it was completed only in 1693 CE, by the Mughal emperor Aurangzeb. Masons and stone-cutters from Persia and Arabia played a key role in its construction.\n\nLocated just 100 yards south-west of Charminar, the mosque takes its name from the Grand Mosque at Mecca, on which it is patterned. The hall measures 67 by 54 metres and is 23 metres high; the roof is supported by 15 arches, five on each of three sides, with the western side blocked by a high wall to provide the ''Mehrab.'' \n\nThis mosque is one of the largest in India and can accommodate ten thousand people for prayer at a time. According to French traveller Tavernier, the stone for the prayer niche was hewn out of a quarry and carried by several hundred pairs of bullocks to the site; when he visited in 1676 CE, nearly four thousand labourers, eight thousand masons, and two thousand cutters were engaged. It is believed that some bricks made from earth brought from Mecca were inserted over the central arch — this is why the mosque became popularly known as Mecca Masjid. Tavernier was very much impressed by the beauty of its pillars and arches.\n\n### Qutb Shahi Tombs\n\nThe tombs constructed by the Qutb Shahi Sultans became very popular for their magnificent architectural beauty. They are located one kilometre away from Golconda fort, near Banjara Darwaza.\n\n*   The tomb of Sultan-Quli-Qutb Shah is 9.5 metres square on a platform of 30.5 sq. metres; the tomb of Jamsheed is constructed on its south-western side.\n*   The tomb of Ibrahim-Quli-Qutb Shah is a quadrangular building with two rows of five arches, one over the other, giving it a two-storey effect.\n*   The tomb of Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah, founder of Hyderabad, is another wonderful construction — 42.5 metres high with a dome of 18.2 metres.\n*   The tombs of Muhammad-Qutb Shah and Abdullah-Qutb Shah are also built within this complex, as is the tomb of Hayat Bakshi Begum, daughter of Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah — a small mosque attached to her tomb is where Aurangzeb is said to have offered prayers during his Golconda campaign.\n\nToday the Qutb Shah Tombs stand as an example of excellent medieval Deccani Islamic architecture, are on the list of world monuments, and attract thousands of tourists every day. French traveller Tavernier was deeply impressed by their beauty; according to him, poor-feeding activity took place near the tombs every day at 4:00 pm.\n\n### Hayatnagar Mosque (1626 CE)\n\nHayat Baksh Begum, the only daughter of Muhammad-Quli-Qutb Shah, was known for her great qualities and knowledge in public service and state affairs.\n\n*   She constructed a tank named Ma-Saheba tank near Saifabad in 1625 CE.\n*   She built the Khairatabad Mosque in memory of her tutor, Ak-hund-mulla-Abdulmalik.\n*   She also built a village (16 miles east of Hyderabad) named Hayatnagar in 1626 CE, where a palace and mosque were also constructed.\n*   A grand Sarai was constructed by her near the Carvan area to facilitate travellers.\n\nShe died at the age of 76 in 1666 CE. Abdullah Qutb Shah, her son, built a beautiful mosque adjacent to her tomb within the complex of the Qutb Shahi Tombs.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter12-mughal-interregnum', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter12-mughal-interregnum-p1", "partName": "Fall of Golconda and the Beginning of Mughal Rule (1687–1724 CE)", "bodyMd": "In 1687 CE, Aurangzeb conquered the Golconda kingdom. The last Golconda Sultan, Abul Hasan Tana Shah, accepted defeat and was taken captive, imprisoned at Daulatabad fort. He died there in 1699 CE, and his body was buried adjacent to his guru Shah Raj Kakkal''s burial site near Khurdabad.\n\nWith this, the Qutb Shahi kingdom, which had ruled the Telangana region from Golconda for nearly 175 years since 1518 CE, came to an end. Golconda became part of the Mughal Empire, and the Telugu people were brought under the Mughal emperor''s direct rule. The vast wealth of the Golconda Sultans — gold, jewels, silver, diamonds, and other precious stones — was taken into Mughal possession. The territories once ruled by the Golconda Sultans came under the control of Mughal viceroys from 1687 CE to 1724 CE.\n\nAfter Aurangzeb''s death in 1707 CE, his weak successors failed to control the Deccan effectively. Combined with the selfish conduct of the Mughal viceroys, this encouraged revolts among the people — Sarvai Papanna''s revolt against the oppressive rule of the Mughal viceroys being one such act.\n\n### Sources\n\nInformation about the fall of Golconda and the establishment of the Hyderabad state comes from several sources: Kafi Khan''s _Muntakhab-ul-Lubab_, and the travel accounts of Ferishta, Tavernier, Bernier, Thevenot, and Martin, which throw considerable light on the events of this period.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter12-mughal-interregnum-p2", "partName": "Early Mughal Administration in Hyderabad (1687–1700 CE)", "bodyMd": "### Rahulla Khan and Zulfiqar Khan\n\nBefore leaving for Delhi, Aurangzeb appointed Rahulla Khan as a temporary representative of the Mughal emperor in Golconda. Soon after Aurangzeb''s departure, the outskirts of Hyderabad city were attacked by the Marathas, who ravaged several villages, leaving the ordinary people without protection. Unable to counter the Maratha attacks, Rahulla Khan took shelter in Golconda fort.\n\nAurangzeb then handed over the responsibility of ruling Hyderabad to Zulfiqar Khan, who before 1687 CE had been the subedar of Bijapur and had settled the situation there.\n\n**Zulfiqar Khan''s reforms:**\n\n*   He was an efficient military general and an able ruler, who strengthened the army and protected Hyderabad from Maratha attacks.\n*   To consolidate Hyderabad''s position, he undertook repairs of the forts of Kaulas, Elgandal, Koilkonda, Warangal, and Panagal, and stationed military forces at these forts.\n*   He continued the fauzdars appointed during the Qutb Shahi period — most of whom were Dakkani Muslims — who held authority to impose and collect taxes from lands within a 10-kilometre radius of the Haveli Parganas.\n*   To control the Dakkani Muslims and revolts from local Pindaris, Zulfiqar Khan appointed cavalry in the parganas and established special police stations.\n\n### Fauzdars, Circars, and Local Administration\n\nAfter 1687 CE, under the rule of the Mughal viceroys, there were nine fauzdaris in Karnataka: (1) Kaulas, (2) Elgandal, (3) Narsapur, (4) Kohir, (5) Bhongir, (6) Warangal, (7) Khammammet, (8) Chicacole (Srikakulam), and (9) Narsapur (East Godavari). Similarly, four more fauzdars were appointed in Cuddapah, Kurnool, Adoni, and Nellore in Rayalaseema.\n\nOn the east coast, Chicacole, Rajahmundry, Machilipatnam, and Nizampatnam were formed into Circars. For easy and efficient administration, each Circar region was divided into several districts, with each district placed under the headship of a ''Deshmukh''. Alongside the Deshmukhs, landlords (zamindars) belonging to the Velama, Kamma, and Reddi castes also played an important role in administration before 1687 CE.\n\n**Revenue machinery:**\n\n*   Deshpande prepared the list of land taxes and other information related to cultivable land.\n*   Fauzdars, Deshmukhs, and Havaldars were appointed to collect various taxes from the people, working with the help of local zamindars.\n\n### Zamindars under Mughal Rule\n\nDuring the Qutb Shahi period, there were one or two zamindars in each district. These zamindars had the right to collect taxes from the local people and were also conferred titles such as ''Manne-Sultan'' and other honours from the Sultan, which strengthened their position.\n\n**Revolt after 1687 CE:** Taking advantage of the disharmony and violence caused by the Maratha attacks in the Deccan, some zamindars declined to accept Mughal superiority and revolted against them.\n\n*   Important zamindars from Telangana who questioned the authority of the Mughal Viceroy came from Kaulas, Palvancha, Jataprolu, and Noorkoel.\n*   In coastal Andhra, zamindari estates existed at Vizianagaram, Peddapuram, Mogaltur, Nuzividu, Vinukonda, and Kondavidu.\n*   In Bobbili, Velama zamindars emerged strong; the families of Malhraju of Vinukonda and Manikyarao of Kondavidu were bitter enemies. The Vasireddi family took advantage of this rivalry and occupied Chintapalli, later occupying more villages in Guntur Circar. The most famous of the Vasireddi Zamindars was **Venkatadri Naidu**, during whose time Amaravati emerged as an important centre.\n*   In Nellore, the zamindars of Venkatagiri, Chundi, and Muthyalapadu were strong.\n\n**Poligars of Rayalaseema:** In Rayalaseema, the Poligars (palegallu) revolted against the Mughal officer Rustumdiq Khan, who suppressed the revolts of the zamindars and Poligars with the help of a huge army.\n\nThe zamindars and Poligars finally signed a peace treaty, recognizing and accepting Mughal authority in the region. Further, it was agreed between the Mughal viceroy, zamindars, and poligars that they should maintain an army, construct forts, and collect fixed taxes from farmers in their parganas — the zamindars in turn helping the Mughal viceroy with administrative matters. Consequently, peaceful conditions prevailed in Hyderabad by 1701 CE.\n\n### Fiscal Reforms (1689–1700 CE)\n\nAround 1689–1700 CE, the Mughal emperor instructed the Mughal viceroys in the Deccan to introduce new and innovative mechanisms for enhancing profits, maintaining the army, and meeting miscellaneous expenditure.\n\n*   Mohammad Shafi abolished the ''Ijara'' system during his diwanship, and prepared a list (''zaam-i-kamir'') of all the taxes to be collected from every district.\n*   As a result, taxes that farmers from coastal districts had to pay to the government increased from 10 percent to 13 percent.\n*   Commercial taxes, sales taxes, toll taxes, market taxes, and others were also increased simultaneously; Chaudharis were appointed to collect these taxes.\n*   The profits from Hyderabad increased to approximately 2.20 crores.\n*   In 1691 CE, when the Mughal officers and employees humiliated the people of Pulivendula (Cuddapah district), the merchants of the place migrated to other provinces; the local officer, Mohammad Jaffar, later recalled the migrants by promising them special concessions, facilities, and protection.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter12-mughal-interregnum-p3", "partName": "Administrative Matters of Hyderabad (1700–1707 CE)", "bodyMd": "In 1700, Khan Sifar Khan, appointed by Aurangzeb as the subedar of Hyderabad, died. The Padshah (emperor) then selected Prince Kambaksh as the subedar of Hyderabad, with Rustumdil Khan appointed to assist the prince in administrative matters. Aurangzeb had decided to make Kambaksh the ruler of Hyderabad and Bijapur, but Rustumdil Khan — who had held important positions in Hyderabad between 1687 and 1700 CE — developed differences with Kambaksh.\n\n**Maratha attacks and unrest:**\n\n*   Aurangzeb fell ill during this time and lost control over Deccan affairs. In 1702 CE, nearly fifty thousand Marathas under the leadership of Tarabai attacked Hyderabad and plundered it for three days, torturing and terrorizing the people.\n*   The selfish Rustum Khan made a secret agreement with the Maratha army, paying them seven lakh rupees from the treasury — creating commotion and tension among the local Hyderabadi Muslims.\n*   Soon after recovering from illness, Aurangzeb took measures to set matters right in Hyderabad and the Deccan. He transferred Riza Khan, the Fauzdar of Hyderabad, to Ramgir in Bidar.\n*   Riza Khan, however, violated the emperor''s orders — with his own army of ten thousand soldiers, he robbed travellers and merchants moving between Hyderabad and Machilipatnam, creating terror among the people and questioning the authority of the Mughal emperor.\n*   Around the same time, from 1701–1702, severe drought conditions prevailed in the Hyderabad Suba. According to contemporary European accounts, nearly 20,000 people died as a result.\n*   By 1704 CE, the people of Hyderabad had lost faith in the Mughal emperor. The Marathas ransacked the outskirts of Hyderabad again in 1704 CE, looting the people, and Maratha armies marched on Machilipatnam.\n*   Unable to control the situation, the Mughal Fauzdar of Machilipatnam fled. Under these precarious circumstances, the East India Company came to the rescue of the people and local traders — with its limited army and a well-executed plan, it was able to drive away the Marathas.\n*   The people subsequently lost confidence in the Mughal emperor entirely.\n\n### The Six New Subas of the Deccan\n\nAfter Aurangzeb''s occupation of the Golconda kingdom, the Telugu people became an integral part of the Mughal Empire. Between 1687 and 1724, the Deccan was under the authority of the Mughal viceroys.\n\nAurangzeb''s plan was to keep Golconda and Bijapur separated as two different kingdoms:\n\n*   The **first kingdom** consisted of parts of Telangana and coastal Andhra, along with the Hyderabad–Karnataka regions.\n*   The **second kingdom** comprised the Rayalaseema region along with Bijapur–Karnataka.\n\nThis arrangement led to the establishment of **six new subas** in the Mughal Deccan: (1) Khandesh, (2) Berar, (3) Aurangabad, (4) Bidar, (5) Hyderabad, and (6) Bijapur. Later, the Hyderabad suba came to be known as **Balaghat** or **Painghat**.\n\n*   In the Balaghat suba, there were the Siddhavatam, Gandikota, Gutti, and Kambham Circars, which after some time were merged to form the ''Cuddapah'' kingdom, under the control of Pathan kings.\n*   The second suba spread from Guntur to Kaleman on the Coromandel Coast and was called the ''Arcot'' suba, with Vellore, Jinji, and Thiruchirapalli joined to it.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter12-mughal-interregnum-p4", "partName": "War of Succession and the Restoration of an Independent Golconda (1707–1713 CE)", "bodyMd": "Aurangzeb died in 1707 CE at the age of 99. After his death, a war of succession broke out among his sons — Kambaksh (Deccan Subedar), Azam Shah (Viceroy of Gujarat), and Shah Alam (Viceroy of Kabul) — all three fighting each other to occupy the throne.\n\n*   In 1708 CE, **Shah Alam** defeated and killed Azam Shah in the Battle of Jajau near Agra, and later crowned himself Mughal emperor as **''Bahadur Shah''**.\n*   During this time, **Kambaksh** declared himself Sultan of Golconda in Hyderabad. By 1708 CE, an independent Golconda kingdom was thus reestablished, bringing together previous territories of the Qutb Shahi kingdom once again under one rule.\n\n**Kambaksh''s reign:** Local people hoped their problems would be solved under Kambaksh''s rule. However, Rustum Khan had become his enemy, encouraged by the new Mughal emperor Bahadur Shah I. Rustum, overambitious, planned a conspiracy against Kambaksh; Kambaksh tried to implement Bahadur Shah''s Farmans in Golconda, but ultimately deceived Rustum, killed him, and confiscated all his properties.\n\nKambaksh also looted the khazanas (treasuries) in Machilipatnam, imposed ''Jizia'' in Hyderabad, and raised taxes — the merchants and traders of Hyderabad were terrorized by his atrocities. All classes of people grew resentful of his acts; Mughal officers and zamindars who had until then supported him stopped paying taxes and violated his orders. These conditions worsened further as a band of raiders took advantage of the situation to rob travellers on the roads — among these bands, **Sarvai Papanna** of Khilashapur (Warangal) was prominent.\n\nBy January 1707, Hyderabad had become an epicentre for Mughal politics: both Kambaksh and Bahadur Shah I contested for the Mughal throne. In the combat that followed, Kambaksh was defeated and killed, and Bahadur Shah I was crowned Mughal emperor.\n\n**Bahadur Shah I''s Darbar (1707 CE):** Soon after his coronation, the new emperor organized a People''s Darbar in 1707 CE and invited several zamindars, including Sarvai Papanna.\n\nThe emperor not only recognized Sarvai Papanna as a king but also honoured him with a ''Robe of Honour''; in return, Papanna offered the emperor vast wealth. This honour displeased the local high-class Muslims (Shah-i-Nayath), who immediately complained to the Mughal emperor about him.\n\nBahadur Shah I ordered Hyderabad''s new Governor, Yousuf Khan, to punish Papanna — but according to contemporary historian Khafi Khan, the Hyderabad Governor passed this responsibility to his military commander, ''Dilawar Khan''.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter12-mughal-interregnum-p5", "partName": "Sarvai Papanna (1695–1710 CE)", "bodyMd": "After the end of Qutb Shahi rule, Sarvai Papanna strived to liberate the people of Telangana from the rule of the Mughal viceroys between 1695 and 1710 CE. He made Khilashapur — located in the north-east of Warangal, 50 kilometres from Hyderabad in the Golconda kingdom — his main centre of activity. His struggle was mainly directed against the dominant social groups and ruling classes.\n\n**Background:**\n\n*   Sarvai Papanna''s native village was ''Tarikonda'', located 25 kilometres north-west of Warangal.\n*   Papanna belonged to the ''Kallu gita'' or Gaundla caste. From the very beginning, his aim was not to take up his caste-based profession.\n*   This is evident from Telugu folk literature, in which Papanna, addressing his mother, is recorded as saying: _\"Tying toddy pots to the palm trees, getting them down and getting a share in that is not my taste. My hand should fall on the walls of the Golconda fort.\"_\n*   In a similar vein, a Telugu verse written in the first half of the 20th century notes that Papanna considered \"toddy tapper\" a resemblance of hard work, someone possessing good leadership qualities and knowing well the proper utilization of resources — such a person alone being eligible to occupy the Golconda fort and rise as leader of the various castes.\n\nVarious heroic acts of Sarvai Papanna as a rebel against Mughal rule, in the aftermath of the fall of the Golconda kingdom, are recorded in historic literature, folklore, and oral traditions. Aurangzeb''s contemporary Khafi Khan also provides valuable information about Sarvai Papanna in his work, _Muntakhab-ul-Lubab_.\n\n**Rise to prominence:**\n\n*   Papanna''s activities began shortly after the Mughal army occupied the Golconda kingdom. He constructed a small fort at Tarikonda and opposed the fauzdars and zamindars who supported the Mughal emperor.\n*   He also worked for some time, along with his followers, in the army of Venkata Rao, the zamindar of Kaulas. He later moved to Shapur, which became his base.\n*   The soldiers of the Kaulas zamindar attacked and arrested Papanna, but the zamindar later released him along with his followers.\n*   By 1701 CE, Zamindar Venkata Rao accepted Aurangzeb''s authority and gave away his army — for this act, he was given the position of ''Mansabdar'' in the Mughal emperor''s army, becoming the first Telugu zamindar to join the Mughal army.\n*   Under these circumstances, Aurangzeb appointed Qasim Khan, the fauzdar of Kolanupak, to suppress Sarvai Papanna. In the battle that ensued between the forces of Papanna and Qasim Khan at Kolanupak, Qasim Khan lost his life — making the rise of Papanna an important issue for the Mughal empire.\n\n**Confrontations with Rustumdil Khan:**\n\n*   In 1702 CE, the deputy governor of Hyderabad suba, Rustumdil Khan, confronted Papanna with a huge army. Papanna''s key follower ''Sarvadu'' escaped, and the fort constructed by Papanna was partially destroyed — the Mughals, however, failed to achieve complete victory.\n*   After Rustumdil Khan''s return to Hyderabad, Papanna reached Khilashapur along with his followers and built a new stone fort in place of the old one, helped in this effort by Sarvadu and Purdhil Khan. Shortly afterward, both of these men turned enemies and killed each other; after this, Sarvai Papanna was recognized as the sole opponent of Mughal authority in the Telangana region.\n*   In 1706 CE, Rustumdil Khan made another futile attempt to suppress Sarvai Papanna.\n\n**Later years and death:** After Aurangzeb''s death in February 1707 CE, a war of succession broke out among his sons. Considering this an opportune time, Sarvai Papanna occupied the forts of Warangal in April 1708 CE and Bhuvanagiri in June 1708 CE. During this period, he also greatly encouraged agriculture in the Khilashapur region, increasing his profits and replenishing his treasury.\n\nAfter 1709 CE, war broke out between the armies of Sarvai Papanna and Dilawar Khan. In this battle, Papanna lost not only his army but also his fort; under these circumstances, he left Khilashapur along with a few followers and reached Tarikonda. In **1710 CE**, Sarvai Papanna fought valiantly against the armies of Dilawar Khan and died on the battlefield.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter12-mughal-interregnum-p6", "partName": "Mughal Officers in the Deccan (1713–1724 CE)", "bodyMd": "After Aurangzeb''s death, his eldest son **Bahadur Shah** was made Emperor of Delhi, ruling from 1707 to 1713 CE. After him, the Delhi throne was occupied by **Jahandar Shah**, who ruled for less than a year, followed by **Farrukh Siyar**, who ruled from 1713 to 1719 CE. It was mainly during Farrukh Siyar''s rule that the Deccan Suba and Hyderabad were placed under two efficient Sardars: **Mubariz Khan** and **Mir Qamruddin Chin Qilich Khan**.\n\n### Mubariz Khan as Governor of Hyderabad\n\nFarrukh Siyar appointed Mubariz Khan (Kwaja Mahmad), who belonged to the Turani clan, as the governor of Hyderabad in June 1713. Before 1713, he had been doing minor jobs but, due to his hard work, rose to a high position under Aurangzeb''s rule; Kambaksh had appointed him as his ''Bakshi'' (paymaster, military inspector).\n\nAs governor of Hyderabad, Mubariz Khan used his experience and efficiency against invasions led by the region''s enemies, saving the people of Hyderabad and restoring peace and order to the province. He received several privileges and concessions from Emperor Farrukh Siyar that no previous governor of Hyderabad had received:\n\n*   He acquired absolute authority to rule the coastal districts, collect taxes, and so on, in addition to the governorship of Hyderabad.\n*   He gained full power to control Machilipatnam, Nizampatnam, Chicacole, Eluru, Rajahmundry, Kondapalli, Khammammet, the diamond mines, and other territories.\n*   Farrukh Siyar also wanted to curb the power of Mughal officers in coastal Andhra who were trying to become independent — the emperor issued an important Farman cancelling the powers of Abid Khan, who had until then served as Fauzdar and Amin of Chicacole, and transferred this responsibility to Mubariz Khan, who became the sole authority in Machilipatnam and Medak fort and other places.\n\nThese changes encouraged Abid Khan not only to violate the emperor''s orders but also to instigate a revolt against Mubariz Khan. Even before Mubariz Khan reached Hyderabad with his military, Abid Khan took his family members and huge wealth and sought shelter at Gandikota. Considering this an appropriate opportunity, Mubariz Khan sent his son-in-law Tala-Ullah-Khan to occupy all the provinces hitherto under Abid Khan''s control — however, Mubariz Khan''s attempts to completely suppress Abid Khan were not successful.\n\n### First Victory of Mubariz Khan over the Marathas (1724 CE)\n\nSoon after Mubariz Khan took up his responsibility as Governor of Hyderabad, the Marathas, under the leadership of Krishnamalhar, attacked Hyderabad. In a fierce battle on the outskirts of the city, Mubariz Khan''s armies defeated the Maratha forces — nearly 150 Maratha soldiers died and more than 100 were injured. The Maratha army chief Krishnamalhar escaped.\n\nDuring the same period, Mubariz Khan''s army also occupied Elgandal fort and defeated Venkata Naidu, and successfully occupied Chengapetta fort — which Mubariz Khan renamed **Farrukh Nagar**, in honour of the Mughal emperor. In a similar way, he defeated the brother of Venkata Nayaka and occupied Gopalpet in Panagal district. These victories further strengthened Mughal supremacy in the Deccan.\n\n**Subjugation of the zamindars:** Mubariz Khan also tried to make the Telugu Reddi, Velama, and Kamma zamindars, along with the chiefs of coastal Andhra, accept Mughal supremacy, collecting taxes and arrears of tribute from them.\n\n*   He called the zamindars of Nuzividu and Eluru — Meka Appa Rao and Koldindal Ranga Rao — to Vijayawada and made them repay all arrears of tribute and accept Mughal supremacy.\n*   In 1715 CE, Diwan Mohammad Jaffar, along with other zamindars, competed with the Hyderabad Governor to protect his position and privileges; these efforts were also suppressed by Mubariz Khan.\n*   Based on orders from Nizam-ul-Mulk (Deccan Subedar), he confiscated the ships of zamindars and prevented their mercantile activities.\n*   In September 1714, when Tularam of Kondavidu tried to rule independently, Mubariz Khan defeated him and established his authority over him.\n\n### Treaty between Mubariz Khan and Hussain Ali Khan (1715 CE)\n\nBetween 1714 and 1715, several changes took place in the internal politics of the Delhi darbar. After the death of Bahadur Shah, the brothers **Hasan Ali** and **Hussain Ali** — known as the Sayyed Brothers — emerged as kingmakers.\n\n*   Jahandar Shah was pressured by the Sayyed Brothers to replace Dawood Panni, the subedar of the Deccan, with the Turani leader **Nizam-ul-Mulk (Mir Qamruddin)**. Nizam-ul-Mulk was accordingly appointed subedar of six provinces in the Deccan in 1713.\n*   In May 1715, however, the Sayyed Brothers again forced the Mughal emperor to remove Nizam-ul-Mulk from the position of Subedar of the Deccan. In his place, **Hussain Ali Khan** — one of the Sayyed brothers who had played a key role in placing Farrukh Siyar on the Mughal throne — was appointed Subedar of the Deccan.\n\nWith this, the Deccan and Hyderabad became the main centres for political intrigue and conflict. For nearly one year, a war of supremacy ensued between Mubariz Khan and Hussain Ali Khan. At last, Mubariz Khan won over Hussain Ali with huge wealth and tried to place his son as ruler of Golconda fort.\n\n**Later campaigns and death:**\n\n*   In 1717–1718 CE, Mubariz Khan invaded coastal Andhra and established his authority there.\n*   Between 1718 and 1720 CE, he fought with the Marathas; in 1718 CE, he was defeated by the Maratha armies and finally made peace with them by agreeing to pay ''Chauth''.\n*   In 1724 CE, the Battle of Shakarkhed (80 kilometres from Aurangabad in Berar) was fought between the Mughal forces of Mubariz Khan and the armies of Nizam-ul-Mulk. In this battle, the Mughal forces were defeated and **Mubariz Khan lost his life**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter12-mughal-interregnum-p7", "partName": "Role of Nizam-ul-Mulk in the Deccan (1687–1724 CE)", "bodyMd": "One of the most significant events in Indian history was the establishment of the independent Hyderabad State in 1724 CE. This state was founded by **Mir Qamruddin Chin Qilich Khan**, popularly known in history textbooks as **Nizam-ul-Mulk Asaf Jah** or the first Nizam. His successors ruled the modern Telangana, Andhra, and Rayalaseema regions from 1724 CE to 1948 CE.\n\n### Family Background\n\nThe ancestors of Nizam-ul-Mulk belonged to Balapur village in the Samarkhand province of Central Asia; the family claimed descent from the first Caliph, Abu Bakr.\n\n*   **Khwaja Abid Bujaro**, the first-generation member of the family to migrate to India, arrived during the rule of Mughal emperor Shah Jahan. He was admitted into Shah Jahan''s court and won the emperor''s confidence through dedicated service.\n*   When Aurangzeb succeeded to the throne after the war of succession following Shah Jahan''s death, Khwaja Abid joined his service. In 1687 CE, he lost his life fighting against the Golconda rulers.\n*   One of Khwaja Abid''s five sons, **Shihabuddin**, returned from Samarkhand to join Aurangzeb''s service in 1669 CE. He participated in the wars between the Rajputs and Mughals on behalf of the Mughal army and gained Aurangzeb''s confidence — the emperor conferred on him the titles ''Ghazi-ud-din'' and ''Firoz Jung''.\n*   Shihabuddin''s wife was Saphia Khanam, daughter of Sadullah Khan (a minister in Shah Jahan''s court). **Qamruddin**, born in 1671, was their only son; he participated in several battles alongside his father.\n\n### Rise under Aurangzeb and Bahadur Shah\n\n*   In 1691 CE, Qamruddin commanded the Mughal army to occupy the fort of Adoni. Aurangzeb appreciated his courage and bravery and conferred on him the title ''Chin-Qilich-Khan''.\n*   Through his intelligence and bravery, he also became the leader of the ''Turanis'' in the Mughal Darbar.\n*   Between 1700 and 1707 CE, Aurangzeb''s Prime Minister Asad Khan and his son Zulfiqar Khan dominated the Mughal court; during this period, Qamruddin worked as Governor of Bijapur.\n*   In the war of succession following Aurangzeb''s death, Qamruddin acted as a mere spectator. Bahadur Shah I, who emerged victorious in the war of succession, appointed Zulfiqar Khan as Subedar of the Deccan in 1708 CE; Zulfiqar Khan continued in this position until he was killed by the Mughal emperor Farrukh Siyar in 1713 CE.\n*   In December 1707 CE, Bahadur Shah I appointed Qamruddin as Royal Representative of Awadh and as Fauzdar of Gorakhpur. After some time, Qamruddin stayed away from all types of positions due to the increasing influence of Zulfiqar Khan in the Mughal court.\n\n### Subedar of the Deccan and Conflict with the Sayyed Brothers\n\nIn May 1713 CE, Mughal emperor Farrukh Siyar appointed Mir Qamruddin Chin-Qilich-Khan as Subedar of the Deccan; similarly, the Mughal emperor appointed Kwaja Mahmad (Mubariz Khan) as Governor of Hyderabad. By the time Qamruddin and Mubariz Khan reached the Deccan, there was political unrest, economic disorder, rebellions by the zamindars, and a weak army. Both Subedars worked hard to set these precarious conditions right and succeeded in establishing the Mughal emperor''s supremacy over the Deccan permanently.\n\nAs Subedar of the Deccan, Qamruddin also strove to save the people from Maratha attacks and restore peace. He took advantage of the internal wars between Maratha Sahu (Satara) and Tarabai (Kolhapur), and stopped paying them Chauth and Sardeshmukhi. He arranged military camps in all the important cities of the Deccan Suba. Diwan Riyasath Khan supported Qamruddin in these endeavours; his aim was to rule independently, without interference from the central government.\n\n**Removal and rebellion (1715–1720 CE):**\n\n*   Between 1715 and 1720 CE, Qamruddin faced several problems. In Delhi, the Mughal emperor, influenced by the Sayyed brothers, discontinued Qamruddin as Subedar of the Deccan and transferred him to Muradabad.\n*   The emperor appointed Hussain Ali, one of the Sayyed brothers, as Deccan Subedar, who then entered into an agreement with Maratha Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath, granting the Marathas the right to collect Chauth and Sardeshmukhi. In return, Chhatrapati Sahu delivered 15,000 Maratha cavalry to the Sayyed brothers.\n*   Consequently, the Marathas and Hussain Ali established a competing government against the Mughal emperor — leaving Mubariz Khan, the Governor of Hyderabad, in an embarrassed and humiliated position, with no way to raise his voice. In 1718 CE, the Marathas invaded Golconda; Mubariz Khan paid Chauth and accepted the defeat.\n*   In Delhi, Farrukh Siyar grew weak because of the Sayyed brothers'' uncontrolled authority and carefully planned to end their supremacy in the Mughal court. Hasan Ali learnt of the plan and immediately recalled his brother Hussain Ali, then Subedar of the Deccan, to Delhi. Hussain Ali appointed his son Alam Ali Khan as his representative at Aurangabad and reached Delhi. Within a short period, the Sayyed brothers dethroned Emperor Farrukh Siyar and installed a weak ruler, Rafi-ud-din-Raza, as king, continuing their influence in the Mughal court.\n*   In 1719, **Mohammad Shah** was crowned Emperor of Delhi. Nizam-ul-Mulk, the Turani leader, had observed these politics carefully and refused to extend his help to the Sayyed brothers.\n*   In 1719 CE, the Sayyed brothers influenced the emperor to appoint Nizam-ul-Mulk as Subedar of Malwa. Sensing the Sayyed brothers'' thirst for power and their treacherous acts, Nizam-ul-Mulk decided to attack them while stationed at Malwa, working to bring together their enemies and adversaries. Learning of these moves, the Sayyed brothers forced Mohammad Shah to remove Nizam-ul-Mulk as Subedar of Malwa in **1720 CE**.\n*   This act encouraged Nizam-ul-Mulk to rebel directly against the Sayyed brothers and their supporters — he had decided that his future, and the future of his supporters, lay in his own hands. It was with this ambition that he took up arms: in **May 1720 CE**, he conquered the forts of Asirgarh and Burhanpur and openly questioned Mughal authority.\n*   The Sayyed brothers, with the help of their supporters, dared to challenge Nizam-ul-Mulk but failed. In **October 1720 CE**, Hussain Ali was murdered, and Sayyad Abdullah was defeated and killed in battle by Nizam-ul-Mulk''s followers. In this way, the Sayyed brothers and the Marathas were both defeated at the hands of Nizam-ul-Mulk''s army.\n\nWith this, Mughal emperor Muhammad Shah appointed Nizam-ul-Mulk chief of the six subas in the Deccan.\n\n### The Battle of Shakar Kheda and the Founding of Independent Hyderabad (1724 CE)\n\nIn 1722 CE, the Mughal emperor appointed Nizam-ul-Mulk as the Wazir. In this position, Nizam-ul-Mulk decided to strengthen the administration in the Deccan suba, but he faced opposition from selfish and corrupt officers from Rajasthan and from local zamindars. Even Muhammad Shah did not give him his full support.\n\nDisappointed, Nizam-ul-Mulk decided to move from Delhi to the Deccan. Muhammad Shah treated Nizam-ul-Mulk''s departure without taking his permission as an act of defiance, and ordered the then-governor of Hyderabad, **Mubariz Khan**, to either kill or capture Nizam-ul-Mulk and hand him over.\n\nNizam-ul-Mulk had already planned for confrontation and was ready. On **October 1, 1724**, in the historic **Battle of Shakher Kheda**, he defeated and killed Mubariz Khan, the Mughal representative, along with his army.\n\n**Consequences:**\n\n*   With this victory, Nizam-ul-Mulk emerged as the sole ruler of the Deccan and Hyderabad; the Mughal emperor did not even have the courage to oppose him.\n*   Consequently, the Mughal emperor recognized him as an independent ruler — Nizam-ul-Mulk had become the founder of the independent Hyderabad state.\n*   The victory at the Battle of Shakher Kheda earned Nizam-ul-Mulk the subedarship of the Deccan, the land with vast wealth, and Hyderabad. He received an annual profit of nearly 160 million rupees from the six subas.\n\nNizam-ul-Mulk ruled Hyderabad between 1724 CE and 1748 CE, recognized by the Mughal emperors as an independent ruler. The Mughal supremacy over the Deccan, which had begun in 1687 CE with the conquest of Golconda, thus came to an end in 1724 CE.\n\nIn the Deccan, the foundations of the **Asaf Jahi rule** were laid — a rule that continued till 1948 CE, bringing about several changes in the political, social, economic, and cultural spheres of the Hyderabad State and the Telangana region.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p1", "partName": "Asaf Jahi Dynasty — Establishment and Administration (1724–1857)", "bodyMd": "Between 1687 and 1724, after the decline of the Qutb Shahis, Hyderabad Suba was a part of Deccan under Mughal rule. It was only after 1724 that the Asaf Jahs came to power in the Hyderabad region and ruled until 1948 — a span of nearly 225 years. \n\nThe Hyderabad State comprised regions of Karnataka and Maratwada besides the Telangana core, and lay on the Deccan plateau between the Godavari and Krishna rivers. Under Asaf Jahi rule, Hyderabad developed all-round and built its own identity in culture, language, art, architecture, and literature, entering a distinctly modern phase. The founder of this dynasty was Nizam-ul-Mulk, and the Hyderabad State that he founded went on to become the largest princely state in India.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p2", "partName": "Ancestors of Nizam-ul-Mulk", "bodyMd": "### Alam Shaik and Khwaja Abid\n\nThe ancestry of Nizam-ul-Mulk goes back to **Alam Shaik**, a Sufi scholar (Pandit) belonging to the Bukhara kingdom of Samarkhand and a descendant of the first Khalifa, Abu Bakar. Alam Shaik was a well-learned man across Samarkhand and held the title **Alum-ul-Ulema**. His wife descended from the famous Mir Humdum clan of Samarkhand.\n\n**Khwaja Abid**, son of this couple, left Samarkhand and joined Aurangzeb''s service as a Manasabdar.\n\n*   **Rise under Aurangzeb**: In 1680 he helped suppress the revolt of Prince Akbar, Aurangzeb''s son, earning the title **''Chin Qilich Khan''** along with jewellery and horses.\n*   **Service record**: He assisted in strengthening administration and peace during Aurangzeb''s early reign, helped in the conquest of Bijapur (1686), was rewarded as Subedar of Bidar (Jafarabad), and later held charge as Subedar of Ajmer and Multan.\n*   **Death**: While marching from Multan to assist in the Golconda campaign, Khwaja Abid lost his right arm in battle and retired to barracks. He died on **30 January 1687** and was buried at Attapur, near Himayatsagar. His severed arm, found on the battlefield three days later and identified by a signet ring, was buried separately at Kismatpura near Golconda; Asaf Jah VII repaired this tomb in 1942.\n\n### Mir Shihabuddin Siddiqui (Firoz Jung)\n\nKhwaja Abid had five sons; the eldest was **Mir Shihabuddin Siddiqui**. Born at Samarkhand in 1649, he reached Delhi in 1668 to meet his father and was appointed mansabdar of 300/70 by Aurangzeb.\n\n**Titles and campaigns**\n\n*   Earned the title **''Khan''** for his role in the Mughal campaign against the Rana of Udaipur.\n*   Earned **''Firoz Jung''** for success against an attack by Shambaji.\n*   Earned **''Farzund Arjumand''** for valour during the Bijapur campaign.\n*   Took part as army chief in the 1687 conquest of Golconda and subjugated the Udgir and Adoni forts.\n*   In 1705, defeated the Sindhia of Malwa and was made Subedar of Elichpur, Berar and Gujarat, receiving the title **''Sipah Salar''**.\n\nHe married Safiya Khanam, daughter of Saadulla Khan Bahadur (Wazir of Shah Jahan). Late in his career he was blinded by smallpox and died in Gujarat in 1711. Their eldest son was **Mir Qamruddin**, who later took the title Nizam-ul-Mulk and established an independent state in Deccan.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p3", "partName": "Nizam-ul-Mulk — Early Career at the Mughal Court", "bodyMd": "Mir Qamruddin (Nizam-ul-Mulk) was born on **11 August 1671** at Agra, son of Ghaziuddin Firoz Jung and Safiya Khanam. As an infant he was named \"Nek Bhakt\" (fortunate). At age six he visited the Mughal court and drew Aurangzeb''s attention; the emperor at once appointed him Mansabdar of 450 horses, doubled a year later.\n\n**Rise through military service**\n\n| Year | Event | Reward/Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1688 | Captured Adoni fort (age 17) with his father | Mansabdar of 2000/500, Arabian horse, jewellery |\n| — | Courage in early conquests | Title **''Khan''** |\n| 1698 | Occupied Nagori near Bijapur; then captured Nagarkot | Ornated dagger; Mansabdar raised to 3000/500, then 3500/3000, then 4000/3000 |\n| 1700 | Cut off supply lines to Parali fort (after fall of Satara) | Helped Mughal occupation of the fort |\n| 1705 | Attacked Wajinjar fort in Berar with Aurangzeb, assisted by Tarbiyat Khan | Two Arab horses studded with gold; Mansabdari raised to 5000/5000, plus 1 crore 50 lakh dhams, an elephant, and jewellery |\n\nBy this stage Aurangzeb had begun taking Chin Qilich Khan''s advice in administrative matters.\n\n**Bijapur retreat and Aurangzeb''s death**: With Aurangzeb''s permission, Chin Qilich Khan went to Bijapur to arrange a retreat for the ageing emperor at Devapur. Aurangzeb fell sick there and was cared for by Chin Qilich Khan, who was honoured with a ring bearing the title **''Chin Qilich Khan Bahadur''**. In 1706 he was made Fauzdar of Feroznagar Fort. Aurangzeb died on 28 March 1707.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p4", "partName": "Nizam-ul-Mulk Through the Mughal Wars of Succession", "bodyMd": "A prolonged struggle for the Mughal throne followed Aurangzeb''s death, and Chin Qilich Khan navigated it carefully without taking sides until his position was secure.\n\n1.  **Azam Shah''s brief reign**: Azam Shah declared himself emperor amid the succession dispute. Chin Qilich Khan and his son stayed away from the contest. To win him over, Azam raised his Mansabdari to 7000/7000 and gave the title **''Khan-e-Duran''**. Azam Shah died in the war of succession.\n2.  **Bahadur Shah I (1707–1712)**: His brother Muazzam succeeded as Bahadur Shah I, reconfirmed Chin Qilich Khan''s earlier titles, and posted him as Subedar of Oudh Suba and Faujdar of Lucknow (1707), then in charge of Awadh administration from 9 December 1707. During this period Chin Qilich Khan showed little interest in state affairs, distributed his wealth to the poor, and withdrew from politics for a time.\n3.  **Succession crisis of 1712**: On Bahadur Shah I''s death at Lahore, his four sons contested the throne.\n    *   Chin Qilich Khan marched toward Lahore with 3000 Turani soldiers to support Prince Azam us Shan, but learned en route that Azam us Shan, Jahan Shah, and Rafi us Shan had all been killed, and Jahandar Shah had seized the throne. Disillusioned, he retired for five years into the company of musicians, poets, and intellectuals.\n    *   He declined Jahandar Shah''s request to re-enter administration despite receiving fresh titles — ''Ghaziuddin Khan'', Bahadur Feroz Jung, and Fateh Jung — and a posting to safeguard Agra.\n4.  **Farrukhsiyar''s accession and the title ''Nizam-ul-Mulk''**: An understanding was reached that Chin Qilich Khan would stay neutral between Jahandar Shah and Farrukh Siyar.\n    \n    *   Though it was believed his neutrality in practice favoured Farrukh Siyar, and when Farrukh Siyar became emperor, he rewarded Chin Qilich Khan with the title ''Nizam-ul-Mulk'', appointing him Subedar of six Subas of Deccan and Faujdar of Karnataka, along with an ornate Arabian horse, royal turban, jewelled sword, and jewellery.\n    *   By age 43, his name and fame in the Mughal empire had reached its peak — a standing he would hold for another 35 years until his death.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p5", "partName": "First Tenure as Subedar of Deccan", "bodyMd": "Nizam-ul-Mulk reached Aurangabad by June 1713 to take up the Deccan subedari amid volatile and economically poor conditions.\n\n**Checking Maratha revenue claims**: Since 1707 the Marathas had been collecting chauth and sardeshmukhi in Aurangabad Suba from the then Mughal prince Azam Shah, enforcing their domination. Disliking this, Nizam-ul-Mulk discontinued tax collection around Aurangabad first, instructing his Faujdars and Zilladars to drive away Raja Sahu''s tax-collecting Tahsildars.\n\n**Revenue reforms**: He introduced a uniform revenue policy in Aurangabad Suba, deputed Shaik Muhammad Azad and Diwan Khembiran (Diwan-i-Sarkar) to correct the corruption of Dawood Khan (a Maratha agent) at Sagar and Amardh, focused on efficient revenue administration across the Deccan subas without overburdening peasants, punished corrupt officials, and kept a close watch on prices — measures that raised both income and productivity.\n\n### Nizam-ul-Mulk''s Relations with the Marathas (Early Phase)\n\nNizam-ul-Mulk was as much a political strategist as a soldier, and he exploited the rivalry between the Maratha rulers of Kolhapur and Satara.\n\n*   Raja Sahu of Kolhapur sent his envoy Haibat Rao, and Chandrasen Jadav of Satara sent his governor Appa Rao, to open dialogue with Nizam-ul-Mulk.\n*   After a meeting at Aurangabad involving Rambajirao Nimalkar and Sarjarao Ghatne, both Sarjarao Ghatne and Chandrasen Jadhav defected from the Marathas to Nizam-ul-Mulk''s camp.\n*   In return, Chandrasen Jadhav received a Mansabdari of 7000/7000 and jagirs at Balki, Brahmanabad, Illandur, and Chandarghad (worth 25 lakh revenue); a fort was built at Chandargadh against the Marathas. Sarjarao received a jagir at Kagal.\n*   After consolidating his position, Nizam-ul-Mulk massed his army on the Godavari to display his strength. Raja Sahu and his Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath made counter-preparations, and the two sides fought at Purandhar, where Nizam-ul-Mulk emerged victorious. A treaty followed between Nizam and Balaji Vishwanath, under which Poona and the forts lost by Sahu were handed to Chandrasen Jadav in return for his service.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p6", "partName": "Retaining the Subedari of Deccan & Nizam-ul-Mulk as Wazir", "bodyMd": "### Retaining the Subedari of Deccan\n\nBoth Nizam-ul-Mulk and the Sayyed Brothers sought to dethrone the ruling Mughals from Delhi. Simultaneously, Nizam-ul-Mulk sought to retain the Deccan subedari for himself.\n\n*   On **9 May 1719**, vexed with the Sayyed brothers'' court politics, Nizam-ul-Mulk moved toward Deccan, accompanied by the Faujdar of Bijapur Rustum Khan Beg; at Burhanpur, Rambaji Nimbalkar and Prince Sahu (son of Maratha ruler Rajaram) joined him. Here he exposed the Sayyed brothers'' plot and led a movement against their domination of the Mughal court.\n*   Fearing him, the Sayyed brothers sent two military expeditions against Nizam-ul-Mulk in **1720** — one under Dilawar Khan, the other under Alam Ali Khan. Nizam-ul-Mulk defeated both, and the emperor, left without option, issued a farman confirming him as Subedar of Deccan.\n\n### Nizam-ul-Mulk as Wazir\n\nMuhammad Amir Khan (prime minister) died in February 1721 and Inayatulla Khan succeeded him. Nizam-ul-Mulk was invited to the Mughal Durbar at Delhi; he appointed Ijjat ud Daula as his representative in Deccan and set out in January 1722, escorted with honour by Shamsud Daula. He was appointed **Wazir (prime minister)** of the Mughal Empire and honoured with a diamond ring, a jewel-studded dagger, and royal cloaks.\n\n**Difficulties as Wazir**\n\n*   Court intrigue and treachery against him increased, and even the emperor began to believe the complaints made against him.\n*   He tried to end the Sayyed brothers'' court politics and to abolish the Peshkash and Ijara systems, which he viewed as degrading the emperor''s prestige by functioning as bribery rather than taxation.\n*   He pushed to revive court traditions from the times of Shah Jahan and Aurangzeb, including reintroduction of the **Jijya tax** on non-believers, and supported the Persians against the Afghans to check Afghan influence in Mughal politics.\n*   Court officials feared that success for these policies would make Nizam-ul-Mulk''s name and fame grow unchecked; the emperor, though unable to reject him outright, avoided implementing his suggestions.\n\nDejected, Nizam-ul-Mulk resigned the Wazir post and left Delhi for Muradabad. By the time he reached Agra, the Marathas had attacked Malwa and Gujarat (then under his elder son Ghaziuddin''s control), so he sought and obtained permission to mobilize against them. \n\nBy the time he reached the Narmada, the Marathas had already retreated; he then rested at Sirha near Sironj, where he learned of a plot by Mubariz Khan in the Deccan, and by 1724 marched to Aurangabad. Mediation efforts by Khalifa Diwan and Raja Gujarimal Saksena to clear Nizam-ul-Mulk''s name with Emperor Muhammad Shah stopped after Raja Gujarimal''s death in December 1723.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p7", "partName": "The Battle of Shakarkheda and Founding of Asaf Jahi Rule (1724)", "bodyMd": "With no option but to move to Deccan as its Subedar, Nizam-ul-Mulk again sought to reclaim the charge he had assigned to Ijjat ud Daula as his Naib.\n\n*   In February 1724, his enemies at court arranged for **Abdul Mabat Khan**, son of Mubariz Khan, to be appointed Deccan Subedar on behalf of the minor Prince Shahriyar. After the young prince''s death, Mubariz Khan declared himself Deccan Subedar, appropriated five lakhs from the treasury, and raised an army from revenue funds.\n*   From his base at Siroha, Nizam-ul-Mulk tracked these developments and, on being informed of Mubariz Khan''s movement from Hyderabad, marched for Aurangabad via Malwa to neutralize him.\n*   Emperor Muhammad Shah ultimately declared Mubariz Khan Subedar of Deccan and transferred Nizam-ul-Mulk''s wazirship and subedari charges elsewhere, though he was given the option of staying on in the Deccan Suba.\n*   Defying the emperor, Mubariz Khan marched on Aurangabad. Nizam-ul-Mulk wrote repeatedly asking him not to shed innocent blood, but all efforts failed, and the two armies met at **Shakarkheda in Berar** for a final showdown on **23 October 1724**.\n*   Nizam-ul-Mulk won the battle; Mubariz Khan died in it. Soldiers and common people welcomed Nizam-ul-Mulk, who thereby became Subedar of the six Deccan subas — **Ahmadnagar, Bidar, Berar, Bijapur, Khandesh, and Hyderabad**. The Mughal emperor accepted this and conferred on him the title **''Asaf Jah''**.\n\nThis victory marked the start of Nizam rule in Deccan (1724 AD), and the dynasty he founded — the Asaf Jahi dynasty — lasted nearly 225 years, ending only with Mir Osman Ali Khan''s rule in 1948.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p8", "partName": "Nizam-ul-Mulk''s Position and Administration as Subedar of Deccan", "bodyMd": "**Political standing**: Politically his position was formally no more than a Sipah Salar or Subedar, though titles such as Nizam-ul-Mulk Fatehjung, Sipah Salar, and Asaf Jah appeared in Mughal farmans. He was appointed Wazir/Vakil-e-Mutluk of the Mughal Empire — once appointed, a Wazir was traditionally never removed. Vakil-e-Mutluk ranked higher in designation than Wazir. Though rarely present at the capital, he was exempted from attendance owing to his experience, recognition, and loyalty.\n\n**Loyalty to the Mughal throne**: Nizam-ul-Mulk never intended to sever Hyderabad from the Mughal Empire. He had the Qutba read in the name of Emperor Muhammad Shah at Friday prayers, minted coins in the emperor''s name in Deccan, and all letters and decrees he issued bore the emperor''s signature. His subedari was raised to **9000/9000 (duaspa)** on becoming Subedar of Deccan. He regarded himself, throughout, as a servant of the Mughal Empire.\n\n**Administrative charge**: As Subedar of Deccan he took on civil, legal, legislative, and military responsibilities, ran an independent administration with the help of Diwans, organized his own army, raised his own flag, and appointed his own officers — while still maintaining cordial relations with the Mughal Empire.\n\n### Revenue System — Serf-i-Khas and Diwani\n\nHe used wealth generated in the Suba — from mines, mineral wealth, diamond mines, and war booty — and divided his Deccan Suba into three parts:\n\n*   **1/3rd** kept directly under his own control, known as **Serf-i-Khas**, to meet state expenditure.\n*   **2/3rds**, known as **Diwani**, distributed among officers and persons he deemed loyal.\n*   Jagirdars, Zamindars, and Deshmukhs, in return for recognition of their positions, gave him gifts called **Nazaranas**.\n\n### Territorial Expansion\n\nUnder Nizam-ul-Mulk''s state, expansion took place between the **Narmada in the north and the Kaveri river in the south**, and between **Bijapur in the west and Machilipatnam in the east**. Coming from Malwa, he brought along many Muslim and Hindu followers who had backed his success in Deccan; he appointed Muslims to military posts and Hindus to administrative posts, rewarding both with jagirs.\n\n### Conflict with the Marathas (1725–1728)\n\nNizam-ul-Mulk took steps to abolish the collection of Chauth and Sardeshmukhi by the Marathas in his state. This led to war with the Marathas beginning **August 1727**, continuing till **March 1728**. Nizam-ul-Mulk was defeated at **Palkhed**, near Daultabad, and was forced to sign a treaty: Chatrapati Sahu was recognized as ruler of the Marathas, and Nizam-ul-Mulk agreed to permit the Marathas to collect Chauth and Sardeshmukhi in the Deccan.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p9", "partName": "Invasion of Nadir Shah and the Mediation of Nizam-ul-Mulk (1738)", "bodyMd": "Nadir Shah invaded India in 1738 after crossing the Hindukush. Nizam-ul-Mulk sent his army to Karnal to join the Mughal contingent, but it failed to check the invader. Nadir Shah looted Delhi, and Emperor Muhammad Shah, unable to stop the devastation, asked Nizam-ul-Mulk to negotiate with him.\n\n*   As Mughal ambassador, Nizam-ul-Mulk not only extended talks but influenced Nadir Shah through direct appeal, warning him that killing could not continue once none were left alive to kill. Nadir Shah stopped the killings and offered Nizam-ul-Mulk the Mughal throne, which he refused.\n*   Emperor Muhammad Shah, satisfied with how he had handled the crisis, awarded him further titles: ''Amir ul Umra Bakshi ul Mumlik'', Jamlat ul Mulk, Madrul Maham, Nizam-ul-Mulk, Bahadur Fathejung, Mominus Sultanat, Shuruknus Sultanat, Asaf Jah.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p10", "partName": "Character, Achievements, and Death of Nizam-ul-Mulk", "bodyMd": "**Career profile**: Nizam-ul-Mulk served under eight Mughal emperors between Aurangzeb and Muhammad Shah, actively serving four of them. He chose Deccan, not Delhi, as his centre of power, having governed there three separate times — **1713–1715, 1720–1722, and 1724 till his death** — administering Deccan continuously for thirty years in his final tenure. He took part in 87 battles over his lifetime.\n\n**Character**: Though he occupied the highest positions in Mughal administration, he lived simply and was regarded by others, and occasionally even by emperors, as a protector figure. He was a man of character and values who avoided treachery, treason, and deceit — earning him widespread respect. He is regarded as the most extraordinary ruler of eighteenth-century India; his efficiency was such that even Nadir Shah offered him the Mughal throne.\n\n**Cultural side**: He was proficient in Persian and Turkish, composed Persian poetry under the pen name **''Shakir''**, and shared his experiences during his leisure time. His personal assistant, Lala Mansaram, compiled ninety such stories reflecting his mental maturity, intelligence, analytical knowledge, and love of nature.\n\n**Death and succession**: Nizam-ul-Mulk died in **1748** at the age of 76 and was buried at Shaik Burhanuddin Garib Chisti Mazar, Khulabad, near Aurangabad — the same site as Aurangzeb''s tomb.\n\n*   He was survived by five sons and a daughter: Mir Ghaziuddin Siddique Bahadur Firoj Jung, Mir Ahmad Ali Khan Siddique Bahadur Nasar Jung, Sayyad Mahamud Khan Siddique Bahadur Salabat Jung, Mir Nizam Ali Khan Siddique Bahadur Fatehjung, and Mir Mahamud Sharif Khan Siddique Bahadur Basalat Jung. \n*   At his death, his elder son Ghaziuddin was serving at the Mughal court. A war of succession broke out among his sons, which the English and French East India Companies successfully exploited to dominate the Deccan and its trade and commerce.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p11", "partName": "The Carnatic Civil War", "bodyMd": "The Hyderabad State was the largest state in India. After Nizam-ul-Mulk''s death, civil war broke out over succession, and this coincided with growing European intervention in state politics — a period this section covers between **1748 and 1857**, tracing the civil war, the state''s position, and its relations with Europeans.\n\nUnder the rule of the first Nizam, Carnatic was under the control of Deccan Subedar Nizam-ul-Mulk. Sadatullah Khan, the childless Nawab of Carnatic, had adopted his two sons-in-law, Dost Ali and Bakar Ali. Sadatullah got Mughal permission to appoint **Dost Ali** as his political successor without informing the Nizam. Once enthroned, Dost Ali refused to pay taxes to the Subedar.\n\n**Maratha intervention**: Angered, Nizam-ul-Mulk decided to act, but before he could, Maratha rulers under Ragoji Bhonsle and Fatheh Singh invaded Carnatic in 1740 for chauth collection, attacked Arcot, and defeated Chanda Sahib (ruler of Tiruchinapally and Dost Ali''s son-in-law), taking him prisoner to Satara. \n\n*   Dost Ali died at Damerlacheruvu defending Arcot. The Marathas then appointed **Safdar Ali** as Nawab of Carnatic and **Murari Rao Gherpade** as ruler of Tiruchinapally, with Safdar Ali required to pay 40 lakh rupees to the Marathas as chauth compensation; Nizam-ul-Mulk separately ordered him to clear all dues and taxes.\n*   Safdar Ali raised taxes across Carnatic to meet these demands, causing widespread public discontent.\n\n**Succession chaos**: Safdar Ali''s brother-in-law, Murtiza Ali Khan of Nellore, was unhappy over the tax rises and killed Safdar Ali with the help of Safdar Ali''s own wife and sister. Murtiza Ali''s attempt to declare himself ruler angered Carnatic''s Mokasadars and Amirs. **Syed Muhammad Khan**, Safdar Ali''s son, became ruler of Carnatic in 1742.\n\n**Nizam-ul-Mulk''s settlement**: Nizam-ul-Mulk came to Carnatic and restored peace, appointing Sayeed Muhammad Khan as Nawab of Arcot and Anwaruddin Khan as his protector, while sending the Marathas back from Tiruchirapally. Conditions improved by 1744, and Nizam-ul-Mulk returned to Deccan. \n\n*   War then broke out on the east coast between the French and English; when Anwaruddin (Nawab of Carnatic) sent his son Mahfuz Khan to help the British, the French defeated him. Nizam-ul-Mulk sent his son **Nasir Jung** to Carnatic in 1747 to restore peace, appointing him Nawab of Arcot, Kadapa, and Kondanur, with powers to collect dues from Mysore. \n*   In 1748 Nasir Jung came to Aurangabad on news of his father''s illness. Nizam-ul-Mulk, founder of the Asaf Jahi dynasty, died in **June 1748**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p12", "partName": "First Carnatic War (1746–1748)", "bodyMd": "The three Carnatic Wars reshaped politics across Southern India.\n\n**Causes**\n\n*   Commercial rivalry between the French and English East India Companies, both seeking a monopoly over trade in Southern India.\n*   French Governor **Dupleix**''s aim to drive the English out of India, which made war increasingly likely.\n*   The **Austrian Succession War** (1740–1748) in Europe, in which England and France backed opposite sides — England supporting Austria (Queen Maria Theresa, 1740–1780) and France supporting Prussia (Frederick II, 1740–1786) — extended its consequences into Southern India.\n\n**Course of the war**: With no favourable conditions for the French in Carnatic, Dupleix asked Nawab Anwaruddin to prevent a British attack on the French company. Anwaruddin secured English agreement to maintain peace, and when the English commander Bornet captured French ships on a complaint from Dupleix, Anwaruddin ordered their return and had Bornet sent back to England.\n\nIn July 1746, the French Mauritius Governor **Labordinies** arrived at Coromandel with ships and army; Dupleix and Labordinies jointly captured **Madras** in September 1746. The British sought Anwaruddin''s help; Labordinies initially agreed to return Madras for 1,00,000 Pagodas, but Dupleix refused and recaptured it. \n\nWhen Anwaruddin ordered Dupleix to hand Madras to the British, Dupleix refused, and Anwaruddin sent his army against the French — the resulting **Battle of Adayar** (Santhome, 1746) ended in defeat for the Nawab''s forces, leaving Madras under full French control.\n\n**Aftermath**: The Austrian Succession War concluded with the **Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748)**; Prussia won and occupied Silesia, and per the treaty both companies in India returned to their original centres — France returning Madras to the British, a considerable humiliation for the British given Dupleix''s rising fame. \n\nBoth sides released captured soldiers. The war exposed Anwaruddin''s weakness, the Nawab''s outdated weapons, and the lack of a regular navy — factors that reduced the standing of both the Nizam and the Mughal power in the region.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p13", "partName": "Second Carnatic War (1749–1754)", "bodyMd": "This war began as a succession struggle between local rulers.\n\n*   **Anwaruddin vs Chanda Sahib**: Chanda Sahib, grandson of Dost Ali and holder of Mughal-recognized succession rights, had been captured and jailed at Satara during Maratha attacks. After Nizam-ul-Mulk''s death, the Marathas released him; on reaching Carnatic he declared himself Nawab. Anwaruddin tried to control him.\n*   **Deccan succession dispute**: With Nizam-ul-Mulk''s death in 1748, a parallel dispute erupted over the Deccan subedari — his grandson **Muzafar Jung** declared himself the rightful successor. The French, drawn into both disputes, recognized Chanda Sahib as Nawab of Carnatic and Muzafar Jung as Subedar of Deccan, backing both.\n*   **Battle of Ambur (1749)**: Chanda Sahib, Muzafar Jung, and Dupleix''s combined forces fought Anwaruddin at Ambur (near Vellore) in August 1749. Anwaruddin was defeated and died; his son **Muhammad Ali** escaped to Tiruchirapally. Chanda Sahib, with French help, took the Carnatic throne, and rewarded the French with the region between Coromandel and Pondicherry plus valuable gifts.\n*   **British counter-move**: The British, siding with Nasir Jung and Muhammad Ali, saw Nasir Jung reach Carnatic in 1750 and send ambassadors (Meropanth and Qaze Dayem) demanding the surrender of Muzafar Jung, Chanda Sahib, and Dupleix — diplomacy failed. On 18 March 1750, Mughal forces occupied Gingi; Chanda Sahib, beset by Maratha and Muhammad Ali pressure, fled to Pondicherry, and Muzafar Jung surrendered, protected without cruelty by the Nizam''s Peshkar Ramdas Pandit.\n*   **Death of Nasir Jung and rise of Muzafar Jung**: Pathan Nawabs in Nasir Jung''s own camp — Abdul Nabi Khan of Kadapa, Himmat Khan Bahadur of Kurnool, Abdul Karim Khan of Savanoor, and the Nawab of Bankapur — turned on him; Nasir Jung was killed in firing by the Kurnool Nawab, and the Kadapa Nawab beheaded him, carrying the news to Muzafar Jung. \n\n*   On **31 December 1750**, Muzafar Jung declared himself Sultan in a grand Darbar, distributed 50,000 pounds to French officials, gave Dupleix the title **''Jafar Jung''**, and appointed Nawabs for Carnatic, Mysore, and the region south of the Krishna, handing Masulipatnam, Yanam, and Karaikal to the French.\n\n*   **Death of Muzafar Jung and rise of Salabat Jung**: The Pathan Nawabs conspired against Muzafar Jung; the two sides fought near Rayachoti (25 miles south of Kadapa) in January 1751. Abdul Karim Khan (Nawab of Savanoor) died and the Kurnool Nawab was gravely wounded, but Muzafar Jung was killed. \n\n*   The French officer **Bussy** immediately declared **Salabat Jung**, another son of Nizam-ul-Mulk, as Nawab of Hyderabad (1751). Salabat Jung gave Kondapalli, Guntur, Elluru, Rajahmundry, and Chikakole (Srikakulam) to the French, and Dupleix was made Governor of the coastal regions south of the Krishna — sharply raising French influence.\n\n*   **British counter-offensive**: Taking Sandars'' advice, the British concluded a treaty with Muhammad Ali of Tiruchirapally and, with Robert Clive and Lawrence, attacked Chanda Sahib, who was killed. Muhammad Ali was made Nawab of Arcot. \n\n*   The British defeated the French at Arcot, Aarani, and Kaveripak, establishing British power in Carnatic. Dupleix lost the confidence of the French government and returned to France; in 1759 the French officer **Gadehue** concluded the **Treaty of Pondicherry** with the British — neither side would interfere in local politics, both would recognize existing Mughal designations, and both returned to their prior centres.\n*   Bussy remained in Deccan pursuing French interests there, while the British announced support for Salabat Jung — closing the Second Carnatic War.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p14", "partName": "Battle of Bobbili (1757)", "bodyMd": "Zamindars across the region revolted against French power in Deccan — notably Shah Nawab Khan of Rajahmundry and Fouzdar Ibrahim Khan of Chicacole (Srikakulam), while **Vijaya Rama Raju** alone sided with the French. Bussy came from Hyderabad to Rajahmundry on 19 December 1756 (via Bezawada and Elluru) to suppress the Circar revolts. Ibrahim Khan escaped to the ruler of Bobbili. Other Zamindars surrendered, but the **ruler of Bobbili, Raja Ranga Rao**, remained independent and refused to submit.\n\nVijaya Rama Raju instigated the French to attack Bobbili and to strip its Zamindari rights. Ranga Rao learned of this but refused to leave. When 30 French soldiers passing through Bobbili territory were killed, Bussy declared war on **24 January 1757**.\n\n*   Ranga Rao sent Ibrahim Khan away safely, then defended his fort with Vijaya Rama Raju, Hyder Jung, and the Zamindar of Peddapuram fighting on the French side.\n*   Realising defeat was inevitable, Ranga Rao ordered the women in his fort to be killed and set all houses on fire; his forces fought to the end, and he himself died of a bullet wound. The French won and took revenue and other spoils; when French soldiers entered the fort, they were shocked by what they found. Ranga Rao''s son was saved by the servant **Dasi Lakshmi**, and Bussy welcomed and protected the boy.\n*   **Tandra Papaiah**, Bobbili''s representative, learned of the war, gathered his friends Devulapalli Peddanna and Buddha Raju Venkaiah, and after three days killed **Vijaya Rama Raju**.\n\nBussy received 2,00,000 from Bobbili''s Zamindari revenue. Except for the Gumperu tenant (Palegar), all tenants in Bobbili began paying taxes to Bussy; after this war, all Zamindars paid land revenue regularly to the French, and French influence increased across the coastal region.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 15, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p15", "partName": "Third Carnatic War (1756–1763)", "bodyMd": "The dispute arose again between the French and English in Europe, both sides being on opposite ends of the **Seven Years'' War** (started 17 May 1756) between Austria (Queen Maria Theresa) and Prussia (Frederick II) over Silesia — with consequences again playing out in Carnatic (the war there began in 1758). France sent **Count-de-Lali**, who lacked knowledge of Indian conditions — a fact that favoured the British; in 1758 Lali reached Pondicherry and attacked Tanjavur but failed.\n\n**British consolidation**: Robert Clive''s victory in the **Battle of Plassey (1757)** established British power in Bengal. After Plassey, British forces joined Carnatic and prepared to attack Madras and Tanjavur. Lali ordered French officer Bussy to leave Hyderabad for Carnatic; the Nizam''s Nawab thereupon declared support for the British, since he disliked Bussy''s domination at his court. Robert Clive attacked the French-held Northern Circars.\n\n*   The **Chandurti War** (7 December 1758) saw British forces, aided by **Ananda Gajapati** (son-in-law of Vijaya Rama Raju, holding Vijayanagaram Zamindari succession rights and maintaining good relations with the British of Madras), capture Visakhapatnam, Chebrole village, and finally win at Chandurti under Colonel Ford, chasing the French away. British forces under Captain Nocks and Maclein captured Rajahmundry.\n*   The British occupied Northern Circars and concluded a treaty with **Salabat Jung on 14 May 1759**, under which Salabat Jung gave up eight districts in the Circars — Masulipatnam, Nizampatnam, Kondaveedu, and Akulamanadu — to the British. This was the **first political treaty between the British and the Nizam**, and marked a turning point for British dominance in Hyderabad State.\n*   Count-de-Lali and Bussy jointly tried but failed to attack Madras. British officer **Sir Eyre Coote** defeated the French and captured Bussy as prisoner in the **Wandwasi War (1760)**; Lali surrendered to the British on 16 January 1761.\n*   In 1763 the Seven Years'' War concluded with the **Treaty of Paris**: Prussia''s King Frederick prevailed and the British too gained, since Austria lost Silesia entirely. \n\n*   The French were defeated in the Third Carnatic War and restricted to Pondicherry; the remaining French centres were destroyed. With the French defeated, there was no opponent left to the British in India, and the Carnatic Nawab became a British puppet after this war — though the British still maintained good relations with the rulers of Hyderabad State for a time.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 16, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p16", "partName": "Nizam–British Relations Under Nizam Ali Khan", "bodyMd": "During Salabat Jung''s rule (1751–1762) as Subedar of Deccan, French dominance grew in Hyderabad following control of the Northern Circars, which the Nizam disliked. Princes **Nizam Ali Khan** and **Basalat Jung** criticized the French and were consequently captured and sent to Daulatabad fort by Salabat Jung. The Marathas then attacked and captured Ahmadnagar fort.\n\n**Rise of Nizam Ali Khan**\n\n*   Salabat Jung, watching Maratha actions, reconciled with his brother Nizam Ali Khan, released him from Daulatabad, and appointed him Subedar of Berar (1757).\n*   Nizam Ali fought the Marathas twice — first in December 1757, concluding a treaty with the Peshwa at Sindkhed and giving up Darur fort and Rs. 25,00,000 as levy; and again in 1759 at **Udgir**, where he was defeated and had to cede Ahmadnagar, Daulatabad, Burhanpur, and Bijapur to the Marathas.\n*   After the Marathas were themselves defeated by Ahmad Shah Abdali in the **Third Battle of Panipat (1761)**, Peshwa Baji Rao (who never recovered from the Udgir defeat) died in June 1761. Nizam Ali seized the moment, attacked Pune, and destroyed the city. In January 1762, Peshwa Madhava Rao concluded a treaty with Nizam Ali, ceding regions worth Rs. 27,00,000 revenue; Nizam Ali also captured Naldurg during the Pune attacks and destroyed the Sindhia treasury building at Srigonda.\n*   Salabat Jung welcomed Nizam Ali back at Bidar after the Pune war and agreed that Salabat Jung would remain Subedar of Deccan while Nizam Ali conducted state administration. On **6 July 1762**, Nizam Ali captured and killed his brother Salabat Jung, declaring himself Nizam of Hyderabad — the first ruler to adopt the title **''Nizam''**, which became the dynasty''s hereditary style from then on. In 1762 the British identified Nizam Ali as a useful friend.\n\n**Northern Circars question**: Conditions in the Northern Circars were unfavourable after Ananda Gajapati''s death (1760). Salabat Jung had issued a Sanad (Charter of rights) to Colonel Ford and officer James Alexander of Masulipatnam, but they failed to secure Nizam Ali''s approval for it. \n\nNizam Ali instead appointed Hussain Ali Khan as Fauzdar of Elluru, Rajahmundry, and Mustafanagar, and assigned Chicacole revenue collection to the king of Vijayanagaram — all of which created British tension, since the British had not helped Nizam Ali against the Marathas, and he in turn acted strictly against them.\n\n*   In 1764, the British succeeded in the **Battle of Buxar** against Bengal''s Nawab, the Mughal Emperor Shah Alam, and the Nawab of Ayodhya; a treaty at Allahabad (1765) gave Northern Circars to the British company via a Mughal firman. Nizam Ali did not recognize this firman, creating problems for the British.\n*   The British sent **Kandregula Jogi Panthulu**, a well-connected Dubasi (agent for tax collection), to negotiate; he had good relations with Diwan Ruknudoula in Hyderabad. Amid revolts in Rajahmundry (which Nizam Ali went to suppress with Ruknuddoula) and among Nizam forces at Gollapudi (over unpaid salaries), Jogi Panthulu used the moment to secure Nizam Ali''s agreement to give five Northern Circar districts to the British on contract for Rs. 9,00,000.\n*   On **12 November 1766**, the British (represented by General Kailand and General Smith) concluded a treaty: Rajahmundry, Elluru, Mustafanagar, Murtizanagar, and Chicacole Circars were leased to the company for Rs. 9,00,000 per year — four lakhs for Rajahmundry, Elluru, and Mustafanagar combined, two lakhs each for Murtizanagar and Chicacole. \n\n*   Basalat Jung, Nizam Ali''s brother, had received Murtizanagar as jagir in 1761; the company was to take over that Jagir and continue lease payments only after Basalat Jung''s death or loss of the Nizam''s faith.\n\n*   Though reluctant, the Nizam''s poor economic conditions forced him to give up the Northern Circars. He was further angered and, seeking Mysore support, came to an understanding with Hyder Ali to remove Muhammad Ali (seen as a British puppet) and install Tipu Sultan as Nawab of Carnatic. \n\n*   The British, moving first against Hyder Ali, defeated Nizam Ali''s forces at Tiruchirapally. \n*   On **23 February 1768**, Nizam Ali concluded a new treaty with the Madras British Company recognizing Muhammad Ali as real ruler of Carnatic, ceding Diwani (civil administration, revenue) rights on Balaghat of Carnatic to the British for a yearly payment of Rs. 7,00,000 — effectively removing the Nizam''s powers over Carnatic and the Northern Circars.\n\n### Possession of Guntur Circar\n\nPrior to the British treaty, Murtizanagar (Guntur) was under Basalat Jung. The Elluru, Rajahmundry, Mustafanagar, and Chicacole Circars had already passed to the British in 1766. In the case of Murtizanagar, it was only to pass to the company after Basalat Jung''s death or loss of Nizam Ali''s confidence.\n\nBasalat Jung, in conflict with his brother, recruited French troops and held talks with the British against Nizam Ali. Pressured by the British, he concluded a treaty on **27 January 1779**, removing the French from his employment and handing Guntur Circar to the British — angering Nizam Ali, who protested in writing. \n\nBritish officer Holland relayed the Nizam''s anger to the Bengal Government; Governor-General **Warren Hastings**, recognizing the resentment, assured the Nizam of continued friendship, and the British returned Guntur to Basalat Jung. Basalat Jung died in 1782; after discussion with **Cornwallis**, Nizam Ali handed Guntur over to the British permanently on **18 September 1788** — placing the entire coastal Andhra region from Ganjam to Guntur under British control.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 17, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p17", "partName": "Triple Alliance and the Third Mysore War (1790–92)", "bodyMd": "British power grew steadily in the Deccan, worrying Mysore ruler **Hyder Ali** on two fronts — the British and the Marathas. Hyder Ali defeated the British in the **First Mysore War (1766–68)** and fought them again in the **Second Mysore War (1780–84)**, dying in 1782. His son **Tipu Sultan** concluded the Mangalore treaty with the British in 1784, though the British felt badly humiliated in both wars.\n\nThe British sought Nizam Ali''s help to defeat Tipu; the Marathas also agreed to join. The British, Nizam, and Marathas formed the **Triple Alliance** in July 1790 and declared war on Mysore — the **Third Mysore War (1790–92)**. Tipu tried, unsuccessfully, to win over the Nizam and Marathas. Finding Tipu difficult to defeat, General Medos judged direct intervention necessary, and Cornwallis personally surrounded Srirangapatnam. \n\nTipu lost and agreed to the **Treaty of Srirangapatnam**, ceding half his state to the three allied parties. Nizam Ali received Gurramkonda, Kadapa, Gandikota, Khammam, and Ballery as his share. The British showed resentment toward the Nizam, feeling Prince Sikandarjah and Minister Azeem-ul-Umra had driven the Nizam''s forces slowly through the war.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 18, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p18", "partName": "The Loss of Kharda (1795)", "bodyMd": "After the Third Mysore War, disputes resumed between the Marathas and the Nizam. Nizam Ali resisted Maratha collection of Chauth and Sardeshmukhi in his territory; in 1791, **Nana Fadnavis**, the Peshwa''s minister, demanded these dues. Nizam Ali had long tried to weaken Maratha power by different methods, and now encouraged **Mahadaji Sindhia** against Fadnavis; the two agreed to work together, but Sindhia disclosed Nizam Ali''s plan to Fadnavis, who ordered immediate payment of the dues. Sindhia died on 12 February 1794, but Fadnavis stood firm.\n\nOn **11 March 1795** the two states fought the **Kharda War**. Nizam Ali requested British help, but British officer Captain Kirkpatrick rejected the request. The Nizam fought bravely but lost, and on 15 March 1795 agreed to an insulting treaty: Doulatabad, Ahmednagar, and Sholapur forts, together worth Rs. 34,50,000 in annual revenue, were handed to the Marathas, and the Nizam paid three crore rupees as war indemnity.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 19, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p19", "partName": "Raymond Services", "bodyMd": "Under an earlier pact of 1768, the British were obliged to help the Nizam whenever necessary, but they had not done so during the Kharda war. Afterward, Nizam Ali sent the British forces stationed in his territory back to British territory. He summoned Resident Captain Kirkpatrick to express his displeasure, stating that maintaining the two regular British battalions at heavy cost for so many years, without their actually being available for use, was meaningless, and had them removed from Hyderabad immediately.\n\nTurning his attention instead to the French, Nizam Ali found their officer **Raymond** eager to please him. Raymond''s French forces had fought at Kharda for the Nizam and lost 3,000 soldiers. Nizam Ali increased the \"Raymond French Forces\" to 15,000, paying a subsidy of Rs. 1,30,000 per month for their upkeep. Raymond asked for Kadapa to help maintain his forces and requested to be sent there under the pretext of providing security; **Mir Alam** informed Resident Kirkpatrick of these developments in 1795, heightening British anxiety over Nizam''s activities.\n\nAt the same time, Nizam Ali''s eldest son and Prince **Ali Jah** revolted against his father, reaching Bidar with the support of Medak Zamindar Sadashiva Reddy. Nizam Ali sought British help to suppress the revolt; British forces under Major Robert were sent toward Bidar. \n\nAli Jah, seeing the situation, surrendered, and after a few days, on his way back to Hyderabad, he committed suicide on **22 November 1795**. Raymond''s forces also assisted in suppressing this revolt. Raymond himself died on **25 March 1798**; after him, **Perron** took charge of the French forces, whose strength and influence subsequently declined.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 20, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p20", "partName": "The Subsidiary Alliance (1798)", "bodyMd": "**Lord Wellesley** was appointed Governor-General on 17 May 1798, succeeding Sir John Shore. British officials judged that Nizam''s support was essential both to control Tipu Sultan and the Marathas, and to remove French influence from the Deccan. \n\nWellesley ordered Kirkpatrick to conclude a new security treaty with the Nizam; Kirkpatrick explained its terms and Nizam Ali signed it on **1 September 1798** — known as the **Subsidiary Alliance**. Where two British battalions had previously served the Nizam, six more were added to protect the state, for which the Nizam was required to pay **Rs. 24,17,100 every year** to maintain the six battalions. Hyderabad thereby became a protected state of the British.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 21, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p21", "partName": "Fourth Mysore War & The Ceded Districts (1799–1800)", "bodyMd": "### Fourth Mysore War (1799)\n\nWellesley, as Governor-General, moved to control Tipu Sultan and declared war against Mysore in 1799 — the **Fourth Mysore War**. Nizam Ali supported the British; Mir Alam led Nizam forces, which met British forces under General Harris at Nellore, and together they marched on Srirangapatnam. \n\nTipu fought to the last and died defending his state; the combined British and Nizam forces won. By the resulting treaty, the British handed over Gooty, Gurramkonda, and Jerimulla in Chitradurg and Kolar regions to the Nizam, while the remaining, smaller Mysore territory was restored to the royal Wadayar family. The Nizam was dissatisfied that the British had kept the strategically valuable regions while he received the ordinary ones.\n\n### The Ceded Districts (1800)\n\nAfter Tipu Sultan''s death, Wellesley remained concerned about the continued strength of the Marathas in the Deccan and sought a stronger alliance with the Nizam to counter them. A fresh treaty was concluded on **20 October 1800**, under which the Subsidiary Alliance forces were further increased — one more Cavalry regiment along with proportional guns, artillery men, and eight battalions for the Nizam State. \n\nIn exchange, the territories the Nizam had gained under the Srirangapatnam treaty (18 March 1792) and the Mysore treaty (22 June 1799) were permanently ceded to the British for maintaining these forces in Hyderabad: **Bellary, Anantapur, Kadapa, and Kurnool districts** — thereafter known as the **Ceded Districts**. This 1800 treaty reduced the Nizam to the position of a subservient ally of the British. \n\nA commercial treaty followed in 1802, under which goods produced in either state paid a 5 percent tax when entering the other; Hyderabad also collected a 5 percent tax on British imports. Nizam Ali died in 1803, shortly after this treaty.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 22, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p22", "partName": "Sikandar Jah (1803–1829)", "bodyMd": "**Sikandar Jah**, Nizam Ali''s second son, ruled from 1803 to 1829 (Ali Jah, the elder son, had already revolted and died by suicide in 1795). Secunderabad city was named after him.\n\n### Second Maratha War and Territorial Gains\n\nWar between the Marathas and the British began in 1803, soon after Sikandar Jah''s accession — the **Second Maratha War**. Under the 1800 treaty, the Nizam was obliged to send all his forces, but he chose not to; only a small contingent went, under the control of his friend, Berar Governor Mahipat Ram, with Salabat Khan (Nawab of Ellichpur) and Raja Shukradar responsible for escorting them to the front — though in action the Nizam forces did not actively help the British.\n\nOn **23 September 1803**, at Assai, the British defeated the Marathas. The Nizam received Maratha territory as his share of the victory: the Ajantha hills, the middle Godavari region, land south of the Satpura mountains, and Nagpur-king-held districts west of the Wardha river — bringing the entire Berar region under Nizam control and extending the Nizam''s territory considerably. \n\nWellesley wrote to the Nizam expressing resentment against certain nobles and the general of the Nizam''s army during the war; the Nizam compromised and concluded a treaty on 15 December 1803, adding a further article on 9 January 1804 providing for mutual co-operation between civil and military officers and open access to forts and regions during wartime. \n\n**Azim-ul-Umra**, Diwan (Prime Minister) of Hyderabad, died on 12 April 1804, shortly after the treaty; **Mir Alam**, favourable to the British, was appointed Diwan in his place.\n\n### Chandulal and the Rise of British Influence\n\nThe Nizam wanted his friend Mahipat Ram as Diwan instead of Mir Alam, but the British preferred Mir Alam, who was accordingly appointed. When the post of Peshkar fell vacant, the Nizam wished to appoint Mahipat Ram, but Mir Alam''s son-in-law Munir-ul-Mulk and Raja Chandulal contested it; the Nizam sent Mahipat Ram to Berar, and **Chandulal** was appointed Peshkar, though the Nizam had no good opinion of him — his appointment came through British pressure and Mir Alam''s influence. \n\nChandulal continued as Peshkar with the support of British Resident **Syden Ham**. Mir Alam died on 28 February 1808, and the post of Diwan fell vacant; Munir-ul-Mulk and Shams-ul-Umra contested it. The British Governor-General proposed Shams-ul-Umra, but the Nizam disagreed, and Munir-ul-Mulk was finally appointed Diwan, while Chandulal continued as Peshkar.\n\nIn 1810 Syden Ham was transferred from Hyderabad, and **Russell** came as the new Resident, taking over administration and powers through Chandulal — the Resident thereby playing a key role in Hyderabad state administration. From 1811 onwards, Nizam Sikandar Jah took no interest in administration.\n\n### The Russell Brigade\n\nRussell arrived as Resident in 1811 and immediately reported the atrocities of Zamindars in Hyderabad to the British Bengal Government. He noted that the Nizam''s forces were not being used for internal crises, and found three types of Nizam forces poorly managed: the Nizam''s own private army (neglected since the 1800 treaty), a Diwan-controlled force made up largely of Rohillas (also acting irresponsibly), and a third force, the Linewala, formed by Mahipat Ram and based in Berar. Since all three acted irresponsibly, Russell sought to reorganize the Nizam''s forces.\n\nWith the permission of the Indian Government, Russell established a separate force called the **''Russell Brigade''**, later evolved into the **''Hyderabad Contingent.''** It took part in the Maratha war of 1818, suppressed the Pindaris in 1817, and controlled local revolts from 1818–1820. \n\nRussell captured **Mubariz-ul-Doula** and **Shams-ud-Doula**, who were opposing the British, and with Nizam Sikander Jah''s help sent them as prisoners — first to the Nizam''s palace on 23 August 1815, then to Golconda fort, where they were imprisoned until their release in 1820.\n\nThe Nizam became permanently free from the Marathas after the Marathas lost the **Third Maratha War (1818)**; Vizapur, the Kannada talukas, and the Ellora and Aurangabad talukas were merged into Hyderabad state, and the Nizam was freed from Chauth and Sardeshmukhi levies. \n\nHowever, the expenditure of the Russell Brigade proved unbearable; Russell, judging it impossible for the Nizam to pay these costs directly, arranged for the state to take loans from the **Palmer and Palmer Company** to fund the forces'' salaries.\n\n### Palmer and Palmer Company\n\nPalmer and Palmer Company was a business house founded by **William Palmer**, who had started his career in Nizam service in 1791 as the first British citizen to join the Nizam army, rising to the rank of Brigadier before retiring in 1812 to start a business career. He applied to the Governor-General''s Council on 30 March 1814 for permission to open a bank in Hyderabad and received it; Russell took a keen interest in the bank''s establishment. \n\nMembers of the company included William Palmer, Hastings Palmer, William Karri (Resident Surgeon), Bankati Das, Shamul Russell, South Be, George Lamb, and Engineer Russell.\n\n**Economic exploitation**: The company took deposits from individuals at 12 percent interest and lent money at 25 percent interest. Chandulal obtained British Government permission in 1819 for a loan of 60,00,000 rupees; the Palmer Company agreed to disburse this in six installments at 18 percent interest, while itself receiving 24,00,000 per year from the Nizam Government and taking the Berar district (worth Rs. 30,00,000 revenue) on lease from the Nizam — a transaction yielding the company a profit of Rs. 6,00,000. \n\nPalmer family members additionally drew pensions of Rs. 30,000 each per month from the Hyderabad treasury. This affair gave rise to the popular sayings **\"Nizam Looted\"** and **\"All Pay Nizzy\"** current in the Deccan at the time. William Rambold, son-in-law of the Madras Governor and relative of Governor-General Hastings, joined as a shareholder in 1816, after which the exploitation reached its peak; it continued until 1820, when **Metcalf** arrived as Resident and brought it under control.\n\n### Reforms of Metcalf\n\n**Charles Metcalf** was appointed Resident of Hyderabad in place of Henry Russell in December 1820. He quickly recognized how the Palmer and Palmer Company was exploiting the state, provided the Nizam with a loan facility from the Government of India at 6 percent interest, and freed the Nizam from the Palmer Company, which went bankrupt within a year. \n\nThe Nizam took Rs. 7,00,000 from the British Government to clear the Palmer Company dues, and permanently handed over his rights on the Northern Circar peshkash to the British on 4 November 1823.\n\nMetcalf carried through administrative reforms in Hyderabad:\n\n*   Ordered revenue collection on Khalsa, Jagir, and Jatidad lands based on net production.\n*   Reviewed expenditure on forces and pensions, identifying a Rs. 10,00,000 deficit in Nizam Government revenue.\n*   Fixed village-level peasant tax payments to the government, calculated land value village by village, and reported this to the government.\n*   Appointed European officers to receive peasant complaints directly, giving peasants a channel to reach the Nizam.\n*   Gave European supervisors powers to check crimes and atrocities committed by feudal lords.\n*   Appointed hereditary police officers.\n*   Concluded a new treaty with the Nizam in 1822, exempting the Nizam from the Chouth levy.\n\nThese reforms systematized the Hyderabad state. Metcalf left Hyderabad in 1825. Nizam Sikandar Jah died on **21 May 1829**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 23, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter13-asafjahi-rule-p23", "partName": "Nasir-ud-Doula (1829–1857)", "bodyMd": "The Government of India recognized Sikandar Jah''s son, **Nasir-ud-Doula**, as the next Nizam. On acceding, he removed the European superintendents appointed under the Metcalf reforms. Hyderabad forces under Minister Chandulal controlled revolts by the Zamindars of Mungal, Amalapalem, Mensur, Chitagani, and Hingoli, and by the Zamindars of Bheed (Dharmaji Appa Rao), Hatkar, Nowa (Nousoji Naik), Veerappa Naik of Koppal, Venkata Narsinga Rao of Amalapalem, and the Zamindars of Chintagani, Munagala, Sironcha, and Mahadevapuram — all revolting against European attacks or official domination. \n\nHyderabad forces gradually controlled these revolts. **General Fraser** was appointed Resident in 1838; around this time, the Wahabi movement reached the Deccan.\n\n### The Wahabi Movement\n\n**Syed Ahmed Brailvey** started the Wahabi Movement in India, active first in Bengal, the North-Western frontiers, and the United Provinces during the 1820s, aimed against Sikh dominance in Punjab. Syed Ahmad''s followers, called **Muzahids**, were urged by him in a letter to Sikandar Jah to be given wealth and forces to fight not only the Sikhs but also the Europeans and their domination.\n\nSyed Ahmad himself died in 1831; his followers reached the Deccan in 1838, led by **Moulvi Vilayat Ali and Saleem**, who came to trust **Mubariz-ud-Doula** and made him their leader in India.\n\nMubariz-ud-Doula started a revolt against the British. Captain Malcom informed Resident General Fraser of the revolt and of Mubariz''s secret contact with rulers of different regions; Fraser discussed the matter with the Nizam for his arrest, and Nizam Nasir-ud-Doula finally sent his own brother to Golconda fort. \n\nThe Tonku, Rampur, Udayagiri, and Kurnool Nawabs supported the Wahabi Movement and joined it against the British, but the British suppressed and controlled them. Mubariz-ud-Doula died on **25 June 1854** in Golconda fort under government custody; the Wahabi Movement declined after his death.\n\n### The Cession of Berar (1853)\n\nBy January 1851, the Nizam''s dues to the British East India Company had reached Rs. 70,00,000. The Nizam had promised to pay Rs. 12,00,000 annually to clear the dues, but Governor-General **Lord Dalhousie** was strict and wrote demanding immediate full payment. **Siraj-ul-Mulk** was appointed Diwan on 29 June 1851; he paid half the due amount, Rs. 34,08,485, on 15 August 1851 to build British confidence, promising the balance by 31 October 1851 — but only Rs. 8,73,547 more was paid by year''s end. **Colonel John Low** was appointed the new Resident of Hyderabad in 1853.\n\nLow held that a definite arrangement was needed from the Nizam''s Government for controlled monthly expenditure by the British and pressed the Nizam to hand over district revenues directly to the British. With no option left, the Nizam signed the **Berar Treaty on 21 May 1853**, under which Berar state, Osmanabad, and Raichur districts were handed to the British. Diwan Siraj-ul-Mulk died shortly after, on 23 May 1853; his son-in-law **Salarjung** was appointed the new Diwan.\n\n### Summary\n\nThe British used the Hyderabad state to serve their economic needs and those of their forces, acquiring fertile lands and mineral-rich regions from the state between 1766 and 1853 through a succession of treaties. They grew economically strong through control of the Northern Circars, the Ceded Districts, and Berar. \n\nThe Nizam opposed British policy at several points, but with little effect given his relative weakness against British power; figures such as Mir Alam and Chandulal, along with other government officials, aided the British in this process, alongside British political manoeuvring, which contributed to the Nizams'' declining standing. \n\nHyderabad faced repeated difficulties arising from Russell''s and John Low''s policies, and the Nizam had to struggle each time war broke out with the Marathas or the Mysore state. From 1853 onward, the Nizam''s Hyderabad state moved toward modernity.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p1", "partName": "The Hyderabad State and Its Financial Crisis", "bodyMd": "The Hyderabad state was founded by Nizam-ul-Mulk Asafjahi in **1724**, and his descendants continued to rule it until **1948**. Under Mir Osman Ali Khan (1911–1948), the Nizam Government surrendered following the Police Action of the Indian Government, and the state was merged into the Indian Union on **17th September 1948**.\n\nThroughout the long period of Nizam rule, the administration was run by Diwans (Prime Ministers) assisted by ministers and other officials. **Siraj-ul-Mulk** served as prime minister to Nizam **Nazir-ud-Doula (1829–1857)**. When Siraj-ul-Mulk died in 1853, **Salarjung** was appointed Prime Minister in his place, with the British intervening to secure his appointment.\n\n**Financial and administrative distress before Salarjung:** Repeated territorial and financial losses steadily weakened the state, plunging it into a severe financial crisis by 1853.\n\n*   In 1766, the Northern Sarkars (coastal districts) were taken over by the British.\n*   In 1800, further ceded districts were taken by the British.\n*   In 1853, the Nizam surrendered three rich districts — **Berar, Raichur, and Darashiv** — to the British under the Treaty of Berar.\n*   The state suffered financial exploitation through the Palmer and Company dealings, and through Chouth and Sardeshmukhi tax collection imposed by the Marathas.\n*   The Nizam state was also drawn into Maratha and Mysore wars under British pressure.\n*   With no proper administrative machinery to manage these pressures, the state and the Nizam were forced to mortgage their lands at high interest merely to meet day-to-day administrative expenses.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p2", "partName": "Salarjung''s Early Life and Rise to Power", "bodyMd": "Salarjung''s original name was **Mir Turab Ali Khan**; \"Salarjung\" (1829–1883) was his popular title. Before becoming Prime Minister, he served as Talukdar (Collector) of Medak District in Telangana.\n\n*   He became Prime Minister (Diwan) of Hyderabad State on **31st May 1853**, following the death of his uncle Siraj-ul-Mulk, at the young age of **24**. He was a western-educated young man, appointed with British support.\n*   He continued as Diwan until his death in **1883**, serving under three rulers in succession: **Nasir-ud-Doula (1829–1857)**, **Afzal-ud-Doula (1857–1869)**, and **Mir Mahaboob Ali Khan (1869–1911)**.\n*   When the sixth Nizam, Mir Mahaboob Ali Khan, ascended the throne in 1869 as a **three-year-old minor**, Salarjung gained the opportunity to effectively and independently establish his authority over the administration of the state.\n\n**Why the British backed Salarjung''s measures:**\n\n1.  During the **1857 revolt**, Salarjung and the Nizam sided with the British and helped suppress the uprising in the state.\n2.  The modernization of Hyderabad ultimately served colonial interests, so the British supported his reform program.\n\nSalarjung directed his first attention toward resolving the financial crisis of the state, and from there proceeded to comprehensive administrative reform.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p3", "partName": "Administrative Reforms", "bodyMd": "By Salarjung''s time, the Hyderabad State covered **82,698 sq. miles**, with a population, per the 1881 census, of **98,45,594**. The kingdom was a union of three regions: **Marathwada, Telangana, and Karnataka**. For administrative convenience, it was organized into subhas (provinces), sarkars (districts), and mahals (sub-districts).\n\nAdministrative reform proceeded in two stages: skillfully formulating measures, and implementing them with ability — Salarjung succeeded in both.\n\n### Provinces (Subhas)\n\nUnder the first Nizam, Nizam-ul-Mulk Asafjah, the state had **six subhas**: Aurangabad, Berar, Bijapur, Bidar, Hyderabad, and Khandesh. Due to later political developments and treaties with the British, all except Khandesh remained under Nizam control by Salarjung''s time.\n\n### Divisions (1867)\n\nIn 1867, Salarjung divided the Hyderabad State into **five divisions**:\n\n| Division | Headquarters |\n|---|---|\n| North Western | Aurangabad |\n| Western | Bidar |\n| Southern | Gulbarga |\n| North | Patancheruvu |\n| East | Bhuvanagiri |\n\nEach division was headed by a **Sadar Talukdar**, equivalent to a Revenue Commissioner in British India. The Sadar Talukdar held all revenue, civil, and criminal powers within the division, and also supervised the district officers (Talukdars/Collectors) below him. He was assisted by two officers — one for revenue matters, one for judicial matters — both paid officials who executed the instructions of the concerned ministry. Each division comprised three to four districts.\n\n### Districts\n\nPrior to Salarjung, districts were controlled by tax farmers or contractors. In **1855**, he replaced this system by appointing **paid Talukdars (Collectors)** over district administration, and in **1865** introduced the **\"Zilabandi\"** system.\n\nUnder Zilabandi, the state was divided into **fourteen districts**:\n\n| No. | District |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Aurangabad |\n| 2 | Parbhani |\n| 3 | Nanded |\n| 4 | Indur (Nizamabad) |\n| 5 | Bhir |\n| 6 | Bidar |\n| 7 | Medak (Methuku Seema) |\n| 8 | Elagandula (Karimnagar) |\n| 9 | Naldurg |\n| 10 | Sholapur |\n| 11 | East Raichur |\n| 12 | West Raichur |\n| 13 | Warangal (Khammam was then part of Warangal) |\n| 14 | Nalgonda |\n\nBy **1880**, two more districts — **Nagarkurnool** and **Gulbarga** — were created, based on total revenue, bringing the count to sixteen. Districts were graded into three categories by annual revenue:\n\n| Category | Annual Revenue |\n|---|---|\n| First | More than Rs. 12,00,000 |\n| Second | Up to Rs. 10,00,000 |\n| Third | Up to Rs. 8,00,000 |\n\nEach district was headed by a Talukdar or Collector, assisted by other officials. Talukdars themselves were divided into three categories, each with three internal grades and a distinct salary scale:\n\n| Talukdar Category | 1st Grade | 2nd Grade | 3rd Grade |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| First category | Rs. 1,000/month | Rs. 800/month | Rs. 600/month |\n| Second category | Rs. 450/month | Rs. 400/month | Rs. 300/month |\n| Third category | Rs. 300/month | Rs. 250/month | Rs. 200/month |\n\nThe first Talukdar (Collector) headed the district with subordinate staff and was responsible for revenue income and expenditure, collection of revenue, export and import taxes, and resolving some civil and criminal disputes. He was assisted by Deputy Talukdars, clerks (Gumasthas), Judicial Officials, Revenue Officials, and Treasury Officials; district records were maintained with the help of \"Amla\" clerks or departmental clerks.\n\n### District Reorganization (1868)\n\nA further round of district reorganization took place in **1868**, modeled on the **Bombay pattern** rather than the Madras pattern followed elsewhere. Revenue administration posts of **Avval, Duyyam, and Suyyam Talukdar** were created (Talukdar being equivalent to Collector), with a **Board of Revenue** established to supervise the Collectors. For administrative convenience, the state was divided into **five divisions, sixteen districts, and 114 taluks**:\n\n| Division | Districts | Taluks |\n|---|---|---|\n| North | Medak, Indur (Nizamabad), Elgandal (Karimnagar) | 23 |\n| East | Khammam, Warangal, Nalgonda, Nagarkurnool | 26 |\n| West | Bidar, Nanded, Naldurg | 25 |\n| South | Raichur, Sholapur, Gulbarga | 14 |\n| Northwest | Aurangabad, Parbhani | 26 |\n\n### Taluks\n\nSalarjung further divided districts into **Taluks**. At the start of his period there were **74 taluks** in the Diwani region; by 1880–81 this had risen to **107**. The distribution by district included: Aurangabad-8, Bhir-7, Parbhani-6, Bidar-5, Nanded-8, Naldurg-7, Raichur-5, Lingasagoor-4, Sholapur-4, Gulbarga-6, Indur-9, Medak-5, Elagandula-7, Khammam-9, Nalgonda-5, Nagarkurnool-8, Sirpur Tandur-3, and Altaf Balda-1.\n\nTaluks were headed by **Tahasildars**, graded into three levels by salary:\n\n| Grade | Monthly Salary |\n|---|---|\n| First | Rs. 150 |\n| Second | Rs. 125 |\n| Third | Rs. 100 |\n\nTahasildars administered revenue, judiciary, police, and other matters within the taluk, assisted by subordinate paid clerks (Gumasthas), revenue officials, and judicial officials.\n\n### Cities\n\nAfter the taluk, the next administrative unit was the city, headed by a **Kotwal** — the Chief Officer of the city responsible for police, peace, and order. Separate officials handled city revenue, income and expenditure, and judicial and other city matters.\n\n### Villages\n\nThe village was the last administrative unit. Salarjung introduced several reforms in village administration, involving the following functionaries:\n\n*   **Patwaris** — kept land records of the village and assisted in collecting land revenue from villagers, paid on a **2 paise commission** basis.\n*   **Revenue Patels** — assisted the Patwari in land revenue collection, also on a **2 paise commission** basis.\n*   **Police Patel and Talari** — looked after law and order in the village, paid a commission of **2 paise** from tax collected in the villages.\n*   **Dade** — assisted visiting state officials and worked as a postman, paid a **4 paise commission** from the local fund.\n*   **Nirukadu** — looked after water storage and release to fields from the tanks.\n*   **Sethusindu** — each protected fifty houses in the village and assisted the Police Patel in maintaining peace and order.\n\nAll of these village functionaries drew their income from the **\"Grama Seva Funds.\"**\n\n### Central Administration\n\nThe overall administration of the state was carried on under the control of the Prime Minister or Diwan, who was also called Regent. He was assisted by the Deputy Prime Minister, the British Resident, the Finance Minister, and Heads of Departments/Branches, who gave suggestions and advice. The British Resident''s role was advisory, limited to political and economic issues.\n\n*   By **1880–81**, the administration had **14 departments**: Judiciary, Revenue, Police, Public Works, Education, Health, Municipal Corporation, Army, Finance, Postal, Railway and Telegraph, Nizam Sarf-i-Khas land department, Political affairs, and Judicial & Legislative Acts department.\n*   These fourteen departments were broadly grouped under **seven departmental ministers** — Judiciary, Revenue, Police, Public Works, Education, Health, and Municipal Corporation — each titled **\"Sadar-ul-Miham.\"** They were assisted by Secretaries and subordinate staff, and were ultimately responsible to the Prime Minister, who in turn — along with his bureaucracy — was responsible to the king. The king could accept or reject ministerial advice; supreme authority in the kingdom rested with him.\n*   In **1868**, administration was brought under **four departmental ministers**, called **\"Sadar-ul-Mahams\":**\n\n| Department | Minister |\n|---|---|\n| Revenue | Nawab Mukaram-ud-Doula Bahadur |\n| Judiciary, Jails | Bhasheer-ud-Doula |\n| Police | Shamsher Jung Bahadur |\n| Public Works, Education & Health | Shahebjung Bahadur |\n\nAlongside these ministers, the Deputy Prime Minister, \"Ashja-ud-Daula\" Munirjung Bahadur, gave key suggestions and advice on administrative issues; his advice was always accepted by the Prime Minister, who would discuss it with the Nizam before decisions were taken. Ultimately, the Nizam himself was the highest authority — his orders were law, his court the highest judicial court, and he was Commander-in-Chief of the army. He could accept or reject any advice given to him.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p4", "partName": "Judicial Reforms", "bodyMd": "Salarjung established the judicial institution most effectively and gave the judiciary **autonomy**, with separate **civil and criminal** jurisdictions. An independent judicial branch, separate from executive administration, already existed in the state from **1846**; Salarjung built on this foundation and strengthened it considerably, establishing **Sessions Courts** at district headquarters and organizing the High Court at Hyderabad with a Chief Judge and four judges to hear Diwani (civil) and Fauzdari (criminal) appeals.\n\n### Judicial Institutions Before Salarjung''s Later Reforms\n\nAfter becoming Prime Minister, Salarjung initially established judiciary of three types:\n\n1.  **Adalat-e-Padushahi**, formed in 1853.\n2.  A judicial institution to decide disputes between the Nizam''s soldiers and Sikh soldiers, established in 1855.\n3.  A new judicial institution established by an official named **Govind Rao** in 1860, to decide civil disputes in the Ilaka Peshkar areas.\n\n### Later Reforms Under Diwan Salarjung\n\n*   He established the **Sadar-ul-Miham Adalat** in **1869** for judicial matters in the Diwani region.\n*   The first Law Minister appointed in the Nizam kingdom was **Nawab Basheer-ud-Daula Bahadur**, assisted by a secretary and Gumasthas (clerks). \n\n*   The Law Minister handled civil and criminal cases in the city and Muffasil regions, and issued orders to protect law and justice. If a judgment was found erroneous and no justice had been done, it was mandatory to take the Prime Minister''s permission to re-enquire and decide the case.\n\n*   In **1872**, Diwan Salarjung made key changes to the judiciary by establishing the **Court of Appeal**, named **\"Mahakama-e-Murafa-e-Ajla.\"** It had a Presiding Officer and four members, and was described as the \"Appellate Court of Judicature.\" \n\n*   It decided all types of civil and criminal appeal cases from the city and districts, made rules and regulations for the smooth functioning of the various courts, and was the final authority in all judicial matters after the Diwan.\n\n*   In civil judicial administration, **\"Majlis-Aliya Adalat\"** (the **High Court**) was the key judicial court. A person punished in a lower court could file a petition in the High Court. Its powers and structure:\n    *   It decided civil disputes in Hyderabad city and cases involving monetary value.\n    *   Below it were Divisional courts, District courts, Taluka courts, City courts, and courts to decide religious disputes; there were also separate courts for border disputes.\n    *   It had powers to reduce or cancel severe punishments, including death sentences.\n    *   It could impose life sentence and **39 rope beats**.\n\n### Punishment Powers by Judicial Level\n\n| Level | Officer | Imprisonment | Fine & Rope Beats |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Division | Sadar Talukdar | 10 years | Rs. 4,000; 39 rope beats |\n| District (1st) | First Talukdar | 4 years | Rs. 1,000; 39 rope beats |\n| District (2nd) | Second Talukdar | 2 years | Rs. 500; 15 rope beats |\n| District (3rd) | Third Talukdar | 1 year | Rs. 300; 15 rope beats |\n| Taluk | Tahasildar | 6 months | Rs. 150; 12 rope beats |\n| City | Kotwal/Police Commissioner | 3 years | Rs. 500; 39 rope beats |\n| Village | Police Patel | 4 days | Rs. 3; — |\n\n### Court Statistics During Salarjung''s Period\n\nThere were **five divisional courts, seventeen district courts** under the first Talukdar/Collector, **22 courts** under the second Talukdar, **19 courts** under the third Talukdar, and **103 courts** under Tahasildars. There was one city court under the Kotwal/City Police Commissioner and one High Court at the capital, Hyderabad. \n\nIn the Diwani area of Hyderabad State, there were **168 criminal courts**, and among civil courts there was a separate Arabs Court, a Kazi Court, and a city border court to deal with disputes not settled elsewhere; there were also 6 courts for disputes with other cities in the kingdom. The **total civil courts** in the Diwani area came to **172**.\n\n### Judicial Salaries\n\n| Post | Monthly Salary |\n|---|---|\n| Chief Judge, High Court | Rs. 1,200 |\n| Other Judges, High Court | Rs. 600 |\n| Border court judges (city border disputes) | Rs. 2,400 |\n| Kazi Court Chief | Rs. 500 |\n\nNotably, border court judges were paid more than the Chief Justice of the High Court. Judges were appointed purely on merit, and only moral and honest people could secure appointments.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p5", "partName": "Police Reforms", "bodyMd": "Salarjung separated the **police department from the revenue department**, a key structural reform.\n\n### Structure\n\n*   An **Amin (Inspector)** was appointed for every two Taluks, with an **Additional Inspector** for a large Taluka. Each part was assisted by an Attached Police Official.\n*   The kingdom was organized into **five parts**, each headed by a **Naib Sadar Muhatamin**, titled **Deputy Inspector General of Police**.\n*   Each district had a **Superintendent of Police (Muhatamin)**, responsible for supervising the district''s entire police force and controlling the Inspector of each Taluka.\n*   Police Constables were appointed for each district, along with police personnel known as **\"Sovers.\"**\n*   Both Superintendents and Inspectors of Police had assistants called Gumasthas.\n*   There were **three classes each** of Superintendents of Police and Inspectors of Police.\n\nAppointments in the police force were made purely on merit and moral character.\n\n### Salaries by Grade\n\n| Post | 1st Grade | 2nd Grade | 3rd Grade |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Superintendent of Police (District Head) | Rs. 200/month | Rs. 170/month | Rs. 140/month |\n| Inspector (Taluka Police Head) | Rs. 110/month | Rs. 100/month | Rs. 90/month |\n\nIn **1869**, the police force was reorganized, and a separate **police secretary** was appointed to oversee the entire police administration, brought under ministerial control. The first police minister was **Nawab Shamsher Jung Bahadur**.\n\n### Force Divisions and Strength\n\nThe police force was divided into two divisions: the **General Police Force** and the **Village Police Force**.\n\n*   General Police Force constables earned Rs. 7 or 8/month and could be promoted up to Head Constable based on performance; Inspectors were also promoted on performance.\n*   By **1879–90**, the General Police Force strength stood at **14,055**, of which **3,250** were allotted to city duties and **10,805** to district duties. At the Taluk level, **\"Out Posts\"** were established under a Head Constable or Jamedar.\n*   At the village level, law and order was entrusted to the **Police Patel**, assisted by the Talari and Sethusindus — each Sethusindu protecting fifty houses.\n*   City police administration was led by the **Kotwal (City Police Commissioner)**, with subordinate staff and a force protecting the lives of the people.\n\nOverall, the police hierarchy ran: **Deputy Inspector General** (division) → **Superintendent of Police** (district) → **Amin/Inspector** (taluka) → **Police Patel and Sethusindu** (village).", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p6", "partName": "Military Reforms", "bodyMd": "Salarjung reorganized the military of the Nizam Kingdom.\n\n*   In **1864**, he built a dedicated building for supervising the Nizam''s army, staffed by a Secretary and an Assistant. The Secretary of the Military Branch handled expenditure, appointments, transfers, and issue of ordinary military orders, and specially reviewed the permanent battalions.\n*   **Kalandhar-Bardhars** (officials) submitted representations and appointments of temporary soldiers to the Diwan.\n*   In **1875**, the government established the **\"Daftar-e-Najam Jamiyat\"** (the Military Office).\n*   In **1869**, the **Mansaf Office** was established.\n*   In **1876**, a **Military Secretary** was appointed, responsible for military appointments and reviews, as well as other issues concerning temporary soldiers; the **Mansabdar offices** were established under his supervision.\n*   Military personnel were trained under European officials, with overall supervision by the British.\n\nDespite these efforts, Salarjung''s attempts to fully modernize the armed forces — including plans to introduce machinery in state workshops — did not succeed; the Hyderabad army remained un-modernized on modern lines despite his best efforts.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p7", "partName": "Revenue Reforms", "bodyMd": "Salarjung''s main intention was to give the state economic stability, and revenue reform was the most important strand of his overall reform program.\n\n### Land Classification\n\nThe entire state land was divided into **four parts**, each carrying different tax levies:\n\n| Land Type | Share of Total Area | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Khalsa (Diwani) | 60% | Under direct government control |\n| Sarfekhas | 10% | Expenses of the Nizam Nawab''s daily maintenance |\n| Paigah lands | (combined 30% with Jagirs) | Maintenance of Paigah military nobles |\n| Jagirs | (combined 30% with Paigah) | Granted to various people on different merit |\n\nIn the Sarfekhas, Paigah, and Jagir areas, different land revenue collection systems prevailed and there was no direct administrative control by the Diwani government.\n\n### Abolition of the Tax Farming System\n\nCorrupt land revenue collection through tax farming — under which daftardars paid a fixed amount to the state while collecting more from peasants — exploited both peasants and the state, and kept peasants out of direct contact with the state. Recognizing this defect, Salarjung, after assuming office, abolished the tax farming system and removed the tax farmers (daftardars).\n\n*   He appointed paid **Talukdars (Collectors)** in place of the daftardars, bringing peasants into direct contact with the state, relieving them of daftardar exploitation, and enabling Salarjung to know the exact income of the state.\n*   The basis of revenue collection was shifted from the **village to the individual**, with differential rates based on land quality — eliminating middleman exploitation while increasing government revenue.\n\n### The Sarbarasta System and the Rise of Landlordism\n\nEven before Ryotwari was extended across the state, revenue collection in Khalsa (government) villages was carried out through a **middleman auction system**. Under this arrangement, known as **\"Tahutta\"** or **\"Sarbarasta\"** (also called **\"Bilmakta\"**), the right to collect a fixed amount of tax from a locality for a set period was auctioned off, and the strongest bidders in each area — typically the locally powerful — secured these rights. Once the auction term ended, the right could change hands again. \n\nDuring the Sarbarasta period, the contractors (tahutdars/bilmaktadars) were required to pay the government only the fixed contracted amount, while collecting far more from the peasants for themselves — building up substantial wealth in the process. Salarjung abolished this system, surveyed and settled land holdings directly, and introduced the practice of the government''s own machinery collecting revenue straight from the peasant. \n\nUntil then, prominent contractors under the Sarbarasta system had been granted titles such as **Deshmukh, Deshpandya, Deshai,** and **Sardeshai**, along with compensation payments (parihars) when the system was withdrawn.\n\n*   These titleholders — the Deshmukhs, Deshpandyas, Deshais, and Sardeshais — had, over the years, registered vast tracts of land in their own names and become large landlords, particularly in the Khammam, Nalgonda, Mahbubnagar, and Warangal districts, where landlords holding over 500 acres each controlled **60 to 70 percent** of the land.\n*   A survey conducted in **1942** found **3,668** such Deshmukhs, Deshpandyas, Deshais, and Sardeshais across the state.\n*   This concentration of land in the hands of a landlord class, rooted in the old revenue-farming system, later became a central grievance behind the Telangana peasant movement.\n\n### Revenue Board (1864)\n\nSalarjung established the **Revenue Board** in **1864** to supervise the revenue administration institution in the Diwani regions and areas taken over from the British. It had a President and four members, and Mukkaram-ud-Daula signed the orders issued by the Board, which supervised revenue administration under the Diwan''s control. \n\nIn **1875**, the British Government at the Centre established an **Inam Office**, headed by an Inam Commissioner, which took possession of unnecessary and misused Inam lands and merged them with the Khalsa/Diwani lands.\n\n### Zilabandi System (1865)\n\nSalarjung introduced **\"Zilabandi\"** in **1865** to streamline revenue administration, alongside the **Jamabandi** system of annual land recording.\n\n*   The state was divided into **fourteen Zillas (Districts)**, each headed by an **Avval Talukdar (District Collector)**, assisted by **Doyam Talukdars (Sub-Collectors)** — all paid officers responsible for revenue collection and law and order.\n*   Districts were divided into Taluks administered by paid Tahsildars and their subordinate staff, responsible for revenue, law, and order.\n*   The fourteen districts were grouped into **five regional divisions**, each headed by a Sadar Talukdar — equivalent to a Revenue Commissioner of British India — responsible for revenue and other matters across the division and lower levels.\n*   At village level, Patwaris handled revenue collection with Patels assisting them, both paid on **commission, not salary**, and working on a **hereditary basis**, meaning a successor would take over the position after the incumbent — intended to build good local understanding over the long run.\n\n### The Ijara (Lease) System\n\nTo resettle villages that had become depopulated, Salarjung introduced another arrangement alongside Ryotwari, known as the **Ijara (lease) system**, aimed at bringing suitable land back into cultivation. Under this scheme, land was leased out at a nominal rent in the initial stage; the rent was then increased gradually and systematically, and eventually the full assessed rate was collected. The Ijara system was itself withdrawn in **1908**.\n\n### Ryotwari Settlement (1857)\n\nSalarjung introduced the **Ryotwari system** in the state in **1857**. The settlement period under this system was initially fixed at **20 years**, and later reduced to **15 years**; each new settlement carried an opportunity to raise the assessed tax.\n\n*   In line with this settlement policy, **wet (tari/magani) cultivation** was made compulsory in suitable areas.\n*   Pattadars who had, at their own expense, developed dry land into wet land — for instance by digging wells — were nonetheless assessed at the higher wet-land (tari) rate on such land.\n*   Wet land was assessed at **Rs. 20–25 per acre**, against **Rs. 10–12 per acre** for dry land — a heavier tax that remained in force until it was abolished after the formation of Andhra Pradesh.\n\n### Survey and Settlement\n\nThe **\"Survey and Settlement Department\"** was established to survey lands, fix boundaries, and decide land type, headed by a special Commissioner. A Superintendent of Revenue Surveys was appointed, assisted by Deputy Superintendents; there were four or five such Superintendents across the Nizam kingdom.\n\n*   The survey was first successfully conducted at **Aurangabad**, and afterwards, in **1877**, at **Naldurg**.\n*   The **Ryotwari system** of land revenue was adopted, and **patta rights** were granted to individual ryots.\n*   The annual recording of lands was called **\"Zamabandi,\"** carried forward from this point.\n*   Land measurement used a **10-yard chain**; a **\"Biga\"** — equivalent to **6/6 chains, or 3,600 square yards** — was the unit of land, still in use as a unit in Telangana.\n*   Ryots were permitted to pay land tax in either cash or cereals, which the government could store in warehouses.\n\nThese revenue measures together led to an increase in land revenue of around **Rs. 2,09,21,971 by 1880–81**.\n\n### Overall Revenue Growth\n\nThe increase in land revenue and customs revenue placed the state''s fiscal system on a sound footing.\n\n*   Total public revenue was around **Rs. 0.8 million in 1853**, rising to about **Rs. 35.0 million by 1883**.\n*   This increase was accompanied by a **reduction in public expenditure**, achieved by abolishing the private military troops of Rohillas and Pathans and their salaries, and by reducing irregular troops, allowances, assignments, and mansabs.\n*   Increased revenue combined with reduced expenditure enabled the government to reduce its enormous public debt to a minimal amount — public debt fell from around **Rs. 35.0 million in 1848** to **Rs. 4.1 million by 1882**.\n*   This drastic debt reduction, in turn, enabled the government to abolish **tankahi mahalat Jagirs** and the system of contracting or farming in Diwani areas.\n*   The improved fiscal position was accompanied by the establishment of a **central treasury**, replacement of private bankers with **state treasuries** in districts and taluks, and introduction of a formal **budget system** with a special **Accountant General** and subordinate staff for maintaining income and expenditure accounts.\n*   **Private mints owned and financed by Jagirdars were abolished**, and a state monopoly on minting was established, introducing a **uniform system of coinage** based on the **\"Halisicca\" rupee**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p8", "partName": "Financial Reforms", "bodyMd": "When Salarjung became Diwan, there was no dedicated Revenue Institution or Administrative Institution in the Nizam kingdom. The Nizam had mortgaged valuable lands and jewellery and taken loans for government maintenance, and was due to pay **Rs. 50,00,000** to the British for loans taken. The resource-rich Raichur, Darashivam, and Berar regions had been taken under British control.\n\n### Recovery of Mortgaged Lands and Territory\n\n*   After 1853, Salarjung was able to increase the area of Diwani land under his administration.\n*   The Nizam gave the British the following, under the 1853 Agreement:\n\n| Region | Income |\n|---|---|\n| Berar valley | Rs. 30,60,309 |\n| Balghat | Rs. 5,48,601 |\n| Western districts | Rs. 2,29,588 |\n| Raichur district | Rs. 1,51,342 |\n\n*   Before 1861, Diwani land covered **40,000 square miles**, but this later rose to **71,598 square miles** due to the cancellation of mortgages on lands, for which Salarjung was responsible.\n\n### Role in the 1857 Revolt and British Rewards\n\n*   Nizam and Salarjung assisted the British during the **1857 Indian Sepoy Revolt**: they controlled the attack on the Hyderabad Residency and imprisoned the rebels **Turrebaz Khan** and **Moulvi Allauddin**.\n*   The British praised the Nizam Government''s role in controlling the mutiny as unforgettable, and as reward, waived the loan of **Rs. 50,00,000** due from the Nizam.\n*   The **Raichur** district, Ahmednagar, Darashivam, and Naldurg were returned to the Nizam in **1861**.\n*   Separately, when **Raja Venkatappaiah Naik** of Sholapur revolted during the 1857 mutiny, Salarjung and the Nizam had him arrested (the Wanaparthi ruler Raja Rameshwar Rao arrested Naik and sent him to the Nizam, who surrendered him to the British); as a result, the British rewarded the Nizam with Sholapur territory.\n*   Inam lands worth **Rs. 8,37,205** and Jagirs worth **Rs. 8,86,875** were surveyed and taken into government possession, further improving the kingdom''s economy.\n\n### Customs and Other Taxes\n\n*   In **1860**, Salarjung prohibited and abolished taxes on imports and exports, while the Customs Department was given power to impose tax on **salt**; a taxation policy on goods was introduced.\n*   **Highway taxes**, nominal before his tenure, were increased and collected more effectively.\n*   A district-wise \"cordon\" (bandi) excise system was set up, auctioning off the rights to collect duty on toddy, opium (moha/afim), bhang, and ganja separately in each district, and a stamp duty was imposed on sale and purchase transactions.\n*   In **1871**, coal was discovered in the **Singareni** region, prompting British efforts to introduce transport facilities to the coal mines.\n*   In **1867**, a separate **forest department** was created under a Superintendent of Forest, bringing the state''s forest resources under government control.\n*   In **1861**, the stamp paper office was brought under the control of **Munshikhana**, and the sale of stamp paper became a government monopoly.\n\n### Currency Reform and \"Currency Profit\"\n\nAt the time, government employees'' salaries were paid by weight in the old **\"Sugur Sicca\"** coin. Salarjung had these converted and paid out instead in the new **\"Hali Sicca\"** coinage; the margin gained through this conversion — around **10 percent** — was credited to the state treasury and came to be known as **\"Currency Profit.\"** \n\nThis currency profit, together with peshkush payments from zamindars, local fund cess, stamp duties, and other fees, became one of the state''s principal channels for increasing government revenue.\n\nThese financial measures together achieved fiscal stability and allowed Salarjung to carry forward his broader administrative reform program.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p9", "partName": "Educational Reforms", "bodyMd": "Effective administration required educated persons and trained technical staff, motivating Salarjung''s educational reforms.\n\n*   In **1855**, he established the first western educational institution, the **Darul Ulum School**, in Hyderabad city, teaching in Urdu, Persian, and English mediums.\n*   The **City High School** (1870) and **Chadarghat High School** (1872) were established subsequently; in these, alongside oriental language subjects, **English was made a compulsory subject**.\n*   A **School of Civil Engineering** was established in 1870 to produce technical staff for the Public Works Department — a unique government experiment. This school''s location later became the site of **Hyderabad College**, which came to be known as **Nizam College** from **1887**.\n*   **Madarasa-e-Aliya** was established in 1873 for the children of Salarjung and the nobility.\n*   **Madarasa-e-Aizza** was opened in 1878 for children of the royal family.\n*   The Nizam also donated funds for the establishment of the Aligarh Educational Institution — the present **Aligarh Muslim University** — with Hyderabad officials **Vicar-ul-Mulk** and **Mohsin-ul-Mulk** contributing to its improvement.\n*   A separate **education department** was created in **1869**, tasked with establishing government schools in Hyderabad city and almost all districts. During Salarjung''s administration, **162 schools** were established across the kingdom, comprising **105 Persian schools, 34 Maharastrian schools, 4 English medium schools, and 19 Telugu medium schools**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p10", "partName": "Transportation Facilities and Public Works", "bodyMd": "Improved fiscal and budgetary conditions, the product of Salarjung''s revenue and financial reforms, enabled him to launch transportation and public works programs.\n\n### Railways\n\n*   A railway line between **Hyderabad and Sholapur** was built in **1868**.\n*   An agreement was made in **1870** between the British India Government and the Hyderabad State Government for a line between Wadi and Hyderabad, on the Madras–Bombay railway route.\n*   Following the discovery of high-quality coal at **Singareni** in 1871, and rising demand for coal driven by railway expansion, the line was further extended to the coal-mine region via **Dornakal and Ellandu**, and on to **Vijayawada**.\n*   Coal mines were also found in the north of the Nizam kingdom, at **Chanda** (present-day Maharashtra), and it was decided — and approved during Salarjung''s tenure — to extend the railway there. The **Railway Company** collected three lakh pounds as capital.\n*   The construction of this line was kept secret from the public, and a rumour spread in the kingdom that it would be unprofitable and lead to irreparable loss.\n*   The first line, between **Secunderabad and Wadi**, opened to traffic in **1874**, but its returns remained low, as the line primarily served **British strategic interest** rather than the Hyderabad economy.\n*   Hyderabad''s efforts to independently raise capital in London money markets were strongly resisted by the colonial Indian government. The state eventually convinced the Home Office instead, and with help from the **National Provincial Bank of England**, floated the **\"His Highness The Nizam''s Guaranteed State Railway Company Limited.\"**\n*   The lines to the coal fields and cotton-producing districts materialized only more than a decade after Salarjung''s death.\n*   During this period, initial surveys and negotiations were also undertaken with the colonial government to begin coal mining, and eventually in **1887**, the **Hyderabad (Deccan) Mining Company** — with two-thirds of its capital from the state government — began operations.\n\n### The Chanda Railway Scheme Controversy\n\n*   In **1880**, an intellectual group formed in Hyderabad State to discuss the state''s problems. They formed a committee to examine the Chanda Railway Scheme when it was about to be implemented, headed by the Principal of Hyderabad College, **Dr. Agoranath Chatopadhya**, with **Mulla Abdul Qayyum** as a member.\n*   The committee wanted to bring the full details of the railway scheme to public knowledge, but its request to the Nizam Government for scheme details was rejected, being treated as a threat to the government.\n*   As a result, Agoranath Chatopadhya was removed from his job and, on **20th May 1883**, banned from the Nizam kingdom; the government also gradually suppressed the members opposing the Chanda Railway Scheme.\n*   The scheme was nonetheless executed, and the **Bombay–Madras Railway Route**, via Gulbarga and Wadi in the Nizam kingdom, was started by the British; the Hyderabad railway route was completed in **1878**, connecting Hyderabad via Wadi to Bombay and improving transport facilities.\n\n### Postal, Telegraph, and Roads\n\n*   During **1856–57**, the **Telegraph Institution** was established, connecting Hyderabad to Bombay via Kurnool.\n*   In **1862**, the **Postal Department** was established; from **1871**, the Postal Institution spread regular postal connections to all districts, talukas, and cities, functioning under a Post Master General. **Nawab Shah Sawar Jung** was appointed the first Post Master General of Hyderabad, and the first series of stamps were introduced.\n*   **Telephone services** connected several districts starting in **1885**.\n*   Roads between different districts were improved, along with construction of bridges and culverts, undertaken by the **Public Works Department (PWD)**. In **1868**, the Hyderabad–Sholapur road was laid, and further road works were pursued vigorously thereafter, including repair and maintenance of irrigation works.\n\n**Broader significance:** Hyderabad became the first native state to finance and eventually own its own road and rail transport, coal mines, and later power generation. \n\nThe infrastructural development and improvement of trade mechanisms — together with the restructuring of feudal social relations, especially the shift in revenue basis from village to individual — paved the way for **commercialization of the agrarian economy**, seen in the growth of cotton in Marathwada and, later, oil seeds in Telangana. Thus, Salarjung''s reforms under his Prime Ministership laid the basis for **industrialization in Hyderabad**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p11", "partName": "Public Services and Recruitment", "bodyMd": "To improve administrative standards, Salarjung established the **Public Service Commission** during his tenure and recruited talented people from across the country.\n\n*   **Mohammedia and Kayastha youth from Northern India** were selected in large numbers as employees of the Hyderabad State — including Syed Hussaini, Syed Bilgrami, Mahib Hussain, Syed Mehdi Ali, and Mohin-ul-Mulk — who became able administrators after joining service.\n*   **Aghoranath Chatopadhyay**, a Bengali educationist and father of **Sarojini Naidu**, joined the Education Institution in the Nizam kingdom and later became Principal of Hyderabad College (present-day Nizam College).\n*   **Ramachandra Reddy** and **Barrister Rudra** also joined service in the kingdom.\n*   **Mirza Asha Baig**, grandson of the famous Urdu poet **Mirza Ghalib**, was invited to Hyderabad to educate Prince Mahaboob Ali Khan.\n*   Salarjung introduced an **automatic promotion system** based on talent and hard work for government employees.\n\nThe recruitment of talented outsiders into state administration, over time, brought a new element into Hyderabad''s social structure — the **\"non-mulkis\"** — emerging as a powerful social category. This led to conflict between **mulkis and non-mulkis**, which influenced state policy in various ways in later years and **laid the foundation for the Telangana Movement**. \n\nAt that stage, the Nizam took measures to suppress mulki agitation against the non-mulkis. Through these reforms, Salarjung became the guiding figure of modern Hyderabad''s administrative history.\n\n### Personality and Legacy of Salarjung\n\nSalarjung himself was a leading Jagirdar, belonging to a **Shia family** that had originally migrated from **Bijapur, Deccan**. His growing control over the state apparatus became a source of tension with the **Sunni aristocracy**, especially the **Paigah nobles**, whose maneuvers constrained several of his initiatives. \n\nAfter the death of the co-regent in 1877, he bitterly opposed the appointment of Paigah nobles to the post, but ultimately had to yield to British pressure. There was also serious tension between him and the traditional bureaucracy, particularly the daftardars (contract Talukdars) whom he replaced with paid Talukdars.\n\n**The changing standing of the Diwan:** Up to the time of the sixth Nizam, Prime Ministers had held a much greater role in state administration than Salarjung eventually enjoyed. Earlier Diwans had drawn correspondingly large salaries tied directly to state income — **Arastu Jah**, who served as Diwan from 1777 to 1802, took one-eighth of the state''s income as his salary; **Mir Jumla** received Rs. 22 lakh; **Munirul Mulk** received Rs. 6 lakh; and **Chandu Lal** drew one-sixteenth of state income. \n\nWhen state revenue began to shrink continuously, British Resident-General **Fraser** advised the Nizam that Diwans could not go on drawing salaries at the old scale and would need to reduce their own pay — a recommendation that led **Salarjung to cut his own salary to Rs. 14,000**.\n\n**Relations with the Nizams:**\n\n*   He served directly under two Nizams — **Asafjah IV and Asafjah V** — and his relationship with both was marked by contradictions.\n*   In his early years, every major initiative he took was met with suspicion from the ruler, who perceived him as a British agent. Even at the height of his authority, whenever Salarjung attended the Nizam''s court, he would humbly announce his own entry — \"prevesapettu\" — behaving like an ordinary servant seeking permission to enter, a telling sign of the ambiguity in his position despite his real power over the administration.\n*   On at least **three occasions — 1859, 1860, and 1867** — he was on the verge of losing his position, and a fourth time faced deportation, but the British intervened in his favor each time:\n    *   In 1860, British Resident **Colonel Davidson** intervened to let him continue as Diwan.\n    *   The same occurred in 1867, with British Resident **Yule** intervening.\n*   With the death of Nizam **Afzal-ud-Daula** in 1869, his three-year-old son **Mahbub Ali Khan** became ruler, and the British appointed **Salarjung and Kabir Bahadur** as co-regents to the young Nizam — giving Salarjung sufficient time to implement reforms successfully and act as **de facto ruler** of the kingdom.\n*   During the rule of his father-in-law Siraj-ul-Mulk, Berar had been given to the British, creating distrust among the people toward the Salarjung family. Salarjung worked intensely to regain public trust, and — because of his loyalty to the British during the 1857 revolt — all regions **except Berar, Raichur, Darashivam, Osmanabad, and Sholapur** were returned to the Nizam, partly removing the earlier distrust.\n\n**Relations with the British:**\n\n*   His relationship with the British was also marked by extremes: he was honoured with the title of **Knight Commander of the Star of India (KCSI)** by the Queen, yet on one occasion was threatened with deportation to the Madras Presidency.\n*   The relationship was governed by the shifting unity or conflict of interest between the colonial rulers and the native government: his support to the British during the 1857 disturbances pleased them, and his later infrastructural measures received colonial support when they suited British interests — but on issues where the British perceived a threat to their power, he was opposed by the Residents. \n\n*   Notable contentious issues included the restoration of cotton-surplus Berar to Hyderabad (Berar remained with the British) and his unsuccessful attempts to modernize the armed forces.\n\n*   In all, during his thirty years as Prime Minister, he was threatened four times with dismissal by the Nizams, including once with deportation. He submitted his resignation four times but always withdrew it. He survived **two assassination attempts**.\n\n**Death and assessment:**\n\n*   He died suddenly on **8th February 1883**, at the age of **53**, officially ascribed to cholera.\n*   He was trained under an English teacher, **\"Dieton,\"** whose guidance provided a base for his personality and his ability to work continuously under hard circumstances.\n*   When he took up the Prime Ministership in 1853, the state was in a fall of chaos and corruption; under these circumstances, he brought economic and administrative stability to the state on all fronts, paving the way for industrialization in Hyderabad.\n*   The **University of Oxford** honoured him with the title **\"Doctor of Civil Law.\"**\n*   Historian **Abdul Waheed Khan** described Salarjung as \"one of the best statesmen of 19th century Hyderabad,\" while **William Diggbe** held that his reform measures led to the \"renaissance of Modern Hyderabad.\"", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter14-salarjung-reforms-p12", "partName": "Summary", "bodyMd": "Salarjung got the loan due from the Nizam to the British waived, secured the return of regions taken by the British, and expanded the extent of the kingdom. He took possession of the Diwani lands, cancelled debts on mortgaged lands, removed the Jagirdars from tax collection, and introduced new tax policy in the Diwani region. \n\nHe introduced the **Ryotwari** and **Zilabandi** policies in 1865, established the **Board of Revenue**, and appointed Ministers of Revenue, Police, and Law. He established the **High Court** and reformed the judicial institution, appointed talented youth to key government posts, and brought reform to the Transport, Railways, Postal, and Telegraph departments — transforming the Nizam kingdom into a modern state, and making his role in nineteenth-century India a unique one, poised between tradition and modernity.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p1", "partName": "Dalhousie and the Doctrine of Lapse", "bodyMd": "**Lord Dalhousie''s Arrival and Expansion Policy:**\n\n*   Dalhousie took charge as Governor-General of India on **12 January 1849**, in his 35th year\n*   From the moment he arrived, he pursued a policy of territorial expansion\n*   Under Company rule, states had fallen into three categories: independent states, subordinate-allied states, and dependent states; Dalhousie scrapped this arrangement and introduced three new classifications instead\n*   Independent states such as **Berar, Sikkim, and Punjab** were defeated in war and absorbed into the British Empire\n*   Contingent forces were stationed on large states like the Nizam''s, and their most fertile territories were seized as payment\n*   Dependent states such as Ayodhya were annexed on the pretext of misgovernance\n*   The pensions of the rulers of **Karnataka and Thanjavur** were stopped, and the Mughal Emperor himself was reduced to a British pensioner\n*   Dalhousie''s policies brought comfort only to the British while plunging India into turmoil\n*   He left India heartbroken on **5 March 1856**, suffered from heart trouble, and died in a state of destitution on **19 December 1866**\n\n**Two Systems of Inheritance:**\n\n*   Two inheritance principles were in practice in India: **\"Warasin\"** and **\"Janshin\"**\n*   Under **Warasin**, when a king died, the kingdom passed to his sons or close blood relatives\n*   Under **Janshin**, a family without natural heirs could adopt a son and pass on authority to him\n*   Dalhousie abolished the Janshin system, and by doing so annexed numerous states\n*   This policy of non-recognition struck at the roots of many kingdoms, and dissatisfaction spread across the country like a forest fire as a result\n\nDalhousie''s abolition of the adoption principle led directly to a wave of annexations that fuelled nationwide resentment:\n\n*   The ruler of **Jhansi**, Gangadharrao, died in 1858; Jhansi state was under Peshwa rule\n*   Since Gangadharrao had no children, he had adopted a boy; Dalhousie rejected the petition for permission submitted by his wife, **Rani Lakshmibai**, for this adoption to be recognized\n*   In 1855, the Karnataka Nawab **Mahammad Gaur** died; orders were issued denying his heir a pension\n*   In 1851, the second Peshwa **Bajirao** died; while he was alive, the British had arranged a pension of 8 lakh rupees per year for him\n*   They decided this pension would not continue for his adopted son, **Nanasaheb**\n*   In 1851, the Raja of Thanjavur died; while alive, he had declared his second daughter as his heir, since women were not eligible to inherit paternal property\n*   Thanjavur was accordingly annexed\n*   **Sambalpur** was among the earliest states to face this treatment\n\nFearing that they too might one day be swallowed up in this manner, other states — even those not directly threatened by these policies — grew apprehensive and were fully prepared to rebel when the moment came.\n\n**Economic Fallout:**\n\n*   As so many states were abolished at once, people employed there in various occupations became unemployed\n*   Artists and scholars dependent on royal patronage were left without support\n*   Meanwhile, Company officials, aiming to bring farmers into the army, raised land taxes excessively", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p2", "partName": "Background — The Fifth Nizam and the Political Climate of 1857", "bodyMd": "*   **Afzal-ud-Daula** of the Asaf Jahi dynasty became the 5th ruler of Hyderabad State in 1857, inheriting **Salar Jung** as his exceptionally able Minister\n*   The fifth Nizam ruled for only 12 years — the shortest reign of any Nizam — yet this brief period saw some of the most consequential events in the state''s history\n*   His accession coincided with a powerful explosion across India: the 1857 Revolt\n*   By this point the British had taken more than a hundred years to secure their position in India, and their authoritarian administration had generated deep dissatisfaction among conservative Indians, described as resembling \"fire covered by water\"\n\n**Grievances Under British Administration:**\n\n*   Peasants lost their lands and became laborers; craftsmen were forced to abandon their customary professions\n*   Employees became unemployed, and the revenue administration left deep dissatisfaction in Indian village institutions\n*   Corruption in the Revenue and Police Departments caused widespread suffering\n*   The British kept the local rulers and kings at a distance, and the Princely States (Samasthanams) were crushed under British administrative control\n*   Dalhousie''s succession system (Doctrine of Lapse) evoked deep distrust among local rulers, as it was seen as a tactic to annex kingdoms and Samasthanams\n\n**Symbolic Grievances:**\n\n*   The Mughal sovereign **Bahadurshah Zafar** was forcibly removed from the Red Fort and ordered by Dalhousie to spend his time in Mehrauli\n    \n*   Lord Canning proclaimed Bahadurshah Zafar the last Emperor, declaring his successors would not inherit his titles — a severe blow to Mughal self-respect after nearly 300 years of rule\n    \n*   Rulers of princely states became suspects in British eyes\n    \n*   Hindus feared religious conversion by Christian missionaries operating through education\n    \n*   Indian soldiers (Sepoys) faced torture and humiliation, and there was resentment over differences in promotions, pay, and allowances between British and Indian soldiers\n    \n*   This accumulated dissatisfaction reached a flashpoint, and the Enfield rifle cartridge controversy provided the spark\n    \n*   Mutiny broke out first in Meerut, and lacking time to organize a system to contain it, the British faced battles across North India, with help for the rebels coming from rulers of princely states", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p3", "partName": "The Delayed Onset of Revolt in Hyderabad", "bodyMd": "*   Unlike North India, the revolt did not break out instantly in Hyderabad, though people showed extreme displeasure toward the British there too\n*   The South was strategically sensitive for the English Government, which remained watchful of antagonism building in Hyderabad against the British\n\n**Early Armed Resistance:**\n\n*   Alija and his associates Sadasiva Reddy and Raja Mahipatram had earlier attempted armed rebellion at different times\n*   This resistance was connected to the origins of **Mubariz-ud-Daula**, son of Nasiruddaula\n\n### The Wahabi Movement''s Influence\n\n*   Initiated by **Abdul Wahab Nejdi** of Saudi Arabia, the Wahabi movement aimed to resurrect the greatness of Islam and entered India in 1820 with Peshawar (Afghanistan) as its center\n*   **Sayyed Ahmed Brailvi** started the movement in India, opposing English rule and Christian influence\n*   In Hyderabad, the movement was carried on by Mubariz-ud-Daula and his followers, and by **Ghulam Rasool Khan**, Nawab of Kurnool, against the British\n*   Mubariz-ud-Daula was caught and imprisoned; his followers, though also imprisoned, were released before 1857 but had already succeeded in spreading anti-British sentiment\n\n### Resentment\n\n*   **Berar**, part of the Nizam''s state, was taken over by the English in 1853 as debt repayment, badly damaging the Nizam''s finances — the British needed the cotton-rich region to feed textile mills in England\n*   The early losses of **Kurnool and Berar**, combined with lingering resentment over the death of Tipu Sultan in Mysore, deepened anti-British feeling\n*   Parallel unrest elsewhere fed the mood: the **Mopla Muslims** revolted in Kerala inspired by the Wahabis, and Muslims of Madras considered following suit; followers of **Tantiya Tope**, disguised as mendicants and saints, moved through Hyderabad preaching against the British\n*   Many soldiers lost jobs as state army branches were disbanded, breeding hatred toward the British; some nobles also showed hostility", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p4", "partName": "The Strategic Stakes for the British", "bodyMd": "*   Among nearly 500 princely states in the country, Hyderabad was the largest, occupying a position described as the \"heart of South India\"\n*   The British feared that if revolt broke out in the Nizam''s kingdom, it could turn the region from Nagpur to Madras into a \"fire furnace\"\n*   With 15 per cent of Hyderabad''s population Muslim, and large Tamil, Kannadiga, and Marathi communities present, the British feared that if the Nizam supported the rebel cause, the entire Muslim population of South India might revolt and Marathwada, Madras, and Mysore could follow\n*   Unless the Nizam actively helped crush the revolt, they believed the wider uprising could not be contained\n*   The Resident is recorded as worrying that \"if Nizam was lost, then everything was lost\"\n\n**British Strategy:**\n\n*   The British closely monitored the Nizam''s decisions and worked to keep him aligned with them\n*   They promised to write off his loan and return Berar once the revolt ended\n*   In response, the Nizam declared his trustworthiness, and even at his death Nasir-ud-Daula advised his son to remain loyal to the English\n*   Through this maneuvering by Salar Jung, Hyderabad was kept safe for the British from the mutineers", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p5", "partName": "The Hyderabad Army in 1857", "bodyMd": "By 1857, the Hyderabad Army had been considerably reduced:\n\n| Force | Strength | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main battalion | ~30,000 men | In disorder, weak discipline, mostly hired soldiers |\n| Hyderabad Contingent | 10,000 soldiers | Organized, trained, armed; commanded by ~80 European officers |\n| Nizam''s Army (native soldiers) | Not more than 30,000 men | Under Jamedars, mostly Sikhs, Arabs, and Rohillas; not used regularly |\n\n*   The **Contingent Army** was stationed across Elichpur, Aurangabad, Mominabad, Gulbarga, Hingoli, Bollaram, Warangal, Maktal, Lingasugur, and the hilly tracts of Buldana, moving between locations as required", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p6", "partName": "The Jihad Posters Episode", "bodyMd": "*   Within a month of Afzal-ud-Daula taking the throne, provocative posters appeared on the walls of the capital, spreading across mosques and populous areas\n\n**Contents of the Posters:**\n\n*   Declared that delaying the killing of a \"Christian Kafir\" would result in excommunication from Muslim society\n    \n*   Urged Tahaniyat Ali Khan Bahadur and Afzal-ud-Daula to gather Muslims, suppress the \"Kaffirs,\" and march to Delhi with the promised support of Allah and the Prophet\n    \n*   Called on the king and Diwan to join the movement against Christians in the name of the Prophet, or else \"wear bangles\" and face humiliation, and warned that non-participants would be reduced to road repair workers within a year\n    \n*   Claimed a diktat of the Prophet demanding public reading of the notice, threatening that seven generations of descendants would be cursed for non-compliance\n    \n*   Called for Maulvies to issue Fatwas compelling Zamindars and Qazis to declare war on the English\n    \n*   Declared that anyone dying in the cause — Poor, Rich, Maulvi, Qazi, Mufti, Kotwal, or Subedar alike — would become a Ghazi and find a place in the sacred world\n    \n*   Set a date: all Muslims were to congregate at **Mecca Masjid** on Friday, 25th Shaval 1273, and proceed from there\n    \n*   The rage stirred against the English united Hindus and Muslims at this juncture, and the British grew fearful, moving to mobilize their forces", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p7", "partName": "Rebellion by the Aurangabad Contingent Force", "bodyMd": "**Timeline:**\n\n1.  **May 1857** — Due to the critical condition in North Central India, the British mobilized as many men as possible; the Third Cavalry of Hyderabad was sent to Malegaon at the request of the Bombay Government\n2.  **11 June 1857** — The First Cavalry under Capt. **H.D. Abbott** was sent from Mominabad to Aurangabad as a replacement\n3.  When the First Cavalry reached **Chota Pimpalgaon** (14 km from Aurangabad), the horse-riders suspected a conspiracy to send them far from Aurangabad, deepening their doubts\n4.  They reached Aurangabad and were lodged in **Kala Chabutra**; Captain Abbott, seeking to verify the mood, learned from **Risaladar Mohammed Buran** that Aurangabad was not their final destination and longer travel awaited\n5.  The furious cavalrymen chanted the Mughal Emperor''s name and shouted the slogan **\"Deen ke upar kamar nahi bandenge\"** (we won''t gird our loins against religion), refusing to go beyond the Nizam''s boundary\n6.  **14 June 1857** — Abbott called a meeting of all Jamedars and ordered the Cavalry shifted to **Amba**; Cavalry and Infantry were lodged separately near the river Kaum\n    *   Officers sent their families to safety and sought help from Poona and Maggur\n    *   Secret parleys and signals were exchanged between Infantry and Cavalry, and Burhanbooks of the Third Cavalry informed Abbott that soldiers had waved a green flag\n7.  **24 June 1857** — Additional forces under **Major General Woodburn** arrived from Ahmednagar: 14 light dragoons, cannons with European gunmen, and 25 Bombay Infantry\n\n**Suppression:**\n\n*   Mutineers were lined up for inquiry; **Jamedar Amir Khan**, first named, caused commotion among the soldiers, and Woodburn opened fire\n*   Many cavalrymen of the First and Second Cavalry were declared guilty and disarmed — Amir Khan escaped in the chaos\n*   **Dafedar Fida Ali** fired at Abbott but missed, and was later hanged after trial\n*   The leaders of this uprising, named elsewhere as **Ameer Khan and Waheed Khan**, had a reward of **2,000 rupees** each announced by the British Government for their capture.\n\n**Casualties/Punishment:**\n\n*   One Risaladar, three Jamedars, nine Dafedars, 76 Troopers, and four Trumpet blowers were involved\n*   Of these, 21 persons were fired upon and killed and three were shot with guns\n*   Some were exiled, some whipped, and some removed from service; the disloyal regiment divisions were disarmed, released, and sent away\n\n**Aftermath at Buldana:**\n\n*   Cavalrymen of the Third Cavalry at Buldana grew angry on hearing these developments\n*   The Third Cavalry, under **Lt. Lenox**, was confronted by **Capt. Gall** with 25 Bombay Native Infantry, causing outrage\n*   Thirteen persons escaped from Buldava under **Jamedar Chidakhan**, reaching Hyderabad after 20 days in July, hoping the Hyderabad Government would shelter them\n*   Instead, the Government announced a **reward of 3,000** on Chidakhan\n*   When the group entered Hyderabad, **Salar Jung arrested them** and sent them to the Residency for trial, along with Chidakhan''s followers", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p8", "partName": "Attack on the Residency", "bodyMd": "**Backdrop:**\n\n*   News of Chidakhan''s arrest spread rapidly; people congregated in mosques with emotional speeches\n*   Salar Jung dispersed them using Arab troops but warned the Resident that the Residency might be attacked\n*   Organizers sent four Maulvies to the King demanding Chidakhan''s release, threatening to attack the Residency if refused\n*   When their demand went unmet, an attack was planned for **17 July 1857**\n\n**Defensive Preparations by Salar Jung and Col. Davidson:**\n\n*   Contingent troops in Hyderabad were retained only to the extent required, with the rest removed\n*   Six cannons and one Cavalry squad were shifted to the Residency\n*   The day before the attack, additional armory was mobilized: three cannons, two mortars, one detailed Cannon firemen unit, one cavalry troop, 25 troopers of the Madras Light Infantry, and 200 foot soldiers of the Madras Regiment\n*   Every entry point of the Residency was fitted with bayonets\n\n**Officer Assignments:**\n\n| Officer | Role |\n|---|---|\n| Lt. Col. S.C. Briggs | Military Secretary, overall control |\n| Capt. George Holms | Control of Madras Horse Artillery |\n| Capt. Allen Scott | Command of Hyderabad Contingent guns |\n| Capt. Bradley, 24th Regiment | Protection of Madras troops |\n| Major Thornhill & Capt. Campbell | Volunteer assistants to the Resident |\n\n*   Col. Davidson effectively turned the Residency into a Fort\n*   Salar Jung warned Davidson that a crowd assembled at Mecca Masjid might proclaim Jihad and begin rioting around 1 p.m. on Friday 17th July — this did not occur as feared\n*   At about 6 p.m., Salar Jung sent a spy to inform Davidson that 500 Rohillas under **Turrebaz Khan** and **Allauddin** were advancing toward the Residency\n\n**The Attack:**\n\n*   At 6 p.m., **600 armed men** marched on the Residency, led by **Jamedar Turrebaazkhan**; roads leading there turned into what is described as a sea of militant mutineers\n*   Every Residency gate was closed and patrolled\n*   Rohillas occupied two structures on the western side of the Residency — belonging to traders Jaigopal Das and Abbin Sahib — and also the area toward Dilsukh Gardens\n*   From these positions, they urged the Indian guards at the Residency Gate to secure Chidakhan''s release, expecting the guards to join them\n*   **Risaldar Ismail Khan** instead came forward to oppose the militants; surprised by his loyalty to the \"foreign masters,\" they fired at him, but he escaped\n*   Residency Commander Briggs''s strategy was not to attack the militants that night; the Arab Guards regiment sent by Salar Jung surrounded the occupied buildings\n*   Rohillas broke open adjoining walls and launched a ferocious attack, during which the **Putlibowli gate collapsed**\n*   Firing continued for about half an hour; Briggs retaliated with cannon fire, continuing until **4 a.m.**\n*   Under the severity of the retaliation, the Rohillas escaped into the darkness, aided by the Arab Guards\n*   **32 men of Madras Artillery died** of gunfire and injuries\n\n**Aftermath:** Militant leaders Turrebaz Khan and Maulvi Allauddin were declared offenders with a reward of 5,000 on them.\n\n*   **Turrebaz Khan**, while on the run, was arrested on 22nd July at Mogulaguda and produced before the Karmat Ali Magistrate Criminal Court, where Civil Court Magistrate Fazlulla recorded his statement\n    *   While being transferred to Andaman under a life sentence, he escaped at Mogiligidda in Mahbubnagar district, and was rearrested near Toopran with the help of Kurban Ali\n    *   At the time of his final capture, Turrebaz Khan refused to be taken alive and drew his sword to strike at Salar Jung''s soldiers; seeing his fearsome bearing, the soldiers did not hesitate and shot him down\n    *   His body was beheaded, and the head was bound with garlands and paraded to the main gate of the Residency as a trophy — a display meant to demonstrate to the public that the revolt had been crushed\n*   **Maulvi Allauddin** fled to Bangalore, was caught at Mangalapalli, tried, sentenced to life imprisonment, and sent to Andaman on 28th June 1859; he died there in 1884\n\nThe attack on the Residency ultimately **failed for lack of cooperation from the countrymen**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p9", "partName": "The Rebellion and Conspiracy in Different Places of the State", "bodyMd": "The rebellion did not end with the failed attack on the Residency — disturbances continued in different parts of the State until 1860, fuelled further by the agents of **Nana Sahib**, **Tantiya Tope**, and **Raosahib Peshwa**, who went among the people preaching against the British.\n\n### Revolt of Raja Venkatappa Naik (Shorapur)\n\n*   Shorapur, an area under Gulbarga''s control, was ruled by descendants of the Bedar dynasty\n*   **Philips Meadows Taylor** was appointed political administrator and protector of Raja Venkatappa Naik after his father died while Naik was still a minor\n*   In 1853, once Naik was no longer a minor, Taylor handed over the reins of the Samasthanam and went to Hyderabad\n*   Shorapur was initially a Maratha area before coming under Nizam control\n*   During the 1857 revolt, Naik expected to become independent of both the Nizam and the British; **Nanasahib and the Zamindar of Raichur promised him help** and that he would be made king of the area from Shorapur to Rameswaram\n*   Motivated by these promises, the Raja took part in the rebellion, assembling a battalion of six hundred Rohillas and Arab fighters\n*   He ignored Col. Davidson''s letter demanding he cancel the mobilization; Davidson sent **Campbell**, whose order to disband the army was also disregarded\n*   To suppress the Raja, Campbell sent **Windhum and Col. Malcolm**; the battalion disintegrated, and the Raja fled to Hyderabad to meet Taylor, where he was caught\n*   **Col. Davidson awarded him the death penalty**, later commuted to life imprisonment through Taylor''s intervention; while being transferred to Chengalput near Madras, he committed suicide by shooting himself with a soldier''s gun\n\n### Rebellion by Malked Koppal\n\n*   Led by **Bhimrao**, Jamindar of Mundargi (also Tahsildar of Ballari, removed for bad conduct) and **Kenchan Gowda**, Desai of Hummigi — both located north of the Tungabhadra\n*   Bhimrao rebelled alongside the Desais of Sartur, Dambal (Srinivas Venkatadri), Hummigi (Kenchan Gowda), and Govinkos (Survada Goud)\n*   Bhimrao gathered 400 men under the pretext of digging a pond and obstructed British tax collection\n*   On investigation, the Collector of Bellari found large quantities of ammunition; using it, the rebels robbed the **Dambal Treasury** and destroyed government property in Gadag\n*   The British sent forces to Dharwad, Raichur, and Bellary; **Major Hews besieged Koppal Fort**, joined by forces of Major Pindar of Hyderabad and Lt. Taylor of Raichur\n*   Bhimrao, Kenchan Goud, and their accomplices **died in the battle of Koppal Fort on 1 June 1858**; 150 persons were jailed, subsiding the mutiny\n\n### Rebellion of Nargund Zamindar\n\n*   **Bhaskara Rao**, Zamindar of Nargund, rebelled with the aim of becoming independent, arranging a small battalion and killing British officer **Manson**\n*   He maintained good relations with Nanasahib; Jamindars of Bombay and Karnataka planned to rebel alongside Samasthana rulers on **27 May 1858**\n*   The British crushed the rebellion, arrested Bhaskara Rao, and took control of his Zamindari\n\n### The Case of Conspiracy by Rangarao\n\n*   In 1858, alongside the mutiny of Bhils and Gonds, a major conspiracy formed involving **Rangarao** (an agent of Nana Sahib), **Raja Deep Singh**, Zamindar of Koulas, and **Safdar-ud-Daula** — all connected to **Rao Rambha Nimbarkar**, a high official in the Nizam''s court\n*   Their plan was to launch a rebellion in the name of Nana Sahib Peshwa, but they lacked financial resources\n*   While the British were crushing the Rohillas, the conspiracy came to light: letters written by the Kaulas Raja and Safdar-ud-Daula were recovered during searches, including one from **Sonaji Pandit** (Daftardar under Roy Rayan of the Nizam''s court) addressed to Nana Sahib in Kanpur\n*   Sonaji Pandit was removed from service; he had been sending letters to Nana Sahib via **Rangarao Phage**, who in turn relayed letters from Nana Sahib to Zamindars, Patels, and Kulkarnis\n*   After Sonaji Pandit''s death on **26 February 1859**, Rangarao continued delivering letters to Hyderabad; en route, he met Deep Singh disguised as a doctor and secured his sympathy, then went to Maniknagar and met the Patels\n*   The British grew suspicious and arrested him at **Ankalska**; he had wanted to build an army of 10,000 men for Nana Sahib\n*   Rangarao, the King of Kaulas, and Safdar-ud-Daula were arrested and tried: **Rangarao was sent to Andaman**, **Safdar-ud-Daula received life imprisonment**, and **Rajdeep Singh received a three-year prison term**\n\n### Mutiny by Rohillas (Basmatinagar and Nirmal)\n\n*   Rohillas created disturbances in Basmatinagar and Nirmal, and Bhils did so in Ajanta, looting and setting fire to properties\n*   Serious riots occurred in **1858–59** in Ajanta, crushed indiscriminately by **Hewroj**\n*   Sporadic opposition continued until 1860, requiring continuous British deployment\n*   Rohilla leaders ultimately claimed to be operating under the leadership and control of **Nana Sahib**, moving village to village and displaying posters in populated areas\n\n### Ramji Gond Rebellion\n\n*   Another Rohilla-linked rebellion occurred at **Nirmal** in Adilabad district, led by **Ramjigond**\n*   He freed neighboring areas of Adilabad, made Nirmal his capital, and ruled\n*   A battle took place between the Collector''s forces and Rohillas/500 Gond troops at Nirmal in **April 1860**; many Rohillas and Gonds died in the firing\n*   Their leader Ramjigond escaped but was caught and tried, sentenced to death by hanging\n*   Along with him, his **one thousand associates were hanged from a banyan tree**, which became popularly known as _''veyyi urula marri''_ (banyan tree of a thousand hangings)\n\n### Mutiny by Bhils\n\n*   In 1857, Bhils rebelled in the Ajanta area under the leadership of **Bhagoji Nayak**, continuing attacks and burglaries through 1857\n*   **Vaijapur Deshpandes** assisted them in the burglaries; Contingent forces eventually prevented the burglaries and repressed the Bhils\n\n### The Case of Jahangir Khan\n\n*   After successfully repressing the 1857 mutiny, Col. Davidson went to Calcutta, where the Governor General sent a gift (\"kharita\") to the Nizam via the Resident\n*   While returning after presenting the gift on **15 March 1859**, **Jahangir Khan** fired at the Resident with a gun (missed), then attacked with a sword; the Resident''s guards overpowered him, and Jahangir Khan died due to torture by the security forces\n*   Some accounts place this attack against the backdrop of the honors the British were conferring on the Nizam around this period\n    *   When the Governor-General''s official letter/gift was being carried toward the Nizam, **Jahangir Khan**, a Rohilla warrior, viewed this display as a grave insult to the Nizam''s dignity and launched a furious attack on the Resident''s party\n    *   Owing to unfortunate circumstances, the attack is said to have struck the wrong target — a Nizam official standing near the intended mark — rather than the Resident himself\n    *   Though captured, Jahangir Khan reportedly showed no remorse for any punishment that might follow, and the true depth of the conspiracy behind him was never fully traced\n\n### The Kukatpalli Episode\n\n*   In **April 1859**, Army Officer **Kaston Macintray** was attacked by a returning soldier who pierced him with a sword; the soldier was caught and hanged without trial or inquiry\n*   Investigation revealed soldiers hostile to the British planning rebellion against both the Nizam and British — **Ajmat Jung, Mirdachand, Baajikhan, and Maulvi Ibrahim**\n*   Col. Davidson told the Nizam to exile Ajmat Jung, alleging he and Roshanuddaula (the Nizam''s brother) were trying to kill the Nizam\n*   **Mirdachand**, an officer in charge of the Chobdar branch under the Nizam administration, was not a direct participant but had been observing the Nizam''s movements; he had participated in the 17 July 1857 attack on the Residency and helped soldiers who wounded Brigadier Mekanjie at Bollaram Cantonment, and was a follower of Jahangir Khan\n*   **Baajikhan** sheltered mutineers in his house; **Maulvi Ibrahim** was trying to subdue the English people. All four were arrested and tried.\n\n### The Case of Conspiracy of Bhir\n\n*   **Bala Sahib**, a relative of Pratap Singh (king of Satara, whose kingdom had merged with the British Empire under the State Annexation Policy), came to Deccan with followers to start a rebellion, indulging in robbery and vile acts\n*   **Devrao Kishan, Vamanrao Kishan, Vithalrao alias Ambaji, Bhir Deshmukh, and Srinivas Sankarrao Deshpande** attempted rebellion against the British under Balasahib''s leadership and were sentenced to rigorous imprisonment after trial\n\n### Rao Sahib Peshwa (Begum Bazar Conspiracy)\n\n*   **Rao Sahib Peshwa** secretly came to Hyderabad in 1862, hiding in Begum Bazar after the failure of the rebellion in the North, protected by local people and traders\n*   Revolutionary **Kishan Rao** was already hiding in Begum Bazar; the two mobilized forces and presented shawls to those who joined them\n*   Associates included landlord **Rukmareddy** of Mortad (Armoor Taluka, Nizamabad district), priests **Lingayya and Antayya** and **Baldev Prasad, Madhura Prasad, Ganesh Singh** of Balamukund area (who worked in the British Army), and an anonymous lady the British could never identify despite extensive searches\n*   Many were arrested; **Rukmareddy, Tuljaram, and Ratansingh** were caught — Ratansingh escaped but committed suicide by poison when recaptured\n*   Rao Sahib and Kishanrao escaped; **Seth Mohanlal Pithi** hid Rao Sahib as a monk in a Gosai Mutt under Muslimjung bridge (he had earlier also rescued Puranmal, Turrebaz, and Maulvi Allauddin)\n*   The British arrested Seth Mohan Lal and Puranmul, fining them **75,000 and 10,000** respectively\n*   This episode is known as the **Begum Bazar conspiracy case**\n*   When Rao Sahib was traced to Narsingi in Medak District, police caught only a follower — the leader escaped again, but **Rao Sahib was ultimately caught in Kanpur and hanged in August 1862**\n\n### Khalki Conspiracy Case\n\n*   **Rama Rao alias Jung Bahadur** arranged hundreds of followers for the King of Satara, issuing them appointment letters (_Kaulnama_)\n*   The Government arrested his followers **Rama Rao, Bhimrao, Balakrishna, and Vithoba**, sending them to Andaman\n\n### Episode of Vasudev Balvant Phadke (1879)\n\n*   Noted Maratha rebel **Vasudev Balvant Phadke** toured the border districts of Gulbarga in 1879, requesting help from the Rohillas\n*   The Bombay Governor sent a telegram informing the Resident at Hyderabad; the Resident appointed **Abdul Haque** as Special Officer\n*   Phadke was arrested, tried, and sentenced to **seven years of rigorous imprisonment**\n\n### Rekapalli Rebellion\n\n*   Central districts had no restriction on Podu cultivation, taxed at four annas per axe; the **Madras Government enhanced this tax threefold**, imposing atrocious taxes on certain trees and causing severe hardship\n*   A rebellion broke out in **Rampa**; rebels attacked the Vaddegudem Police Station with help from **Ambulreddy**\n*   The Government sent forces in a steamer from Rajahmundry to punish them; discovering the steamer carried no arms, the rebels attacked it\n*   Police patrolling with 120 soldiers was introduced on the Godavari river, stopping the attacks\n*   As the rebellion subsided, the Government handed over local administration in **October 1880 to Tammidora**, who instead looted villagers\n*   Rebels from Rampa joined forces with Tammidora and influenced people in Rekapalli, together looting the **Palvancha Taluka treasury** and destroying neighboring police stations\n*   The Madras Government wrote to the Hyderabad Government warning that the Rampa rebels might reach Hyderabad from Rekapalli; the Collector of Khammam confirmed arrangements were made to arrest them\n*   **Salar Jung issued a farmana** on the Resident''s orders, naming Ramareddy, Ambulreddy, Kakurreddy, Bundel Dora, and Tammidora for arrest, with a reward of Rs 200\n*   Police surveillance was increased at both Madras and Hyderabad; the rebels escaped capture but stopped their activities under the pressure of enhanced security, and the rebellion **subsided within one year**\n\nEven after the formal end of the 1857 mutiny, its effects lingered in Hyderabad State for nearly twenty years, with the suppressed dissatisfaction among Makhtedaars, officers, and Zamindars continuing to surface through armed rebellions and, eventually, the National Movement for Independence.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p10", "partName": "Reasons for the Failure of the Rebellion", "bodyMd": "Lack of competent leadership, unity, and organizational strength among the rebels, combined with the Nizam and Salar Jung siding with the British, meant the rebellion in Hyderabad ultimately failed.\n\n**British Rewards to the Nizam and Salar Jung for Loyalty:**\n\n*   **Raichur, Antarvedi, and Naldurg** districts — taken from the Nizam in 1853 to maintain the Hyderabad Contingent — were returned; the British retained only Berar\n*   The **Shorapur Estate** was given to the Nizam\n*   A debt of **50 lakhs** was forfeited\n*   A new agreement was signed in **1860**, suitably modifying the original 1853 agreement\n*   The Nizam was gifted **ten thousand pound sterling**; Salar Jung received presents worth three thousand sterling\n*   The Nizam, in turn, sent gifts to the Governor General valuing fifteen thousand pound sterling\n*   The British announced the title **''The Star of India''** for the Nizam — though people opposed this and protested through posters across the city against the Nizam and the Diwan", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p11", "partName": "Result of the Mutiny", "bodyMd": "**On the Mughal Empire:**\n\n*   With the mutiny crushed, the Mughal Empire — which had existed only in name — formally ended; the last Mughal Emperor was sent to Burma (Rangoon)\n*   The Governor General advised the Nizam to introduce his own currency and coins; Nizam Bahadurshah initially refused to mint currency during his lifetime, but the Governor General did not yield\n*   Coins called **''haali sikka''** were released, bearing ''Nizam-Ul-Mulk Asafza Bahadur'' on one side and ''Julus Mymanath Jarb Farkunda Buniyad Hyderabad'' on the other\n*   **Khutba** was thereafter read in the name of Afzaluddaula, not the Emperor\n*   The post of Mughal Subedar in the Hyderabad Nizam''s Court was **abolished**\n*   The Nizam was pressured into declaring Hyderabad a **sovereign state**, marking the effective end of Mughal authority — though the word \"independence,\" meaningful in 1724, had become meaningless by 1858, since the Nizam remained far from a \"free bird\" under continuing British oversight\n\n**On British Policy Toward Princely States:**\n\n*   The British realized they needed to change their attitude toward the Samasthanams\n*   It was announced that the rights, special powers, dignity, and respect of Indian rulers would be treated as matters of honor for the British Government itself\n*   Lands belonging to mutineers were forcibly confiscated; titles and lands were instead granted to those who remained loyal to the British during 1857, as recognition of their loyalty and valuable service\n\n**Honors Conferred:**\n\n*   The Nizam was addressed as a trustworthy friend — **''Faithful Ally''** and **''Yaare Waffadar''**\n*   He was awarded the title **''Grand Commander Star of India''** on **30 August 1861**, along with the knighthood of the **''Most Exalted Order''**\n*   As part of the same honors, Resident **Davidson** presented the Nizam with a **portrait of Queen Victoria** in a specially arranged durbar — a gesture that, for a time, fostered in the Nizam the impression of being treated on equal footing with the British monarchy, though this sense of parity soon proved illusory as British interference only deepened afterward\n\n| Title/Honor | Recipient | Date/Detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| Grand Commander Star of India | Nizam | Conferred 30 August 1861 |\n| Faithful Ally / Yaare Waffadar | Nizam | Trustworthy friend address |\n| Presents worth three thousand sterling | Salar Jung | For loyalty during 1857 |\n\n**Long-Term Impact:**\n\n*   Even when the Government in England took direct control from the East India Company as a result of the mutiny, the relationship between the Nizam and the British saw no fundamental change\n*   British interference in Hyderabad''s affairs increased further\n*   The functioning of the Nizam''s \"sovereign\" state was restricted; at minimum, the sovereign ruler did not even have the right or power to appoint or remove his own ministers", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p12", "partName": "The Paloncha Samsthan Episode", "bodyMd": "*   In 1842, when the head of the **Paloncha Samsthanam** rebelled, the British seized the Samsthanam into their own territory without the Nizam''s knowledge\n*   As a mark of gratitude for the Nizam''s loyalty during the 1857 revolt, this Samsthanam was later returned to the Nizam\n*   The outstanding contingent dues of **50 lakhs** owed by the Nizam were also written off", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p13", "partName": "Salar Jung''s Later Power Struggle with the British", "bodyMd": "*   Despite the extensive help Salar Jung had rendered to the British, they were unwilling to see him grow too powerful\n*   Complete administrative authority resting in one man''s hands ran counter to British political interests\n*   With the sixth Nizam, **Mir Mahbub Ali**, still a six-year-old minor, the British appointed a rival co-guardian (contingent-in-charge) — **Shams-ul-Umra** — to check Salar Jung''s authority\n*   When Shams-ul-Umra died in 1877, the British appointed another rival in his place, **Vikar-ul-Umra**\n*   Salar Jung strongly opposed this appointment; the British warned him: \"If you do not accept this, we will arrest you and send you to Madras\"\n*   Faced with no alternative, Salar Jung was ultimately compelled to accept\n*   Whatever the case, in an era when the British had removed many rajas and nawabs who opposed them from power, it was the Nizams alone who managed to protect their kingdom by staying in the good graces of the British", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter15-1857-revolt-in-hyderabad-p14", "partName": "Change in Residency–Nizam Protocol After 1857", "bodyMd": "*   Once the crisis of 1857 had passed, the British began to conduct themselves with full authority\n*   Until 1857, whenever the Resident came to the Nizam''s court, he would remove his shoes and bow low, offering repeated salutations, out of deference to the Nizam; all such courtesies now ceased\n*   In official correspondence, the Governor-General had addressed the Nizam as **''Niyaz-mand''** (a humble petitioner) while the Nizam addressed the Governor-General as **''Mabadaulath''** (your lordship) — these forms of address were dropped entirely\n*   The Nizam''s hope of one day attaining a status equal to the British crown was thoroughly shattered\n*   From this point on, the Nizam became a wholly subordinate ally of the British", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p1", "partName": "Gadwal Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Gadwal was the **oldest Samsthan**, situated between the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers. It was separated from Raichur district and later joined Mahbubnagar district, ruling for nearly **600 years** before merging into Andhra Pradesh state in **1956**.\n\n**Geography and Resources**\n\n*   Situated 1,063 feet above sea level.\n*   Lakes, wells, and ponds were the main water sources.\n*   Had 360 villages, bordered by Alampur Taluk (east), Tungabhadra river (south), Raichur district (west), and Krishna river (north).\n\n**Administration**\n\n*   Annual income was 10 lakh rupees; annual expenditure was Rs. 7,31,308.\n*   Rulers had authority over civil and criminal judgements and could appoint their own administrative staff.\n*   Maintained infantry, cavalry, and artillery forces.\n*   Historic and spiritual villages under the Samsthan included Pudur, Vallur, Rajavolu, Aija, Maldakal, Chagadona, Beechupalli, and Sri Rama Avadhuta Agraharam.\n\n**Dynasty and Origins** The rulers belonged to the **Mustipalli dynasty** and were Pakanati Reddis who followed the Veera Vaishnava tradition and built many temples. Construction of the Gadwal fort began in the 17th century and continued into the early 18th century. Pudur was the first capital, with Chenna Keshava Swamy as the presiding deity — it appears in gazettes as Keshavapuram. Pudur gained prominence during the Chalukya period; Chalukya-Pallava wars fought here were won using the _Gadha_ (mace) and _Walu_, giving the town its earlier name Gadavala, which later became Gadwal.\n\n**Founder — Polani Reddy alias Buddha Reddy** He was a great warrior and expert in war strategy and politics, appointed as ruler of Manalibeludoni, Raichur, Alampuram Adavani, Aija, and Mosalakallu by the Kakatiya ruler Pratapa Rudra. Between 1686–87, his descendants extended the kingdom by winning Nandyala, Siriguppa, Ahobilam, Siddapuram, Bandi Atmakur, and Sirivella, and defeated the Kandenavolu Nawab, seizing his flag and emblem. They held the titles **Nadagowda** and **Sir Nadagowda** until 1600 AD.\n\n**Succession**\n\n| Ruler | Period | Key Contribution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raja Shobhanadri | up to 1712 | Founder of Gadwal Samsthan; built a well and fort in Aija; built Ramalingeswara Temple in Kasi (1712) |\n| Kalla Venkanna | to 1719 | Successor ruler |\n| Ramanaiah (caretaker) | 1719–1724 | Appointed by Shobhanadri''s queens |\n| Ammakkamma (1st wife of Shobhanadri) | 1724–1725 | Ruler |\n| Lingamma (2nd wife of Shobhanadri) | 1725–1738 | Developed the Samsthan; built the Beechupalli fort''s foundation, Sangala and Tandrapati ponds, and Lingamma well; adopted Tirumala Raya |\n| Raja Tirumala Rao | 1738–1742 | Completed the Beechupalli fort |\n| Mangamma (1st wife) | 1742 (brief) | Ruler |\n| Rani Chokkamma (2nd wife) | 1742–1747 | Built the Anjaneya Swamy temple in the Nizam''s fort at Beechupalli |\n| Raja Rama Rao (adopted, brother-in-law) | 1746–1762 | Merged Boravelli villages with Gadwal; defeated Rajaprolu Zamindars, captured 8 villages; defeated the Kurnool Nawab and seized his Dhakka and Flag; built the Chenna Keshava Swamy Temple at Bethendla |\n| Raja Chinna Somabhupal | 1762–1793 | Title _Bhupalu_; defeated Upperu and Gangapalle rulers, merged Dharur Paragana with Gadwal; built the Keshavalaya shikaram, Ramalayam (1779), and Bhudevi temple (1788) |\n| Raja Chinna Ramabhupal | 1793–1806 | Resolved a border dispute with Raichur''s Hanumantha Desai and Narasinga Rao with Nizam''s help |\n| Bhupala (son-in-law) | after 1806 | Became ruler |\n| Rani Venkata Lakshmamma (3rd wife of Sitaram Bhupal) | 1844–1865 | Built Keshavalaya Gopuram, Mahadwaram (1849); adopted Ramana (son of Venkatapuram Gomam Krishna Reddy) with Nizam''s permission, naming him Raja Ram Bhupal |\n| Maharani Adi Lakshmi Devamma | last ruler | Her elder daughter married the Domakonda King; Gadwal merged with Hyderabad State on **17 September 1948** |\n\n**Literature** Gadwal Samsthan actively patronized poets and writers. **Pedda Soma Bhoopal** began the tradition of felicitating poets and singers in the month of Magha. Notable court poets and their works included:\n\n*   **Kanadam Peddana Somayaji** — wrote the Balakanda of the Ramayana under the title _Bhadra Parinayam_.\n*   **Keeriti Venkatacharyulu** — wrote Sanskrit works _Alankara Koustubham_, _Bhava Shatakam_, _Jhunja Marutam_, _Shringaralahari_, _Hayagreeva Dandakam_, and the masterpiece _Achalatmaja Parinayam_.\n*   **Kama Samudram Appalacharyulu** (titled _Vidvatkavikunjara_) — wrote _Krishna Leela Tarangini_ and the Kishkinda Kanda of the Ramayana.\n*   **Karnamadakala Ananthacharyulu** — wrote _Acharya Vimshati_, _Andhra Shabda Chintamani_, _Madana Vijaya Banam_.\n*   **Kottapalli Ramachandra Charyulu** (titled _Vidvanmaha Kavi_) — wrote verse and interpretations of the Ayodhya Kanda.\n*   **Gargyapuram Subba Shastri** (titled _Ubhaya Kavita Nissahaya Sahiti Dhureena_) — wrote verse and interpretations of the Aranyakanda.\n*   **Hosadurgam Vedanthacharyulu** (Administrative Officer) — wrote _Acharya Vaibhavam_, _Sri Krishna Brahma Tantrarya Vedapadastavam_.\n*   **Pullagummi Venkata Charyulu** (master of Raja Seetarama Bhoopala Rao) — wrote _Alamkaravali_, _Adilakshmi Karnapurnam_, _Andhra Vyakarana Sutravali_.\n*   **Pullagummi Ahobila Charyulu** (an eminent astrologer) — wrote _Abhinavatidhi Valli_, _Druksiddha Grahaganitam_, _Surya Siddantasaaram_.\n*   **Chetluri Narayana Charyulu** (Scientific Officer) — wrote _Pratapa Rudreeyasaram_.\n*   **Puranam Deekshacharyulu** — wrote _Ramachampuvu_, _Ramanrupakarnapurnam_, _Kavyotkarsha_, _Keshava Suprabhatam_.\n*   **Puranam Narasimha Charyulu** — engaged in a scholarly argument with Tirupati Venkata Kavulu; wrote _Janaki Parinayam_, _Sri Rama Bhupa Charitra_.\n*   **Gadepalli Veeraraghava Sastri** — wrote _Sri Satyavaralakshmi Dhruva Charitram_, _Sri Rani Satyavaralakshmi Ramayanam_, _Tripurantaka Sthala Mahatyam_.\n*   **Adipudi Prabhakara Kavi** — wrote _Poundarikam_, dedicated to Gadwal Chenna Keshavaswamy.\n*   **Pokuri Kashipathi** (court poet of Rani Adilakshmamma) — wrote _Sarangadhareeya Nirdhostya Nirvachanam_, _Shuddandhra Harishchandropakyanadyanakendra_.\n*   **Peddamandadi Venkata Krishna Kavi** — wrote _Nirvachana Bhakti Vijayam_, _Maruti Vilasam_, _Gadwal Samsthan Charitram_, _Reddykula Nirnayachandrika_, _Keshava Vilasam_.\n*   **Sri Rama Narasimha Murthy Kavulu** (twin poets, in Prince Krishna Bhoopala''s court) — wrote _Nirupamana Deeksha_, _Sri Madhandra Bhagavatam_.\n*   **Sri Dharmavaram Krishnamacharyulu** — awarded the title _Andhra Nataka Pitamaha_ by the Gadwal rulers; wrote many books.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p2", "partName": "Wanaparthy Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Wanaparthy Samsthan covered a total area of **450 sq. miles** with **124 villages**. Its first capital was Nuguru, and the Samsthan was initially known as Nuguru Samsthan; the first ruler, Ramakrishna Rao, later shifted the capital to Wanaparthy, after which the Samsthan took that name.\n\n**Dynasty** The rulers carried the surname **Janumpally** and belonged to the Motati Reddy caste and Penubala Gotram, residing initially at Kurnool. The founder was **Veera Krishna Bhoopati Reddy alias Veera Krishna Reddy**.\n\n**Succession**\n\n| Ruler | Key Details |\n|---|---|\n| Veerakrishna Reddy | Native of Janumpally (Nandyala Taluk, Kurnool); ruled parts of Nandyala as a satrap of the Vijayanagara kings; later occupied villages around Panagallu Fort in Mahaboob Nagar and ruled Nuguru as capital |\n| Vemudi Venkata Reddy | Fourth generation from Veera Krishna Reddy; praised by Golconda Nawab Abdullah Qutub Shah in 1633 after occupying Nellore and Udayagiri forts for the Nawab with a 10,000-strong army; received additional villages as reward |\n| Gopala Rayalu | Son of Venkata Reddy; held the title _Bahiri_ |\n| Venkata Reddy (adopted) | Had no male heir; Gopala Rayalu adopted him. He fell into debt, failed to pay impost to the Nizam Nawab, was defeated by the Nizam''s army, and died by suicide out of humiliation. He was given the title _Sawai_ by the Delhi Sultan; built the village Gopalpet in honour of his foster father Gopala Rao and named his own son Gopal Rao; appointed his brother Ranga Reddy as ruler of the newly built Gopalapet with 12 adjoining villages |\n| Ramakrishna Rao-I | Popular ruler; jailed for three years by the Nizam due to a conspiracy by his cousins, later released on discovery of the truth; received the title _Raja Bahadur_ from the Nizam in 1817; shifted the capital from Nuguru to Wanaparthy |\n| Raja Rameshwara Rao (adopted) | Provided welfare facilities to his people; maintained cordial Nizam relations and assisted in battles; presented with a Sword, Pistol, and Rifle by the Nizam Government in 1861; introduced a permanent tax system on the British model |\n| Raja Ramakrishna Rayalu (adopted) | Died suddenly |\n| Rameshwara Rao-II (adopted) | Efficient administrator; the Samsthan reached its peak under him — implemented welfare schemes, dug wells and ponds for agriculture, and strengthened law and order |\n\nRaja Rameshwara Rao had two sons: the elder, **Sri Krishnadeva Raya**, married Sarala Devi (daughter of Munagala ruler Raja Venkata Ranga Rao); the second, **Sri Ramadeva Raya**, married Kumudini Devi (daughter of Pingali Venkata Ramareddy). Sarala Sagar, a large pond, was constructed in Sarala Devi''s name. After Krishna Devaraya''s death, disputes arose between Rani Sarala Devi and Raja Ramadeva Raya, which relatives and close friends resolved. Sarala Devi''s son, **Raja Rameshwara Rao**, was an educationist and, after the Samsthan''s merger, became a **Member of Parliament**.\n\n**Literature**\n\n*   **Raja Bahari Gopalarayalu** (titled _Asta Basha Kavi_, described by historians as _Asta Basha Gopala Raya_) — wrote _Ramachandrodayam_, _Sringara Manjari Banam_.\n*   **Veera Raghavacharyulu** (court poet) — wrote a 1770 commentary on the Sanskrit Bhagavatam titled _Veera Raghaveeya Vyakya_.\n*   **Akshintala Subba Sastri** (resident of Recherla village, 19th century) — wrote _Bhashyardha Ratnamala_; a great stoic in theology.\n*   **Singampalli Narasimha Siddhanti** (astrologer) — wrote _Bhoobramana Bhranthi Nirasana_.\n*   **Archakam Ayyamacharyulu** (Peddamandadi village) — wrote _Peddamandadi Chennakesava Satakam_ and _Rameshwara Vijayam_.\n*   **Hosadurgam Krishnamacharyulu** (judicial officer) — wrote _Rameshwara Vijaya Champavu_ (a Prabhantham on the Samsthan''s ruler), along with _Alankara Maniharam_, _Unnmatta Pandavam_, _Keshavostava Malika_, _Prasannananda Lahari_, _Raghunatha Vijayam_, _Sri Krishna Pancha Ratnamala_, _Srinivasa Vilasam_, _Sri Ramayana Vaibhavam_.\n*   **Sannidhanam Suryanarayan Sastri** — dedicated his _Khanda Kavyam Puvvula Thota_ to Wanaparthy ruler Late Sri Krishnadevaraya; Krishna Devaraya''s wife Sarala Devi assisted in its publication.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p3", "partName": "Amarachintha (Atmakuru) Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Atmakuru Samsthan, in Mahaboob Nagar district, was earlier known as **Amarachinta Samsthan**, covering an area of 190 sq. miles under the Amarachinta and Vaddemanu (Vardhamanu) paraganas. Tivudampalli was the first capital, before the rulers later shifted to Atmakur.\n\n**Dynasty** The rulers belonged to the **Pakanati Reddy dynasty** and carried the surname **Mukkera**. The founder was **Gopala Reddy**.\n\n**Succession**\n\n| Ruler | Key Details |\n|---|---|\n| Gopala Reddy | From Chandragiri (near Tirupati, Chittoor district); served Gona Buddha Reddy at Tirupati, who in 1292 made him ruler of Vardhamanapuram |\n| Chinna Gopi Reddy | Son of Gopal Reddy; became ruler of Atmakur Samsthan; in 1363 obtained the Sarnadha Gowda Watan region of Panchamahal Taluk as _sanad_ from Katabhupala; assisted the Bahamani Sultans and received the posts of Nadegowda, Sarnadha Gowda, Deshmukh, Deshpandya, and Desaigiri in Ferozegad (Muzaffar Nagar Paragana) |\n| Chandra Reddy | Son of Chinna Gopi Reddy; a great devotee, built the Kurumurthi Temple |\n| Gopi Reddy-II | Grandson of Chandra Reddy; contemporary of Golconda Sultan Abdullah Qutub Shah; fought on behalf of the Golconda rulers, defeated Gopala Rao who had occupied Gurramkonda fort, and jailed him; received the Allipuram Jagir and permission to maintain 500 munsabdari and 200 sawaris; obtained Amarachinta and Vardhamanapuram. His brother **Saheb Reddy** was appointed ruler of Kadechur and Magatha regions by the Nizams |\n| Sarva Reddy | Became ruler after Gopi Reddy |\n| Sarva Reddy-II (grandson) | Assisted Aurangzeb''s army, received the Duppalli Jagir, Flag, Nagara, 500 sawaris, and royal emblems |\n| Timma Reddy-I (son) | Received cooperation from the Peshwa rulers of Maharashtra, who gave him Amarachinta and Vaddemanu Paragana on annual contract of 50,000; Raja ChandraSena built Chandragad and Dharmapuram villages in his wife''s name and made Timma Reddy Deshmukh of those villages |\n| Saheb Reddy (great-grandson of Timma Reddy) | In 1748, during the establishment of the Asaf Jahi Kingdom, helped Asaf Jahi rulers in the Carnatic wars and received the title _Sawai_ |\n| Pedda Venkata Reddy (7th generation from Saheb Reddy-II) | Contemporary of Asaf Jahi ruler Sikander Jha; close to the Sultan; obtained Amarachinta and Vaddemanu Paraganas with the help of Deewan Chandulal; shifted the capital from Tivudampalli to Atmakur town |\n| Balakrishna Reddy | Son of Pedda Venkata Reddy; became ruler |\n| Soma Bhoopal Rao | Son of Balakrishna Reddy; received Amarachinta and Vaddemanu Paraganas as permanent Bilmukhth on payment of Rs. 52,201 (raised to 70,000 during the period of Prime Minister Salarjung) |\n| Raja Sitarama Bhoopal Rao | Son of Soma Bhoopal; maintained friendly relations with the Nizam rulers |\n| Sawai Raja Sri Rama Bhoopal Balwant | Son of Sitarama Bhoopal; developed Atmakur |\n| Bhagyalakshmamma | Wife of Sri Rama Bhoopal Balwant; last ruler; after her, the Samsthan was merged into Hyderabad state |\n\n**Literature**\n\n*   **Surapuram Keshavaiah** (c. 1760) — prominent poet; wrote _Niroshtya Ramayanam_, dedicated to Atmakur ruler Sri Raja Somabhoopal.\n*   **Tirumala Bukkapatnam Rangacharyulu** — wrote _Gunaratnakara_, _Padmini Parinaya Champuvu_, _Geervana Kriti_.\n*   **Tirumala Bukkapatnam Srinivasa Charyulu** (titled _Tarka Teertha_, _Balasaraswati_) — wrote _Jambhavati Parinayam_, _Tatva Marthanda Prabha Mandalam_, _Durmada Nirmadhanam_, _Nanjaraju Champavu_, _Rajasekhara Charitra_, _Surapura Venkataguru Charitra_.\n*   **Deekshitula Narasimha Sastri** (given the title _Mahakavi_ by Amarachinta rulers) — wrote _Lakshmana Parinayam_, _Nidurashwatta Mahatyam_, _Sri Ranga Mahostava Varnamalika_, _Satyanarayana Vratakalpam_.\n*   **Harithi Deeksha Charyulu** — wrote _Alankara Manimala_, _Acharya Vimshati_, _Mahantharlapamala_.\n*   **Poet Arishanapalamu Venkatadwari** — wrote _Laxmi Sahasra Vyakhyanam_. Other poets included Tirumala Venkatacharyulu, Tirumala Bukkapatnam Butchi Venkatacharyulu, and Mudigonda Nagalinga Sastri.\n*   **Deekshitula Papa Sastry** — wrote _Chenchu Natika_, _Hanumath Charitra_.\n*   The rulers patronized **Tirupati Venkatakavulu**, who dedicated their book _Srinivasa Vilasam_ to Sitarama Bhoopal; poet **Karyamapudi Rajamannaru** wrote _Sri Sitarama Bhoopala Vilasam_ at the rulers'' request.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p4", "partName": "Jataprolu (Kollapur) Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Part of Mahboob Nagar district, Jataprolu Samsthan had **9 villages** across **191 sq. miles**, with an annual income of **2 lakh rupees**. Its rulers served as satraps of the Vijayanagar kings, Golconda Sultans, and Asaf Jahis. After the capital shifted from Jataprolu to Kollapur, the Samsthan came to be known as **Kollapur Samsthan**.\n\n**Dynasty** The rulers were **Padmanayakas**, from the royal families that ruled Devarakonda and Rachakonda, carrying the surname **Surabhi** and belonging to the Recharla gotra. The dynasty''s founder was **Chevvi Reddy**, popularly known as **Bethala Nayaka**.\n\n**Succession**\n\n| Ruler | Key Details |\n|---|---|\n| Sarvangyasinga Bhoopala Surabhi | Founder of the Padmanayaka Dynasty |\n| Mahipati | Belonged to this dynasty |\n| Surabhi Madhava Rayulu (5th generation) | A great scholar; wrote _Chandrika Parinayam_; received the Jataprolu Samsthan as a gift from the Vijayanagara kings |\n| — | His sons received the Jataprolu Samsthan as a _sanad_ from Golconda King Abdulla Qutub Shah in AD 1650 |\n| Surabhi Laxmana Raya | Shifted the capital from Jataprolu to Kollapur in 1840; rulers thereafter were called Kollapur Kings |\n| Laxmi Jagannadha Rao (son of Raja Laxmana Rao) | Ruled 1851–1854; received the titles Raja Bahadur and Nizam Nawaz Wanth from the Nizam |\n| Venkata Jagannadha Rao | Last ruler; during his regime, Kollapur Samsthan merged with Hyderabad State |\n\n**Literature**\n\n*   **Madhava Rayulu** and his son **Narsing Rao** were both great poets — Madhava Rayulu wrote _Kshatrakhelanam_, and Narsing Rao wrote _Shudrasmriti_.\n*   **Chintapalli Gopala Mantri** (17th century) — felicitated by the Samsthan rulers for his Sanskrit work _Arya Satakam_.\n*   **Veera Raghava Kavi** — wrote _Madhura Vani Vilasam_.\n*   **Akshintala Subba Sastri** (1807–1871) — wrote an explanation to the _Brahma Sutrabhasyam_ titled _Dhashyardha Ratnamala_.\n*   **Akshintala Singara Sastri** — wrote _Annapurnastakam_, _Dwadasha Manjari_, _Bhaskara Khandam_, _Srisaila Mallikarjuna Ratnapanchakam_.\n*   **Yunayavalli Krishnamacharyulu** — wrote _Astaprasarama Satakam_ (Sanskrit), _Niroshtya Krishna Satakam_, _Jataka Chandrika Vyakya_, _Rasagyanandam_, _Sri Krishna Champuvu_.\n*   **Vellala Sadashiva Sastri** (1861–1926) — on the orders of ruler Surabhi Laxmaraya, wrote a noted commentary on Madhava Rayulu''s _Chandrika Parinayam_; other works include _Kavyalankara Sangraha Vimarshanam_, _Andhra Dasharupaka Vimarshanam_, _Veerabhadriya Khandanam_, _Velugoti Vamsha Charitra_, _Surabhi Vamsha Charitra_, _Kanteerava Charitra_, _Ramachandra Charitra_, _Namireddy Charitra_, _Yatininda Nirakanaram_, _Ramanuja Gopala Vijayam_.\n*   **Sridhara Krishna Sastri** — wrote _Sri Rama Mandahasam_.\n*   **Hosadurgam Krishnamacharyulu** — wrote _Laxmi Vilasa Champuvu_, _Madanagopala Mahatyam_.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p5", "partName": "Papannapeta Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Papannapeta was the **oldest Samsthan in the Nizam State**, established during the reign of Delhi Sultan Feroze Shah Tughlaq. The entire Methuku region was part of the Samsthan and it continued in that form until 1707; its size subsequently reduced under Mughal rule, the Deccan Subedars, and later under the Asaf Jahi regime.\n\n**Succession**\n\n| Ruler | Key Details |\n|---|---|\n| Rani Shankaramma | Belonged to the 12th generation of the Samsthan''s rulers, from which its recorded history is available; mistress of Raja Venkata Narasimha Reddy; took charge after her husband''s demise. An able administrator and warrior, she raised a large army and repeatedly defeated the Maratha Army that forcibly collected Chauth and Sardeshmukhi taxes from the public, earning accolades from the Nizam kings, who presented her the title **Raibagan** (\"lioness\") and a royal emblem — the title gradually changed to Saibagan and Raibegum. She dug ponds and irrigation canals for agricultural development, and, having no male heir, adopted Sadashiva Reddy of Domakonda Samsthan |\n| Sadashiva Reddy | Popular ruler and great warrior; his mistress **Parvathi** (daughter of the Gadwal ruler) was herself an expert in warfare and fought alongside him as bodyguard, saving his life several times. In 1795, when the elder son of Asaf Jahi ruler Nizam Ali Khan revolted with the help of Galib Jung and Sadashiva Reddy, the Nizam''s army — initially defeated by Sadashiva Reddy — returned with French forces'' assistance, defeated him, and took him captive; he and Parvati were imprisoned and killed there. His brother ruled the Potlacheruvu Samsthan, and his son **Narasimha Reddy** succeeded him. Rulers of this Samsthan held the title **Char Hazar** |\n| Raja Venakta Durga Reddy | Ruled in the early 20th century; after his sudden demise, the Samsthan came under the control of the Court of Wards until 1927 |\n| Rani Venkata Laxmamma Sardesai (widow) | Took charge after the Court of Wards period |\n| Raja Ramachandra Reddy | Became the final ruler; **Papannapet Samsthan merged into Hyderabad State in 1948** |\n\nThe Samsthan''s rulers built forts at Sangareddy, Velamakanne, and Ramayampet.\n\n**Literature**\n\n*   **Rani Venkata Laxmamma Sardesai** — a great poetess; wrote _Manasa Bodha_.\n*   **Feelkhana Shankara Rayakavi** (court poet) — wrote _Sugnana by Deepa Guru Geethas_.\n*   **Chidere Laxmana Sastri** — wrote _Medak Samsthandheeswara Charitra_.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p6", "partName": "Domakonda Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Domakonda Samsthan was part of Nizamabad district, initially called **Bikkavolu Samsthan** (Bikkavolu is today''s Biknur). Its rulers served as satraps to the Golconda Nawabs and Asaf Jahi rulers. **Rajanna Chowdhary** shifted the capital from Bikkavolu to Kamareddypeta, and his son **Rajeshwara Rao** later shifted it from Kamareddypeta to Domakonda, after which the Samsthan took its name.\n\n**Dynasty** The rulers were **Pakanati Reddis**, close relatives of the Gadwal and Atmakur rulers. Belonging to the Kaminedu dynasty, they were called **Kamineni**, with Rachulla as their gotra and Bikkavolu Siddarameshwara as their presiding deity. The Nizams, impressed by the Kaminedu''s services, gave them responsibility of Bikkavolu (in the Metuku region); Kamineni was renamed **Kamireddy**, giving rise to Kamareddi town.\n\n**Succession**\n\n| Ruler | Key Details |\n|---|---|\n| Kacha Reddy-I | Ruled Rayadurgam |\n| Reddy Bhoopati alias Bhogi Reddy (son) | Mistress: Rani Dechambika; had three sons — Kacharreddy-II, Malla Reddy, Bomma Reddy |\n| Kacharreddy-II | Wife Machamamba; son Yella Reddy |\n| Kacharreddy-III (son of Yellareddy-Mallamamba) | Had four sons: Jangama Reddy, Kama Reddy, Yella Reddy-II, Malla Reddy |\n| Kama Reddy | In AD 1636, obtained the Domakonda Samsthan as _sanad_ from the Golconda rulers |\n| Malla Reddy | Great devotee; given many titles by the Golconda Sultan; many villages (Yella Reddy, Macha Reddy, Kamareddy, Jangampalli) are named after this line of rulers and exist today |\n| China Kamireddy (son of Yella Reddy) | Held the title Deshmukh |\n| Pota Reddy (grandson) | Held the title Desai |\n| Rajanna Chowdhary (son) | A strong ruler who shifted the capital from Bikkavolu to Kamareddy; survived an army sent by the Nizam after enemies complained against him, and thereafter maintained cordial relations with the Nizam, who was impressed by his war skills |\n| Rajeshwara Rao-I (son) | Shifted the capital from Kamareddy to Domakonda; helped the Nizam in a war against the Chennur rulers, and was appointed ruler of Bikkavolu and Chennur; the Samsthan grew significantly under his regime |\n| Pedda Rajeshwar (son) | Close friend of the Nizam''s Prime Minister Chandulal; had no children and appointed his brother Anna Reddy as ruler |\n| Anna Reddy | Ruler of Domakonda Samsthan |\n| Raja Ramachandra Rao (son of Raja Umapati) | Ruled Domakonda during 1927; married the daughter of the Gadwal ruler |\n| Raja Someshwara Rao | Last ruler; his son Krishna Ram Bhoopal went to Gadwal on adoption; during his rule, **Domakonda Samsthan merged with Hyderabad State** |\n\n**Literature**\n\n*   **Pattametta Somanatha Somayaji** (Kamareddy''s court poet) — wrote _Sootasamhita_, _Brahmottara Khandam_, _Vishista Andhrokti_.\n*   **Lakshmipathi** — wrote _Sri Krishna Vilasam_, _Acharya Vijayam_, _Sri Madhupakyonam_, _Neela Vivaham_.\n*   **Adipudi Prabhakara Kavi** — wrote _Umapatabhyudayam_ on the instructions of the Domakonda ruler, comprising 286 prose and poetry sections, in which the Kamineni dynasty''s history was recorded.\n*   **Seshadri Ramana Kavulu** and Domakonda court poet **Pedda Mandadi Venkata Krishna Kavi** — jointly wrote _Reddy Kula Nirnaya Chandrika_.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p7", "partName": "Munagala Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Munagala Samsthan, also called Munagala Zamindari or Munagala Paragana, is one of the **oldest Samsthans**, located in Nandigama Taluk of Krishna district. In 1959, after the formation of Andhra Pradesh, it was made part of Nalgonda district for administrative convenience, covering an area of around **100 sq. miles**.\n\nDuring the last Kakatiya rulers, Rudrama Devi and Pratapa Rudra, the Samsthan''s army chiefs — **Mukundappa, Surappa, and Narasanna** — were highly popular; no further history of the Samsthan is recorded until the 18th century. In 1790, during the battle between the French and the East India Company, ruler **Keesara Lachhamma Rao** provided military assistance to the British, who felicitated her for it. In the early 20th century, the Samsthan''s income was one lakh twenty thousand rupees, and its rulers bore the title **Jabdatulakran**.\n\n**Succession**\n\n| Ruler | Key Details |\n|---|---|\n| Gurlapati Ayanna Desai | Ruled 1652–1693; his son died during this period |\n| Daughter-in-law (of Keesara family) | Unable to handle affairs, handed responsibility to her brother Mukundappa |\n| Keesara Mukundappa | A great warrior; assisted Mughal King Aurangazeb in Deccan battles and got Munagala as _Sanad_ |\n| Narasanna (son) | Ruled briefly after his father''s death |\n| Lacchayamma (wife of Narasanna) | Great politician and efficient administrator; the Samsthan saw great development under her regime |\n| Venkata Ramanna (son of Lacchayamma) | — |\n| Venkata Narasimha Rao (elder son) | Assisted the British during 1802 and received Sanade Malkiyat and Istemirar |\n| Kodanda Ramaiah (son) | Died in 1814; the Samsthan was under Court of Wards for four years (1814–1818) |\n| Venkata Narasimha Rao (came to power 1818) | Ruled 18 years till death in 1836; no children — adopted a boy named Kodanda Ramaiah; the Samsthan again went under Court of Wards for 14 years |\n| Kodanda Ramaiah (adopted) | Became ruler in 1850, ruled only 4 years, died 1854 |\n| Rukkamma (wife) | Ruler 1854–1873 |\n| Lacchamma Rao (daughter of Rukkamma) | Took charge in 1873; husband Deshmukh Nayani Venkataramaiah; ruled efficiently for 19 years till her death in 1892; her adopted son Sri Nayani Venkata Ranga Rao was only 13 at her death, so the Samsthan again went under Court of Wards |\n| — | The Keesara family contested in court that Venkata Ranga Rao''s adoption was invalid; per the court''s orders, Ratnavaram and Tellabelli villages of Munagala Paragana were handed over to them |\n| Raja Nayani Venkata Ranga Rao | Got charge of Munagala Paragana, became ruler in 1900; his Diwan Komarraju Venkata Laxmana Rao brought many reforms |\n\nRaja Nayani Venkata Ranga Rao was a great patron of the Telugu language. Although Munagala Samsthan was part of British India, Ranga Rao''s literary and cultural activities were carried out in the Nizam State — he helped establish the **Sri Krishna Devaraya Andhra Bhasha Nilayam**, reconstructed the Maha Linga Temple in the Samsthan''s village Tadwai, and brought the inscribed rock pillars of the Cheruku Reddy dynasty to Nadigudem fort for preservation. He had two sons, Ramakrishna Reddy (elder) and Gopala Krishna Reddy (younger), and a daughter, **Sarala Devi**, who married Wanaparthy ruler Raja Krishnadeva Raya. In **1947, Munagala Samsthan was merged into Madras State**; Raja Venkata Ranga Rao died in 1958.\n\n**Literature**\n\n*   **Varada Raja Nandikeshwara Kavi** — wrote _Shivatatva Sudhanidhi_, _Sethu Mahatyam_, _Rameshwara Kshetra Mahatyam_.\n*   **Komarraju Ramalinga Kavi** — wrote _Veerabhadra Satakam_, _Srigiri Malleswara Satakam_, _Veerabhadra Karunya Nidhi_.\n*   **Komarraju Venkata Shivudu** — wrote _Repala Rajalinga Satakam_ (127 seesa poems).\n*   **Komaraju Venkata Lakshmana Rao** (Diwan of Munagala Samsthan) — established the _Vignana Chandrika Grantha Mandali_ and edited _Andhra Vignana Sarvaswam_ as a researcher.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p8", "partName": "Paloncha Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Paloncha, in present-day Khammam district, was popularly known as **Shankaragiri Samsthan**. Before the separation of Khammam district from Warangal, it extended across 6 Taluks with an area of **800 sq. miles**; being a forest area, its population and income were limited. Its rulers were satraps of the Nizam kings.\n\nRekapalli and Bhadrachalam were originally part of this Samsthan. Bhadrachalam was initially with the Nizam State, later passed to British possession in 1860, joining the Central Province, and in 1874 became part of Madras State. During the initial period, the Samsthan received 5,000 Peshkas from the Nizam (raised to 18,000 in 1935); its income was 6 lakh rupees and it paid forty thousand rupees as annual impost.\n\n**Origins** During the regime of Golconda king Mohammed Quli Qutub Shah, his army chief **Ashwarao**, son of Kalamalayam of the Padmanayaka dynasty, was highly popular. Paloncha rulers descend from Ashwarao. Paloncha, Bhadrachalam, and Ashwaraopet were the capitals of his descendants; they lost control of the Samsthan for a time, during which it was ruled by the Kandimalla, Tandra, Jalagam, Damera, and Mutyala families. The Rekapalli Samsthan (8,000 peshkas) passed to the Mareddy dynasty, belonging to Vararamachandrapuram.\n\n**Succession**\n\nThe Paloncha rulers, descendants of the Padmanayaka dynasty, served as army chiefs and cavalry heads under the Kakatiya ruler Pratapa Rudra.\n\n*   **Appanna** — ruler of Shankaragiri and Husnabad during 1324; his descendants continued as satraps of the Musunuri, Padmanayaka, Bahamani, and Golconda Sultans until 1698.\n*   The 18th-generation ruler received **Badshahi Munsabdari** from Aurangazeb.\n*   **Zaffer-ud-Doulah**, Army Chief of the Moghuls, murdered Paloncha ruler **Narasimha Ashwa Rao** in 1769 and robbed the treasury; after this, Paloncha became a Samantha (feudatory) Kingdom of the Asaf Jahis. **Raja Narasimha Ashwa Rao and Ramachandra Ashwarao** ruled the Samsthan for some time thereafter.\n*   In **1796**, **Venkata Rama Narsimha Apparao** received the titles Raja Bahadur and Sawai Munsabdar, along with a Flag, Nagara, and Nobuth, and permission to maintain a cavalry of 2,000 and infantry of 3,000.\n*   **Narasimha Ashwarao** succeeded him as ruler. Settipalli Zamindars filed a court case to claim the Paloncha Samsthan, dragging on until 1858 and financially weakening the Samsthan as a result.\n*   **Sri Sita Rama Chandra Sawai Ashwa Rao**, with the help of Hyderabad Diwan Salarjung-I, won this case and became ruler; however, during his regime the Samsthan fell further into debt, and the British took possession of the Bhadrachalam and Rekapalli regions. Unable to bear the debt burden, he died.\n*   **Lakshmi Narasayamma** (his mother) took over the Samsthan. Due to non-payment of Rs. 6,00,000, a court order allowed the lender to collect taxes for 12 years and take possession of the Samsthan; the Nizam paid Rs. 3,00,000 to the lender and, for the remaining balance, handed over the villages of Malluru and Ramanuja Varam. Rani Lakshmi Narasayamma died in 1875.\n*   **Parthasarathi Apparao** (her adopted son) ruled Paloncha efficiently and received many titles from the Nizam.\n*   **Vijaya Apparao** was the last ruler; the Samsthan merged with Hyderabad State after the **Police Action**.\n\n**Literature**\n\n*   **Srinathuni Venkata Rama Kavi** — the Samsthan''s first poet; wrote _Sri Rama Pattabishekam_ (also called _Ashwaraya Charitra_).\n*   **Raja Parthasarathi Apparao** (titled _Sahitya Visharada_) — a great literary patron.\n*   **Sri Kottapalli Venkatarama Lakshmi Narayana Sharma** (his court poet) — honoured Krishnamurthy Sastri with 1,000 rupees and a stoned ring for translating the Aadi and Sabha Parvas of the Mahabharata; also wrote _Paloncha Samsthan Charitra_.\n*   **Sri Raja Vijaya Apparao** patronized the _Andhra Vangmaya Samithi_, established at Burgampadu and Paloncha.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter16-samsthanas-in-hyderabad-state-p9", "partName": "Boravelli Samsthan", "bodyMd": "Boravelli was a popular Samsthan of Telangana. Its rulers were **Pakanati Reddys**, belonging to the Middimalla Gothram, carrying the surname **Mushtipalli**, with **Polukanti Someshwara** as their presiding deity. This Samsthan merged with **Gadwal** after a few years.\n\n**Succession**\n\n| Ruler | Key Details |\n|---|---|\n| Nadagoudu Tamma Reddy | First ruler; ruled the Aija region with Boravelli as capital; wife Machamma |\n| Nalla Reddy (son) | Two wives — Mallamba and Timmamba |\n| — | Children of Mallamba: Pedda Soma and Chinna Soma. Children of Timmamba: Tammareddy, Papareddy, and Mallamba |\n| Pedda Soma | Married Lacchamamba; son Venkata Reddy, who wrote the composition _Raghavabyudayam_ and ruled the Samsthan for a few years |\n| Raja Thirumala Rao | Ruled the Samsthan 1738–1742 |\n| Chokkamma (wife) | Ruled 1742–1747 after Thirumala Rao''s death |\n| Rama Rao (adopted, brother-in-law of Chokkamma) | Came to Boravelli on adoption; Chokkamma called him to Gadwal and asked him to take over the kingdom and protect her sons. He adopted Raja Chinna Soma Bhupal and Raja Chinna Rama Bhupal, sons of Rani Chokamma, and **merged Boravelli Samsthan into Gadwal Samsthan** |\n\n**Literature**\n\n*   **Garudachala Kavi** — wrote _Kausaleya Charitram_.\n*   **Veera Raghava Kavi** — wrote _Madhura Vani Vilasam_.\n*   **Sanjeeva Kavi** — wrote _Chandragadopakyanam_.\n\n* * *\n\nThe Samsthanas that began under Kakatiya rule continued through the Qutub Shahi and Asaf Jahi periods before finally merging into India. Their rulers introduced numerous welfare programmes, constructed lakes for agricultural development, and built temples. They were also the first to introduce printing machines in Telangana, and the newspapers this enabled brought public awareness and drew people towards the independence movement. As patrons of literature who encouraged scholars and poets, they contributed significantly to the development of the Telugu and Sanskrit languages and to Telangana''s culture more broadly — some of their constructions were destroyed over time, while others were developed into educational institutions or remain as tourist spots today.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule-p1", "partName": "Administrative Hierarchy under Asafjahi Rule", "bodyMd": "*   Nizam-ul-Mulk Asafjah separated the Deccan from the Mughal Empire, ruling under his own centralized control while nominally retaining regard for the Mughals.\n*   Appointed Mansabdars without Mughal consent — his own Mansabdars were called **\"Aasifias\"**.\n*   His heirs did not continue Mughal traditions.\n*   Capital shifted from **Aurangabad to Hyderabad in 1764**.\n*   **1724–1762**: internal conflicts among heirs shaped State formation.\n*   **1762–1803**: **Nizam Ali Khan''s** reign brought internal stability — expansion of forts, army, and administrative buildings.\n*   The resulting upper administrative class was called **Stately Officers (Prabhuvargam)**.\n\n### Jagirdari System\n\n*   **Jagir** meant a right ensuring permanent income through land — the practice of honoring persons for army-related service.\n*   One-third of the State was held as Jagirs.\n*   Big landlords (**Jagirdars**) collected taxes independently, with civic and judicial powers.\n*   Upper-level rulers'' council: **Umra-E-Ajam**.\n*   No uniform classification of ranks — due to (1) lack of standard classification and (2) heredity-based ownership.\n*   Standing depended on: (1) military service/relation with Nizam, (2) court reputation.\n*   Under Mohammedan rule, Jagir = owning landed property (also called **Inam, Altamshoo, Madaa-e-Maash**).\n\n**Threefold division of Hyderabad land:**\n\n| Division | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Jagirdari | Land-owning/non-land-owning, held by Stately Officers |\n| Sarfekhaas | Lands belonging to the King (Nizam) |\n| Dewani | Government lands |\n\n*   Jagirdari + Sarfekhaas = held by Stately Officers; Dewani formed two-thirds of total land.\n*   Stately Officers'' land, four parts: **Sarfekhaas, Pygah** (maintaining King''s forces), **Jagirs**, **Samsthanams** (pre-Nizam Hindu kingdoms/feudatories).\n\n**Social hierarchy (highest to lowest):** King → Pygah nobles → Umar-E-Ajam/Samsthanaadheeswaras (Stately Officers) → Jagirdars (big/middle/small) → I & II class Govt. employees → III class employees/traders → IV class employees/labourers.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule-p2", "partName": "Zamindars", "bodyMd": "*   Hereditary position; held titles like **Raja, Maharaja, Deshmukh, Sardar, Nawab, Reddy, Chowdhury**.\n*   Collected taxes, kept peasants under control; some held independent/sovereign status in their Jagirs.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule-p3", "partName": "Vatandars", "bodyMd": "*   **Vatandari**: cultivator-as-landowner system (Bhookamatamu), ancient, held by local landowners: **Patels, Patwaris, Deshmukhs, Sardeshmukhs, Deshpandes, Sardeshpandes**.\n*   Under **Salaar Jung''s** revenue reforms, Patels/Patwaris got land titles (**Pattas**) and cash pay.\n*   **Deshmukhs and Deshpandes** were the revenue authorities of the Paraganas (provinces).\n*   After the **Zilaabandi system**, Paragana authorities lost these responsibilities and land grants.\n*   **Rusum-e-Zamindari**: 5% (Deshmukhs) + 2½% (Deshpandes) of collected taxes, paid as hereditary service fee.\n*   **''Omlis''**: fixed taxes regardless of actual collection (mainly Karnataka region); 2/3 went to Government.\n*   **''Sera''/''savaram''**: free land grants to Deshmukhs/Deshpandes for service.\n*   **Reddys**: high-pedigreed landowners, also called Deshmukhs, Deshpandes, or **''Dora''/''Desai''**; British employed them as tax collectors and soldiers.\n*   **Raj Bahadur Venkat Ram Reddy**: first Hindu Kotwal of Hyderabad (1920s), served 14 years.\n*   Zamindars who fought against the Nizam (armed struggle from **1946**): **Ravi Narayana Reddy, Laxmi Narayana Reddy, Kodanda Rami Reddy, Baddam Ella Reddy**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule-p4", "partName": "Village Devidies — Gadis of Zamindars", "bodyMd": "*   **Gadi** (Fort) — term confined to Telangana; built of sand and lime; Zamindars held court there with family.\n*   Doras kept horses (some used cars); joint family system; village Zamindars also called **Jagirdars/Maktedars**.\n*   Icons of village Doras: **Nawab Salar Jung, Maharaja Kishan Parshad, Nawab Kamal Jung**.\n*   Lack of education → unbridled Patel/Patwari authority; **Karanams** (addressed as \"Patwaris\") held complete land-holding knowledge.\n*   **95% of Deshmukhs were Reddys; 95% of Deshpandes were Brahmins** — yet no matrimonial ties between the groups.\n*   **Zamindars wielded more authority than the Kings.**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule-p5", "partName": "Society — Caste System", "bodyMd": "*   Hyderabad was religiously/culturally mixed — **\"Ganga Jamuna Tehjeeb\"**.\n*   **1881 census**: 90.32% Hindu, 9.40% Muslim, 0.09% Christian, 0.08% Jain, 0.06% Parsi, 0.0031% Sikh, 47 Jews.\n*   By **1911 census**, Hindu % decreased, Muslim % increased in the city overall, but villages stayed more Hindu.\n*   Caste order: **Brahmins → Kshatriyas → Vaishyas → Shudras** (originally 4, expanded to hundreds).\n\n**Brahmins**: Two branches — Dravida, Gowda; by practice — Vaishnavas (Vishnu), Smarthas (Shiva); by occupation — **Vaidika** (priests/astrologers, poor) and **Niyogi** (army/agriculture/business; became **Karanams**, Sub Collectors, Translators under British).\n\n**Kshatriyas**: Second position; wore sacred threads; observed purdah; branches — Surya, Chandra, Matsya Vamsha; shifted to agriculture.\n\n**Vaishyas**: Third position; commerce/trade; **Komatis** in Andhra/Telangana; also money-lending; vegetarians.\n\n**Shudras**: Fourth, majority population; agriculture; many occupational sub-castes.\n\n**Caste-by-occupation table:**\n\n| Class | Castes |\n|---|---|\n| Agricultural | Kapu, Reddy, Velama, Kamma |\n| Animal-tending | Golla, Jalagari, Besta, Boya |\n| Panchannam vaaru | Vadrangi, Gayadla, Pinjaari, Chitrakarulu, Jogata, Netavaru, Salevaru, Vannegallu |\n| Trading | Komati, Balija, Telaga, Perika, Gandlavaaru |\n| Service | Chakali, Mangali, Katika, Katipapala, Medara, Idiga, Talari, Masti, Jetti, Chatri, Charma Karulu |\n| Poojari | Brahmins, Jangalu, Lingayats, Janagalu, Jogulu, Jatadharulu |\n| Dependent | Picchakuntla, Bhavanas, Runjulu, Pambalulu, Budabukkalu, Bhattu, Chandala, Maatangulu, Kommuri Dasas, Taviri Dasas, Suddulu |\n| Entertaining | Dommari, Bhogamvaaru, Vipra vinodulu, Aindrajaalikulu, Bommalaatala vaaru |\n| Avadhoota | Paramahamsas, Sanyasulu, Satani, Sivasattulu, Tambala, Yogulu, Jaina, Veera mushti, Kaleparasulu |\n| Asprusyulu | Mala, Madiga, Adihindu, Maliar, Mehatar, Chambaar, Mochi |\n| Girijanulu (Tribal) | Chenchu, Boya, Yaanadi, Gond, Pavara, Koli, Erukala, Lambadi, Bhil, Koya, Kondaredlu |\n| Videseeyulu (Foreign) | Parangulu, Ingileesulu, Paraasulu, Turaka, Odde, Gurjapu, Gowda, Mesra |\n\n**Key caste notes:**\n\n*   **Kapus/Reddys**: cultivating landowners; sub-sects Patangi, Motati, Udati, Pedakanti, Aare, Kamma, Velama, Konda, Aadi, Gona, Konidi.\n*   **Velamas**: North Indian immigrant origin; leaders called **Samasthanaadeswaras**.\n*   **Kammas**: agriculturists, claim Kshatriya status; women observe Ghosha.\n*   **Panchanamvaaru** (Vishwakarma lineage): Vadrangi (carpenter), Kamsaali (goldsmith — most important), Kasevaru (sculptor), Kammarulu (iron), Kancharulu (brass).\n*   **Kummari**: potters; village elders (Ayagars).\n*   **Salevaru** (weavers): sub-castes — Devanga, Togata, Padmasaali (weaving), Pattusaali (silk), Devangas (cotton), Togata (zari); deity Yellamma/Renukamaata.\n*   **Mangali (Barber)**: called **Nayee Brahmins**; women did midwifery, men surgery.\n*   **Gollas**: animal husbandry, also known as Yadavas.\n*   **Idiga/Goundla**: toddy extraction, low social rung.\n*   **Telagas**: soldiers under Golconda/Qutub Shahi rulers, called Batroths; till 1870 mostly worked as Deshmukh troops.\n*   **Asprusyulu (Untouchables)**: Parahaa caste, 5th position; lived outside villages.\n*   **Mala**: agricultural labourers; ate meat, didn''t visit Hindu temples.\n*   **Madiga**: hide-cleaning/chappal-making.\n*   **Adivasis**: Chenchus (villages), Koyas (forests) — Koya language; 6th category.\n*   **Gonds**: warrior class, Adilabad forests, ruled **1240–1750**.\n*   **Erukalu**: known for robbery/crime; women (Erukala Saanulu) did divination.\n*   **Lambadies (''Sugle''/''Banjaras'')**: migrated from Rajasthan, sold salt.\n*   **Balotadars**: village servants (priest, carpenter, blacksmith, potter, washerman, barber, Madiga, Mala) paid in paddy (\"Balota\") for service; included Karanam, Patel.\n*   **Muslims**: arrived 14th century; Shia/Sunni; foreign-origin called **''Afaakies''**, local called **''Deccanis''**; mostly army/administration/commerce.\n*   **Dodekula caste**: Hindu-to-Islam converts, spoke Telugu.\n*   **Europeans**: traded from Machilipatnam, Yanam, Ganjam, Narsapur, Kakinada.\n*   **Looso Indians**: Portuguese-Deccan hybrid race.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule-p6", "partName": "Condition of Women", "bodyMd": "*   Hyderabad: 6th-biggest princely state, 17 million population; culture called **''Ganga Jamuna Tehjeeb''**.\n*   Women''s condition studied in **three phases**: (1) first Nizam to 19th century, (2) intermediate reform period, (3) 20th century.\n\n**Phase 1** (worst period, till Salar Jung I''s appointment as PM):\n\n*   Women confined to four walls; even royal/rich women lacked individual liberty.\n*   Outside this norm: **Ayahs, Dayaas** (harem servants), **Tawaifs** (dancers — NOT prostitutes, respected artistes), and **Harlots** (practiced prostitution, socially disrespected).\n*   Nizam maintained Tawaifs via office **''Daftar-e-Arab Nishat''**, spending **Rs. 11,000** on salaries.\n*   **Purdah** was rampant among pedigreed families; unmarried daughters of the Nizam were exempt.\n\n**Notable women (Phase 1):**\n\n*   **Qairunnisa Begum**: married British Resident **Kirkpatrick**; Nizam adopted Kirkpatrick as son, title **''Hashmat Jung Bahadur''**; residency built became **Govt. Women''s College**.\n*   **Mahalakha Chandabai** (b.1766): first Urdu poetess of India; title **''Umrao''**; pen name **Chanda**; poems published 1824 as **''Guljar-e-Mahalakha''**; 125 Ghazals preserved in British Museum; died in the **1824 famine**.\n\n**Phase 2** (**1853–1883**): **Salar Jung** as PM; first Primary School for girls started; reduction in child marriages; seeds of women''s education sown.\n\n**Prominent women (18th–20th c.) — key facts:**\n\n*   **Sowgra Humayun Mirza** (b.1882): founded **Anjuman-E-Khavaateen Deccan**; started Safdaria Girls School.\n*   **Tayyaba Begum Sahiba Bilgrami** (b.1873): **first woman in India to get a B.A. degree** (1910, Madras University).\n*   **Tayyaba Begum**: founded first **Arts & Crafts School** and first **Domestic Science College** in Hyderabad.\n*   **Sarojini Naidu** (b.1879): \"**Bul-bul-E-Hind**\"; wrote _The Golden Threshold_, _The Bird of Time_; President of INC (Kanpur, 1925); **Kaizar-E-Hind medal**; Governor of West Bengal post-independence; fought purdah and child marriage; poem **''Tears of Asaf''** on Musi floods.\n\n**Women''s education milestones:**\n\n*   **1907**: Janana school for Muslim girls started (Sixth Nizam); **79 schools by 1930**.\n*   **1898**: **''Tahajib-un-Nisan''** paper by Sayyed Mumtaz Ali & Mohammadi Begum.\n*   **1904**: **''Khatoon''** paper by Sheik Abdullah Hadis & Jahana Begum, Aligarh.\n*   **Mir Osman Ali Khan** (25-year rule): established **677 primary, 28 middle, 4 special training schools**.\n*   **1938**: Domestic Science syllabus introduced for women.\n*   **Madapati Hanumantha Rao**: started a Girls School in Narayanaguda.\n\n**Women in Independence Movement (key names & roles):**\n\n*   **Jnana Kumari Heda**: first Satyagrahi from Hyderabad State.\n*   **T. Varalakshmamma**: President, 2nd Andhra Mahila Sabha; started Women Health Education Parishad.\n*   **Nadimpalli Sundaramma**: founded **Andhra Sodari Samajam (1915)**.\n*   **Arutla Kamaladevi**: first woman opposition leader of a State (Deputy Leader, Communist Party, 1962).\n*   **Sangem Lakshmibayamma**: only woman jailed for the National movement; first-generation Telangana graduate; later Lok Sabha member from Medak.\n\n**Organizations:**\n\n*   First women''s society: **Bharata Mahila Samajam (1907)**.\n*   **Andhra Sodari Samajam (1925)**, **Andhra Mahila Sabha (1930)**, **Andhra Yuvati Mandali (1935)**.\n\n**Prominent Ladies (key facts only):**\n\n*   **Princess Durru Shevar**: last Ottoman heir; established Hyderabad''s first Airport, Osmania General Hospital.\n*   **Nilofeur**: last Ottoman princess; built Nilofeur Hospital; did not observe purdah.\n*   **Masuma Begum**: **first Muslim lady to become a Minister in India** (1960).", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule-p7", "partName": "Social Evils and Bad Practices", "bodyMd": "Main evils: **bonded-labour, bagela, begari, illiteracy, child marriages, polygamy, dowry, purdah, Jogini system, prostitution.**\n\n### Bonded Labour\n\n*   Confined to tribals/backward castes in theory, but rampant across Telangana society.\n*   Harijan families forced to spare a member for unpaid service to Patel, Patwari, Karanam, Deshmukhs.\n*   Numerous free-service obligations: toddy, cloth, tools, washing, transport, food, and — most heinous — sending girls into service at landlord weddings.\n*   **Jagirdars, Zamindars, Paigahs, Samsthanaadheeswaras** collected ~**Rs. 10 crores** in illegal taxes (State''s lawful revenue before 1940 was ~**Rs. 8 crores**).\n*   Example: **Visunoor Ramchandrareddy** (Deshmukh, Janagaon) controlled bonded labour across ~40 villages.\n*   **Armed struggle in Telangana began in 1946** due to such excesses.\n*   **Mir Osman Ali Khan prohibited Begar in 1911**; law helped end bonded labour only with 20th-century education/reform.\n\n### Bagela\n\n*   Peasant unable to pay taxes became a bonded servant of the landlord.\n*   Prevalent in **Nalgonda and Warangal** districts.\n*   Debt passed to heirs if unpaid in lifetime.\n*   **Abolished by Nizam Government in 1930.**\n\n### Begari\n\n*   Forced unpaid labour, in vogue since ancient times, in towns and villages alike.\n*   **1911**: Osman Ali Khan''s farmana to abolish — ineffective.\n*   **1922 Farmana**: wages fixed (12 seers paddy/men, 8/women, 6/children); children under 10 exempted.\n*   **Abolished with Nizam''s merger into Indian Union, 1948.**\n\n### Child Marriages\n\n*   More common among Hindus (especially Brahmins) than Muslims; none among Christians.\n*   Caused: poverty, illiteracy; led to health issues/early death.\n*   **1899**: Age of Consent for Marriage Act.\n*   **Sarada Act, 1930**: abolished child marriage.\n\n### Widow Marriages\n\n*   Common in lower castes, rare in upper castes; Nizam State had **4x more widows than widowers**.\n*   **Widow Remarriage Act, 1856** — never enforced.\n*   **Salar Jung** married a widow, setting an example.\n\n### Polygamy\n\n*   Muslim Shariat permitted up to **4 wives**; upper-caste Hindu men also practiced it. Women could not remarry.\n*   Declined due to social reform and 20th-century Government efforts.\n\n### Dowry\n\n*   **Varasulkam** (dowry to bridegroom''s family) more common in Telangana than **Kanyasulkam** (dowry to bride''s family).\n*   Most prevalent among **Reddys and Velamas**; still continues today.\n\n### Devadasi–Jogini System\n\n*   Temple girls dedicated to God (Devadasis), later exploited by landlords — evolved into the **Jogini system**, mainly in North Telangana.\n*   Abolished due to reform efforts (Raghupati Venkata Ramiah Naidu in Andhra; Andhra Maha Sabha/Yuvati Mandali in Telangana).\n\n### Aadapapa\n\n*   Girls kept as bonded slaves/concubines by rulers and Zamindars, mostly in lower castes; children also became Aadapapas.\n\n### Prostitution\n\n*   Encouraged by Nawabs/rulers; performers called **''Tawaiffs''**.\n*   Office: **''Daftar-E-Nishan Kanchen Kacheris''**; supervisor titled **''Mamasharifa''**.\n*   This Tawaif system was **unique to Telangana, not found in Andhra**.\n\n### Purdah\n\n*   Spread from North India by Muslims; adopted by Hindu elites too.\n*   Entered Telangana **during Qutub Shahi period**, continued under Asafjahis.\n*   Survives today mainly in Muslim families.\n\n* * *\n\n# Land Tenures and Land Revenue Systems during Asafjahi Rule\n\nLand Tenures and Land Revenue Systems during Asafjahi Rule — as Hyderabad State was essentially agriculture-based, land ownership shaped both livelihoods and social standing.  \n\n*   **68% of population** depended on agriculture; land ownership = social status marker.\n*   Land revenue formed **50.29% of total tax revenue** (1933–34).\n\n### Land Classification\n\n1.  **Diwani/Khalsa lands** — direct Govt. administration; **54.8%** of State''s 82,698 sq. miles.\n2.  **Non-Khalsa lands** (**45.2%**) — (a) **Sarf-I-Khas** (Nizam''s privy purse), (b) **Jagir/Inam** grants.\n\n**Table: Types of Land Tenures**\n\n| Diwani/Khalsa | Other Khalsa Tenures | Non-Khalsa |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ryotwari (Pattedari, Pot-Pattedari, Shikmidari, Asami Shikmi) | Pan-Maqta; Tahud/Sarbasta; Ijara; Agrahar | Sarf-I-Khas; Samsthan/Peshkash; Jagir (Paigah, Al-Tamgha, Zat, Tankha, Mashrooti, Madad Mash); Inam |\n\n*   **Ryotwari tenure** (Bombay-system based): most important — over **50% of area**, more than **20 million acres**.\n*   Pattedar keeps land as long as revenue paid; evictable only for public purpose with compensation.\n\n### Cultivating Tenures & Tenants\n\n*   **Pattedari**: simple occupancy (self/hired cultivation).\n*   **Pot-Pattedari**: joint-stock cultivation, equal rights, not much in vogue.\n*   **Shikmidari**: land handed to cultivators (Shikmidars) under agreement — protected tenants.\n*   **Asami-Shikmi**: tenants-at-will; became Shikmidar after 12 years'' continuous occupation.\n*   Most leases (**75%**) were oral, tenancies-at-will terminated yearly — landlords avoided written agreements to prevent tenant rights.\n*   **Committee (1940) → Asami-Shikmi Act (1945)** → operative 1947 — recognized 6-year continuous occupants (1933–43) as protected tenants; poorly implemented.\n*   Non-Ryotwari tenures (Jagir, Maqta, Ijara, Agrahar, Inam) had even worse tenant conditions — cultivators of up to **90 years'' standing** still treated as tenants-at-will.\n*   **1,137 of 5,800 non-Diwani villages** remained unsurveyed by 1949, enabling record manipulation by landlords.\n*   **Hyderabad Tenancy and Agricultural Lands Act, 10 June 1950** — finally secured tenant rights (implemented post-1950).\n\n### Other Khalsa Tenures\n\n*   **Pan-Maqta**: contract tenure, fixed quit-rent, permanent/life grants; Maqtedars behaved like Jagirdars, manipulating records via Patel/Patwari posts they held.\n*   **Tahud/Sarbasta/Watandari**: tax-farming system; **abolished by Salar Jung I in 1866**. Ex-tax-farmers (Deshmukhs/Deshpandyas) compensated with land, strengthening their position.\n    *   **Rusum-i-zamindari**: hereditary pension = **7½%** of revenue (5% Deshmukhs, 2½% Deshpandyas), no service required.\n    *   Village-level Watandars: Patels, Patwaris, Talari, Sethsindhi, Neredu, Dhein.\n*   **Ijara** (1878): land-development lease, light rates rising to full assessment over **5–40 years**; abolished **1909 (1318 Fasli)**.\n*   **Agrahara**: quit-rent tenure for religious institutions; in practice, absentee landlordism and exploitation like other tenures.\n\n### Sarf-I-Khas\n\n*   **10% of Dominions**, spread over 18 Taluqs; Nizam''s privy purse; administered by Diwani officials under **Sadar-ul-Moham**.\n*   Miserly spending on welfare/irrigation despite good revenue collection; low staff salaries.\n*   **Taken over by Government in February 1949.**\n\n### Paigahs\n\n*   **''Paigah''** = Persian for stable/cavalry; also **Jamiat Jagirs**; created by **Nizam Ali Khan (1761–1803)** as counter-balancing military force after Minister **Rukhnuddaula''s** death (1775).\n*   Over **1,000 villages**, 2,000+ sq. miles, across 237 Taluqs, 10 Districts; none in Nalgonda (poor soil/irrigation).\n*   Full internal administrative/judicial control; **Reilly Commission (1927)** recommended disbanding — Nizam''s **1929 Firman** rejected this.\n\n### Jagirs\n\n*   Introduced by **Nizam-ul-mulk**; 1/3rd of territory distributed to nobles (Muslims: military; Hindus: administration).\n*   Types: **Al-tamgha** (revenue-free, permanent), **Zat** (unconditional maintenance grant), **Mashrooti** (service-conditional), **Madadmash** (gratuity/supplement), **Tankha** (pre-1853 salary substitute, redeemed by **Salar Jung I in 1853**).\n*   **Exempted Jagirdars**: big Jagirdars; **222 in Medak Suba alone** — ran private governments (own courts, police, revenue, survey depts.) — \"State within a State.\"\n*   **Non-exempted Jagirs**: smaller, e.g., 531 in Medak Suba; paid **Haq-e-malikana royalty (2–25%)**.\n*   Jagirdars collected **25–50% more revenue** than Khalsa villages; extracted **nazarana** before granting Pattedari rights.\n*   **35 types of illegal cesses** existed (e.g., Shadi-Patti/marriage tax, Ghar-Patti/house tax, Mohtarfa/handloom tax, grazing fines).\n*   **Vetti** (forced labour) was the most-hated practice — banned by Firman **1926**, ignored in practice.\n*   Jagir administration neglected education (75% villages had no primary school) and health/irrigation.\n*   Popular anger against Jagirdars fueled the **Peasants'' Armed Struggle (1946–51)**.\n\n### Inams\n\n*   ''Inam'' (Arabic: favour/reward) — revenue-free/partial land grant for service, charity, or reward; often hereditary.\n*   **Balota Inams**: 1/4th assessment, for village service.\n*   **Inam Commission (1865–1912)** — validated/resumed fraudulent grants.\n*   Total Inamdars: **83,000** (57,000 Diwani, 28,000 Jagir).\n*   **Circular No.4, 1298 Fasli (1889)**: allowed Inamdars to legally occupy excess land — worsened land concentration.\n\n### Samsthanas\n\n*   **''Peshkash''**: fixed annual tribute paid by conquered rulers/chiefs in exchange for self-administration.\n*   Only **3 true Peshkash Guzars**: **Gadwal (Rs.86,540), Anagundi (Rs.10,000), Gurgunta (Rs.7,050)**.\n*   Pan-Maqta-style Peshkash payers: **Wanaparti, Gopalpet, Jatpol, Bhadrachalam**.\n\n| Ruler | Peshkash |\n|---|---|\n| Wanaparti | Rs.83,862 |\n| Jatpol | Rs.71,944 |\n| Gopalpet | Rs.22,663 |\n| Bhadrachalam | Rs.45,875 |\n| **Total** | **Rs.2,13,844** |\n\n*   **14 Samsthanas** total; only **5 exempted** from Diwani jurisdiction — 3 of these (**Gadwal, Wanaparti, Jatpol**) from Mahaboobnagar District.\n*   Unlike scattered Jagirs, Samsthanas were **compact territorial units**, with own Police/Judicial systems and revenue surplus.\n*   Some Samsthandars — **Raja Rameshwar Rao of Wanaparti (d.1922)**, **Raja Venkat Lachman Rao Bahadur of Jatpole (d.1929)** — were benevolent, modernizing rulers.\n\n### Survey and Settlement\n\n*   **Survey and Settlement Department established 1875** (post tax-farming abolition by Salar Jung I).\n*   Bombay system adopted, adapted: Telangana rice areas assessed high in wet years, remitted in failure years (unlike Bombay''s flat rate).\n*   **Ayacut** (irrigable area) rule allowed corrupt over-classification as \"wet land\" — corrected only in **September 1949**.\n*   Work began: **1899 (1298 Fasli)** in Medak/Nizamabad Taluqs; **1915–16** in Mahaboobnagar.\n*   Settlement period: originally 30 years, reduced to **15 years** for Telangana.\n*   **Record of Rights Act (1936)** — flawed, recognized only nominal Pattedars; replaced by a **new Act in 1949** recognizing actual land holders.\n\n### Land Revenue Assessments\n\n**Table: Rate of Assessment (1912–1945), Rs-Annas-Paisa**\n\n| Division | Before 1912 Dry/Wet | 1912–22 Dry/Wet | 1934 Dry/Wet | 1945 Dry/Wet |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Marathwada | 0-13-10 / 5-0-9 | 1-0-1 / 5-8-4 | 1-1-11 / 5-15-5 | 1-2-2 / 9-12-3 |\n| Telangana | 0-11-11 / 11-1-3 | 0-12-5 / 11-6-7 | 1-0-8 / 12-3-6 | 0-14-2 / 12-5-3 |\n| **State Avg.** | 0-12-10 / 8-1-0 | 0-14-3 / 8-7-5 | 1-0-8 / 9-1-6 | 1-0-7 / 11-2-0 |\n\n*   Assessment rates rose steadily 1912–1945; **dry land assessment higher in Marathwada, wet land assessment higher in Telangana**.\n*   Telangana wet rate (Medak Suba: **Rs.23-0-0/acre**) was 3x higher than Madras State (**Rs.7-1-6**).\n*   **S.M. Bharucha (1936–37 study)**: on a 16-acre farm, land revenue (Rs.30) was 9.4% of net yield (Rs.320), leaving little margin after other costs.\n*   **50% extra rate** charged on tank-irrigated double-crop lands, discouraging second cropping.\n*   **Mr. O''Connor** (Nizam''s Govt.) suggested a **25–30% reduction** in land revenue — never implemented.\n\n### Rents\n\n*   **1901–1911**: rent-receivers increased **1,748.8%** — landlords shifted from owner-farming to renting out.\n*   **Cash rents**: 40% of leased land (mainly dry land); risk borne by tenant; rents were **2–3x assessment rates** (tenants effectively paid 4–6x assessment).\n*   **Batai** (crop-sharing, 50-50): mainly for wet lands; risk shared between landlord and tenant.\n*   Rent burden: **35–45%** of produce (tank-irrigated), **25–30%** (well-irrigated), **15–40%** (dry, food vs cash crops).\n*   **Famine Commission (1901)**: noted tenants had sunk to the condition of \"mere serfs.\"\n\n### Remissions\n\n**Table: Remission Sub-heads, 1324 Fasli (1914–15)**\n\n| Head | Marathwada % | Telangana % | Total % |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Water excess/deficit | 0.8 | 35.5 | 36.3 |\n| Breach of irrigation sources | 1.2 | 20.2 | 21.4 |\n| Submergence before cultivation | 0.1 | 5.8 | 5.9 |\n| Talaf Mal (crop failure) | 0.0 | 0.8 | 0.8 |\n| Change of crop/irrigation | 0.2 | 19.3 | 19.5 |\n| Double-crop cash remission | 0.5 | 8.8 | 9.3 |\n| Other | 0.7 | 6.1 | 6.8 |\n| **Total** | **3.5** | **96.5** | **100.0** |\n\n*   Telangana got **96.5% of remissions** (wet/chalka soils); Marathwada only 3.5% (dry black-soil tracts).\n*   Remissions mostly benefited **wet lands only**, and mainly wells built before **1908/1915** — corrected only in 1949.\n*   In practice, remissions rarely helped actual cultivators — officials extracted \"mamuls\" (bribes) to process claims; big landlords benefited most.\n*   **Sarf-I-Khas and Jagir villages** were denied remissions in practice, despite Government orders.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter17-social-conditions-and-land-revenue-systems-asafjahi-rule-p8", "partName": "Summary", "bodyMd": "Land tenures and revenue administration in Hyderabad State were dominated by feudal, hereditary, authoritarian power groups (**Deshmukhs, Deshpandyas, Patels, Patwaris, Maqtedars, Agraharaiks, Paighas, Jagirdars, Samsthandars**). \n\nDiwani and non-Diwani tenures differed only in degree, not kind — both featured high assessments, rack-renting, forced/free labour (vetti), and remission systems that mainly benefited big landlords. Education, health, and infrastructure development in non-Diwani areas were minimal, with no channel for popular grievance redressal — conditions that fed directly into the **Telangana Peasants'' Armed Struggle (1946–51)**.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter18-andhra-jana-sangham', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p1", "partName": "Background — The Vivekavardhini Theatre Incident (1921)", "bodyMd": "Until 1921, no organized movement of any kind existed among the Andhras of the Nizam''s Dominions. A single humiliating incident that year changed this and led directly to the founding of the Andhra Jana Sangham.\n\nAt noon on 12 November 1921, a Hindu Samskara Sabha (Hindu Reformation meeting) was held at the Vivekavardhini Theatre in Hyderabad, presided over by **Dhonde Keshav Karve**, founder of the Pune Women''s University. Many speakers addressed the gathering in English, Urdu, and Marathi.\n\nTwo prominent Andhra representatives were present, both High Court lawyers: **Madapati Hanumantha Rao** and **Aalampalli Venkata Rama Rao**. Hanumantha Rao spoke in Telugu, which annoyed the Maharashtrian-majority audience, though they let him finish.\n\n*   Aalampalli Venkata Rama Rao then rose to speak in Telugu as well. The audience responded with jeering claps of protest, but he continued speaking regardless.\n*   The audience grew disorderly and would not let him stop; he kept speaking anyway. They eventually turned their backs to the stage in a further show of contempt.\n*   The Andhras present could not bear this misconduct and insult, and walked out of the meeting.\n\nThat same night, the Andhra delegates assembled at **Tekumalla Rangarao''s** house to decide on a course of action. To protect Andhra self-respect and establish a distinct Andhra identity, they resolved to found an organization.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p2", "partName": "Formation of the Nizam Rashtra Andhra Jana Kendra Sangham", "bodyMd": "The organization founded that night was named the **Nizam Rashtra Andhra Jana Kendra Sangham**, with **Rao Bahadur Shyamala Reddy** as President and **Madapati Hanumantha Rao** as Secretary.\n\n**Founders:** Tekumalla Rangarao, Madapati Hanumantha Rao, Burgula Ramakrishna Rao, Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy, Mandumula Narasinga Rao, Mandumula Ramachandra Rao, Adiraju Veerabhadra Rao, Pandit Ramaswamy Naidu, R. Rajagopala Reddy, Mitta Lakshminarasayya, and Kommavarapu Subba Rao.\n\nWith this seed sown, the Andhrodyamam (Andhra movement) spread across the state, bringing awareness to the people, and later showed the way for similar Maharashtrian and Kannada parishats (associations) to form. Until then, any such movement in the state had stayed confined to the city; the Andhras were the first to carry theirs out into the village areas. The leaders behind this effort were themselves the source of the unity, awakening, education, and advancement of women that followed among the Andhras — they awoke to these causes first, and then awakened others to them.\n\nUntil this point, the rulers of the state and their dependents had long propagated a notion of a single, undivided \"Deccani\" identity, history, kingdom, and culture; this notion now broke apart. The current of Andhra culture surged forth and took concrete shape in Hyderabad itself, on the banks of the Musi river. Driven by the enthusiasm and pride of the youth, the Andhra Jana Sangham set out with ambitious goals.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p3", "partName": "Early Work — Confronting Rural Hardship", "bodyMd": "On one front, the Sangham worked to raise rural awareness by establishing libraries; on another, it worked to relieve farmers'' hardships through farmers'' associations, and to improve trading conditions through merchants'' associations — supporting such existing bodies and founding new ones where none existed, under a programme they called \"Aikya\" (Unity).\n\nThis work responded to a real and pressing need. In that period, the tyranny of Patels, Patwaris, and the police over villagers went on unchecked:\n\n*   Farmers were subjected to unjust and crushing revenue demands.\n*   They were forced into unpaid labour (_vetti chakiri_).\n*   Officials would take goods from merchants without paying for them, and would abuse and beat those who objected.\n*   There was no authority to whom the people could appeal for redress.\n\nIt was for the welfare of such powerless people that the Andhra Jana Sangham worked, with its leaders touring the districts to spread awareness.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p4", "partName": "First Meeting — Hyderabad (1923)", "bodyMd": "The first meeting of the Andhra Jana Sangham was held on **27 July 1923** at Madapati Hanumantha Rao''s residence in Hyderabad. Seven elected members from eight districts of Telangana took part, along with six more who attended from Hyderabad as observer-workers. **Raja Bahadur Venkatarama Reddy** presided.\n\n**Objectives adopted at this meeting:**\n\n1.  Establish libraries, reading rooms, and schools.\n2.  Provide assistance and encouragement to students.\n3.  Honour scholars.\n4.  Acquire palm-leaf manuscripts and copies of inscriptions, and conduct research on them.\n5.  Hold lectures and meetings, and publish pamphlets and small books, to advance the growth of knowledge.\n6.  Take measures for the development of the Telugu language.\n7.  Provide emergency assistance to orphans.\n8.  Encourage physical exercise and the arts.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p5", "partName": "Second Meeting — Nalgonda (1924)", "bodyMd": "The second meeting was held on **21 March 1924** at Nalgonda, in the office premises of **Shabnavisu Venkata Ramanarasimha Rao**, editor of the _Nilagiri_ newspaper. **Rao Bahadur Venkat Reddy** presided; seven representatives from Telangana districts and 50 people from various districts attended. **Pingali Venkatramareddy** presided over the concluding session.\n\n**Resolutions:**\n\n1.  Condolences were recorded on the deaths of Chitrada zamindar and literary patron **Chelikani Lachcha Rao**, and of litterateur **Vanguri Subba Rao**, author of a history of the _shataka_ (centum) poets.\n2.  A full-time, paid propagandist should be appointed.\n3.  A **\"Nizam State Andhra Fund\"** (Nijam Rashtra Andhra Nidhi) should be set up to spread the Andhrodyamam within the Nizam''s Dominions, with **Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy** to administer it.\n4.  The body then known as the **Lakshmana Raya Parishodhaka Mandali** (earlier called the Andhra Parishodhaka Mandali) should be brought under the management of the Kendra Sangham.\n5.  Students placing first in Telugu in the matriculation and intermediate examinations of Osmania University should be honoured with gold and silver medals.\n6.  Examinations should be conducted to promote Andhra literature.\n7.  Small, low-priced booklets describing the conditions of Andhras in the Nizam State should be published and sold at a few annas apiece.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p6", "partName": "Third Meeting — Madhira, Warangal (1925)", "bodyMd": "The third meeting was held on **21 February 1925** at Madhira, Warangal district, in a spacious pavilion within the premises of **Pingali Venkatarama Reddy''s** factory. High Court lawyer **Madapati Tirumala Rao** headed the reception committee.\n\nCondolences were expressed for the untimely deaths of Raja Krishnadevaraya Bahiri Balwant (ruler of Wanaparthy samsthanam and son-in-law of Munagala Raja Nayani Venkata Rangarao), Sitaram Bhupal (ruler of the Gadwal samsthanam), Jogipet merchants, and public worker Shivappa.\n\n*   **Attendees:** Professor Shirugoori Hanumantha Rao (President), Adiraju Veerabhadra Rao (Secretary), Nayani Venkata Rangarao (ruler of Munagala samsthanam), Madapati Hanumantha Rao, and Raja Bahadur Pillai (members).\n*   It was resolved to honour with gold medals those who passed with the highest distinction in the examinations of the Hyderabad Government Medical College and Engineering College.\n*   Approval was given to award Rs. 25 to the author of a small book titled _\"The Path to the Progress of the Andhras of the Nizam State,\"_ and to publish it on behalf of the sabha.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p7", "partName": "Fourth Meeting — Suryapet (1928)", "bodyMd": "The fourth meeting was held over **28–30 May 1928** at Suryapet, with **Kodati Ramakrishna Rao** as president of the reception committee. At this meeting the Sangham laid down formal rules of conduct for its sessions:\n\n1.  Political matters, and matters contrary to loyalty to the ruler (_rajabhakti_), were not to be discussed.\n2.  Cigars, beedis, and cigarettes were not to be smoked at the venue, nor were any other prohibited substances to be consumed.\n3.  The reception committee held the authority to take action against anyone who violated these rules.\n4.  Speakers had to submit their names to the reception committee in advance.\n\nAs previously decided, **Rao Bahadur Venkat Reddy** was to preside, but since he could not attend, **Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy** presided in his place.\n\n*   Condolences were expressed for the deaths of Raja Surabhi Venkata Lakshmana Rao Bahadur, ruler of the Jatprole samsthanam, and of Andhrodyamam workers Sadashiva Reddy and Chirugu Veeranna.\n*   Congratulations were extended to **Adilakshmi Devamma**, who had taken up the rule of the Gadwal samsthanam as its Maharani with government recognition.\n*   Medals were awarded to students placing first in the Intermediate and B.A. examinations of Osmania University.\n*   Congratulations were extended to the Bombay-based Andhra dignitary **Shankar Sayanna Pargagaru** for donating Rs. 500 (in kaldar currency) toward the propagation of the Andhrodyamam.\n*   A request was made to the government to permit Osmania University students to gain admission into colleges affiliated with Andhra University.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p8", "partName": "Growth of the Sangham and Its Impact", "bodyMd": "From the founding of the Andhra Jana Sangham onward, **Madapati Hanumantha Rao** worked tirelessly for its growth. Within three to four years, the Sangham had established as many as 50 branches across various places, and around 90 libraries had also come up in Telangana, furthering the cause of the Telugu language and the spread of knowledge.\n\nThis activity created a genuine stir among the people. In Telangana, the darkness gradually lifted, and, as the notes describe it, the light of dawn began to spread.\n\nAt the Madhira and Suryapet meetings, the Sangham also held its first and second Library Conferences. Madapati Hanumantha Rao took particular care to keep the people''s surging enthusiasm in check and to channel it onto a constructive path.\n\nThe concrete benefit of the Andhra Jana Sangham''s work was this: urban students, who had once regarded speaking Urdu itself as a mark of civilization, gradually began delivering their speeches in Telugu instead, and even ordinary, uneducated people began listening to Telugu speeches. \n\n*   Meetings and gatherings on this theme became frequent everywhere. Telugu-language newspapers began reaching not only the towns but the villages as well, carried out by postal runners with bundles of copies, and a new generation of writers and poets emerged, publishing Telugu poems, stories, and essays in these papers.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p9", "partName": "Why \"Andhra\" and Not \"Telangana\"", "bodyMd": "A natural question arises: the people who founded these organizations were themselves from Telangana, yet they chose the names \"Andhrodyamam\" and \"Andhra Jana Sangham\" rather than \"Telangana Jana Sangham.\" The answer lies in how they read their own history.\n\n*   Historically, people who spoke the Telugu language were known as **Andhras**.\n*   The Urdu-speaking officials of the Nizam''s administration, by contrast, called Telugu speakers \"**Telangi**,\" and mocked the language itself as \"_Telangi bedhangi_\" — meaning it had no proper grammatical system — out of an inability to understand or learn it, and a contempt that came from not being able to speak it.\n*   It was in this climate that the term \"Andhra bhasha\" (Andhra language) took hold as the accepted name for the language in the region.\n*   The word \"Andhra\" was already established in the names of the earliest Telugu-language institutions of Telangana, such as the **Sri Krishnadevaraya Andhra Bhasha Nilayam**, the **Raja Rajanarendra Andhra Bhasha Nilayam**, and the **Vemana Andhra Bhasha Nilayam**.\n\nGiven this historical precedent, the founders styled their movement and organization as the \"Andhra Jana Sangham\" and the \"Andhrodyamam.\"", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p10", "partName": "Propaganda and Publications", "bodyMd": "Madapati Hanumantha Rao wanted the messages and objectives of the Andhra Jana Kendra Sangham to reach even the most remote villages of Telangana. To this end, he appointed a capable worker, **Unnava Venkataramayya**, as the Sangham''s propagandist, and arranged for the publication of small booklets on its behalf to keep the people informed of ongoing events.\n\nAmong the booklet titles published were: _Trade Freedom_, _Forced Labour (Vetti Chakiri)_, _Libraries_, _Our Freedom of Speech_, _Liquor Consumption_, and _Debt Relief_.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch18-andhra-jana-sangham-p11", "partName": "From Andhra Jana Sangham to Andhra Mahasabha", "bodyMd": "As the Andhrodyamam continued to spread, Hanumantha Rao judged that the scope of the Andhra Jana Sangham''s activities needed to widen further. Prominent and educated figures came to feel that a full Mahasabha (grand assembly) was needed to understand the problems of the more than sixty lakh Andhras of the Nizam State and to give shape to their collective opinion. This is what led directly to the founding of the **Andhra Mahasabha**.\n\nIn those days, the Andhra Mahasabha stood as the sole major organization representing the Andhras, and it can genuinely be described as a forerunner of the Congress: it served as the foundational structure from which the Congress organization in Hyderabad State later emerged, at a time when the Indian National Congress had confined its own founding activities to British India and had not yet established branches in the princely states.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p1", "partName": "Background — Early Organizational Efforts", "bodyMd": "The roots of the Andhra Mahasabha lie in an incident of linguistic humiliation. In November 1921, a Nizam State Social Reformation meeting (Sanga Samskarana Mahasabha) was held at Vivekavardhini School, Hyderabad, under the chairmanship of D.K. Karvay. Speakers at this meeting addressed the audience in Urdu, Marathi, Kannada, and English. When the Hyderabad advocate **Aalampalli Venkat Rama Rao** attempted to speak in Telugu, Marathi and Kannada attendees opposed him and mocked him with whistles and jeers.\n\nThis humiliation became the spark for Telugu organization in the Nizam''s Dominions. Telugu people gathered at Tekumalla Rangarao''s house on 12 November 1921 and established the **Nizam State Jana Sangam** (Nizam Rashtra Andhra Jana Sangam). **Madapati Hanumanth Rao** played the leading role in its formation.\n\n*   The 1st meeting of the Nizam State Jana Sangam was held on 14 February 1922 at Reddy Hostel, Hyderabad, presided over by **Konda Venkat Ranga Reddy**.\n*   On 1 April 1923, representatives gathered at Hanumakonda and renamed the body the **Andhra Jana Kendra Sangam**, with **Barrister Raja Gopal Reddy** as its 1st President and Madapati Hanumanth Rao as its 1st Secretary.\n\n### Andhra Jana Kendra Sangam (1923)\n\n*   Its 1st meeting was held at Madapati Hanumanth Rao''s house in Hyderabad in July 1923, where **Rao Bahadur Venkat Reddy** was elected President.\n*   Its 2nd meeting was held in 1924 at the office of the _Nilgiri_ newspaper in Nalgonda, whose editor was Shabnavisu Venkat Narsimha Rao.\n\n**Activities:**\n\n*   Collection of palm-leaf manuscripts (Talapatra Grandham).\n*   Research into ancient history to highlight the greatness of the Andhra people.\n*   Establishment of libraries.\n*   Publication of pamphlets and books, including works campaigning against the _Sirbarahi_ system (the free supply of goods to visiting government officials), against _Vetti Chakiri_ (forced labour), and against the _Mohathurba Maggam_ tax imposed on weavers — the last of these covered in Mateti Papayya''s book _Mohathurba Maggam Pannu_.\n*   In 1930, a _Kakatiya Charcha Goshti_ (discussion meeting) was held at Warangal and the _Kakatiya Sanchika_ was published, which in turn carried _Andrula Sangika Charitra_ and Adiraju Veerabhadra Rao''s _Telangana Inscriptions_.\n*   Many Telugu-medium schools were founded under the Sangam''s auspices; in 1928 Madapati Hanumantha Rao established the Andhra Girls School.\n*   Adiraju Veerabhadra Rao founded the **Andhra Parishodaka Sangam** under the Kendra Sangam''s aegis (later renamed **Lakshman Raya Parishodana Mandali**) and collected numerous palm-leaf manuscripts and inscriptions.\n\nIn 1930, the Andhra Jana Kendra Sangam itself was renamed the **Nizam Rashtra Andhra Mahasabha** — Telangana''s first political organization.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p2", "partName": "Formation and Ground Rules of the Andhra Mahasabha", "bodyMd": "The Nizam government permitted the Andhra Mahasabha to be convened only under strict conditions: the presiding officer had to be a resident of Hyderabad State (not an \"outsider\"), nothing was to be said that would hurt the sentiments of other religious communities or cause insult, and no political matters were to be raised in the sabha.\n\nBetween 1930 and 1946, **13 sessions** of the Andhra Mahasabha were held. Alongside these, **Andhra Mahila Sabhas** (women''s sessions) were also convened, largely through the efforts of **Durgabhai Deshmukh**.\n\nThe overall purpose of these sessions was to build social and cultural awareness among the people. Madapati Hanumantha Rao regarded the Andhra Mahasabha as Telangana''s equivalent of the national Indian National Congress — just as the Congress represented the national movement for all Indians, the Andhra Mahasabha represented the movement (Andhrodyamam) for the Telugu people of Telangana.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p3", "partName": "First Andhra Mahasabha — Jogipet (1930)", "bodyMd": "The Andhrodyamam''s second phase began in 1930 when the first Andhra Mahasabha convened at **Jogipet**, in the Andole taluk of Medak district, with the merchants of Jogipet providing financial support for the session.\n\n*   **President:** Suravaram Pratapa Reddy (B.A., B.L.; editor of the _Golkonda Patrika_, prolific author, and a champion of the Telangana people).\n*   **President of the inaugural committee:** Tekumalla Ananta Venkat Rao (High Court lawyer).\n*   **President of the 1st Andhra Mahila Sabha:** Nadimpalli Sundaramma.\n\nThe session opened with the song \"Andhradesapu matti adi maku kanakamu\" (\"The soil of the Andhra land is gold to us\"), reflecting the movement''s early emphasis on cultural pride in Telugu identity. Madapati Hanumantha Rao and other leaders were careful to keep this pride from turning into arrogance toward other communities, since the movement in its first phase was chiefly focused on this cultural self-respect.\n\n**Resolutions of the Jogipet Sabha:**\n\n*   Repeal of **Gasthi Nishan–53**, an ordinance passed by the Nizam in 1926 banning public meetings and conferences.\n*   **Bhagya Reddy Varma** moved a resolution for the abolition of untouchability.\n*   The Nizam government should provide educational facilities for Adi-Hindus.\n*   Compulsory primary education should be introduced.\n*   Regional languages should be encouraged in state libraries.\n*   Child marriage should be discouraged, and widow remarriage encouraged.\n*   The government should depute veterinary doctors and agriculture department officials to raise awareness among the rural population (a request the Nizam government accepted).\n*   The Sabha also worked toward village reconstruction and resolving farmers'' problems.\n\nA parallel women''s session, the first Andhra Mahila Sabha, was held during the same days under Nadimpalli Sundaramma''s presidency, discussing the condition of ancient and contemporary women, marriage problems, chastity, widowhood, and the practice of concubinage.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p4", "partName": "Second Andhra Mahasabha — Devarakonda (1931)", "bodyMd": "The second Andhra Mahasabha was held at **Devarakonda**, Nalgonda district, from 3–5 March 1931.\n\n*   **President:** Burgula Ramakrishna Rao.\n*   **President of the Mahila Sabha:** T. Varalakshmi.\n*   **President of the inaugural committee:** Pulijala Venkat Ranga Rao.\n\n**Ravi Narayana Reddy** and fellow students of Reddy Hostel walked from Nizam College to Devarakonda to attend this session. Many delegates spoke in Telugu at this meeting — a direct result of the Andhra Mahasabha''s efforts to promote the Telugu language within Hyderabad State.\n\nBurgula Ramakrishna Rao belonged to a jagirdar family of Burgula village, Palamoor taluk, Mahbubnagar district. He trained as a lawyer in Pune, and it was during his student days there that his family surname came to be recorded as \"Rankinsonrao Burgula/Burgula Ramakrishna Rao.\" He went on to become Hyderabad State''s first Chief Minister after Independence, and was known as a scholar, poet, and author fluent in several languages.\n\nAt Devarakonda, Burgula took care that the session remain non-political and focused on people''s welfare and development. **Vaman Naik** attended and supported Andhra causes regardless of the movement involved, and donated to the Sarada Niketan in Guntur; he held somewhat orthodox views on religious and social questions, however, which limited his influence over the social resolutions passed earlier at Jogipet.\n\nAt the same Devarakonda session, **Arya Samaj** leader Keshavrao also attended and tried to advance social reform among the Andhras present. Burgula, as president, tactfully steered the session away from political speeches made by visiting Maharashtrian delegates.\n\n*   **Second Mahila Sabha:** presided over by Smt. T. Varalakshmamma. This sabha called for compulsory elementary education for women, higher education through the mother tongue, and training in medicine and the treatment of illnesses for women, and it discussed the beginnings of ministership for women.\n*   It also opposed the purdah system and called for reform of the practice of child marriage. Overall, the resolutions of this sabha were directed toward women''s advancement.\n\n**Bhagya Reddy Varma Resolution (Devarakonda):** A resolution was passed concerning untouchability and the _Vetti_ (forced labour) imposed on lower castes, calling for equal rights and respect for them.\n\nSessions were meant to be held annually, but after the second Devarakonda session it took **three years** for the third session to be convened, owing to delays in obtaining government permission.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p5", "partName": "Third Andhra Mahasabha — Khammam Mettu (1934)", "bodyMd": "Background to the delay: nine months after the Devarakonda session, the organizing committee in Jatprole estate (Mahbubnagar district) began efforts to hold the third session at Pentlavalli village, led by the prominent lawyer Chakrapani Narasaraju. Since Jatprole was itself a samsthanam (estate), permission had to be obtained from the samsthanam''s own administration rather than directly from the Nizam government.\n\nWhen the organizers sought this permission, the samsthanam administration wrote back stating in writing that it did not have the authority to grant it, and referred the matter to the Nizam government.\n\n*   The invitee committee then approached the Mahbubnagar Collector, requesting that the state administration not interfere in the affairs of the samsthanam and that the same procedure followed for the Devarakonda sabha be applied here as well. The Collector agreed.\n*   The organizers also petitioned the Nizam Sarkar directly, and the matter eventually reached the Prime Minister, Maharaja Sir Kishan Pershad Bahadur. His reply stated that permission could not be granted either to hold the session in the samsthanam or elsewhere.\n*   Public dissatisfaction grew as an entire year passed without resolution, provoking questions about why a Collector''s permit should even be necessary for such a small matter, and whether it reflected excessive intolerance from a government with no regard for free speech.\n*   A fresh attempt to convene the session began on 2 March 1932. After repeated petitions, the Police Department minister Karnal Trench indicated the matter would be decided within six months, later postponing his reply further.\n*   Madapati Hanumantharao and Burgula Ramakrishnarao argued in this dispute that their movement was purely non-political, concerned only with libraries and the spread of knowledge, invoking the precedent of the library movement in Bengal, where such movements had been associated with revolutionary societies but were still permitted. \n\n*   They asked whether the government feared the community could not afford to pay a possible fine, and affirmed it was ready to bear whatever expense was needed to hold the sabha, since dropping it would lower the community''s standing in the eyes of the state''s people. Karnal Trench nonetheless refused permission.\n\n*   Permission was ultimately obtained only after community leaders accepted \"Bramhandam\" of government conditions in full.\n\nThe third Andhra Mahasabha was eventually held at **Khammam Mettu**, presided over by **Pulijala Rangarao**, a Nalgonda advocate. At this session, unlike earlier sessions, the government banned discussion of social issues altogether, permitting only non-political matters. The Collector repeatedly sent word to the presiding officers to keep proceedings within bounds even in the middle of the night while the session was underway.\n\nFaced with the choice of what to discuss if neither politics nor social reform were allowed, the leaders settled on **farmers'' problems**, and resolutions on agrarian issues were passed. It was at this third Mahasabha that farmers first gained real prominence in the movement, followed also by farm labourers.\n\n*   **Third Andhra Mahila Sabha:** presided over by Smt. Ellapragada Seetakumari.\n\nBeyond the farmers'' resolutions, the third Mahasabha also passed resolutions on the eradication of the Devadasi and Begar systems, strengthening the education system, extending the benefits of the postal system to the public, and using the religious cess collected from merchants and businessmen to fund primary education.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p6", "partName": "Fourth Andhra Mahasabha — Sircilla (1935)", "bodyMd": "The fourth Andhra Mahasabha was held in **Sircilla**, Karimnagar district, from 26–28 January 1935, under the presidency of **Madapati Hanumantha Rao**; his wife, **Madapati Manikyamma**, presided over the accompanying Mahila Sabha.\n\n*   Delegates at Sircilla made a special effort to encourage speaking in Telugu, and pride in the Telugu language and culture found renewed encouragement at this session.\n*   It was at this Sircilla session that the Andhra Mahasabha itself adopted a formal set of rules (niyamavali) for the organization, incorporating the aspirations of the youth alongside the movement''s existing goals.\n*   Resolutions passed here (12 in total, including amendments to the Andhra Mahasabha''s own rules) covered: promotion of education, compulsory primary education, higher education for Andhra girls through the mother tongue, abolition of child marriage, protection of the rights of jagir tenants, village reconstruction, facilities for farmers, relief for indebted farmers, local self-government, and the upliftment of depressed classes.\n*   Six months after the Sircilla session, Hanumantha Rao met the government''s Propaganda Department chief Aliyawar Jung to press the demand that Andhra-majority districts in the state be merged with the Andhra districts of Madras. The government replied that it suspected this demand actually aimed at merging all Andhra districts of the state with those of Madras, and Hanumantha Rao expressed doubt about how the matter had reached the political department.\n*   The leaders, however, denied that this had ever been the movement''s actual objective, and the government dismissed the demand as constitutionally untenable.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p7", "partName": "Fifth Andhra Mahasabha — Shadnagar (1936)", "bodyMd": "The fifth Andhra Mahasabha was held at **Shadnagar** in 1936, under the presidency of **Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy**.\n\n*   This was the first Andhra Mahasabha not held in a village, a samsthanam, or a taluk headquarters — instead it was convened in the Kishen Prasad Bahadur jagir near Hyderabad, reflecting the state''s growing tolerance toward the sabha by this stage.\n*   Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy, who was elected president here, was being groomed by Kottaal Venkataramireddy for future leadership. Encouraged by Hanumantha Rao, he had joined the Andhra Jana Sangham and was active in public-welfare work; before this, he had not been active as an \"Andhra\" figure specifically in the Hyderabad legislative assembly.\n*   He was regarded by both the public and the government as upright and impartial, though his outlook reflected a somewhat pronounced pro-Andhra regional bias, and he was proficient in Telugu and Urdu. He later went on to hold ministerial office after self-government and to become Deputy Chief Minister of Andhra Pradesh; a district was subsequently named after him (\"Rangareddy district\").\n*   **Fifth Andhra Mahila Sabha:** presided over by **Burgula Ananta Lakshmi Devi**, giving particular attention to farmers'' problems at this women''s session.\n*   The government''s attitude toward the Shadnagar sessions showed some hostility: it insisted the organizers advertise it as being held \"under government auspices\" and set up an exhibition of government departments at the venue, for which purpose a special structure was built at Shadnagar under the supervision of Vallури Basavaraju.\n*   This was V.B. Raju''s first participation in an Andhra Mahasabha; he was by then already an established, well-known engineer in Hyderabad, still young at the time.\n*   Because Shadnagar was close to Hyderabad, a large number of leaders and workers attended.\n*   The Sabha opposed the _matantarikarana_ (religious conversion) campaign undertaken by Bahadur Yar Jung; Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy and his co-workers personally visited households where people had converted to Islam to learn of the hardships faced by converts.\n*   The Shadnagar sabha also called for education for the people through their mother tongue, and demanded a total ban on liquor.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p8", "partName": "Sixth Andhra Mahasabha — Nizamabad (1935)", "bodyMd": "By this stage the political climate was shifting: under the Government of India Act of 1935, elections had brought Congress governments to power in nine British Indian provinces, and this naturally influenced political currents within Hyderabad State. Muslims in the state were also pressing for reforms, and the government could not entirely refuse them; it appointed a reform committee under Diwan Bahadur S. Aravamudu Ayyangar.\n\nIt was against this changed backdrop that the sixth Andhra Mahasabha convened at **Nizamabad** under **Mandamula Narasimha Rao**.\n\n*   Narasimha Rao was the editor of the Urdu daily _Rayyat_, distinguished for its style and a paper that rendered great service to the Andhrodyamam. He was deeply affected by farmers'' problems, and whenever legislative measures were needed to address them, the government would bring in another individual to introduce the relevant bill in his place.\n*   He maintained friendly relations with Muslim leaders in the state. After self-government, his brother-in-law Burgula Ramakrishna Rao''s government appointed him chairman of the Land Commission, and he later became a minister after the formation of Andhra Pradesh.\n*   At Nizamabad, the presidency was, as usual, held by a state resident, and delegates resolved that they must act with a sense of responsibility toward the state. Provincial-style reforms were sought following the British Indian pattern, and resolutions were framed accordingly.\n*   Through this session the Andhra Mahasabha''s true character became clearer, and the aspirations and intentions of the Andhra people became more openly expressed; it also marked the point at which the movement acquired real political weight within the state.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p9", "partName": "Seventh Andhra Mahasabha — Malkapuram (1938)", "bodyMd": "The seventh Andhra Mahasabha was held at Malkapuram in 1938 under **Mandumula Ramachandra Rao''s** presidency, at a time of acute land-revenue grievance in Telangana:\n\n*   In Telangana, land assessed as _tari_ (wet/irrigated) land was taxed at rates ranging from Rs. 15 to Rs. 22 per acre, compared with Rs. 5 to Rs. 10 per acre in British India.\n*   When Prakasam Pantulu became Revenue Minister in Madras, he reduced the tax rate there by roughly one-fourth (25%); the Sabha demanded that Telangana''s rates be reduced in line with the Madras pattern.\n*   On the occasion of the Nizam''s Silver Jubilee, the Diwan had granted an overall remission of Rs. 40 lakhs, of which the Sircilla region alone received Rs. 42 lakhs in remission. Special permission was required even to conduct personal (individual) inspection of the survey-settlement staff.\n*   The Sabha called for removing this discrepancy between the Diwan''s remission figures and the Sircilla-area figures.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p10", "partName": "Eighth Andhra Mahasabha — Chilkuru (1941)", "bodyMd": "The eighth Andhra Mahasabha was held in 1941 at **Chilkuru** village, Huzurnagar taluk, Nalgonda district, under the young leader **Ravi Narayana Reddy**.\n\n*   Narayana Reddy was among the state''s first satyagrahis, and rose in politics as a young leader with socialist leanings within the State Congress. He holds a place in Telangana''s history from the movement''s beginning to its end, and served as a steadfast leader (_kanaka nilichina karthadhari_) throughout this struggle.\n*   At this session, the constitution was amended and the membership fee reduced from one rupee to four annas.\n*   After the eighth Mahasabha, a week-long campaign known as **\"Begar Week\"** was conducted, protesting forced/unpaid labour.\n*   From this point onward, the Andhra Mahasabha also began paying attention to Communist-organized programmes.\n*   **Mahila Sabha:** presided over by **Rangamma Obul Reddy**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p11", "partName": "Ninth Mahasabha — Dharmavaram (1942)", "bodyMd": "The ninth Andhra Mahasabha was held at **Dharmavaram**, Warangal district, in 1942, under **Madiraju Ramakoteswara Rao**.\n\n*   Communists and anti-Communists within the sabha openly debated their differing approaches to world affairs at this session, and it was here that the theme of the national-flag movement (_jatiyapataka udyamam_) first entered public discussion.\n*   This session was held two years after the eighth Mahasabha, with Mandumula Narasimha Rao presiding over its Mahila Sabha. Rangamma Obul Reddy again presided over the women''s session, which was noted for being the largest of the joint women''s sessions held alongside the Andhra Mahasabhas.\n*   By the Dharmavaram session, three factions had emerged within the Andhra Mahasabha: Congress-aligned moderates, Communists, and a middle group. Government recognition of the Andhra Mahasabha''s growing public support led to it being acknowledged as a major organization in the state.\n*   As the Andhra Mahasabha increasingly took on the character of a people''s organization (_prajasangha_), class divisions surfaced openly — landed-interest moderates on one side, and Communists championing farmers'' interests against them on the other, leading to friction between the two camps.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p12", "partName": "Tenth Mahasabha — Hyderabad (1943)", "bodyMd": "The tenth Andhra Mahasabha was held in the state capital, Hyderabad, on 23–25 May 1943.\n\n*   The session was grandly organized at Reddy Hostel; Madapati Hanumantha Rao and his followers attended in large numbers, as did a significant contingent of Communist sympathizers.\n*   The presidential contest was between **Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy** and **Baddam Yellareddy**; the moderate candidate Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy won.\n*   Baddam Yellareddy, Arutla Lakshminarasimha Reddy, and Ravi Narayana Reddy were appointed as working-committee members. They were known as respected Communists and were treated with special regard.\n*   Because the session was held in Hyderabad, it gained added prominence; Sarojini Naidu, Raja Bahadur Venkataramareddy, Major M.J. Naidu, and several samsthana chiefs took part.\n*   The chief demand debated was the establishment of a form of \"responsible government\" in Hyderabad State. This session was a major turning point, since it marked the first time a clear rivalry over control of an organization arose openly between Communists and Congress-aligned members, with the Congress side winning a majority within the Andhra Mahasabha.\n*   In 1942, Communists had opposed the Quit India movement, branding it a \"movement against the people''s interest,\" which by then had already made them controversial at the all-India level.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p13", "partName": "Subsequent Sessions (11th–13th) and the Moderate–Extremist Split", "bodyMd": "**Eleventh Mahasabha — Bhuvanagiri (1944):** Held on 27–28 May 1944 under **Ravi Narayana Reddy**. The influence of ideological factions (_chilika prabhavam_) was conspicuous at this session, and many who had been prominent leaders and workers since the start of the Andhrodyamam did not take part.\n\nNational, communal, and international issues were extensively debated, and the membership fee was reduced from four annas to one anna.\n\n**Twelfth Mahasabha — Madikonda (1945):** Held on 21–23 March 1945 in Madikonda, Warangal district, under **Mandumula Narasimha Rao**. Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy took particular interest in ensuring this session''s success.\n\n**Thirteenth Mahasabha — Kandi (1946):** Held on 10–12 May 1946 at Kandi, Medak district, under **Sardar Jamalapuram Keshava Rao**, a leading figure in the State Congress satyagraha movement and a committed nationalist. After this, no leader of comparable stature emerged again for the Khammam district Congress.\n\n**By 1946, the movement in Telangana — which had begun as a limited cultural movement (Andhrodyamam) — had transformed step by step into a revolutionary movement.** This thirteenth session was the last of its kind: the very first session had been held in Jogipet, Medak district, and, in a symbolic circle, the last was also held in Medak district, at Kandi.\n\nFollowing this session, moderate leaders within the Andhra Mahasabha moved into the Congress party, while the extremists joined the Communist Party. Until that time, the single major organization that had represented the Andhra people had been the Andhra Mahasabha alone.\n\n### Summary Table — Andhra Mahasabha Sessions (1930–1946)\n\n| No. | Year | Venue | President |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1930 | Jogipet | Suravaram Pratapa Reddy |\n| 2 | 1931 | Devarakonda | Burgula Ramakrishna Rao |\n| 3 | 1934 | Khammam Mettu | Pulijala Ranga Rao |\n| 4 | 1935 | Sircilla | Madapati Hanumantha Rao |\n| 5 | 1936 | Shadnagar | Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy |\n| 6 | 1935 | Nizamabad | Mandamula Narasimha Rao |\n| 7 | 1938 | Malkapuram | Mandumula Ramachandra Rao |\n| 8 | 1941 | Chilkuru | Ravi Narayana Reddy |\n| 9 | 1942 | Dharmavaram | Madiraju Ramakoteswara Rao |\n| 10 | 1943 | Hyderabad | Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy |\n| 11 | 1944 | Bhuvanagiri | Ravi Narayana Reddy |\n| 12 | 1945 | Madikonda | Mandumula Narasimha Rao |\n| 13 | 1946 | Kandi | Jamalapuram Keshava Rao |", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p14", "partName": "Women''s Participation — Andhra Mahila Sabhas", "bodyMd": "The Andhra Mahila Sabhas were held independently alongside each Andhra Mahasabha, functioning entirely without interference from the men''s sessions — the first all-Telangana women''s sabhas of their kind. They strengthened women''s organizational goals, worked for the upliftment of women, and helped awaken vitality and development among them.\n\nWomen who otherwise avoided public gatherings came forward enthusiastically to attend the Mahila Sabhas.\n\nGiven the low status of women''s rights across India at the time, these sabhas pursued whatever progressive change was practicable. They supported widow remarriage; opposed polygamy, child marriage, and prostitution; called for daughters to receive a share in parental property; and demanded representation for women in the legislature.\n\nThe patronage extended by the Andhra Mahasabha leaders and the freedom given to these sabhas were praiseworthy. However senior a leader, none was permitted to interfere in the discussions of the Mahila Sabhas, which were conducted with firm independence and free from disorderly disruption.\n\n**Presidents of the Andhra Mahila Sabhas, in order:** Nadimpalli Sundaramma; T. Varalakshmamma; Ellapragada Seetakumari; Madapati Manikyamma; Burgula Ananta Lakshmi Devi; Nandagiri Indira Devi; Yogya Sheela Devi; Rangamma Obul Reddy; Rangamma Obul Reddy; Nimmagadda Sathyavathi; Ellapragada Seetakumari.\n\nAt Sircilla (the fourth Mahasabha), the women''s session was held under Madapati Manikyamma; it was there that Ellapragada Seetakumari raised the idea of forming a separate \"Andhra Yuvathi Mandali\" (Andhra Young Women''s Association).", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 15, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p15", "partName": "Andhra Yuvathi Mandali", "bodyMd": "Following the suggestion raised at the Sircilla Andhra Mahila Sabha, Ellapragada Seetakumari met Burgula Ramakrishna Rao''s wife, Smt. Ananta Lakshmi Devi, and laid the groundwork for founding the Andhra Yuvathi Mandali. She then met further with the Rani of Gadwal, Adilakshmi Devi, and the three of them established the Andhra Yuvathi Mandali in 1935.\n\nThe Rani of Gadwal served as its president, Burgula Ananta Lakshmi Devi as vice-president, and Seetakumari as secretary. This was 15 years before Durgabai Deshmukh''s Hyderabad Andhra Mahila Sabha was founded.\n\n*   Later, accomplished Andhra women in the city were drawn onto its committee, including Illindala Saraswati Devi (treasurer) and Illindala Shyamala Devi, among other office-bearers.\n*   The Mandali began its activities soon after formation. The Gadwal Rani Adilakshmi Devi, using her special influence, arranged for shops on Reddy Hostel Road to be given rent-free to support the association''s work.\n*   A staff member was appointed to procure good books from the Sri Krishnadevaraya Andhra Bhasha Nilayam library and elsewhere, which were sent out weekly to women who wished to read. Through this the Mandali promoted language development and dispelled ignorance among women.\n*   The Andhra Yuvathi Mandali, founded in 1935, went on in 1952 to acquire its own premises through the efforts of the era''s leading figures, built the Andhra Yuvathi Mandali building, and opened a primary school there. This grew steadily and became a high school by 1954.\n*   In 1955 a child-development centre (Shishu Vikas Vihar) was added, followed by CMPF women''s hostels, a library, and a tailoring-training centre. The Mandali went on to expand into numerous festivals and literary gatherings, reaching a high level of distinction.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 16, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch19-andhra-mahasabha-p16", "partName": "State-Level Women''s Sabhas", "bodyMd": "Although Hyderabad State had a numerical majority of Telangana residents, Andhras had no representation in government employment or in high offices, nor was there any organizational framework guaranteeing this. New temples could not be built anywhere in the state, and repairs to existing ones required special permission. Public meetings and assemblies were barred under Gasthi Nishan-53 (Circular 53).\n\nFacing such restrictions, the Telugu people''s self-respect had to be asserted step by step. Women''s status was even more constrained, hedged with numerous further restrictions and limitations.\n\nIn 1916, Mrs. Cousins visited Hyderabad, at a time when the All India Women''s Conference had already been established in Delhi. In Hyderabad city, Smt. Sarojini Naidu, Lady Hamida Hyder, and Rustomji Sardarji, among others, founded a women''s association for education and social organization at this gathering, with Rajkumari Dhanraj Girji elected as its president.\n\n*   Sarojini Naidu was deeply engaged in national Indian politics; other women leaders organized sabhas focused on women''s education and social advancement.\n*   After the Nizam government''s Kotwal took over the running of this body, several appealing programmes followed. Membership, however, remained largely confined to the wives of officials and the wealthy rather than reaching ordinary women.\n*   In those days, the Nizam''s senior official Durru Shehvar Begum served as the association''s president and patron. Wives of British ministers such as Lady Tussaud and Lady Trench, along with Christian women workers such as Cornelius and Williams, also took part in its activities.\n*   Later, Rangamma Obul Reddy, Rani Saralamdevi, and Sundaramma also worked to bring about change in this body.\n\nAs the reform movement grew and the influence of the national movement spread, the women of Telangana came, gradually, to take their place within the wider national movement.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter2-ikshvakus', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p1", "partName": "Sources for Ikshvaku History", "bodyMd": "Both archaeological and literary sources exist for Ikshvaku history.\n\n**Archaeological sources**\n\n*   Inscriptions, coins, monuments and pieces of architecture/sculpture.\n*   **Key inscription sites:** Nagarjunakonda, Jaggayyapeta (Krishna District), Ramireddi Palli, and Phanigiri.\n*   Inscriptions were written in Prakrit and the Brahmi script; from the time of **Ehuvala Santamula** onward, inscriptions began to be issued in **Sanskrit**.\n*   **Four classes of Ikshvaku inscriptions:**\n    1.  Carved Mahastupa and Ayaka Pillar inscriptions\n    2.  Chaityagriha inscriptions\n    3.  Detached pillar inscriptions\n    4.  Inscriptions on sculpture\n*   **Coins:** Found at Nagarjunakonda, Phanigiri, Nelakondapalli, Vaddemanu (Mahaboobnagar District) and Yeleswaram (Nalgonda District) — indicating the kingdom''s extent covered these areas. Unlike the Satavahanas, whose coins are found extensively, Ikshvaku coins are limited in number and small in size; the Ikshvakus did not issue gold or silver coins.\n*   At Nagarjunakonda itself, 148 coins of Satavahana kings have also been found, showing the site''s importance continued from Satavahana times. Roman coins were found too — coins of the Roman empress Tiberian Hedrian Faustina at Nagarjunakonda, and coins of Roman emperors such as Septimas Severes at Yeleswaram.\n*   **Religious/architectural remains:** Buddhist stupas, chaityas, toranas and sculptures at Nagarjunakonda, Ramireddi Palli, Nelakondapalli, Phanigiri and Anupu — informative about contemporary religious and social conditions.\n*   **Excavation history at Nagarjunakonda fort:** Buddhist stupas, viharas, chaityas, aramas, temples and bathing ghats of the Ikshvaku period (3rd century CE) have been unearthed here.\n    *   1926 — A. Ranaganadha Saraswati, Telugu Assistant in the Archaeological Department, first identified the remains at Nagarjunakonda.\n    *   1954 — Large-scale excavations carried out under Rayaprolu Subrahmanyam. Ancient sculptures, inscriptions and other objects were unearthed; some were shifted to the top of Nagarjunakonda hill, and some to the nearby Anupu.\n\n**Literary sources**\n\n*   **Puranas:** Matsya, Vayu, Brahmanda and Vishnu Puranas are important — they give the names of Ikshvaku kings and their periods of rule. Nearly all of them draw on information originally given in the Bhavishya Purana (written in the 2nd century CE). The Puranas provide dynastic details up to the 4th century CE, i.e., up to the start of Gupta rule (Chandragupta I).\n*   **Dharmamritam** (11th century CE Kannada work, based on an ancient Prakrit Kavya by Nyayasena): states that Yasodhara, an Ikshvaku king of Angadesa, established a kingdom at the mouth of the Krishna river, with Pratipalapuram (Bhattiprolu) as his capital.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p2", "partName": "Original Home of the Ikshvakus", "bodyMd": "There is no unanimous opinion among historians on the Ikshvakus'' original home.\n\n**\"Andhrabhrityulu\" theory:** In the beginning, the Ikshvakus were Andhrabhrityulu (servants of the Andhras) — they served as Mahatalavaras and Mahasenapatis under the Satavahanas. Like the Satavahanas, they had names starting with their mothers'' names and ending in \"anaka\" (with \"Skandha\"), showing clear Satavahana influence on their naming tradition.\n\n**Puranic tradition (North Indian origin):**\n\n*   Per the Vayu Purana, Ikshvakuvu was the head of the Suryavamsa, the eldest among Manuvu''s nine sons. He ruled with Ayodhya as his capital and had a hundred sons.\n*   After Ikshvakukuvu, Vikakshi (the eldest son) became king of Ayodhya. Of the remaining fifty sons, the rest became heads of smaller kingdoms in Northern India, and the remaining forty-nine princes established kingdoms in Southern India.\n*   Per the Vishnu Purana, the Kosala kingdom (Southern region) was established by Kusha, ruling from Kosasthali.\n*   Bauddhayana (a writer of the Dharmasastra) records the South Indian practice of marrying the daughter of one''s paternal aunt. Since Virapurushadatta married two daughters of his aunt, some scholars conclude the Ikshvakus were South Indians only — this marriage practice was not found among North Indians.\n\n**Linguistic/ethnic theories:**\n\n*   Stenkonow and Vogel: opined the Ikshvakus were Kannadigas (Western Deccan), placing them in the Dravidian language family.\n*   Dr. K. Rajagopalachari: stated the Ikshvakus were Tamils.\n*   Rapson and Buhler: opined the Ikshvakus came on exile from Kosala in Northern India.\n*   Kannada Jain work \"**Dharmamritam**\": states Yasodhara, king of Angadesa, established a kingdom at the mouth of the Krishna river, with Pratipalapuram (Bhattiprolu) as capital.\n*   Bishop Caldwell: stated the Ikshvakus were local Andhras only, not exiles from Northern India.\n*   B.S.L. Hanumantha Rao: opined the Ikshvakus were old local accountants, with \"Ikshu\" as their symbol.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p3", "partName": "Sriparvatiya Ikshvakus — Origin", "bodyMd": "Historians also differ on the meaning and origin of the term \"Ikshvakus.\"\n\n*   The Puranas and Buddhist works refer to them variously as Sriparvatiyas, Andhrabhrityas and Sriparvatiyandhras. The area from Nagarjunakonda up to the Nallamala hills surrounding Srisaila mountain was called **Sriparvata**.\n*   Prakrit inscriptions from **Jaggayyapeta and Nagarjunakonda** reveal that Srisantamula was an Ikshvaku Maharaja whose capital, Vijayapuri, lay on the western side of Sriparvata. During the 3rd and 4th centuries CE, Sriparvata itself carried the name Vijayapuri.\n*   Ikshvaku kings continued the Satavahana tradition of prefixing their mothers'' names before their own — a tradition that ended with them, as later dynasties (Pallavas, Brihatpalayanas, Anandagotris, Salankayanas) did not follow it.\n*   The titles \"Siri\" and \"Sami\" (as in Kodabalasiri, Santisiri, Hammasiri) indicate that the Ikshvakus were Satavahana bhrityas (servants/feudatories), and they also held the office of mahatalavara under the Satavahanas.\n*   **K.P. Jaiswal''s conclusion:** Ikshvakus and Sriparvatiyas are one and the same, and Sriparvatiyandhras were those belonging to the Andhra-Satavahana bhritya dynasty. Hence Sriparvatiyas, Ikshvakus and Andhrabhrityas are one and the same and not different — this is the settled scholarly position on the naming question.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p4", "partName": "Chronology of the Ikshvakus", "bodyMd": "There is no unanimity among historians regarding the exact rule period of the Ikshvakus, though the broad frame of 220–300 CE (80 years) is accepted.\n\n**Textual basis for chronology:**\n\n*   Matsya Purana states that after the Andhras, their bhrityas the Sriparvatiyas — seven kings in all — ruled for 52 years.\n*   Basing this on the word \"Dvipanchasatam\" in the Matsya Purana, Pargiter opined that either 52 years or 100 years should be considered.\n*   Inscriptions issued in the 13th regnal year of Santamula show he ruled for at least 13 years. After him, his son Virapurushadatta ruled for twenty years. The latter''s son, Ehuvala Santamula, ruled for 24 years. Per the Phanigiri inscription, Rudrapurushadatta reigned for 18 years.\n\n**D.C. Sircar''s reconstruction** (rule period at least 75 years, based on Nagarjunakonda inscriptions and the Virapurushadatta inscription):\n\n| Ruler | Reign (Sircar) |\n|---|---|\n| Virapurushadatta | 255 – 275 CE |\n| Ehuvala Santamula | 280 – 335 CE |\n| Rudrapurushadatta | 335 – 345 CE |\n\nSircar dated the Nagarjunakonda inscription to 278 CE and held that the Abhiras defeated the Ikshvakus and controlled Nagarjunakonda for five years (275–280 CE). Maremanda Rama Rao and V.V. Mirasi rejected this view, arguing that extensive Buddhist and Hindu construction during Virapurushadatta''s and Ehuvala Santamula''s reigns would not have been possible if an Abhira conquest had actually occurred. Rama Rao held instead that the kings named in the Abhira Vasusena inscription were relatives of the Ikshvakus, and that the inscription simply records their meeting on the occasion of the installation of the idol of Ashtabhuja Swamy.\n\n**P.V. Parabrahma Sastry''s chronology:**\n\n| Ruler | Reign |\n|---|---|\n| Santamula | 230 – 245 CE |\n| Virapurushadatta | 245 – 270 CE |\n| Ehuvala Santamula | 270 – 285 CE |\n| Rudrapurushadatta | 285 – 300 CE |\n\n**Final/accepted chronology used for the reign-by-reign account below** (220–300 CE, 80 years, per the Manchikallu inscription evidence for the Pallava invasion marking the kingdom''s decline around 300 CE, and no difference of opinion on the Satavahana fall being placed at 220–225 CE):\n\n| Ruler | Reign |\n|---|---|\n| Santamula | 220 – 233 CE |\n| Virapurushadatta | 233 – 253 CE |\n| Ehuvala Santamula | 253 – 277 CE |\n| Abhira Vasusena (interim rule) | 277/278 – 283 CE |\n| Rudrapurushadatta | 283 – 301 CE |\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p5", "partName": "The Ikshvaku Rulers", "bodyMd": "### Caste of the Ikshvakus\n\nScholars hold differing opinions on the caste of the Ikshvakus — variously identified as brahmins, kshatriyas, or a local tribe.\n\n*   **Kshatriya claim:** Virapurushadatta claimed in his Nagarjunakonda inscription that the Ikshvakus were kshatriyas of the solar race, tracing descent from Sriramachandra of the Ikshvaku dynasty and from the lineage of Sakyamuni Gautama Buddha. Dr. Bhandarkar held that the Ikshvakus claimed descent from a kshatriya king of the solar family.\n*   **Local-tribe view:** The Ikshvakus were originally one of the local tribes. Ancient Indian kings customarily linked their dynasty to Puranic lineages for legitimacy.\n*   **Brahmin claim:** Since the Ikshvakus added their mothers'' brahmin gotra to their own names, some scholars concluded they were brahmins. King Santamula performed the Asvamedha, Vajpeya, Agnistoma and Agnihotra sacrifices; since only kshatriyas were traditionally held to perform such sacrifices, other scholars used this same evidence to argue the Ikshvakus were kshatriyas instead.\n*   **Bhandarkar''s mixed-lineage view:** The Ikshvakus may have descended from a mixed lineage — father a kshatriya, mother of brahmin caste. Bhandarkar further felt that the Ikshvakus may originally have belonged to local republican tribes and, after becoming rulers, attained social eminence and embraced brahmanism, much like the Satavahanas had done.\n\nAs per the inscriptions, information is available regarding only four Ikshvaku kings:\n\n1.  **Vasishtiputra Santamula** — 220–233 CE\n2.  **Madhariputra Virapurushadatta** — 233–253 CE\n3.  **Ehuvala Santamula** — 253–277 CE\n4.  **Rudrapurushadatta** — 283–301 CE\n\n### Vasishtiputra Santamula (220 – 233 CE)\n\nSantamula was the founder of the independent Ikshvaku kingdom. Joining with the Hiranyakas and members of the Pugiya dynasty, he dethroned Pulumavi IV, the Satavahana king, and declared independence. He brought Telangana and Coastal Andhra under his rule.\n\n*   **Inscriptions:** Found at Rentala and Kesanapalli. His greatness is described in the Nagarjunakonda inscriptions issued by his son, Virapurushadatta, which record that he donated crores of gold coins and thousands of cows, oxen, ploughs and acres of land.\n*   **Memorial sculpture:** On the fifth slab of his memorial stupa, Santamula is depicted clean-shaven, holding a stick, with deer skin over his shoulders, wearing ordinary clothes, barefoot, with an umbrella over his head; a servant holds a pot of water nearby, and among five Brahmins present, one receives his donation with the right hand — the scene depicts him performing the **Agnistoma sacrifice**.\n*   **Religion:** Santamula followed Vedic religion and is described in inscriptions as a worshipper of Skanda Kartikeya (Mahasena), whose worship was centred in Ujjain. He performed the Asvamedha, Agnistoma, Agnihotra and Vajapeya sacrifices — the Vajapeya being a sacrifice reserved for samrats (emperors) alone. An Asvamedha platform belonging to him has been unearthed in the Nagarjunakonda excavations. He held the title \"maharaja.\" No information survives on the extent of his empire beyond this.\n*   **Marriage alliances:** He had marriage alliances with the contemporary Pugiya and Danika dynasties.\n    *   His sister Santisri was married to Mahaskandhasri of the Pugiya dynasty, who held the ranks of Mahasenapati and Mahatalavari; inscriptions accordingly describe Santisri as Mahadanapatni and Mahatalavari. They had a son, Skandasagara.\n    *   Of his other sister, Harmyasri, details of her husband''s name and dynasty are not available.\n    *   Santamula gave his daughter, Adavi Santisri, in marriage to Skanda Visakhanaga of the Dhanika family, who held the ranks of Mahasenapati and Mahadandanayaka.\n    *   Although Santamula himself followed **Vedic religion**, both his sisters kept their faith in **Buddhism.**\n\n### Madhariputra Sri Virapurushadatta (233 – 253 CE)\n\nVirapurushadatta, son of Santamula, was the greatest ruler of the Ikshvaku dynasty. His inscriptions are found at Nagarjunakonda, Jaggayyapeta and Alluru.\n\n**Marriage alliances:** He contracted matrimonial alliances with several contemporary royal dynasties, whose support helped him establish a stable kingdom. As Per the Nagarjunakonda inscription, he had five wives, of whom four are named::\n\n*   Bapisri, daughter of his aunt (father''s sister) Harmasri\n*   Santasri, daughter of another aunt\n*   Mahadevi Rudrabhattarikaa, a Saka Kshatrapa princess of Ujjain, descended from Chasthana\n*   Mahadevi Bhattideva — his fifth wife\n\n**Daughter''s marriage:** His only daughter, Kodabalisri, was married to Vishnurudra Sivalananda Satakarni of the Chutu dynasty (Banavasi), the maharaja of Kuntaladesa. This was recorded in an **inscription issued by Kodabalisri** herself in the 11th regnal year of Ehuvala Sri Santamula.\n\n**Religion:** Virapurushadatta initially followed Vedic religion but later embraced Buddhism, and his reign is regarded as one of the brightest periods in the history of Buddhism in the Andhra region.\n\n*   A sculpture at Nagarjunakonda shows him touching a Sivalinga with his right leg. Some scholars once read this as evidence of Virapurushadatta forsaking Saivism, but it has since been established that the sculpture is only part of the Mandhata Jataka and does not actually relate to Virapurushadatta.\n*   His aunt Santisri was responsible for the Buddhist structures at Nagarjunakonda — during Virapurushadatta''s sixth regnal year, she built a Maha Chaitya at Nagarjunakonda over Buddha''s relics. As per the **Nagarjunakonda inscription**, she had great affection towards Brahmins, sramans and poor people and longed for their welfare. During Virapurushadatta''s 18th regnal year, she issued another inscription wishing for his success, better health and longer life.\n*   Because of Santisri''s influence, the Ikshvaku queens — including the five wives of Virapurushadatta — honoured and patronised Buddhism.\n\n### Ehuvala Santamula (253 – 277 CE)\n\nEhuvala Santamula also called Vasishtiputra Bahubala Santamula or Santamula II, he was the son of Mahadevi Vasishtibhattideva and Virapurushadatta.\n\n*   **Naming tradition:** The tradition of adopting the **grandfather''s name** began with the **Ikshvakus**; it was later followed by the Guptas, Vakatakas, Pallavas, Vishnukundus and Salankayanas.\n*   **Military affair:** His inscriptions have been found in the Nagarjunakonda excavations. One inscription records that before his 11th regnal year, a battle was fought in which his commander-in-chief Elisri achieved victory, attributed to the mercy of Kartikeya. This inscription was issued by Elisri himself in Ehuvala Santamula''s 11th regnal year. Elisri is also credited with building Yeleswaram town and constructing a Saiva temple there known as \"Sarvadevalaya.\"\n*   **Abhira Vasusena episode:** An inscription found in the Nagarjunakonda excavations, issued by Abhira king Vasusena in his 30th regnal year, references Avanti king Saka Rudradaman, the Abhira king Vasusena, and Vishnurudra Sivalananda Satakarni, king of Banavasi (Chutu) — recording that they installed the idol of Ashtabhujaswami. Since the reigning Ikshvaku king''s name is notably absent from this inscription, it has given rise to considerable scholarly doubt.\n    *   D.C. Sircar''s view: these kings supported Abhira Vasusena''s invasion against the Ikshvakus in capturing Vijayapuri, where they installed the idol of Ashtabhujaswami; the Abhiras ruled the Nagarjunakonda region for some years thereafter. Since the inscription of Abhira Vasusena is found in the very capital of the Ikshvaku kingdom, it has to be regarded as evidence of his invasion.\n*   **Language shift:** By Ehuvala Santamula''s time, **Sanskrit** had become stabilised as the court language in place of Prakrit.\n*   **Religion:** He followed the Vedic religion and was a devotee of Swami Mahasena. During his reign, the Kartikeya temple, Pushpabhadraswami temple, Nodigiswara temple and Devi temple, among others, were built at Vijayapuri.\n\n### Rudrapurushadatta (283 – 301 CE)\n\nBasing on the script found on coins recovered at Gurajala (Guntur District), Nagarjunakonda and Phanigiri, and on the name found in the inscriptions, historians have identified Rudrapurushadatta as the last Ikshvaku king. He was the son of Ehuvala Santamula. An inscription records that in his fourth regnal year, one Nodukasri donated land to his god Halampuraswamy — an event historians have identified with Nagulapuram in Guntur District.\n\n### Virapurushadatta II\n\nThe brother of Rudrapurushadatta, he is learnt from inscriptions to have died while still Yuvaraja (crown prince), never having ruled as king.\n\n### Decline of the Ikshvaku Kingdom\n\nThe Ikshvaku kingdom declined due to **Pallava aggression** from Kanchi. The Pallavas went on to occupy Vijayapuri, the Ikshvaku capital.\n\n*   The inscription of Pallava Simhavarma was found in Manchikallu village, Guntur District. The script of this inscription, which is in Prakrit, resembles the script of the Ikshvaku inscriptions. Among Pallava inscriptions found so far, this is the oldest — and its discovery so near the Ikshvaku capital indicates that a Pallava invasion did take place.\n*   With Pallava decline → their feudatories, the Brihatpalayanas in the Krishna valley and the Anandagotris in Guntur mandal, declared their independence.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p6", "partName": "Administrative Organization", "bodyMd": "The Ikshvakus adopted the Satavahana administrative system with only minor changes.\n\n### Central Government\n\n*   The king was the supreme authority and a despot in administration, with all powers vested in his hands. He carried on administration as detailed in the Dharmasastras and Smritis, including duties towards the Brahmins.\n*   Kings issued inscriptions describing their own victories and praising the greatness of their fathers and grandfathers. **Vasishtiputra Santamula** assumed the title \"**maharaja.**\"\n*   Kings showed active interest in conquering neighbouring kingdoms; Asvamedha, Vajapeya and Agnistoma sacrifices were performed as signs of victory.\n*   **Divine right monarchy:** a principle that existed in seed form during Satavahana times became clearly established by the time of the Ikshvakus. Through Vedic sacrifices, later kings declared themselves divine right monarchs; ministers and officials held that the king was the personification of God on earth, and that opposing the king amounted to opposing God.\n*   **Key officials:** mahatalavara, mahasenapati, mahadandanayaka and kostagarika (treasurer). One person often held two or more of these posts simultaneously — e.g., Vasishtiputra Skandasri of the Pugiya family and Vasishtiputra Skandachaliki Remmanaka of the Hiranya family held the titles of mahasenapati, mahatalavari and mahadandanayaka together.\n    *   **Mahatalavaras** were officials with feudatory status, responsible for maintaining law and order.\n    *   **Mahasenapati and Mahadandanayaka** were part of the Central Government — the Mahasenapati looked after military affairs, while the Mahadandanayaka conducted criminal trials and discharged judicial duties.\n    *   Officials frequently had marriage alliances with the royal family.\n\n### Local Government\n\n*   The empire was divided into **rashtras**. Allur and Amaravati inscriptions refer to \"rattha\" (rashtra). A rashtra consisted of several villages and was the equivalent of the \"**ahara**\" of Satavahana administration.\n*   Names of rashtras found in inscriptions include Pugi rashtra, Hiranya rashtra and Munda rashtra.\n*   \"Rathikudu\" and \"rathi,\" found in Ikshvaku inscriptions, refer to the administrator of a rashtra.\n*   Rashtras were divided at different levels with different names: five villages together were called \"**grama panchaka**.\"\n*   The administrative division \"**mahagrama**\" was kept under the control of an official called _**mahagramika**_.\n*   Village administration authority was hereditary. Ikshvaku inscriptions refer to \"**Talavara**\" as the village official; one who had authority over several village officials could be termed \"mahatalavari.\"\n\n### Judicial Administration\n\n*   The king was the highest judicial authority. The Mahadandanayaka conducted trials of crimes at the Centre and imposed punishments. Special courts also existed for judicial trials.\n*   Punishments were severe: for treason and offences against the king, the death sentence was imposed; for smaller offences, fines and beating with sticks were used.\n*   Sources on the Ikshvaku judicial system are limited, but the Palamur inscription of Madhava Varma III (Vishnukundi king, post-Satavahana age) describes several divine tests used to decide guilt, which he claims to have held personally. **Nine types of divine tests (divya):**\n    1.  **Tula-divya** — weighing the criminal to decide culpability\n    2.  **Agni-divya** — the accused walks on fire without getting burnt\n    3.  **Jala-divya** — arrows sent into water must be retrieved by diving in\n    4.  **Visha-divya** — the accused must take a ring out of a pot containing a cobra, without being bitten\n    5.  **Kosa-divya** — the accused must drink the water used to clean idols of gods/goddesses and survive\n    6.  **Tandula-divya** — rice over which mantras were administered must be chewed\n    7.  **Tapta maska-divya** — a coin must be taken out of boiling oil\n    8.  **Phala-divya** — a burning iron rod must be held, then grain pressed hard in the same hand\n    9.  **Dharmadharma-divya** — idols personifying dharma and adharma are placed in a jar; the idol the accused draws out decides the crime/guilt\n\n### Army Organization\n\n*   The Ikshvakus followed the Satavahana army organisation, with the traditional four-fold division: **chariots, elephants, cavalry and infantry.**\n*   When the king was on expedition, villagers were required to supply the army''s daily needs (milk, curd, vegetables) as well as sitting material and cots.\n*   Army strength and victories were central to a kingdom''s survival, so army maintenance received the highest percentage of the kingdom''s revenue.\n*   The king himself typically led the army at war. Enemy military strengths and weaknesses were gathered through spies, and battle plans were made accordingly.\n*   Swords and spears were the main weapons; headgear and shields provided protection; arrows were also used. Wars caused heavy damage to crops in nearby villages and brought losses and a frightful atmosphere for the people.\n\n### Income and Expenditure\n\n*   **Land revenue (\"bhaga\")** was the chief source of revenue — the king''s \"share,\" generally 1/6th of the crop.\n*   **\"Bhoga\"** was another land tax, collected by local administrators and enjoyed by them.\n*   **\"Deyam\"** (cash) and **\"meyam\"** (kind) were also collected.\n*   **\"Kara\"** was a tax levied on industries, crafts and trade; taxes were also levied on commercial items such as salt and sugar.\n*   Duty was levied on highways. Forests and green plains belonged to the king. Tax was collected on water supplied for irrigation.\n*   Among taxes levied in kind, an important one concerned cattle — agriculturists had to give the first calf born to their cows to the government as a duty (per Mirasi).\n*   **Expenditure was in four parts:**\n    \n    1.  Maintenance of the government, army maintenance, and salaries/allowances for officials\n    2.  Temple construction, worship of gods and goddesses, and celebrations\n    3.  Patronage of poets, scholars and intellectuals\n    4.  Gifts to different religions\n    \n    *   Additionally, money was spent on constructing tanks and canals to aid the growth of agriculture, since it employed the largest number of people.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p7", "partName": "Social Conditions", "bodyMd": "*   Post-Satavahana society retained the four varnas, but several mixed castes and sub-castes emerged. Every person adopted the occupation of their ancestors at birth and received the attached social status; in the later period, castes formed on occupational lines.\n*   As per Nagarjunakonda inscriptions, distinct social branches existed: Pugiyulu, Hiranyakulu, Mundalu, Dhanikulu, Nagulu, Selakulu and Kulahakulu. The Ikshvakus contracted marriage alliances with local families such as the Pugiyas and Hiranyakas, and with Ujjain Kshatrapas.\n*   **Brahmins:** their position improved through the performance of Vedic rituals by Ikshvaku kings, their employment in higher posts, and temple construction, all of which improved their economic condition. Kings gave land to Brahmins.\n    *   King''s officials were not to enter such donated villages; these villages had exemption from 18 types of taxes, and the donee (recipient) gradually grew enriched. These lands were cultivated only by Sudras.\n    *   A class of feudal lords emerged between the king and the people.\n    *   Based on the Vedic branch studied and the dharma sutras followed, Brahmins came to be divided into three branches: Vedic scholars, temple priests, and employees of the king.\n    *   By the 2nd century CE, foreign communities such as Sakas, Yavanas, Pahlavas and Abhiras had become sanskritised and joined the Indian stream of life; because they were rulers, they received kshatriya social status.\n*   **Sudras:** gained political emergence, and patronised Brahmins performing Vedic rituals also came to be considered kshatriyas.\n*   **Vaisyas:** resorted to trade and business; large businesses were run only by merchant associations, which formed into guilds. Besides Vaisyas, weavers, oil extractors and blacksmiths also conducted trade.\n*   The majority of people in society were **Sudras**, following diverse occupations. Agriculturists were called \"**halikas**.\" There were many handicraft persons — blacksmiths, potters, carpenters, weavers, washermen, barbers and leather workers.\n    *   Inscriptions of this age record donations made to the \"rathakarulu.\" These craftspeople, even today, wear the sacred thread (yagnopaveetam) and call themselves Viswabrahmins, descendants of Viswakarma. Goldsmiths, blacksmiths, kanchara, kasi (workers) and carpenters were regarded as one and the same group; kings also granted them lands.\n*   **Women:** Amaravati and Nagarjunakonda inscriptions record that women of high families made independent donations even during Ikshvaku times. Women of the royal family made free donations for the mahachaitya at Nagarjunakonda. Although Ikshvaku kings followed Vedic religion, women of their families patronised Buddhism, building many chaityas and making donations. Like the Satavahanas, Ikshvaku kings continued to add their mother''s name before their own.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p8", "partName": "Economic Conditions", "bodyMd": "### Feudalism\n\n*   The post-Satavahana state system depended on feudalism, based on the theory that the king was overlord of all land — reinforced by the system of divine right monarchy.\n*   As kings freely declared village donations to Brahmins and religious organisations, ryots (cultivators) lost their earlier rights over the land they cultivated and were reduced to the status of agricultural serfs, while the donees gained all rights over the land.\n*   In the later period, higher officials in the administrative machinery, instead of receiving salaries, were given lands yielding equivalent income.\n*   Since agriculture was vital for government revenue, kings made efforts to develop it. Santamula donated lakhs of ploughs, cows, oxen and gold. Forests were cleared and new lands brought under cultivation; canals and tanks were dug for irrigation, and evidence of such artificial construction has been unearthed at Nagarjunakonda.\n*   Land grants by kings also contributed to agricultural development — Brahmins, who received such lands, possessed knowledge of seasons, panchangam (calendar) and seeds, and the effects of soils. Donated lands were exempt from 18 types of taxes (this fact is corroborated by the Hirahadagalli and Maidavolu inscriptions of Pallava Sivaskanda Varma), and even the king''s own officials were barred from entering such donated villages.\n*   Under the new land relations of the post-Satavahana age, feudalism took root: local chiefs remained obedient to the king and paid tribute, maintaining the army and supplying it to the king in times of war; village officials, in turn, remained obedient to the local chief and remitted part of their revenue to him.\n\n### Agriculture: Crops\n\nAgriculture became the main source of livelihood for the people.\n\n*   **Main crops:** paddy, wheat, sugarcane; dry crops such as bajra and ragulu; pulses like red gram; oil seeds such as castor; cattle fodder crops like jute and cotton.\n*   Cotton was cultivated both as a dry crop and a wet crop.\n*   Coconut, a commercial crop, contributed to the economic development of coastal areas. The **Nasik inscription** records that the **Saka ruler Rishabhadatta** had donated two coconut gardens to Brahmins, consisting of 32,000 coconut trees in one garden and 8,000 in another.\n*   Since transport facilities and sea voyages were not well developed in that period, local markets alone served as trading centres for crop production.\n\n### Handicrafts and Guilds\n\n*   Excavations at Nagarjunakonda unearthed houses of craftsmen; one house yielded goldsmith''s craft materials such as \"atchulu\" and \"musalu.\" Several types of gold and silver artistic objects were found.\n*   Ikshvaku inscriptions refer to the Parnika Sreni (betel leaf craftsmen''s guild) and the Pusika Sreni (confectioners'' guild); another inscription mentions \"Kulika Pramukhudu\" (leader of the guild).\n*   The **Vilopatti inscription** indicates that handicraft industries continued in villages.\n\n### Internal and External Trade\n\n*   Trade with Rome continued during Ikshvaku times; Roman coins found at Nagarjunakonda confirm this. Vijayapuri (Nagarjunakonda) itself functioned as a Roman trade centre.\n*   Internal and external trade was controlled by **Vaisyas**.\n*   While Satavahana coins found in Telangana and Andhra Pradesh are numerous, post-Satavahana royal dynasty coins are very few. After the Satavahanas, no ruler issued coins bearing the figure of a ship.\n*   After the 3rd century CE, trade with the Roman empire continued but mostly through land routes rather than sea routes as before — as a result, trade centres of North India gained greater importance.\n*   A **Nagarjunakonda inscription** issued in the 14th regnal year of Ikshvaku king Virapurushadatta refers to **Ghantasala**, which prospered as the main port owing to its location at the main entrance of the Krishna river. Ports continuing from Satavahana times included Barukachham (Broach), Kalyani (Maharashtra), Sopara and Mysolea (Machilipatnam).\n*   **Exports from India:** spices, iron, steel, hand-woven clothes, coconuts.\n*   **Imports into India:** gold, silver, copper, intoxicants and glass material.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p9", "partName": "Cultural Development", "bodyMd": "During Ikshvaku rule, literature, architecture and art achieved remarkable progress. Vedic, Buddhist and Jain religions all flourished. Though Prakrit remained the official language, Sanskrit achieved good patronage and development. Buddhist stupas, viharas, chaityas, and the Navagraha and Sarvadevalaya Hindu temples serve as mirrors of the styles of architecture and sculpture of the age.\n\n### Religious Development — Buddhism\n\n*   Among the Ikshvaku kings, all except Virapurushadatta were followers of Vedic religion; however, their queens patronised Buddhism and made donations.\n*   By the 2nd century CE, Nagarjunakonda had become a famous Buddhist arama in India itself.\n*   **Buddhist sects at Nagarjunakonda:** Among the Mahasanghikas, the sects Poorvasaileeyulu (Pubbasailulu), Aparasaileeyulu and Bahusruteeyulu emerged — Amaravati and Nagarjunakonda served respectively as the main centres for Poorvasaileeyulu and Aparasaileeyulu. Other sects present at Nagarjunakonda included Rajagirikulu, Mahisasakulu and Siddhantikulu.\n*   Virapurushadatta''s period is regarded as the brightest one for Buddhism. His aunt Santisri, during his sixth regnal year, built a mahachaitya at Nagarjunakonda containing the dhatuvu (relics) of Buddha and erected nine ayaka pillars. She took pity on Brahmins, sramans and poor people and made several donations, wishing that all people and beings should be happy in both worlds — a sentiment reflecting Mahayana Buddhism''s emphasis on the welfare of all rather than of the self alone. The Buddhist sculptures found at Nagarjunakonda testify to the flourishing of Mahayana Buddhism there.\n*   Not only royal ladies but also common ladies made donations to Buddhist viharas and issued inscriptions — e.g., Upasika Bodisri, niece of Bodisarma (treasurer of the kingdom), built a vihara at Chuladammagiri in Vijayapuri, which became famous as the **Simhala Vihara**. Buddhist propagators from Ceylon were said to propagate dharma in Kashmir, Gandhara, China, Chilata, Tosali, Aparanta, Vanga, Vanavasa, Yavana, Damila and Palura. Kodabalisri, daughter of Virapurushadatta, is recorded to have built a vihara for the Mahisasakas. During Ehuvala Santamula''s time, his mother Mahadevi Bhattideva built a mahavihara and mahachaitya for the Bahusrutiyas.\n*   Buddhistic relations existed between Simhalam (Ceylon) and Nagarjunakonda, with Buddhist monks from Sri Lanka regularly visiting. As per Nagarjunakonda inscriptions, Mahasanghika Acharyas are recorded to have known by heart the Dighanikaya and Majjimanikaya, two of the five nikayas.\n*   **Chief Buddhist centres:** in Andhra Pradesh — Nagarjunakonda, Jaggayyapeta, Chinaganjam, Ramireddi Palli and Bavikonda; in Telangana — Nelakondapalli, Tummalagudem and Gajulabanda (Nalgonda District).\n*   As per the Digha and Majjima nikayas, Badanta Ananda belonged to the Aparamahavina saileeya branch and lived in the 3rd century CE. It was possibly on his persuasion that Virapurushadatta embraced Buddhism.\n*   Aryadeva, Dharmanandi and Badanta Ananda were disciples of Nagarjuna. Aryadeva wrote a work titled \"**Chittasuddhi Prakarana**.\" Because he made light of Vedic practices, he was murdered at the hands of Vedic followers.\n*   **Later Buddhist figures (post-Satavahana age, 3rd–early 7th century CE):** Bhavaviveka, Buddhaghosha, Dharmakirti and Siddha Nagarjuna carried on their activities in this period.\n    *   **Bhavaviveka** (5th century CE) wrote a commentary called \"Pragna Pradeepa\" on Nagarjuna''s Madhyamika Sastra.\n    *   **Buddhaghosha** was to Theravada (Hinayana) Buddhism what Acharya Nagarjuna was to Mahayana. He went to Sri Lanka intending to translate \"**Atta Kathalu**\" (written in the Sri Lankan language) into Prakrit; living at Anuradhapura Vihara, he instead wrote the work \"Visuddhamogga\" for the Theravadis there.\n    *   **Dharmakirti** was educated at Nalanda University and was a disciple of Dharmapala of Kanchi; he earned great name and fame in the world of Buddhist dharma.\n    *   **Siddha Nagarjuna** resided at Dhanyakatakam and Sriparvata. He belonged to the Vajrayana sect, which stood for tantric vada (based in mantras, tantras and magic). The Ramireddipalli inscription records that he was a Telugu person; he received rasasiddhi, swarnasiddhi and Vajrayana siddhi.\n    *   **The five acts (Pancha-makara) of Vajrayana practitioners:** madhuvu (taking intoxicating drink), mamsam (meat eating), matsyam (eating fish), mudra (body posture) and maithinam (sexual intercourse). Followers justified these acts by holding that ordinary moral principles and regulations did not apply to them.\n    *   Dhanyakatakam, Sankhavaram, Ramatirtham and Salihundam remained centres of Vajrayana practice.\n*   Buddhism, which enjoyed royal patronage and popular support during Ikshvaku times, started declining in the later period.\n\n### Religious Development — Jainism\n\nDuring the time of the Ikshvakus, Penugonda, Pithapuram, Nedunuru, Tatipaka, Aryavatam and Draksharamam were the main centres of Jainism. At Aryavatam, statues of six Tirthankaras belonging to the 3rd century CE have been found.\n\n### Religious Development — Puranic Hinduism (Saivism, Vaishnavism)\n\n*   Ikshvaku king Srisantamula performed Vedic sacrifices like Asvamedha, Vajapeya, Agnistoma and Agnihotra. He was a devotee of the Puranic gods Virupakshapati (Siva), Mahasena (Skanda) and Kartikeya.\n*   **Ehuvala Santamula** constructed a Siva temple named Pushpabhadraswamy at Nagarjunakonda. His commander-in-chief Elisri built a Siva temple for Kumaraswamy named \"Sarvadeva.\"\n*   A temple named \"Nodagisvara\" existed at Nagarjunakonda; a merchant''s daughter, Ratavasya, together with others, made donations to Nodageswaraswamy, per an issued inscription.\n*   **Sivasena**, commander-in-chief of Abhira Vasusena, is recorded (in an inscription he issued) to have built a Vaishnava temple at Nagarjunakonda known as \"Ashtabhujaswamy,\" during his attack on the Ikshvaku kingdom in the time of Ehuvala Santamula.\n*   **Mother Goddess worship** also existed during Ikshvaku times — a Hariti temple was built at Nagarjunakonda, and inscriptions record that women, for begetting children, used to offer bangles at the Saptamatrikas in the Hariti temple.\n*   Puranic, Saiva, Vaishnava and Sakteya religions were all honoured during Ikshvaku times; Skanda, Ganapati, Yaksha, and Hariti gods and goddesses were worshipped.\n\n### Language and Literature\n\n*   Though Prakrit was the official language during the time of the Ikshvakus, from the 4th century CE onward, Sanskrit occupied the place of Prakrit in inscriptions and literature.\n*   Inscriptions issued before Ehuvala Santamula''s 11th regnal year were in Prakrit; those of the later period were in Sanskrit. **Inscriptions of Virapurushadatta**, who ruled before Ehuvala Santamula, were in Sanskrit. The Sarvadeva inscription issued by Elisri, commander-in-chief of Ehuvala Santamula, was also in Sanskrit.\n*   Contemporary regional evidence: the Kondamudi inscription of Jayavarma, the Brihatpalayana king, was in Prakrit; the Salankayanas and Anandagotris used both Prakrit and Sanskrit in their inscriptions; all inscriptions of the **Vishnukundus were in Sanskrit**.\n*   Sanskrit received royal patronage; kings donated villages and lands to Vedic scholars. During Ikshvaku times, famous universities flourished at Dhanyakatakam (Amaravati) and Nagarjunakonda.\n*   **Udyotana**, who belonged to the 8th century CE, in his work \"Kuvalayamala,\" records that Buddhist monks from Sri Lanka, Tibet, Nepal and all regions of India used to come to Nagarjunakonda for education.\n*   Aryadeva, disciple of Acharya Nagarjuna, wrote the work \"**Chittasuddhi Prakaranam.**\"\n*   **Ikshvaku inscriptions** contain references to sciences such as Astronomy, Medicine and Mathematics.\n*   **Hiuen-tsang**, the Chinese traveller who visited India in the 7th century CE, recorded that the **Sriparvata Vihara** had by then been subjected to lack of patronage.\n\n### Architecture and Sculpture\n\n*   In the 3rd and 4th centuries CE, Buddhist arts and the style of architecture flourished gloriously; even though the Satavahana empire had declined, the arts of architecture and sculpture did not become extinct.\n*   **Nagarjunakonda excavations** unearthed 3rd-century-CE structures including: an impregnable fort, a moat, many buildings inside the fort, an open-air stadium, the Asvamedha platform of Santamula, and Buddhist stupas, viharas, chaityas, aramas, temples and bathing ghats.\n    *   The playground/stadium was constructed in different stories — no similar structure has been unearthed anywhere else in India. Some scholars believe it may have been built with the help of Roman experts.\n    *   Approximately 30 Buddhist aramas existed at Nagarjunakonda, built by adherents of different Buddhist branches. Nagarjunakonda stands as testimony to the changes that came about within Buddhism, as the impact of branches such as Aparamahavinasaileeya, Maha Viharavasini, Mahisasika and Bahusruteeya fell upon its architecture and sculpture.\n    *   Earlier, there had been a taboo on depicting Buddha in human form; because of support from the **Aparamahavinasaileeya branch**, statues of Buddha came to be carved in a beautiful, humanised manner.\n*   **Buddhist stupas and viharas built in the 3rd century CE** existed in Telangana at Nelakondapalli (Khammam District), Gajulabanda (Nalgonda District), Tummalagudem (Nalgonda District) and Nandikonda (Nalgonda District); in Andhra Pradesh at Goli, Chandavaram, Dupadu, Namburu, Uppugunduru and Rentala.\n*   **Three types of stupas at Nagarjunakonda:**\n    1.  Stupas built over the remains of body parts of Buddha or a leading Buddhist monk (bones, teeth, hair, nails, etc.)\n    2.  Stupas built over the begging bowls and articles used by Buddhist monks\n    3.  Stupas built as memorials of Buddha or his disciples, without remains or objects\n*   The Amaravati tradition of sculpture continued through the last phase into Ikshvaku times, reaching its greatest heights in the construction of Nagarjunakonda sculpture, which became the centre for this art form.\n    *   The practice of sculptors depicting Buddha in the form of emblems continued: his feet, a horse, the bodhi tree, the dharmachakra, a stupa and the trisula relating to his life were carved as emblems.\n    *   Stories such as the meeting of Buddha, Yasodhara and Rahul; the Sibi Jataka; and the Mandhata Jataka, among others, were carved in the chaityas at Nagarjunakonda, Ghantasala, Gummadidurru and Goli.\n    *   Masterpieces of Ikshvaku sculpture include images of Buddha, Santamula''s memorial pillar, depictions of Virapurushadatta''s hatred toward brahmana religion and his embrace of Buddhism, Virapurushadatta suppressing a rebellion, and erotic sculptures.\n*   **Nelakondapalli:** the most important stupa built during the post-Satavahana period. A mahastupa, viharas of the Chatussala type, and many man-sized Buddha statues have been unearthed here, constructed between the 3rd and 6th centuries CE. A bronze statue of Buddha and nine marble statues of Buddha were also unearthed. Because marble pieces and water containers were found near where the statues were located, V.V. Krishna Sastry opined that this was a factory where Buddha statues were prepared. Nelakondapalli Buddha images stand as specimens of Amaravati-style sculpture.\n*   **Gajulabanda:** Buddhist stupa, vihara and chaitya remains have come to light; dolls of two lions made of limestone have also been found here.\n*   **Nandikonda:** some Buddhist structures have been unearthed.\n*   **Hindu temple architecture** also commenced during Ikshvaku times. Remains at Nagarjunakonda show temples of Kartikeya, Pushpabhadra, Ashtabhujaswamy, Hariti, Kubera and the Navagrahas built during Ehuvala Santamula''s time. The Sarvadeva temple was built at Yeleswaram (Nalgonda District). Nagarjunakonda temples were built per agama sastra, with sanctum, antarala, mantapa, dhvaja stambha and prakara (compound wall).\n*   The practice of depicting stories from the Itihasas and Puranas in temple sculpture continued during Ikshvaku times. In the female sculptures at Nagarjunakonda, erotic appeal became more pronounced, and women''s sculptures were carved in a slim and attractive manner.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-ch2-ikshvakus-p10", "partName": "Summary", "bodyMd": "The Ikshvakus began their authority as feudatories of the Satavahanas and, after the latter''s decline, established independent rule while carrying forward the Satavahana administrative system and cultural trends — including, like the Satavahanas, prefixing their mothers'' names before their own. \n\nThrough marriage alliances with nearby royal dynasties, they worked to secure and expand their authority. By freely making donations of land along with oxen, ploughs and cows in the thousands, Ikshvaku rulers demonstrated their commitment to agricultural development, backed by the construction of tanks and canals. During their rule, Sudras and women gained a degree of independence and progress in the social sphere. \n\nIn the economic sphere, besides agriculture, handicraft industries and internal and external trade developed, with foreign trade carried on with Rome and Ghantasala — at the mouth of the Krishna river — serving as the main port. The spread of Buddhism, Jainism, Saivism and Vaishnavism contributed to the spread of the Sanskrit language and to progress in architecture and sculpture. \n\nThe Buddhist stupas, viharas, memorial emblems, and Saiva and Vaishnava temples that the Ikshvakus built at places like Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda, Nelakondapalli, Tummalagudem and Gajulabanda stand as ample testimony to their contribution to art and cultural development.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p1", "partName": "Origins and Significance of the Library Movement", "bodyMd": "The library movement was old in the Andhra region generally, and it began in Telangana in the early twentieth century just as it did in other parts of the country. From its earliest days, the library movement played an outstanding role in Andhra national life.\n\n*   Suravaram Pratap Reddy called the Library Movement the **first movement** in Telangana.\n*   **Father of the Telangana library movement:** Komarraju Lakshmana Rao.\n*   Libraries gave the public access to newspapers and books, and served as venues for meetings, poetry gatherings, and various organisations'' anniversary celebrations. They brought political and literary writing within easy reach of students and townspeople, feeding the public''s thirst for knowledge.\n*   It was largely through libraries that national consciousness took root in Telangana, and it was through libraries that the Andhra Movement (Andhrodyamam) spread rapidly across the state.\n\n| Library | Year | Founder(s) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Secunderabad Library | 1872 | Somasundar Mudaliyar | First library in Hyderabad and Telugu-speaking regions; merged with Mahbubia College in 1884 |\n| Shankarananda Library (Shankarmatt) & Sarvajanika Library (Secunderabad) | 1872 | Mudigonda Shankaracharyulu | — |\n| Young Men''s Improvement Society Library | 1879 | Aghoranath Chattopadhyaya | — |\n| Asafiya State Library | 1886 | Abdul Khayyum (nationalist), Moulvi Sayyad Hussain Bilgrami (Nawab), and Imadul Mulk | Established for public use; today known as the **State Central Library**, located in the Afzalgunj area |\n| Bharat Gunavardhak Association Library | 1895 | Established by Marathi patriots at Shalibanda (Laldarwaza area) | For the development of Marathi language and culture; holds rare compositions of ancient poets and books in many languages, and is still functioning |\n| Albert Reading Room (Bollarum) | 1896 | — | — |", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p2", "partName": "Sri Krishna Devaraya Andhra Basha Nilayam (1901) — The Movement''s First Library", "bodyMd": "Sri Krishna Devaraya Andhra Basha Nilayam was established at Sultan Bazar, Hyderabad, in 1901 by **Komarraju Lakshmana Rao**, in the personal house of Ravichettu Ranga Rao.\n\n*   **Founders/supporters:** Munagala zamindar Raja Nayini Venkata Ranga Rao and Ravichettu Ranga Rao.\n*   **Aims:** to adorn the Telugu mother tongue with a golden crown, eradicate illiteracy, promote historical research, and stand in opposition to the Nizam''s suppressive policies.\n*   It was the **first library established as part of Telangana''s library movement**.\n*   **Literary festivals organised by this library:**\n    *   Pothana Death Anniversary Week — 1940\n    *   Reddy Yuga Saraswatha Week — 1941\n    *   Bhuvana Vijaya Week — 1942\n    *   Ramayana Kalpa Vruksha Week — 1950", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p3", "partName": "Libraries Founded by Komarraju Lakshmana Rao and Other Early Libraries (1901–1908)", "bodyMd": "Lakshmana Rao went on to found a cluster of libraries across the region:\n\n*   **Sri Raja Raja Narendra Andhra Basha Nilayam** — Hanumakonda, 1904.\n*   **Andhra Samvardhani Library** — Secunderabad, 1905.\n*   **Vignana Chandrika Mandali** — Hyderabad, 1906. Ravichettu Ranga Rao served as its secretary. Its headquarters were at Rasidenchi Bazar, in Ravichettu Ranga Rao''s own house. It conducted novel-writing competitions and Telugu-language examinations, and published books on history, Andhra literature, and science (Vignana Shastram). The Nizam''s police kept close watch on Ravichettu''s activities, and the Mandali''s office eventually had to be shifted to Madras.\n    *   **Books published by Vignana Chandrika Mandali:** Chilkuri Veera Bhadra Rao — _Andhrula Charitra_; Gadicherla Hari Sarvothama Rao — _Abraham Lincoln History_; Achanta Lakshmipathi — _Jeeva Shastram (Biology)_; Suravaram Pratap Reddy — _Andhrula Sangika Charitra_; Khandavalli Lakshmi Rajanam — _Andhrula Charitra-Samskruthi_; Kattamanchi Ramalinga Reddy — _Arthashastra, Musalamma Maranam_; Vishwanadha Sharma — _Chemistry_.\n\nSeparately, the effort of Munagala Raja Nayini Venkata Ranga Rao, Komarraju Lakshmana Rao, Gadicherla Harisarvottama Rao, and Ayyadevara Kaleswara Rao also brought about the **Vignana Niketana Grandhalayam** in Khammam, realised through Kodati Narayana Rao''s persistent effort.\n\nSeparately, Hanumakonda''s **Rajarajanarendra Andhra Basha Nilayam**, established in 1908, counted **\"Andhra Pitamaha\" Madapati Hanumantha Rao** among its secretaries.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p4", "partName": "Reddy Hostel Library (1918) and Suravaram Pratap Reddy", "bodyMd": "*   In 1918, **Raja Bahadur Venkat Rami Reddy** established Reddy Hostel in Abids, Hyderabad, to provide accommodation for students. The Reddy Hostel Library was set up within its premises.\n*   From 1924–32, **Suravaram Pratap Reddy** served as Secretary of the Reddy Hostel Library.\n*   Suravaram wrote a book, _Telangana-Andhrula Kartavyam_, on the maintenance of libraries and the duties of those in the library movement.\n*   The Nizam government had banned Veer Savarkar''s _War of Independence_, and when a copy of this banned book was found in the Reddy Hostel Library, Suravaram lost his position as secretary as a result.\n*   Other key figures of the movement alongside Komarraju Lakshmana Rao: **Somanatha Rao** and **Kodati Narayana Rao** (whose autobiography, _Narayanatrayam_, records the library movement).", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p5", "partName": "Other Libraries Founded Across Telangana", "bodyMd": "| Library | Year | Place | Founder |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andhra Basha Nilayam | 1910 | Khammam | — |\n| Pratapa Rudra Andhra Basha Nilayam | 1913 | Madikonda, Warangal | — |\n| Samskruta Kala Vardhini | 1913 | Secunderabad | — |\n| Andhra Vignana Prakashini | 1918 | Suryapet | Puvvada Venkatapayya (also founded _Krushi Pracharini Grandhamala_ and printed Telugu books; dismissed from his government job by the Nizam administration for this) |\n| Andhra Saraswaati Library | 1918 | Nalgonda | Shabnavisu Venkata Rama Narasimha Rao |\n| Basha Kalpavalli Library | 1920 | Secunderabad | Maduri Raghavulu |\n| Sri Vignana Vidhyuth Pravahini Andhra Basha Nilayam | 1918 | Khammam | Published a monthly magazine, _Jyothi_, edited by Yellapragada Krishnamurthy and Kodati Narayana Rao; later converted into a students'' library |\n| Vignana Pracharini Printing Press | 1919 | Inugurthi, Mahabubabad | Oddiraju brothers |\n| Bahiramiya Library | 1921 | Kolanupaka | — |\n| Bala Saraswathi Library | 1923 | Afzalgunj | — |\n| Vemana Andhra Basha Nilayam | 1923 | Hyderabad | Konda Venkat Ranga Reddy (funded from his own money) |\n| Vignana Pracharini Library (also called Osmania Andhra Basha Nilayam) | 1923 | Manthanni | Avadhani Krishnaiah |\n| Andhra Student Association Library | 1923 | Khammam | — |\n| Andhra Sodari Samaja Grandhalayam | 1925 | Hyderabad | — |\n| Adi Hindu Library | 1926 | Hyderabad | B. S. Venkat Rao |\n| Deccan Vaishya Sanga Grandhalayam | 1926 | — | — |\n| Jogipet Library | 1930 | Medak | — |\n| Vidyabi Vardhini Grandhalayam | 1939 | Tatapalli, Karimnagar | Boinpally Venkat Rama Rao (also set up a mobile library, a Harijan school, and a collection of Oriental manuscripts) |\n| Gandhi Library | 1940 | Hanumakonda | Bandari Naga Bhushan Rao |\n| Raithu Grandhalayam | 1941 | Chilukuru | Raavi Narayana Reddy |\n| Sri Hanumadaandhra Grandhalayam | 1943 | Mulkapalli, Khammam | Established by Andhra Sarasvatha Parishad |\n| Viveka Vikasini Grandhalayam | 1943 | Pillalamarri, Suryapet | Ummethula Ramanuja Rao |\n\nBy 1922, on the eve of the Andhra Movement gathering pace in Telangana, roughly **fifteen libraries** had already been established, including the Sabdanusasana Grandhalayam (Warangal), Gyana Vidyut Pravahini Basha Nilayam (Khammam), Mahaboobia Grandhalayam (Errupalem), Siddhi Maleswara Grandhalayam (Remidicherla), Andhra Saraswathi Grandha Nilayam (Nalgonda), Balasaraswathi Andhra Grandhalayam (Golconda Gadda, Hyderabad), and Vemana Andhra Basha Nilayam (Nampalli).", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p6", "partName": "Innovations in Reaching the Public", "bodyMd": "**Telangana''s first mobile library — T. K. Balaiah**\n\n*   T. K. Balaiah, a taluka officer at Armoor in Nizamabad district, started Telangana''s first mobile library, carrying books by bullock cart to remote villages and distributing them there.\n\n**Korutla''s rice-for-books model**\n\n*   At Korutla in Karimnagar district, Satavadhani Krishnamacharyulu went door to door collecting rice, sold the rice he had gathered, and used the proceeds to buy books and periodicals. Library workers also toured on cycles to distribute books — an early form of the \"travelling library.\"\n\n**Arjun Pusthaka Bandagar (Suryapet)**\n\n*   Established and secretly run by Dharma Biksham, Kannaiah, and their friends, this book depot supplied Arya Samaj literature and the _Golconda_, _Mejaan_, and _Raiyat_ newspapers, and became a centre for political and literary discussion.\n\n**The Chennur \"feast\" library (Adilabad district)**\n\n*   Chennur''s Brahmin-majority public loved ceremonial feasts but would not come forward to fund a library. A Maharashtrian doctor at the government hospital, understanding this, announced that he wished to host a _samaradhana_ (ceremonial feast) and asked the village for the needed items — rice, jaggery, betel nut, coconut, dry fruit, vermicelli, and more — which poured in generously, since everyone was eager to meet a doctor''s request but too polite to ask about his fee directly.\n*   The doctor then sold off the surplus feast provisions the next day for about three thousand rupees, used the money to buy roughly three hundred _gajas_ of land at the village centre, put up a tin shed, and bought petromax lamps and books, naming the result the **\"Godavari Vachanalayam.\"** He then told the village elders that the library had been built from what remained of the feast they themselves had funded. More libraries followed in Adilabad town after this.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p7", "partName": "Obstacles Faced by the Library Movement", "bodyMd": "Running a library under Nizam rule was far from easy, and the movement''s workers faced repeated official harassment:\n\n1.  **Suryapet — Andhra Prakasini Grandhalayam seized.** Just as the Andhra Movement was about to gather pace, the local collector abruptly sealed this library, treating its public-funded, community-run operation as an act of overreach by its officer-in-charge, and demanding to know whether government permission had been obtained to run it.\n    *   When the workers said no, the collector ordered the library shut.\n    *   The matter was carried to Madapati Hanumantha Rao''s group in Hyderabad, who wrote to the government''s Home Secretary, Sir Akbar Hyderi (also chairman of the district local fund board, from which libraries were known to receive grants).\n    *   The Home Secretary confirmed no government permission was required to run a library, and the library reopened.\n2.  **Madikonda library** was similarly denied permission to operate for a full year.\n3.  **Karimnagar district (Sirisilla)** — when a visiting tahsildar inspected the library there, he reportedly remarked that government business was conducted entirely in Urdu and there was no need to \"accumulate dead Telugu-language books\" — reflecting the intense official hostility toward Telugu at the time, despite it being the majority language of the state.\n4.  **Chasing funds:** since no one came forward to donate or pay subscriptions, village elders and enthusiasts had to raise money creatively — selling collected goods, books, newspapers, and furniture — to keep libraries running.\n5.  **Nalgonda and Suryapet anniversary celebrations:** the district collector tried to impose a condition that all lecture content be submitted to him for prior approval. Library workers refused this humiliating demand, and even under an ensuing policy of non-cooperation from the administration, their enthusiasm did not wane.\n    *   **Outcome:** the High Court eventually ruled that the government had no authority to disperse such meetings — a landmark moment in the history of the movement.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p8", "partName": "Ana Grandhamala and Deshoddaraka Grandhamala — Cheap Books for Mass Circulation", "bodyMd": "Founded in 1939 by **Vellutri Manikya Rao** and **Gundavarapu Hanumantha Rao**, with the support of **K. C. Gupta** \n\n*   The scheme was to price books at one anna each, offering roughly a hundred books for just five rupees. Gupta''s house served as the series'' distribution centre, and copies were sold in front of Subbarao''s Hotel in Golconda and in Sultan Bazar, Hyderabad.\n*   **First publication (November 1939):** _Hyderabad Rajyanga Samskaranalu_ (Hyderabad Constitutional Reforms) — a Telugu translation, by Vellutri Manikya Rao, of a Nizam-government-confiscated edition originally in Urdu, with a foreword by Madapati Hanumantha Rao. It went through two reprints without meeting demand.\n*   Vellutri Manikya Rao then published **\"Raithu\" (Ryot)**, urging farmers to unite and detailing their grievances — this book was **banned by the Nizam government**.\n*   To catch those distributing Ana Grandhamala titles, the Nizam''s administration posted police watch outside two hotels — a measure of how seriously the regime feared this cheap-book campaign.\n*   In 1943, Ana Grandhamala also published stories by **Kaloji Narayan Rao**: _Maname Nayam_ (\"We Are Better,\" on cruelty to animals), _Teliyaka Prema Telisi Dwesham_ (\"Love Without Knowledge, Hatred With Knowledge,\" on caste discrimination), and _Vibhuthi_ (a critique of modernisation).\n\nVattikota Alwar Swamy established the **Deshoddaraka Grandhamala** in 1938, with a publishing span across **1938–41 and 1953–61**.\n\n*   **Publications:** Kaloji Narayana Rao — _Naa Godava_ (Poems); Suravaram Pratap Reddy — _Prajadikaramulu_.\n*   Over this period Vattikota personally distributed around thirty books, slinging a bag over his shoulder and selling them not for profit — charging just ten rupees for books worth four times as much.\n*   This practice sparked a culture of reading and book circulation, and works illuminating Telangana''s history and culture while addressing contemporary problems helped awaken both literary spirit and political consciousness across the region.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p9", "partName": "Telangana Literary Organisations", "bodyMd": "*   In 1934, **Suravaram Pratap Reddy** compiled the works of **354 Telangana poets** across the region''s four districts into the _Golconda Kavula Sanchika_ — a landmark work of self-respect for Telangana.\n    *   **Background:** some had claimed that Telugu was spoken only in Telangana''s villages and that its towns had no poets of note — a claim that deeply hurt Suravaram and drove him to compile this anthology in response.\n*   In 1911, **Chandala Kesavadasu** wrote the drama _Kanaka Tara_, which became a foundation for modern Telugu drama.\n*   **Kodati Narayana Rao** ran the _Pragathi_ and _Bala Saraswati_ newspapers.\n*   **Ratnamamba Desai** wrote poems in the _Hithabodhini_ newspaper encouraging women''s education.\n*   **Pasham Narayan Reddy''s** literary works include _Life History of Dayananda Saraswati_, _Thayaga Murthulu_, and _Sadashiva Reddy_ (on the Papannapet ruler who opposed the Nizam out of loyalty/friendship).\n*   Telangana was, in any case, home to a long medieval poetic tradition — **Pothana, Palkuriki Somanadha, Pillalamarri Pinaveerabhadrudu, Vemulawada Bhimakavi, Mallinatha Suri, Anantudu**, and **Vidyanadhudu** were all born and raised in this region.\n\n### Literary Bodies\n\n**Sahiti Mekala (1936)** — Telangana''s first literary organisation, started in Nalgonda by **Ambatipudi Venkat Ratna Shastri**.\n\n*   Books published: Dasharathi Krishnamacharyulu — _Agnidhara_; Ambatipudi Venkat Ratnam — _Tarka Basha_; Punna Anjaiah — _Nilgiri Kavulu Sanchika_; Pulijala Gopal Rao — _Kadga Tikkana_.\n\n**Hyderabad Sahitya Parishad (1932)** — founded with **Acharya Rayaprolu Subbarao** as president and **Acharya Kurugganti Seetharamayya** as secretary.\n\n**Sadhana Samithi (1939)** — key founders included **Burgula Ranganatha Rao**, **Bhaskarbattla Krishna Rao**, and **Veldurthi Manikya Rao**\n\n*   Books published: Devulapalli Ramanuja Rao — _Navya Kavitha Nirajanam_ (Essays); Burgula Ranganatha Rao — _Vahyali_ (Stories); Bogi Narayana Murthy — _Pariksha–Chaduvu_ (Drama); Gadiraju Venkata Ramanaiah — _Bojaraju_ (Poem).\n*   Members read their new compositions aloud at the Samithi''s gatherings.\n\n**Viveka Vardhini Parishad (1941)** — established by Suravaram Pratap Reddy, publishing his own works _Ramayana Visheshalu_, _Mruthyu Siddantham_, and _Andhra Prataprudra Yashobhushanam_.\n\n**Other named literary organisations:** Navya Sahithi Samithi (Raavi Narayana Reddy) and Vaithalika Samithi (Kaloji Narayana Rao).\n\n**Vignana Chandrika Grandhamala at Nemalikonda** — established by well-known writers in Jagtial taluka''s Nemalikonda village, publishing four valuable books on village agriculture, medicine, industry, and historical subjects.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p10", "partName": "Andhra Saraswatha Parishad", "bodyMd": "In the context of the 10th Andhra Mahasabha, **Vattikota Alwar Swamy** initiated an early effort with support from **Burgula Ranganatha Rao** and **Bhaskarabhatla Krishna Rao**, assembling Hyderabad''s leaders and regional literary figures at the Reddy Hostel library building on **26 May 1943**, with **Lokanandi Shankaranarayana Rao** presiding. It was unanimously decided to establish a single literary organisation dedicated to promoting Andhra language and culture.\n\n*   Disputes arose over what to name the new body; as a stop-gap, **Rayaprolu Subbarao, Mahammad Khasim Khan,** and **Kurugganti Seetharamayya (Rahasi)** jointly set up a token organisation called the **\"Hyderabad Andhra Sahitya Parishad\"** while the naming question was settled\n*   With government cooperation, the organisation published its own books as well as books praising the Nizam Nawab, and it was accordingly decided to name the body the **\"Nizam Rashtra Andhra Saraswatha Parishat.\"**\n*   An eleven-member subcommittee was chosen to draft rules and a working plan: Madapati Hanumantha Rao, Bukkapatnam Ramanujacharyulu, Suravaram Pratap Reddy, Lokanandi Shankaranarayana Rao, Burgula Ramakrishna Rao, Chidiremarthi Veerabhadra Sarma, Adiraju Veerabhadra Rao, Nandagiri Venkata Rao, Kodati Narayana Rao, Gadiyaram Ramakrishna Sarma, and Bhaskarabhatla Krishna Rao.\n\n**First Mahasabha (1 June 1943, Reddy Hostel Library):**\n\n*   **President:** Lokanandi Shankaranarayana Rao\n*   **Chief secretary:** Bidudu Venkata Seshaiah\n*   The organisation''s headquarters was initially the _Golconda_ newspaper office; when Lokanandi took up a government job and had to leave the city, **Suravaram Pratap Reddy** became president.\n*   **1949:** the words \"Nizam Rashtra\" were dropped from the name, leaving it simply **Andhra Saraswatha Parishad**.\n\n**Objectives:** (1) foster respect for the mother tongue; (2) reward outstanding literary work; (3) raise literacy by running night schools in villages.\n\n**Published book series:** _Panditha Saraswatham_, _Praja Saraswatham_, and _Bala Saraswatham_, alongside the newspaper _Andhra Sri_.\n\n| Series | Authors and works |\n|---|---|\n| Panditha Saraswatham | Suryanarayana Shastri — _Kavyalankarana Sangraham_; Burgula Ramakrishna Rao — _Saraswatha Vyasa Mukthavalli_; Divakarla Venkata Avadhani — _Sahitya Sopanalu_, _Andhra Vagmaya Charitra_; Rallapalli Anantha Krishna Shastri — _Shalivahana Gadha_, _Saptha Shathisaram_ |\n| Praja Saraswatham | Suravaram Pratap Reddy — _Andhrula Sanghika Charitra_; Nelaturi Venkaiah — _Andhrula Charitra_; Krishna Shastri — _Pallepadalu_, _Streela Pouranika Patalu_ |\n| Bala Saraswatham | Devulapalli Ramanuja Rao — _Mana Desham_; Gadiyaram Ramakrishna Sharma — _Veera Gadhalu_; Adiraju Veera Bhadra Rao — _Mithai Chettu_ |\n\n**Second Anniversary (Warangal):**\n\n*   **President:** Udayaraju Rajeshwara Rao; **secretary of the reception committee:** Kaloji Narayana Rao.\n*   The gathering was held in the compound of Rajeshwara Rao''s own bungalow; roughly thirty poets and pandits were served meals in silver vessels as a mark of special hospitality.\n*   Kosthal Venkataramireddy inaugurated proceedings, with Suravaram Pratap Reddy presiding.\n*   Razakar goons destroyed the pandal set up for the morning poet-conference at Warangal Fort, but **M. S. Rajalingam**, with his youth organisation, took charge of the venues and ensured the conference proceeded successfully regardless.\n\n**Third Mahasabha (Nalgonda):** held with much difficulty, presided over by Kanchanapalli China Venkata Rama Rao; amid political instability in the state, meetings closed with little enthusiasm. In the Nizam''s dominions, the Ittehad-ul-Muslimeen could not tolerate the Parishad''s activities, and atrocities followed.\n\n**Mahbubnagar district:** Devulapalli Ramanuja Rao''s friend, collector Illindala Ramachandra Rao, helped arrange Parishad meetings there despite strong opposition from local Muslim hardliners, refusing to let their tactics succeed.\n\n**Impact of 1947–48:** after British India''s independence on 15 August 1947, the Nizam declared Hyderabad independent and the Razakar movement intensified. During this period of lawlessness, Parishad secretary **Biduru Venkateshayya** left the state for a safer region, and the Parishad''s activities were paralysed for a time.\n\n**Overall contribution:** the Andhra Saraswatha Parishad worked toward the all-round development of Andhra culture and literature — eradicating illiteracy, spreading mother-tongue education, holding meetings and conferences, collecting and publishing rare literary material, compiling regional-dialect glossaries, conducting Telugu examinations, encouraging writers, opening branch offices, and publishing quality books. Burgula, Suravaram, Madapati, Chennareddy, and P. V. Raghavacharyulu, among other leaders, were all associated with it, belonging to no single faction. **Devulapalli Ramanuja Rao** played a particularly prominent role in nurturing the organisation, and **Dr. C. Narayana Reddy** later took up this responsibility, building new premises and shaping modern programmes for it.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p11", "partName": "Preserving Telangana''s Historical Record", "bodyMd": "Doopati Venkataramanacharyulu travelled extensively across his home region, collecting over five thousand palm-leaf manuscripts for copying and preservation, often facing suspicion from villagers reluctant to part with their ancestral texts. Through prolonged research, he established that **Pothana''s birthplace lay in the Warangal region**.\n\nFounded in 1922 by **Adiraju Veerabhadra Rao** as a wing of the Andhra Jana Sangham, dedicated to bringing Telangana''s suppressed history to light. After Adiraju''s death, **Komarraju Lakshmana Rao** headed the council, which published two volumes of _Telangana Sasanalu_ (Telangana Inscriptions).", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p12", "partName": "Nizam Rashtra Grandalaya Mahasabha (Library Conferences)", "bodyMd": "*   **First conference:** held at **Madhira in 1925**, attended by representatives of all libraries in Telangana. **Pingali Venkat Rama Reddy** presided; he also served without salary as a Sessions Judge — a rare achievement, as no Telugu person had held so senior a post at the time. This conference laid the foundation for organised library-building efforts across the Nizam''s dominions.\n*   **Second conference:** held at **Suryapet in 1928**, alongside meetings of the Andhra Jana Sangham. **President:** Vaman Nayak, described as a devoted Maharashtrian patriot. Held together with this library sabha were the Andhra Yuvaka Sabha, Sangha Samskara Sabha, Andhra Mahila Sabha, and Vaishya Yuvajana Sabha — six sabhas over three days as a single festival, whose enthusiasm helped spur the formation of the Andhra Mahasabha.\n*   After the Suryapet gathering, separate library mahasabhas were not organised again; library-movement activity was absorbed into the broader Andhra Mahasabha meetings.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter20-libraries-p13", "partName": "Telangana Rachayitala Sangham and the Poetry Movement in the Freedom Struggle", "bodyMd": "The **Telangana Writers'' Association** was formed in 1952, and from its founding it stirred a wave of literary fervour across Telangana, with writers'' associations springing up in villages everywhere. It gave voice to long-suppressed expression across the region, with **Dasarathi** credited as its most resonant voice. Other members who enriched the association included Acharya Lakshmikanta Ranjanam, C. Narayana Reddy, Kaloji, Vanamamalai, Ramaraju, Palla Durgaiah, Kappagantla Lakshmana Sastry, Irivento Krishnamurthy, and V. P. Raghavacharyulu.\n\nPoetry recitation became an expected feature of library annual functions, school functions, festivals, and poet gatherings, and political mahasabhas were led off by poets'' verses — there was scarcely a place in Telangana without poetry, expressed in the phrase \"in huts, in prisons, in thatched homes, in temple-towered villages.\"\n\n*   The linguistic subjugation imposed by the Nizam''s Hyderabad administration weighed more heavily on Telangana than elsewhere, and the desire for political freedom in turn opened the path to linguistic freedom, fuelling the region''s literary and poetic revival.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter21-telangana-newspapers-literature', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter21-telangana-newspapers-literature-p1", "partName": "Newspapers and the Press in Telangana", "bodyMd": "India''s first newspaper was the **Bengal Gazette**, started by **James Augustus Hicky in 1780**. In the Hyderabad State, the Nizam Government''s ordinance **Gasti Nishan 53** banned freedom of expression and the press. English, Telugu, and Urdu newspapers were published across the state precisely to create awareness among the people while working around this restriction.\n\nDiverse linguistic communities — Telugu, Marathi, Urdu, and Kannada speakers — lived together in the erstwhile Hyderabad State, though Telugu speakers formed the majority and Urdu held official-language status. In such conditions, learned Telugu individuals took up book publication, library founding, socio-cultural reform movements, and newspaper launches to awaken the public.\n\n### Printing Presses\n\n*   **Brahma Vidya Vilasam Printing Press (1870)** — established by the **Wanaparthy rulers**; the first printing press in the Hyderabad State.\n*   In **1875**, a printing press was established at **Gadwal Samsthanam**.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter21-telangana-newspapers-literature-p2", "partName": "Early Newspapers of Hyderabad State (Urdu, Persian, English)", "bodyMd": "| Newspaper | Year | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Jubdoot-Ul-Akbar | 1833 | One of the earliest newspapers of India; established at Hyderabad. |\n| Risala Tabbi | 1859 | A medical journal in Urdu; the first newspaper published in Hyderabad. |\n| Octareshfak | 1878 | Published in Parsi language; some scholars consider it the first newspaper in the Nizam State. |\n| Deccan Times | 1864 | First English newspaper from Hyderabad; established by Abdul Khader. |\n| Hyderabad Recorder | 1885 | English newspaper; the first newspaper banned by the Nizam Government (1892), after it described the British Resident as \"Kaizer\" and Britain pressured the Nizam to act. |\n| Hyderabad Bulletin | 1904 | English-language paper started by Cameroon; despite Nizam-era restrictions, it boldly published news against the government and survived for nearly 50 years. |\n\n**Maulim-e-Niswan (1892)** was started by **Maulvi Mohib Hussain** to propagate reforms for Muslim women; he is known as the father of journalism in Hyderabad and carried the titles \"Hyderabad Women''s Education Vaithalika\" and \"Telangana Sir Syed Ahmed Khan.\"", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter21-telangana-newspapers-literature-p3", "partName": "Telugu Newspapers of Telangana", "bodyMd": "| Newspaper | Year | Founder/Editor | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shedya Chandrika | 1886 | Munshi Mohammed Musthak Ahmed Malik | Considered by recent researchers, including **Sangishetty Srinivas**, to be the first Telugu newspaper published in Telangana. It was a translation of the Urdu magazine _Punoon_ and provided agricultural information. |\n| Deenvarthaman | 1890–91 | Narayana Swamy Mudaliar (Sultan Bazar, Hyderabad) | Per researchers **Rapolu Andhra Bhaskar** and **Veludurthi Manikya Rao**, the first daily Telugu newspaper. Narayana Swamy had earlier run the weekly _Asaf-Ul-Akbar_ from 1878. |\n| Hithabodhini | 1913 | Bandaru Srinivasa Sharma | A weekly magazine published from Mahabubnagar; the first Telugu newspaper distributed all over Telangana. Sharma was a Sanskrit scholar and Ayurvedic doctor from Koyala Konda, Mahabub Nagar. The **Atmakuru ruler Sri Rama Bhupala Bahiri Balawant Bahadur** financed the printing machine for this paper. |\n| Nilgiri | 1922 | Shabnaveesu Venkatarama Narsimha Rao | The first political weekly magazine in Telangana. The editor also founded a library named \"Samskarini\" and wrote the poem _Balika Vilapam_. |\n| Tenugu Vara Patrika | 27 August 1922 | Oddiraju Sitaramachandra Rao and Oddiraju Raghava Ranga Rao | The Oddiraju brothers printed this paper at their own Inugurthi printing press in Inugurthi village (Mahabubabad district); they had also founded **Vignana Pracharini Sri Grandhamala (1919)** for educational books. |\n| Golconda Patrika | 10 May 1926 | Suravaram Pratap Reddy (editor); centred at Nalgonda | Published Political, Economic, Social, and Cultural news of Telangana as a half-weekly (Wednesday and Saturday). Suravaram first wanted to name it _Andhra Patrika_, but the Nizam Government opposed this, so he chose the name \"Golconda\" instead. In **1947** it became a daily; it was closed in **1966**. |\n| Sujatha Patrika | 1927 | Pasumamula Narsimaha Sharma (editor) | Literary monthly magazine from Hyderabad; its name was suggested by **Rayaprolu Subba Rao**. |\n| Bhagyanagar Patrika | 1931 | Bhagya Reddy Varma | Founded for the development of Dalits; its front page carried images of Charminar, Mecca Masjid, and Ellora–Ajanta paintings. From 1937 it was renamed **Adi Hindu Newspaper**. |\n| Phula Thota Patrika | 1930–35 | Kambampati Appanna Shastri (editor); Hanumakonda | The first children''s paper in Telangana. |\n| Telugu Talli | 1939 | Rachamalla Satyavati Devi (editor) | A monthly magazine; the first paper published by a woman editor in Telangana. |\n| Telangana Patrika | 1942 | Rajgopal Mudaliyar (founder); Bukkapatnam Ramanuja Chary (editor) | The first daily newspaper from Telangana. |\n| Kakatiya Vaara Patrika | 1946 | P.V. Narasimha Rao (founder); Pamulaparthi Sadashiva Rao (editor) | — |\n| Shoba | 1947 | Devulapalli Ramanuja Rao | A literary monthly magazine. Devulapalli also ran a secret newspaper from Kurnool titled \"State Congress Vartha Sanchika\" in 1947. |\n\n### Nilgiri–Tenugu Patrika Rivalry\n\nThe **Nilgiri Patrika**, founded in **1922** by editor **Shabnaveesu Venkatarama Narasimha Rao**, was published from **Nalgonda** as the first fully-fledged political weekly in Telangana. Just three days after Nilgiri began, on **27 August 1922**, the **Oddiraju brothers** launched **Tenugu Patrika** from **Inugurthi** in the Manukota taluka.\n\nBecause Nilgiri was centred in Nalgonda rather than at the district''s administrative headquarters, Tenugu Patrika enjoyed some regional advantage, being published from a village that was itself gaining prominence. However, this created printing difficulties for Tenugu Patrika: since the press was village-based, it struggled to find people who could work the printing machinery — local youths had to be trained specifically for the task — and readers had to arrange postage from Mahabubabad to receive their copies.\n\nThere being no radio or television at the time, newspapers were the sole channel through which people learned of national events, and news itself did not travel quickly. Because of this, newspapers depended on the nearest railway station to receive dispatches — someone even had to fetch coastal/overseas news via a personal contact.\n\nSince editorial responsibility for Tenugu Patrika rested solely with the Oddiraju brothers, both had to write content themselves regardless of who was available, and with a limited subscriber base, running the paper for **five years** from such a small village was regarded as a remarkably bold achievement. Alongside publishing the paper, the Oddiraju brothers also ran a book series called **Vignana Pracharini Grandhamala**.\n\n**Nilgiri Patrika** was edited with great competence by **Shabnaveesu Ramanarasimha Rao** until **1925**. Prominent contemporary leaders — **Madapati Hanumantha Rao**, **Bandaru Ramakrishna Rao**, and **Pulijala Rangarao**, among others — contributed poems and essays on various subjects to this paper.\n\nAround this time, Nilgiri refused to publish a sensational story written by **Gudipati Venkatachalam**; Shabnaveesu''s decision to reject it itself created a stir. The pioneer of the Andhra movement, **Ramanarasimha Rao**, died in **1929**.\n\n### Suravaram Pratap Reddy and Golconda Patrika\n\n**Suravaram Pratap Reddy** awakened the Telangana public continuously through the **Golconda Patrika** from **1926 to 1946**. It was initially a bi-weekly with outstanding editorials that were a thorn in the side of the Nizam government; it became a daily in **1947**, with Prataparao (Pratap Reddy) as editor throughout, an editorship he never relinquished.\n\nUntil Golconda Patrika emerged, the library movement, the Andhra movement, and papers such as Nilgiri and Tenugu Patrika had been mutually dependent for survival. In **1923**, **Mudigonda Veerabhadra Sastry** started a monthly magazine titled _Shaiva Pracharini_ from Warangal; in that period, Tenugu Patrika and Shaiva Pracharini were engaged in a spirited (healthy) rivalry.\n\n### Sujatha, Andhrakesari, Deshabandhu and Related Papers\n\n**Sujatha**, edited by **P.N. Sarma**, concluded with its fourth issue''s first number in **1927**. In **1924**, **Adusumilli Dattatreya Sarma** started a weekly paper, _Andhrakesari_, in the Andhra-Telugu language from Secunderabad; it ran for **five years**. The monthly _Deshabandhu_, from Vaddepalli village of Atraf-e-Balda district, was run for three years by **Bellamkonda Ramanujacharyulu** and his brother **Narasimhacharyulu** together.\n\n**B.N. Sarma**, a resident of Ramannapet taluka, Nalgonda district, was proficient in Telugu, Sanskrit, and English. He trained in journalism under Nilgiri Patrika editors **Shabnaveesu Venkatarama Narasimha Rao** and **Madapati Hanumantha Rao**.\n\nWhen the Nizam government harassed him with a battery of legal questions over his paper''s printing decisions, he could not bear it, moved to Bezwada, and ran a paper called _Andhravani_ there for five years; it achieved wide circulation, and the street where its office stood is still called \"Andhravani Street.\" Sarma, who worked at many papers, died in **1970**.\n\n### Ramanujacharyulu and the \"Telangana\" Daily\n\nIn **1929**, **Bukkapatnam Ramanujacharyulu** started the **Hyderabad Bulletin** paper in Hyderabad city. He was the pioneer who first introduced English and Telugu newspaper publication in Telangana.\n\nIn **1942**, the **Telangana** daily was published under his editorship; it featured a daily \"Charminar Gossip\" column. It disclosed the secrets of Nawabi-era administrative offices and high officialdom, which readers eagerly consumed the next day. He was skilled in both English and Telugu writing.\n\nHe later joined his friend and colleague **Rajagopal** to start the **Deccan Chronicle** weekly in **1937**, of which he was the editor; it became a daily in **1939**. After his ties with Deccan Chronicle ended, he started an evening paper called **Garrison**.\n\nIn **1947** he started the English daily **Daily News**, but died within a month of its launch.\n\n### Bhagyanagar Patrika and Adi Hindu Patrika\n\nIn **1925**, the prominent social-reform figure **Bhagya Reddy** established the **Bhagyanagar Patrika**. When Bhagya Reddy fell ill and had to leave for Mysore, the paper was discontinued.\n\nAfter recovering and returning from Mysore, he restarted it, in the cause of Harijan upliftment, as the weekly **Adi Hindu Patrika**. This paper discussed many political issues and championed the political movements of the Nizam-era public.\n\n### Other Papers of the 1920s–1940s\n\nThe **Vibhuti** paper was run under the editorship of **Veerabhadra Sharma**. The **Shobha** paper, run from Warangal under the editorship of **Devulapalli Ramanujarao**, was a prominent literary paper but had to close within a single year due to financial constraints.\n\nBetween **1938 and 1945**, few new papers emerged, owing to the Nizam government''s severe restrictions on freedom of speech. In **1945**, **Challa Subbarao** started a Telugu monthly called **Tarani**. The prominent Urdu paper _Payam_''s editor **Khwaja Abdul Gaffar** started a Telugu paper, **Sandesham**; it ran for only six months.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter21-telangana-newspapers-literature-p4", "partName": "Urdu Newspapers", "bodyMd": "| Newspaper | Year | Founder/Editor | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Payam | 1935 | Khaji Abdul Gaffar | An Urdu daily that supported the independence struggle of the Nizam''s subjects. |\n| Rayyat | 1927 | Mandamula Narsingh Rao | An Urdu weekly (\"Rayyat\" means a farmer); it informed national leaders of the anti-Nizam movement in Hyderabad, demanded a responsible government in the Nizam State, and fought for people''s rights. **Shoebullah Khan**, later editor of _Imroze_, initially worked here. |\n| Imroze | 1947 | Shoebullah Khan | An Urdu weekly (\"Imroze\" means fire). Shoebullah began his career at the Urdu weekly _Tej_, then worked at _Rayyat_ before starting Imroze, through which he criticised the Razakars and wrote editorials advocating Hyderabad''s merger into the Indian Union. As a result, he was murdered by the Razakars on **20 August 1948**. |\n| Rahbar-e-Deccan | — | Ahmed Mohinuddin and Abdullahkhan (editors) | Supported by the MIM party. |\n| Meezan | 1944 | Gulam Mohammed (Gulam Alauddin) of Calcutta | The word \"Meezan\" is Arabic for \"the balance.\" It was the first paper in Telangana published in three languages — Telugu, English, and Urdu — and the first daily newspaper to run serialised novels. |\n\n### Meezan''s Structure\n\nMeezan''s Urdu edition supported the Razakars, its English edition supported the Nizam, and its Telugu edition supported communists and other radicals. The Telugu edition''s editor was **Adavi Bapiraju**, with **Vidwan Viswam** as sub-editor.\n\nAdavi Bapiraju published stories and songs by **Avula Pichaiah** of Suryapet, and his own serialised novels — _Himabindu_, _Gona Gannareddy_, and _Toofan_ — appeared in Meezan. Contributors to the paper included **Tirumala Ramachandra**, **Rachakonda Krishnamurthy**, **Srinivasa Chakravarti**, and **Bommakanti Subbarao**. Bapiraju''s own columns \"Dhoopadeepalu\" and \"Mirchi Masala\" entertained and informed readers.\n\nMeezan ran for three years; as Razakar atrocities intensified across the state, Bapiraju resigned his editorial duties on **7 August 1947**.\n\n**Vatan News (Momibbe Vatan)** was founded by **Mustafa Qadri**: at a time when the pro-Nizam news agency **Deccan News** was disseminating pro-government propaganda, Qadri — a critic of Muslim fundamentalism — started the news agency **Vatan News** and its paper _Momibbe Vatan_ (\"patriot\") to publish unbiased news. **Ganapati Rao Akhilekar**, through his agency **Modern News**, similarly circulated the pro-government news supplied by Deccan News agency''s _Deccan News_.\n\n**Masawat** (meaning \"Equality\") was an Urdu weekly started by **Prem Kumar**, Vice-President of the Hyderabad Depressed Classes Federation.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter21-telangana-newspapers-literature-p5", "partName": "Other Notable Papers and Editors", "bodyMd": "| Paper | Year | Editor/Founder |\n|---|---|---|\n| Andhra Matha Paper | 1917 | Swamy Venkat Rao; published on behalf of the Divine Life Society, Secunderabad |\n| The Panchama | 1918–20 | J.S. Mathaiah |\n| Desivajmaya | 1921 | Telangana Literacy Association |\n| Shaivamata Pracharini | 1923 | Mudigonda Veeresha Linga Shastri |\n| Shaivamani | 1924 | Mudigonda Buchaiah |\n| Ahakam Sube Warangal | 1925 | Run by Revenue officers from Warangal; considered the first fortnightly paper in Telangana |\n| Nedu Patrika | 1925 | Bhaskar (Secunderabad) |\n| Andhra Abhyudayam | 1925 | Kokila Sitarama Sharma |\n| Deshabandhu | 1926 | Bellamkonda Narasimha Charyulu; tagline \"Andhra Vignana Prabodha\" monthly magazine |\n| Kashtukar | 1931 | Mohammad Abdul Razaq Bismil; a bilingual Urdu–Telugu peasant journal published from Dablipura, Hyderabad |\n| Vaidyakala | 1931 | M. Anantharanga Charyulu |\n| Deccan Kesari | 1934 | Adusumalli Venkat Dattatreya Sharma |\n| Divyavani | 1937 | Chivukula Appaiah Sastry |\n| Andhravani | 1938 | Bonguluri Narasimha Sharma; published from Vijayawada owing to unfavourable conditions in Hyderabad during the State Congress Satyagraha |\n| Vibuthi | 1939 | Chidiremattam Veerabhadra Sharma |\n| Sandesham | 1941 | Quazi Abdul Gaffar |\n| Andhra Kesari | 1941 | Gundavaram Hanumanth Rao |\n| Samstana Vidyartri | 1945 | Bheema Goud |\n| Telugu Desam | 1948 | Suryadevara Rajya Lakshmi |\n| Sudarshan Patrika | — | Sudarshana Devi; monthly, published from Wanaparthy |\n| Janashakti | 1950–54 | Juvvadi Goutam Raju, Raghuveerarao |\n| Prajavani | 1951 | Suravaram Pratap Reddy |\n| Navayuga | 1954 | Telangana Writers Association |\n| Padma Prakash | — | Burgula Ramakrishnaiah (monthly magazine) |\n| Hyderabad Weekly | — | Maari Chenna Reddy |\n\n### Telugu Book Distribution under the Nizam\n\nDuring the Nizam period, besides the **Srikrishna Devaraya Andhra Basha Nilayam** in Hyderabad, there was only one Telugu book stall in the entire Nizam State, run by **Nampalli Gouri Shankar Sharma**. He distributed Telugu books across the state and, for this service, was regarded as a \"language servant.\"", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter21-telangana-newspapers-literature-p6", "partName": "Pioneers of Research and Literature in Telangana", "bodyMd": "### Komarraju Venkat Laxman Rao (1876–1923)\n\n*   The first person to conduct research into Telangana''s literature.\n*   Born in **Penuganchiprolu** (Krishna district); later settled in **Bhuvanagiri**, Telangana.\n*   Worked as Diwan to the Munagala Zamindar, **Raja Nayani Venkat Ranga Rao**.\n*   Established several libraries and authored many books; proved that the ancient city of **Ekasila** was not Ontimetta in Kadapa district but was, in fact, **Orugallu** (Warangal).\n*   **Books:** Shivaji Chritra, Haindava Chakravarthulu, Hindu Mahayugam, Mohammadain Mahayugam, and Andhra Vignana Sarvasvam (the first Vignana Sarvasvam/encyclopaedia in Telugu).\n*   In **1910**, he wrote Telangana''s first short story, _Ebadivella Bheramu_, though some scholars instead credit **Madapati Hanumanthrao''s** 1912 story _Hrudaya Shalyamu_ as the first.\n\n### Manavalli Ramakrishna Kavi (1866–1957)\n\n*   Served as personal secretary to the Wanaparthy ruler **Sawai Rameshwar Rao Bahadur**.\n*   Collected inscriptions and published them as _Lithic Records of Hyderabad State_.\n*   Published ancient texts in a series titled _Visrutha Kavulu_.\n\n### Seshadri Ramana Kavulu\n\n*   Established the **Historical Research Council** (Charitraka Parishodaka Mandali) at Hanumakonda.\n*   In **1931**, organized the **Akhilandhra Historical Conference**.\n*   Key literary work: _Andhra Veerulu_.\n\n### Adiraju Veerabhadra Rao\n\n*   Born in **Dendukur** (Madhira Taluka, Khammam district); given shelter in Hyderabad by **Ravi Chettu Ranga Rao**.\n*   Established the **Andhra Research Council** (Andhra Parishodaka Mandali) under the Andhra Jana Kendra Sangam.\n*   The first person to give a speech on Hyderabad Radio.\n*   Collected Telangana''s inscriptions and published two volumes as _Telangana Inscriptions_.\n*   **Other writings:** Mana Telanganamu, Ratnaprabha, Chaitanyamu, Jeevita Charitavali, Lalitha Kalavali, Navvula Puvvulu, and _Ancient Cities of Andhra_ — covering the history of twelve ancient cities including Kondapur, Kolanupaka, Anegondi, Alampur, Bhuvangiri, Rachakonda, Warangal, and Pangal.\n\n### Suravaram Pratap Reddy (1896–1953)\n\n*   Born in **Itikyalapadu** village, Jogulamba Gadwal district.\n*   Because contemporary Andhra poets claimed Telangana had no poets, he was moved to research Telangana''s literature.\n*   In **1934**, compiled **Golconda Kavulu**, a list of the works of **354 Telangana poets**, to prove that Telangana possessed a rich literary and poetic tradition.\n*   Wrote _Andhrulu Saanghika Charitra_, a social history of the Telugu people, which won the **Kendra Sahitya Academy Puraskaram in 1955** — making him the **first Telugu poet** to receive the award.\n*   Autobiography: _Svavishaya Sangraham_.\n*   Started and edited the **Golconda Patrika** in 1926; founded the **Vigyana Vardhini Parishad** in 1941; ran **Prajavani Patrika** in 1951–52.\n*   Wrote under several pen names, including Chitra Gupta, Jagam Basavaiah, Vishwamitra, Yugapati, Amrutha Kalasi, Sangrama Simha, Bhava Kabi Ram Murthy, and Bahadur.\n*   **Stories:** Mogalayi Kathalu; **comic/satirical writings:** Verrivengalappa, Kavilekattalu.\n*   **Research works:** Golconda Kavula Sanchika (1934), Hinduvula Pandugalu (1931), Ramayana Visheshalu (1943), Prapancha Grandalaya Charitra, Lipi Samskaranalu.\n*   **Dramas:** Uchchala Vishadamu (based on Kalhana''s Rajatarangini), Baktha Tukaram.\n*   **Novels:** Arevirulu, Suddantha Katha.\n*   **Poetry & Prose:** Sangala Panthulu, Hamsaveera Sambhavam, Padmini, Mohammed Ghazni, Deshanata.\n*   In the **first general elections of Hyderabad State (1952)**, he was elected to the legislative assembly (Congress Party) from the Wanaparthy constituency.\n\n### Biruduraju Ramaraju\n\n*   Native of **Devanuru** village, Warangal district.\n*   Researched folklore literature; awarded a Ph.D. in Telugu literature by **Osmania University in 1956**.\n*   **Literary works:** Maruguna Padina Manikyalu, Charitra Kekkina Charitardulu, Andhra Yogulu.\n*   Discovered and researched: Kakatiya Prataparudra''s _Usha Ragodhayam_, Sri Krishna Devaraya''s _Jambavati Parinayam_, Palkuriki Somanatha''s _Panditharadhya Charitra_ and _Basavodaharanam_, Srinatha''s _Shiva Ratri Mahatyam_, and Madiki Singanna''s _Padma Puranam_.\n\n### Daasarathi Krishnamacharyulu (1925–1987)\n\n*   Born in **Chinnaguduru** village, Warangal district (now in Mahabubabad district).\n*   Held a B.A. in English literature from Osmania University; worked as a teacher and Akashavani speaker.\n*   Served as **Court Poet of Andhra Pradesh, 1977–83**; titles: Kalaprapurna and Abhyudhaya Bhaavi.\n*   Won the **Kendra Sahitya Academy Award in 1974** for _Timiramto Samaram_ (\"Fight against Darkness\").\n*   As part of the **Andhra Mahasabha**, travelled village to village to raise public awareness; arrested by the Nizam government in **1947** and imprisoned at **Induru Fort Jail, Nizamabad** for his role in the public agitation — along with poet **Vattikota Alwar Swamy**. He used to write poems on the jail walls with charcoal (meant for brushing teeth); police mistakenly attributed and punished Vattikota for these.\n*   Founded **Telangana Rachayithala Sangam** (Telangana Writers'' Association) in **1953** and served as its president.\n*   Entered cinema in **1961**, writing lyrics for _Iddaru Mitrulu_.\n*   **Literary works:** Agnidhara (on the Telangana Armed Struggle), Rudraveena, Navami, Punarnavam, Mahashilpi Jakkanna, Mahandrodyamam, Kavitha Pushpakam, Timiramto Samaram, Amruthabishekam, Mahaboddhi, Ghalib Geethalu, Alochanalochanalu. He received the **Andhra Pradesh Sahitya Academy Award** for _Kavitha Pushpakam_.\n*   The **1st Daasarathi Krishnamacharya Award** was given by the Telangana Government to **Tirumala Srinivasa Charyulu** (Karimnagar).\n\n### Daasarathi Rangacharya (1928–2015)\n\n*   Born in **Chinnaguduru** village, Warangal district; brother of Daasarathi Krishnamacharya.\n*   Expelled from school in the 6th standard for organising a strike (wearing Gandhiji''s cap instead of the Nizam-prescribed uniform); worked as a teacher from **1951–57**.\n*   Known as **Akshara Vachaspathi** and **Telangana Karadeepika**; participated in the Telangana Armed Struggle.\n*   Autobiography: _Jeevanayanam_.\n*   **Literary works:** Chillara Devullu, Modugupulu, Janapadam, Maya Jalataru, Saratalpam, Ranunnadi Edi Nizam, Sri Madramayanam, Sri Mahabharatam — with **Chillara Devullu** and **Modugupulu** the most prominent, describing the Telangana Struggles and the conditions before Telangana''s liberation.\n*   **Modugu Pulu** won the Kendra Sahitya Academy Award; **Chillera Devullu** won a National Literature Award and was adapted into a successful film directed by **T. Madhava Rao**.\n*   Translated all four Vedas into Telugu, and translated the Urdu classic _Umrao Jaan_ into Telugu.\n\n### Vattikota Alwar Swamy (1915–1961)\n\n*   Born in **Cheruvu Madaram** village, Nalgonda district; called **Telangana Vaithalika** (Pioneer) and **Telangana Gorki**.\n*   Raised by his teacher **Seetharama Rao** after his father''s early death.\n*   Jailed for participation in the **Quit India Movement (1942–43)**; came under communist influence and turned communist thereafter.\n*   Active in the library movement; fought against the Nizam and, for this, was imprisoned at **Induru Jail, Nizamabad, 1946–1951**, under rigorous punishment.\n*   **Literary works:** Jailu Lopala (life inside jail), Prajala Manishi (conditions of Telangana people, 1934–38), Gangu (Telangana people''s history, 1940–45), Telangana, Chinnappude, Ramappa Rabhasa (against social evils; published in _Telugu Vidyarthi_ patrika from Machilipatnam).\n*   Established **Deshoddaraka Grandhamala**, whose first published book was Suravaram Pratap Reddy''s _Prajadhikaramulu_.\n\n### Devulapalli Ramanuja Rao (1917–1993)\n\n*   Born in **Desai peta** village, Warangal district.\n*   Ran the newspaper _Shobha_ (literary monthly); served as President of the **Andhra Saraswatha Parishad** in 1953 (held at Alampur).\n*   **Literary works:** History of Telangana Armed Struggle, Telanganalo Jathiyodhyamalu, Saraswatha Navaneetham, Telugu Seemalo Samskrutika Punarjeevam, Hyderabad Swatantryodhyamam, Fifty Years of Remembrance (1929–79), Tenugu Literature, Veguchukkalu, Upanyasa Toranam, Kavya Mala, Talupula Dumaramu, Five Year Plans, My Radio Speeches, Our Village – Orugallu, Mana Desham – Telugu Seema.\n\n### Gadiyaram Ramakrishna Sharma\n\n*   Autobiography **Satapatram** (\"Hundred Petals\") won the **Kendra Sahitya Academy Award in 2007**, posthumously.\n*   **Literary works:** Madhava Vidyaranya Charitra, Alampura Kshethra Charitra, Veera Gadalu, Vignana Vallari, Dasharupaka Saram, Panchajanyam, Mana Vastu Sampada, Umamaheshwara Charitra.\n\n### Dr. C. Narayana Reddy (1931–2017)\n\n*   Born in **1931** at **Hanumajipet**, Karimnagar district.\n*   Won the prestigious **Jnanpith Award in 1988** for _Viswambhara_, becoming the **first person from Telangana** to receive it.\n*   Held a post-graduate degree and doctorate in Telugu literature from **Osmania University**, later becoming a professor there.\n*   **Awards:** 1973 – Kendra Sahitya Academy Award (for _Mantalu-Manavudu_); 1977 – Padma Shri; 1978 – Kala Prapoorna; 1988 – Jnanpith Award; 1992 – Padma Bhushan; 2014 – Kendra Sahitya Academy Fellowship.\n*   **Literary works:** Navvani Puvvu (1953, his first work), Ruthu Chakram, Vyakthithvam, Karpura Vasantha Rayalu, Bhugolamantha Manishi, Mattimanishi Aakasham, Mukhamukhi, Manishi–Chiluka, Ajanta Sundari, Vishwambhara, Kalam Sakshiga, Mantalu Manavudu, Nagarjuna Sagar, Bhoomika, Marpu Na Teerpu, Ramappa, Divvela Muvvalu.\n*   Entered the film industry as a lyricist with _Gulebhakavali Katha_ (1962), going on to write more than **3,000 songs**; became widely popular with the song \"Nannu Dochukunduvate Vannela Dorasani.\"\n\n### Other Literary Figures\n\n*   **Tadakamalla Krishna Rao (1830–1890)** wrote Telangana''s first novel, _Kambukandhara Charitra_.\n*   **Palla Durgaiah (1916–1983)** wrote Gangiredhu (Abull), Prabhanda Vajmaya Vikasam, and Palavelli. Together with Gangula Sai Reddy''s _Kapu Bidda_ and C. Narayana Reddy''s _Ruthu Chakram_, Palla Durgaiah''s _Gangireddu_ is treated as part of the modern-dialect **Kavyatrayam** (trio of poetic works).\n*   **Bunna Ilaiah** wrote the research work _Telangana Sahitya Samasthalu_, documenting the founding history of literary centres from Adilabad to Hyderabad, and depicted the lives of Telangana''s people in the story _Kaluva Mallaiah_. His other works include _Nippu Kanika_ and _Rahasya Chitram_.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p1", "partName": "Arya Samaj", "bodyMd": "**Background:** Arya Samaj gained strength in the Hyderabad state only after the movement for religious conversion into Islam had already begun. The organisation itself originated in North India and was especially powerful in Punjab, and certain peculiar local circumstances within the princely state helped it take root here too. In Arya Samaj''s own terminology, its followers described themselves not as \"Hindus\" but as \"Aryas.\"\n\n*   In **1875**, **Swamy Dayananda Saraswati** founded Arya Samaj in Bombay for the protection of Vedic dharma/culture. It is known as the **\"Vedic Dharma reform movement.\"**\n*   Dayananda gave the slogan **\"Back to Vedas.\"** His book **Satyarth Prakash** (\"Satyarth Prakashika\") was treated as a sacred text of the Samaj; in it he criticized Islam, Christianity, and Sikhism.\n*   The book was translated into Telugu by **Aadipudi Somanatha Rao**; its first Telugu edition was published in **1921**.\n*   Dayananda instructed all Arya Samaj members to perform **\"Sandhya Vandanam.\"** The Samaj''s principles were publicized through the newspaper **\"Vaidika Adarsh.\"**\n\n**Principles of Arya Samaj**\n\n*   No person is to be ascribed a caste by birth — people are categorized as Brahmin, Kshatriya, or Vaishya based on occupation.\n*   The Vedas are held to be eternal, infallible truths; the Samaj opposed priesthood, religious rites, and idol worship, while showing keen interest in Western scientific knowledge.\n*   It opposed the caste system, untouchability, and child marriage.\n*   It encouraged inter-caste marriage and widow remarriage.\n*   Followers sought to make Hindu religion accessible to all castes by eliminating idol worship and polytheistic ritual, reducing unnecessary orthodoxy, and removing untouchability.\n\n### Hyderabad Branch and Leadership\n\n*   The Hyderabad Arya Samaj branch was established in **1892** at Residency Bazar (Sultan Bazar) by **Swamy Nityananda**.\n\n| Position | Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1st President | Pandit Kamal Prasadji Mishra |\n| Secretary | Lakshman Desji |\n\n*   Important members included Swamy Shraddhanand, Pandit Narendraji, Vandemataram Ramachandra Rao, Keshav Rao Karotkar, Aadipudi Somanadha Rao, Aghoranath Chattopadhyaya, Narayan Rao Pawar, Vaman Rao Nayak, Damodara Satyalekar, Pandit Narendra, and Shyam Lal.\n*   **Pandit Keshav Rao Koratkar** was a leading public figure of the early 20th century, a great patriot and a prominent judge who served on the Hyderabad High Court under the Nizam''s government. He headed the state Arya Samaj until his death.\n\n### Conflict with Religious Conversion Movements\n\n*   During the Nizam period, an organisation called **\"Tabligh Islam\"** was established to convert non-Muslims to Islam.\n*   **Siddiqui Deendhar** preached that all Hindus should convert to Islam, claiming to be an incarnation of the god Chenna Basaveswara Swamy and asserting that the modern form of the Lingayat tradition was Islam; he denigrated Rama and Krishna in the process, and authored a book titled **\"Survere Alam.\"** Arya Samaj propagandists **Mangalorao Dev** and **Pandit Ramachandra Dahelvi** toured the state opposing his preaching.\n*   In **1930**, Arya Samaj resolved to firmly counter internal and external attacks on Hindu society, organizing programs to popularize Satyarth Prakash, havan-kund rituals, devotional hymns, and processions.\n*   **M.I.M. president Bahadur Yar Jung** attempted to convert Dalits to Islam through secret circulars; these were exposed to Arya Samaj by a postal employee, **Venkat Swamy**.\n\n### The Shuddhi Movement\n\n*   Dayananda Saraswati started the **\"Shuddhi Program\"** to re-convert Hindus who had converted to other religions.\n*   In Hyderabad, the program was led by **Swamy Shraddhanand**, for which he was murdered by **Khaja Hasan Nizam**.\n\n### Nizam Government''s Repression (1894–1936)\n\n*   In **1894**, the Nizam government expelled **Balakrishna Sharma** and **Nityananda** from the state for organizing Shuddhi programs.\n*   The government arrested Arya Samaj leaders **Vishvananda Saraswati** and **Vidhyananda Brahmachary**.\n*   Pandit Keshavrao proposed a resolution in **1930** in the Hyderabad state legislature to revive Hindu widow remarriage; conservative Hindu and Muslim members alike united to oppose it, but it was eventually enacted into law through the persistent efforts of **Pandit Vinayakarao Vidyalankar**.\n*   Keshavrao died on **21 May 1932**; his death was an irreparable loss to Arya Samaj. His son, **Pandit Vinayakarao Vidyalankar**, took over leadership and continued its activities with dedication, which increasingly troubled the Nizam government.\n*   In **1934**, the government banned the Arya Samaj newspaper **Vaidika Adarsh**, and separately barred central Arya Samaj propagandists **Pandit Ramachandra Dahelvi** and **Chandrabhanu** from touring the state. The same year, it made prior government permission compulsory for performing homas and havans in the provinces.\n*   In response, the central Arya Pratinidhi Sabha protested in 1934, claiming no formal ban on propagation existed. A delegation of **Narayanaswami, Swami Swatantranand, and Acharya Randevu** toured the state; although no explicit restriction was placed on this tour, conditions afterward reverted to their earlier adverse state.\n*   In **1935**, the Beedar (Bidar) district collector attacked Arya Samaj representatives at **Nilanga**, destroying havan vessels and temples. Arya Samaj protested strongly; the then Home Department secretary, **Zaheeruddin**, who investigated the matter, found fault with the collector''s conduct and recommended he personally bear the cost of rebuilding the temples and havan-kunds.\n\n### Arya Samaj Satyagraha (1938–39)\n\n*   On **24 October 1938**, the Hindu Civil Liberty Union and Arya Samaj conducted a **Satyagraha** against the Nizam government. **Pandit Narendraji** was arrested and sent to **Mannanuru jail** in Mahbubnagar district — called Telangana''s **\"Kalapani\"** (Black Water), situated near Amrabad in the Nallamalla forests.\n*   Narendraji had been associated with Arya Samaj since 1932; in 1938 he was arrested at Pandit Vinayakarao''s house during a religious ceremony and sent under ambush to Mannanuru, where he spent three years.\n*   After his release, Narendraji toured Warangal; **Kaloji Narayan Rao** wrote a poem in his honour — _\"Dairyam leni daddammalaku dairyamu cheppaga vachavu enadu\"_ (\"You came to instill courage in the fearful\").\n*   Narendraji''s remark that **\"Hyderabad state is a larger area jail\"** led the government to brand him a traitor and charge him with sedition, leading to re-arrest, despite numerous petitions from the central Arya Samaj body.\n*   A 17-year-old, **Ramachandra Rao**, continued shouting \"Vandemataram\" while being beaten by police, earning him the name **Vandemataram Ramachandra Rao**.\n*   Negotiations between Arya Samaj representative **Lala Deshbandhu Gupta** and the Nizam government led to the Satyagraha being withdrawn on **7 August 1939**.\n\n**All India Arya Samaj Conference, 1938**\n\n| Detail | Name |\n|---|---|\n| Held at | Sholapur, December 1938 |\n| President | Sri Madhava Rao Anne |\n| Chief Guest | Veer Savarkar |\n\n*   The conference discussed the \"Hyderabad problem\" and resolved to observe **20 January** as **\"Hyderabad Liberation Day\"** nationwide, sparking countrywide agitation.\n*   On **31 January 1939**, **Mahatma Narayanaswami**, attempting Satyagraha, was arrested as he entered the Hyderabad state border and externed at Sholapur. Twenty volunteers then continued Satyagraha in successive batches (_jattu_) at Gulbarga: the 2nd batch led by Kunwar Prasad, 3rd by Kushal Raj, 4th by Rajguru and Dhirendranath Shastri — all arrested. Despite mounting restrictions, workers'' enthusiasm only grew; the 5th batch was led by Pandit Vedavrata, 6th by Mahoshaya Krishna, and 7th by Gyanendra. The 8th batch was to be led by Vinayakrao Vidyalankar, but with the government announcing plans for political reforms, Arya Samaj — along with other political parties — temporarily suspended its movement.\n*   Across the state''s broader liberation struggle, around **12,000 Arya Samaj workers** were arrested in various circumstances.\n\n### Local and Educational Contributions\n\n*   The **\"Sadhanagandi\" Gym** was established in Warangal by Arya Samaj leader **Bolugodu Achari**, and further developed by **Bathini Moghilaiah Goud**.\n*   **Bommagani Dharmabhiksham** contributed to building the Arya Samaj mandir in **Suryapet**, and led a campaign against animal sacrifice during Dussehra and the Lingamanthula Swamy fair in the Suryapet region.\n*   Arya Samaj organized Hyderabad''s **first Ganesh celebrations** in **1895** at Shalibanda, Chadarghat.\n*   In **July 1940**, it established the **Kesav Memorial Educational Institutions** at Hyderabad.\n*   In **1954**, it established **Upadeshik Educational Institutions** at Nalgonda.\n\n### G.O. No. 53 and Renewed Repression (1937–1941)\n\n*   **G.O. No. 53 (1937)** — the _\"Firman\"_ order curbing freedom of speech — came into force, requiring government permission to construct havan-kunds. Arya Samaj never accepted this order.\n*   Communal disturbances broke out at **Gulbarga** on a Holi festival day when colors were thrown on a Muslim individual. **Lal Singh, Hanumantha Rao**, and other leading Arya Samaj workers were arrested.\n*   Riots that broke out in **Dhoolpet** on **16 April** took a long time to subside; **21 Arya Samaj workers** were arrested in connection.\n*   On **22 January 1938**, **Dharma Prakash** was murdered at Kalyani.\n*   During Dasara festivities at **Udgir**, **Shyamlal** was arrested on the pretext of maintaining peace; he died in **Bidar jail on 17 December**, a death the government never investigated.\n*   In **1941**, Pandit Vinayakarao Vidyalankar was elected head of the state Arya Samaj, with Narendraji as secretary. Though the organisation had temporarily suspended active agitation, government restrictions on it did not ease:\n    *   **''Om'' pendants** were banned in Damaragidda, Gulbarga district.\n    *   Workers **Gangaram, Ganapatirao, Dattatreya**, and others were murdered in Udgir, Nanded district.\n    *   The Arya Samaj temple at **Alland** was sealed on **4 June 1941**.\n    *   Fourteen workers were arrested; schools they ran at **Ahmedpur** and **Chakur** were shut; a havan school at Sakoli was never allowed to open. Some workers were subjected to severe punishment despite limited scope for wrongdoing.\n\n### Freedom Struggle (1942–1947)\n\n*   **1942–43** was an especially harsh period: workers lived in constant fear for their lives; local Urdu papers spread propaganda against Arya Samaj; the Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen''s campaign branding it a purely religious-fanatic body failed to convince the public — in reality, Arya Samaj workers fought shoulder-to-shoulder with the State Congress in the freedom struggle.\n*   At the **1942 Ughir Arya Pratinidhi Sabha**, Vinayakarao and Narendraji were re-elected chair and secretary; twelve resolutions were passed, chiefly opposing the government''s rushed implementation of constitutional reforms, and warning the government would bear responsibility for any adverse consequences.\n*   Hindu shops were looted in **Owrad, Bidar district**; a Hindu worker who protested was murdered — an incident that deeply pained Mahatma Gandhi, who sent Rs. 1,500 in relief to the affected.\n*   Arya Samaj itself split into two factions.\n*   On **3 March 1942**, communal rioting during an Arya procession at **Hanmakonda** killed **Shivachandra** and three of his followers.\n*   Similar communal disturbances occurred at **Nagarkurnool** and **Hingoli**, where the government one-sidedly restricted only the Hindu side, exposing its bias; comparable organised attacks occurred at **Jogipet** and **Tarafeed**.\n*   In **December 1942**, police at Gulbarga failed to prevent the looting of Hindu shops; that same year, communal clashes at **Nizamabad** went uninvestigated.\n*   By **1944**, an estimated **300 Arya Samaj branches** were active in the state, though exact participation figures in the freedom movement are unclear.\n*   At the **1945 Gulbarga annual Arya Samaj convention**, attending volunteers faced a police lathi-charge, in which **Pandit Narendraji** was severely injured.\n\n### Towards Integration (1947–1949)\n\n*   On **15 August 1947**, India gained independence, but the Nizam dreamed of separate sovereignty for Hyderabad state.\n*   Police arrested **Pandit Narendraji**, who was organizing to have Hyderabad State merge immediately with the Indian Union.\n*   On **2 September 1947**, at a national flag-hoisting meeting in **Parkal** attended by 1,500 people, police firing injured many.\n*   Between **August 1947 and September 1949**, severe unrest gripped the state; Razakar atrocities forced residents to take temporary shelter in camps at **Sholapur, Pandharpur, Barsi, Bijapur, Umarkhed, Balda, Amravati, and Vijayawada**. Arya Samaj gave them generous financial assistance.\n*   **Vinayak Kumar, Satyanarayan Singh, Madan Mohan**, and others gathered secret intelligence on Razakar activities and passed it to Pandit Vinayakarao; on discovering this, the government arrested him.\n*   The \"Vandemataram brothers,\" undeterred by the risks, continued secret exchanges and passed information about foreign arms being smuggled into the state to **India''s Agent-General, K.M. Munshi**.\n*   **Mahatma Anand Swami** sent Rs. 2,000 every month to patriots suffering under police repression.\n*   Arya Samaj maintained a central office in Hyderabad, with branches across several districts extending later to taluka centres. Some Muslim traders under government patronage opposed it, while Marwadi and north Indian settlers tended to support it; its strength within Telangana outside Hyderabad city remained comparatively limited.\n\n### Legacy\n\n*   Arya Samaj was fundamentally a religious organisation, and its members strongly opposed Gandhian non-violence in principle even as they took an active part in politics.\n*   **Pandit Vinayakarao Vidyalankar**, the leading Arya Samaj figure of the era, later became a minister in **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao''s** cabinet. With the fall of the Nizam''s government and the establishment of Congress rule, the organisation lost its distinct political standing.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p2", "partName": "Brahma Samaj and Theosophical Society", "bodyMd": "*   **Raja Ram Mohan Roy** founded Brahma Samaj in **1828** in Bombay.\n*   Its Hyderabad branch was established in **1869**.\n*   Hyderabad''s first Brahma Samaj meeting was held at Sultan Bazar in **1914**, presided over by **Narayana Govind Vellinkar**.\n*   The important Andhra-region member of Brahma Samaj at the time was **Raghupati Venkataratnam Naidu**.\n\n### Theosophical Society\n\n*   Founded in **New York in 1875** by **Madam Helena Blavatsky** (Russia) and **Colonel Olcott** (America).\n*   Its Hyderabad branch was established in **1882** at Chaderghat.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p3", "partName": "Other Socio-Political Organizations", "bodyMd": "| Organisation | Founded | Founder(s)/Leaders | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hindu Social Club | 1892 | Raja Murali Manohar Bahadur | Opposed discrimination against Hindu students sent to England for higher education under the Nizam |\n| Society of Union & Progress | 1929 | Mir Akbar Ali Khan, Padmaja Naidu | Formed against religious indifference shown by the Nizam government |\n| Swadeshi League | 1930 | Padmaja Naidu (Secretary: Fazlu Rehaman) | Spread the importance of Khadi |\n| Nizam Subjects League (also \"Mulki League,\" 1935) | 1934 | President: Sir Nizamath Jung | For protection of Mulki and civil rights |\n| Hyderabad Social Service League | 1915 | Keshav Rao Koratkar, Vaman Nayak | With Ganapati Rao Hardikar, founded the Vivekavardini (Telugu-medium) school in 1906 |\n| Hyderabad Young Men''s Union | 1917 | President: Vaman Nayak | — |\n| Hyderabad State Reforms Association | 1918 | Keshav Rao Karotkar, Vaman Nayak | — |\n| Hindu Dharma Parishad | 1925 | Presided by Raja Pratap Giriji | Bhagya Reddy Varma introduced a proposal for Harijan development; special guest Pandit Sheshadri |\n| Hindu Standing Committee (Hindu Sthayi Sangam) | 1932 | Kashinath Rao Vaidya | Founded at Residency Bazar |\n| Humanitarian League | — | Rai Balamukund (with Bhagya Reddy Varma, Lalji Meghaji) | Fought for Harijan welfare and development |\n| Comrades Association | 13 Dec 1939 | Raja Bahadur Gour, Makhdoom Mohiuddin, Omkar Prasad, Syed Ibrahim | Inaugural president: Kashinath Rao Vaidya |\n", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p4", "partName": "Student Organizations", "bodyMd": "*   India''s first student organisation, the **All India Students Federation**, was founded in **1936** in Lucknow.\n*   Its subsidiary branch, the **All Hyderabad Student Union (AHSU)**, was founded in **1941** at Hyderabad.\n*   **Aquil Ali Khan** and **Omkar Prasad** played important roles in AHSU.\n*   **Anabheri Prabhakar Rao**, from Karimnagar, was among the students who joined AHSU.\n*   With AHSU''s establishment, student hostels turned into political centres and political discussions flourished there.\n*   Many student leaders active in AHSU later became members of the **Andhra Maha Sabha** and the **Communist Party**.\n*   AHSU also played an active role in the **\"Join India\"** campaign before Hyderabad''s merger with the Indian Union.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p5", "partName": "Women''s Organizations", "bodyMd": "| Organisation | Year | Founder(s) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bharatha Mahila Samajam | 1907 | Ravichettu Lakshmi Narasamma (with Sitabai) | First women''s organisation in Hyderabad; founded at Narasamma''s house |\n| Hyderabad Women''s Social Development Association | 1916 | Sarojini Naidu (with Margaret sisters, Amina Begum Hydari, Rustonji Fardhun) | Worked as an affiliate of the All India Women Federation |\n| Yuvati Sharanalayam | 1922 | Yamini Purnatilakam (Secunderabad) | Provided shelter/support for oppressed women, including prostitutes |\n| Andhra Sodari Samajam | 1925 | Nadimpalli Sundaramma, Pandita Veera Raghavamma, Lokanandi Janakamma, Puram Rukminamma, Chatrati Laxminarasamma | Published the magazine \"Savitri\" |\n| Andhra Mahila Sangam | 1930 | Durgabhai Deshmukh and others | — |\n| Andhra Yuvati Mandali | 1935 | Ellapragada Sitakumari, Illandula Saraswati Devi | Later developed by Burgula Ananta Laxmi, Obulreddy Rangamma; established a Shishuvihar in Hyderabad |\n| Arya Yuvajana School | 1938 | Sumitradevi (Chikkadpally) | — |\n| Navajeevan Mandali | 1937 | Chaired by Prameela Bhai | — |\n| All India Mahila Sabha (Hyderabad hostel) | 1931 | — | Opened a hostel for working women; set up rural medical units, education, adult education, training and employment programs |\n\n*   **Sangam Lakshmibai**, popularly known as **\"Telangana Jhansi Lakshmibai,\"** took part in the anti-Nizam struggle and was jailed.\n*   **Muslim Women''s Associations** included Anjuman-e-Kavatik-Deccan, Anjuman-e-Islam, Anjuman-e-Sirajul Kawatik, Lady Hyder Club, and Lady Burdans Club.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p6", "partName": "Labour Unions and Trade Associations", "bodyMd": "*   India''s first labour union, the **All India Trade Union Congress (AITUC)**, was founded by **N.M. Joshi**.\n*   Hyderabad''s first labour union, the **Calendar Company Workers Union**, was founded by **V.V. Giri** in Secunderabad in **1920**.\n*   In **1928**, railway employees went on strike for the first time in Hyderabad — the **first strike in Hyderabad''s history** — led by **Fatehullah Khan** and **Raghavendra Rao**.\n*   In **1935**, **Devuri Shesha Giri Rao** founded the **Singareni Collieries Workers Union**.\n*   The labour union leader of **Warangal Azam Jahi Mills** was **Sarvadevabhatla Ramanadham**.\n*   **Vattikota Alwar Swamy** became a popular labour union leader in Secunderabad during this period.\n*   In **1941**, the Railway Workers Union went on strike, supported by the **Comrades Association** under **Makhdoom Mohiuddin**.\n*   All the state''s labour unions eventually united to form the **All Hyderabad Trade Union Congress (AHTUC)** — President: Makhdoom Mohiuddin; Secretary: **Dr. Raj Bahadur Gour**.\n*   On **16 August 1946**, AHTUC merged with AITUC.\n\n### Trade (Merchant) Associations\n\n*   Under the Nizam, traders faced repeated exploitation: they were required to supply goods free of cost to visiting government officials, and at meagre prices to Deshmukhs, Deshpandes, Patels, and Patwaris.\n*   The **Suryapet Traders Association** was founded on **2 December 1922** by **Puvvada Venkatappaiah, Bhagari Laxmaiah**, and others; its rules and regulations, drafted by **Madapati Hanumanth Rao**, became a model for other trader associations.\n*   The **Bhuvanagiri Traders Association** (Bhuvanagiri Mitra Mandali) was formed with the support of **Arutla Laxmi Narsimha Reddy**; its secretary was **G.S. Gupta**.\n\n**Traders Mahasabhas**\n\n| Sabha | Year |\n|---|---|\n| Suryapet Sabha | 1923 |\n| Miryalagudem Sabha | 1928 |\n| Nalgonda Sabha | 1928 |\n| Tipparthi Sabha | 1929 |\n\n*   The president of the Tipparthi meetings was **Pulijala Venkat Ranga Rao**. At the Tipparthi meeting, **Pabbati Yellaiah** published a book, **\"Vartaka Swatantryam,\"** written by **Mandamula Ramachandra Rao** on behalf of the Tipparthi Traders Association.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p7", "partName": "Caste Associations", "bodyMd": "| Sangam | Founded/Year | Key Persons | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Reddy Sangam | 1918 | Raja Bahadur Venkata Rama Reddy | Reddy hostel admitted students irrespective of caste or religion |\n| Yadava Sangam | 1920 | Seetaramaiah Yadav | — |\n| Mudiraj Sangam | 1922 | President: G. Ramakrishnaiah | Mudiraj Mahasabha held in 1922 |\n| Munnuru Kapu Sangam | 1922 (Mahasabha 1935) | — | 1935 Nizam Rashtra Andhra Munnuru Kapu Mahasabha; book \"Munnuru Kapu Kula Abyudayam\" authored by Bojja Narsimhulu |\n| Gouda Sangam | — | Chiragu Veeranna | Established hostels for Goud students |\n| Padmashali Sangam | — | Konda Laxman Bapuji, Guntaka Narsaiah Panthulu, Hakim Narayan Das | 1929 All India Padmashali Sabha held in Hyderabad; 1950 Hyderabad Handloom Weavers Central Co-operative Association founded (later renamed **HYCO**); book \"Mohturbha Maggam Pannu\" (criticizing the weaving tax) by Mateti Papayya; 1946 Nizam State Padmashali Mahasabha held in Warangal, presided over by Konda Laxman Bapuji |\n| Vaishya Sangam (Vaishya Yuva Jana Sangam) | 1939 | K.C. Gupta | — |\n| Vishwa Brahman Sabha | — | Chintapalli Raghava Chary | — |\n| Sri Vaishnava Mahasabha | — | Tirunagari Venkatappayya | — |\n| Kalavanthula Sangam | — | Tirunagari Venkatappayya | — |\n", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter23-telangana-adi-hindu-movement', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter23-telangana-adi-hindu-movement-p1", "partName": "Madari Bhagya Reddy Varma", "bodyMd": "The Adi-Hindu movement emerged in Hyderabad state to fight discrimination against Dalits, untouchability, and caste differences. **Madari Bhagya Reddy Varma** was the central figure behind this movement.\n\nBhagya Reddy Varma is regarded as the **Father of the Telangana Dalit Movement** and was named the **\"Dalit Vaithalika.\"**\n\n### Early Life\n\n*   Born on **22nd May, 1888** into a \"Mala\" family in Hyderabad, to Madari Venkaiah and Rangamamba.\n*   His original name was **Madari Bhagaiah**; his family guru renamed him Bhagya Reddy.\n*   **Raavichettu Ranga Rao** financially supported his education.\n*   He left home during childhood due to a family issue and joined a Roman Catholic Brothers'' Tennis Court as a tennis ball boy, growing close to the Catholic Church family.\n*   He later worked as an electrician in the electricity department, and subsequently joined the **Brahma Samaj** and **Arya Samaj**.\n\n### Titles\n\n| Title | Given By |\n|---|---|\n| Varma | Balaji Krishna Rao (Arya Samaj member, recognizing his services to Arya Samaj) |\n| Shiva Shresta | Vaman Nayak |\n| Sangamanya | Given at the Adi Andhra Mahasabha held in Elluru |\n\n### Religious and Literary Contributions\n\n*   He was drawn to Buddhism and celebrated **Buddha Jayanthi** every year from **1913 to 1937** (first celebrated in 1913 on Vaishaka Shudda Purnima; last celebrated under his leadership on **25th May, 1937**).\n*   He wrote **\"Vetti Madiga,\"** the first Dalit story in Telugu literature.\n\n### Organizations Established\n\nBhagya Reddy Varma founded a series of organizations as part of the Adi Hindu movement:\n\n| Organization | Year |\n|---|---|\n| Jagan Mitra Mandali | 1906 |\n| Adi Hindu Karyakartala Dalam | 1906 |\n| Vaidika Dharma Prachaini Sabha | 1910 |\n| Manya Sangam | 1911 |\n| Ahimsa Samajam | 1912 |\n| Swastik Volunteers Sangam | 1912 |\n| Jeeva Raksha Gnana Pracharak Mandali | 1915 |\n| Vishwa Gruha Paricharak Sammelanam | 1916 |\n| Adi Hindu Social Service League | 1922 |\n\n**Jagan Mitra Mandali (1906):** Established with the aim of spreading awareness among Dalits, promoting educational development, and uniting them.\n\n*   In 1910, a primary school was established at Islamia Bazar under this initiative; many more Adi Hindu Schools followed.\n*   In 1929, **Gandhiji visited** the Adi Hindu School established by Bhagya Reddy Varma.\n*   At a meeting organized by Bhagya Reddy Varma at **Victory Play Ground, Chaderghat**, Gandhiji warned Hindus that practicing untouchability is a sin and a bad mark for Hinduism.\n*   From 1934, the **Nizam government** took over responsibility for **26 Adi Hindu Schools** founded by Bhagya Reddy Varma.\n*   Separately, schools for Dalit girls in Hyderabad were first established by **Katta Ramakka** and **Jangam Sarvaiah**; Katta Ramakka started a school in front of her house at Secunderabad Kalva Basthi for Dalit girls.\n\n**Manya Sangam:** Jagan Mitra Mandali (1906) was renamed Manya Sangam in 1911, and later renamed **Adi Hindu Social Service League** in 1922. Valthati Sheshaiah was the president of the Manya Sangam working committee.\n\nImportant programs of Manya Sangam:\n\n*   Spreading awareness regarding education.\n*   Abolition of the Devadasi and Jogini systems.\n*   Abolition of child marriages.\n*   Ban of alcohol.\n*   Prohibiting meat on special occasions.\n\nRelated initiatives:\n\n*   Bhagya Reddy Varma established a **Devadasi eradication organization** to abolish the Devadasi system.\n*   In 1915, he established the **Sanga Samskara Nataka Mandali**, having Harijans perform dramas.\n*   In 1916, he established the **Vishwa Gruha Paricharika Sammelanam**, which worked for the welfare of maid servants.\n\n### Self-Respect Movement and Adi Andhra Conferences\n\nBhagya Reddy Varma led self-respect movements for Dalits in both Hyderabad and the Andhra region.\n\n*   In 1917, the **1st Adi Andhra Conference** (also called the 1st Pradeshika Panchama Mahajana Sabha) was held in Vijayawada by **Guduru Ramachandra Rao**, with **Bhagya Reddy Varma as president**.\n*   At this meeting, he named Dalits as **\"Adi Hindus\"** and urged them to identify by that name.\n\n### Adi Hindu League Conferences (1921–24)\n\nFour Adi Hindu League Conferences were held in Hyderabad under Bhagya Reddy Varma''s leadership between 1921 and 1924.\n\n*   **1st Conference (1921):** Presided over by **Papanna**. **Kusuma Dharmanna**, an attendee, sang the song **\"Maa Koddhu Nalla Dorathanam.\"**\n*   **2nd Conference (1922):** Presided over by **Keshav Rao Koratkar**.\n\n### All India Adi Hindu Social Conference (1922)\n\n*   In March 1922, Bhagya Reddy Varma organized the **1st All India Adi Hindu Social Conference** at Residency Bazar, Hyderabad. **M.L. Adaiah** presided over this conference.\n*   Due to his sustained efforts, the terms \"Panchama\" and \"Parayalu\" were removed from government records — communities began to be officially called **Adi Dravida** (in Tamil regions) and **Adi Andhra** (in Andhra regions), as per a **G.O. issued by the Madras Government on 25th March, 1922**.\n\n### Adi Hindu Sabha and Related Activities (1925)\n\n*   In 1925, Bhagya Reddy Varma organized the **Adi Hindu Sabha**, with **Subedar Sayanna** (prominently known as a leader of the Arundhatis) as president.\n*   In 1925, he organized an exhibition of portraits and sculptures created by Adi-Hindus at Residency Bazar, Hyderabad.\n*   In 1925, he conducted **Adi Hindu Youth Gymnastic competitions** at Prem Theater Ground, Hyderabad, under the leadership of **Kodi Rammoorthy Nayudu**, to encourage Adi-Hindu youth.\n*   When **Plague and Cholera** spread in Hyderabad in 1925, he established **Volunteer Medical Camps** for prevention and treatment.\n\n### National-Level Activities (1927–1931)\n\n*   **1927:** Represented South India at the **All India Downtrodden Castes Conference** held at Allahabad.\n*   **March 1927:** Presided over the **Mathangi Sabha** held at Mallepalli, where a resolution was passed banning alcohol.\n*   **November 1930:** Attended the **All India Adi Hindu Conference** held at Allahabad.\n*   **27–28 September 1931:** Elected president of the **All India Adi Hindu Special Conference** held at Lucknow, attended by representatives from across the country, who demanded **separate constituencies for Dalits**.\n    *   At this conference, a unanimous resolution was passed electing **Dr. B.R. Ambedkar** as the sole representative of the 9 crore Adi Hindu Samaj of India.\n    *   Bhagya Reddy Varma **renounced the title \"Varma\"** from his name at this conference, after a participant pointed out that the term denoted upper caste.\n*   **November 1931:** The **Adi Hindu Political Conference** was held at Bollarum, Secunderabad, presided over by Bhagya Reddy Varma.\n    *   **Bandhela Chittaraiah** chaired the Invitation Committee.\n    *   **Vemula Kurmaiah**, president of the Andhra State Independent Adijana Conference, inaugurated the conference.\n    *   With Ambedkar as leader, resolutions were passed for the **abolition of Vetti** and for the **appointment of Adi Hindus in military and police jobs**.\n*   Bhagya Reddy Varma also participated in the **Andhra Mahasabha**, passing several resolutions related to Dalits.\n*   Due to his efforts, the **Nizam government officially recorded untouchables as \"Adi-Hindus\" in the 1931 census**.\n\n### Later Life and Legacy\n\n*   In 1937, he founded the **Jambavarna Seva Samithi**, which worked for the upliftment of Dalits.\n*   Bhagya Reddy Varma also ran a **parallel judicial system** in Telangana, conducting **Nyaya Panchayats** at the village level with the help of his disciples.\n*   He died in **February 1939**.\n*   After his death, his son **M.B. Goutham** established the Adi Hindu Social Service organization.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter23-telangana-adi-hindu-movement-p2", "partName": "Arige Ramaswamy", "bodyMd": "*   Born in **Ramankola, Ranga Reddy district, in 1875**; completed his studies at Secunderabad.\n*   Worked as an office boy, a carpenter, and later as a **Ticket Collector for Nizam Railways**.\n*   Spread the principles of **Vaishnavism, Brahma Samaj, and the doctrine of Achala**.\n*   Established the **Sunitha Bala Samajam in 1912** at Secunderabad, working for Dalit education, abolition of the Devadasi system, and prohibition of alcohol.\n\n**Adi Hindu Jathiyonnathi Sabha:**\n\n*   Established by Arige Ramaswamy in **1922** to work for the upliftment of Hindu society and the eradication of the Devadasi system.\n*   President: **Konda Venkat Swamy**; Secretaries: **Papayya** and **Arige Ramaswamy**.\n*   Main aims: opposing the concept of Shudras/Neecha Janma theory, and establishing equality among people.\n*   In **1927**, it was renamed the **Adi Hindu Mahasabha**.\n\nLater activities:\n\n*   In 1931, he established the **Arundhatiya Mahasabha**, and on **1st June 1931** conducted the Arundatiya conference at the Reddy hostel of Nizam State.\n*   In 1934, contesting on behalf of the Adi Hindu Social Service League, he was elected the **1st Dalit Councillor in the Hyderabad Municipal Corporation**.\n*   He was the only Dalit poet to take part in the **Golconda Kavula Sanchika**, published by **Suravaram Pratap Reddy**.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter23-telangana-adi-hindu-movement-p3", "partName": "B.S. Venkat Rao", "bodyMd": "Known as **\"Hyderabad Ambedkar,\"** B.S. Venkat Rao was born on **11th December, 1898** at Hyderabad. His original name was **Bathula Ashaiah**, and he was popularly known as **\"Rao Saheb\"** among his followers.\n\n*   Worked at the Nizam government engineering college, and later in the Nizamsagar project department of the Nizam''s government.\n*   In **1922**, established the **Adi Dravida Sangam**, aimed at eradicating the Devadasi system and bringing awareness and unity among Dalits.\n*   In **1927**, founded the **Adi Hindu Mahasabha**, under which he built libraries and temples for Dalits at Ghasmandi, Secunderabad (later renamed **Adeyya Nagar**).\n*   Presided over the **Mahar Mahasabha** held at Pune in **May 1936**.\n*   In **1936**, established the **Ambedkar Youth League** for the upliftment of Dalit youth.\n*   In **1938**, established the **Hyderabad State Depressed Classes Association**.\n*   Convinced the Nizam to create the **Scheduled Castes Trust Fund**, with **1 crore rupees** for Dalit development — the first welfare fund of its kind in the country.\n*   Established the **Adi Hindu Library**.\n\n### Political Career\n\n| Year | Position |\n|---|---|\n| 1938 | Secunderabad Cantonment Board member |\n| 1939 | Hyderabad Municipal Councillor |\n| 1946 | Member of Legislative Assembly (MLA) from local bodies |\n| 1947 | Education Minister, Mir Layak Ali cabinet |\n| 1952 | Rajya Sabha member from Hyderabad Legislative Assembly |\n\nAs recognition for his services, the Nizam awarded him the title **\"Khusro-e-Alim.\"**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter23-telangana-adi-hindu-movement-p4", "partName": "Other Leaders of the Adi-Hindu Movement", "bodyMd": "### M.L. Adeyya\n\n*   Called the **\"Bhishma of Dalit Communities.\"**\n*   In **1906**, established a school for Adi Hindus named **\"Sir William Barter\"** at Secunderabad, on land donated by William Barter.\n*   The school was later renamed the **M.L. Adeyya Memorial High School**.\n\n### Peesari Veeranna\n\n*   In 1937, when Gandhiji visited Hyderabad, Peesari Veeranna **opposed the word \"Harijan\"** used by Gandhiji, arguing that the community should be called Adi-Hindus instead.\n*   He fought against **Vetti**, urging villagers not to perform it.\n\n### B. Shyam Sunder\n\n*   Authored the book **\"They Burn,\"** describing the conditions of Dalits in Hyderabad at the time.\n*   **P.R. Venkat Swamy** described his entry into the Dalit movement as \"a red-letter day in the history of the depressed classes movement.\"\n*   Presided over the **Depressed Classes Conference** held at Parbhani, Marathwada, on **30th May, 1942**.\n*   Convinced the Nizam to contribute **5 lakh rupees** to the people''s education trust created by Dr. B.R. Ambedkar in Aurangabad.\n*   Was part of the Nizam''s delegation to France, along with **Moin Nawaz Jung and Zaheer Ahmed**, for matters relating to the formation of the Nizam State.\n*   Explained the conditions of Dalits at the **United Nations Security Council** and other international forums.\n*   Installed statues of Ambedkar at **Bidar** as a mark of self-respect for Dalits.\n*   In **1954**, became president of the **All India Depressed Classes Association**.\n\n### P.R. Venkat Swamy\n\n*   Wrote a book on Telangana''s Dalit movement titled **\"Our Struggles for Emancipation.\"**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter23-telangana-adi-hindu-movement-p5", "partName": "Dalit Press and Literature", "bodyMd": "### Dalit Newspapers\n\n| Newspaper | Details |\n|---|---|\n| The Panchama (1918) | First Dalit newspaper published in Telangana; English monthly magazine; edited by **J.S. Mathaiah** |\n| Bhagya Nagar (1931) | Started by Bhagya Reddy Varma; renamed **Adi Hindu Newspaper** in 1937 |\n\n### Important Literary Works of Dalits\n\n*   **Unnava Lakshmi Narayana** — _Maalapalli_\n*   **Boyi Bhimanna** — _Paleru nundi Padmashri varaku_ (Paleru to Padmashri); popularized the term **\"Mamalu\"** (Mala–Madiga)\n*   **Gurram Jashuva** — _Gabbilam_", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana-p1", "partName": "Peasant Rebellions in Telangana", "bodyMd": "Peasant unrest across Telangana in the pre-Independence decades grew out of a single underlying condition: landlords (Jagirdars, Deshmukhs, Zamindars, and Maktadars) extracted illegal taxes, forced labour (Vetti), and land from the peasantry, and local agitations broke out wherever this exploitation became unbearable.\n\n### Kolanpaka Peasants Agitation\n\n*   Kolanpaka village lies near Alair in Bhuvanagiri Taluq.\n*   **Andhra Maha Sabha leaders from this village:** Arutla Lakshmi Narsimha Reddy and Arutla Ram Chandra Reddy.\n*   The Kolanpaka Jagirdar, Nawab Turaj Yar Jung, collected illegal taxes from farmers and forced them into Vetti (unpaid labour).\n*   The Jagirdar also ran a programme of forcibly converting Dalits in the village to Islam. The Arutla brothers opposed this, brought in Arya Samaj members, and re-converted the affected Dalits to Hinduism through the Shuddhi programme.\n*   The forced religious conversions carried out by the Jagirdar were the main reason for the agitation of the Kolanpaka peasants.\n\n### Bethavolu Peasants Agitation\n\n*   Bethavolu was a Zamindari region, a cluster of Makta villages, then in Huzur Nagar Taluq of Nalgonda district (now in Suryapet district).\n*   **Bethavolu Maktadar:** Tadakamalla Sitha Ramchandra Rao.\n*   The collection of illegal and exorbitant taxes was the important reason behind the revolt of farmers and villagers.\n\n**Taxes imposed by the Bethavolu Maktadar:**\n\n| No. | Tax |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Cents Tax |\n| 2 | Pullari Tax |\n| 3 | God Tax |\n| 4 | Marriage Tax |\n| 5 | Relatives Tax |\n| 6 | Adoption Tax |\n| 7 | Illarikam Tax |\n| 8 | Adabidda Gampalu (Baskets) |\n| 9 | Koulu Pannu (Tenant Tax) |\n| 10 | Maggam Pannu (Weavers Tax) |\n| 11 | Manthrasani Pannu (Midwife Tax) |\n| 12 | Santhana Pannu |\n| 13 | Prostitute Tax |\n\n*   The agitating peasants'' demands were abolition of these illegal taxes and rights over the land.\n\n**Key roles:**  \n\n*   Suryapet Pleader Amrutha Reddy fought the case in favour of the peasants.\n*   Vemulapalli Venkata Krishna fought on behalf of the Zamindar.\n*   Bethavolu Patwari Pragada Raj Gopala Rao sided with the peasants.\n\n### Shaik Bandagi''s Land Agitation against the Visnoor Deshmukh\n\n*   The Visnoor Deshmukh of Janagaon Taluq, Rapaka Ramchandra Reddy, was known for cruelty and committed many atrocities on the people.\n*   Shaik Bandagi, a poor Muslim peasant of Kamareddy Gudem village, had four brothers (five including himself); the eldest, Abbas Ali, worked as a clerk under Ramachandra Reddy while the rest cultivated land.\n\n**Sequence of events:**  \n\n1.  The five brothers divided their land; Abbas Ali, being the eldest, received 8 acres more.\n2.  Abbas Ali later sold off his own share and tried to annex his brothers'' land.\n3.  Shaik Bandagi protested on his brothers'' behalf; a panchayat was held before the Deshmukh, who ruled in favour of Abbas Ali.\n4.  Bandagi then approached the court. The Deshmukh, angered, humiliated and threatened him.\n5.  Bandagi persisted and won the court judgment in his favour.\n6.  The humiliated Deshmukh conspired with Abbas Ali to kill Bandagi.\n7.  On 26 July 1940, while Bandagi was travelling to Hyderabad to collect the judgment copy, the Deshmukh''s goons murdered him near a bus stop.\n\n*   Bandagi''s brothers and friends buried him at the site, where a tomb was built; his death anniversary (Urus/Ursu) is observed there every year.\n*   This incident became a major inspiration for the Telangana Armed Struggle, and the play _Maa Bhoomi_ opens with a tribute to Shaik Bandagi.\n\n### Munugodu Peasants Revolt\n\n*   Munugodu village was in Nalgonda Taluq; its Deshmukh was Kancharla Rami Reddy, who harassed the general public.\n*   While travelling to Nalgonda in a bullock cart, the Deshmukh was stopped, killed, and his cart set ablaze by the people.\n\n### Paritala Peasants Struggle\n\n*   The Paritala Jagirdar was Nawab Kamal Yar Jung, who harassed people in his Jagir and extracted money through illegal taxes.\n*   The Paritala Jagir villages were not administratively connected to either the Nizam government or British-ruled Andhra.\n*   The Jagirdar''s agent, Lanka Subramaniya Shastri, tortured the people in various ways; the people revolted and killed him.\n\n### Other Landlord Agitations\n\nSimilar agitations against landlords also took place in the following villages:\n\n| Village | Landlord/Zamindar |\n|---|---|\n| Ammula Palem | Kalluri Rajeshwar Rao |\n| Mella Cheruvu | Chennuru Veera Bhadra Rao |\n| Bakkamanthula Gudem | Boghala Veera Reddy |\n| Errabadu | Jinna Reddy Pratap Reddy |\n| Dharmapuram (Zamindari) | Vekuri Raghav Rao |\n| Mundrai | Kadari Narsimha Rao |\n| Mulkala Gudem | Pingali Ranga Reddy |\n\n*   In Dharmapuram and Mundrai villages of Jangoan Taluq, Lambada tribal people held 200 acres of land that was taken over by landlords. With the help of the Andhra Mahasabha and the Communists, the Lambadas protested and reclaimed their land.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana-p2", "partName": "Tribal Rebellions in Telangana", "bodyMd": "Tribals traditionally had an inseparable relationship with the forests they lived in. With colonial and Nizam-era forest administration, the landlords and merchants of the plains began exploiting tribal communities in forest regions, and the state progressively curtailed tribal rights over forest land.\n\n### Forest Policy and Its Impact on Tribals\n\n*   Salar Jung established the Forest Department in Hyderabad State for the first time in 1867; forest policies followed in 1890 and 1900, expanding government rights over forests.\n*   With this, the traditional rights of tribals over the forests were abolished — Podu (shifting) cultivation in reserved forests and the collection of forest produce were both prohibited.\n*   **Effect:** major changes in the social and economic condition of tribal communities.\n*   Under the 1917 Act brought by the Nizam government:\n*   Tribals had to pay \"Bancherai Pannu\" and \"Pay Dumpa Patti\" for grazing cattle in the forest and for collecting firewood respectively.\n*   Ghar Patti, Nagar Patti, Fasal Patti, and Choubina were further taxes levied on tribal people.\n*   Tribals were additionally forced to pay various illegal taxes and fines to forest officials, failing which false cases were filed against them.\n*   Frustrated by these Nizam-era laws, the Gond community of Adilabad revolted under Ramji Gond and later under Komaram Bheem, against the interference of officials and non-tribals, demanding tribal control over forests.\n\n### Ramji Gond Rebellion (1857–60)\n\n*   Ramji Gond, active from around 1851–60, was the first tribal freedom fighter to resist British rule, making Jangaon (Asifabad) in Adilabad district his centre.\n*   During the 1857 Sepoy Mutiny, a large body of Rohilla soldiers under Tantia Tope entered Adilabad and made Nirmal their centre to fight the British.\n*   From 1857–60, Gond tribals and Rohilla Muslims of Adilabad rebelled together under Ramji Gond and Haji Rohilla.\n*   Ramji Gond raised and trained an army of Marathas, Telugus, Rohillas, and Gonds, liberated Nirmal and surrounding areas, and briefly ran a self-government with Nirmal as its capital.\n*   The British sent a large force under Army Chief Colonel Robert to suppress the rebellion at Nirmal; the British and Nizam armies attacked and tortured the Gonds and Rohillas.\n*   Ramji Gond initially fought a successful guerrilla campaign, but his forces were eventually overpowered by the superior weapons and numbers of the British and Nizam armies.\n*   Ramji Gond was captured along with 1,000 of his soldiers and hanged from a banyan tree on 9 April 1860 at Nirmal, near Khajana Cheruvu Gattu. The tree is since known as **Veyyi Urila Chettu** (or Veyyi Urila Marri).\n\n### Nayakpod and Kolam Rebellion (1879–80)\n\n*   The Nayakpod, Kolam, and other tribal communities of the forests between Bhadrachalam and Rekapalli revolted together during 1879–80.\n*   They revolted jointly against the forest officers and the government because these officers had tripled the tax on Podu cultivation in violation of forest acts.\n\n### Konda Reddy Rebellion (1915–16)\n\n*   The Konda Reddy tribes of the Papikonda forest area, between Bhadrachalam and West Godavari, revolted in 1915–16.\n*   They rose against forest officers and the police for supporting extraction of forest resources by non-tribals with government backing.\n\n### Komaram Bheem Rebellion (1938–40)\n\n**Early life and background**\n\n*   Komaram Bheem, a Gond tribal leader, was born on 22 October 1901 in the forest region of Sankepalli, Jodeghat, Asifabad division of Adilabad district, to Kumram Chinnu and Sombai.\n*   The Gonds and other tribals of the Jodeghat region practised Podu cultivation.\n*   The 1917 forest act, nominally for \"Protection of Tribal Lands,\" instead disrupted tribal life by imposing restrictions and taxes; a Tahsil Office was set up at Utnoor in 1918 to begin collecting forest and revenue taxes.\n*   In 1935, a paper mill was established at Sirpur–Khagaznagar, and large tracts of forest land were taken over for it; tribals who could not resist ended up as labourers in the mill.\n\n**Path to rebellion**\n\n*   Bheem''s family migrated to Surdapur village in Kerimeri mandal, where tribals practised Podu cultivation, but their crop produce was forcibly taken by the Zamindar Siddique.\n*   Bheem revolted against Siddique''s extortion and, in a confrontation, struck him with a stick on the head, killing him instantly.\n*   Facing a Nizam government murder case, Bheem fled to Ballarsha (Maharashtra) with his friend Kondal, then to Chandrapur, where he worked in a printing press under a social worker, Vitthoba, and learned to read and write under his influence.\n*   After Vitthoba''s arrest by the police, Bheem moved to Assam and worked in tea and coffee plantations for five years, where he was inspired by the Indian freedom struggle.\n*   He then returned to Jodeghat in Adilabad to fight for tribal rights.\n\n**The struggle**\n\n*   Bheem made Jodeghat and Babejari his centres of operation and raised the slogan **\"Jal–Jungle–Jameen\"** (Water–Forest–Land).\n*   He fought for the independence of 12 tribal regions under the banner **\"Maavenot–Maaveraj\"** (Maa Gudem lo Maa Rajyam).\n*   Bheem, with four other Gonds, travelled to Hyderabad to seek land titles from the Nizam, Osman Ali Khan, but was not granted an audience.\n*   He wrote several letters to the Divisional Forest Officer and the Sub-Collector detailing the harassment by forest employees, but received no response.\n*   Concluding that armed resistance was the only option, Bheem raised his own army from the youth of the 12 tribal villages of Jodeghat, arming them with conventional weapons and locally made muzzle-loading guns.\n*   He revolted against the local Taluqdar, Sattar, and continued a guerrilla campaign against the Nizam''s forces around Asifabad.\n\n**The Jodeghat encounter and aftermath**\n\n*   On 27 October 1940, acting on information from the informer Kurdupatel, Asifabad Taluqdar Abdul Sattar led an unannounced attack on Bheem''s camp at Jodeghat with 300 armed police.\n*   The police opened indiscriminate fire, killing 140 tribals. Komaram Bheem fought bravely but was killed by the Nizam''s soldiers in the encounter.\n*   Following the Jodeghat incident, the Nizam, Mir Osman Ali Khan, appointed the Austrian ethnologist and London University professor Christopher von Fürer-Haimendorf to study the conditions of the Gonds.\n*   The Gonds are numerically the largest tribal group in the country; their native region was originally Bastar (present-day Chhattisgarh), and in Telangana they are concentrated in Adilabad and Asifabad, where they are known as Raj Gonds.\n*   Haimendorf stayed at Marlawai village to study Gond conditions and later submitted recommendations to the Nizam government.\n*   Based on these recommendations, the Nizam and Revenue Minister Wilfred Gregson introduced the **\"Dastar-Ul-Amal\"** Act in 1940.\n*   Dr. B. R. Ambedkar later framed the rules under the Fifth and Sixth Schedules for the protection of tribal lands as a consequence of the Komaram Bheem struggle, which in turn led to the enactment of the 1/70 Act in India.\n\n**Legacy**\n\n*   Komaram Bheem is revered as the eternal hero of the Gond people; his death anniversary is observed by his heirs and the Gond tribals of Adilabad on the Aswayuja Pournami (full moon) following Dussehra, in October.\n*   The Telangana government has observed Komaram Bheem''s death anniversary as a **\"State Festival\"** since 2014.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter24-peasant-and-tribal-movements-in-telangana-p3", "partName": "Telangana Armed Struggle", "bodyMd": "The Telangana Armed Struggle was conducted between 1946 and 1951 by the Communists, with the central objective of securing land, food, and freedom — **\"Bhoomi, Bhukti, Vimukti.\"** Around 4,000 members were martyred in the course of the struggle, which freed many villages from the atrocities of the Razakars and landlords; peasants fought specifically against the extraction practised by Deshmukhs, Patels, Patwaris, and landlords.\n\n### Main Reasons for the Armed Struggle\n\n**1\\. Landlordism (Feudal System)**  \n\n*   Hundreds of acres of land were concentrated under landlords, with peasants reduced to tenants who had to work the landlord''s fields before cultivating their own.\n*   The scale of landholding among Jagirdars and Zamindars:\n\n| Landlord | Area (acres) |\n|---|---|\n| Janna Reddy Pratap Reddy (Suryapet Deshmukh) | 1,50,000 |\n| Kalluru Zamindar (Khammam) | 1,00,000 |\n| Narsapur Zamindar (Laksettipet) | 50,000 |\n| Visnoor Ramachandra Reddy (Jangoan) | 40,000 |\n\n*   To end this landlordism, peasants launched the armed struggle themselves; \"Sangams\" were later formed in villages by the Communists through the Andhra Mahasabha to organize and support the peasants.\n*   Landlords extorted crop produce in the name of taxes, and peasants additionally had to bribe the Patel, Patwari, and Revenue Inspectors.\n\n2.  **Money-lenders and the Nagu Vaddi System** — under Nagu Vaddi, the loan amount doubled within a year.\n3.  **Hukki Malikana Tax**, levied on toddy workers/tappers.\n4.  **Vetti / Baghela systems** of forced labour.\n5.  **Programmes against Hinduism** (forced conversions).\n6.  **Formation of the Andhra Mahasabha.**\n7.  **Formation of the Telangana Communist Party.**\n\n### Formation of the Telangana Communist Party\n\n*   In December 1939, Maqdoom Mohiuddin, Sayyad Ibrahim, and Raj Bahadur Gour established an organization called the **\"Comrades Association.\"**\n*   In 1940, Raavi Narayana Reddy and Baddam Yella Reddy founded the Communist Party in Hyderabad.\n*   **Important Communist leaders of Hyderabad:** Raavi Narayana Reddy, Baddam Yella Reddy, Maqdoom Mohiuddin, Bheem Reddy Narsimha Reddy, the Arutla Brothers, and Devulapalli Venkateshwar Rao.\n\n### Phases of the Telangana Armed Struggle\n\nFrom the founding of the Communist Party in 1940 to 1951, the Armed Struggle is divided into four phases:\n\n| Phase | Period | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1st | 1940–46 | Consolidation of Communist strength |\n| 2nd | 1946–47 | Revolt against landlords and Deshmukhs (Doras) |\n| 3rd | 1947–48 | Armed rebellion against the Nizam |\n| 4th | 1948–51 | Revolt against the Indian government |\n\n#### Phase 1 (1940–46)\n\n*   Communists conducted \"Education Week\" and \"Beghar Week\" campaigns to build public support and gradually strengthened their base in Hyderabad.\n*   Members of the Andhra Mahasabha organized themselves as \"Sangams\" and went to villages to spread awareness.\n\n**Key event — Shaik Bandagi''s murder:** Bandagi, a poor Muslim farmer of Kamareddy Gudem in Jangaon Taluq, held 4 acres of agricultural land under the Visnoor Deshmukh Rapaka Venkata Ramachandra Reddy. A land dispute arose with relatives Fakir Ahmed, Abbas Ali (who held the post of police patel), and their brothers. The Deshmukh ruled in Abbas Ali''s favour, but Bandagi defied the verdict, approached the local court, and won. Unable to bear the defeat, Abbas Ali and his brothers murdered Bandagi with axes on 26 July 1940, with the Deshmukh''s backing.  \n\n*   This murder became a founding inspiration for the Armed Struggle; the play _**Maa Bhoomi**_ — written by **Sunkara Satyanarayana** and **Vasireddy Bhaskar Rao**, built around the theme \"Dunne Vadide Bhoomi\" (land for the tiller) — opens with a tribute to Shaik Bandagi and was performed extensively in villages during the struggle.\n\n#### Phase 2 (4 July 1946 – 12 June 1947)\n\n**Chakali Ailamma Struggle (Chityala Ailamma)**\n\n*   Chakali Ailamma, of the Rajaka caste, belonged to Palakurthi village in Jangoan Taluq, an area under Visnoor Deshmukh''s control; her husband was Chityala Narsaiah.\n*   Ailamma''s family cultivated 10 acres of wet land and 20 acres of dry land as tenants under Utham Raju Raghava Rao, the Maktedar of Mallempally.\n*   She supported the Andhra Mahasabha and the Communist Party against Visnoor Deshmukh Ramchandra Reddy and police patel Veeramaneni Sheshagiri Rao. In retaliation, the \"Palakurthi Conspiracy Case\" was filed against her husband Narasaiah and sons Somaiah and Lachhaiah, who were arrested with other party leaders.\n*   The Deshmukh seized Ailamma''s leased land, had it re-registered in his own name through the Mallampally Maktedar, and sent goons to seize the crop.\n*   Andhra Mahasabha members Chakilam Yadagiri Rao, Bheem Reddy Narsimha Reddy, and Nalla Pratap Reddy fought off the goons and recovered the crop for Ailamma''s family, and her courage during the episode made her a heroic figure of the movement.\n\n**Palakurthi Conspiracy Case**\n\n*   Andhra Mahasabha leader Arutla Ram Chandra Reddy visited Palakurthi village to inaugurate a village library.\n*   The Visnoor Deshmukh sent goons to disrupt the meeting; the villagers retaliated and severely beat the gang leader, Vanamali Venkaiah.\n*   The angered Deshmukh attacked the village with his goons and the police, filing false cases — including an attempted-murder charge against 12 people, among them Arutla Ramachandra Reddy — an episode remembered as the Palakurthi Conspiracy Case.\n*   **Lawyer for the villagers:** Konda Laxman Bapuji.\n\n**Doddi Komaraiah''s Death**\n\n*   Doddi Komaraiah, of the Kuruma (shepherd) caste, belonged to Kadavendi village in Jangaon Taluq, and was the brother of Doddi Mallaiah, a committee member of the Andhra Mahasabha.\n*   Janakamma (\"Dorasani\"), mother of Visnoor Deshmukh Ramchandra Reddy, lived in Kadavendi and committed many atrocities against the people there.\n*   Andhra Mahasabha leaders Erram Reddy Mohan Reddy, Nalla Narsimhulu, Yerramreddy Kondal Reddy, and organizer Katkuri Ramachandra Reddy revolted against Janakamma; she in turn hired goons under Mishkin Ali to attack Sangam members'' houses.\n*   Sangam members under Erramreddy Mohan Reddy marched with sticks toward the Deshmukh''s Gadi (fort/residence) along with villagers; 23-year-old Doddi Komaraiah joined the march with a stick.\n*   On 4 July 1946, the goons opened indiscriminate fire on the marchers; Doddi Komaraiah was killed, becoming the **first martyr** of the Telangana Armed Struggle.\n*   News of his death drew people from surrounding villages to Kadavendi for retaliatory action; newspapers including the Communist Party–Andhra Maha Sabha press, _Meezan_, _Praja Shakthi_, and _Rehanuma-e-Deccan_ widely reported the martyrdom and the Deshmukh''s tyranny.\n*   Doddi Komaraiah''s death ignited the movement into a full Armed Struggle that continued from 1946 to 1951.\n*   A statue of Doddi Komaraiah has been erected in Moscow by the Russians.\n*   Mashetty Ramachandraiah''s house served as the headquarters of the Kadavendi Andhra Mahasabha Gram Sangham, functioning as the village''s public platform at the time.\n\n**Other incidents of Phase 2**\n\n*   In October 1946, at Balemulla village, Patel Matta Reddy and Garlapati Ananth Reddy died during the revolt.\n\n**Akunur Incident**\n\n*   Akunur village, under Visnoor Deshmukh''s control in Jangaon Taluq, saw police forcibly extract grain from farmers in the name of levy.\n*   The villagers, led by village police patel Sithal Prasad, refused to pay and revolted; a similar incident occurred at Machireddy Palli.\n*   Humiliated officers attacked both villages with police support and molested women there.\n*   The incidents were reported in the national paper _Free Press Journal_ (editor: Sadananda), and Devulapalli Venkateshwar Rao wrote a book on them, _Akunur, Machireddy Palli Duranthalu_.\n*   Mahatma Gandhi, reacting strongly to the police atrocities, asked Padmaja Naidu to investigate and report; she visited both villages with the cooperation of party organizer Sadineni Dharmaiah, criticized the Nizam police''s repressive conduct, and sent her report to Gandhi.\n*   Gandhi then wrote to Hyderabad''s Prime Minister, Sir Akbar Hydari, who appointed a committee of enquiry.\n\n**Errabadu Incident**\n\n*   The Errabadu landlord of Suryapet Taluq, Janna Reddy Pratap Reddy, had annexed 1,000 acres from surrounding farmers.\n*   Farmers revolted against him under the leadership of Gajula Ramachandraiah of Erravelli village.\n\n**Cultural mobilization and state repression**\n\n*   Communists mobilized public support through Burra Katha, Oggu Katha, and the _Maa Bhoomi_ drama; the government subsequently banned the drama.\n*   In October 1946, the government set up military camps at Warangal and Nalgonda to suppress Communist activity, prompting the Communists to shift their base to Vijayawada — known during this period as \"Stalingrad.\"\n*   In November 1946, the Nizam government banned the Communist Party and appointed a commission, chaired by Mirza Ali Hyder, to suppress the Armed Struggle.\n\n#### Phase 3 (12 June 1947 – 17 September 1948)\n\n*   India became independent on 15 August 1947, but the Nizam declared that Hyderabad State would remain independent, joining neither India nor Pakistan. The peasants'' struggle against the feudal system thus moved into a new stage — resistance against the Nizam, aimed at merging Hyderabad into India.\n*   On 12 June 1947, Nizam Mir Osman Ali declared himself a sovereign independent ruler and suppressed those who opposed him.\n*   **Effect:** on 11 September 1947, the Communists announced the start of armed struggle against the Nizam. The declaration was made by Raavi Narayana Reddy, Baddam Yella Reddy, and Maqdoom Mohiuddin.\n\n**Objectives of the Armed Struggle**\n\n*   Seizing weapons from landlords, police, and Razakars.\n*   Occupying landlord and government wasteland for distribution among the poor.\n*   Obstructing government functioning by refusing to pay taxes.\n*   Boycotting government offices, courts, and educational institutions, while expanding Communist Party training.\n*   Hoisting the national flag and the Communist flag to declare Telangana liberated.\n*   Establishing guerrilla camps at the district, taluq, and village level to protect the people, with the ultimate aim of abolishing Nizam rule and the feudal system.\n\n**Three-tier organizational strategy** — the Communists organized villagers into three groups:\n\n1.  **Village Protection Group** — formed of enthusiastic young men who patrolled village outskirts from trees, signalled the village through distinctive sounds when Razakars or Nizam police approached, and confronted them using gathered Barmars (shells), spears, and common weapons.\n2.  **Abolition Group (Nirmulana Dalam)** — tasked with blocking enemy entry into villages, including by destroying bridges to stop Razakars and Nizam soldiers.\n3.  **Guerrilla Group** — equipped with modern weapons and guns, this group retaliated against landlords, Razakars, and police, attacking Deshmukh houses and police camps; in some villages it succeeded in dismantling government authority and establishing local self-rule.\n\n**Key engagements**\n\n*   The guerrillas'' first attack was on the police camp at Ravula Penta, aimed at seizing weapons; it failed, and leaders Venkat Reddy and Devabathini Venkateshwar Rao were killed.\n*   On 29 November 1947, around 6,000 people under Asireddy Narsimha Reddy attacked the large Razakar camp of Jagirdar Kolanpaka in Bhuvanagiri Taluq and hoisted the red flag there.\n*   Bheem Reddy Narsimha Reddy initiated the **\"Udara\"** programme to clear Razakar and soldier camps: fires were set to smoke out occupants, who were then disarmed as they emerged for air.\n*   People''s guerrillas bombed Kotapadu village, killing five Razakars and seven policemen, including landlord Vangeti Linga Reddy for aiding the Razakars and police.\n*   Anabheri Prabhakar Rao (Karimnagar) and Bhupathi Reddy (Siricilla) took control of villages and ran parallel governments during this phase.\n*   In January 1948, Anabheri Prabhakar Rao and Singireddy Bhupati Reddy, along with 14 others, were killed in counter-fire at Mandapuram village, Karimnagar district.\n\n**Guerrilla leaders martyred in Phase 3:**\n\n| Leader | Region |\n|---|---|\n| Anabheri Prabhakar Rao | Karimnagar |\n| Bhupathi Reddy | Siricilla |\n| Renikunta Rami Reddy | Bhuvanagiri |\n| Pasunuri Venkat Reddy | Nalgonda |\n| Erra Satyam | Huzur Nagar |\n| Gopal Reddy | Suryapet |\n\n*   On 29 November 1947, Nizam Osman Ali Khan signed a Standstill Agreement with the Indian government.\n*   On 17 September 1948, Hyderabad State was merged into the Indian Union through **Operation Polo**, closing the third phase.\n\n#### Phase 4 (17 September 1948 – 21 October 1951)\n\n*   Following a call from national Communist Party secretary Randive, the Armed Struggle continued from 17 September 1948, now directed against the Indian government.\n*   Indian Home Minister Vallabhbhai Patel visited Hyderabad on 6 October 1950 and, addressing a meeting at Fateh Maidan, declared that there would not be a single Communist left on Telangana soil.\n*   J. N. Chaudhuri, Military Governor of Hyderabad, was given full powers to suppress the Communists.\n*   The military government drew up the **\"Briggs Plan\"** under Nanjappa to suppress the movement; Nanjappa subsequently killed many Communists indiscriminately.\n\n**Conclusion of the Armed Struggle**\n\n*   On 21 October 1951, on the advice of Soviet Communist Party leader Stalin, the Communists called off the armed struggle.\n\n### Women in the Armed Struggle\n\n*   Women from the Koya and Chenchu tribal communities supported the guerrilla forces and Communist cadres throughout the struggle.\n*   The first woman martyr of the Armed Struggle was **Ramulamma**, an 11-year-old girl from Mangalpally village in Nalgonda Taluq, who died from torture at the hands of the Nizam''s police and Razakars.\n*   Other notable women participants: Mallu Swarajyam (Nalgonda), Arutla Kamala Devi (Bhuvanagiri), Lalitha (Suryapet), along with Raavi Sitha Devi, Brij Rani Gaur, Thai, and Sarpu Behan.\n\n**Mallu Swarajyam**\n\n*   Born in 1931 in Kothagudem village, Thungathurthy mandal, Suryapet district; her family, inspired by the freedom movement''s \"Swaraj\" slogan, named her Swarajyam.\n*   She was drawn into revolutionary politics after reading Maxim Gorky''s novel _Mother_.\n*   Responding to the Andhra Mahasabha''s call, she fought against Vetti Chakiri (forced labour).\n*   She went on to command a Dalam (armed squad) fighting the Zamindars, prompting the Nizam government to place a reward of Rs. 10,000 on her.\n*   Her husband, Mallu Venkat Narsimha Reddy, and her brother, Bheem Reddy Narsimha Reddy, were both influences on her.\n*   As part of the struggle, she took land from Zamindars for distribution among the poor and used folk songs to mobilize villagers.\n*   She was elected to the Andhra Pradesh Legislative Assembly from the Thungathurthy constituency in 1978 and 1983.\n\n### Literature of the Armed Struggle\n\n**Books on the Armed Struggle:**\n\n| Author | Work |\n|---|---|\n| Devulapalli Venkateshwara Rao | History of the People''s Armed Struggle of Telangana (Telangana Prajala Sayudha Porata Charita) |\n| Puchalapalli Sundarayya | Telangana People''s Struggle & Its Lessons; Veera Telangana Viplava Poratam, Guna Patalu |\n| Devulapally Ramanuja Rao | History of Telangana Armed Struggle |\n| Nalla Narsimhulu | Telangana Armed Struggle – My Experiences |\n| Madiraju Ramakoteshwar Rao | Telangana Abhyudhaya Amshalu (Progress Factors) |\n| Raavi Narayana Reddy | Veera Telangana, Naa Anubhavalu Gnapakalu (My Experiences – Memories) |\n| Arutla Ram Chandra Reddy | Telangana Porata Smruthulu |\n| Chandra Rajeshwar Rao | Veera Telangana Viplava Poratam |\n| Ayyapu Venkata Ramanaiah | Naji Naijam |\n| Maqdoom Mohiuddin | Haveli |\n| Kundurthi Anjaneyulu | Telangana, Nagaramlo Vana |\n| K. V. Ramana Reddy | Bhuvana Gosha, Adavi (Forest) |\n| Vattikota Alwar Swamy | Telangana |\n| Krishna Prasad | Simha Garjana (novel) |\n| Mahendar Ram Mohan Rao | Onamalu, Radha Chakralu, Mruthyuvu Needalo (novel) |\n\n**Notable contemporary comments/slogans:**\n\n*   Dasarathi Krishnamacharya — \"Maa Nizam Raju Janma Janmala Buju\"\n*   Rentala Gopala Krishna — \"Pagaleyi Nizam Kota, Egarey Erabavuta\" (Destroy the Nizam Fort, hoist the red flag)\n*   Raavi Narayana Reddy — \"Maa Nizam Rajyam Kalthi Leni Madya Yugapu Bhuswamya Vyavasta\"\n*   Chandra Rajeshwar Rao — the \"Andhra Thesis\" slogan\n*   Kaloji — \"Manakompalarchina, Manalni Champajusina, Manava Adamulanu Mandalidheeshulanu Kalambu Ragane Katesi Teerale\"\n\n### Results of the Telangana Armed Struggle\n\n*   Village protection forces helped politically enlighten the peasantry.\n*   The struggle freed people from the domination of Zamindars and Razakars.\n*   Landlordism (feudalism) was substantially altered.\n*   Vetti and other illegal taxes and bribery practices were abolished.\n*   Land reforms were introduced in 1949 as a direct result of Communist efforts.\n*   The Tenancy Act was passed in June 1950, granting ownership rights to tenants cultivating Deshmukhs'' lands.\n*   The struggle brought agrarian revolution to national attention and became a model for other peasant struggles across India, eventually pushing the Congress government to bring in land reforms.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter25-gandhijis-tours-in-telangana', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter25-gandhijis-tours-in-telangana-p1", "partName": "Gandhi''s 1928 Letter to Hyderabad", "bodyMd": "Before his personal visits, Gandhi''s connection with the Nizam''s dominions began through correspondence on the khadi movement.\n\n*   On **7 June 1928**, Gandhi wrote a letter from the Satyagraha Ashram to **Maharaja Kishan Prasad** (a nobleman of Hyderabad state).\n*   In the letter, Gandhi acknowledged that Kishan Prasad and his household were personally practising and promoting khadi, and that he had learned this through **Roopalal**.\n*   Gandhi noted that the **Mysore princely state** was at the forefront of the khadi movement among Indian states and held it up as an example.\n*   He appealed to Kishan Prasad to lead the khadi movement in the Nizam''s dominions as a mission, framing it as service to the poor of that state.\n*   He requested Kishan Prasad to send him a sample of khadi cloth woven by his eldest son, to be displayed at exhibitions.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter25-gandhijis-tours-in-telangana-p2", "partName": "Gandhi''s First Visit to Hyderabad (April 1929)", "bodyMd": "### Arrival and Reception\n\n*   Gandhi''s **first Hyderabad tour began in April 1929**.\n*   **Krishnaswami Mudiraj** took his Andhra volunteer corps to the station to receive Gandhi and brought him to the venue amid a warm welcome.\n*   The platform was decorated, and as the crowd swelled with people climbing onto the platform, the situation grew increasingly congested.\n*   Recognising the difficulty of moving Gandhi through the dense crowd, Mudiraj, using presence of mind, arranged for a train stop at a different point and, with the protection of his own volunteers, safely brought Gandhi out from the rear, away from the crowd.\n*   Gandhi was seated in a car that had been readied in advance and was taken in a procession through the city.\n*   **Andhra band troupes** performed music during the procession.\n\n### Public Meeting at Vivekavardhini Grounds\n\n*   Gandhi reached the Vivekavardhini grounds accompanied by local leaders **Vaman Naik**, **Madapati Hanumantha Rao**, **Mandumula Narasimha Rao**, **R.S. Naik**, **Raja Bansilal**, and **Mukunddas Malani**.\n*   A grand public meeting was held there under the chairmanship of **Vaman Naik**.\n*   **Speech themes:**\n    *   Gandhi described **khadi and the Kamadhenu (wish-fulfilling cow)** as things that fully provide for the nation.\n    *   He said Hyderabad had good scope for wider adoption of khadi, and that with further progress, help and cooperation could also be extended to people elsewhere in India.\n    *   He mentioned that **Sarojini Naidu** had told him about the good prospects for khadi weavers in the region.\n    *   He noted that untouchability persisted extensively within the Hyderabad state; he called it unwarranted and an urgent problem needing to be addressed, adding that this practice had no basis in scripture and urged Hindus to free themselves from it.\n\n### Donations and Other Engagements\n\n*   Following the speech, **Raja Dhanraj Girji** offered **Rs. 2,000** and **Mukund Rao** offered **Rs. 100** to Gandhi.\n*   Gandhi responded to a memento presented by **Vaman Naik** on behalf of patriotic citizens, thanking them for the contribution made for \"Daridranarayana\" (the poor as manifestations of God), and spoke about the condition of poverty in India, noting that many families survive on one meal in three days and describing cattle as their \"Kamadhenu.\"\n*   He mentioned that he himself had toured extensively — travelling 5–6 miles daily even in villages — in order to reach out to the poorest.\n*   He appealed to the wealthy to help the poor reach the standard of British India, adding that if unity and dedicated effort continued for just one more year, all of India would achieve the necessary prosperity.\n*   He acknowledged people''s fondness for fine and foreign cloth but appealed that, keeping fellow Indians in mind, they should wear coarse cloth and thereby help them; this, he said, would also let liquor consumption naturally decline, since British India''s education was funded partly from liquor revenue, which he called shameful.\n*   He condemned viewing \"Adi Hindus\" (Harijans) as untouchable, calling it a stain on Hinduism with no scriptural or religious basis, and appealed to Hindu brethren to give up this sin; he mentioned feeling satisfied on being told that the garland placed around his neck had been made by Harijans, adding he would never look down on such a garland.\n*   The speech collected donations of **Rs. 12,000**.\n*   Gandhi visited the **Balika Hindi Pathashala** (girls'' Hindi school) established by Krishnaswami Mudiraj at Chudi Bazaar and expressed satisfaction.\n*   On **7 April 1929**, a women''s meeting in Gandhi''s honour was organised at the **Prem Theatre**, Sultan Bazaar.\n\n### Departure\n\n*   Gandhi departed Hyderabad by train for Vijayawada at **9:15 pm**.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter25-gandhijis-tours-in-telangana-p3", "partName": "Gandhi''s Second Visit to Hyderabad (March 1934)", "bodyMd": "### Arrival\n\n*   Gandhi''s second tour began on **9 March 1934**, about six years after the first.\n*   **Sarojini Naidu** arrived in Hyderabad three days in advance to oversee arrangements for the visit.\n*   Two days before, on the 7th, Harijan Sevadal chief secretary **Rao Narayana Reddy**, **L.R. Dacha**, **Basanta Rao**, and **Kumari Padmajanayudu** went to receive Gandhi at the station, but were able to meet him only at **Vikarabad station**.\n*   To shield Gandhi from the crush of the crowd, he was brought down instead at **Lingampalli**, 15 miles from the city.\n*   Since word of this had reached in advance, **Sarojini Naidu**, **Kumari Leelamani Naidu**, Major **Muthyala Govindarajulu Naidu**, **Vamana Naik**, **Sirajul Hasan Tirmizi**, **Vinayaka Rao Vidyalankar**, **K. Tatachari**, and **Ramakrishna Dhat**, among others, went to receive him there.\n*   Sarojini Naidu gave the first welcome, garlanding Gandhi with khaddar flowers, and took him to her residence, **\"Golden Threshold\"** in Abids.\n*   Family members, including **Mira Behn**, accompanied Gandhi.\n\n### Donations at Lingampalli and the Public Programme\n\n*   At Lingampalli station, a Muslim woman named **Musalamma** rushed to Gandhi''s car carrying two bangles as a gift; Gandhi accepted the gift with a smile.\n*   Another woman addressed Gandhi as **\"Lokarakshaka\"** (protector of the world) and offered a rupee, which Gandhi gratefully accepted, thanking her.\n*   Police Commissioner **Kotwal Venkatarama Reddy** arranged tight security at Golden Threshold, where Gandhi was staying.\n*   Gandhi later drove out along Lal Darwaza, where crowds on both sides greeted him; streets were decorated with festoons.\n*   He visited the **Adi Hindu Vidyalayam**.\n*   Further visits planned for that programme — to **Shanti Bhavan**, the **Marwadi Vidyalayam**, and the **Kachiguda Harijan hostel** — had to be cancelled because Gandhi was unwell/fatigued, disappointing the crowds who had gathered enthusiastically; the government subsequently did not permit the remaining scheduled programmes.\n*   **Mejaru Muthyala Govindarajula Naidu** led a large deputation to greet Gandhi; local Harijan Sevaka Sangham president **Vaman Naik** presented a welcome address.\n\n**Donations collected on this occasion:**\n\n| Donor | Amount (Rs.) | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Harijan Seva Sangham | 1,630 | Harijan fund |\n| Lal Darwaza citizens | 75 | Harijan fund |\n| Marwadi Vidyalayam | 100 | Harijan fund |\n| Arya Samaj | 150 | Harijan fund |\n\n### Speech on Humanity and Equality\n\n*   Gandhi''s address at this stage covered several points:\n    *   All human beings are one; there should be no distinctions of gradation among them.\n    *   There should be no difference between Harijans and others.\n    *   There should be no divisions between Hindus and Muslims.\n    *   God may be called \"Allah\" or \"Ishwara\" — whatever name is used, all serve the same God.\n    *   Hindus must eradicate the stain of untouchability, and he appealed to fellow Hindus to help remove it.\n    *   He expressed happiness on learning that the garland of \"chaste thread\" placed around his neck had been made by Harijans, and said he had never looked down on such thread.\n    *   He appealed to everyone to come forward for the Harijan upliftment programme.\n*   His speech that day raised **Rs. 12,000**.\n*   Gandhi also visited and expressed satisfaction with the **Balika Hindi Pathashala** established by Krishnaswami Mudiraj at Chudi Bazaar during this tour.\n\n### Secunderabad Meeting (9 March 1934)\n\n*   On the evening of **9 March 1934**, at **5 pm**, a large public meeting was held at the **Karbala grounds, Secunderabad**.\n*   Citizens'' representative **Bajeekrishna Rao** delivered the welcome address and donated money to the Harijan fund.\n*   Girl students of the **Begumpet Kanyagurukula Balika Pathasala** sang \"Vande Mataram\" and then recited a **\"panasa\" verse from the Vedas**.\n*   Gandhi, surprised at this, remarked that children reciting the Vedas was, in itself, the removal of untouchability as far as he was concerned.\n*   Gandhi told the gathering: he had come to Hyderabad for the eradication of untouchability, and asked for the people''s cooperation in this work, which he said he was undertaking for his own spiritual satisfaction (atmasuddhi).\n*   He said his mission had no ill will towards any particular religious community, and mentioned **Dr. Latif Sayeed**, a doctor of an upper-class religious background, who was devoted to treating Harijan patients — Gandhi called service to Harijans \"the divine duty of Hindus.\"\n*   **R.S. Naik**, known for successfully conducting auctions, was invited to auction articles at the meeting.\n*   Gandhi took about 15 minutes to reach the platform from where the car was parked because of the crowd.\n*   At **Secunderabad station**, in a short span, **Rs. 225** was collected.\n*   At **Kazipet station**, **Ravi Narayana Reddy** presented Gandhi with **Rs. 1,900**.\n*   **Total collections from Hyderabad and Secunderabad cities during this tour reached about Rs. 10,000.**\n\n### The Mango Tree Memorial\n\n*   When Gandhi had earlier undergone imprisonment at the **Yerawada jail**, he had once rested beneath a mango tree there.\n*   A sapling grew from a seed of that tree, and he carried this sapling with him.\n*   As a memento of that occasion, this sapling was planted at **Golden Threshold**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter25-gandhijis-tours-in-telangana-p4", "partName": "Gandhi''s Third Visit — Khammam, Dornakal, Manukota and Warangal (1946)", "bodyMd": "### Background: The Andhra Mahasabha''s Invitation\n\n*   In late **January 1946**, Gandhi went to Madras to attend the celebrations of the **Dakshina Bharat Hindi Prachar Sabha**.\n*   On his return journey via the **Khazipet route**, the **Andhra Mahasabha** decided that if Gandhi visited the key centres of Telangana — **Khammam** and **Warangal** — it would give the freedom struggle in Telangana fresh impetus.\n*   Gandhi initially declined this request.\n*   Mahasabha leaders then sent **M.S. Rajalingam**, who was already acquainted with Gandhi, to Madras to press the request in person.\n*   Rajalingam met Gandhi in Madras and also consulted with **Gollapudi Seetaramasastri**.\n*   Rajalingam explained to Gandhi that Telangana was, on one side, facing Muslim League propaganda, and on the other side, activity by Communists, creating adverse conditions in the region, and that a visit by Gandhi would give the people moral strength.\n*   Hearing this, Gandhi agreed and assured that he would visit wherever necessary in the region.\n*   A **special train** was arranged for Gandhi''s tour.\n\n### Mobilisation Before the Visit\n\n*   As soon as Gandhi''s acceptance was known, Rajalingam informed the Mahasabha leaders of the news.\n*   Since only two to three days remained, workers at **Madhira**, **Manukota**, and **Dornakal** were informed at once.\n*   Leaflets were sent out to villages without delay.\n*   **Swami Ramananda Tirtha** was not in town at the time; a worker was specially sent to bring **Mandumula Narasimha Rao**.\n*   Rajalingam accompanied Gandhi on the train.\n\n### Khammam\n\n*   In Khammam, Andhra Mahasabha town committee president **Gellu Keshava Rao** and Khammam Merchants'' Association president **Suggula Akshaya Linga Gupta** collected a fund of thousands of rupees.\n*   The special train was stopped in a large open ground near the **Venkatagiri level crossing gate** in Khammam.\n*   Crowds thronged in wave after wave to see Gandhi.\n*   **Sardar Jamalapuram Keshava Rao** managed and controlled the crowds.\n*   A platform **15 feet high** was built about a quarter-mile from the railway station.\n*   The gathering of people began from **8 pm the previous night**.\n*   Volunteers collected **Rs. 1,500** overnight from the crowd waiting there.\n*   The special train arrived at **8:30 am**.\n*   Gandhi, along with **Krishna Das**, **Unnava Rajagopala Krishnayya**, and **M.S. Rajalingam**, who had come along, sat on the platform.\n*   A welcome address was presented to Gandhi along with **1,000 gold sovereign coins** wrapped in a silk cloth.\n*   Gandhi humorously remarked, **\"So this silk cloth also isn''t mine?\"**, drawing laughter, and addressed the silent crowd.\n*   **Gandhi''s appeal to the gathering:**\n    *   All the people should stay united, setting aside caste and religious differences.\n    *   Everyone should wear khadi cloth.\n    *   Everyone should give up liquor.\n    *   The people should earn the worthiness needed for the self-rule that would soon come.\n\n### Dornakal\n\n*   Despite being a small town, about **700 people** of Dornakal came to see Gandhi and offered a donation of **Rs. 4,000**.\n*   At **Gundrati Madugu**, **400 Harijans** devotedly met Gandhi and offered **Rs. 2,000** to the fund.\n*   At the Dornakal station halt, Gandhi wished to bathe; the stationmaster initially refused permission, fearing for his job, but eventually agreed and, with help from those present, arranged buckets of water so that Gandhi could bathe in the station bathroom.\n\n### Manukota\n\n*   About **800 people** gathered at Manukota, and they contributed **Rs. 700** to the Harijan fund.\n\n### Warangal\n\n*   Propaganda that Gandhi would come to Warangal began circulating from **5 February 1946**.\n*   Two to three days beforehand, leaders had campaigned vigorously in the surrounding villages.\n*   Volunteers of the Warangal and Hanamkonda student associations, about **800** in number, gathered at Warangal station shouting slogans of **\"Swatantra Bharatiki Jai.\"**\n*   Near **Goodur station**, a decision was made on the exact spot for Gandhi''s darshan and message.\n*   Though the scheduled time was **9:20 am**, people began gathering from as early as **5 am**.\n*   The special train fell behind schedule; it was reaching stations along the way **12–20 hours late** at intermediate points.\n*   People who came to see Gandhi at these stops collected donations amounting to tens of thousands of rupees; some women contributed gold ornaments generously.\n*   The reception committee formed to make Gandhi''s tour a success included **Hayagrivachari**, **M.S. Rajalingam**, **Bhandaru Chandramouleshwara Rao**, **S. Sreeramulu Naidu**, **S. Chandramouli**, and **B. Ranganayakulu**.\n*   Committee president **T. Hayagrivachari** donated **Rs. 15,000** to the Harijan fund.\n*   **Chandra Kanthaiah** alone gave **Rs. 5,000**.\n*   A Harijan girl applied a tilak to Gandhi''s forehead with a smile, which he accepted graciously.\n*   At **12:30 pm**, the special train halted on the Warangal platform; Gandhi came down, stood briefly, and looked at the vast, devoted crowd that had waited since morning enduring sun and hunger — a scene described as truly moving, with tears of joy in the eyes of onlookers.\n*   It was said that Warangal''s soil became sanctified by Gandhi''s footsteps, and that his ten minutes there were equal to ten years.\n*   **Hayagrivachari**, **Rajalingam**, and some other leaders accompanied Gandhi on the special train up to **Khazipet**.\n*   As a result of the leaders'' efforts, Gandhi''s tour brought about a great awakening (chaitanyam) among the people of Telangana.\n\n### Farewell Message from Kazipet\n\n*   Before the special train crossed the borders of Telangana, Gandhi issued a message to the people of Andhra, from **Kazipet**, dated **5 February 1946**.\n*   **Key points of the message:**\n    *   He had great hope for the Andhra region and trusted it would fully realise his aims.\n    *   Untouchability should be completely removed.\n    *   All temples should be opened for Harijans.\n    *   Everyone should wear khadi clothing.\n    *   Liquor should be given up.\n    *   National unity should be protected.\n    *   Along with the mother tongue, the national language, **Hindustani**, should be learned in both the **Devanagari** and **Urdu** scripts.\n*   The message was signed: **M.K. Gandhi, Kazipet, 5-2-1946.**", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p1", "partName": "Formation and Early Goals of State Congress", "bodyMd": "The **State Congress** was established in **1938**, with the objective of establishing responsible government (praja prabhutvam) within the Nizam''s dominion.\n\n**Winning trust — an early struggle:**\n\n*   Its leaders worked hard to convince the Nizam''s government that the State Congress was not a communal organisation.\n*   They also worked to convince fellow Muslim leaders that it had no links to the party politics of British India''s Congress.\n*   This effort bore little fruit before their labour even began to show results.\n\n**Siraj-ul-Hassan Tirmizi:**\n\n*   Played a prominent role in the Hyderabad freedom struggle.\n*   Was known among Congress circles as a Muslim leader who did not subscribe to religious separatism.\n*   Worked with unwavering courage against injustices done to Hyderabad State under the pretext of protecting minority interests.\n*   Fought resolutely against communal politics, suffering hardships and insults from fellow Muslims.\n*   Remained an uncompromising nationalist to the end.\n\n**Ittehad-ul-Muslimeen**, convinced that any form of responsible government in Hyderabad would lead to Hindu domination, opposed such a movement outright.\n\n*   **1939 onward:** circumstances gradually began to change.\n*   **1942:** Mahatma Gandhi launched the **Quit India** movement, which turned into a single unified movement across the whole of India.\n*   **3 June 1946:** taking note of the changing situation, the Nizam''s government banned the State Congress.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p2", "partName": "Chhatari''s Second Premiership and Communal Tension (1941–47)", "bodyMd": "*   **August 1941** — **Nawab Chhatari** became Prime Minister of the state, in place of Sir Akbar Hydari.\n*   **1942** — a grave incident occurred at **Dichpalli** village, Nizamabad district:\n    *   Christian missionaries were running a leprosy hospital there.\n    *   Some Muslim residents built a shed (\"chabutra\") within the hospital premises, gave it the shape of a mosque, and began holding congregational prayers there.\n    *   This was against hospital regulations, so health authorities demolished the structure.\n    *   To Ittehad-ul-Muslimeen, this small administrative act was seen as an attack on their faith.\n\n**The Bolarum riot:**\n\n*   Muslims began disorderly agitations.\n    \n*   Grigson, the Revenue and Police Minister, and the (European) Resident, were naturally sympathetic to the missionaries.\n    \n*   Neither Nawab Chhatari (a Muslim himself) nor his mostly-Muslim ministers could do anything about the matter.\n    \n*   Angered, Muslims attacked **Bolarum** — where the Resident''s residence (Grigson''s residence, now Greenlands) was located — carrying torches.\n    \n*   Chhatari''s cattle-shed was burned down.\n    \n*   Grigson himself was not found in Greenlands; his library and house were burned.\n    \n*   **Cause:** Even a single act of government administration (however necessary) sparked the belief that faith itself was under attack → **Effect:** Anarchic mob violence, attacks on official residences.\n    \n*   The government fired only a single shot that day, and the Razakar movement did not yet raise its head.\n    \n*   The Police Commissioner did not cooperate, so Grigson remained a silent spectator while the disturbances took their course.\n    \n*   This unchecked lawlessness eventually led to the removal of Nawab Chhatari.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p3", "partName": "Sir Mirza Ismail''s Premiership", "bodyMd": "Following this, **Sir Mirza Ismail** became Prime Minister.\n\n**Profile:**\n\n*   A nationalist, skilled in business/administration, with a modern outlook.\n*   Had earlier brought about progressive changes as Prime Minister of Mysore and Jaipur.\n*   His attempts to introduce modern reforms into the sluggish Hyderabad state did not succeed.\n*   Hyderabad''s Muslims nicknamed him **\"Thod-Phod Khan\"** (\"the break-and-demolish Khan\").\n*   His policies pleased neither Ittehad-ul-Muslimeen nor, ultimately, the Nizam''s own government.\n*   Unable to bear the resentment directed at him, he resigned as Prime Minister within a few months, in **May 1947**.\n*   After resigning, he advised the Nizam that Hyderabad should join the Indian Union — the Nizam rejected this advice.\n\n**Leadership change in Ittehad-ul-Muslimeen:**\n\n*   After Bahadur Yar Jung''s death, **Kasim Razvi** took over the leadership of Ittehad-ul-Muslimeen.\n*   Razvi practised law in Latur.\n*   A \"Razakar\" means, literally, a volunteer.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p4", "partName": "The First State Congress Conference and the Independence of India", "bodyMd": "*   By this time, the ban on the Congress had been lifted.\n    \n*   **8 May 1947** — under the presidency of **Swami Ramananda Tirtha**, the first general body meeting (Maha Sabha) of the State Congress was held at **Chikkadpally**, Hyderabad — among the largest public meetings in Hyderabad''s history.\n    \n*   Swami Ramananda Tirtha''s presidential address stirred the public, and the blood of the youth boiled with fervour.\n    \n*   That same day, Ittehad-ul-Muslimeen''s leaders raised the cry of an independent Hyderabad.\n    \n*   It was under Kasim Razvi''s leadership that the Razakar movement began.\n    \n*   **15 August 1947** — India attained independence, its free sun shining from the Himalayas to Cape Comorin.\n    \n*   But in the princely state of Hyderabad, the Nizam blocked out this independence, creating a dense darkness instead.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p5", "partName": "Mogilayya''s Sacrifice", "bodyMd": "**Background:**\n\n*   A day before India''s independence, the Nizam had already proclaimed himself independent.\n*   Because of this, the Nizam''s regime treated India''s national flag as a \"foreign\" flag.\n*   The **Andhra Mahasabha**, jointly with left-wing groups, resolved at Warangal fort to hoist the national flag; the **Andhra Jatiyapaksham** was formed for this purpose and carried out the flag-hoisting.\n\n**The incident:**\n\n*   One of the volunteers, **Mogilayya**, at 8 a.m., shouted slogans of \"Mahatma Gandhi ki Jai,\" \"Congress ki Jai,\" \"Trivarna Patakam ki Jai,\" and \"Bharat Mata ki Jai\" with great heroism.\n*   Everyone present joined the slogan-shouting, and the whole fort echoed with the sound.\n*   Two to three hundred armed goons then arrived at the spot.\n*   On seeing Mogilayya raising the slogans, the goons attacked him savagely with sticks.\n*   One of the assailants was cut and began bleeding.\n*   The mob of goons then rampaged through the town.\n*   The District Collector, who had come to Aminpet town, ordered the killer to be garlanded.\n*   The killer, with blood still on his hands, cheekily removed the garland and threw it upon his companions as they congratulated him, in open mockery.\n*   **The killer''s name was Sharif**, known by the epithet \"Lakshmi Pahalwan.\"\n\n**Aftermath:**\n\n*   No newspaper covered this incident. The murder of Mogilayya was no ordinary event — in normal times it would have set the whole country ablaze.\n*   This killing took place on **9 August 1946**, before Sir Mirza Ismail took charge as Prime Minister.\n*   It took Kaloji Narayana Rao a week''s time to fully take in the circumstances; the government did not permit him to bring the body along with him.\n*   Kaloji wrote fiery lines in protest after this incident. _\\[Telugu verse in the original source is omitted here.\\]_\n*   Police Minister Grigson, who arrived on a tour around this time, was seen leisurely photographing forts, gopurams, and temples rather than addressing the matter.\n*   In the police machinery, an official responsible for archaeology — not one accountable for law and order — was made to handle the case instead.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p6", "partName": "British Policy and the Partition of the Country", "bodyMd": "**British strategy before departure:**\n\n*   The British imperialists, experienced in devious politics, created serious problems for the national government before leaving India.\n    \n*   In line with their imperial interests, they split the country into two — India and Pakistan.\n    \n*   Behind the scenes, they created political conspiracies so that the new national strength would not consolidate.\n    \n*   They had sown similar seeds of discord in Korea, Vietnam, Laos, and Germany.\n    \n*   **Cause:** The British wished not merely to divide the country, but to create domestic strife that would linger on even after their departure → **Effect:** A formal declaration on this line was issued on **3 March 1947**.\n    \n*   The declaration''s gist: princely rulers within India could, of their own accord, merge with Pakistan, merge with India, or declare independence.\n    \n*   With Sardar Patel''s courage and political wisdom, nearly all the princely states were merged into India without bloodshed.\n    \n*   In doing so, Patel added an eternal and enduring page to the history of free India.\n    \n\n**Communal calculations around Hyderabad:**\n\n*   A false narrative was spread that Hindus and Muslims were mutually hostile groups, keeping the Muslims, Majlis, and Kashmir issue linked to the question of Hyderabad''s merger.\n*   Hindus would suffer if Hyderabad were merged into Pakistan (on the pretext of being loyal partisans on both sides).\n*   Muslims did not wish for merger into India either.\n*   The Nizam therefore proclaimed that it would be best for his kingdom to remain independent.\n*   He announced this so-called independence through a special firman, concealing his territorial ambition and religious bigotry.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p7", "partName": "The Standstill Agreement", "bodyMd": "*   There was no public support for the Nizam''s declaration of independence.\n*   As soon as this became known, the Nizam began negotiations with the Government of India.\n*   He engaged the eminent British jurist **Sir Walter Monckton**, appointing him constitutional advisor at a fee of one lakh rupees per day.\n\n**Delegation to Delhi:**\n\n*   Sent to Delhi: Prime Minister Nawab Chhatari, constitutional advisor Sir Walter Monckton, and law secretary Aliyawar Jung.\n*   The Government of India stated firmly there would be no change to the conditions of **15 August 1947** — foreign affairs, defence, and communications would remain with the Government of India (the same arrangement as under the British era).\n*   This first, temporary arrangement was called the **Standstill Agreement**.\n\n**Three further concessions sought by Hyderabad:**\n\n1.  If war broke out between India and Pakistan, the Hyderabad government would remain neutral.\n2.  Hyderabad should have the power to appoint Agents-General in foreign countries.\n3.  Hyderabad should be free to act independently of anything the Government of India did regarding the Commonwealth.\n\n*   Governor-General Lord Mountbatten made clear that even a change of \"full stop, comma\" in the original agreement could not be permitted.\n*   The Hyderabad government appointed the celebrated engineer **Nawab Zain Yar Jung**, an extremely nationalistic and modest man, as its Agent-General in Delhi.\n*   There was, in fact, no need for such uproar over the Hyderabad matter: historically and geographically the independence claim was untenable, and it was only the Centre''s preoccupation with partition-related problems that gave Hyderabad the opening it exploited.\n\n**Signing and its aftermath:**\n\n*   The delegation the Nizam sent to Delhi accepted the Standstill Agreement, then returned to Hyderabad on **27 August** to seek the Nizam''s assent.\n*   Ittehad-ul-Muslimeen leader Qasim Razvi also went to see the Nizam, arguing he should not sign — that a firmer stance would make the Government of India accept Hyderabad''s terms — and tried to blame the Nizam for weakness.\n*   The Nizam, a seasoned, administratively astute politician, understood the risks of delay and had no wish to see matters spiral out of control.\n*   The night the signed letter was ready to be sent to Delhi, **50,000 unofficial armed Razakars** (funded by the state) surrounded Prime Minister Chhatari''s residence in a menacing demonstration.\n*   Humiliated, Chhatari did nothing about it, and **resigned on 1 November 1947**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p8", "partName": "Change of Prime Ministers", "bodyMd": "*   The Nizam appointed **Mehdi Yar Jung** as temporary Prime Minister in Chhatari''s place.\n*   Mehdi Yar Jung actually submitted the agreement to the Government of India, against the Nizam''s wishes.\n*   Angered that Mehdi Yar Jung had done the very thing he did not want done, the Nizam insisted on removing him.\n*   Mehdi Yar Jung resigned, and the Nizam appointed **Laik Ali** as Prime Minister on **29 November 1947**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p9", "partName": "Laik Ali and \"Nalayak\" (Incompetent) Deeds", "bodyMd": "\"Laik\" means one who is wise/competent — but this \"Laik\" man''s deeds turned out one after another to be entirely \"Nalayak\" (incompetent) ones.\n\n**Early missteps and conditions under his rule:**\n\n*   As soon as it took power, the Laik Ali government paid Pakistan a loan of **20 crore rupees** — a further source of unease for the Central Government, given ongoing negotiations with Pakistan over partition-related asset values worth crores of rupees.\n*   Many Muslims who came from the north during partition found rehabilitation in the Nizam''s state.\n*   Razakars ravaged homes street by street; the peace and security problem worsened further.\n*   A British sailor, notorious as \"the smuggler of the sea,\" secretly brought weapons into the state.\n*   The Government of India appointed **K.M. Munshi**, a great political thinker and writer, as its Agent-General in Hyderabad; he resided at Dakshin Sadan, Bolarum (now Raj Bhavan), and wrote the historic novel _Jai Somnath_ during his stay.\n*   In those days it was not the Nizam, nor Laik Ali, but the **Qasim Razvi regime** that actually governed the state.\n*   Razakar leaders boasted that \"the waves of the Bay of Bengal and the Arabian Sea would wash our Nizam''s feet,\" and that \"the flag of an unrivalled kingdom would fly over the Red Fort in Delhi.\"\n\n### Expenditure Incurred by the Laik Ali Government\n\n| Item | Expenditure |\n|---|---|\n| War preparations against the Indian Union | About 8 crore 60 lakh rupees |\n| Propaganda for an \"independent Hyderabad\" in India and abroad | 50 lakh rupees |\n| Advocacy for Hyderabad''s cause before the Security Council | 50 lakh rupees |\n| Deficit in state budget for 1947–48 (due to excessive military expenditure) | 4 crore rupees |\n\n*   The Nizam sought British government support at the UN Security Council over the Hyderabad issue, but British Prime Minister Attlee said Britain could not separate Hyderabad from India and could not support Hyderabad on this matter.\n\n**The assembly and the \"Abhyudaya Prajapaksham\":**\n\n*   The State Congress and other national parties boycotted the assembly.\n*   Wealthy men and jagirdars supporting the government, along with those seeking the perks of membership, got themselves elected unopposed.\n*   This group called itself the **\"Abhyudaya Prajapaksham\"** (\"progressive people''s party\") when the ministry was formed.\n*   **Pingali Venkatramireddy**, from this group, obtained a seat in Laik Ali''s cabinet.\n\n**Elder statesmen sidelined:**\n\n*   Most elder statesmen of the state withdrew, sidelined by the Razakars'' atrocities.\n    \n*   Ali Yavar Jung, Zain Yar Jung, Mehdi Nawaz Jung, and Hosh Yar Jung took their pensions and shut themselves indoors.\n    \n*   Sarwar Jung, who did not heed Razvi''s word, was frightened by Razvi into fleeing the state.\n    \n*   Some fanatics conceived the idea of turning Hyderabad into a \"South Pakistan.\"\n    \n*   A local university professor, Dr. Latif, proposed relocating Hyderabad''s Hindus to Madras and Madhya Pradesh, and bringing Muslims from those regions into Hyderabad, as an \"easy solution.\"\n    \n*   This impossible, fantastical proposal was not accepted.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p10", "partName": "Kasim Razvi''s Communal Declarations", "bodyMd": "*   The entire government machinery fell into Kasim Razvi''s hands; he treated the \"Laik Ali\" government as his puppet, while being openly hostile to it.\n*   Thousands gathered to hear his communal speeches at Razakar assemblies. \n\nExamples of his declarations:  \n\n1.  \"A Hindu is a _kafir_ (infidel) — he worships stones and monkeys (laughter in the audience). He eats beef under the name of religion. He drinks cow urine. Can the state remain in the hands of such uncivilised people?\" (met with wild laughter and clapping).\n2.  \"Hyderabad will inevitably annex the surrounding districts. This Nizam''s rule is not confined to Hyderabad alone — Bidar too is under the Nizam.\"\n3.  \"I shall hoist the flag of an unrivalled kingdom over the Red Fort.\"\n4.  \"If ever any danger arises to Muslims'' existence, they will not spare people of other faiths.\"\n\n*   Such irresponsible declarations tested the patience of the Government of India.\n*   **4 May 1948** — the state government lifted the ban on the Communist Party.\n\n**Campbell Johnson''s mediation:**\n\n*   Lord Mountbatten, while still holding office, wished to resolve the Hyderabad problem and sent his press secretary **Campbell Johnson** to Hyderabad as special envoy.\n*   Johnson held extensive talks with the Nizam, Razvi, and Laik Ali.\n*   He argued that constitutional, Western practices had no relevance to Hyderabad.\n*   Razvi''s hatred toward Hindus extended even to Johnson.\n*   Gandhiji''s remark that \"Hindus, after killing their leaders, will later turn around and deify them\" (made regarding Razvi''s murderous plans) drew a disproportionate hatred from Razvi toward Gandhi as well.\n*   With this communal fanaticism, no fruitful negotiations took place — Razvi sought gain without loss, and the actual agreement remained an honoured-in-name-only document, violated repeatedly by the Hyderabad government.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p11", "partName": "Currency Measures and Repression", "bodyMd": "*   **24 December 1947** — the Hyderabad government withdrew Indian currency from circulation in state trade.\n*   **27 December** — a currency ordinance repealed rule 17 of Act 1321 Fasli, which had required state trade transactions to occur only in state currency.\n*   Two more ordinances banned the export of gold — all intended to undermine the actual (Standstill) agreement.\n*   **18 September 1947** — entry into the state was restricted to Muslim refugees only.\n\n**Impact on Hindus:**\n\n*   Refugees who arrived seeking revenge, aided by Razakars, looted homes street by street; no village escaped their plunder.\n*   For protection of life and property, the state''s Hindus fled to neighbouring states.\n*   Sardar Patel told Parliament that 8,000 Hyderabad Hindus arrived in Madras state alone.\n*   The government warned that lands and properties left behind by those who fled Hyderabad must be returned to their owners by **15 May 1948**.\n*   **January 1948** — the Nizam''s government took over the Bhadrachalam Ramalayam village-grant land.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p12", "partName": "Attacks on Border Villages", "bodyMd": "*   The frustrated state government began raiding Union-government villages along the border with the help of Razakars.\n*   Near Usmanabad district, Razakars raided a village called **Nanaj**; Union-government village guards killed eighteen Razakars in that incident.\n*   The humiliation of the Nanaj incident inflamed the fire of revenge in the state.\n*   **9 August 1948** — Razakars held a \"Nanaj mourning meeting\" at Jamrud Mahal Theatre, Hyderabad, and took an oath to avenge those killed.\n*   Twenty Razakars from Hyderabad State entered Madras district and looted 50,000 rupees'' worth of property — disclosed by Madras state''s Home Minister, Dr. Subbarayan, in the legislative assembly on **27 February 1948**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p13", "partName": "The Murder of Shoaib-Ullah Khan", "bodyMd": "**Background — the \"Rayyat\" and \"Imroze\" newspapers:**\n\n*   The \"**Rayyat**\" newspaper, which openly expressed independent views, was banned by the Nizam''s government.\n*   The same day, intelligence official Fazal Rasool Khan delivered the ban order to its office.\n*   Its editor, Mandumula Narasingarao, who had won public affection through his independent views, had to bow to the autocratic ruler''s orders. Shoaib worked as sub-editor there.\n*   With Narasingarao''s assistance, Shoaib set up the \"**Imroze**\" newspaper; its first issue was published on **15 November 1947**.\n*   Financial support came from Burgula Ramakrishna Rao; the Imroze office was later shifted into his own house.\n*   Circulation, and readership, grew gradually.\n\n**Shoaib''s stance against the Razakars:**\n\n*   Hyderabad state''s population then was **1 crore 56 lakh**, of which **20 lakh 70 thousand 312** were Muslims — only 5 lakh of whom supported the Razakars.\n*   Shoaib wrote that the Razakars'' \"unrivalled kingdom\" slogan was baseless propaganda supported only by that minority.\n*   In those days, nearly all Urdu newspapers backed the Razakars; Shoaib, young and alone, had the courage to expose their inhuman conduct.\n*   The Razakars came to see him as a thorn in their side.\n*   Razvi indirectly warned his followers that forces \"trying to shatter... and destroy our unity must be uprooted, root and branch,\" and spoke openly against Shoaib with a vengeful heart.\n\n**21 August 1948 — atrocities intensify:**\n\n*   With the ambition of establishing an Islamic state astride free India, the Nizam, egged on by the Razakars, subjected citizens to atrocities.\n*   The lives, property, and dignity of Hindus in particular depended on the Razakars'' whims.\n*   All Hindus in the city locked their doors by evening; after seven o''clock the streets became entirely deserted.\n*   Many Hindu families fled the state, abandoning everything.\n\n**The murder itself:**\n\n*   Chappal Bazaar (on the route to Kachiguda railway station) also went dark that night; only the part of Burgula Ramakrishna Rao''s house holding the Imroze office had lights burning.\n*   Shoaib, heading to his rented lodging at Mahmood Ismail Khan''s house in Koti, reached the Lingampalli crossroads near his own house at midnight.\n*   A group of men ran up from behind; one fired a pistol, and three bullets struck Shoaib, who fell.\n*   The assailants then slashed both of Shoaib''s arms with swords, and slashed the fallen Ismail as well, before fleeing in the darkness.\n\n**Aftermath:**\n\n*   Shoaib''s murder shocked national newspapers across the country.\n*   Nehru, who had until then treated Hyderabad''s affairs as merely a Hindu–Muslim problem, gradually began to grasp the reality — this led the way toward Police Action.\n\n**Press censorship:**\n\n*   **8 February 1948** — the Nizam government ordered the Deccan Chronicle, Imroze, Payam, and Daily News to submit editorials, criticism, and letters to the editor to the censor before publication.\n*   The Golconda newspaper was barred from writing editorials for nearly two months.\n*   **28 March 1948** — State Congress women volunteers observed a 24-hour fast in protest; this was reported in Madras''s \"Hindu\" newspaper but remained unknown within Hyderabad.\n\n**The Muslim leaders'' memorial:**\n\n*   Seven Muslim leading citizens submitted a memorial to the Nizam urging steps to restore normalcy and peace, following the state''s suffering over Hyderabad''s proposed merger into the Indian Union.\n*   Signatories: Nawab Manjoor Jung (former Subedar, close to the Nizam), Mahmood Hussain Zafari (Director of Education), Farid Mirza (a Tehsildar who resigned his post to sign), Bakr Ali Mirza (educated abroad), Mullah Abdul Basit, Ahmed Mirza, Hussain Abdul Munir, and Mir Ahmed Alikhan.\n*   Their memorial created a sensation in Delhi; Sardar Patel forwarded a copy to the U.N.O.\n*   The signatories lived afterward in fear of losing their lives for this act.\n*   **30 January 1948** — Mahatma Gandhi''s assassination plunged the whole country into darkness.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter26-state-congress-movements-p14", "partName": "Aftermath: Judicial Proceedings", "bodyMd": "*   Judges ruled that, given the extraordinary conditions prevailing in Hyderabad State, cases could not be heard in court there — an unprecedented occurrence.\n*   Even during India''s non-cooperation movement era, a boycott of the courts had earlier been carried out here in full measure.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter27-bhoodan-movement', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter27-bhoodan-movement-p1", "partName": "Bhoodan Movement — Background and Origins of the Padayatra", "bodyMd": "India''s land problem could be addressed through three approaches: **violence**, **legislation**, and **donation (dāna)**. Communists in India, and particularly in Telangana, pursued the path of violence for some time but failed to achieve a lasting solution through it. The government, for its part, followed the legislative route — enacting laws for land ceiling and redistribution — but implementation of such laws in full has always been a slow process, and legislation alone could not fully resolve the problem.\n\nWhat was needed alongside legal reform was for landowning communities to voluntarily understand their social responsibility and donate land out of genuine goodwill. It was **Vinobha Bhave** who conceived and led this third path — the Bhoodan (land-gift) movement.\n\nThe idea that the land problem could be solved through Bhoodan came to Vinobha suddenly. In **March 1951**, he was invited to attend the Sarvodaya Sammelan being held at **Shivarampalli**, near Hyderabad. He walked **300 miles** from his ashram to reach Hyderabad for this gathering.\n\nDuring this visit, he learned that communists in the Telangana region were resorting to violence and that the rural population lived in constant fear because of it. He resolved to spread a message of love and peace across the region. It was in the context of this visit that the historic Bhoodan movement began in Telangana.\n\n### Launch of the Tour\n\n*   **April 16, 1951 (Sriramanavami)** — Vinobha Bhave announced that he intended to tour the Telangana region as part of his message.\n*   The journey began at **5 a.m.**, with Vinobha''s party covering **10 miles** on foot from Hyderabad city to reach **Hayathnagar**.\n*   Prominent figures who accompanied him included **Dr. G. S. Melkote** and **Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy**, among others.\n*   **April 18, 1951** — As planned, Vinobha Bhave''s party set out from **Batasingaram**. The route ahead passed through hilly, forested terrain, where there was a risk of ambush by dacoits at any point. Vinobha Bhave, however, set out without regard for any such danger, and the party reached **Pochampalli** safely.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter27-bhoodan-movement-p2", "partName": "Pochampalli, the Continuing Yatra, and National Recognition", "bodyMd": "Vinobha Bhave, along with his followers, walked **10–12 miles** each morning, halted in a village, and addressed public meetings there with a message of peace. He visited the huts of Harijans, inquired after their welfare, and looked into their needs.\n\nAfter reaching **Pochampalli village** in **Nalgonda district**, on **April 18**, two Harijans approached him and explained that **forty families** in Pochampalli had no land of their own, and they had no land in the village either; they requested that some land be given to them.\n\nThe local **Deshmukh, V. Ramachandra Reddy**, on hearing this, offered to give **100 acres** from his own land. Vinobha Bhave, addressing the Harijans, said that if they were to accept it, the land should be cultivated under a cooperative system.\n\nThat evening, at the public meeting held at **6 p.m.**, following prayers, Vinobha Bhave announced that the Harijans had asked landlord Ramachandra Reddy for 100 acres, and declared that if even one landlord were to honor such a promise, it could set in motion a great undertaking (_mahattara karyam_) in India. **The Bhoodan Yajna began from that day.**\n\n### Continuation of the Yatra\n\nVinobhaji''s tour proceeded through the **Nalgonda, Devarakonda, and Miryalaguda taluks** and on to **Suryapet**. Communists did not obstruct him anywhere along the way. Vinobhaji sought land donations at every stop, and donors came forward; on some days, as much as **200 acres** of land was donated in a single day.\n\n### National Recognition\n\nVinobha''s Telangana tour proved highly fruitful, and Prime Minister **Jawaharlal Nehru** announced this in Parliament. Nehru''s remark was that, instead of deploying **50,000 troops** to maintain peace and security in Telangana, one selfless individual — Vinobha — was accomplishing that very task on his own.\n\nThis announcement drew the eyes of the world to Vinobha''s padayatra. Whether or how much Vinobha''s message actually curbed acts of violence remained uncertain, but it drew ordinary people to him in large numbers.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter27-bhoodan-movement-p3", "partName": "The 1955 Visit and Palamuru District", "bodyMd": "In **1955**, Vinobha Bhave visited Hyderabad and, at the request that he visit **Palamuru (Mahbubnagar) district**, went there.\n\n**Palleria Hanumantha Rao** offered **50,000 acres** for the Bhoodan movement, declaring that Palamuru would lead the way in this effort. This offer was accepted by all without hesitation. Vinobha toured the entire district, collecting land for the Bhoodan movement from landlords in the **Kalwakurthy, Kollapur, and Nagarkurnool** taluks.\n\n### Madhavarao Palle Village\n\nVinobha Bhave arranged for the donation of **Madhavarao Palle village** in Kollapur taluk. This same village later became the site of the inauguration of **Vanaparthi Polytechnic College**, an event attended by the then Prime Minister, Jawaharlal Nehru — a fact still recalled as a notable historical memory of the region.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter27-bhoodan-movement-p4", "partName": "Vinobha''s Philosophy and Government Recognition of Donated Lands", "bodyMd": "Vinobha held that in a free, democratic nation, problems ought to be resolved harmoniously. He maintained that a non-violent movement against violence should proceed among the people themselves, within the framework of democracy.\n\nIn the context of this tour, the government gave legal standing to the lands received through Bhoodan in Telangana. At that time, **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao** was serving as **Revenue Minister** in **M. K. Vellodi''s** cabinet. He promptly introduced the **Bhoodan Special Rules**, which proved of great use in systematically regularizing the lands that had been received as donations.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p1", "partName": "Hyderabad''s Princely Status and the Early Demand for Merger", "bodyMd": "When India became independent on **15th August, 1947**, the state of Hyderabad did not merge into the Union of India. At independence, India had **562 princely states**; all but four of these were integrated into the Indian Union soon after independence. The four holdouts were:\n\n1. **Travancore** (Kerala)\n2. **Junagadh** (Gujarat)\n3. **Kashmir** (Jammu & Kashmir)\n4. **Hyderabad**\n\nOf these four, Hyderabad was the largest. Its eventual merger came about through a police action on **17th September, 1948**, code-named **\"Operation Polo,\"** which brought Nizam rule to an end.\n\nHyderabad''s freedom movement was not seen by Swamiji (the movement''s leadership) as a purely regional issue — India''s final struggle for freedom was understood to be continuing on Hyderabad soil, and this was seen as requiring the support of all Indians.\n\n- **Ayyadevara Kaleswara Rao** headed the Hyderabad State Congress Committee, which belonged to the Andhra Rashtriya Congress; funds for the movement were collected from Andhra people and passed on to Swamiji.\n- On **27th December, 1947**, a \"Hyderabad Sahayaka Samithi\" (Hyderabad Aid Committee) was formed in Madras, chaired by the Madras Mayor **Sri Krishna**, with **Venkataswaminaidu** and **Smt. Ammu Swaminathan** as vice-presidents and **Pappu Somasundaram** as secretary.\n- Separately, in **June 1946**, a conference of princely-state representatives was held under the chairmanship of **Jawaharlal Nehru**, following the British government''s announcement earlier that year that independence would be granted to India.\n  - Hyderabad State Congress members who attended: **Swamy Ramananda Tirtha** and **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao**.\n  - Andhra Maha Sabha members who took part: **Raavi Narayana Reddy** and **Laxmi Narsaiah**.\n  - All of them expressed the intention of merging Hyderabad state with the Indian Union.\n- At a public meeting in **Guntur** on **21st June, 1948**, **Jayaprakash Narayan** declared that there was no way to resolve Hyderabad''s problems except through war, and called for Hyderabad to be surrounded on three sides using the armies of the neighbouring states.\n- **Acharya Bhansali**, a close associate of Gandhiji at Sevagram, undertook a fast unto death in **Vijayawada** against the atrocities being committed on people by the Razakars in Telangana and the insults being inflicted on women; the Nizam government refused to allow him to enter the state.\n\nThe State Congress intensified its efforts and met the Nizam''s high-handedness with equal firmness; the Nizam''s kingdom turned into a powder keg. All parties, including Congress, carried the political struggle forward together on the principle that action follows conviction.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p2", "partName": "Jayaprakash Narayan''s Visit and the Nizam''s Declarations of Independence", "bodyMd": "- **7th May, 1947** — **Jayaprakash Narayan**, General Secretary of the Socialist Party, visited Hyderabad and addressed a public meeting at **Karbala Garden**, stating that Hyderabad state should merge with the Indian Union. Labour leaders **Madhav Singh** and **Narendra Prasad Saxena** also addressed the meeting.\n- The Nizam government, angered by Jayaprakash Narayan''s statement, expelled him from Hyderabad state.\n- The Hyderabad State Congress opposed this expulsion and agitated under a committee led by **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao**.\n- **12th June, 1947** — Nizam **Osman Ali Khan** declared himself a sovereign, independent ruler.\n- **13th August, 1947** — He again declared Hyderabad state to be an independent state.\n- During this period, the Nizam sent **Walter Monckton**, **Chattari Nawab**, and **Sultan Ahmed** to negotiate with **Mountbatten**.\n- **7th August, 1947** — Hyderabad State Congress leaders observed **\"Join India Union Day.\"**\n- **Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel** and **Nehru** subsequently mobilised armed forces around the boundaries of Hyderabad.\n- Faced with this pressure, the Nizam government entered into a **Standstill Agreement** with the Indian government.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p3", "partName": "The Standstill Agreement (1947)", "bodyMd": "- **29th November, 1947** — The Indian government and the Nizam signed the Standstill Agreement for a period of one year. The Nizam''s prime minister at this time was **Mehdi Yar Jung**.\n- Signatories: **Nizam Mir Osman Ali Khan** and Indian Governor-General **Lord Mountbatten**.\n- Under the agreement, **K.M. Munshi** was appointed as the Indian Government''s Agent-General in Hyderabad, and **Zain Yar Jung** was appointed as the Hyderabad Agent-General in Delhi.\n\n### Key Terms of the Agreement\n- All relations that existed between the Nizam and the British government before 15th August, 1947 would continue temporarily, unchanged, between the Nizam and the Indian government — hence the name \"Standstill Agreement.\"\n- Hyderabad''s defence, foreign affairs, and communications would come under Indian government control.\n- Indian currency would be valid in Hyderabad state.\n- Indian banks and insurance companies would be permitted to operate in the Nizam''s state.\n- Citizens of Hyderabad would be granted freedom of speech and freedom of assembly; arrested State Congress leaders, including **Ramananda Tirtha**, were to be released.\n- A democratic government was to be formed in Hyderabad within one year of the agreement.\n- If war broke out between India and Pakistan, Hyderabad was to remain neutral.\n\n### Attack on the Nizam\n- **4th December, 1947** — **Narayan Rao Pawar** of the Arya Samaj Kranti Kar Dal attacked Nizam Osman Ali Khan with a bomb at King Kothi, Hyderabad; the attack failed. Police arrested Narayan Rao Pawar, Jagadeeshwar, and Gandaiah; the seventh accused in the case was **Konda Laxman Bapuji**. Narayan Rao Pawar is remembered as the **\"Telangana Bhagat Singh.\"**\n- Mehdi Yar Jung resigned as prime minister on the very day the Standstill Agreement was signed; the Nizam then appointed **Laiq Ali** as prime minister.\n- **18th December, 1947** — The Nizam abolished the Council of Ministers and formed a new cabinet under Laiq Ali, which included four Hindu ministers:\n\n| Minister | Portfolio |\n|---|---|\n| Pingali Venkatrami Reddy | Deputy Prime Minister |\n| B.S. Venkatrao | — |\n| Mallikarjun Rao | — |\n| J.V. Joshi | — |\n\nLaiq Ali''s appointment marked the start of the breach of the Standstill Agreement.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p4", "partName": "Breach of the Standstill Agreement and India''s Response", "bodyMd": "- The Nizam government declared Indian currency invalid in Hyderabad state.\n- The Nizam sent **General Sayyed El Edroos** to Europe to purchase weapons.\n- **Sydney Cotton**, an Australian arms dealer, illegally supplied weapons to the Nizam from Goa; this illegal trade was brought to the Indian government''s notice by **Vandemataram Ramchandra Rao** and **Rama Rao**.\n- A separate ammunition deal was made with **T.T. Moore**, a former British army officer.\n- Prime Minister Laiq Ali attempted to purchase Goa from the Portuguese.\n- In violation of the treaty''s foreign-affairs clause, the Nizam gave a loan of **₹20 crores** to Pakistan out of Indian securities.\n- The Nizam''s last prime minister, Laiq Ali, coordinated with Pakistan''s first prime minister, **Liaquat Ali**, against India.\n- The Nizam set up ordinance factories to manufacture arms at **Moti Mahal, Golkonda,** and **Chaderghat**.\n- Consequently, the Indian government imposed financial restrictions on Hyderabad, and the Indian army took control of a place called **\"Nanaj\"** in Hyderabad state.\n- During this period the Nizam again sent Walter Monckton, Chattari Nawab, and Sultan Ahmed to negotiate with Mountbatten.\n\n## Indian Government''s Restrictions on Hyderabad\n\n- **Deccan Airways** of Hyderabad was banned, cutting off the state''s transport links and communication with the outside world.\n- Telephone lines were disrupted.\n- Restrictions were placed on Indian government securities held by Hyderabad; export of gold, diamonds, and coins from the state was banned.\n- Relations between the **Imperial Bank of India** and the Hyderabad State Bank were severed, worsening relations further.\n- Governor-General **Mountbatten** attempted mediation and sent his secretary **Allen Campbell** to Hyderabad to mediate, but this effort failed.\n\n## K.M. Munshi''s Negotiations\n\n- As agreed under the Standstill Agreement, **K.M. Munshi** was appointed Indian Government Agent-General in Hyderabad.\n- The Nizam, considering the Agent-General''s status unequal to that of a British Resident, refused to give Munshi the Residency building; Munshi instead stayed at **Deccan House, Bollaram**, an Indian government building.\n- Munshi urged the Nizam either to hold a plebiscite or to form a democratic government immediately.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p5", "partName": "Popular Resistance Inside Hyderabad", "bodyMd": "### Bhagya Nagar Radio\n- On the Nizam government''s **Deccan Radio**, Hyderabad Commander-in-Chief **Syed Ahmed El Edroos** warned the people to be prepared for all circumstances.\n- Since Deccan Radio was spreading false news, resistance leaders started the **Bhagya Nagar Radio** station to keep people informed with accurate news.\n- It was established by **Paaga Pulla Reddy**, operated as a secret radio station; socialist leader **Achyut Patwardhan** arranged the radio apparatus for Pulla Reddy in Bombay.\n- Broadcasts were made in **Telugu, English, and Urdu** — Telugu broadcasts by **Ramakrishna Sharma**, Urdu broadcasts by **T. Nagappa**.\n- These broadcasts raised awareness of Razakar and Nizam atrocities and helped stage protests against the Nizam; the Nizam government eventually banned the station.\n\n### Lawyers'' Agitation\n- Hyderabad''s lawyers agitated against Nizam rule, demanding merger with the Indian Union.\n- **February 1948** — a Lawyers'' Protest Committee was formed under **Vinayakarao Vidyalankar**.\n- Later, the **Nyayavadula Prathighatana Samithi** (Lawyers'' Resistance Committee) was formed under **Ganapathi Lal**, which wrote to the Nizam demanding a boycott of court duties.\n\n### Bakar Ali Mirza and the Muslim Appeal\n- Under **Bakar Ali Mirza''s** leadership, a number of Muslims wrote to the Nizam urging merger with India; the Nizam responded by placing Bakar Ali Mirza under house arrest.\n- **Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel** described Hyderabad as **\"an ulcer in the heart of India which needed to be removed surgically.\"**\n- As Indian Home Minister, Patel ordered Southern Commander-in-Chief **E.N. Goddard** to prepare a plan for police action on Hyderabad; Goddard drew up the plan and handed it to Patel.\n\n### The Congress Satyagraha Campaign\nThe State Congress intensified its own agitation alongside these efforts. Congress volunteers established satyagraha camps in **21** places in Madras province, **23** in Bombay province, and **4** in Madhya Pradesh, and carried out an anti-Razakar satyagraha campaign from them.\n\n**Camp leadership**\n- **Bejawada (Vijayawada) camp:** Jamalapuram Keshava Rao, V.B. Raju, T. Hayagrivachari\n- **Sholapur camp:** Konda Lakshmi\n- **Chanda camp:** P.V. Narasimha Rao\n\n**Scale of the movement**\n- **14,000** volunteers were arrested during this agitation.\n- **15,000** students boycotted schools.\n- **6,000** village officials resigned their posts.\n- Trees worth over **₹5 lakh**, planted to reduce the government''s excise revenue, were cut down entirely by the movement.\n- Various taxes levied by the Nizam government were prevented from being collected.\n- **615** police stations and Razakar camps were razed in this struggle.\n- Of **750** customs (\"cordon\") posts set up around the state''s artificial boundaries, **500** posts were destroyed.\n- Village defence forces (**Gramarakshaka Dalas**), formed for village protection, carried out **175** attacks on police and Razakars.\n- In this guerrilla struggle, **200** Razakars and Nizam policemen were killed.\n- Congress volunteers who lost their lives: **28** in Telangana, **22** in Marathwada, and **15** in Karnataka.\n\nThe struggle was directed from outside the state''s borders, with resistance camps set up in places like Bejawada, Sholapur, and Chanda, as listed above.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p6", "partName": "Razakar Propaganda on Deccan Radio", "bodyMd": "Razakar leader **Qasim Razvi** feared that his organisation would be entirely destroyed and wrote to his father — the Nizam''s second son, **Mohajanja** — asking for **₹5 crore** to be able to flee to a safe region of the country, but received no reply.\n\nRazvi also pinned many hopes on Pakistan. He sent a delegation under his brother-in-law **Mohin Nawaz Jung** to Karachi to meet **Jinnah**; owing to Jinnah''s illness, the delegation was not granted an interview.\n\nRazvi himself considered going to Karachi with no other option, but [Discrepancy between sources: Source 1 (English) — Jinnah died on 11th September, 1948; Source 2 (Telugu) — Jinnah died on 12th September, 1948 — needs verification]. The Police Action began on **13th September**, two days after Jinnah''s death.\n\nRazvi tried to deceive the Razakars and keep up their morale, entrusting overall command to Hyderabad''s Commander-in-Chief, **Major-General El Edroos**, even though Razvi himself did not have the army''s confidence. When the Razakars themselves complained that they needed proper weapons to fight, Razvi dismissed their concern and urged them to keep fighting on faith alone, insisting that victory would come regardless.\n\nThe weapons that actually reached the Razakars had, in truth, arrived through smuggling and were of no real use to trained soldiers, and the Razakars did not know how to use them properly. Razvi''s propaganda went further than even that of Hitler, and he kept the Deccan Radio firmly under his own control. Deccan Radio broadcast a number of false claims, including:\n\n1. If the Indian government initiated military action against Hyderabad, Muslim states such as Iran, Afghanistan, Pakistan, Turkey, and Egypt would come to Hyderabad''s rescue, with the Arab League extending its support.\n2. America would enter the picture, since Hyderabad served US interests as a protected outpost to check the spread of communism in South-East Asia.\n3. India, already weakened by numerous internal problems, would not be able to sustain the struggle — the Sikhs were demanding a separate state, and princely rulers everywhere were waiting for a good opportunity to revolt against Indian rule, with the Indian army in any case concentrated in Kashmir.\n4. Hyderabad had a secret agreement with Pakistan, under which Pakistan would build two air bases in Goa from which attacks would be launched on India.\n5. Within a few days, the port of Bandar would be captured, after which Hyderabad would establish direct relations with foreign countries.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p7", "partName": "Hyderabad''s Appeal to the United Nations", "bodyMd": "- Hyderabad took its case to the UN Security Council. The Nizam government''s committee before the UN Security Council was headed by Hyderabad''s External Affairs Minister, **Moin Nawaz Jung**.\n- Before this, on **21st August, 1948**, the Nizam government had already sent a complaint against the Indian government via cablegram to the UN Security Council, under **Article 35(2)**.\n- **Sir Walter Monckton** was engaged to assist the Nizam''s case before the Security Council; the UN Security Council president at the time was **Alexander Cadogan**.\n- The Indian government sent its own delegation to the Security Council under **Sir Ramaswamy Mudaliar** of Madras.\n- The Nizam wrote personally to several world leaders seeking support against the merger — **British Emperor George VI**, **British Prime Minister Clement Attlee**, **British Opposition Leader Winston Churchill**, and **American President Harry S. Truman** — all of whom expressed their inability to help.\n- While the Hyderabad case was still under the Security Council''s consideration, the Indian government carried out its police action on Hyderabad. News of this, as reported in the foreign press, was read out to the Security Council by Moin Nawaz Jung, leading Hyderabad''s delegation.\n- Sir Ramaswamy Mudaliar, leading the Indian delegation, argued that Hyderabad was India''s internal problem and did not fall within the Security Council''s jurisdiction.\n- As these arguments continued at the UN, the Police Action was completed on **17th September**. [Discrepancy between sources: Source 1 (English) — the Nizam withdrew his UN complaint on 22nd September, 1948; Source 2 (Telugu) — the Nizam informed the Security Council by telegram, on the same day the Police Action concluded (17th September), that he was withdrawing the case — needs verification.]\n\nAngered by the Nizam''s actions, and following the death of **Muhammad Ali Jinnah** — the Nizam''s biggest supporter — the Indian government ordered military action two days later.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p8", "partName": "Operation Polo — the Police Action", "bodyMd": "### Was It a War?\nThis was not a war in the true sense — the Nizam''s soldiers barely fought anywhere. In reality, Hyderabad possessed no army of its own; for the second Nizam''s entire reign, the force had effectively been under British control, and whatever army the Nizam did have was thoroughly weak. All the military cantonments in Hyderabad were dominated by British troops.\n\nHyderabad''s army had no modern weapons, and there were no factories capable of producing war material. Two hundred years of dependence had reduced Hyderabad''s military strength to a mere token force. It was the Razakars — not the Nizam''s army — who fought and resisted the police action.\n\n### The Operation\n- The Indian government informed the Pakistani High Commissioner and the American government that \"Operation Polo\" was being conducted in Hyderabad to stop the atrocities of communists and Razakars and to establish peace.\n- It was termed a **\"Police Action\"** rather than a military action because, under UN principles, one independent country cannot conduct a military action against another independent country. Accordingly, the expenses of the operation were booked under the Education Department''s accounts rather than the Home or Defence Ministry''s.\n- The operation was named \"Police Action\" by **C. Rajagopalachari**; it is also referred to as **Operation Polo** or **Operation Caterpillar** in a military sense, and as the **Goddard Plan**.\n- **Started:** 13th September, 1948. **Ended:** 17th September, 1948. **Planned by:** E.N. Goddard.\n\n**Key figures during Operation Polo**\n\n| Role | Person |\n|---|---|\n| Indian Governor-General | C. Rajagopalachari |\n| Indian Army Chief | Sir Roy Bucher |\n| Indian Defence Minister | Baldev Singh |\n| Indian Home Minister | Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel |\n| Indian Secretary of Princely States | V.P. Menon |\n| Hyderabad Army Commander-in-Chief | General El Edroos |\n| Hyderabad Prime Minister | Mir Laiq Ali |\n| Hyderabad Police Commissioner | Deen Yar Jung |\n\n- The operation was conducted under Southern Commander **Lt. General Maharaja Rajendra Singh**, who divided the army into the following forces:\n\n| Sector | Commanding Officer |\n|---|---|\n| Solapur | J.N. Chaudhari |\n| Vijayawada | A.A. Rudra |\n| Berar/Hospet | Shiva Dattu Singh |\n| Bombay | D.S. Dhar |\n\n- **Pune airbase**, under the command of **Mukherjee**, was used to support attacks with aircraft.\n\n### Course of the Operation\n- **13th September, 1948** — Military attacks began from all corners of Hyderabad. Lt. Col. Ram Singh, of the strike force under J.N. Chaudhari, annexed **Naldurg** (in Maharashtra), **Sholapur**, **Thamalwadi**, and **Thuljapur**. The same day, under Major Rudra''s leadership, the army occupied **Bonakallu**. Naldurg was the first city annexed as part of Operation Polo.\n- **14th September** — The Indian army occupied **Doulatabad, Jalna, Manikhed,** and **Kanegao**.\n- **15th September** — Forces led by Major General Rudra entered **Old Suryapet** village.\n- **16th September** — **Zahirabad, Hingoli, Raichur, Parbhani, Koppal, Munirabad,** and other towns were occupied.\n- Meanwhile, Hyderabad''s military commander **Sayyed Ahmed El Edroos**, who had been secretly passing updates to the Indian army, ordered Nizam forces to fall back rather than fight. This allowed the Indian army under Major General **J.N. Chaudhari** to enter Hyderabad quickly, and without El Edroos''s cooperation this rapid advance would not have been possible.\n- Had the matter instead been taken up before the UN Security Council on the evening of 17th September, Hyderabad might have retained an independent status, similar to the earlier position of Jammu & Kashmir.\n- Prime Minister **Mir Laiq Ali**, before resigning his post, suggested destroying all secret documents in his office and the Nizam''s office.\n- He also instructed **General Mustak Ahmed Khan**, Hyderabad''s agent in Pakistan, to transfer all the funds in his office to his own account, and sent a telegram to the London Agent-General, **Mir Nawab Jung**, to credit £5,000 to his account — saying the money could be used for a future independence movement.\n\n### Fall of the Nizam''s Government\n- **17th September, 1948** — Nizam **Osman Ali Khan** met K.M. Munshi at the **Lake View Guest House**, Hyderabad, and told him that Hyderabad state would merge with the Indian Union.\n- The same evening, at **7 p.m.**, the Nizam announced on Deccan Radio, in the name of Indian Governor-General Rajagopalachari, that Hyderabad had become part of the Indian Union, and that Indian army camps would be permitted at **Bollaram** and **Secunderabad**. He also ordered the immediate release of **Swamy Ramananda Tirtha**, president of the State Congress.\n- **Deccan Radio** was the Nizam government''s official radio station; its first director was **Fazlur Rehman**.\n- **18th September, 1948** — Hyderabad Commander-in-Chief **El Edroos**, along with his troops, surrendered before Indian Major General **J.N. Chaudhari**. Under military rules, Chaudhari — being the first Indian officer to enter Hyderabad — was appointed Hyderabad''s Military General.\n- The same day, Razakar leader **Qasim Razvi** was arrested and imprisoned at the military camp in **Tirumalgiri**, and Prime Minister Laiq Ali was placed under house arrest. Nizam Osman Ali Khan welcomed **Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel** at **Begumpet Airport** on his visit to the state.\n- **22nd September, 1948** — the Nizam formally withdrew his complaint against the Indian government from the United Nations (see discrepancy note above regarding the withdrawal date).\n\n### Remembrance of the Day\n- In **Karnataka**, 17th September is observed as **Hyderabad–Karnataka Liberation Day**; in **Maharashtra**, as **Marathwada Sangram Mukti Divas**.\n- In **Telangana**, communists and Muslims refer to 17th September as **\"Betrayal Day,\"** while others call it **Telangana Liberation Day**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p9", "partName": "Aftermath of Operation Polo", "bodyMd": "The entire military action was executed so swiftly and effectively that it can fairly be called a nearly bloodless conquest completed within a week. Indian forces subdued Hyderabad in a matter of days.\n\nThe Razakar organisation, which had prospered on the strength of religious extremism, vanished into thin air without a trace — leaving behind no compensation, no accountability, for its actions.\n\n**Casualties:** During the Police Action, roughly **2,000 Razakars** were killed and **4,500 people** were arrested, according to the account left by Major General J.N. Chaudhari, who successfully led the operation, in his memoir. The Police Action concluded within four days.\n\nAs the Police Action progressed, **Khasim Razvi** — who had postured as a **\"Field Marshal\"** while whipping up the Razakars — came to be nicknamed a **\"Paper Tiger.\"** In his final message broadcast on Deccan Radio, Razvi admitted defeat, told people not to lose heart, and said that they would have to look after their own safety since the Nizam''s forces could not withstand India''s military strength.\n\n- As the Indian army entered Hyderabad, Razvi hoped to escape to Pakistan and stood ready at the airport, but the aircraft left before he could board, and he was caught. He was given a seven-year prison sentence in the **Bibinagar case**.\n- The State Congress held a majority in the state and enjoyed public confidence, so responsibility for state administration would naturally have passed to it after the Police Action.\n- However, because of factional splits within Congress and the strength of pro-communist elements, along with a lack of confidence among some that the State Congress could pursue consistent policies, the party was not handed administrative authority at that stage.\n- **Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru** and **Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel** visited Hyderabad and received a grand welcome.\n- It was decided that, since the State Congress could not immediately take over administration, a provisional government should be set up, and Major General **J.N. Chaudhari** was appointed Military Governor.\n- People initially welcomed the military government wholeheartedly. However, the police and military administration filled most official posts with a large number of officers brought in from Madras and Bombay rather than from the local population.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p10", "partName": "J.N. Chaudhari''s Military Rule and the Sundarlal Committee (1948–49)", "bodyMd": "- **18th September, 1948** — **Jayanto Nath Chaudhari** took charge as Hyderabad''s Military Governor in the presence of Jawaharlal Nehru; his period of rule is dated from **24th September, 1948 to 31st December, 1949**.\n\n**Working committee of the Military Governor**\n\n| Role | Person |\n|---|---|\n| Military Governor | J.N. Chaudhari |\n| Chief Civil Administrator | D.S. Bakley |\n| Additional Chief Civil Administrator | D.R. Pradhan |\n\n- Chaudhari was born in **Haripura, Bengal**, and had served in the British army at Secunderabad''s Bison Division, then the largest military station in India. He took part in the **Second World War**, and later served as Indian Army Chief during the **1965 war with Pakistan**.\n\n### Chaudhari''s Farmana (6th February, 1949)\n- The Nizam''s currency, **Hali Sikka**, was to be banned (it was completely withdrawn on **1st April, 1955**).\n- The Nizam''s personal lands, known as **Sarf-e-Khas**, were taken over by the government, with **₹3 crore** paid as compensation.\n- Government institutions across the state were given holidays on **Sunday** instead of **Friday**.\n- In **August 1949**, the **\"Hyderabad State Abolition of Jagirdars & Regulation Act\"** was passed, under which all Jagir lands were taken over by the government.\n\n### Attacks on Communists and Muslims\n- During Operation Polo and under the Chaudhari government, the Indian military indiscriminately attacked Communists and Muslims — hundreds of Communists were killed and thousands of Muslims were massacred.\n- These attacks were brought to Nehru''s notice by journalist **Younis Salim**, prompting Nehru to appoint the **Sundarlal Committee** in **October 1949** to investigate.\n\n## The Sundarlal Committee (1949)\n\n| Role | Person |\n|---|---|\n| Chairman | Pandit Sundarlal |\n| Member | Khazi Abdul Gaffar |\n| Member | Maulana Abdul Misri |\n\n- The committee visited Hyderabad, met Hindus, Muslims, Congress leaders, and students, and investigated the attacks. It began its investigation on **29th November, 1949** and submitted its report to the government on **21st December, 1949**.\n- According to the report, nearly **40,000 people** died in Hyderabad state after the Police Action.\n- The government did not release the report in full; it is now held at the **Nehru Memorial Museum & Library**, Delhi.\n- Alongside the Sundarlal Committee, the conditions of Muslims in Hyderabad were separately studied by **Farid Mirza**.\n- During Operation Polo, *Times* magazine reporter **Ramesh Thapar** visited Hyderabad and wrote the book *Hyderabad Toofan*.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter28-police-action-operation-polo-p11", "partName": "M.K. Vellodi''s Civil Government", "bodyMd": "- After the Sundarlal Committee''s report was submitted in December 1949, the central government removed **J.N. Chaudhari**.\n- In his place, the ICS officer **M.K. Vellodi** (full name Mullath Kadingi Vellodi Menon), from Kerala, was appointed Chief Minister of Hyderabad state, ruling from **26th January, 1950 to 6th March, 1952**.\n- **26th January, 1950** — Nizam Osman Ali Khan was appointed **Hyderabad Raj Pramukh**, a position he held until the formation of Andhra Pradesh on **1st November, 1956**.\n- Nizam Osman Ali Khan later contested and won general Lok Sabha elections in **1957 and 1962**. He died on **24th February, 1967**, and was buried at the King Kothi Palace; his tomb is named the **\"Judi Mosque.\"**\n\n**Ministers in M.K. Vellodi''s Government**\n\n| Minister | Portfolio |\n|---|---|\n| M.K. Vellodi | Civil Administrator (Chief Minister) |\n| Burgula Ramakrishna Rao | Education, Revenue, Excise |\n| M. Seshadri | Home, Communication & Law |\n| V.B. Raju | Labour, Customs, Industries & Housing |\n| Mulchand Gandhi | Medical, Health & Regional Institutions |\n| Yar Jung Bahadur | Public Works |\n| CVS Rao | Finance & Commerce |\n| Vinayakarao Vidyalankar | Agriculture, Veterinary |\n\n### Key Points of Vellodi''s Administration\n- **Sir Ramaswamy Mudaliar**, India''s representative at the UNO in 1949, announced that a civil administration had been formed in Hyderabad state and that the Nizam now stood only as its constitutional head.\n- **Burgula Narsinga Rao** stated at the same time that, since a civil administrative government had been formed, the \"Hyderabad Dominion\" no longer existed, and the UNO should therefore drop the question of Hyderabad''s independence.\n- **Telugu and English** replaced **Urdu** as the state''s official languages; Andhra people from Madras province benefited from this change by securing a large number of jobs in Hyderabad state, at the expense of the local population.\n- Land records that had existed in Urdu were translated into English; in this process, many land rights were misappropriated, records were spoiled, and the lands of Hyderabad state were exploited.\n- **April 1952** — Hyderabad and Secunderabad Municipal Corporations were formed; **Madapati Hanumantha Rao** became Hyderabad''s first Mayor, and **Vasudeva Mudaliar** became Secunderabad''s first Mayor.\n- Hyderabad''s **first general elections** were held in **1952**, in which **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao** was elected Chief Minister.\n\n## Key Books on Hyderabad''s Integration Movement\n\n| Book | Author |\n|---|---|\n| The End of an Era | K.M. Munshi |\n| Tragedy of Hyderabad | Laiq Ali |\n| Hyderabad in Retrospect | Ali Yavar Jung |\n| Hyderabad of the Seven Loaves | General El Edroos |\n| Destruction of Hyderabad | A.G. Nurani |\n| Hyderabad Swatantra Porata Smruthulu | Swamy Ramananda Tirtha |\n| Veera Telangana – Na Anubhavalu | Raavi Narayana Reddy |\n| History of Hyderabad Independence | Veldurthi Manikya Rao |\n| Integration of Indian States | V.P. Menon |\n| Operation Polo | J.N. Chaudhari |\n| The Last Nizam – The Fallen Empire | Zubaida Yazdani |\n| Hyderabad of Introspect | Maulvi Sayyed Mehdi |\n| Telangana: The Era of Mass Politics | B. Narsing Rao |\n| Hyderabad Under Salar Jung | Maulvi Chirag Ali |\n| Hyderabad Toofan | Ramesh Thapar |", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter29-formation-of-andhra-pradesh-and-early-state-politics', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter29-formation-of-andhra-pradesh-and-early-state-politics-p1", "partName": "Andhra Rashtra Nirmanam (Formation of Andhra State)", "bodyMd": "Potti Sriramulu began a fast unto death in Madras city on **19 October 1952**, demanding a separate Andhra state. After a 58-day continuous fast, Sriramulu died — his self-sacrifice set the Andhra region ablaze with anger.\n\nCoastal Andhra, Rayalaseema, and the Telangana-adjacent border areas erupted in unrest: processions, demonstrations, public meetings, and student agitation broke out everywhere. Wherever there were Telugus, that unrest was felt.\n\nWith this pressure, the government''s hand was forced. On **1 October 1953** PM Nehru announced in Parliament that an Andhra state would be formed with undisputed districts.\n\n### The Contest for Andhra Congress Legislature Party Leadership\n\nAt the time Andhra state was being formed, the number of elected Congress representatives from the Andhra region was comparatively small. Ranga''s Kisan Mazdoor Praja Party and Prakasam''s Praja Party had split Congress''s strength, and the Communists too stood as a united rival bloc. In this situation, Andhra Congress needed to elect a legislature party leader.\n\n*   **Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy** contested for this post.\n*   The then minister **Shankar Reddy**''s name was proposed by minister **Pattabhirama Rao**; it was also rumoured that Rajaji campaigned for Shankar Reddy.\n*   Once nominations closed, sentiment reversed in Srikakulam, Visakhapatnam, and East Godavari district circles.\n*   On **Gopala Reddi**''s advice, Shankar Reddy withdrew from the race.\n*   Sanjeeva Reddy accepted both the state Congress presidency and the leadership of the Congress Legislature Party.\n\n### The Capital City Debate\n\nDeciding the capital of the new Andhra state proved contentious, with three parties each pushing rival cities.\n\n*   **Visakhapatnam** was too distant for Rayalaseema.\n*   The **Kisan Mazdoor Lok Party** first proposed Tirupati, but Prakasam disliked Tirupati.\n*   Sanjeeva Reddy''s own home town, Anantapur, was unacceptable to others as he himself knew.\n*   Rajaji''s name being attached to a proposal made some hold a grudge, so Nellore too was dropped.\n*   The remaining choice was **Kurnool**, which all three parties eventually accepted.\n\nEven so, when the Andhra formation bill came up for debate in the Madras legislature, the Communists — weak in Rayalaseema — proposed keeping Kurnool only as a temporary name while shifting the real capital to Vijayawada. The Kisan Lok Party pushed for Tirupati instead, seen by critics as an attempt to build KLP''s own strength there.\n\nWith five non-Andhra members voting in Kurnool''s favour, neither the Vijayawada nor the Tirupati proposal secured enough votes. The arrangement collapsed back to Kurnool.\n\n### The First Andhra Ministry — T. Prakasam (1953–54)\n\nWhen Andhra state was carved out of Madras, the 140-seat assembly''s party strength stood as:\n\n| Party | Seats |\n|---|---|\n| Congress | 51 |\n| Communists | 43 |\n| Krishikar Lok Party | 14 |\n| Praja Party | 6 |\n| Praja Socialist Party | 15 |\n| Scheduled Caste Federation | 2 |\n| Independents | 9 |\n\nCongress lacked a full majority and could not form a government without other parties'' support. On top of this, even Nehru was said to be against a Prakasam-led ministry, and critics called a Prakasam ministry \"a headless torso.\" Sanjeeva Reddy and **Alluri Satyanarayana Raju**, using their full political skill, brought Prakasam back into the Congress fold.\n\nPM Nehru inaugurated Andhra state on **1 October 1953**. Prakasam became Chief Minister, with the following ministry:\n\n| Minister | Portfolio/Position |\n|---|---|\n| T. Prakasam | Chief Minister |\n| Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy | Deputy Chief Minister |\n| Tenneti Viswanatham | Minister |\n| S.B.P. Pattabhirama Rao | Minister |\n| Sanjeevayya | Minister |\n| Kadapa Kotireddy | Minister |\n\nUniquely in the history of parliamentary parties, Prakasam was Chief Minister without being party leader. Since Tenneti did not leave the Praja Party, Andhra''s first government was, from the outset, a mixed/coalition government. Later Gouthu Latchanna too was brought in, making it a three-party mix.\n\nFour ministry members were from Rayalaseema. The assembly''s real working strength was only around 70–75, making the government inherently unstable.\n\nThe government appointed the Shanti Ramamurthy Committee to look into prohibition, and the committee recommended lifting the ban. Congress''s own ideology opposed implementing this recommendation, and a no-confidence motion was brought against the government. Congress members Shankar Reddy and Adusumilli Subrahmanyam ended up voting against their own government, and the Prakasam ministry fell by a single vote on **6 November 1954**, bringing the state under Governor''s rule.\n\nAround the same time, Rajaji''s new education scheme created a storm in the Madras assembly, but Tamil members did not press for his resignation. Telugu leaders, in this period, proved unable to hold ministries stable — the state remained under President''s/Governor''s rule for about sixteen months.\n\n### The Gopala Reddi Ministry and the 1955 Elections\n\nWith Governor''s rule from December 1954, mid-term elections were held in **1955**. Remembering their 1952 defeat, Congress and former Congress members prepared cautiously in advance. To counter the Communists, Congress formed a **United Front** with the Praja Party and the Kisan Lok Party.\n\nDelimitation increased the assembly''s seats from 140 to 194, and Communists, anticipating power, entered the contest with major preparation.\n\nThe 1955 elections were seen as a special referendum for Andhra to avoid repeating the \"Communist mistake.\" A great movement arose to safeguard democracy in the state, winning huge public support — described as a \"people''s victory.\" What swung the middle classes to vote overwhelmingly for Congress was concern over \"certain issues, not certain leaders\" — a zealous desire to protect democracy and back a national party. Congress achieved a decisive win, and state-level leaders again came to the fore.\n\n**Election results (1955):**\n\n| Party | Contested | Won |\n|---|---|---|\n| Congress | 196 | 146 |\n| Communists | 169 | 15 |\n| Praja Socialist Party | 45 | 13 |\n| Independents | 1,692 | 22 |\n\nOf 13 contesting ministers, Tenneti Viswanatham and Shankar Reddy lost. Kurnool, which Congress had won in 1952, went to the Communists this time. Communist stalwarts Makineni Basavapunnayya, Tarimela Nagi Reddy, Moturi Hanumantha Rao, and Chandra Rajeshwara Rao, along with many others, also lost. Prakasam won — at 86 years of age.\n\nThe question of leadership arose again. Gopala Reddi, among the senior leaders, stood for it. Sanjeeva Reddy, mindful of state-building responsibilities and of having earlier been crowned deputy under Prakasam, did not press his own claim too far. Delhi''s Congress leadership decided on **Gopala Reddi** as leader, and Sanjeeva Reddy agreed to remain as Deputy CM.\n\n**United Front Ministry (1955):**\n\n| Minister | Position |\n|---|---|\n| Bezwada Gopala Reddi | Chief Minister |\n| Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy | Deputy Chief Minister |\n| Kalluri Chandramouli | Minister |\n| Kala Venkatrao | Minister |\n| Gouthu Latchanna | Minister |\n| A.B. Nageswara Rao | Minister |\n| Oblu Reddy | Minister |\n| D. Sanjeevayya | Minister |\n| Neerukonda Ramarao | Minister (Food) |\n\nNeerukonda Ramarao, who had taken oath as Food Minister, died on **4 May 1955** at the age of 41. With Gopala Reddi as CM, Alluri Satyanarayana Raju became PCC president.\n\nIt must be said that after the 1955 elections, the era of the \"old-guard generation\" ended in Andhra. Prakasam, due to old age, did not contest for leadership and himself showed little interest in the matter. Dr. Pattabhi Sitaramayya became Governor of Madhya Pradesh and left state politics. Acharya Ranga, who contested for state Congress president and lost, remained a lone figure thereafter, surviving in memory only as \"the farmers'' friend.\"\n\n### Kurnool as the Capital City\n\nKurnool''s population at the time was only **40,000**. The Collector''s office was converted into the Secretariat, and the Collector''s office itself was shifted to Nandyal. The Assembly was housed in the District Court building, and Governor Trivedi brought 4,000 tents from Delhi.\n\nThe Collector''s residence became the Chief Secretary B. Pullareddy''s residence. Even so, department heads and directorate offices could not be accommodated conveniently in Kurnool. Until Hyderabad became the AP capital, these offices continued to function out of Madras city itself.\n\nOn cabinet meeting days, department heads travelled from Madras by train, alighting at Guntakal and being driven onward to Kurnool by car. The Executive Engineer''s residence on the K.C. Canal bank was converted into Raj Bhavan.\n\nWith Prakasam''s encouragement, a 45-day budget session of the assembly was held in Visakhapatnam. Tikkavarapu Ramireddy Hall, a university hostel, was converted into the assembly hall, and legislators were lodged in university hostels. At this Vizag session itself, the bill for Venkateswara University was approved, and primary education was nationalized.\n\nGovernor Trivedi worked with great personal dedication on the question of Andhra''s new capital, working through the night with engineers so the work would not stall — down to arranging the water-supply pipes needed for the roads of the town destined to become the state capital.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter29-formation-of-andhra-pradesh-and-early-state-politics-p2", "partName": "Peddamanushula Oppandam (The Gentlemen''s Agreement, 1956)", "bodyMd": "Before Andhra Pradesh was formed, an agreement was reached in **1956** between the leaders of the Telangana and Andhra regions, known as the **Peddamanushula Oppandam (Gentlemen''s Agreement)**.\n\n**Signatories:**\n\n| From Andhra | From Telangana |\n|---|---|\n| Bezwada Gopala Reddi (CM) | Dr. Burgula Ramakrishna Rao (CM) |\n| Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy | Konda Venkata Ranga Reddy |\n| Alluri Satyanarayana Raju | J.V. Narsingarao |\n| Gouthu Latchanna | Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy |\n\n### Terms of the Agreement\n\n1.  Central and general administrative expenditure to be borne proportionately between the Andhra and Telangana regions; Telangana''s surplus reserved for that region''s own development. This arrangement to last five years, after which Telangana''s legislature members could ask for a further five years.\n2.  Prohibition to be implemented in Telangana as decided by Telangana''s own legislature members.\n3.  Higher educational facilities in Telangana reserved for Telangana students and to be further developed; all technical and other educational institutions in the state to reserve admissions proportionately for Telangana students.\n4.  If unification required reducing certain jobs, the reduction to happen proportionately.\n5.  Recruitment to government jobs to be based on the population ratio of the two regions.\n6.  For five years, Urdu to retain its existing position in Telangana''s administration and courts; regional bodies could later review this. No prior knowledge of Telugu to be a precondition for joining service, though a rule could require learning it within two years of appointment.\n7.  Candidates for posts reserved for Telangana to have twelve years'' residence in the region.\n8.  A **Regional Committee** to be set up for the all-round development of Telangana.\n9.  Sale of agricultural land in Telangana to be under the control of the Regional Committee.\n10.  The Regional Committee would have 20 members — two elected MLAs from each of Telangana''s nine districts, plus six members chosen from Telangana representatives in Parliament or the state legislature.\n11.  All Telangana ministers except the Chief Minister and Speaker to be members of the Regional Committee.\n12.  The Regional Committee to be constituted under Article 371 of the Constitution and appointed by the Governor.\n13.  Though not a statutory body, the Regional Committee would deliberate on procedure, development, irrigation and other agricultural schemes, industrial development, Telangana employment, public health, and prohibition.\n14.  Disagreements between the state government and the Regional Committee to be referred to the Government of India.\n15.  In the absence of a fresh agreement, the arrangement to be reviewed after ten years.\n16.  Cabinet composition to follow an Andhra:Telangana ratio of **6:4**, with one Muslim minister from Telangana.\n17.  If the Chief Minister is from Andhra, the Deputy Chief Minister must be from Telangana, and vice versa. Of the Home, Finance, Planning, Development, Commerce, and Industries portfolios, at least two must go to Telangana.\n18.  Hyderabad''s Pradesh Congress leaders sought a separate Congress committee for Telangana until 1962; Andhra Congress leaders raised no objection to this.\n\nThe same \"Peddamanushulu\" met again on **14 August 1956** and finalised further points in a joint note. The new state''s name was to be \"Andhra-Telangana,\" as Telangana representatives wished, and this appeared in the draft bill — but the Joint Select Committee changed it to \"**Andhra Pradesh**,\" as Andhra representatives had wanted. On the High Court, Telangana representatives wanted the main bench to remain in Hyderabad with a branch at Guntur, while Andhra representatives wanted the entire High Court to stay in Hyderabad.\n\n### Telangana Safeguards\n\n**Regional Standing Body**\n\n1.  Andhra Pradesh to have a single joint legislature for the whole state, one Governor, and a Council of Ministers responsible to that legislature.\n2.  For certain special matters, Telangana would be treated administratively as one region.\n3.  Telangana to have its own Standing Committee, comprising Telangana''s ministers and legislature members; the Chief Minister would not be a member of it.\n4.  Legislative matters on specific subjects to be referred to this Committee; it could also make proposals on general financial matters (excluding routine or emergency expenditure).\n5.  The Committee''s advice would normally be accepted by government and legislature; disagreements to go to the Governor, whose decision would be final.\n\n**Subjects entrusted to the Regional Committee:**\n\n1.  Development and economic planning within schemes framed by the legislature\n2.  Local administration — municipal corporations, constitutional powers, development trusts, district boards, village administration bodies\n3.  Public health, sanitation, local hospitals and treatment centres\n4.  Primary and secondary education\n5.  Admissions to Telangana''s educational institutions\n6.  Prohibition\n7.  Sale of agricultural land\n8.  Cottage and small-scale industries\n9.  Agricultural cooperative societies, markets/fairs\n\n### Residence Rules and Employee Retrenchment\n\nRecruitment to posts reserved for Telangana was to treat the region as a single unit, filled according to the domicile rules then in force under Hyderabad regulations. The Government of India was to advise the state government on measures needed to preserve Urdu''s position in the existing administrative and judicial system.\n\nOn retrenchment, the Government of India did not intend that serving employees should be removed unless unification made it necessary. Even then, without any deliberate scheme, the aim was to absorb Hyderabad state employees into the wider Andhra Pradesh service as far as possible. Any necessary retrenchment was to be applied uniformly across the enlarged state''s employees.\n\nDeciding expenditure based on the state''s financial needs remained a matter for the state government and legislature. But the Government of India accepted the principle of proportionate sharing of central/general administrative expenditure and dedicating Telangana''s surplus to Telangana''s development, as agreed by both regions'' representatives — and wanted this specifically brought to the notice of the Andhra Chief Minister. This letter carried the signature of Hyderabad Chief Minister Dr. Burgula Ramakrishna Rao, dated **14 August 1956** — the day the Hyderabad government was itself dissolved.\n\n### Fate of the Agreement''s Provisions\n\n*   Points 2, 3, 7, 8, 10, and 11 of the agreement were incorporated into the Andhra Pradesh Regional Committee Order.\n*   Point 9 was incorporated into Article 371 of the Constitution.\n*   Safeguard points 9 and 6 were incorporated into the Andhra Pradesh Regional Committee Order, 1956.\n*   Point 6-B, on Domestic Rules of State Government (state employment rules), was incorporated into the Regional Committee Order, 1958.\n*   The remaining points were never formally recognised as part of the \"Gentlemen''s Agreement.\"\n\nUnion Home Minister **Govind Ballabh Pant** directed leaders on both sides to obtain a legal expert''s advice on which points were constitutionally valid, and to finalise the matter on that basis. This task was left to Telangana''s leaders while Andhra''s leaders departed for Kurnool.\n\nThe bill was introduced in Parliament by Pant as the \"Andhra-Telangana Pradesh Bill,\" but on a member''s suggestion the name \"Andhra Pradesh\" was adopted instead. Andhra High Court Chief Justice Koka Subba Rao suggested to Rangareddy that Hyderabad''s High Court alone be retained. Rangareddy, familiar with Urdu-speaking judges, accepted this advice, and the Andhra and Telangana High Courts merged into one.\n\nIn April 1955, the bill (with some amendments) was placed before the Kurnool legislature: the new state''s name would be **Andhra Pradesh**, with capital and High Court at Hyderabad. The 1957 elections would apply only to Telangana, with elections across the whole of Andhra Pradesh only in 1962, and the upper house would have 72 members, with some amendments. On **13 April 1956**, the Hyderabad legislature approved this bill, and it received presidential assent on **31 August**.\n\nAndhra Pradesh''s legislature strength was fixed at 301. At the time of formation, Andhra''s own legislature had 196 members, and Telangana''s members in the Hyderabad legislature numbered 101 — giving a combined total of **297** with which the Andhra Pradesh legislature began.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter29-formation-of-andhra-pradesh-and-early-state-politics-p3", "partName": "Andhra Pradesh Avatarana (The Birth of Andhra Pradesh)", "bodyMd": "PM Nehru inaugurated the new state of Andhra Pradesh on **1 November 1956**.\n\n### The Contest for Chief Ministership\n\nThe question of who would lead the new ministry arose immediately. Though Prakasam again wished to lead, for many reasons this was not universally acceptable, and by then, being very old, he himself showed little interest. From the Andhra side, Bezwada Gopala Reddi and Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy entered the contest, but both declared they would step aside if Burgula Ramakrishna Rao were made leader. Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy, though not entirely opposed to Ramakrishna Rao, pledged to contest so that Ramakrishna Rao would not be elected unopposed.\n\nWith Telangana emerging as a fresh political force of nine districts, its swing mattered greatly. Telangana''s leader was **Konda Venkat Ranga Reddy**, whose followers included Marri Chenna Reddy and the Velama leader J.V. Narsingarao. Within Telangana, two factions existed:\n\n*   **Telangana-vadulu**, led by Konda Venkat Ranga Reddy\n*   **Vishalandhra-vadulu**, prominent among them Valluri Basavaraju — a capital-city politician without Ranga Reddy''s grassroots strength in Telangana''s villages\n\nAll Andhra and Telangana members gathered at Kurnool, and Congress observer **Shriman Narayan** came from the high command to oversee proceedings. Ranga Reddy, unwell, did not attend. Chenna Reddy went in with his own supporters, while Narsingarao''s and Ramakrishna Rao''s groups voted for **Sanjeeva Reddy**, who was elected AP Congress Legislature Party leader and became the first Chief Minister of united Andhra Pradesh.\n\nGopala Reddi, though a contestant, immediately joined Sanjeeva Reddy''s ministry. Ramakrishna Rao, who had helped, accepted the post of Kerala Governor and withdrew from AP politics. Chenna Reddy, who had opposed, alone remained without a post.\n\n**Sanjeeva Reddy''s first AP ministry (Telangana members):** Konda Venkat Ranga Reddy, V.B. Raju, J.V. Narsingarao, Mahdi Nawaz Jung, and Mandumula Narsingarao. As per the Gentlemen''s Agreement, ministry posts were to be shared between Andhra and Telangana proportionately, but of Telangana''s several factions, only five ministerial posts were given in total. Nookala Ramachandra Reddy, who deserved a sixth post, was instead given the position of Pradesh Congress Secretary.\n\nIn this stronghold of Telangana politics, Neelam and Alluri worked their political strategy skilfully, outmanoeuvring the Vishalandhra advocates while dividing the Telangana camp. Per the Gentlemen''s Agreement, the Deputy Chief Minister''s post was to go to a Telangana leader, but it was left unassigned for a time. This exploited the mutual rivalries among Telangana''s own leaders and became an early sign of Andhra–Telangana disunity.\n\nTelangana''s demand for separate development for itself was not unreasonable, but concrete action lagged. Rayalaseema representative Sanjeeva Reddy, drawing on his own region''s experience under the earlier **Sri Bagh** agreement (which was similarly not implemented), came to treat the Telangana Gentlemen''s Agreement as similarly unnecessary to honour, with the Neelam–Alluri leadership acting in unison throughout. Even though the Marathwada and Karnataka regions had separated from the old Hyderabad state, the larger Andhra Pradesh — comparatively bigger than those regions — still received Hyderabad as its capital.\n\n### Nehru''s Gandhi Bhavan Speech and Composite Culture\n\nSoon after arriving in Hyderabad, PM Nehru addressed Congress legislators and workers at Gandhi Bhavan. He described Hyderabad''s transformation as one of the greatest changes on India''s map since independence — not merely political but psychological, since a princely state had vanished from the map. The Hyderabad state had been split into three regions, one of which merged into Andhra state, marking a great national transformation.\n\nOn **composite culture**, Nehru observed that Hyderabad''s people had developed a distinctive culture with both good and less desirable elements. Old \"durbari\" traditions unsuited to modern India needed gradual replacement, he said, but he stressed that Hyderabad''s genuinely good elements, its composite culture, should not be discarded for anyone''s convenience, and that this would ultimately benefit Andhras too. He said Telangana should have a prominent place within Andhra Pradesh, and that both regions must accommodate each other with tolerance and goodwill, neither harming the other.\n\nAt the historic inauguration at **L.B. Stadium**, Nehru spoke in Hindustani, which the coastal and Rayalaseema audience did not understand. Ramakrishna Rao translated it into Telugu, easing the unrest in the gathering. His subsequent departure from the state left behind an impression of a divide between the two regions, making mutual understanding difficult.\n\nHyderabad — a city of about 96 square miles, with fine government buildings, High Court, general hospitals, and assembly building — became the Andhra capital. Telangana''s industries — Sirpur Paper Mills, Sirsilk, Bodhan Sugar Factory, Singareni collieries, Bellampalli coal mines, Praga Tools, and the Secunderabad railway workshop — were significant not just in India but across Asia. Shifting the capital away from Madras brought real hardships and struggles that were personally experienced.\n\n### Administrative Friction: \"Andhra Procedure\" vs \"Telangana Procedure\"\n\nAndhra people, mainly in Telangana, hoped that with their long-cherished Vishalandhra achieved, Andhra language and culture — long suppressed — would flourish. They hoped that the vast coastal region, Krishna–Godavari waters, Telangana''s industrial resources, and Rayalaseema''s minerals combined would bring prosperity through planned, unified development.\n\nOnce Sanjeeva Reddy settled in as CM, for about a year, staff transfers and paperwork dominated. Laws and rules from the two erstwhile administrations were never properly harmonised, and \"Andhra–Telangana\" distinctions grew more entrenched in government rather than less.\n\nBecause Telangana was treated merely as \"attached\" to Andhra, officialdom held that the whole administration should follow \"Andhra procedure\" — essentially Madras procedure, popularly called \"Boston Home\" procedure — with no need to consider \"Telangana procedure.\" Hyderabad had earlier followed the \"Bombay pattern,\" and the two systems were fundamentally incompatible.\n\nCopies of the \"Boston Home\" procedure manual were not even available in print, and Telangana staff had no familiarity with it at all. Kurnool officials pushed ahead with implementation regardless. Telangana employees felt shut out of jobs \"from the Governor downward,\" with the middle rungs of Telangana employment unable to secure a foothold.\n\nThere was no sense of shared purpose from the outset. Kurnool''s high-handedness was resented in Hyderabad, and Hyderabad''s grievances found no hearing in Kurnool.\n\n### Sanjeeva Reddy''s First Term and the Neelam–Alluri Rivalry\n\nIn **1957**, assembly elections were held only for the Telangana region, since the Hyderabad Provincial Congress Committee — as per the Gentlemen''s Agreement — remained separate until 1962. This was intended to shield Telangana Congress leaders'' influence in candidate selection from Andhra Congress leaders'' pressure. After the 1957 elections, the question of merging the still-separate Hyderabad Provincial Congress with the Andhra Provincial Congress arose. The high command advised a single unified state Congress, and the Hyderabad committee accepted.\n\nAfter 1957, Sanjeeva Reddy again became CM. A Legislative Council was formed the same year. As CM, Sanjeeva Reddy grew stronger while his deputy Alluri consolidated his hold as PCC president.\n\nBut when AICC president **U.N. Dhebar** appointed Alluri as his secretary, new turbulence began in Andhra politics. From here, the **Neelam–Alluri rivalry** took centre stage. Sanjeeva Reddy''s administration lasted three years and two months, after which he went to Delhi as AICC president.\n\nSatyanarayana Raju''s own ambition to become CM was not unreasonable. He was an attractive, capable, and skilled campaigner who knew Hindi well and had translated Rahul Sankrityayan''s _Volga se Ganga_ into Telugu. \"Truth\" (Satyam) had been Sanjeeva Reddy''s own watchword, yet his trusted associate turned against that very ideal, and enmity grew between former friends.\n\nA.C. Subba Reddy also nursed ambitions for the CM post, encouraged by friends. Sanjeeva Reddy watched these moves closely, while Alluri worked to crush them and tried instead to bring Kerala Governor Ramakrishna Rao into contention.\n\nDhebar had consulted both sides in advance. Sanjeeva Reddy and Gopala Reddi were initially not contestants; Ramakrishna Rao clearly refused to re-enter state politics; Gopala Reddi showed no enthusiasm either. The view emerged that Satyanarayana Raju, lacking administrative experience, was fine as a minister but unsuited to be CM. Eventually his own side brought Sanjeevayya and Pattabhirama Rao into active campaigning.\n\nIn the end, Sanjeeva Reddy''s opponents got their one moment of triumph — they backed Alluri for CM. Alluri, unable to take the post himself, agreed instead to **D. Sanjeevayya** as CM, becoming the chief architect behind that choice.\n\nThough Sanjeeva Reddy had accepted the AICC presidency, his focus stayed fixed on state politics, feeling that post to matter less to him than leading the state legislature party. Congress rules barred organisational office-holders from also holding legislature posts. Alluri, after his term, did not seek Rajya Sabha membership, but Sanjeeva Reddy himself remained an AP Legislative Assembly member from Kalahasti until the end.\n\n### Damodaram Sanjeevayya as Chief Minister\n\n**D. Sanjeevayya** took oath as AP''s second Chief Minister on **10 January 1960**, at the age of 38 — the state''s first Harijan Chief Minister. His ministry formation appeared straightforward: four expected persons became ministers, Telangana''s senior leader Konda Venkat Ranga Reddy received the Deputy CM post, and another Telangana leader, **Nookala Narottama Reddy**, became PCC president.\n\nNarottama Reddy''s unanimous election as PCC president stirred fresh discontent in Telangana''s districts. It fed the criticism against Alluri that just as Rayalaseema leaders had once been favoured unfairly, Alluri was now favouring Telangana leaders selectively for his own ends while curbing others'' opportunities.\n\nAfter Nookala Ramachandra Reddy became a minister, questions arose over why his brother Narottama Reddy also needed the PCC post, and accusations spread that Alluri was repeating Sanjeeva Reddy''s earlier mistakes in a new form. Caught in this conflict, Narottama Reddy had to resign as PCC president, and both sides settled on **Mallipudi Pallam Raju** as the new Congress president.\n\nAs Alluri''s missteps mounted, Sanjeevayya grew stronger and increasingly independent of him, and disagreement grew between the two. Sanjeevayya was self-made, born into a Harijan family, having endured great hardship before reaching prominence. His rise was one many attributed to Rajaji''s early patronage, and Rajaji, Prakasam, Gopala Reddi, and Sanjeeva Reddy had each shown patience toward him while he served as minister under them.\n\nOnce Sanjeevayya rose, however, his rivals began scheming against him from the start. Before assembly elections he declared his own intention to contest for the CM post, setting the stage for the high command''s intervention.\n\n### Sanjeeva Reddy''s Return and Resignation\n\nThough Sanjeeva Reddy still had eight months left as AICC president and could have sought re-election, the high command showed no inclination to retain him there. He pressed the urgency of the upcoming Prime Ministerial contest to justify his return to state politics, and built an atmosphere ensuring his unanimous election as AP Legislature Congress leader. Fresh candidates were fielded with the specific aim of making him CM again.\n\nIn the **1962 elections**, internal factionalism cost Congress around 50 seats, while the Communists won 51. In Telangana, Congress''s strength fell sharply — from 68 seats won in 1957 to no more than 62 this time. In Communist strongholds like Khammam and Nalgonda, Congress''s own campaigning backfired; in Warangal, its earlier 14 seats fell to no more than 7.\n\nEven so, Congress won one seat in Khammam, two in Medak, and an additional three in Karimnagar, while losing one seat in Hyderabad and two in Adilabad. Notable losers included Deputy CM Konda Venkat Ranga Reddy, ministers Konda Lakshman Bapuji and Smt. Masuma Begum, former minister J.V. Narsingarao, Telangana Regional Development Committee chairman Achyuta Reddy, and Bommakanti Satyanarayana Rao. The Jana Sangh failed to win a single seat.\n\nAfter 1962, **Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy** became CM for a second time. This time Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy finally found a place in the ministry, and the ministry was formed on a broader basis than before, though Alluri had no place in it, and neither did Sanjeevayya (who, not having become AICC president, bore no active grievance against the ministry at the time). The China war emergency had not yet arisen.\n\nAlluri Satyanarayana Raju died on **2 September 1963**, after which AP politics briefly lacked the accustomed rivalry framing Sanjeeva Reddy''s leadership. In 1964, the Communist Party split into two.\n\nKurnool district''s bus routes were nationalised, and bus owners went to the Supreme Court and won. An expert committee had earlier ruled that Nellore, Chittoor, and Kadapa districts'' routes should be nationalised before Kurnool''s — a sequence not followed. Opposition leaders Pidatala Rangareddy and Vijayabhaskara Reddy''s attacks on the CM over this were seen by some as personally motivated, but their complaints were upheld by the Supreme Court on **23 February 1964**. Taking the blame, Sanjeeva Reddy resigned as CM immediately after.\n\nJawaharlal Nehru died on **27 May 1964**; Lal Bahadur Shastri became Prime Minister.\n\n### Brahmananda Reddy''s Tenure\n\n**Kasu Brahmananda Reddy** became CM on **29 February 1964**, retaining all of Sanjeeva Reddy''s previous cabinet members except Sanjeeva Reddy himself, and initially made no departmental changes. Four months later, without Sanjeeva Reddy''s consent, he inducted Thota Ramaswamy, Chenchu Ramanaidu, and Tanneti Veeraraghavulu into the ministry.\n\nA pattern noted in the state''s politics was that whenever a leader rose to national prominence, his local strength within the state correspondingly weakened, and Sanjeeva Reddy followed this pattern. A rival faction emerged around **A.C. Subba Reddy**, encouraged by his allies.\n\nOnce Subba Reddy was inducted into the ministry, his Nellore district rival Chenchu Ramanaidu also secured a ministerial post. This emboldened Subba Reddy to intensify factional politics, splitting the ministry into two camps that in turn shaped district-level leadership contests. In Telangana, the long-standing rift between Nookala Ramachandra Reddy and Marri Chenna Reddy meant that when Chenna Reddy (Sanjeeva Reddy''s opponent) joined the Brahmananda Reddy faction, Nookala aligned with Sanjeeva Reddy''s faction.\n\nLal Bahadur Shastri died suddenly in **January 1966**; within 19 months, Congress''s parliamentary party again needed a new leader. Sanjeeva Reddy, present at Shastri''s last rites, joined other chief ministers (without consulting Brahmananda Reddy) in backing and strengthening Indira Gandhi. He felt that not being consulted on the PM question signalled his own declining prominence, and from here began his thoughts of removing Brahmananda Reddy.\n\nIn the contest held on **19 January 1966**, Indira Gandhi defeated Morarji Desai to become Prime Minister. In the **1967 elections**, Congress lost in many states but retained a narrow majority (22 members) at the centre. Non-Congress governments were formed in Tamil Nadu, Punjab, Kerala, West Bengal, Orissa, Bihar, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, and the Union Territory of Delhi.\n\nAgriculture and food ministers at both centre and state levels uniformly lost their seats. After the elections Indira Gandhi again became PM, Morarji Desai accepted the Deputy PM post, and Sanjeeva Reddy became Lok Sabha Speaker.\n\nIn the state, Congress won **164 seats**, and Brahmananda Reddy emerged unchallenged as leader. In the same period, Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy was inducted as a Union Minister at the centre — seen by some as the CM''s deliberate move to keep Chenna Reddy away from state politics, and by others as V.B. Raju''s own strategy with an eye on future state politics.\n\n### Death of the Last Nizam\n\n**Nizam Mir Usman Ali Khan**, who lived 89 years, died on **21 February 1967**. He ascended the throne in 1911, ruled Hyderabad state for 37 years, held the Raj Pramukh post for a time after accession, and, following Andhra Pradesh''s formation, lived out his remaining years without state authority, dying as a private citizen. Indira Gandhi''s condolence message called it \"the end of an era\" — a description genuinely apt in Telugu history.\n\nThough Brahmananda Reddy wished otherwise, the ruling establishment blocked a Secunderabad Lok Sabha candidacy in the 1967 elections as punishment for having supported Morarji Desai in the PM contest. Dr. Burgula Ramakrishna Rao passed away on **15 September** — the man who had helped shape and envision the comprehensive form of Andhra Pradesh, who had served outside its borders as Kerala and then Uttar Pradesh Governor, dying in an unfulfilled dream, distant from both regions he had once led.\n\nIn **April 1968**, the Hyderabad High Court ruled against Chenna Reddy on his election petition, and he appealed to the Supreme Court. That year, the All India Congress annual sessions, held with great pomp in Hyderabad, won Brahmananda Reddy wide approval, securing him membership of the Congress Working Committee and national prominence by early 1968.\n\n### Chenna Reddy''s Political Trajectory\n\nThe Supreme Court eventually ruled against Chenna Reddy in the election petition, forcing his resignation from both his Union Minister post and Rajya Sabha membership. Some police evidence surfacing in his favour (with Brahmananda Reddy''s reported help) suggested the case against him was weak, according to his trusted associates, though newspapers reported this help proved of no use to him.\n\nChenna Reddy — a man of intense ambition unable to bear political isolation — responded by raising the banner of a \"Democratic Front\" against Congress whenever circumstances turned against him. He remained, nonetheless, the one Telangana leader recognised everywhere as CM material without ever having actually become AP''s chief minister, despite Telangana''s 1956-era claim to the post rightfully belonging to him. A skilled orator and campaigner, he was given the chairmanship of the Road Transport Corporation as a face-saving honour whenever V.B. Raju''s faction found itself in difficulty; he had first become a minister as early as 1952.\n\n### Brahmananda Reddy''s Resignation (1971)\n\nAfter the Bangalore Congress session, CM Brahmananda Reddy announced a new ministry in **July 1969**, excluding Nookala Ramachandra Rao and Ramabhupala Reddy. With Nookala''s exclusion, the Deputy CM post went to **J.V. Narsingarao**.\n\nFacing numerous difficult tests and trials, Brahmananda Reddy steered the state firmly through the turbulent years of 1969–70 with skill, and his capability was widely credited as the reason Andhra Pradesh did not break apart during that period.\n\nThe promise made by the \"Peddamanushulu\" (Gentlemen''s Agreement) went unfulfilled, creating conditions that threatened state stability. CM Brahmananda Reddy resigned on **24 September 1971** — a democratically formed government effectively overthrown through agitation and pressure rather than through law.\n\nBrahmananda Reddy described his resignation as an unparalleled sacrifice for state unity, a framing Indira Gandhi echoed in her own statement, though in substance it amounted to his being eased out. Because he chose to resign rather than actively push for a preferred successor of his own, AP''s legislature members had to accept whichever leader was selected in Delhi.\n\nWith Indira Gandhi consolidating full central authority and pursuing her vision of socialism and modern political methods, she moved to install new leadership loyal to her policies within Congress governments and party organisations across the states. She toppled long-standing established leaderships to varying degrees, and this happened in Andhra Pradesh as elsewhere: a person suited to her policies was made both AP Chief Minister and PCC president, just as Sukhadia was removed in Rajasthan and V.P. Naik in Maharashtra.\n\n### The 1969 Presidential Election and the Congress Split\n\nIndependent India''s most intense Presidential contest occurred in **1969**, between **V.V. Giri** and **Sanjeeva Reddy** — an election that triggered a historic split in the Congress party. President Dr. Zakir Hussain''s death had made Vice President V.V. Giri the acting President.\n\nA section of Congress opposed making Giri''s acting presidency permanent. When the party''s official effort to field its own candidate failed, Indira Gandhi supported Giri''s resignation as acting President and his contest as an independent candidate. When the Congress Parliamentary Party leadership issued a whip directing MPs to vote for the party''s official candidate, Indira Gandhi disregarded it, instead advocating a \"conscience vote\" — openly signalling rebellion.\n\nThe Jana Sangh, Swatantra Party, the BKD, and nearly every other major party and faction decided to back Giri, blurring party lines in a contest fought almost entirely on ideological grounds. V.V. Giri won not through first-preference votes but through second-preference votes alone. In the Presidential election, **Andhra Pradesh gave Giri more votes than any other state**.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p1", "partName": "Vakatakas — Overview and Territory", "bodyMd": "The Vakatakas hold a special place among the dynasties that ruled the Deccan, spanning the middle of the third century to the middle of the sixth century CE. They were the most important dynasty influencing Deccan culture in this period.\n\n*   After the fall of the Satavahana empire, the **Abhiras** declared independence near Nasik in Maharashtra, while the **Vakatakas** declared independence in **Eastern Berar**.\n*   The Vakatakas had earlier been feudatories under the Satavahanas before asserting independence.\n*   Vakatakas ruled for roughly three centuries, from **250 CE to 560 CE** (also given elsewhere as up to 650 CE), providing political stability across the Deccan and Central India.\n*   They ensured law and order and, being liberal in religious matters, patronized education, literature, and the arts.\n\n**Matrimonial Alliances**\n\n*   Guptas and Nagas in the north\n*   Vishnukundus in the eastern Deccan\n*   Kadambas in the western Deccan\n\n**Territorial Extent**\n\n*   From the Arabian Sea in the west to Chhattisgarh in the east\n*   From Malwa, Gujarat, Bundelkhand and Bagelkhand in the north to the banks of the Tungabhadra River in the south\n*   The Nanded and Aurangabad districts of Maharashtra, and parts of Telangana such as **Adilabad**, were ruled by the **Vatsagulma branch** of the Vakatakas\n*   Vidarbha and Bagelkhand areas formed the key political center of the Vakatakas\n\n**Capitals** Early Vakataka inscriptions mention ''Purika Pattanam'' and ''Chanaka'' as their earliest capitals. Later, ''Pravarapura'' (Paunar), near Wardha, became their capital.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p2", "partName": "Historical Sources", "bodyMd": "### Inscriptions\n\nAmong the inscriptional sources of Vakataka history, only a few are stone slab inscriptions — the rest are inscribed on copper plates.\n\n*   Among the copper plate inscriptions, only a few belong to the **Vatsagulma (Basim)** branch; the majority belong to the **Nandivardhana–Pravarapura** branch.\n*   Vakataka inscriptions are written in **Sanskrit** language and **Brahmi** script.\n\n**Inscriptions of the Nandivardhana–Pravarapura Branch Rulers**\n\n| Inscription | Details |\n|---|---|\n| Mandhal copper plate | Issued by Rudrasena in his fifth regnal year; indicates his affection for Vaishnavism |\n| Indore, Yavatmal, Masod, Mandhal | Inscriptions of Pravarasena II. Masod inscriptions were issued while granting land to 19 Brahmins; Yavatmal inscriptions record the re-allotment of already-donated land |\n| Mandthal, Maharjari | Inscriptions of Prithvisena II |\n| Miregaon | Issued by Prabhavatigupta during the reign of Pravarasena II; found near Poona in 1912 |\n\nThe Miregaon inscriptions are an important source for Vakataka history: Prabhavati Gupta is learnt to have been the **wife of Rudrasena II** and the **daughter of Gupta ruler Chandra Gupta II**. In these inscriptions, she describes herself as the mother of Vakataka Maharajasri Damodarasena and Pravarasena. These inscriptions serve as the main source for determining Vakataka chronology on the basis of contemporaneity between the Gupta and Vakataka rulers.\n\n**Vatsagulma Branch Inscriptions**\n\nThe Basim inscriptions of Vakataka King Vindhyasakti II, issued in his 37th regnal year and found in 1939 in the Akola District of Vidarbha, were the first to throw light on the Vatsagulma branch. Before this discovery, historians held the opinion that there was only one Vakataka branch. According to Prof. V.V. Mirasi, after the death of Pravarasena I, the Vakatakas divided into four branches — Gouthamiputra established the Nandivardhana–Pravarapura branch, and Sarvasena started the Vatsagulma branch. Mirasi has stated that nothing is known about the other two branches.\n\n**Hisse–Borala Stone Inscription of Devasena**: Found near Basim in the Akola District. It records that Arya Svamilladeva, an official of Devasena, constructed the Sudarsana lake. Devasena, in his fifth regnal year, also issued Bidar inscriptions granting a village near Bidar.\n\n**Ramtek Inscription of Prabhavati Gupta**: Praises the chivalry of Prabhavati Gupta; issued by her son and her daughter, whose name is not known.\n\n### Coins\n\n*   Contemporary neighbouring dynasties — Kadambas, Vishnukundus, and Ikshvakus — issued their own coins, but the Vakatakas appear not to have issued their own coinage; very few of their coins have been found.\n*   It is assumed the Vakatakas allowed the use of coins minted by other contemporary ruling dynasties. Given their friendly relations with the Gupta kings and Vishnukundus, it is thought they may have used Gupta or Vishnukundu coinage.\n*   Saka Kshatrapa silver coins have been found in the Vakataka Kingdom, both separately and in heaps — from which scholars infer that the Vakatakas permitted the use of Saka Kshatrapa coins in their kingdom.\n*   Silver punch-marked coins (Karshapanas) remained in circulation in India until the medieval period. Since Satavahana and Kshatrapa coins are found in Vakataka territory, P.S. Agrawala opined these coins remained in circulation through the Vakataka period.\n*   Fahien, the Chinese Buddhist traveler (400–414 CE) who visited India during Gupta rule, recorded that people used **cowries** as a medium of exchange.\n*   Some coins of the Nandivardhana–Pravarapura rulers, minted from cheap metals, have been found recently at Paunar near Wardha. On copper coins of Prithvisena II, \"Sri Maharaja Prithvi\" is inscribed in Brahmi script; on coins of a ruler regarded as Narendrasena, \"Sri Rendra\" is inscribed in Brahmi script.\n\n### Material Remains\n\nPhysical remains of Vakataka times have been found at Paunar, Mansar, Nandapur, Ramtek, and Nagara.\n\n*   Excavations carried out in 1967 at Paunar (Pravarapuram) by the Ancient Indian History and Archaeology Department of Nagpur University brought many historical aspects to light, including the material culture of the period.\n*   Habitations existed at Paunar as early as 1000 BCE, before the Vakatakas; by the time of the Vakatakas, Paunar had developed into a political, trade, and cultural center. Coins ranging from ancient punch-marked types to those of the modern Bonsles were found in these excavations.\n*   Excavated buildings followed well-laid plans; finds include bricks of house-tops, round wells, black-and-red earthenware pottery, various implements, household articles, jewellery, and a thread worn round the neck to ward off evil spirits.\n*   Sculptures found include a Durga statue (Mandthal), a statue of Kevala Narasimha in a sitting posture, Vishnu lying on Sesha, and a Buddha image — indicating the dress style, hair decoration, and social and religious beliefs of the period.\n*   The paintings of Buddha and Bodhisattvas in **Caves XVI and XVII at Ajanta**, renowned across the country, stand as symbols of the arts of the period; these were carved under the patronage of Vakataka kings and their feudatories.\n\n### Literary Sources\n\n*   Though the **Puranas** are religion-oriented works, they provide information about ancient India''s royal dynasties and reign periods. Genealogies of royal dynasties appear in the **Vayu, Matsya, Vishnu, Brahamanda, and Bhagavata Puranas**, part of the 18 Puranas — this information is said to be based on the **Bhavishya Purana**. The Puranic account of these dynasties draws to a close with the emergence of the Gupta dynasty.\n*   The Puranas state that the royal dynasty **''Vindhyakula''** was established by Vindhyasakti, and that this dynasty was impure Yavanas. The Puranas also mention that Pravara, son of Vindhyasakti, extended the empire and performed four Asvamedha sacrifices.\n*   **Vistrutha Charitra**, part of Dandi''s _Dasakumara Charitra_, describes in detail how the downfall of the Vakataka Kingdom took place — narrating the conspiracies hatched by the feudatories after Harisena, and the weaknesses of the Vakataka kings. Though written a century after the decline of the Vakataka Kingdom, it remains a valuable account.\n*   The writings of **Kalidasa**, who lived during the time of Chandragupta II, focus on the social, economic, and cultural conditions of the period. Ramagiri, mentioned in Kalidasa''s _Meghadutam_, was identified as ''Ramtek'' by Prof. Mirasi.\n*   The poetical work **''Setubandham''**, written by Vakataka ruler Pravarasena II, also reflects the events of those times.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p3", "partName": "Original Home of the Vakatakas", "bodyMd": "Scholars are not unanimous about the original home of the Vakataka royal family, and definite sources locating it beyond doubt are not available. Two main opinions exist — one placing it in north India, the other in south India.\n\n### North India Argument\n\n*   **K.P. Jayaswal**, following Puranic references, located the origin of the Vakatakas in the Bundelkhand–Bagelkhand region (the Vindhya region), specifically near the Kilakila river close to Panna in Bundelkhand. He derived the name \"Vakataka\" from a village called ''Vakat'' or ''Bagat'' in the Jhansi District. He further linked this to the identification of Rudradeva, mentioned in Samudragupta''s Allahabad Pillar Inscription, with Rudrasena I of the Vakataka dynasty.\n*   The Puranas regard the Vakatakas as ''Vindyakas'', with their first ruler named as Vindhyasakti, whose capital was ''Purika'' or ''Chanaka''; Pravara, son of Vindhyasakti, is said to have ruled for 60 years. Identifying Pravara with Pravarasena and Purika with ''Rikshavath'' near the Vindhya mountains, **V.A. Smith** opined that Central India (the Vindhya region) was the original home of the Vakatakas.\n*   In the Nimad District of Madhya Pradesh, four villages carry the name ''Vakad''; since ''Vakatakanam'' in Vakataka inscriptions meant the Vakataka region, **S.V. Sohani** opined that Madhya Pradesh was the first home of the Vakatakas.\n*   Basing his argument on the Puranas, **D.C. Sircar** tried to prove that Eastern Malwa (Vidisa) was the original home of the Vakatakas.\n*   None of the Vakataka inscriptions have been found in northern India. The inscription of Rudrasena I was found at Devtek in Chanda District — suggesting the Vakatakas first established their kingdom in the Vidarbha region, south of the Vindhya mountains. **H.C. Roychoudhary** has stated that the Berar region was the first habitation of the Vakatakas.\n\n### South India Argument\n\n*   **Prof. V.V. Mirasi** opined that South India was the original home of the Vakatakas. He cited an Amaravati inscription of the third century CE, which records a Gahapati (Gruhapati) named ''Vakataka'' who, with his two wives, relatives, and friends, visited the Anala Stupa and made donations for their health and welfare. Since \"Vakataka\" means \"habitant of Vakataka village\", Mirasi felt the village ''Vakata'' might be located in the Amaravati region, and that this Vakataka Gahapati may have been the founder of the Vakataka dynasty. \n*   Other historians counter that, since Amaravati — like Sanchi and Barhut — was a famous Buddhist centre, pilgrims may have come from places like Rajagriha and Pataliputra to visit it, so there is no firm basis for claiming the Vakataka Gahapati actually originated from near Amaravati.\n*   Basing his argument on the similarity of technical terms in the **Basim copper plate inscriptions** of Vakataka King Vindhyasakti II and the Hirahadagalli and **Mydavolu inscriptions** of Pallava Sivaskanda Varma, Mirasi held that the Pallava and Vakataka kingdoms were neighbouring kingdoms in South India. The Basim copper plates refer to Vakataka King Vindhyasakti as ''Haritiputra'' — a title otherwise held only by the Kadambas (Karnataka) and Badami Chalukyas. These inscriptions also praise Vakataka rulers Pravarasena I, Sarvasena, and Vindhyasakti with the title ''**Dharma Maharaja**''.\n*   The **Ghatotkacha Cave Inscription** of Vakataka minister Varahadeva states that ''Vallur'' Brahmins (Hastibhoja, Varahadeva, etc.), belonging to South India, had served as ministers for generations. Mirasi identified this Vallur as the ''Velur'' village in present-day Karimnagar District.\n*   Mirasi held that ''Paunar'', 8 km from Wardha, was the capital of the Vakatakas, built by **Pravarasena II** — a view supported by many historians, since five inscriptions of Pravarasena II have been issued from there.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p4", "partName": "Chronology", "bodyMd": "There is no unanimity among historians regarding the ruling period of the Vakatakas.\n\n*   Dr. Buhler held that the Vakatakas belonged to the fifth century CE; Dr. Fleet and Kielhorn held that they belonged to the eighth century CE.\n*   The discovery of the Prabhavati Gupta inscriptions near Poona brought historians closer to unanimity on Vakataka chronology. These inscriptions revealed that Prabhavati Gupta was the daughter of Chandragupta II — and on the basis of this contemporaneity between the Vakatakas and the Guptas, it became possible to fix the Vakataka chronology more firmly.\n*   The **copper plate inscriptions** of Vakataka King Vindhyasakti II, found in 1939 at Basim (Vasim) in the Akola District of Vidarbha, added further information. Until this discovery, it was believed that the Vakatakas had only one branch; but based on the **Basim inscriptions**, it was observed that four branches emerged after the death of Pravarasena I.\n*   Overall, it may be said that the Vakatakas ruled from **250 CE to 650 CE**, a period of roughly three centuries.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p5", "partName": "Caste", "bodyMd": "*   The Puranas make no mention of the caste of the Vakatakas, though they state that the Vindhyakula royal dynasty was established by Vindhyasakti, whose son Pravara performed four Asvamedha sacrifices.\n*   Historians hold the opinion that the ''Vindhyakulu'' and ''Pravarulu'' mentioned in the Puranas, and the Vakatakas mentioned in inscriptions, are one and the same. Others hold that, just as \"Satavahana\" could denote a region or family name, \"Vindhyakulu\" might be the name of a place — since it is common for a family to take its name from its area.\n*   On the basis of the **Ajanta stone inscription**, it has been held that the Vakatakas belonged to the Greek race, and further that — like the Sunga and Kanva rulers — they performed certain rites and obtained brahmanhood, declaring themselves Brahmanas in their inscriptions. It is described in inscriptions that Vindhyasakti was a Brahmin belonging to the ''Vishnu Vruddha'' gotra.\n*   The Vakatakas maintained matrimonial relations with contemporary royal dynasties such as the **Guptas, Nagas, Vishnukundus, and Kadambas.**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p6", "partName": "Political Developments — Pravarapura–Nandivardhana Branch", "bodyMd": "### Vindhyasakti I (250 – 270 CE)\n\nVindhyasakti is named in the Puranas as the founder of the Vakataka dynasty, and is also mentioned in the Ajanta inscriptions — the XVI cave inscription describes him as ''**Vakataka Vamsadhvaja**'' and ''**dvija**''.\n\n*   The Puranas describe him as the ruler of the Vidisa region (near Bilsa and Bhopal) and Purika (Vindhya region).\n*   He ruled over the Nagpur and Berar regions as a **feudatory of the Satavahanas** and did not assume any royal titles.\n\n### Pravarasena I (270 – 330 CE)\n\nAfter the death of his father Vindhyasakti, Pravarasena succeeded to the throne. The Puranas state that he ruled for sixty years, and he is regarded as the **real founder of the Vakataka Empire**.\n\n*   His kingdom included northern Maharashtra, Berar, Madhya Pradesh, South Kosala, and Telangana areas.\n*   He occupied Purika territory, earlier ruled by Sisuka.\n*   A follower of Vedic religion, he performed Vedic rites such as Vajapeya, Athiratra, and Agnistoma, along with **four Asvamedha sacrifices**.\n*   In the **Basim inscription of Vindhyasakti II**, he is referred to as ''Samrat'' and ''Dharma Maharaja''; he also declared himself ''**Haritiputra**'' and made large-scale grants to Brahmins.\n\n**Sons of Pravarasena I** According to the Puranas, Pravarasena I had four sons.\n\n*   His eldest son, **Gouthamiputra**, was married to the daughter of Bhavanaga, ruler of the Bharasivanaga dynasty of the Padmavati Kingdom — but Gouthamiputra predeceased his father.\n*   His second son, **Sarvasena**, established the Vatsagulma branch of the Vakataka dynasty.\n*   Neither the names of the other two sons nor the dynasties they may have established are known.\n\n### Pravarapura – Nandivardhana Branch Ruler Table\n\n| Ruler | Reign | Key notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rudrasena I | 330 – 355 CE | Grandson of Pravarasena I (son of Gouthamiputra); capitals at Pravarapura and Nandivardhana |\n| Prithivisena I | 355 – 380 CE | Ruled 25 years; peaceful reign |\n| Rudrasena II | 380 – 385 CE | Adopted Vaishnavism under Prabhavati Gupta''s influence |\n| Divakarasena | 385 – 400 CE | Minor king; Prabhavati Gupta acted as regent |\n| Damodarasena (Pravarasena II) | 400 – 440 CE | Also called Pravarasena II; built Pravarapura city |\n| Narendrasena | 440 – 460 CE | Married Kadamba princess Ajjitabhattarika |\n| Prithvisena II | 460 – 480 CE | Last king of the main branch |\n\n### Rudrasena I (330 – 355 CE)\n\nAfter Pravarasena I, his grandson (son of Gouthamiputra), Rudrasena I, came to the throne.\n\n*   He ruled with Pravarapura (Paunar) in Wardha District and Nandivardhanam in Nagapur District as his capitals.\n*   He maintained relations with the Guptas. His only stone inscription is found at Chandrapur.\n*   Since Bhavanaga of Bharasivanaga is referred to in Vakataka inscriptions, it is thought Rudrasena I may have received Bharasivanaga support.\n*   Rudrasena faced several political convulsions — the Vakataka Kingdom got divided into four parts due to the opposition of his uncles, and Vakataka authority became blurred for a time. With the help of his maternal grandfather, the ruler of Padmavati Kingdom, he restored the earlier glory of the Vakataka state.\n\n**Historiographical Debate — Rudrasena I and Rudradeva**\n\n1.  **K.P. Jayaswal''s view**: Rudrasena I was the same as Rudradeva, one of the nine rulers of Aryavarta defeated by Samudragupta.\n2.  **A.S. Altekar''s counter-view**: Rudrasena and Rudradeva were not one and the same, but different rulers.\n3.  **Mirasi''s view**: There is no evidence that the Vakatakas remained subordinate to Samudragupta — the Vakatakas did not use the Gupta era in their inscriptions, but their own regnal years. There is also no reference to Vakataka rulers in Samudragupta''s Allahabad Pillar Inscription, suggesting Samudragupta had no enmity with the Vakatakas and instead maintained friendly relations with them — with this strength he may have defeated the Western Kshatrapas.\n\nRudrasena I was a devotee of **Mahabhairava**. During his last days, Vindhyasena, the Basim (Vatsagulma branch) ruler, conquered the Kuntala Kingdom and Bagelakhand.\n\n### Prithivisena I (355 – 380 CE)\n\nRudrasena I was succeeded by his son Prithivisena I, who ruled for 25 years. He was truthful, charitable, knowledgeable, and versatile, and his reign remained peaceful.\n\n*   During his reign, Chandragupta II, the Gupta ruler, defeated the Saka Kshatrapas of Malwa with the help of Rudrasena II.\n*   To strengthen the bond between the two dynasties, Chandragupta II married his daughter **Prabhavati Gupta** to Rudrasena II.\n*   Crown prince Prithivisena shifted the Vakataka capital to Nandivardhana, near Nagpur.\n\n### Rudrasena II (380 – 385 CE)\n\nPrithivisena I was succeeded by his son Rudrasena II, who died within a short period of four or five years.\n\n*   Unlike his ancestors, he did not embrace Saivism; under the influence of his wife Prabhavati Gupta, he adopted Vaishnavism.\n*   During the time of Chandragupta II, Gupta dominance over the Vakataka Kingdom increased, and the Vakataka Kingdom became almost like a feudatory state of the Guptas.\n\n### Divakarasena and Damodarasena (385 – 440 CE)\n\nRudrasena II had two sons — **Divakarasena** and **Damodarasena**.\n\n*   Prabhavati Gupta kept her minor son Divakarasena on the throne and herself acted as regent.\n*   After Divakarasena''s death, Damodarasena was placed on the throne. In this way, acting as regent for her two sons, Prabhavati Gupta discharged administrative responsibilities for **13 years**.\n*   In the **Poona inscriptions**, Prabhavati Gupta declared herself the mother of Divakarasena and Damodarasena, and also declared herself the daughter of Devagupta — historians hold the opinion that Devagupta was none other than Chandragupta II Vikramaditya.\n\n### Pravarasena II (400 – 440 CE)\n\nDamodarasena himself carried the alternate name **Pravarasena II**.\n\n*   His seventeen copper plate inscriptions are found at places such as Amaravati, Wardha, Bitul, Nagapur, Chipndwara, Bhandara, and Balaghat in the Vidarbha region.\n*   The entire Berar area was included in his kingdom.\n*   He built **Pravarapura** city and made it his capital — identified as Paunar in Wardha District.\n*   He was a Saiva devotee who showed tolerance towards other faiths.\n*   A poet and scholar, he wrote the poetic work **''Setubandha''** in Maharashtra Prakrit.\n*   In his new capital, Pravarapura, he built a notable temple for Sreeramachandra.\n\n### Narendrasena (440 – 460 CE)\n\nPravarasena II was succeeded by his son Narendrasena.\n\n*   He married the Kuntala princess **Ajjitabhattarika**, daughter of the Kadamba king Kakutsa Varma.\n*   The rulers of Kosala and Mekala (the kingdom near the Amarakantthaka mountains), and of Malwa, accepted his sovereign authority.\n*   Narendrasena, with the help of the Kadambas, recovered the lost areas and averted further decline of the Vakataka Kingdom. He also occupied territories of the Nala Kingdom in Bastar (Chhattisgarh).\n\n### Prithvisena II (460 – 480 CE)\n\nNarendrasena was succeeded by his son Prithvisena II, the **last king of the main branch** of the Vakatakas.\n\n*   His **Balaghat inscription** states that he restored Vakataka Lakshmi (fortune) twice.\n*   Harisena of the Vatsagulma branch and Bhavadatta Varma of the Nala dynasty are thought to have carried out these raids.\n*   Madhava Varma of the Vishnukund dynasty from Andhra and Telangana also raided and defeated Prithvisena.\n*   Prithvisena gave his daughter in marriage to Madhava Varma; as Prithvisena had no sons, Madhava Varma also received the Vakataka Kingdom.\n*   **Harisena''s Ajanta inscription** mentions the Asmaka Kingdom among the feudatory states subordinate to him. It is learnt that the Vishnukunds themselves fought on Harisena''s side in overthrowing the main Vakataka dynasty.\n*   Coins of the Vishnukunds have been found on a large scale in South Maharashtra, Nagapur, Vidarbha, and Nasik. Vikramendra Bhattaraka Varma II, son of Madhava Varma, declared himself in his inscriptions to be the son of Vakataka Mahadevi, thus linked to both dynasties, and described himself as a very pious person and great poet.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p7", "partName": "Vakataka — Vatsagulma Branch", "bodyMd": "| Ruler | Reign | Key notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sarvasena | 330 – 355 CE | Founder of the Vatsagulma branch; author of ''Harivijayam'' |\n| Vindhyasena | 355 – 400 CE | Referred to as Vindhyasakti in Basim inscriptions |\n| Pravarasena II | 400 – 415 CE | Succeeded by an eight-year-old son whose name is unknown |\n| Devasena | 450 – 475 CE | Entrusted administration to minister Hastibhoja |\n| Harisena | 475 – 500 CE | Greatest ruler of the branch |\n\n### Sarvasena (330 – 355 CE)\n\nThe Basim copper plate inscriptions and Ajanta inscriptions, found in 1939, provide information about the Vatsagulma branch.\n\n*   Sarvasena, the younger brother of Pravarasena I, founded this branch, making **Basim (Vatsagulma)** in the Akola District of Vidarbha (Berar) his capital.\n*   He conquered the area between the Sahyadri mountains and the river Godavari.\n*   A great patron of literature and a poet himself, he bore the title ''Dharma Maharaja'' and authored **''Harivijayam''**, a work in Prakrit.\n\n### Vindhyasena (355 – 400 CE)\n\n*   Basim inscriptions refer to him as Vindhyasakti; he issued these inscriptions in his 27th regnal year.\n*   It is learnt that Pravara, his minister, defeated the king of Kuntala in the south.\n\n### Pravarasena II (400 – 415 CE)\n\nAfter Vindhyasena, his son Pravarasena II ascended the throne. After his death, his eight-year-old son came to the throne, but sources giving information about the son''s name or rule are not available.\n\n### Devasena (450 – 475 CE)\n\nThe Ghatotkacha cave inscription of Varahadeva II described Devasena as ''Devaraja''.\n\n*   He spent his time in luxuries but entrusted the administration of his kingdom to his able minister, Hastibhoja.\n*   Swamilladeva, an official of Devasena, built a water source called ''Sudarsana'' near Basim.\n\n### Harisena (475 – 500 CE)\n\nAfter Devasena, his son Harisena became ruler — the **greatest ruler among all those of the Vatsagulma branch**. During his period, the name and fame of the Vakataka Kingdom reached its highest point.\n\n**Conquests**\n\n*   Ghurjara (Gujarat)\n*   Malwa\n*   South Kosala (Chhattisgarh)\n*   Kalinga\n*   Asmaka (Telangana)\n*   Kuntala (Southern Maharashtra)\n\n**Extent of Empire**\n\n*   His empire extended from Malwa in the north to Kuntala in the south, and from the Arabian Sea in the west to the Bay of Bengal in the east.\n*   In India, no other contemporary kingdom had such a huge expanse of territory.\n*   Without annexing the conquered kingdoms, he permitted them to continue independent rule on condition that they pay yearly tribute to him.\n\n**Administration and Legacy**\n\n*   He earned a name not only as a conqueror but also as an administrator.\n*   **Varahadeva**, son of Hastibhoja, was his minister, and got Cave 16 excavated at Ajanta; Varahadeva''s inscription has become the main source for knowledge about the Vatsagulma branch.\n\n**Decline After Harisena**\n\n*   After Harisena, the Vakataka Kingdom became weak. Though one or two of his heirs ruled after him, their names are not known.\n*   The Rushika or Asika (Asmaka) region (Karimnagar, Adilabad, and Nizamabad Districts) was ruled by the Vishnukundus as feudatories of the Vakatakas.\n*   At this time, the Kadambas from Karnataka, the Kalachuri kings from northern Maharashtra, and the Nala dynasty rulers from Bastar made raids and occupied several parts of Vakataka territory.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p8", "partName": "Administrative System", "bodyMd": "Inscriptions are the main source of information on Vakataka administration. The Vakatakas established a stable and effective administrative system.\n\n*   They regarded themselves as the father of their people and did not consider themselves divine-right monarchs; kings did not become autocratic and generally governed according to the Smritis and Dharmasastras.\n*   Vakataka administration was **centralized**. Since transport facilities were not well developed, some decentralization became necessary.\n\n**Administrative Divisions**\n\n*   The empire was divided into **states or provinces** — inscriptions mention Bhojakata State, Arammi State, and Varuchi State.\n*   Each province was divided into **Vishayas**.\n*   Vishayas were divided into **Aharas** and **Bhogas/Bhuktis** (the precise difference between Ahara and Bhoga/Bhukti is not known).\n*   Each Bhoga contained cities, towns, and villages.\n*   In some areas, Vishayas were divided into **Pattes** and **Margas** — the term ''Marga'' is not found in Satavahana or Gupta inscriptions.\n*   Village administration was carried out by village officials; whenever kings made land grants, they used to assemble and inform the village officials. The term ''Gramakuta'' in inscriptions is understood to mean village elders.\n\n**Ministry and Officials**\n\n*   There are not many references to a Mantri Parishat (council of ministers) in Vakataka inscriptions.\n*   Officials named in inscriptions include Mantri, Sachiva, Sarvadhyaksha, Senapati, and Dandanayaka, along with Bhatulu, Chatrulu, Rajukas, Gramakuta, Kulaputra, and Devarika.\n\n**Revenue**\n\n*   Land tax was the main source of government revenue — generally 1/4th to 1/6th of the crop yield was collected as tax.\n*   The government also earned revenue from direct taxes, export and import duties, and held a monopoly over the manufacture of salt and income from liquor manufacture.\n*   Lands granted to scholars, Brahmins, and temples enjoyed exemption from some taxes.\n*   Employees were also given lands in place of salaries.\n*   When government officials visited villages, it was customary for them to be provided with daily necessities — free and compulsory services were required of villagers.\n*   Income from taxes was spent on army maintenance, wars, state administration, and temple construction.\n\n**Military and Judicial System**\n\n*   The Vakatakas maintained a large army; the Senapati supervised military matters.\n*   Vakataka inscriptions mention three kinds of feudatory states: \n\n*   those who surrendered out of fear of invasion\n*   those who surrendered as a result of invasion\n*   those states that were conquered in war.\n\n*   Feudatories looked after law and order in their area, maintained an army, and supplied it to the ruler in times of war.\n*   In judicial organization, the king remained the highest authority; judgments were based on the Smritis and customs. At the village level, village officials decided cases.\n*   The term ''Sadanda-Vigraha'' in **Vakataka inscriptions** suggests that fines were imposed for small offences, possibly collected in cash.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p9", "partName": "Economic Conditions", "bodyMd": "*   Internal and external trade, which had flourished under the Satavahanas, declined by the time of the Vakatakas; Srenis (guilds) and towns suffered. The decline of trade between Rome and India is also reflected in the scarcity of Vakataka coins.\n*   Vakataka rulers encouraged agriculture and provided irrigation facilities — dams, canals, and reservoirs were built.\n*   The Hisse–Borala inscription of Vakataka king Devasena states that his official Swamilladeva built the Sudarsana lake. The Ganj Stone inscription of Vyagharadeva also mentions the construction of a reservoir named ''Viraka''.\n*   Vakataka rulers increasingly gave land grants to scholars, Brahmins, government officials, soldiers, and village mahattaras; for the sake of good rewards for their ancestors, they also gave away cows, elephants, horses, and villages, along with special facilities and exemptions for donated villages.\n*   **Basim inscriptions of Vindhyasakti II** list conditions attached to such grants: king''s servants were prohibited from entering the granted villages, some tax exemptions were given, and recipients of land grants were ordained not to commit offences against the ruler or harm other villages.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p10", "partName": "Social Conditions", "bodyMd": "*   In Hindu society, besides the four varnas, several mixed castes emerged; during Vakataka times, castes had not taken strong roots, and varnas also started losing their purity.\n*   Marriages were mostly celebrated among persons of the same caste — for example, Rudrasena II, a Brahmin, married Prabhavati Gupta, daughter of Chandragupta II.\n*   The Vakatakas maintained relations with the Kadambas and Vishnukundus. Even though the Vakatakas were Brahmins by origin, they followed the Kshatriya profession and were called **Brahma Kshatriyas**.\n*   In this age, respect for Brahmins increased; they not only became scholars but also earned fame as administrators. The Vakatakas employed Brahmins as ministers — kings of the Vatsagulma branch employed Brahmins such as Hastibhoja and Varahadeva as ministers.\n*   Women like Prabhavati Gupta took up administrative responsibilities.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p11", "partName": "Religious Conditions", "bodyMd": "Both Vedic religion and Buddhism flourished in the Vakataka kingdom. The majority of Vakataka rulers were **Saivites**, and some were Vaishnavas.\n\n*   **Pravarasena II**, due to the influence of his mother, queen Prabhavati Gupta, converted to Vaishnavism; he was described as a devotee of Chakrapani and as ''Paramabhagavata''. Inscriptions describe Prabhavati Gupta herself as ''**Atyanta Bhagavadbhakta**'' and ''**Paramabhagavata**''.\n*   **Prithvisena II** was also a Vaishnava. Other Vakataka rulers such as **Pravarasena I, Rudrasena I, and Prithivisena I** were Saivas. \n*   Rudrasena I was a devotee of Mahabhairava; Prithivisena I was a devotee of Mahesvara and built a Siva temple known as Dharmasthana, dedicated to his beloved goddess. Pravarasena II was a devotee of Sambhu and built a Ramachandra temple in his capital, Pravarapuram.\n*   Temple construction went on briskly during Vakataka times — at Ramtek (Maharashtra), the Ramachandra temple, Varaha temple, Vamanavatara (Trivikrama) temple, and Kevala Narasimha temple were built.\n*   While the rulers of the main Vakataka branch followed Vedic religion, those belonging to the Vatsagulma branch were followers of **Buddhism**. The rulers of the Anoopa and Rishika regions, feudatories of the Vatsagulma branch, patronized Buddhism.\n*   **Varahadeva**, minister of Harisena, was a supporter of Buddhism and got cave temples cut at Ajanta and Ghatotkacha for the sake of Buddhists.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p12", "partName": "Educational and Literary Development", "bodyMd": "Sanskrit and Prakrit literatures flourished during the time of the Vakatakas. The Vakatakas issued their inscriptions in Sanskrit, and the rulers themselves not only patronized literature but also wrote poetical works and works on morals.\n\n*   Dandi particularly praised the Sanskrit style of **Vaidarbhi** associated with Vakataka times.\n*   Vakataka king Sarvasena wrote **''Harivijayam''**, a poetical work in Prakrit, along with some Prakrit stories (verses). Because of his efforts, Vatsagulma flourished as an important educational and cultural centre. ''Harivijayam'' — praised by Dandi — narrates the story of Srikrishna obtaining the Parijatam tree from heaven; though the work itself is not available today, later writers referenced events narrated in it.\n*   **''Setubandham''**, a poetical work in Prakrit regarded as written by Pravarasena II, was praised by Banabhat and Ramadas as the most famous poetical work of its time, containing 15 aswasas (chapters). Kshemendra, in his work ''Ayochitya Vichara Charcha'', also praised it as most wonderful. Since Pravarasena mentioned Kalidasa''s name at the end of each aswasa in ''Setubandhu'', some scholars regard it as a combined work of both Pravarasena and Kalidasa.\n*   Some scholars hold that Kalidasa was sent to the Vakataka Kingdom by the Gupta ruler Chandragupta II, and that at Ramagiri, Kalidasa wrote **''Meghasandesam'' (Meghadutam)**; Ramagiri was identified with present-day Ramtek by V.V. Mirasi.\n*   Pravarasena, like his grandfather and mother, was a **paramabhagavata** and a devotee of Rama. His poetical work mentions the episode of Sriramachandra''s raid on Sri Lanka, referencing the mountains along Rama''s route — Vindhya, Sahya, Malaya, and Mahendragiri.\n*   During Vakataka times, Pravarapura (Paunar) and Vatsagulma (Basim) flourished as great educational centres; Brahmadeyas and Agraharas emerged as centres of Vedic study.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p13", "partName": "Development of Architecture, Sculpture and Painting", "bodyMd": "The architecture and sculpture of the Vakatakas can be seen in their temples and the Ajanta caves. Structural temples and cave temples were built at places such as Paunar, Mansar, Nandapur, Ramtek, and Nagapur, showing the influence of Gupta architecture.\n\n*   At **Ramtek**, Vakataka rulers built the Ramachandra temple, Varaha temple, Vamanavatara (Trivikrama) temple, and Kevala Narasimha temple.\n*   **Pravarasena II** built the Ramachandra temple at Pravarapura. According to the Ramtek inscription, Rudrasena I built a Saiva temple known as ''Devasthanam'' at Chikkamburi.\n*   Vakataka sculptures are characterized by a long body, a lean and delicate waist, and strong upper parts — features resembling the Satavahana sculptures at Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda, and Jaggayyapeta. Sculptures at Paunar, Mandthal, Ramtek, and Jabalpore stand as symbols of Vakataka sculptural art. Nandivardhanam and Pravarapura flourished as the main art centres.\n*   In the 15th cave at Ajanta, Ganga-Yamuna sculptures were carved; in Cave numbers 16, 17, and 19, danceuse figures were carved in place of Ganga-Yamuna figures — the carving of danceuse figures in Buddhist sculpture began in Vakataka times.\n\n**Ajanta Sculptures and Paintings**\n\n*   Ajanta''s sculptures and paintings have been described by critics as an extraordinary art creation, continuing over approximately seven centuries. There are 30 caves at Ajanta, unexpectedly recognized by **John Smith in 1919**; some caves belonged to the Theravada tradition and others to Mahayana.\n*   Buddhist monks **Buddhabhadra, Madhuradasudu, and Jaranta Dharmadatta** also carved out Buddha statues at Ajanta. Vakataka-period paintings are also found at Aurangabad, Ghatotkacha (Aurangabad District), and Bagh (Madhya Pradesh). Inscriptions issued by the Vakataka kings and their feudatories are found throughout the Ajanta caves.\n\n| Cave No. | Associated With / Excavated By | Key Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cave 1 | — | Mahajanaka Jataka painted; Vihara has a Buddha statue in Dharmachakrapravartana pose, flanked by Padmapani and Vajrapani — the Padmapani (Avalokitesvara) painting is a masterpiece among Ajanta paintings |\n| Cave 16 | Excavated by Varahadeva, minister of Harisena | Considered the leading cave among all; depicts the end episodes in the life of Buddha along with Jataka stories |\n| Cave 17 | Cut by Upendra Gupta of the Rishika Kingdom, a feudatory of Harisena | ''Maha Hamsa Jatakam'' and ''Mathruposhaka Jatakam'' scenes; has a greater number of paintings than other caves, well-preserved without spots or damage |\n| Cave 19 | — | Scene of Buddha wandering as a beggar before Yasodhara and Rahul in Kapilavastu |\n| Cave 26 | — | Deepankara Buddha of the Deepankara Jataka carved several times; the ''Mahaparinirvana'' of Buddha also carved here |", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p14", "partName": "Conclusion", "bodyMd": "The Vakatakas were the first among the dynasties to rule the Deccan after the Satavahanas, with Adilabad, Karimnagar, and Nizamabad areas of Telangana among their territories. It is notable that the Vishnukunds, who later ruled independently in these areas, had been feudatories of the Vakatakas. \n\nThrough matrimonial relations with the Guptas in the north and the Kadambas in the south, the Vakatakas built strong foundations for stable rule and the maintenance of law and order across their empire. Prominence was given to people''s welfare and to the maintenance and capability of the army in administrative and economic affairs. In educational and cultural fields, steps were taken for the promotion of Sanskrit and Prakrit and the running of educational centres; Saiva, Vaishnava, and Buddhist religions all received patronage, and mixed castes emerged in society. \n\nSeveral Hindu temples at the capitals of Paunar and Basim, and Buddha''s life stories and Jataka stories, were brought out in highly striking modes of sculpture and painting. Hence, the Vakataka rule period stands memorable in Deccan history.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter30-mulki-movement', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter30-mulki-movement-p1", "partName": "Mulki and its Origins in the Bahmani Period", "bodyMd": "The word **mulk** means country or motherland, and a **mulki** is a native of that country. **Gair-mulki** (or non-mulki) refers to a foreigner or outsider. In the Deccan, mulkis were also understood as **swadeshis** (natives of the land), while non-mulkis were outsiders who came in and settled.\n\nThe mulki–non-mulki problem traces back to the Bahmani period.\n\n*   After the decline of the Kakatiyas, the Bahmanis ruled the Deccan.\n*   During the 14th century, the armies of the Khilji and Tughlaq clans of the Delhi Sultanate, along with many Muslims, migrated to the Deccan and settled there.\n*   These settlers came to be called **deccanies**.\n\nAround the same time, Muslims from West Asian countries — Iran, Iraq, Arabia, and Turkey — also came to India and settled in the Bahmani empire.\n\n*   They were called **Aphakis** (Afaqis), meaning non-local or foreigner.\n*   Over time, deccanies came to be regarded as mulkis, and aphakis as non-mulkis.\n\n**Conflict under the Bahmanis:**\n\n*   **Ahmed Shah**, the third Bahmani king, appointed **Mohammed Gavan**, an aphaki, as prime minister. Gavan carried out major administrative reforms and appointed fellow aphakis to prominent posts.\n*   Aphakis came to dominate trade and grew financially and socially powerful, also being hired into high state positions.\n*   This created conflict between the deccanies (mulkis) and aphakis (non-mulkis).\n*   Religious sectarian difference deepened the conflict: aphakis belonged to the **Shia** sect and deccanies to the **Sunni** sect of Islam.\n*   At Bidar, the Bahmani capital, deccani nobles had the 78-year-old prime minister **Khwaja Mahmud Gavan** assassinated on suspicion of treachery; his killing left the Bahmani kingdom shattered.\n*   The aphaki–deccani rivalry intensified until it culminated in the downfall of the Bahmani empire in 1527, which broke up into five successor kingdoms — one of which was the Qutub Shahi kingdom of Golconda.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter30-mulki-movement-p2", "partName": "Qutub Shahi Period — No Mulki Issue", "bodyMd": "The Qutub Shahi dynasty emerged from the Bahmani breakup. **Quli Qutub Shah**, its founder, was himself an aphaki (an immigrant from Iran). Having witnessed the Bahmani collapse caused by aphaki–deccani rivalry, he deliberately offered more jobs to deccanies (mulkis) and encouraged the local language, **Telugu**.\n\nFrom the late 14th century to the late 17th century, Golconda was the capital from which the Qutub Shahis ruled greater Andhra.\n\n*   **Miir Jumla** installed **Madanna** as prime minister in 1674.\n    \n*   He was succeeded by **Abul Hasan Tanisha**, who honoured him with the title **Suryaprakasarao**.\n    \n*   This was the first time in 300 years since Allauddin Khilji''s invasions that a Hindu had been made prime minister.\n    \n*   Madanna''s brother, **Akkanna**, was made revenue minister.\n    \n*   **Abul Hassan Tanisha** appointed Telugu people to important positions: **Akkanna** as peshkar and **Madanna** as mahamantri (prime minister).\n    \n*   Because the Qutub Shahis respected the local language and culture and hired locals extensively, the mulki issue did not arise during this period.\n    \n*   Mulki–non-mulki rivalry eventually caused Madanna''s assassination, which in turn shook the foundations of the Qutub Shahi kingdom.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter30-mulki-movement-p3", "partName": "Asaf Jahi Period — Emergence and Escalation of the Mulki Problem", "bodyMd": "In 1724, **Nizam-ul-Mulk** founded the Asaf Jahi dynasty at Hyderabad. He brought with him loyal followers, whom he made ministers and appointed to noble posts; their descendants settled permanently in the Deccan.\n\n*   The first Nizam brought with him trusted Muslims and Urdu-speaking Kayasthas, granting them large positions.\n*   Among these arrivals, **Abul Hasan Sher Khan** rose to become Nawab.\n*   Under later Nizams, **saranjams** (assignments) went to families such as **Mir Alam**, **Chandulal**, and **Imadul Mulk** — all originally outsiders.\n*   Before India''s independence, large parts of the Nizam''s territory — Karnataka, Uttar Sarkars, Rayalaseema, and Berar — were merged into British territory.\n*   Muslim officials serving in the erstwhile courts of Delhi, Murshidabad, and Lucknow, whose kingdoms had fallen, were dismissed by the British as \"disloyal\" once those regions came under British rule.\n*   Many of them moved to Hyderabad, treating the Nizam as heir to the Mughal emperor.\n*   They took up occupations such as medicine, contracting, and trade, and secured government jobs.\n*   Society under the Nizam''s **jagirdari system** was deeply layered: education was largely limited to the upper classes, who were reluctant to take up employment.\n*   Some who came seeking work chose to settle permanently in Hyderabad.\n*   Administration required staffing district offices, so people were specifically summoned and given jobs, while jagirdars themselves lived lives of leisure.\n*   Outsiders from beyond the state''s borders performed the harder administrative duties.\n*   This coexistence, ideally, ran without regard to religion or language acting as unifying strength — but mulki grievances were never fully resolved.\n*   The mulki problem intensified only from the time of **Afzal-ud-Daulah**, the fifth Nizam, marking the start of organised mulki movements.\n\nThe Asaf Jahi-era mulki–non-mulki conflict is studied in three phases.\n\n### Phase 1 (1853–1883): Salarjung-I\n\n**Background — a dispute of succession triggers the first rift:**\n\n*   Under the fifth Nizam, **Afzal-ud-Daulah**, and his prime minister **Salarjung-I**, disagreement arose over a key appointment.\n*   The Nizam wanted a particular person appointed, but Salarjung would not accept him; Salarjung, aggrieved, was ready to resign.\n*   The British, seeing Salarjung''s rising influence, were not entirely happy but could not risk removing him.\n*   When Salarjung appeared ready to resign, the Nizam was privately relieved that a long-standing matter might resolve itself.\n*   But Salarjung''s resignation was unwelcome to his own body of followers — in reality, all real power lay in his hands, and his supporters feared for their own positions if he left, so they worked out a plan together.\n*   The Nizam had a private weakness for fakirs and often acted on their advice.\n*   His followers bribed a fakir close to the Nizam to speak in Salarjung''s favour.\n*   When the Nizam raised the matter before this fakir, the fakir praised Salarjung as a good man and urged the Nizam to reinstate him at once — and the plan worked.\n*   The man Salarjung had originally proposed got the post, Salarjung''s authority remained unchallenged, and the Nizam himself grew more attached to Salarjung as a result.\n*   From this point on, sympathies split between followers loyal to the Nizam and followers loyal to Salarjung, and the mulki–non-mulki conflict began in earnest.\n\n**Reforms and migration into Hyderabad:**\n\n*   In 1853, **Salarjung-I** was appointed Diwan of Hyderabad and carried out extensive administrative and revenue reforms.\n*   These reforms drew **Kayasthas** and **Khatris** from North India to Hyderabad in search of jobs.\n*   **Sayyed Ahmed Khan** and **Aminuddin Khan**, Salarjung''s assistants, played a key role in facilitating the arrival of non-mulkis.\n*   English-knowing personnel were brought in from Madras Presidency for administrative work and given respectable posts.\n*   After the **collapse of the Mughal empire** following the **1857 Sepoy Mutiny**, many who lost jobs in North India migrated to Hyderabad in search of employment.\n*   Though Salarjung himself was reluctant to employ non-mulkis, certain unavoidable circumstances forced him to appoint educated men from **Aligarh University**.\n*   Officers trained and experienced in British India were hired as Diwanis.\n*   Salarjung''s original expectation — that skilled outside employees would train locals and then return home — did not materialise.\n*   Non-mulkis in high positions unnecessarily split their departments to create new posts, filling them with friends and relatives brought from North India.\n*   This bred growing resentment of gair-mulkis (non-mulkis) among the mulki population.\n\n**Salarjung''s countermeasures:** Fearing that a rising non-mulki population would affect Deccan''s culture and politics, Salarjung took several measures:\n\n*   Non-mulki officers required special permission to meet the Nizam''s family, the Diwan, the British Resident, or other nawabs.\n*   Non-mulki officers could not be granted titles or jagirs.\n*   Non-mulkis were kept out of army recruitment to prevent their domination of the armed forces.\n*   He rejected the proposal to replace Persian with Urdu as the official language.\n*   He famously told pressuring North Indian Muslim officials that Persian was a symbol of Islam''s historic triumph, and that as long as he lived, Persian would live on in Hyderabad even if it had died out elsewhere.\n*   Salarjung would remark that though non-mulkis might be skilled, they could not claim the same status as locals who had been loyal to the Nizam for generations.\n\n**First Mulki Orders (1868):** With the aim of educating mulkis and securing them government jobs, Salarjung-I issued the first-ever order in **1868** confining employment in all administrative offices of Hyderabad to mulkis, during the reign of the fifth Nizam, Afzal-ud-Daulah.\n\n### Phase 2 (1884–1911): Peak Conflict under Mir Mahboob Ali Khan\n\nThe mulki–non-mulki conflict reached its peak during the reign of the sixth Nizam, **Mir Mahboob Ali Khan**.\n\n*   Salarjung-I died in **1883**; **Salarjung-II** became Diwan and was biased in favour of non-mulkis.\n*   Under pressure from non-mulkis, Salarjung-II replaced **Persian** with **Urdu** as the official language on **21 February 1884**.\n*   He also made **English** compulsory, driven by British interests.\n*   Making Urdu official and English compulsory reduced job opportunities for mulkis, who were not proficient in either language.\n*   Non-mulkis were also paid higher salaries than mulkis, and mulkis came to be treated as second-class citizens.\n*   Mulkis submitted a memorandum to Mahboob Ali Khan alleging Salarjung-II''s bias, known as the **\"Mulki Paper.\"**\n*   The Nizam responded by ordering that a list of employees be prepared based on their nativity.\n\n**Employees'' Civil List, 1886:**\n\n| Birthplace | Employees | Percentage | Salary Share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hyderabad State | 246 | 52% | 42% |\n| British India (total) | 230 | 48% | 58% |\n\nBreakdown of the 230 employees recruited from British India:\n\n| Source Region | Employees | Percentage | Salary Share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Madras & Bombay presidencies (excl. below) | 97 | 20% | 24% |\n| Madras Presidency | 66 | 14% | 11% |\n| Bombay Presidency | 36 | 8% | 8% |\n| Other European countries | 24 | 5% | 13% |\n| **Total (non-mulki)** | **230** | **48%** | **58%** |\n\nWhile mulkis formed the majority of employees, their salary share (42%) fell well short of their numbers (52%). Non-mulkis held nearly half the jobs yet drew a disproportionately larger share of pay — and this report escalated the mulki movement further.\n\n**1888 Gazette (Jarida):** To control the growing movement, Mahboob Ali Khan issued a gazette in **1888** (dated 15 Rabi-ul-Sani, 1301 AH per the Islamic calendar). Key provisions:\n\n1.  This gazette used the word **\"mulki\"** for the first time in an official document.\n2.  Persons who had resided in the state for at least **12 years** were to be considered mulkis.\n3.  All posts were to be filled with citizens of the state based on merit, without interference from foreigners.\n4.  Non-mulkis required special permission from the prime minister to obtain a job.\n\nEven after the gazette''s release, the hiring of non-mulkis continued to rise, and enforcement remained weak. Mulki certificates were not easy to obtain regardless of language spoken, and most certificate-holders among employees were, in practice, mulki by category.\n\n**Hindu–Muslim dimension:** Though Hindus formed **90%** of the population, most jobs went to non-mulki Muslims, who made up only about **10%** of the state''s population — a disparity that fed Hindu–Muslim tensions on top of the mulki–non-mulki divide.\n\n**Employees'' Civil List, 1894:** Salarjung-II, disregarding the Nizam''s instructions, continued favouring non-mulkis, further escalating the movement. He resigned as Diwan in **April 1887**, and under the new Diwan, a fresh civil list was released in 1894:\n\n| Category | Employees |\n|---|---|\n| Total gazetted employees | 680 |\n| Mulki employees | 233 |\n| Non-mulki employees | 447 |\n\n*   Non-mulkis thus made up roughly **67%** of higher officials, and their administrative influence grew correspondingly.\n*   Of the 680 total employees, only **63** were Hindus, and among those, only **20** were mulki Hindus.\n*   Non-mulki numbers had risen sharply — from 230 in the 1886 list to 447 by 1894.\n\n**Maharaja Kishan Parshad and mulki protection (1901–1911):**\n\n*   In **1901**, **Maharaja Sir Kishan Parshad**, a Hindu mulki, was appointed Diwan. He sided with the mulkis and worked to curb non-mulki dominance.\n*   His finance minister, **Kaasan Walker**, nonetheless hired many non-mulkis.\n*   Walker also divided Urdu into \"Northern Urdu\" and \"Southern Urdu,\" disparaging mulkis as speakers of an \"uncivilised\" Southern Urdu against a \"civilised\" Northern Urdu spoken by non-mulkis — provoking direct conflict between Kishan Parshad and Walker.\n*   Because of Kishan Parshad''s efforts, the mulki movement achieved partial success.\n*   In **1910**, the government issued a notification protecting mulkis: non-mulki jobs were to be treated as temporary, recruitment was to be by written examination rather than political influence, and locals with sufficient qualifications were to be preferred over non-locals.\n*   Kishan Parshad is remembered as the **\"godfather of the mulki movement.\"**\n\n**The press and the mulki debate:** Contemporary journals actively debated the issue:\n\n*   **Hyderabad Recorder** published news favouring mulki rights.\n*   **Ilahibaksh**, a pro-non-mulki magazine, mockingly asked \"where we find a clever mulki who can administer Hyderabad.\"\n*   **The Mohammadin**, also pro-non-mulki, argued that non-mulkis were descendants of people originally invited by Salarjung.\n*   In **1912**, **Mir Osman Ali Khan** removed Kaasan Walker from his post as finance minister.\n\n**Rai Balamukund:** In 1885, **Rai Balamukund**, a Khatri student from Hyderabad''s old city, graduated with a B.A. from Madras University — the first Hindu mulki graduate in Hyderabad state. In **1908**, he was appointed Chief Justice of the Hyderabad High Court (established in 1890).\n\n### Phase 3 (1911–1948): Mir Osman Ali Khan and the Definition of Mulki\n\nAfter Mir Mahboob Ali Khan''s death in 1911, **Mir Osman Ali Khan**, the seventh Nizam, came to power.\n\n**Osmania University and the language divide:**\n\n*   On the recommendation of an educational conference, Osman Ali Khan established **Osmania University in 1918**, with **Urdu** as the medium of instruction.\n*   However, the university''s Urdu translation department was staffed largely by non-mulki officers from North India.\n*   They used **Lakhnavi (Lucknow) Urdu** in textbooks instead of the **Deccani Urdu** spoken locally, creating a fresh language-based divide: mulkis used Deccani Urdu, non-mulkis used Lakhnavi Urdu.\n*   Osmania University produced educated citizens but was less successful at producing government officers.\n*   **Nizam College**, which taught in English, produced most government officers instead.\n*   Mulki children largely attended Osmania University while non-mulki children attended Nizam College — deepening academic and career differences between the two groups.\n*   Separately, once Osmania University began instruction in Urdu, education gained a degree of freedom from earlier restriction, and the poor and common people gained access to schooling.\n*   Yet without matching economic capacity, many educated but under-resourced young men still could not attain proportionate opportunity.\n*   Jagirdars — regardless of qualification — continued to occupy top posts by virtue of status, and even ordinary Muslims suffered under this feudal arrangement.\n*   The resulting unrest is treated as the fourth phase of the mulki movement''s discontent, originating in the same underlying inequality.\n*   Government translation of Urdu land records into other languages later caused large-scale fraud in land rights, and many original land records were altogether destroyed.\n\n**Ideological currents — Deccan Nationalism vs. Muslim Sovereignty:**\n\n*   **Deccan Nationalism** was proposed by Osmania University''s Urdu professor **Dr. Jorey**; other OU professors joined him in protecting mulkis and Deccani Urdu.\n*   The **Idara-e-Adabiyat-e-Urdu** library, started by OU''s mulki professors in **1930**, published articles on Deccan Nationalism.\n*   This library played a key role in uniting mulkis across caste and religious lines.\n*   Deccan Nationalist leaders raised the slogan **\"Long live Nizam, the royal embodiment of Deccan Nationalism.\"**\n*   **Muslim Sovereignty** emerged in opposition to Deccan Nationalism, initially supported only by non-mulkis.\n*   It later split mulkis along Hindu–Muslim lines, drawing support even from mulki Muslims and thereby weakening the mulki movement.\n*   This theory was later widely invoked by the **Razakars**.\n\n**The 1919 Farman — Definition of Mulki:** To protect mulki rights, Mir Osman Ali Khan issued a Farman in 1919, known as the **Mulki Rules**. Key provisions:\n\n1.  Only mulkis were to be appointed to government jobs. A person qualified as mulki if any one condition was met: (a) born in Hyderabad state; (b) resident of Hyderabad; (c) father completed 15 years as a government employee by the child''s birth; (d) for a woman, being the wife of a mulki.\n2.  A person whose father was a mulki at the time of their birth is considered mulki.\n3.  A person completing **15 years** of permanent residence in Hyderabad state is considered mulki, provided they submit a magistrate-attested affidavit stating they will not return to their former place.\n4.  A mulki woman who marries a non-mulki but continues residing in Hyderabad retains her mulki status and rights.\n5.  A mulki woman living outside the state after marrying a non-mulki, who returns as a permanent resident after his death or divorce, is considered mulki — though her children do not automatically inherit mulki status unless they independently satisfy the rules.\n6.  **Talukdars** were authorised to issue Mulki Certificates under these rules.\n7.  Applicants for a mulki certificate had to furnish full personal details and attest to their accuracy, with liability for punishment under Provision 9 for false information.\n8.  A rejected application could be resubmitted within a month to the **Subedar**, whose decision was final.\n9.  A certificate could be cancelled if the holder was later found to have furnished false information, upon recognition by the government or police.\n\n*   Under Provisions 1(a)–(c), a person disobedient to the Hyderabad state, or one who participated in activities against state politics or worked against government interests, would forfeit their mulki rights.\n*   All non-mulki jobs were treated as temporary.\n*   All government jobs were to be filled through written examinations, not political appointment; some fairness was extended to mulkis in promotions.\n*   The Farman also established the **Civil Services Executive Council**, an independent body for selecting employees.\n*   In **1938**, this council appointed a committee to study civil services.\n*   Based on its report, a Farman issued on **27 April 1947** created the **Hyderabad Public Service Commission**, comprising a chairman and four members appointed by the Nizam on the executive council chairman''s recommendation.\n\n**Mulki organisations of the 1920s–30s:**\n\n*   **Osmania Graduates Association (1920)** — a mulki political organisation formed in Hyderabad; it still operates several educational institutions in Telangana today and also established an exhibition society.\n*   **Hyderabad State Reforms Organisation (1921)** — formed by educated Hyderabadis to pursue constitutional reforms.\n*   **Society of Union and Progress (1926)** — formed in London by Hyderabadi students studying there, to promote the establishment of a responsible government.\n*   **Hyderabad Association (1930)** — started under **Barrister Kishan**, demanding a responsible government and administrative reforms.\n\n**1933 Farman:**\n\n*   In 1930, Khan Sahebs from Punjab were directly appointed to senior posts in Hyderabad, affecting the promotion prospects of local employees and reigniting the mulki–non-mulki dispute.\n*   To ease discontent, Mir Osman Ali Khan issued a Farman in **1933** giving priority to educated, efficient mulkis in recruitment and confining non-mulki appointments to a temporary basis.\n*   **Hyderabad Kotwal Raja Venkatarami Reddy** supported mulki activists throughout.\n*   Mulki activists and the public held a meeting to thank the Nizam for this Farman; attendees included **Kumari Padmaja Naidu**, **Mandamula Narsingarao**, **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao**, **Mir Akbar Ali Khan**, **Dr. Lateef Sayyid**, **Raja Dhonderaj**, and **Nawaz Shamsheer Jung**.\n\n**Nizam Subjects League, 1935 (Nizam Mulki League):**\n\n*   Formed by mulki activists in 1935; called **Jamiath Rifayame Nizam** in Urdu and **Nizam Prajala Sangham** in Telugu.\n*   Slogan: **\"Hyderabad for Hyderabadis.\"**\n*   **President:** Nawab Sir Nizamat Jung (who also served as political minister in the Nizam''s council of ministers).\n*   **Vice-Presidents:** Dr. Lateef Sayyid and Ramachandra Nayak.\n*   **Treasurer:** Barrister Noushid Chinay.\n*   **Secretaries:** Sayyid Abid Hassan, Boorgula Ramakrishna Rao, and Srinivas Sharma.\n*   **Executive committee** (18 members) included Raja Bahadur Venkataramireddy, Vaman Nayak, Nawab Bahadur Yarjung, Kashinathrao Vaidya, Akbar Alikhan, Mandamula Narsingrao, and Mir Hassanoddin (editor of the _Mamlakath_ newspaper), among others.\n\n**Significance of the Nizam Mulki League:**\n\n*   Protected mulki rights while remaining loyal to the Nizam and preserving the local language.\n*   Respected and promoted local culture and traditions.\n*   Promoted unity across social sections while supporting the development of downtrodden groups.\n*   Pushed for government accountability to an elected legislature, aiming for a parliamentary, responsible government.\n*   Declared loyalty to the Nizam while framing its movement as protection of Deccan culture and language, and worked to safeguard the Nizam state''s royal rights.\n*   Under pressure from the League, the government formed a committee under **Aravamudam Iyengar**, whose recommendations led to amendments strengthening the Mulki Rules in **1939**.\n*   The Nizam initially supported the League''s goals, but its demand for a Responsible Government angered him, which cooled public enthusiasm for the League.\n*   Some Muslim intellectuals influenced by the Muslim Sovereignty ideology left to join **Ittehadul Muslimin**, and the Nizam Subjects League was dissolved in **1939**.\n\n**Formation of the People''s Convention (1937):** A split within the Mulki League saw Hindu members leave and form the **People''s Convention**. (Note: League leader Sayyid Abid Hassan authored a book titled _Whither Hyderabad_.)\n\n**Aravamudam Iyengar Committee (1937–38):** Appointed by the Nizam''s government in 1937 for political reforms, it submitted its report in 1938, recommending:\n\n*   Job appointments should strictly follow the Mulki Rules.\n*   Acting on the committee''s recommendations, the definition of mulki was made more stringent under **Article 39 in 1945**.\n*   Candidates for government jobs should be judged on efficiency, skill, and moral conduct, without partiality.\n*   A dedicated system should be established for government employee appointments.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter30-mulki-movement-p4", "partName": "Violation of Mulki Rules, 1948–52: Military Rule and the Vellodi Government", "bodyMd": "Between **1948 and 1952**, Hyderabad state was merged into the Indian federal system and faced considerable social, economic, and political upheaval. The government recognised the need to build public confidence in the new administration through law and order and economic stability, introducing several new employment measures:\n\n*   **Police recruitment:** Under the Nizam, over 90% of civil jobs had been held by a single social class, prompting the new government to broaden participation, especially in the armed forces.\n    *   It resolved that, by **1 January 1954**, **60%** of posts would be filled by other social categories.\n    *   Armed forces personnel from Madras, the Central Provinces, and Bombay were appointed to maintain peace and security.\n*   **Language policy:** Priority was given to Telugu in administration, since most people in Hyderabad state were Telugu speakers, and Telugu-proficient people were preferred for jobs over those favouring Urdu.\n*   **Land reform:** The government decided to abolish the **Jagirdari System** and bring land management under the revenue department, creating a new job category for this purpose.\n*   **Central deputation:** To stabilise the economy and normalise conditions, the Vellodi government brought in **over 200 officials of the Indian Union**.\n*   **Anti-corruption measures:** Employees who had engaged in politics, corruption, or misuse of office under the Nizam were removed.\n    *   The new government appointed **M. A. Rahman** as Public Service Commission officer post-independence, creating fresh openings.\n    *   Military officer **Chowdary** personally reviewed several such cases.\n*   **Military and police reforms:** The Hyderabad Army, numbering **17,870** at the time of Police Action, was reduced to **10,415**, on the assessment that such a large force was unnecessary.\n    *   Police staff appointed during Police Action were later also reduced, with appointments based on district-level need and priority extended to non-Muslims as well.\n*   **Rehabilitation:** Personnel who had resigned from the army after the Second World War were given new jobs through the **Directorate of Rehabilitation**, which continued functioning for a time even after Hyderabad''s merger with the Indian Union.\n*   **Employment exchanges** were set up in **Hyderabad, Warangal, and Aurangabad** to fill lower- and mid-level government posts and place candidates in private-sector jobs within government departments.\n*   The **Bikaner Training Institute** was established to expand opportunities for skilled and technical labour, training around **1,200 people annually across 20 different fields**.\n\n**Operation Polo and its aftermath:**\n\n*   On **17 September 1948**, **Operation Polo** (Police Action) ended Nizam rule in Hyderabad, merging the state with the Indian Union.\n*   **J. N. Chaudhuri** (from Bengal) served as Military Governor of Hyderabad from **September 1948 to December 1949**.\n*   **M. K. Vellodi** (from Kerala) served as the state''s Chief Minister from **January 1950 to March 1952**.\n*   Since both Chaudhuri and Vellodi were non-locals, the inflow of outsiders into Hyderabad''s administration continued.\n*   In the name of modernising the regime, Vellodi introduced **English and Telugu** as official languages in place of Urdu.\n*   Chaudhuri began appointing non-locals proficient in English for administrative work — first from Kerala, then Tamilians from Madras, and finally Telugu (Andhra) people from Madras.\n*   The stated reason was that Telangana people lacked English proficiency; this resulted in large-scale, indiscriminate appointment of Andhra personnel to Telangana jobs.\n*   The translation of Urdu-language land records into English led to widespread land-rights fraud.\n*   Many land records were destroyed, and a significant amount of land in the former Hyderabad state was effectively looted through this process.\n*   Telugu was introduced into the state education system, and teaching jobs went largely to Andhra personnel because of their proficiency in Telugu.\n\n**Bypassing the Mulki Rules:** Government appointments were held to not require compliance with Provisions **1(A)** and **1(C)** of the **1949 Mulki Regulations** issued by the civil government under Vellodi''s administration:\n\n*   **1(A)**: Mulki means a person native by birth.\n*   **1(C)**: The father must have completed 15 years of government service by the time of the person''s birth.\n\nBy exempting appointments from these provisions, the Vellodi government effectively undermined mulki protections.\n\n*   Vellodi appointed Andhra officers as chief administrators, ranking above district collectors of the erstwhile Hyderabad state.\n*   He even appointed the Tahsildar of Krishna district, **Ramachandra Rao**, as chief administrator for Hyderabad district.\n*   These Andhra officers, in turn, brought in relatives and friends from their home region for jobs, fostering favouritism and bribery.\n*   **Padmaja Naidu** raised the issue of corruption in Parliament, to which **Vallabhbhai Patel** replied that such corruption already existed and was not new to Hyderabad.\n*   **Gogineni Ranganayakulu**, from the Andhra region, wrote a book titled _Colonization Policy of HEH Nizam of Hyderabad_, noting that **10 lakh acres** of land in Hyderabad province were suitable for colonists.\n*   The newspaper **The Star of Hyderabad** reported repeated violations of the Mulki Rules after Hyderabad''s merger with India, and hundreds of non-mulkis entered government service during the rebuilding of the state''s administrative structure.\n*   Andhra officers who obtained jobs in this manner began to dominate and mistreat Telangana people, mocking and disparaging Telangana''s culture and language.\n\nThese developments together led to the **Gair-Mulki Movement of 1952**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter30-mulki-movement-p5", "partName": "Situation After Police Action: Language and Employee Grievances", "bodyMd": "After Police Action, large numbers of Union (non-local) employees came to Hyderabad.\n\n*   The shift of administrative business into English, and the growing importance of regional languages, made teachers proficient in regional-language instruction a necessity in schools.\n*   Although the mulki-certificate requirement for state employment remained on the books, mulki certificates were somehow obtained by many, regardless of what language they actually spoke.\n*   A large share of those recruited as employees and teachers held certificates in a mulki category.\n\nInitially this issue attracted little attention.\n\n*   The newly arrived employees behaved as though they were doing the \"uncivilised\" region a favour by modernising it, treating the existing population as somehow inferior.\n*   Many of these new arrivals treated their postings casually, extracted bribes freely, and did not genuinely assist the local public.\n*   Because their conduct was so poor, the already-serving local employees came to look better by comparison.\n*   Union employees who had arrived in these positions then spread deliberate, harmful propaganda against the officers already working locally, further deepening ill-feeling.\n\n**Urdu language and institutional divide:**\n\n*   Urdu, one of India''s languages, was promoted by the Nizam government to university level under British influence.\n    \n*   In principle it was just one language among the nation''s many mother tongues (Telugu, Marathi, Kannada each being the true mother tongue of most residents).\n    \n*   Even so, the state government privileged Urdu, imposing it as an alien administrative language over the people — a policy that reduced the spread of education in Andhra areas.\n    \n*   Prominent literary figures, from Rabindranath Tagore to Rajagopalachari, publicly criticised this policy.\n    \n*   It was only after a Congress government came to power in the state that Urdu''s official status was withdrawn and English was introduced in its place.\n    \n*   **Northern (non-mulki) Urdu** patrons included Dr. Abdul Haq; **Deccani (mulki) Urdu** was patronised by Acharya Peetham of Khadar Mohiuddin Zore.\n    \n*   Northern Urdu was institutionally backed by the body \"Idara Adabiyat-e-Urdu,\" while Deccani Urdu was supported by \"Anjuman Tarraqi-e-Urdu.\"\n    \n*   A translation department, **Darul Tarjuma**, was set up at Osmania University to translate reference texts, drawing on eminent local pandits, scholars, and linguists.\n    \n*   The resulting translations, of high scholarly standard, remain useful in present-day Pakistan.\n    \n*   In the historiography and academic discourse produced around Deccani Urdu''s achievements, however, it was Northern (non-mulki) Urdu that received institutional prominence.\n    \n*   Until the Police Action, official patronage and leadership positions overwhelmingly went to outsiders.\n    \n*   Once instruction at Osmania University began in Urdu, it gave the poor and ordinary people somewhat greater access to education.\n    \n*   But without matching economic means, many educated youths still could not achieve corresponding social advancement, while under-qualified jagirdars continued to occupy top positions purely by status.\n    \n*   This feudal arrangement caused hardship even for ordinary Muslims, and the resulting unrest is described as the mulki agitation''s continuation into the fourth decade (the 1940s).\n    \n\n**Restricted spread of the mulki movement:**\n\n*   The mulki movement, as it stood, was confined largely to Telangana.\n*   The erstwhile Hyderabad-state regions of **Marathwada** and **Karnataka** were not troubled by it in the same way.\n*   Those regions, over time, simply lost the distinction, so that their inhabitants became indistinguishable from later settlers, unlike in Telangana.\n\n**Popular resolutions on language in education (1941):** At the Hyderabad People''s Education Conference session, held under the presidency of **Vinayakarao Vidyalankar** in April 1941, the following resolutions were unanimously adopted:\n\n1.  Instruction up to Matriculation should be in the student''s own mother tongue.\n2.  After Matriculation, instruction in place of Urdu should be in English.\n3.  Hindi and Sanskrit should be taught as languages.\n4.  Students should be permitted to sing **Vande Mataram**.\n5.  Restrictions preventing entry into \"Khaki schools\" should be lifted.\n\nSeparately, in May 1941, **Prajamandali** president **Joshi** submitted a memorandum to the state government with these demands:\n\n1.  Instruction should be conducted in Telugu, Marathi, and Kannada.\n2.  Orders banning Khaki schools should be withdrawn.\n3.  Instruction should not be conducted in Urdu where it is unnecessary for Hindu girls.\n4.  Primary education should be made free and compulsory.\n5.  Prajamandali representatives should have a role in drafting textbooks.\n6.  Regional languages should be declared official languages in their respective regions.\n\n**Burgula Ramakrishna Rao''s 1944 address:**\n\n*   As presiding chairman of the Hyderabad Prajaa Vidya Mahasabha (People''s Education Conference), **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao** delivered a historic address from its platform on **11 November 1944**.\n*   He argued that even while accepting and implementing the principle of instruction in the mother tongue, it made no sense to continue teaching non-native-Urdu-speaking students in Urdu.\n*   He also noted that, of the schools using English as the medium of instruction in the state — a small number relative to the state''s overall population — access for Hindu children remained difficult.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p1", "partName": "Background — Political and Economic Context", "bodyMd": "By the late 1960s, resentment in Telangana had been building for over a decade, and the region lacked a leader with statewide acceptability. Under the Gentlemen''s Agreement, the understanding was that if the Chief Minister came from Andhra, the Deputy Chief Minister''s post should go to Telangana, but this arrangement was inconsistently honoured — Telangana leaders held the Deputy CM post only for limited stretches, with figures like Sanjeevareddy occupying it briefly before moving on.\n\n**Fragmented Telangana leadership:**\n\n*   After Kasu Brahmananda Reddy became Chief Minister in 1967, many of the shortcomings in implementing the Gentlemen''s Agreement became visible.\n*   The regional committee meant to safeguard Telangana''s interests had become largely nominal even though the agreement was not being properly implemented.\n*   Two rival groups, one led by Achyutha Reddy and the other by Jayagriva Chari, worked at cross purposes rather than presenting a united Telangana front, weakening the region''s political bargaining position after Chenna Reddy took over as the movement''s leading figure.\n\n**Unresolved seniority lists:** Even 15 years after the state''s formation, a unified seniority list for employees of the two regions had still not been finalized, and Telangana people continued to point out irregularities and anomalies in the interim arrangements. A recurring complaint was that Telangana funds were being diverted to Andhra, which fed growing unease among Telangana government employees.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p2", "partName": "Reasons for the Movement", "bodyMd": "**1\\. Violation of Telangana Safeguards** Andhra leaders openly disregarded the protections granted to Telangana under the 1956 Gentlemen''s Agreement. (The specific violations between 1956–69 are covered in the previous chapter; the points below are the immediate triggers.)\n\n**2\\. Discontent Among Engineers**\n\n*   During the 1965–66 India-Pakistan war, the state government cut its budget by 10% and removed temporary posts as an economy measure, leaving many Telangana engineers unemployed.\n*   Telangana engineers, on examining the situation, found that most permanent posts were held by Andhra people while the temporary \"supernumerary\" posts went to Telangana staff — so when cuts came, Telangana employees lost their jobs disproportionately. This triggered a campaign demanding that Andhra people vacate posts and return to Andhra, so that Telangana people could be recruited into the vacancies.\n\n**3\\. Dissatisfaction Among Employees — The Mulki Rules Dispute**\n\n*   Cause → Effect chain:\n    *   **1957**: Parliament passed the Public Employment Act, 1957 (Requirement as to Residence), which repealed residency requirements across most of the country but retained them for Telangana (Andhra Pradesh), Himachal Pradesh, Manipur, and Tripura.\n    *   **1959**: Under this Act, the Centre issued the Andhra Pradesh Public Employment Regulations (Requirement as to Residence).\n    *   These \"Mulki Rules\" were first implemented on 21 March, 1959 for a five-year term, later extended for another five years, and in October 1968 the state government asked the Centre to extend the rules again until 20 March, 1974.\n    *   Andhra people challenged the rules in court, and on **28 March, 1969** the Supreme Court ruled the residency requirement rules invalid, holding that residency status should apply to the whole state, not to one region within it.\n    *   Telangana youth, disappointed by this verdict, were galvanized into launching the separate Telangana movement.\n*   The successive governments of Neelam Sanjeevareddy, Damodaram Sanjeeviah, and Kasu Brahmananda Reddy brought in hundreds of amendments to the Public Employment Act and recruited large numbers of Andhra people into Telangana posts; thousands of fake \"mulki\" (local-residency) certificates were issued during this period.\n*   A High Court division bench, during the trial of a related case, observed that the government had granted numerous exemptions without regard to Rule 3 of the Public Employment Act.\n*   Of roughly 300 people serving as Deputy Registrar of Cooperative Societies statewide, only nine were from Telangana, even though the region had more than 70 such posts.\n*   At least one of Telangana''s nine districts had no District Panchayat Officer from the region.\n*   After the integration of Andhra and Telangana, Andhra candidates were recruited into most Telangana vacancies, frequently using fake mulki certificates, in clear violation of the Public Employment Act, 1957.\n*   Telangana employees were also disadvantaged in promotions: a common seniority list was prepared applying the old Madras-region rules, combining the seniority of officers from both regions in a way that caused serious injustice to Telangana staff.\n\n**4\\. Disgruntlement Among Teachers**\n\n*   The expansion of Panchayati Raj institutions after the formation of Andhra Pradesh led to many new schools in Telangana, creating a shortage of maths and science teachers.\n*   While qualified Telangana teachers were available for other posts, Andhra teachers used Zilla Parishad chairmen''s influence to secure nearly all teaching posts in Telangana.\n*   By 1967, the number of illegally appointed (non-local) teachers had reached about 4,000, causing widespread resentment among unemployed Telangana teachers.\n*   In 1967, the Telangana Regional Association asked the government to fill secondary-school teaching posts in Telangana using the region''s surplus funds — but the Finance Ministry responded that Telangana had no surplus funds, deepening the sense of grievance.\n\n**5\\. Discontent Among Students**\n\n*   In 1967, a large college-fee hike triggered a student strike, forcing the government to roll back the increase.\n*   Around the same time, a dispute broke out between Osmania University Vice-Chancellor D.S. Reddy and Chief Minister Kasu Brahmananda Reddy over university autonomy.\n*   Brahmananda Reddy shortened the VC''s tenure from five years to three and replaced D.S. Reddy with Pinnamaneni Narasimha Rao.\n    *   Students, under Jaipal Reddy''s leadership, saw this as unwarranted interference threatening university autonomy and went on strike against the Chief Minister.\n    *   The courts subsequently struck down Narasimha Rao''s appointment; he left for Guntur, and D.S. Reddy was reinstated as Vice-Chancellor.\n*   This episode split the student body into two camps: one loyal to the Chief Minister (led by Venkat Reddy), and another, led by Mallikarjun, demanding a separate Telangana — setting up the factional split that would later shape the student movement.\n\n**6\\. Insulting of Telangana Culture and Traditions**\n\n*   Telangana people faced insults in literary, cultural, and political spheres from people who had migrated from Andhra, who mocked Telangana''s dialect, festivals, rituals, and food practices as inferior, and treated Telangana people as second-class citizens while imposing their own cultural outlook.\n*   This bias was starkly illustrated by contrasting treatment at death: when former Andhra Chief Minister Tunguturi Prakasam Pantulu died in 1957, his funeral was conducted with full government honours; but when former Hyderabad State Chief Minister Burgula Ramakrishna Rao — a signatory to the Gentlemen''s Agreement — died on 14 September, 1967, no such government honours were extended. This contrast was cited as evidence of how ordinary Telangana people were likely treated.\n\n**7\\. Political Marginalization of Marri Chenna Reddy**\n\n*   Marri Chenna Reddy, referred to by northern newspapers as a \"fire eater,\" won the 1967 general election and served as a minister under Chief Minister Kasu Brahmananda Reddy, but their rivalry over the Chief Ministership was intense.\n*   Indira Gandhi, to keep him out of state politics, appointed him Union Minister of Steel & Mines (he was not yet an MP at the time, and was later elected to the Rajya Sabha).\n*   His opponent Vandemataram Ramachandra Rao challenged his 1967 Assembly election win in the High Court, alleging illegal practices and rigging.\n*   On 26 April, 1968, the High Court invalidated Chenna Reddy''s election and barred him from contesting for six years, forcing his resignation as Union Minister.\n*   The Supreme Court, on appeal, rebuked him, calling his conduct \"the most heinous crime among crimes,\" and the Central Election Commission also dismissed his plea to reduce the ban.\n*   Chenna Reddy was angered that Brahmananda Reddy had remained neutral rather than helping him during the proceedings, despite being in a position to do so.\n*   Having become politically unemployed, Chenna Reddy saw the emerging 1969 movement as an opportunity — a background that shaped his later entry into and leadership of the Telangana Praja Samithi.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p3", "partName": "Roots of the Struggle — Employee Organizing Before 1969", "bodyMd": "### Hyderabad Non-Gazetted Officers Union (HNGO)\n\n*   Telangana employees organized to fight for their rights by forming the Hyderabad Non-Gazetted Officers Union while still under the former Hyderabad state.\n*   After Andhra Pradesh''s formation, HNGO Secretary Surendranath and KR Amos made representations to ministers and department heads about the injustices faced by Telangana employees.\n*   KR Amos had joined the Hyderabad Secretariat as a Typist in the Industrial Trust Fund in 1953; Surendranath was a Section Officer in the Industries Department.\n*   Govinda Raj Pillai was HNGO''s president and Surendranath Nagar Chetty its secretary.\n\n### Telangana Non-Gazetted Officers Union (TNGO)\n\n*   In 1964–65, KR Amos converted the HNGO into the Telangana Non-Gazetted Officers Union.\n\n| TNGO Union | Office-bearer |\n|---|---|\n| Founding President | K.R. Amos (continued until 1969) |\n| Associate President | SLN Chari (Deputy Tahsildar) |\n\n*   Amos estimated that, by 1968, roughly 22,000 non-residents of Telangana (\"non-mulkis\") had secured jobs in the region.\n*   The TNGO launched the Telangana movement for safeguards in response to the government''s arbitrary violation of employee protections, the non-implementation of court and central government orders, the prevalence of bogus mulki certificates, and the appointment of thousands of Andhra people to Telangana-region jobs.\n\n### Telangana Safeguards Day (Telangana Hamila Dinam)\n\n*   On 10 July, 1968, the TNGO, under KR Amos''s direction, celebrated Telangana Safeguards Day, with Telangana Rights Day meetings held across all districts.\n*   Addressing an employees'' meeting in Hyderabad, labour leader Mahadevasingh warned that Telangana would separate from Andhra Pradesh if the region''s wishes were not met.\n*   At a 30 July, 1968 TNGO meeting at the KTPS club in Palvancha, presided over by Rama Sudhakar Raju, speakers demanded that Telangana''s rights protections be extended for five years.\n\n### Telangana Safeguards Movement — Origins at Palvancha\n\n*   The movement first took shape at Palvancha, Khammam District, where the Kothagudem Thermal Power Station (KTPS) had been established in 1961, funded from Telangana''s surplus funds.\n*   Local farmers lost about 1,300 acres of land to the project, so the power station''s jobs were meant to be reserved for local people — yet of about 1,400 total jobs, only 200 went to Telangana natives, and 175 of those were removed by 1968.\n*   In 1968, local employees, backed by Hyderabad''s non-gazetted employees, demanded enforcement of the Mulki Rules at the power station and jobs for locals — a movement that succeeded in displacing several non-native employees.\n*   Kolishetti Ramadasu of Illandu village was the first to expose that most KTPS employees were from Andhra.\n*   In a November 1968 discussion on the Mulki Rules between Chief Minister Kasu Brahmananda Reddy, opposition leaders, and public representatives, Gouthulachanna proposed creating supernumerary posts in Andhra to accommodate the displaced Andhra employees — a proposal accepted by all, including the Chief Minister, until General Secretary M.T. Raju (an Andhra native) openly opposed it, forcing the Chief Minister to step back.\n*   The Chief Minister''s dictatorial style and sarcastic remarks compounded Telangana''s grievances:\n    *   When asked in the November 1968 Assembly session to clarify Telangana''s surplus crores, he responded sarcastically by asking whether the member meant \"old coats or new coats.\"\n    *   On 8 January, 1969, when a Telangana delegation raised the delayed Pochampad project, he arrogantly replied that although construction was delayed, \"the foundations were strong,\" and dismissed them.\n    *   When told that many were approaching the courts over injustices to TNGO employees, he arrogantly remarked that raising court fees would stop them from going to court.\n*   These experiences convinced activists that secession from Andhra Pradesh was the only viable path forward.\n\n### Pioneers of the 1969 Movement\n\n*   VL Narsimha Rao (President, Palvancha TNGO)\n*   Kolishetty Ramadasu (resident of Illandu)\n*   K. Rama Sudhakar Raju (teachers'' union leader)\n\n**Kolishetty Ramadasu''s contribution:**\n\n*   A native of Gatakarepalli village near Illandu (Khammam District), Ramadasu formed the Telangana Regional Council at Illandu in 1968 with 20 young volunteers.\n\n| Telangana Regional Council | Office-bearer |\n|---|---|\n| President | Kolishetti Ramadasu |\n| General Secretary | Muthyam Venkanna |\n\n*   The Council investigated Andhra residents who had obtained government jobs through bogus mulki certificates, and supported Telangana employees through dharnas and hunger strikes.\n*   Convinced that Telangana would neither get jobs nor funds without a separate state, Ramadasu is credited with starting, at Illandu in 1968, the first movement on the Mulki issue outside Hyderabad.\n\n### Separate Telangana Slogan — First Raised at Kothagudem\n\n*   On 30 December, 1968, Legislative Council President Pidathala Ranga Reddy and Telangana Regional Council President J. Chokka Rao attended the admission ceremony of Kothagudem Zilla Parishad Girls'' School.\n*   The Telangana Rights Protection Council staged a large procession raising separate-Telangana slogans on this occasion.\n*   Chokka Rao''s remark in his speech — challenging agitators with \"who cannot safeguard Telangana rights from the past 12 years, how can they achieve separate Telangana?\" — provoked the pro-separatist crowd rather than pacifying them.\n*   The first phase of the 1969 movement is thus traced to Palvancha, Khammam district, driven chiefly by the large number of Andhra-region employees at the Palvancha Thermal Power Station; the first protest took place there on **5 January, 1969**.\n\n### Kothagudem Protest\n\n*   Under KR Amos''s TNGO, concerns were raised that Telangana safeguards were being violated through Andhra appointments in secretariat and department posts.\n*   On **30 April, 1968**, the Andhra Pradesh government issued orders that: (i) posts reserved for Telangana natives should be kept vacant if no eligible local candidate was available, rather than filled by non-natives; and (ii) non-natives conditionally appointed to such reserved posts should be removed within three months and replaced with eligible natives.\n*   This order cost many Andhra teachers and other employees their jobs, and was similarly applied at the Andhra Pradesh State Electricity Board, leading to the removal of previously hired non-locals at the Kothagudem thermal plant.\n*   The dismissed employees challenged this in the High Court.\n*   On **3 January, 1969**, High Court Judge Justice Kuppuswamy ruled in favour of the non-natives, holding that the APSEB, being an autonomous body outside the Public Employment Act''s scope, was not bound by local reservation requirements.\n*   In response, the KTPC labour union, led by PV Giri, launched a strike.\n*   Non-mulki employees at the plant were given until 10 January to be dismissed, failing which a hunger strike would begin; daily-wage leader Krishna accordingly began a hunger strike at Palwancha on 10 January.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p4", "partName": "The Movement Begins (January 1969)", "bodyMd": "### 8 January, 1969 — Ravindranath''s Fast Unto Death\n\n*   Annabathula Ravindranath, a BA student and National Student Union leader, began an indefinite fast at Gandhi Chowk, Khammam, demanding implementation of Telangana safeguards and a separate state — the first person to undertake a fast unto death in the 1969 movement.\n*   He was accompanied by Kavi Rajamurthy (Vice-President, Khammam Municipality, and a poet) and by a nine-year-old student, Anuradha.\n*   A grand procession preceded the fast, with slogans of \"Non-mulkis go back\" and \"implement Telangana safeguards.\"\n*   MLA Purushottam Rao garlanded Ravindranath and Rajamurthy at Gandhi Chowk.\n*   Leaders supporting the satyagraha included T. Purushottama Rao, S. Satyanarayana, Bhagavan Das, Cherukuri Kashaiah, Siddulu, Arusam Komuraiah, Razab Ali, and Hanumantha Rao.\n*   S. Satyanarayana explained how the Gentlemen''s Bond promises had been violated, and declared the movement would continue until the safeguards were strictly implemented.\n*   The day before (7 January), the Telangana Safeguards Movement Association in Warangal had passed a resolution demanding that the Telangana Regional Committee be allowed to independently control spending of Telangana funds without Chief Ministerial interference.\n*   Public meetings and hartals followed in Warangal and Khammam in support of Ravindranath''s strike.\n\n### 9 January — Warangal Meeting\n\n*   A public meeting in support of Ravindranath''s fast, presided over by Purushottama Rao, passed resolutions demanding: removal of illegally hired non-mulki employees and appointment of mulki staff; immediate scrutiny of fake mulki certificates; a senior official committee to calculate surplus funds; and immediate, full-scale execution of the Pochampadu project.\n*   Purushottama Rao pointed out that although the Pochampadu and Nagarjuna Sagar projects were approved simultaneously, hundreds of crores were spent on Nagarjuna Sagar while Pochampadu — meant to bring 28 lakh acres under cultivation across six Telangana districts — had been scaled down to five lakh acres.\n*   He also noted the disparity in higher-education access: Guntur alone had 20 colleges, while Warangal, considered a hub of educational development, had only two.\n*   MSc student Subhash pointed out that in the Orugallu postgraduate centre, only two of the planned seats were meant for Andhra students, yet Andhra got six seats while Telangana received only three.\n\n### 10 January\n\n*   At Palwancha, Potu Krishnamurthy began a fast unto death demanding removal of non-Mulkis from the Kothagudem Thermal Power Station; Palvancha MLA Panuganti Picchaiah undertook a sympathy hunger strike.\n*   In Nizamabad, Vidyarthi Parishad President AS Poshetty criticized the government''s \"stepmother''s love\" for Telangana.\n*   Eleven-year-old Vachaspati and BSc students R. Someswara Rao and A. Subbarao began hunger strikes in Khammam in support of Ravindranath.\n*   The Andhra Pradesh Students'' Federation, under N. Sudhakar Reddy, pledged to press for implementation of Telangana protections.\n*   Andhra newspapers largely ignored Ravindranath''s strike, prompting activists at Illandu to burn newspapers in protest.\n*   **(Background note on prior opposition):** in 1955, Swami Ramananda Tirtha — a defender of Vishalandhra — had already dismissed the separate-Telangana aspiration as meaningless, foreshadowing the later split between \"Safeguards\" and \"Separatist\" camps.\n\n### 11 January — Telangana Student Rights Protection Action Committee\n\n*   Representatives of all Warangal college student unions formed this committee.\n\n| Role | Leader |\n|---|---|\n| President | Muralidhar Reddy (Kakatiya Medical College) |\n| Convenor | Surender Reddy (Arts & Science College) |\n| Organiser | T. Siddulu |\n\n### 12 January\n\n*   **Legislator Satyanarayana''s hunger strike**: Gogineni Satyanarayan began a three-day protest in Khammam supporting Ravindranath, and published a pamphlet demanding additional development funds for Telangana beyond its surplus funds.\n*   **Osmania University student unions meeting**, chaired by union president Venkatrami Reddy, resolved to: hold strikes from 15 January for Telangana safeguards; demand the resignation of nine Telangana ministers and legislators; investigate misuse of surplus funds; send back non-Mulki employees immediately; implement the Gentlemen''s Agreement fully; and release 70 crore of Telangana funds.\n\n### 13 January — The Student Movement Splits\n\n*   The Telangana Students Action Committee was formed at Osmania University, with medical student Mallikarjun elected general secretary; he called on students to be ready for sacrifice. This marked the formal split of the student body into two factions.\n\n**Separatists (sought a separate Telangana):**\n\n*   The Telangana Students Working Committee, led by Mallikarjun, was centred at Nizam College.\n\n**Safeguards group (sought implementation of protections within a united state):**\n\n*   The Telangana Safeguards Committee, led by Venkatrami Reddy, was centred at Vivekavardhini College, Koti, and aimed only at implementing protections while keeping the state united.\n*   This group had the backing of Chief Minister Kasu Brahmananda Reddy, and the CPI''s Student Federation supported this camp''s unionist, egalitarian line.\n\n**Other city-level bodies formed the same day:**\n\n*   **Telangana Protection/Conservation Committee** — Chairman Katam Laxminarayana; members included Mahadev Singh (Socialist Party), Salauddin Owisi (Majlis), E.V. Padmanabhan, Santapuri Raghuveera Rao, Jaffar Hussain, N. Madhava Rao, and others; it fully supported the student movement.\n*   **Telangana Rights Protection Committee** — formed by concerned citizens, meeting at the Adihindu building (established by Bhagya Reddy Varma) in Chadarghat; convened by D. Ramaswamy (ex-MLA).\n\n### 15 January\n\n*   The two student factions held separate processions: separatists marched from Nizam College to the Secretariat and burned the Chief Minister''s effigy — Mallikarjun clarified the movement was not against Andhra people personally — while a Safeguards-aligned procession went from Vivekavardhini College to Nizam College and burned effigies of Telangana ministers for their silence.\n*   K. Achyuta Reddy (former Regional Committee president) argued it was premature for students to worry about a separate state, and that the Chief Minister was sympathetic to Telangana''s development needs.\n\n### 16 January\n\n*   Warangal Arts and Science College students (J. Rangareddy, Sayyad Gousuddin, Muttu Lingam, Vedaraja Singh) began a 48-hour hunger strike.\n*   A large Warangal public meeting, chaired by T. Purushottama Rao, demanded the immediate resignation of Telangana ministers.\n\n### 18 January — First Major Clash and Ultimatum\n\n*   A massive Hyderabad student procession was met with police lathi charges and tear gas.\n*   Two rival processions — Safeguards-aligned (Venkatram Reddy, Sadanand, from Koti to Abids) and Separatist (Mallikarjun, Sridhar Reddy, from Nizam College to Koti) — collided at Abids Chourasta, leading to stone-throwing and further lathi charges; Arts College President Ramakant Reddy was seriously injured, and Puli Veeranna suffered a head injury.\n*   Telangana employees, meeting in Hyderabad, issued an ultimatum threatening direct action unless roughly 6,000 Andhra employees in Telangana were sent back.\n*   **Opposition parties'' joint warning**: Bharatiya Jana Sangh, United Socialist Party, CPI, CPM, and Majlis jointly warned that they would join the students if the Chief Minister did not act.\n\n### 19 January — The All-Party Agreement\n\n*   Chief Minister Kasu Brahmananda Reddy convened an all-party meeting on 18–19 January 1969 (the 18 January session, running five hours, was postponed to the next day).\n*   On 19 January, 45 politicians from various parties across both regions met at the Chief Minister''s residence, Ananda Nilayam, and reached the **All-Party Agreement**, which acknowledged government shortcomings in implementing the Gentlemen''s Agreement.\n*   **Two central issues discussed**: (1) the employment issue, and (2) the surplus funds issue.\n\n**Decisions of the 19 January meeting:**\n\n1.  Employees appointed in violation of mulki rules were to be replaced by locals; displaced Andhra employees were to be given jobs in their own region.\n2.  Employees who had obtained jobs via false mulki certificates were to be inquired into.\n3.  Mulki rules were to be extended to autonomous institutions, not just government departments, and measures taken to prolong the rules'' duration.\n4.  The central government was to enforce court decisions on employee seniority immediately.\n5.  Telangana''s surplus funds diverted to Andhra were to be calculated and used for Telangana''s development, under a senior official appointed for the purpose.\n6.  Educational facilities in Hyderabad city were to be expanded.\n\n*   It was estimated under the agreement that about 4,500 non-Mulki employees were working in Telangana; **G.O. No. 36** was subsequently issued to send Andhra employees back to Andhra.\n\n**Students rejected the All-Party Agreement:** The Osmania Students Union Action Committee resolved to continue the movement; Mallikarjun accused the Chief Minister of holding the meeting merely to placate Telangana public opinion, calling the decisions a repetition of past violations of the Gentlemen''s Agreement.\n\n### 20 January — First Police Firing\n\n*   Students attacked Shamshabad railway station (Umdanagar); police opened fire to disperse them, injuring six students — the first police firing of the movement.\n*   On 21 January, students meeting at Nizam College to protest the Shamshabad firing pelted stones at police who entered the campus; police responded with a lathi charge and tear gas, and arrested 34 students, including Mallikarjun, Sridhar Reddy, Puli Veeranna, Pullareddy, and Madhusudhan Reddy.\n*   On 22 January, Osmania University''s non-teaching staff held a one-day sympathy strike; from this point Osmania University, rather than Nizam College, became the movement''s permanent centre, with students defying the Vice-Chancellor''s warnings.\n\n### 21 January — G.O. No. 36 and the Legal Battle\n\n*   G.O. No. 36 was issued to implement the 18–19 January decisions, stating that all non-natives of Telangana would be sent back by 28 February, 1969.\n*   It specified: non-Mulkis appointed by direct recruitment, promotion, or transfer to Mulki-reserved posts were to be removed immediately and replaced by eligible Mulkis; where eligible natives were unavailable, posts were to remain vacant until they became available; and displaced Andhra employees were to be re-employed in Andhra without disruption to their service, creating additional posts if necessary.\n*   The order, signed by Chief Secretary M.T. Raju, was to be overseen by Revenue Board members I.J. Naidu (IAS) — covering the Secretariat and Hyderabad/Secunderabad offices — and R. Vithal Rao (IAS) — covering Telangana district offices outside the twin cities.\n*   Ex-CM Damodaram Sanjeevaiah publicly opposed the G.O. the same day, remarking that transferring employees was not as simple as \"parcelling vegetables.\"\n\n**Legal challenges to G.O. No. 36:**\n\n*   Andhra employees filed a writ petition in the High Court in January 1969.\n*   On 31 January, 1969, five Telangana women employees also filed a writ, arguing the G.O. would harm their families since their husbands were from Andhra.\n*   On 3 February, 1969, High Court single judge Justice Chinnappa Reddy ruled the Mulki Rules invalid and revoked G.O. No. 36.\n*   On appeal, the High Court''s division bench ruled on 20 February, 1969 that the Mulki Rules were valid and could be applied, but ordered that non-Mulkis should not be sent back to Andhra — supernumerary posts should instead be created where they were.\n*   Andhra employees then appealed to the Supreme Court, which on 28 March, 1969 ruled the Mulki Rules unconstitutional and revoked G.O. No. 36 (this final Supreme Court verdict and its violent aftermath are covered in the next batch).\n\n**Venkatram Reddy withdraws from the Safeguards camp''s protest**: after Kasu Brahmananda Reddy assured him of an all-party agreement''s execution, Venkatram Reddy announced withdrawal of his movement — but Separatist leaders Mallikarjun and Sridhar Reddy resolved to continue agitating.\n\n### 22 January\n\n*   **Withdrawal of Ravindranath''s strike**: under pressure from Jalagam Vengal Rao, Ravindranath ended his fast (begun 8 January); B. Kishan, a founding member of the Osmania University Students Union, persuaded him to take fruit juice.\n*   Journalists Adiraju Venkateswara Rao and Santapuri Raghuveera Rao pledged support to the student movement at an Osmania campus meeting; early political supporters of the student movement included ex-Mayor Rama Murthy Naidu (Congress) and E.V. Padmanabhan.\n*   **Postponement of the Safeguards Movement**: T. Purushottama Rao announced in Warangal that the Safeguards-oriented Telangana Rights Protection Movement was postponed until 28 February — the deadline set for implementing the All-Party Agreement''s guarantees.\n\n### 23 January\n\n*   Potu Krishna Murthy ended his indefinite strike at Palwancha after the government accepted his demands.\n\n### 24 January — The First Martyr\n\n*   At Sadashivpet, police opened fire on a Jai Telangana procession by high school students; Shankar and Krishna were shot and hospitalized at Gandhi Hospital.\n*   Shankar (19), son of handloom-industry worker Narsappa, died on 25 January and is recognized as the **first martyr of the 1969 Telangana movement**; Krishna died later, on 10 February, while under treatment.\n*   The Student Activities Committee met Education Minister PV Narasimha Rao (at VC D.S. Reddy''s building) along with Regional Association president Chokka Rao, attempting to persuade students to withdraw — without success.\n*   A report was separately submitted to Public Relations Minister Konda Laxman Bapuji on the discrimination faced by Telangana employees in the public works, cooperative, forest, and education departments.\n\n### 27 January\n\n*   The Student Action Committee decided to hold a Telangana Conference at Reddy Hostel, Hyderabad in the first week of March, and sent 35 student leaders on a tour of Telangana districts to build support for it.\n*   An unknown assailant set fire to Rangacharya, an Andhra deputy surveyor in Nalgonda, who died the next day — sparking panic among Andhra employees and a counter-movement in Andhra against G.O. No. 36, with Andhra students demanding the capital be shifted from Hyderabad to Vijayawada.\n\n### 28 January — Telangana Liberation Movement Committee\n\n*   Under Kaloji Narayana Rao''s leadership, a summit was held in Warangal, resolving that the Chief Minister should resign and President''s Rule be imposed.\n\n### 29 January — Communal Clashes and \"Shoot at Sight\" Orders\n\n*   The counter-movement in Andhra against G.O. No. 36 intensified; Nandigama residents attacked Kodada residents with stones and sticks, prompting the army to conduct flag marches for peacekeeping in Hyderabad, Warangal, Kodada, Kothagudem, Vijayawada, Guntur, and Vizag.\n*   Andhra University students in Sri Venkateswara began agitating to cancel G.O. No. 36 and preserve a united Andhra (Samaikhyandhra).\n*   Given the threat to life and property, the government issued shoot-at-sight orders; in Warangal, police used lathi charges, tear gas, and firing against students, who retaliated by destroying Andhra-owned property.\n*   Osmania University made students vacate hostels.\n*   MP Mir Akbar Alikhan appealed to the Chief Minister to set up representative peacekeeping bodies across all districts and in Hyderabad city.\n\n### 30 January\n\n*   Narasimhulu, a 12-year-old seventh-class student, died in police firing at Ghazwel.\n*   Lok Sabha Speaker Neelam Sanjivreddy visited Hyderabad and expressed regret over the unfolding events.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p5", "partName": "Formation of the Telangana Praja Samithi (February–March 1969)", "bodyMd": "### 1 February — Andhra Attack on Telangana Workers\n\n*   Andhra people attacked the Eegalapenta colony inhabited by Telangana employees and workers building the Srisailam Dam.\n*   Public Relations Minister Konda Laxman Bapuji visited Eegalapenta on 17 February to investigate, condemned the attack, and distributed clothing provided by the Telangana NGOs'' association to the victims.\n\n### 5 February\n\n*   Telanganists attacked the house of an Andhra assistant manager in the Bellampally coal-mining area; police opened fire on the protesters.\n\n### 17 February\n\n*   Educational institutions, closed since 15 January because of the movement, reopened, but students boycotted classes across Telangana and took to the streets shouting \"Jai Telangana\" slogans.\n\n### 22–24 February\n\n*   The government appointed a five-minister sub-committee to prepare a joint seniority list of state employees.\n*   52 MLAs warned the government they would withdraw support if Telangana protections were not implemented.\n*   On 23 February, KV Rangareddy invited all-party leaders for talks on the Telangana issue.\n*   On 24 February, an Andhra Pradesh Integration Conference, organized by both communist parties, was held under K. China Venkata Rama Rao''s chairmanship.\n\n### 28 February — Formation of the Telangana People''s Convention\n\n*   Under young Siddipet lawyer Anantula Madan Mohan''s leadership, the Telangana People''s Convention was formed, later evolving into the Telangana Praja Samithi (TPS).\n*   Telangana intellectuals, lawyers, doctors, journalists, and business communities united in this body to press for Telangana statehood.\n\n### 3 March — The First Bandh\n\n*   As Assembly proceedings were set to begin, the People''s Convention (led by Madan Mohan) and student leader Mallikarjun called a Telangana bandh, urging peaceful satyagraha and picketing; the Bollarum railway station was attacked by protesters.\n*   The government, through the public relations ministry (without the minister''s consent), issued a press release with figures falsely claiming Telangana was better developed than Andhra between 1958–68.\n*   The Jana Sangh, Majlis, and Communist parties issued a joint statement opposing the bandh.\n*   Students prevented Minister Sheelam Siddha Reddy from holding an anti-bandh meeting in Nalgonda, forcing him to shift it to Khammam.\n*   Madan Mohan told journalists that Telangana''s case for separation mirrored Andhra''s own historical case for separating from Madras.\n*   T. Purushottama Rao and G.V. Sudhakar Rao boycotted the Legislative Assembly in solidarity with the bandh call.\n*   The bandh succeeded, and 52 Telangana legislators warned they would deny the government their support if safeguards were not implemented by 15 March.\n*   **CPI''s parallel push for state unity**: Telangana CPI leader Ravi Narayana Reddy chaired a committee (secretary: Rajabahdur Gour) aimed at implementing safeguards while preserving state integration.\n\n### 6 March — Conference of B.C. Associations\n\n*   Chaired by opposition leader Gouthu Lacchanna, the conference demanded Brahmananda Reddy''s immediate resignation and the appointment of a Backward Classes leader from Telangana as Chief Minister.\n\n### 8–9 March — Reddy Hostel Convention\n\n*   The two-day Telangana Conference at Reddy Hostel, Hyderabad, presided over by T.N. Sadalaxmi, pressed for a separate Telangana state.\n*   Welcoming remarks by Physics professor Ravada Satyanarayana argued for a separate public service commission and budget for Telangana as a practical solution.\n*   C.H. Venkateswara Rao (former Osmania Students'' Union president) said Telangana people continued to survive as second-class citizens dependent on unsympathetic Andhra rulers.\n*   Speakers included S.B. Giri, Mallikarjun, Sridhar Reddy, Vandemataram Ramachandra Rao, Y. Rammurthi Naidu, and Major Rangaswami.\n\n**Resolutions of the convention:**\n\n*   No acceptance of anything short of a separate Telangana.\n*   An indefinite student strike until statehood is achieved.\n*   About 30,000 attendees pledged to work for separate statehood.\n*   A target date of 9 April, 1969 for forming the state, with President''s Rule to be imposed until then.\n*   Legislator T. Purushottama Rao unveiled a map of the proposed Telangana state, prepared on 7 March by E.V. Padmanabham, Adiraju Venkateswara Rao, and Leader Magazine editor Muneer Jamal.\n*   Student leader Sridhar Reddy gave the slogan \"Quit Telangana\" for the first time.\n*   Announcement of a new organization, **Rudra Sena**, to lead the movement.\n*   Advocate Jagan Mohan Reddy administered a pledge to thousands of students to work for statehood (\"We, the brave Telangana sons, pledge that all will sacrifice anything to achieve the status of a separate state...\").\n*   Warangal Students'' Union secretary Siddhulu and Osmania Arts College president Ramakant announced a boycott of colleges and schools until statehood was achieved.\n*   Sada Lakshmi criticized the government''s continuing anti-Telangana policy despite the 19 January All-Party Agreement.\n*   E.V. Padmanabhan''s reading-out of Andhra officials'' names holding key state posts drew \"shame, shame\" chants from the crowd.\n\n**Follow-up organizing:**\n\n*   On 12 March, 1969, Warangal Arts & Science College hosted a meeting of student-union executives, chaired by R.E.C. College president Gopal Reddy, forming the **Warangal Student''s Joint Action Committee**.\n*   On 8 March, Andhra leader Vavilala Gopala Krishnaiah criticized both Telangana attacks on Andhra people and irresponsible press coverage inflaming tensions.\n\n### 10 March — Telangana Employees-Teachers Action Committee\n\n*   An employees'' action committee (led by K.R. Amos) and a teachers'' action committee (led by Balakrishna Reddy) merged to form the Telangana Employees–Teachers Action Committee.\n*   The committee decided to observe Telangana Employees'' Day on 17 March and to launch a strike from the first week of April.\n\n### 12 March — Korrapati Pattabhiramayya''s Fast\n\n*   Korrapati Pattabhiramaiah, from Andhra''s East Godavari district, had begun fasting on 4 March before the Assembly demanding a separate Telangana state; he was arrested on 12 March, his strike broken, and admitted to Osmania General Hospital, where he continued fasting until 16 March.\n*   A former Communist leader and editor of _Nava Shakti_, he had supported the movement from the start and authored the pamphlet _Why Telangana State_ (_Telangana Rashtram Endukoraku_).\n\n### Efforts for \"Emotional Integration\" (Bhavasamaikyata Praja Sangatana)\n\n*   Politicians and Brahmananda Reddy supporters who favoured keeping the state united — believing implementation of safeguards was sufficient — tried to suppress the movement.\n*   Swami Ramananda Tirtha founded the \"Public Events for Emotional Integration\" organization for this purpose, chairing its first meeting on 12 March, 1969; further meetings followed on 17th, 18th, and 19th March under Kodati Narayana Rao''s chairmanship.\n\n### 13 March\n\n*   The Chief Minister announced Rs 9 crore in additional funding (Rs 1 crore per district) beyond the regular Telangana budget allocation, appointed an additional chief secretary for Telangana, and doubled funding for the Pochampahad project.\n\n### 14 March — Slogans in the Assembly\n\n*   14 Telangana students in the assembly press gallery raised \"Jai Telangana\" slogans, including \"Andhra''s predominance should perish, we shall break up.\"\n*   Newly elected Congress MLA Jaipal Reddy argued a separate state would not solve Telangana''s problems.\n*   Student leader Sridhar Reddy was arrested along with three others.\n\n### 15 March\n\n*   Governor Khandubhai Kasamji Desai''s visit to Osmania University for a Golden Jubilee stamp release was disrupted by students under Sridhar Reddy chanting \"Governor go back\"; many, including Sridhar Reddy, were arrested. A similar disruption recurred the next day when the Governor went to unveil the Nehru statue at Abids.\n*   **Telangana Safeguards United Movement**, chaired by Karimnagar Zilla Parishad president D. Hanumantha Rao, warned that if safeguards were not implemented by 25 March, Telangana''s public representatives should resign by 31 March.\n*   The Telangana People''s Convention designated 17 March \"Democracy Protection Day.\"\n*   The Telangana Praja Parishad established local associations in Secunderabad Basti, with S.B. Giri, Agamaiah, and Shankaraiah explaining the case for a separate state.\n*   A \"Telangana Student, Citizen Joint Convention\" in Karimnagar, inaugurated by Sada Lakshmi, drew ten thousand attendees who pledged to continue the struggle until statehood was achieved.\n\n### 16 March\n\n*   The Andhra Pradesh Teachers Association was formed to represent Andhra teachers working in Telangana.\n\n### 17 March — Telangana Struggle Day / Extension of Mulki Rules\n\n*   Telangana teachers observed Telangana Struggle Day, while students marked Democracy Protection Day with picketing at government offices; a Narayanguda student rally clashed with police, leading to stone-pelting.\n*   N.G. Ranga, from Andhra, supported a separate Telangana, arguing that if multiple states existed for Hindi speakers, there was no reason two states could not exist for Telugu speakers.\n*   Parliament passed a bill extending the Mulki Rules for Telangana by another five years (due to expire 20 March, 1969), issued under the Public Employment Act, 1959, and amended the Act to extend Mulki Rules to public-sector corporations and boards.\n\n### 21 March\n\n*   Former Deputy CM Konda Venkata Rangareddy declared separate Telangana his goal, remarking that a Telangana person becoming CM only \"by the mercy of Andhras\" would still have to follow Andhra''s instructions.\n*   Union Home Minister Y.B. Chavan announced in the Lok Sabha that he had no intention of reconsidering a separate Telangana state.\n\n### 22 March\n\n*   Konda Laxman Bapuji appealed to the Prime Minister and Delhi leaders to grant Telangana independent-region status akin to Meghalaya, but received no response; he was accompanied by Regional Committee chairperson Chokka Rao.\n*   Rayalaseema and Coastal Andhra MPs Changalraya Nayudu and Kommareddy Surya Narayana stated in Delhi that Andhra had no objection to a separate Telangana if the Centre approved it.\n*   A student conference for statehood was held in Warangal on 22–23 March, chaired by Purushottama Rao.\n\n### 23 March\n\n*   The city''s civic body (Nagara Poura Samithi) resolved to intensify the movement.\n*   Telangana activists threw tomatoes and rubble at Union Minister Gurupadaswamy''s car and stones at Minister Ramaswamy''s car.\n\n### 24 March\n\n*   On Union Minister J.B. Muthyala Rao''s instructions, the government appointed Abdul Qadar, a senior Telangana official, as additional chief secretary on a temporary basis.\n*   With the student movement intensifying and students disengaged from exams, Osmania colleges declared holidays from 25 March to 9 June.\n\n### 25 March — Telangana Praja Samithi Formed\n\n*   The Telangana People''s Convention (formed February 1969) was converted into the **Telangana Praja Samithi**.\n\n| TPS Office | Holder |\n|---|---|\n| 1st President | Ananthula Madan Mohan (Siddipet lawyer) |\n| Chief Secretary | S. Venkatrami Reddy |\n| Treasurer | Nagari Krishna |\n\n*   A 17-member ad hoc committee, made up of young lawyers, teachers, journalists, and intellectuals, was formed alongside it.\n\n### 26 March\n\n*   Telangana teachers began a hunger strike in front of the Secretariat demanding removal of non-mulkis; police demolished all hunger-strike camps in the city that night.\n\n### 27 March\n\n*   Achyutha Reddy (first chairman of the Telangana Regional Committee) declared that only a separate state would satisfy Telangana''s people.\n*   Congress National President Nijalingappa called the agitation \"a bad wind which does not benefit anyone.\"\n*   (Earlier, in 1955–56, former MLA and AP Bar Council president S. Malakonda Reddy had already suggested a separate Telangana state would be preferable.)\n\n### 28 March — Supreme Court Verdict and Its Aftermath\n\n*   A five-member Supreme Court bench headed by Chief Justice M. Hidayatullah cancelled G.O. No. 36, ruling the Mulki provisions unconstitutional (this followed the Court''s 20 February overturning of the High Court''s earlier pro-Mulki verdict — see the G.O. No. 36 sequence in the previous section).\n*   Riots broke out in Telangana upon the verdict; angered Osmania students set fire to the nearby Jamai Usmania railway station.\n*   Two engineering students, Prakash Kumar Jain (Khammam) and P. Sarva Reddy (Mahabubnagar), were accidentally caught in the fire and died; medical student Nagam Janardhan Reddy also took part in the incident.\n*   A condolence meeting for the students was held at Nizam College Grounds on 29 March.\n*   **Andhra students attacked Telangana students** with iron rods at the Regional Engineering College (REC), Warangal, on 28 March — provoked by Andhra officials at REC after Telangana students sought postponed exams; police arrested only the Telangana students.\n*   **Resignation of Konda Laxman Bapuji**: as Minister of State for Public Relations, he resigned on 28 March after the Centre rejected the proposal to grant Telangana territorial status — becoming the **first minister to resign over the Telangana issue**. PV Narasimha Rao succeeded him as Public Relations Minister. (Bapuji had earlier tendered, and withdrawn, resignations on 11 and 20 March at the Chief Minister''s request.)\n\n### 30 March\n\n*   Konda Laxman Bapuji convened a \"Separate Telangana Congress Committee.\" (He had earlier sought only Meghalaya-style territorial status; his first explicit call for a fully separate Telangana state came later, on 14 May, 1969.)\n*   **Backward Classes meeting**, chaired by Gouthu Lachanna, resolved that: Brahmananda Reddy should resign in favour of a Telangana leader; exams across the state should be postponed to protect the academic year; a judicial inquiry should be held into recent unrest; and National Liberation Front (Scheduled Castes) chairman K.R. Veeraswami warned of possible armed revolt in Telangana if the Centre failed to act.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p6", "partName": "The Eight-Point Formula and National Response (April 1969)", "bodyMd": "### 1 April — Lok Sabha Debate\n\n*   Under pressure from Nizamabad MP Narayana Reddy, the Telangana issue was debated in the Lok Sabha.\n*   Speaker Neelam Sanjivreddy suggested appointing a parliamentary committee to examine the issue, acknowledging that the Gentlemen''s Agreement promises had gone unfulfilled during his own tenure as CM — and that this made him reluctant to act unilaterally.\n*   Home Minister Y.B. Chavan resisted sending a parliamentary committee, and Chief Minister Brahmananda Reddy opposed the proposal outright.\n\n### 3 April\n\n*   **Citizens'' Integration Association** was formed, chaired by MP G. Venkataswamy, to oppose Telangana''s separation and preserve state unity (Venkataswamy later resigned this post in June, having grown critical of the government''s handling of the agitation).\n*   A Central government team, led by Union Home Secretary L.P. Singh (with D.D. Sathe, Lalwani, and Duth), visited Hyderabad to inspect the situation.\n\n### 4 April — Firing at Secunderabad\n\n*   The Communist Party held a \"Vishalandhra Sabha\" at Boorugu Mahadev Hall, Secunderabad, drawing protests from thousands of Telangana activists; leaders present included Rajbahdur Gour, Magdum Moinuddin, and Neelam Rajshekar Reddy.\n*   Police firing on the protesters killed three people (Prakasham, Ramulu, and one other) and injured over 30; District Collector Shravan Kumar was tasked with a magisterial inquiry.\n*   Konda Laxman Bapuji visited the firing site and undertook a hunger strike at home from 5–8 April in protest.\n*   The TPS held a mourning session at Nizam College Grounds on 6 April for the firing victims.\n\n### 6 April\n\n*   Preventive Detention (PD) Act was invoked for the first time in the movement to arrest protesters, including TPS president Madan Mohan, Mallikarjun, Pratap Kishore, Adiraju Venkateshwara Rao, Madhava Reddy, Jaleel Pasha, Purushottama Rao, Raghottama Reddy, T. Siddulu, G. Janardhan, V. Vaikuntam, and S. Satyanarayana.\n*   With Madan Mohan detained, **S.B. Giri was appointed TPS President**.\n*   A Women''s Conference for separate Telangana was held at Gandhi Bhavan, with Sangham Lakshmibai, Shantabai, and T.N. Sadhalakshmi present; Sada Lakshmi argued there was nothing wrong with two states for Telugu speakers.\n\n### 7 April — Coordinating Committee and Rights Resolution\n\n*   Representatives of various unions formed the **Coordinating Committee of Telangana Movement** with KV Ranga Reddy''s help, to prevent the arrests of students and leaders from stalling the movement; Achyutha Reddy was its president.\n*   In the Lok Sabha, SSP leader Madhu Limaye proposed a rights resolution criticizing Brahmananda Reddy''s remark that a parliamentary committee would interfere in state affairs, arguing the state had repeatedly ignored the Regional Committee''s recommendations for Telangana''s welfare.\n*   Jana Sangh leader Vajpayee argued a parliamentary committee would reaffirm parliamentary supremacy; CPI leader Dange supported the committee''s constitutional basis; PNP''s Samarendra Kundu backed it too.\n*   Andhra Congress MPs obstructed the debate in defence of Brahmananda Reddy, while Telangana woman MP Laxmikantamma supported him.\n*   The Lok Sabha rejected Madhu Limaye''s resolution by voice vote, though the debate publicized Telangana''s grievances.\n\n### 9 April — Mass Satyagraha Day and National Party Positions\n\n*   TPS President S.B. Giri called for mass satyagrahas across Telangana; police arrested satyagrahis and Giri himself, after which **Sada Laxmi was elected TPS President**.\n*   Thousands arrested since 5 April turned schools and colleges into makeshift prisons.\n*   The **Swatantra Party** became the first political party to formally demand a separate Telangana.\n*   The **Republican Party**''s National Working Group urged the Centre to impose President''s Rule, and separately appealed for a Supreme Court judge-led high-level committee, invoking the Fazal Ali Commission''s recommendations if a separate state proved necessary.\n*   Prime Minister Indira Gandhi held an all-party meeting in Delhi on the creation of a separate state: only N.G. Ranga (Swatantra) supported it; SSP''s Rabi Ray proposed a constitutional amendment making Telangana a sub-state; Vajpayee said the Jana Sangh would push for integration instead.\n\n### 10–11 April — The Eight-Point Formula\n\n*   On 10 April, Prime Minister Indira Gandhi held a high-level meeting with Telangana representatives (Mari Chenna Reddy, J. Chokka Rao, V.B. Raju, Nukala Ramchandra Reddy) and Andhra representatives (Kakani Venkata Ratnam, Raguramaiah, Sanjeevaiah), formulating the **Eight-Point Formula**.\n*   On 11 April, the PM announced the formula in the Lok Sabha, promising measures to widen employment opportunities and accelerate development programmes for Telangana.\n\n**The Eight Points:**\n\n1.  A high-level committee, under a Supreme Court judge, to calculate Telangana''s surplus funds diverted to Andhra Pradesh, reporting within a month.\n2.  Provision of funds to cover Telangana''s losses from surplus-fund transfers.\n3.  A regional development committee, chaired by the Chief Minister, with Planning Commission, Telangana ministerial, and Regional Committee representation, to plan Telangana''s development.\n4.  A planning-implementation committee at the official level, under a Planning Commission advisor, with Union Home/Finance Ministry and state government representation, for periodic review of Telangana schemes.\n5.  Enhanced powers for the Telangana Regional Committee and related authorities.\n6.  A constitutional arrangement to reserve local jobs for Telangana natives in the Telangana region.\n7.  A UPSC-affiliated committee to address Telangana employees'' service-related issues.\n8.  Six-monthly Telangana Development Committee meetings chaired by the Prime Minister.\n\n*   On 25 April, 1969, the government set up the **Telangana Development Committee**, chaired by the Chief Minister, to implement the formula, with Planning Commission member Venkatraman, Regional Committee president J. Chokkarao, and Revenue Minister VB Raju as key members, alongside other Telangana ministers (J.V. Narsinga Rao, Gurumurthy, PV Narasimha Rao, KV Narayana Reddy, Md. Ibrahim, Arige Ramaswamy); the Planning and Panchayati Raj Secretary served as the committee''s secretary.\n    *   **Functions**: separating Telangana''s schemes within the Five-Year Plan; setting financial and project-specific targets; and reviewing implementation, advising the state government periodically.\n\n**Criticism of the Eight-Point Formula:**\n\n*   Konda Laxman Bapuji argued the problem was fundamentally political, not just about safeguards and jobs, and that central leaders refused to recognize the need for a political solution.\n*   TPS President Sadalakshmi said the Delhi meeting''s proposed solutions were unnecessary.\n*   Jana Sangh''s Vajpayee questioned whether the formula would actually improve the situation, given that the entire linguistic-state theory itself was being challenged in Telangana.\n\n### 13 April\n\n*   The \"Employee Association Unions Joint Conference,\" representing the Safeguards camp, declared it had no connection to the separate-Telangana movement; convener T. Ramabrahmam released a Five-Point Manifesto and announced a strike from 15 April.\n\n### 15 April\n\n*   On TPS instructions, protesters observed Telangana Struggle Day, rejecting the Eight-Point Formula; protesters pelted stones at Labour Minister K.V. Narayana Reddy''s car in Khairatabad.\n*   Gouthu Lachanna (opposition leader) met the Prime Minister to press for a separate state.\n*   The **Telangana Advocates'' Association**, under B.C. Jain, was formed; Telangana advocates boycotted court duties on this day in support of statehood.\n\n### 17 April\n\n*   The Warangal Municipal Council, in a special meeting presided by Uma Reddy, resolved to demand a separate Telangana state given the non-implementation of safeguards.\n*   Konda Laxman reiterated to the Prime Minister his (earlier, more limited) demand for Telangana as an independent sub-state within Andhra Pradesh, modelled on Meghalaya''s status within Assam.\n\n### 20 April — Marri Chenna Reddy Enters the Movement\n\n*   Congress leader Marri Chenna Reddy publicly declared there was no alternative to a separate Telangana.\n*   He noted that, as a signatory and drafter of the Gentlemen''s Agreement, he had trusted Andhra representatives'' assurances at the time, but had since recognized their true attitude, and would have written stronger safeguards had he known then what he knew now.\n*   Until this point the movement had been led by students and young people without major political involvement; the central government had treated it merely as a student agitation and a law-and-order matter.\n*   The TPS concluded that experienced political leaders like Chenna Reddy and Konda Laxman Bapuji were now needed to engage directly with the Centre and Prime Minister.\n*   The movement, under Chenna Reddy''s leadership, entered its **second stage**; in May, he became TPS President, reorganizing it with a more statesmanlike approach and later transforming the TPS into a full political party.\n\n### 22 April — Telangana Deception Day\n\n*   Telangana activists observed \"Telangana Vanchana Dinam\" (Deception Day), burning effigies of Telangana ministers and the Chief Minister and staging black-flag demonstrations.\n*   Police firing on a rally at Cherial (Jangaon Taluq) during the observance killed Shakir Ali and Madiga Mallaiah.\n*   The Samyuktha Socialist Party (SSP), meeting in Vijayawada under Sitharami Reddy, passed a resolution supporting a separate state in view of Telangana''s democratic rights and aspirations.\n\n### Committees on Telangana''s Surplus Funds\n\n**1\\. Kumar Lalith Committee**\n\n*   As part of the 19 January All-Party Agreement, the Brahmananda Reddy government set up this committee (headed by CAG official Kumar Lalith) on 23 January, 1969, tasked with estimating Telangana''s surplus funds for the period 1 November 1956 to 31 March 1968.\n*   Kumar Lalith submitted his report on 7 March, 1969, finding that, combined with excise duty, Telangana''s Revenue Account showed a surplus of Rs 102 crore, of which the net surplus was **Rs 63.92 crore**; after adjusting for capital-account surpluses over the preceding two years, the unspent Telangana surplus remaining was estimated at **Rs 34.10 crore**.\n*   The Telangana Regional Committee alleged the committee had made calculation errors and, factoring in the surplus as of Andhra Pradesh''s formation, put the true total surplus at **Rs 107.13 crore**; it also criticized the committee for excluding statewide boards'' and corporations'' income from its calculations.\n*   To resolve the dispute, the Centre agreed, as part of the Eight-Point Formula (11 April, 1969), to appoint a fresh authority to calculate the surplus — leading to the Bhargava Committee.\n\n**2\\. Justice Vashishtha Bhargava Committee**\n\n*   Announced on 22 April, 1969 under the Eight-Point Formula, this higher-level committee examined Telangana''s surplus funds for the period from the state''s 1956 formation to 31 March, 1968.\n\n| Role | Holder |\n|---|---|\n| President | Vashishtha Bhargava |\n| Members | Vihari Mathur, Hari Bhushan |\n| Secretary | Krishna Swamy |\n\n*   The government asked for the report by 31 May, 1969.\n*   The Telangana Regional Committee argued before this committee — contrary to the Lalith Committee''s approach — that Andhra''s own regional revenue deficit or surplus should not factor into calculating Telangana''s surplus.\n*   The committee submitted a 123-page report in November 1969, which the government did not formally disclose.\n*   **Findings**: the Telangana surplus for 1956–1968 was estimated at **Rs 28.34 crore**; the report confirmed these surplus funds had not been spent in Telangana, and recommended further measures to implement the Gentlemen''s Agreement.\n*   Telangana representatives and the Regional Committee opposed this report''s findings.\n*   Following this, the central government directed the state to allocate an additional **Rs 45 crore** — over and above the usual one-third budget share — for Telangana''s development by the end of the Fourth Five-Year Plan (starting 1 April, 1968); the Centre provided this Rs 45 crore as a grant to the state government.\n\n**3\\. Wanchoo Committee**\n\n*   Appointed by the central government on 19 April, 1969, as promised under the Eight-Point Formula, this panel of judicial experts studied Telangana employees'' issues and the constitutional viability of continuing Mulki-style protections.\n\n| Role | Holder |\n|---|---|\n| President | K.N. Wanchoo (former Chief Justice) |\n| Members | M.C. Sethalvad (ex-Attorney General), Nirendra (Attorney General) |\n\n**Wanchoo Committee findings:**\n\n*   Continuing residency-based protections for Telangana employees within the Andhra Pradesh government was not legally or constitutionally sustainable.\n*   Parliament had the power to legislate a state''s overall preference in state employment, but not for a specific area within a state.\n*   Continuing Mulki-style provisions was therefore not feasible, and a constitutional amendment to preserve them was unlikely.\n*   **Key recommendation**: the Telangana Regional Committee should be delegated authority over subordinate services in the Telangana region, including administrative authority over 3rd- and 4th-class state-level jobs.\n*   It further recommended that recruitment authority for 4th-class employees be transferred from the Andhra Pradesh Public Service Commission to the District Collectors.\n\n### 24 April — Jayaprakash Narayan''s Support\n\n*   Visiting Tirupati, veteran Sarvodaya leader Jayaprakash Narayan said, in response to a question on Telangana, that the formation of smaller states would strengthen the Centre.\n\n### 25 April — TPS Electoral Win and Release of Leaders\n\n*   In the April 1969 Hyderabad District Local Councils elections, TPS candidate S. Venkatrami Reddy (Medchal Panchayat Samiti president) defeated Congress''s Koheda Prabhakar Reddy (Hayatnagar Samiti president) by a narrow margin of **53 votes** (305 to 252) — giving the movement a major morale boost.\n*   The same day, a High Court division bench (Chief Justice Pingali Jaganmohan Reddy and Justice Aavula Sambasivara Rao) ordered the release of Madan Mohan, Mallikarjun, Venkateswara Rao, and eight others detained under the PD Act.\n\n### 27 April\n\n*   Congress National President Nijalingappa, at the party''s 72nd session, argued the country should be restructured on economic rather than linguistic lines.\n*   Former Deputy CM Konda Venkata Rangareddy issued an eight-point statement declaring there was no substitute for a separate Telangana, arguing the past 12 years had shown Andhra rulers lacked \"honesty and selflessness.\"\n\n### 30 April\n\n*   Ravada Satyanarayana (Physics professor) was appointed Osmania University''s Vice-Chancellor upon D.S. Reddy''s completion of tenure.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p7", "partName": "May Day and the Split in Leadership (May 1969)", "bodyMd": "### 1 May, 1969 — May Day / Demands Day, and the Black Day\n\n*   TPS observed 1 May as \"Telangana Demands Day,\" planning a joint parade from Charminar and a procession from Secunderabad to Raj Bhavan, intending to present a report to the Governor.\n*   Police permitted the procession only from public gardens in Nampally rather than Charminar, but the TPS called for large numbers to gather at Charminar regardless, and thousands did so, defying the prohibition.\n*   KV Ranga Reddy delivered a historic speech at Charminar, declaring \"Death is better than slavery to the Andhras,\" calling the state \"the government of the bandits and the government of thieves,\" and condemning the exploitation of backward Telangana for selfish ends.\n*   Mallikarjun, Madan Mohan, and Keshava Rao Jadhav led the Charminar procession; as it reached Siddi Amber Bazaar mosque, police fired, killing three people on the spot.\n*   Protesters set fire to Maharajganj police station in response; two more people were killed when sentries fired in self-defence during this incident.\n*   The Charminar procession reached the public gardens.\n*   The Secunderabad procession, led by S.B. Giri, Nagam Krishna, and Gouthu Lachanna, saw S.B. Giri arrested as it approached the parade ground; it nonetheless reached the public gardens and merged with the Charminar procession.\n*   The combined procession proceeded to Raj Bhavan and submitted a petition to the Governor with a single demand: dismiss the government and impose President''s Rule.\n*   Umender Rao, president of the Secunderabad College Students'' Union, died in police firing on the crowd at Raj Bhavan.\n*   Unofficial estimates put the day''s death toll at up to 20, with many more injured — May Day 1969 is remembered as a \"black day\" in Hyderabad''s history.\n\n**The Prime Minister''s belated outreach:**\n\n*   When the Eight-Point Formula was announced on 11 April, the Prime Minister had not consulted Telangana movement leaders directly; senior journalist Potturi Venkateswara Rao wrote to her flagging this, prompting Indira Gandhi to invite 15 TPS and other Telangana leaders to Delhi talks on 6 May.\n*   However, TPS General Secretary S. Venkatram Reddy wrote back on 5 May stating there was no need for talks with the Prime Minister at that stage.\n\n### 2 May — Bomb Attack Amid Continuing Unrest\n\n*   A citywide strike protested the 1 May violence; advocate E.V. Padmanabham was arrested for his alleged role in the 1 May riots.\n*   On 4 May, protesters Narender and Kumar bombed a police van in Secunderabad in retaliation for the violence, killing constable Farukh Ali; Narender and Kumar were later sentenced to three years'' imprisonment, and P.J. Suri was arrested for possessing bomb-making materials.\n*   On 3 May, Telangana leaders decided to temporarily suspend the movement out of respect for the mourning period following President Zakir Hussain''s death.\n\n### 4 May — Vishalandhra Unity Meet\n\n*   The two Communist parties held a Vishalandhra Unity (Integrity) Convention at Nakirekal, presided over by Nakirekal legislator Narra Raghava Reddy.\n\n### 7 May — TNGO Meeting\n\n*   Telangana NGOs, meeting at Gandhi Bhavan under interim president SNN Chari, declared that a separate Telangana was the only way to protect the region''s employees.\n\n### 8 May — Release of KR Amos\n\n*   The government ordered the release of TNGO president KR Amos, who had been arrested on 31 March.\n\n### 12 May\n\n*   The Andhra Pradesh Teachers Union, meeting in Hyderabad, backed the demand for a separate state.\n*   Addressing Mahabubnagar Congress activists, Marri Chenna Reddy warned the Centre that failure to form a separate Telangana would allow Naxalite forces to exploit the resulting tension, causing serious damage to the country and the state.\n\n### 14–15 May\n\n*   Konda Laxman Bapuji, on 14 May, made his **first explicit demand** for a fully separate Telangana state (having earlier sought only regional/sub-state recognition).\n*   On 15 May, Konda Venkata Rangareddy resigned his post and declared his support for a separate Telangana.\n\n### 17 May — Telangana Dead Heroes Day\n\n*   Hyderabad observed \"Telangana Dead Heroes Day,\" paying tribute to the movement''s martyrs; protesters raised the slogan \"Lathi, Goli khayenge, Telangana layenge\" (\"We''ll face batons and bullets, but we''ll bring Telangana\").\n\n### 20 May — Telangana College Teachers Convention\n\n*   Held at YMCA Hall under Prof. Shahmanjur Aalam''s chairmanship, with an inaugural address by Osmania VC Dr. Rawada Satyanarayana.\n*   Acharya Jayashankar and Ananda Rao presented research on injustices to the Telangana region; their papers were published as _Telangana Movement & Investigative Focus_, and Prof. Jayashankar''s article on the Nagarjuna Sagar project''s impact on Telangana was discussed in Parliament, prompting the Prime Minister to invite Jayashankar to Delhi for consultation.\n*   The event''s closing address was given by Marri Chenna Reddy.\n\n### 22 May — Split in the Telangana Praja Samithi\n\n*   Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy assumed the TPS presidency, causing a split within the organization, as student leaders who had driven the movement so far resented the politicians'' takeover.\n*   TPS leader Sridhar Reddy announced a rival **\"Rebel\" (Poti) Telangana Praja Samithi** the same day in protest.\n    *   Politicians backing the Rebel TPS included Vandemataram Ramachandra Rao, E.V. Padmanabhan, Badri Vishal Pitti, S.B. Giri, Santhapuri Raghuveera Rao, and G.M. Anjaiah.\n    *   Marxist Forum president Y. Srinivasa Rao pledged to work with Sridhar Reddy for a separate state.\n\n### 24 May — Cancellation of TNGO Recognition\n\n*   KR Amos warned employees that there would be bloodshed if a separate Telangana was not formed by 1 June, 1969.\n*   The government responded by cancelling recognition of the Telangana Teachers'' and Employees'' Unions on 25 May, and, under Article 311(2) of the Constitution, the Governor dismissed Amos from his job for statements likely to provoke unrest.\n*   Amos — who had worked in the Industries Department under the Burgula government since 1956, and had fought for Telangana employees'' rights since then — is described as the **pioneer of the separate-state movement**; he was arrested under the PD Act on 27 May.\n*   Students began an indefinite hunger strike on 30 May protesting his dismissal, and on 31 May people sent donations to press offices to support the now-unemployed Amos.\n\n### 26 May — Rebel TNGO, MLA Suspensions, and Satyagraha\n\n*   Government-employee supporters of the Chief Minister met in Hyderabad under Mohammad Ghouse''s chairmanship and formed a **Rebel TNGO**, with Harinath Rao as temporary president; the new body defended the government''s dismissal of Amos, whom it accused of misleading the Telangana NGOs and politicizing the union.\n*   **Six Telangana Congress MLAs** — K. Achyutha Reddy, T. Anjaiah, G. Rajaram, P. Narsinga Rao, MM Hashim, and C. Manikya Rao — were boycotted by the PCC for three years for participating in the movement, having already received show-cause notices from AP PCC President Kakani Venkataratnam.\n*   Chenna Reddy launched the movement''s **second phase of satyagraha**, held across Telangana; leaders arrested included Sada Laxmi (TPS Vice-President, at Abids Chourasta), T. Govind Singh (near the Secretariat), and Nagam Krishna (Rashtrapathi Road).\n\n### 28 May\n\n*   Following the Chief Minister''s 25 May visit to a Vishalandhra-supporters'' meeting in Nagar Kurnool (Mahabubnagar district) — where police lathi-charged and fired on protesting Telangana activists in Nagarkurnool and Jadcharla — Assembly Chief Whip K. Ramachandra Reddy resigned in protest of the violence against Nagarkurnool''s people.\n\n### 31 May — Jail Violence and National Party Support\n\n*   Andhra prisoners attacked Telangana satyagrahi-prisoners with sticks in Chanchalguda Jail, with jail officials siding with the Andhra prisoners; 80 Telangana activists were injured, 24 requiring hospitalization.\n*   Communist leader Dr. Rajabahadur Gour, on a week-long hunger strike demanding the state government''s dismissal, was persuaded by Konda Laxman Bapuji to end his strike with lemon juice.\n*   Charan Singh (president, Indian Kranti Dal) extended support for the movement, joining the Swatantra Party and the Samyuktha Socialist Party, which had already done so.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p8", "partName": "The Movement Through Mid-to-Late 1969", "bodyMd": "### 1 June — Telangana PCC Formed\n\n*   The **Telangana Pradesh Congress Committee (Telangana PCC)** was constituted at Prakasham Hall, Gandhi Bhavan.\n\n| Office | Holder |\n|---|---|\n| President | Konda Laxman Bapuji |\n| Vice Presidents | K. Rajamallu (MLA), Sangam Laxmibai (MP) |\n\n*   Konda Venkata Rangareddy, Dr. Chenna Reddy, and other Telangana Congress leaders attended the formation meeting.\n*   KV Ranga Reddy argued that the Gentlemen''s Agreement''s provision for a Telangana PCC had already been violated when the Hyderabad Pradesh Congress Committee was dissolved in 1958, and that forming the Telangana PCC was a way of asserting Telangana''s independence and energy.\n*   AP PCC President Kakani Venkataratnam dismissed the Telangana PCC as illegal and without recognition, calling it a \"selfish organisation.\"\n*   The **Telangana Magazine Writers Association** was also formed the same day.\n\n**Journalists in the movement:**\n\n*   The **Telangana Press Association** was formed on 1 June, and the Telangana Journalists Union met the same day in Hyderabad under Pratap Kishore (Telangana Journalist Union president).\n*   The meeting released the book _Telangana Pratyeka Rashtram – Prajala Udyamam_, written by Adiraju Venkateshwara Rao.\n*   The Union urged the Centre to appoint a Supreme Court judge to investigate the police firings during the movement.\n\n### 2–5 June — Escalating Violence\n\n*   Up to 30 people died in police firing between 2–5 June.\n*   **2 June — Telangana Protest Day**, observed across Telangana in protest of the 31 May jail attack.\n*   **Prem Kishore''s death**: during a TPS-called bandh, Andhra''s Durga Vilas Hotel at Abids Chourasta remained open; activists who went in to insist it close were attacked with swords by Andhra men inside, killing teenager Prem Kishore.\n    *   Separately, agitator Chunchu Chandramouli, marching in front of the Abids post office shouting \"Jai Telangana,\" dared the police to shoot him — and was shot dead. (The firing police, from northeastern states, reportedly did not know Telugu.)\n*   TPS called for a Telangana bandh on 3 June in response to the 2 June incident; the government nonetheless held high-school exams that day, and students were urged to boycott them; more than 20 people died in police firing on protesters that day.\n*   3 June was also observed, on Sridhar Reddy''s call, as **\"No Revenge Day\"** (Paga Sadhimpu Vyatireka Dinam).\n*   On 4 June, the movement intensified further, with police firing in many locations.\n*   **G. Venkataswamy''s resignation**: the MP resigned as president of the (pro-unity) United Association, stating he had come to believe the mass movement could not be suppressed through restrictive and \"atrocious\" measures, and appealed to \"wise brothers of Andhra\" to recognize Telangana people''s consciousness and their fight for their rights.\n\n### 4 June — The Prime Minister''s Sudden Visit\n\n*   On receiving an intelligence report on the escalating agitation, Indira Gandhi made a sudden visit to Hyderabad, accompanied by Chokka Rao, and held discussions that night with state ministers and Konda Laxman Bapuji.\n*   The TPS urged her to dismiss the government and impose President''s Rule immediately, explaining the violence committed under Brahmananda Reddy''s administration; leaders insisted the movement would not stop short of statehood.\n*   Chief Minister Brahmananda Reddy characterized the movement as merely a peace-and-security issue and claimed he could suppress it if given time; PCC President Kakani Venkataratnam blamed \"some selfish people\" for the unrest.\n*   Chenna Reddy told the Prime Minister that nothing but a separate Telangana state would satisfy the people, adding that had she come a day earlier, many lives would have been saved.\n*   Before departing for Delhi, Indira Gandhi told reporters she had come \"not to achieve any outcome, but to express my concern because of these incidents,\" and appealed to the people of Telangana to remain peaceful.\n\n### 6 June — Writers'' Conference and Continued Hunger Strikes\n\n*   The **Telangana Writers''/Authors'' Conference** was held in Hyderabad; a Writers'' Committee was formed under Kaloji Narayana Rao to fight for a separate Telangana.\n*   CPI leader Raja Bahadur Gour — who opposed Telangana''s separation — began a hunger strike on 6 June to press the Centre for an immediate political solution; he ended it on 14 June, 1969.\n*   Telangana College Teachers Convener Ananda Rao Thota criticized the Eight-Point Formula as unsatisfying, calling it the Chief Minister''s plan rather than a genuine central initiative.\n\n### 7 June — Labour Strike\n\n*   Around two-and-a-half lakh workers in the twin cities struck on 7 June in protest of the previous week''s police firings.\n*   Electricity Board and other Telangana industrial workers convened in Hyderabad and formed an action committee, presided over by Govind Singh.\n*   Home Minister Y.B. Chavan, visiting Hyderabad the same day, was pressed by Chenna Reddy to impose President''s Rule immediately; a 15-member delegation under Mrs. Kumud Nayak told him that Andhra rulers treated Telangana people as second-class citizens.\n*   Before returning to Delhi after his two-day visit, Chavan acknowledged the strength of feeling on the Telangana issue, said the matter needed examination by the PM and Union Cabinet, called for calm to allow a resolution, and urged the withdrawal of all forms of agitation.\n\n### 9 June\n\n*   K.R. Veeraswamy, chairman of the Scheduled Castes and Backward Classes National Emancipation Front, warned that armed revolution could occur in Telangana if the Centre failed to intervene.\n\n### 10–16 June — Employees'' Strike and the Telangana Bandh\n\n*   On 10 June, Telangana employees launched an indefinite strike, defying Home Minister Chavan''s appeal against it.\n*   The strike at Palwancha''s Electricity department caused a statewide power shortage; municipal workers'' strikes left city streets littered with uncollected garbage; teachers announced they would join once schools reopened.\n*   The employees'' strike lasted **almost 37 days**.\n*   With no response from the Centre, on 12 June the TPS and Student Action Committee called for a Telangana bandh on 16 June.\n*   At a 12 June Malakpet conference chaired by Marri Chenna Reddy, he declared the movement''s goal in stark terms: \"It should be achieved or should die.\"\n*   On 14 June, the government suspended 29 Telangana teachers.\n*   The **Hyderabad District Praja Samiti** was formed on 15 June, with M. Manik Rao elected president.\n*   **16 June — Telangana Bandh**: fully successful, called by the TPS. Chenna Reddy said the bandh proved Telangana people could agitate peacefully \"without the interference of police and goons.\"\n    *   Bojja Narasimhulu, a prominent freedom fighter who had worked for Backward Classes and Munnur Kapu communities, and had played an active role in the movement, was dragged from his home in Sultan Bazar by police and thrown into a police jeep — widely condemned.\n    *   Legislative Council Jana Sangh leader V. Rama Rao and founding Jana Sangh member Bangaru Laxman opposed the bandh.\n    *   Chenna Reddy wrote to neighbouring chief ministers asking them to withdraw reserve police forces sent to Telangana, citing police brutality.\n*   **16 June — Anti-Detention Day**, observed in protest of the government''s detention policies.\n\n### 17 June — Telangana Women''s Day\n\n*   Large-scale women''s satyagraha was held; Hyderabad Mayor Kumud Nayak was arrested while participating at Abids — the **first time in the country''s history a city mayor was arrested for agitating against a government**.\n*   Two thousand corporation staff immediately struck in protest of the Mayor''s arrest.\n*   Notable women arrested during the day''s satyagraha: J. Eshwaribai (MLA, Republican Party secretary) near the Secretariat; Sada Laxmi and A.A. Shanthabai near Mojamjahi Market; Laxmi Reddy (TPS Women''s Wing) at Charminar crossroads; Kumud Nayak (Mayor) near the Nehru statue at Abids; and family members of Chenna Reddy and KV Ranga Reddy near Sultan Bazar.\n\n### 20–25 June — Mass Arrests and Delhi Talks\n\n*   Thousands were arrested for striking across Telangana between 20–25 June and lathi-charged; prisons overflowed, and City College was turned into a makeshift prison.\n*   On 20 June, protesters in Abids cut a map of Andhra Pradesh in two, handing the Telangana portion to Laxman Bapuji and burning the Andhra portion.\n*   Telangana leaders travelled to Delhi between 21–24 June but failed to reach an agreement with central leaders: Congress President Nijalingappa said a separate state and President''s Rule were not possible, though more power for the Regional Committee was; Deputy PM Morarji Desai stated flatly there was no option of a separate Telangana.\n\n**Revival of the Telangana Gazetted Employees Union:**\n\n*   On 22 June, the \"Hyderabad Services Association\" was revived by Telangana gazetted officers at a meeting chaired by L.N. Gupta at Kamala Nehru Girls Polytechnic College; **Ramakrishna Raju** was elected president of the newly formed Telangana Gazetted Employees Association (TGO).\n\n**24 June — Attack on Satyagrahis in Musheerabad Jail:**\n\n*   Telangana prisoners on strike in Musheerabad jail were attacked by Andhra prisoners and jail staff.\n*   A crowd gathered at the news, stoning armed forces sent to control the situation; police fired several rounds into the air.\n*   TPS leaders Manik Rao, Rajamallu, S. Venkatram Reddy, I. Eshwari Bai, and M.P. Sangam Lakshmi Bai went to the scene to pacify the agitators.\n*   MP G. Venkataswamy''s jeep, visiting satyagrahis at the jail, was attacked; his head was injured when the jeep rolled over, and Indian Express journalist Avinash Mishra was also hurt.\n\n**25 June — Arrest of top leaders:**\n\n*   16 senior leaders — including Chenna Reddy and Konda Laxman, who had protested the Musheerabad attacks — were arrested and moved to Rajahmundri jail.\n*   Other arrests included TPCC General Secretary T. Hayagriva Chari, Hyderabad Town Congress Secretary N. Narayana, and Secunderabad Branch secretary Anjaiah; also arrested were legislator Badri Vishal Pitti, MM Hashim, T. Anjaiah, Manikya Rao, Achyutha Reddy, and E.V. Padmanabhan.\n*   MPs Dr. Melkote and Sangam Lakshmibayamma prepared to resign their Lok Sabha seats in protest of these arrests.\n\n### 26–27 June — The Chief Minister''s \"Resignation Drama\"\n\n*   On 26 June, lawyers in the twin cities staged a 400-car rally to Raj Bhavan, submitting a memorandum demanding dismissal of the state government and imposition of President''s Rule; students and teachers separately protested demanding ministers'' resignations, and Brooke Bond industry workers joined a strike for the first time, protesting the arrest of Telangana leaders.\n*   Telangana ministers, under pressure from agitators, decided to resign en masse, planning to submit resignations to the Governor on the evening of 27 June — but Industrial Minister Guru Murthy pre-emptively submitted his resignation directly to the Chief Minister instead.\n*   Realizing that a mass ministerial resignation would strengthen the movement and threaten his own position, Chief Minister Brahmananda Reddy staged what amounted to a political drama: he sent his own resignation letter on 27 June not to the Governor, but to Congress President Nijalingappa in Delhi — a technicality that rendered it a non-genuine resignation, since a resignation must go to the Governor to be effective.\n*   With the Chief Minister''s own resignation thus rendered non-binding, Telangana ministers were left in a difficult position; nonetheless, **eight Telangana ministers** did resign to support the movement: J.V. Narsinga Rao, Sheelam Siddha Reddy, PV Narasimha Rao, Arige Ramaswami, VB Raju, BV Gurumurthy, KV Narayana Reddy, and Ibrahim Ali Ansari.\n*   The Governor accepted these eight ministers'' resignations on 14 July, but Brahmananda Reddy''s resignation was rejected by the party high command, as he had expected.\n*   As part of a plan between Brahmananda Reddy and Nijalingappa, the matter of the Chief Minister''s resignation was left to the Andhra Pradesh Legislative Assembly, which met on 6 July under Nijalingappa and Kamaraj.\n*   Minister PV Narasimha Rao moved a resolution expressing complete confidence in the Chief Minister, which the legislature unanimously approved; state Congress legislators separately declared confidence in Brahmananda Reddy.\n*   Notably, this \"unanimous\" confidence vote occurred while 9 Telangana MLAs and several Legislative Council members were in jail and remaining Telangana members were absent — undermining the vote''s representativeness of Telangana opinion.\n\n### 1 July\n\n*   Nearly a thousand auto- and taxi-drivers in Telangana held a procession and submitted a petition to the Governor, followed by a public meeting at Khairatabad railway station grounds expressing full support for the movement.\n\n### 2–7 July — Continued Arrests Under the PD Act\n\n*   On 2 July, student leader PJ Suri was arrested in Secunderabad; Mallikarjuna was arrested on 3 July under the PD Act and sent to Rajahmundry jail. All those detained under the PD Act filed writs in the High Court seeking release.\n*   The same day, the Congress Working Committee, meeting in Delhi, decided against a separate Telangana.\n*   **4 July**: TPS treasurer Ramakrishna Dooth was arrested under the PD Act along with Dr. Gopal Kishan (Assistant Surgeon, Osmania Hospital, and president of the Andhra Pradesh Medical Students Association) and four others; Dr. Gopal Kishan is noted as the **first Gazetted Officer arrested under the PD Act anywhere in India**.\n*   **People gheraoed Minister VB Raju** on 4 July, demanding he accept a separate Telangana and holding a strike at his office for hours, forcing him to sit on the floor with them; the satyagraha was led by pharmaceutical-company owner Gupta.\n*   5 July was observed as **Telangana Liberation Day**.\n*   A separate-Andhra conference was held in Guntur on 5 July, chaired by Nadimpally Narsimha Rao; Independent Party leader N. Vijayaraj Kumar argued the Gentlemen''s Agreement had gone unimplemented for 13 years and that bifurcation into two states would benefit both regions'' development. Telangana leaders Sadalakshmi and Sridhar Reddy sent messages of support to this conference.\n*   Congress President Nijalingappa''s 6 July visit to Hyderabad was met with an absolute bandh; police fired at Khairatabad.\n*   Addressing a Calcutta meeting, Jayaprakash Narayan said Telangana had been neglected, and that forming smaller states would not endanger national unity.\n*   On 7 July, activists bombed the house of Deputy Chief Minister J.V. Narsinga Rao (an advocate for Vishalandhra) in protest; the same day, high-school girls surrounded Nijalingappa at his Hyderabad guesthouse, chanting slogans for separate Telangana.\n\n### 8–12 July\n\n*   On 8 July, Revenue Minister VB Raju told reporters the people of Telangana were discontented and disappointed, and urged Andhra people to hand over the state''s leadership to Telangana.\n*   **10 July — Telangana Protections Day** and **Black Flag Day** were observed. At the AICC meeting in Bangalore, MP J. Rameshwar Rao proposed a Congress-regulation amendment aimed at forming a separate Telangana Pradesh Congress Committee.\n*   On 11 July, the AICC meeting in Bangalore did not discuss the separate-state issue at all, disappointing Telangana supporters; police lathi-charged striking employee families in Abids the same day.\n*   **12 July — Telangana Liberation Day** (again observed) and **Telangana Flag Day**: the TPS organized Flag Day across Telangana; police arrested MP GS Melkote for unfurling a separate-Telangana flag at Isamia Bazaar; a motor-vehicle procession displayed a flag depicting Telangana''s territory, and Telangana flags were hoisted on schools, government offices, and even the Charminar.\n\n### 16 July — TNGO Withdraws Its Strike\n\n*   The TNGO called off its indefinite strike (running since 10 June, for 37 days); employees returned to duty, and all temporary employees hired during the strike were dismissed.\n*   Amos resigned from the TNGO due to internal differences of opinion.\n\n**Reasons for the strike''s withdrawal:**\n\n*   The government lured a few employee leaders, causing splits within the TNGO.\n*   While prominent employee leaders were in jail, Chief Minister Brahmananda Reddy negotiated a deal with INTUC president G. Sanjeevareddy, sowing disturbances within the NGO and prompting the strike''s withdrawal.\n*   AP State Union Congress General Secretary N. Sathyanarayana Reddy criticized the movement, alleging the Birla business group was backing it to further its own interests over Alwin Metals in Hyderabad — though this claim of Birla financial backing was never confirmed.\n*   Osmania University employees also called off their strike on 23 July.\n\n### 18 July — New Cabinet\n\n*   Kasu Brahmananda Reddy rebuilt his cabinet on 18 July after the Governor formally accepted the eight Telangana ministers'' resignations.\n*   J.V. Narsinga Rao was appointed Deputy Chief Minister, and Jalagam Vengal Rao (from Andhra) became Home Minister.\n*   Speaking in Palvancha on 5 August, Vengal Rao vowed to suppress the movement as Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel had suppressed the Razakars.\n*   Roda Mistri, Vasudeva Rao, V. Purushottam Reddy, and G. Sangeeva Reddy were also newly inducted as ministers from Telangana.\n\n### 19 July — Anti-Traitor''s Day\n\n*   The TPS and Student Action Committee organized a Telangana Bandh to protest the Telangana ministers who joined the reconstituted cabinet, observed as \"Anti-Traitor''s Day.\"\n\n### 24–25 July — Parliamentary Committee Push and Private Bill\n\n*   On 24 July, Telangana MP M. Narayana Reddy wrote to the Lok Sabha Speaker proposing a 21-member Parliamentary Committee to study the Telangana issue and report by 26 August, 1969; the letter was signed by more than 40 MPs across states, including Vajpayee.\n*   The **Telangana Regional Committee** formed a 22-member subcommittee to suggest a solution, with TPS president Chokka Rao appointing ministers and legislators from various parties as members.\n*   On 25 July, Independent MP Narayana Reddy introduced a private bill in the Lok Sabha proposing a referendum on Telangana''s future; this bill was debated on 21 August, 1969.\n\n### 1–2 August\n\n*   On 1 August, a TPS conference at Khammam, chaired by Kandibanda Rama Rao, saw acting president T.N. Sada Lakshmi vow to fight for a separate state \"till death.\"\n*   On 2 August, Sridhar Reddy (President, Rebel TPS) and 10 student leaders were arrested under the PD Act — after he had announced on 31 July that an indefinite Telangana bandh would begin 11 August, along with a boycott of Independence Day.\n\n### 5–10 August — Firings and Satyagraha\n\n*   **5 August**: at Home Minister Jalagam Vengal Rao''s congratulatory meeting in Kothagudem, police fired on protesters, killing students Dastagiri (10) and Ram Chander (25).\n*   **6 August**: under P. Vasudev''s (President, Store Clerks'' Association) leadership, clerks staged satyagraha on President''s Road, Secunderabad, and were arrested; separately, young man Chepalabavi Rabindranath was killed in police firing at Rajeshwara Talkies market area, Secunderabad, and Mahadev Singh was arrested there the same day.\n*   Union Home Minister Y.B. Chavan, speaking in the Rajya Sabha, said the Union government was not favourably disposed toward forming a separate state.\n*   **7 August**: State Home Minister Vengal Rao alleged Naxalites were infiltrating the movement and causing violence, claiming 90% of people wanted a united state.\n*   **8 August**: former minister V.B. Raju proposed a Triple Point Scheme — a change in government leadership, release of all political prisoners, and a round-table conference of Telangana leaders.\n*   **9 August**: Sarvodaya activists, under leader Ghora, met in Hyderabad to plan awareness-building efforts about the movement in non-Telangana areas and among Andhra people.\n*   **10 August**: Kaloji Narayana Rao and Warangal medical student leader Kolluri Chiranjeevi were arrested under Section 151 for satyagraha in Warangal.\n\n### 14–16 August\n\n*   Three legislators — J. Eshwaribai, Palwai Govardhan Reddy, and DV Sudhakar — were arrested under the PD Act on 14 August.\n*   At the AP Legislative Council on 14 August, Governor Khandubhai Khasamji Desai''s inaugural address was interrupted by \"Jai Telangana\" slogans, forcing him to return without completing his speech; Student Working Committee president Gottam Gopala Reddy was arrested during this protest.\n*   **15 August**: a large meeting under TPS auspices at Vivekavardhini College — addressed by Sadalakshmi, Sumitradevi, and Konda Venkata Rangareddy — hoisted the National Flag alongside the Telangana Flag on Independence Day. Police lathi-charged students afterward; Sada Lakshmi was injured by a tear-gas shell, and legislator Sumitra Devi was wounded, later displaying her blood-stained clothes publicly in protest. K. Rajamallu raised the issue of the lathi-charge on Sumithra Devi in the Assembly.\n*   **16 August**: the AP Legislative Assembly observed two minutes'' silence for those killed since January 1969; Deputy CM JV Narsingarao moved the resolution, with BV Subba Reddy as Speaker.\n\n**President''s election and its effect on Telangana:**\n\n*   The Presidential election (held 16 August, 1969) became another distraction that allowed the high command to overlook Brahmananda Reddy''s handling of the crisis.\n*   Following President Zakir Hussain''s death, Congress President Nijalingappa and PM Indira Gandhi disagreed over the party''s presidential candidate — Nijalingappa backed Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy, while Indira Gandhi backed VV Giri.\n*   Indira Gandhi tried to keep Brahmananda Reddy on her side ahead of the election, given his following in Andhra Pradesh, but he ultimately declared support for Nijalingappa''s candidate, Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy.\n*   Legislators and MPs seeking a separate Telangana voted instead for VV Giri (Indira Gandhi''s candidate), who went on to win the election.\n\n### 18 August — Lok Sabha and Assembly Debates\n\n*   Jana Sangh member KL Gupta proposed a Lok Sabha resolution urging central action on Telangana.\n*   MPs G. Venkataswamy and Dr. Melkote urged the Centre to intervene, dissolve the state government, and impose President''s Rule; Dr. Melkote stated that 250 activists had been killed over the preceding eight months and nearly 50,000 arrested, displaying photographs of martyrs and vowing continued struggle.\n*   People''s Socialist Party leader S.N. Dvivedi compared the Telangana unrest to the Jallianwala Bagh massacre.\n*   Independent MP N.G. Ranga said the agitation had broad popular support and urged the Centre to quickly decide whether Telangana should be a sub-state or a fully separate state.\n*   S.V. Krishnappa proposed setting up a States Reorganization Committee to examine backward-area problems nationally.\n*   DMK''s N.K. Dappan argued the movement showed that shared language alone did not guarantee unity.\n*   Communist Party member Yogendra Sharma warned his party would also push for separate states for other backward regions if the Centre failed to address regional disparities.\n*   In the AP Legislative Assembly, Congress member P. Narsa Reddy proposed a resolution thanking the Governor''s speech while urging release of detained leaders to enable a round-table meeting, and calling on activists to halt their agitation; independent Andhra legislator Chappidi Vengaiah said the preceding seven months had shown there was no option but a separate Telangana state.\n\n### 21–30 August\n\n*   **21 August**: the Chief Minister announced a Rs 60-crore fertilizer plant at Ramagundam, seen as an attempt to subdue the movement.\n*   The TNGO Association and State Teachers'' Association — dissolved by the government on 24 May — were revived on 21 August.\n*   **22 August**: Home Minister Y.B. Chavan rejected the proposal for a Parliamentary Committee on Telangana, prompting eight Lok Sabha MPs (seven Telangana Congress MPs — G.S. Melkote, Baker Ali Mirja, J. Rameshwar Rao, G. Venkataswamy, P. Gangareddy, Ramapati Rao, R. Surender Reddy — plus Mysore''s M.V. Krishnappa) to walk out of the House.\n*   **23 August**: wives of movement leaders (Mrs. Chenna Reddy, Mrs. Achyuta Reddy, Mrs. Narottam Reddy, and others) met the Prime Minister demanding intervention; she said she would not intervene until the movement ceased and peace prevailed.\n*   **24 August**: 23 detenus held under the PD Act at Chanchalguda and Rajahmundry jails, including TPS treasurer Ramakrishna Dhooth and Student Action Committee leader Gopal, were released on the Advisory Committee''s recommendation.\n*   **25 August**: an AP High Court division bench ordered the release of Marri Chenna Reddy, Konda Laxman Bapuji, and all 34 detenus; they were released in Delhi on 27 August.\n*   **26 August**: a six-member delegation of the Telangana Workers Party met President VV Giri to press for intervention.\n*   **27 August**: Konda Laxman Bapuji and Marri Chenna Reddy met Congress President Nijalingappa and PM Indira Gandhi in Delhi. Bapuji separately met the Home Minister and Deputy PM Morarji Desai, while Chenna Reddy met President VV Giri, both seeking an early resolution. Chenna Reddy also addressed a meeting of Telangana MPs in Delhi, blaming 13 years of injustice for the current movement, and urged them to work toward a Parliamentary Committee.\n*   **28 August**: Lakshminarayana was elected Mayor of Hyderabad, backed by the TPS — a further morale boost for the movement.\n*   **29 August**: Chenna Reddy and Konda Laxman returned from Delhi to a warm welcome in Hyderabad.\n*   **30 August**: at a Reddy Hostel meeting chaired by Sadalakshmi, Chenna Reddy and Mallikarjun vowed not to stop the movement until statehood was achieved — but from September onward, the movement entered a **declining phase**.\n\n### 1–4 September\n\n*   Detainees J. Eshwari Bai, G.V. Sudhakar, and Palwai Govardhan Reddy (held since 14 August) were released on 1 September; Sridhar Reddy, president of the Rebel Praja Samiti, was released on 3 September.\n*   On 4 September, Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy suggested a second State Reorganisation Committee, a proposal Congress President Nijalingappa rejected.\n\n### 5 September — Teachers'' Misery Day\n\n*   Teachers observed \"Teachers Miserable Day,\" wearing black badges and holding meetings demanding implementation of the Kothari Committee recommendations.\n*   Education Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao, speaking in the Assembly the same day, accused politicians of using students for the ongoing movement; 12 Telangana Congress members walked out in protest.\n\n### 6 September — Meetings on Resolution\n\n*   Government Chief Whip Parigi Ram Reddy — a longtime Brahmananda Reddy loyalist — held a meeting of 52 legislators and ministers on steps to resolve the Telangana issue; Telangana activists were absent.\n*   Minister VB Raju separately convened an all-party meeting at the Exhibition Grounds, attended by both Telangana-statehood supporters and others.\n\n### 16–30 September — Internal Splits Deepen\n\n*   On 16 September, at Vivekavardhini College, Mallikarjun warned that Telangana people would form a \"rebel government\" if statehood demands were not met.\n*   TPS president Marri Chenna Reddy launched the _Jai Telangana Weekly_ magazine in Hyderabad on 17 September, published under Sangamreddy Satyanarayana''s auspices.\n*   On 18 September, Warangal Medical Students'' leader K. Chiranjeevi urged students to temporarily suspend the movement, resume classes, and not lose the academic year.\n*   On 22 September, Chenna Reddy and Konda Laxman Bapuji met President VV Giri at Bollaram to discuss Telangana.\n*   On 23 September, the TPS and Student Action Committee — signed by Chenna Reddy and Mallikarjun — announced students should return to classes immediately; Reddy informed President Giri of this decision, and the President expressed his approval.\n*   This decision provoked further internal dissent: Rebel TPS vice-president Veerareddy accused Chenna Reddy of betraying the movement from within; Captain Ansari (TPS leader) criticized the Congress party for turning the movement into an internal party struggle; and Rebel TPS president Sridhar Reddy insisted the decision belonged to students alone, not to Chenna Reddy or Mallikarjun.\n*   Hyderabad old-city students began a relay hunger strike at Charkaman, criticizing the class-attendance decision and demanding Telangana leaders resign; leaders of this protest included Swayam Prakash, Tamizoddin Ahmad, K. Govind, and Mohammad Aliuddin Khureshi.\n*   On 25 September, Assembly Speaker B.V. Subbareddy moved a no-confidence motion, backed by 11 legislators demanding a separate Telangana.\n*   On 27 September, Education Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao announced in the Assembly that 120 non-mulki teachers had been removed from Telangana and would be reappointed in Andhra.\n*   On 28 September, a separate-Telangana meeting at Kothagudem, chaired by Kamal Mustafa Khan, was addressed by Marri Chenna Reddy, Ananthula Madan Mohan, and T. Hayagriva Chari.\n\n### 10–18 October — Renewed Satyagraha and Fasts\n\n*   **10 October**: with students criticized for resuming classes, Chenna Reddy announced he would undertake satyagraha alongside adult (18+) participants from this date; on the first day, three lakh people joined the satyagraha at his invitation.\n*   The same day, Mallikarjun launched an indefinite fast at the Gandhi Statue in Secunderabad, protesting the Centre''s continued inaction on Telangana statehood.\n*   On 18 October, fifteen-year-old Kumari Ramadevi (Convener, Telangana Students'' — girls'' — Action Committee) began an indefinite fast at the Raja Bahadur Venkatarama Reddy statue near Narayanaguda YMCA circle.\n*   Mallikarjun''s fast was ended by police arrest and hospitalization on 3 November, after 24 days.\n*   On 11 October, Swatantra Party member Gauthu Lachanna announced support for dividing the state under the slogan \"Andhra for the Andhras,\" arguing separation would let coastal Andhra develop and increase revenue.\n*   On 12 October, a Youth Congress meeting at Chikkadpally, chaired by G. Venkatswamy, saw Konda Laxman Bapuji inaugurate the \"Telangana Pradesh Youth Congress Committee\" branch.\n*   On 13 October, activists bombed the houses of State Social Welfare Minister Roda Mistri and Home Minister Jalagam Vengala Rao.\n*   On 14 October, MP Mir Akbar Alikhan proposed: (1) granting the Telangana Regional Council extensive powers via presidential order, and (2) a round-table conference chaired by the PM or Home Minister to discuss the presidential order before a constitutional amendment.\n\n### 23–31 October\n\n*   **23 October**: K. Narasimhareddy (President, Telangana Liberation Committee, Warangal) launched a tax-refusal movement, run in the spirit of the national freedom movement, in Warangal district.\n*   **24 October**: Jalil Pasha and Vishnuvardhan Reddy began a fast at the Nehru statue, Abids.\n*   **31 October**: bombs were thrown at the houses of Telangana ministers Rhoda Mistry and Jalagam Vengal Rao.\n\n### 1–27 November — Winding Down\n\n*   **1 November**: Konda Laxman Bapuji began an 11-day fast at his residence, Jaladrushyam, timed to the anniversary of Andhra Pradesh''s formation, to highlight the \"breach of contract\" to Telangana; his daughter Sumitra persuaded him to end it on 12 November with fruit juice.\n*   The TPS declared 1 November \"Anti-Andhra Pradesh Day,\" organizing relay hunger strikes; the Telangana Fourth Class Employees'' Association called it \"Candidates Day.\"\n*   Bakar Ali Mirza resigned from Congress in November 1969 in protest of both state and central governments'' handling of Telangana.\n*   **27 November**: returning from Delhi, Chenna Reddy announced the movement would be postponed and revived from 1 January of the following year — effectively ending the year-long active phase of the movement.\n\n### 5–29 December — Factional Struggle Within TPS\n\n*   **5 December**: former president Sadalakshmi, with three others, issued a statement removing Chenna Reddy from the TPS presidency, criticizing his introduction of a \"person worship\" method and dominating attitude.\n*   **26 December**: at a TPS meeting, Chenna Reddy was unanimously re-elected president.\n*   **29 December**: Mallikarjun was unanimously elected head of the Student United Action Committee.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p9", "partName": "Magazines and Journals of the 1969 Movement", "bodyMd": "*   _Janadharma_, a Warangal weekly edited and owned by Sri Madabhushi Srinivasacharya, was distinctive in publicly and consistently defending the movement, reporting events as they actually occurred.\n*   Alongside _Janadharma_, the _Andhra Bhoomi_ and _Deccan Chronicle_ also published some news favourable to the movement.\n*   In contrast, _Andhra Magazine_, _Vishalandhra Magazine_, and _Andhra Jyoti Magazine_ opposed the separate-Telangana movement and its leaders, publishing false and misleading reports about it.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p10", "partName": "The Role of Various Parties in the Movement", "bodyMd": "### Congress Party\n\n*   Leaders such as Konda Laxman Bapuji initially demanded autonomous status, not full statehood, for Telangana.\n*   In April 1969, eight Telangana Congress MLAs declared a separate state the only solution — but after the Eight-Point Formula''s announcement on 11 April, **48 Telangana Congress MLAs** issued a joint statement instead demanding immediate implementation of the formula, saying a separate state was unnecessary. This division within Congress persisted throughout the year.\n*   Protesters responded to this stance by stoning Labour Minister K.V. Narayan Reddy''s car at Khairatabad, and unidentified persons later bombed the houses of Minister G. Sanjeeva Reddy and Transport Minister Lakshminarasayya.\n*   On 19 September, 1969, 70 Telangana MLAs appealed to the Centre to change the state''s leadership.\n*   On 15 December, 1969, 82 of the 135 AP Congress Committee members from Telangana (including Deputy CM J.V. Narsinga Rao) notified the PM that Telangana did not need a separate Pradesh Congress Committee — leading the PM to conclude the majority of Congress leaders opposed dividing the state.\n*   On 16 December, 1969, however, 90 pro-Chief-Minister Telangana Congress leaders took a unanimous decision favouring the Telangana Pradesh Congress Committee.\n*   **Cause → Effect**: this back-and-forth illustrates that Telangana Congress leaders lacked unity throughout the movement, weakening the political case for statehood even as popular agitation continued.\n\n### CPM\n\n*   Opposed a separate Telangana, giving the slogan _\"Vishalandhra lo Praja Rajyam\"_ (\"People''s rule within a united Andhra\"), while still demanding effective implementation of Telangana''s existing safeguards.\n\n### CPI\n\n*   Also favoured a united state, while demanding implementation of Telangana protections.\n*   The party and its subsidiaries (AITUC, AISF, AIYF) demanded the government''s dismissal and the imposition of President''s Rule.\n*   Rajabahadur Gaur (Hyderabad) and Dasari Nagabhushanam (Vijayawada) undertook hunger strikes to press for the government''s ouster and President''s Rule.\n*   CPI leader Magdum Moinuddin, in internal party discussions, urged the party to press for the legality of the Telangana Regional Committee and a Telangana Public Service Commission.\n\n### Jana Sangh\n\n*   Opposed formation of a separate Telangana state as a matter of party policy.\n*   Hyderabad City Secretary C.M. Jaganmohan Reddy nonetheless defended the movement publicly and was expelled from the party as a result.\n*   In the Lok Sabha, Jana Sangh member Chand Goyal argued that both central and state governments were trying to make Telangana a \"permanent backward state.\"\n\n### Swatantra Party\n\n*   Gave its complete support to a separate Telangana, and — as noted earlier — was the first political party to formally demand it.\n\n### United Socialist Party (USP)\n\n*   At Indira Gandhi''s April 1969 meeting with opposition leaders, the USP proposed making Telangana a sub-state within Andhra Pradesh, rather than a fully separate state.\n\n### Praja Socialist Party (PSP)\n\n*   On 2 April, 1969, issued a resolution demanding the Brahmananda Reddy government''s dissolution and imposition of President''s Rule.\n\n### Praja Party\n\n*   On 25 September, 1969, the party''s working committee in Vijayawada stated that delays in resolving the Telangana issue had deepened Telangana people''s distrust of the Centre, and called for implementing the recommendations of the various committees formed so far.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p11", "partName": "Reasons for the Failure of the 1969 Movement", "bodyMd": "*   The movement began among students, teachers, employees, and intellectuals, but as it escalated, opportunist politicians took over its leadership.\n*   **Cause → Effect**: this shift turned the movement into a Congress Party affair, and ultimately into an internal struggle within the Congress Party itself, diluting its original mass character.\n*   Leadership fractured into three rival groups:\n    1.  Telangana Praja Samithi, under Marri Chenna Reddy\n    2.  Telangana Congress Committee, under Konda Laxman Bapuji\n    3.  Telangana United Front, under VB Raju and Narottama Reddy\n*   Only 24 of 74 Telangana Congress MLAs fully backed the movement; even these dissenters opposed Kasu Brahmananda Reddy personally, not the central government or Prime Minister Indira Gandhi — meaning the movement never developed a coherent anti-Centre political front.\n*   Indira Gandhi eventually had Brahmananda Reddy resign in September 1971, replacing him with Telangana''s own PV Narasimha Rao as Chief Minister; within a short time, the TPS merged into the Congress party, diluting the Telangana statehood aspiration.\n*   The movement remained largely confined to **urban areas, district centres, and cities**, never spreading meaningfully to rural Telangana.\n*   It was fought principally as an issue of jobs and surplus funds, without connecting to agriculture, on which most of Telangana''s population depended — limiting its base of support.\n*   In June 1969, the movement''s top leaders were jailed, and the absence of an effective second line of leadership left the movement weakened thereafter.\n*   National parties such as the Jana Sangh and the Communist parties supported a united state and opposed the separatist movement.\n*   Chief Minister Brahmananda Reddy struck a deal with Congress-affiliated trade union leader Sanjeevayya, engineering the calling-off of the employees'' indefinite strike.\n*   Students became alienated from the movement once its leaders pressed them to resume classes and sit for exams rather than continue the boycott, prioritizing the academic year over the agitation.\n\n**Lack of literature in the Movement:**\n\n*   Another major reason the 1969 movement fell short of its goal was a lack of durable literature.\n*   The movement was driven by aggressive slogans and provocative speeches, but lacked books systematically documenting the injustices, insults, and regional inequalities faced by Telangana people — weakening its capacity to build lasting, informed public support.\n\n**Non-participation of the majority Muslim community:**\n\n*   The majority Muslim community largely stayed out of the movement.\n*   Kingshuk Nag (_Times of India_; Hyderabad resident), in his book _Telangana Movement – Historical Background_, attributes this to the aftermath of Partition-era violence: Razakars of the MIM party had carried out indiscriminate attacks on Hindus before Hyderabad state''s merger into the Indian Union on 17 September, 1948.\n*   After the merger, Hindus who resented the Razakars directed their anger at Muslims generally, which in turn made Muslims distrustful of Hindus and disinclined to join the 1969 movement.\n*   These flaws were corrected only in the movement''s later (second) phase.\n\n**Results of the Movement:**\n\n*   Despite its shortcomings, the movement produced effective future leadership for Telangana — for example, GM Anjaiah, active in 1969, later built huts for the homeless poor in Addagutta, Secunderabad.\n*   Student leaders of 1969 — Jayashankar, Keshava Rao Jadhav, Bhupathi Krishnamurthy, and Kaloji Narayana Rao — went on to lead the movement''s second phase.\n*   A new culture of struggle spread among Telangana''s people, which became instrumental in sustaining the later, second phase of the movement.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p12", "partName": "Important Dates in the 1969 Movement", "bodyMd": "| Date | Observance (Telugu name) | Associated Group |\n|---|---|---|\n| 17 March 1969 | Telangana Fighting Day (Telangana Porata Dinam) | Teachers, Employees |\n| 17 March 1969 | Democracy Preservation Day (Prajaswamya Parirakshana Dinam) | Students |\n| 9 April 1969 | Mass Satyagraha (Samuhika Satyagraham) | — |\n| 15 April 1969 | Telangana Fighting Day (Telangana Porata Dinam) | — |\n| 22 April 1969 | Telangana Deception Day (Telangana Vanchana Dinam / Vidroha Dinam) | — |\n| 1 May 1969 | Telangana Demands Day (Telangana Korikala Dinam) | — |\n| 17 May 1969 | Telangana Martyrs Day (Telangana Amaraveerula Dinam) | — |\n| 2 June 1969 | Telangana Protest Day (Telangana Nirasana Dinam) | — |\n| 3 June 1969 | No Revenge Day (Paga Sadhimpu Vyatireka Dinam) | — |\n| 16 June 1969 | Anti-Detention Day (Nirbandha Vyatireka Dinam) | — |\n| 17 June 1969 | Telangana Women''s Day (Telangana Mahilala Dinam) | — |\n| 5 July 1969 | Telangana Liberation Day (Telangana Vimochana Dinam) | — |\n| 10 July 1969 | Black Flag Day (Nalla Jendala Dinam) / Telangana Protection Day (Telangana Parirakshana Dinam) | — |\n| 12 July 1969 | Telangana Flag Day / Telangana Liberation Day | — |\n| 19 July 1969 | Anti-Traitor Day (Vidrohula Vyatireka Dinam) | — |\n| 1 November 1969 | Candidates Day (Abhyardula Dinam) | — |", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p13", "partName": "The \"Firsts\" of the 1969 Movement", "bodyMd": "*   First person to undertake an indefinite hunger strike (first phase): **Ravindranath**\n*   First lathi charge (first phase): **18 January, 1969**\n*   First firing (first phase): **Shamshabad railway station, 20 January, 1969**\n*   First martyr (first phase): **Shankar**\n*   First minister to resign: **Konda Laxman Bapuji**\n*   First bandh: **3 March, 1969**\n*   First conference: **Reddy Hostel Conference (8–9 March, 1969)**\n*   First to give the \"Quit Telangana\" slogan: **Sreedhar Reddy**, student leader\n*   First political party to demand a separate Telangana: **Swatantra Party**\n*   First Andhra MP to seek separate Telangana in Parliament: **N.G. Ranga**\n*   First Andhra person to fast for separate Telangana: **Korrapati Pattabhiramayya**\n*   First person to win an election during the movement: **S. Venkatramireddy (TPS)**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p14", "partName": "Andhra People Who Supported the Movement", "bodyMd": "*   A. Mala Konda Reddy (ex-MLA, ex-president, AP Advocates Council)\n*   N.G. Ranga (Swatantra Party leader)\n*   Gouthu Lachanna (Swatantra Party leader)\n*   Korrapati Pattabhiramayya (ex-MLA)\n*   Nadimpalli Narsimha Rao\n*   Vavilala Gopala Krishnaiah (senior Congress leader)", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 15, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p15", "partName": "Women in the 1969 Movement", "bodyMd": "**Notable participants:**\n\n1.  Rani Kumudini Nayak (Hyderabad city Mayor)\n2.  Sangam Laxmibai\n3.  Masuma Begum\n4.  T.N. Sadalaxmi\n5.  CH Rama Devi\n6.  Kumari Hamsa Rani\n7.  Kumari Narsamma\n8.  Geetanjali Pillai\n9.  Rhoda Mistry\n10.  Sumithra Devi\n\n**Countering gendered attacks:** during the movement, hostility toward Andhra people extended into scandalous, insulting graffiti about women written on city walls; under Kumudin Naik''s leadership, women organized to remove such writings from Himayat Nagar.\n\n*   In May 1969, a women''s conference was held at Hanumakonda presided over by Devaki, with Eswari Bhai as chief guest.\n*   Freedom fighter Kanakaratnamma (MLC) led the women''s movement in Warangal.\n\n### Sangam Laxmibai (1911–1979)\n\n*   Born in 1911 in a hamlet near Ghatkesar (Medchal district); educated at the University of Karve and Sharada Niketan (founded by Lakshmibayamma).\n*   Played a role in the freedom movement, joining the Salt Satyagraha alongside Durga Bhai Deshmukh and imprisoned at Raivellur Jail until 1933; joined the anti-Simon Commission movement in 1928.\n*   Elected to the Hyderabad State Legislature in 1952 from Banswada; served as Deputy Minister of Education in the Burgula government (1954–56).\n*   Elected to the Lok Sabha from Medak on an Indian National Congress ticket in 1957, 1962, and 1967 — recognized as the **first woman elected to the Lok Sabha from Andhra Pradesh**.\n*   During the 1969 movement, she prepared to resign her Medak MP seat.\n*   Headed Vinoba Bhave''s Bhoodan Movement in Telangana.\n*   While in jail during the movement, she authored _Na Jailu Gnapakalu Anubhavalu_ (\"My Prison Memories and Experiences\").\n*   Founded the **Indira Sevasadan Society**, aiding women and girls, and worked for women''s welfare until her death in 1979.\n\n### Masuma Begum\n\n*   Born in 1901 in Hyderabad; awarded the Lady Baden Powell medal for her Indian Scout services.\n*   Served as Minister of Social Welfare in the Damodaram Sanjeevaiah government while actively participating in the movement.\n*   Recognized as the **first Muslim woman to hold ministerial office in India**.\n*   Founded the _Anjuman-e-Kawathin_ organization, providing services to orphans; served as president of the All India Women''s Conference (1962–64).\n*   Supported the 1969 movement; awarded the **Padma Shri in 1974**.\n\n### T.N. Sadalaxmi\n\n*   Influenced colleagues with powerful speeches during the movement; her famous line — \"If Telangana state comes, I will make it up as Bommarillu\" — remains widely quoted.\n*   Belonged to the Mehar caste, a Dalit sub-caste; recognized as the person who chaired the Telangana Praja Samithi.\n*   Born in Bollaram, Hyderabad, in 1928; recognized as the **first Dalit woman legislator from Telangana**.\n*   Initially elected from Peddapalli constituency, later from Kamareddy.\n*   Served as Minister of Devadaya in Neelam Sanjivreddy''s cabinet, and was the **first woman Deputy Speaker** during Damodaram Sanjeevaiah''s tenure.\n*   After a period with Congress, joined the Telugu Desam Party in 1982.\n*   Founded the Bandhu Seva Mandali and the Leadcop organisation.\n\n### J. Eshwaribai\n\n*   Born in Secunderabad in 1918, into a common Dalit family; studied Ambedkar''s theories and worked to further his aspirations.\n*   Elected to the AP Legislative Assembly from Ellareddy constituency, Nizamabad district, on a Republican Party ticket.\n*   Served as Republican Party secretary; became **National President of the Republican Party of India in 1986**.\n*   In 1951, won as an Independent candidate from Chilakalaguda in the first-ever Hyderabad Municipal Corporation election.\n\n### Kanakaratnamma\n\n*   A member of the Legislature; led the Telangana movement in Warangal.\n\n### Kumudini Nayak\n\n*   Elected **Mayor of Hyderabad in 1962** — the **first woman Mayor of the Hyderabad Municipal Corporation**.\n*   Daughter of Pingali Venkata Rami Reddy, recognized as a Telangana torch-bearer (_vaitalikudu_).\n*   Served as a member of the Legislative Assembly from Wanaparthy constituency, 1962–1972.\n\n### Geetanjali Pillai and Hamsa Rani\n\n*   On 6 August, 1969, under Kumari Geetanjali Pillai''s leadership, twelve young women were arrested for satyagraha in Secunderabad.\n*   Young women also performed satyagraha under Kumari Hamsa Rani''s leadership at Chadar Ghat Bridge, Isamia Bazaar, and under Narasamma''s leadership in Abids.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 16, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p16", "partName": "Important Leaders of the Movement", "bodyMd": "### Marri Chenna Reddy (1919–1996)\n\n*   Born in Siripuram village, Vikarabad Taluk; initially worked as a doctor at Osmania Hospital before leaving the profession to join the national movement.\n*   Organized the Telugu weekly magazine _Hyderabad_, run from Vijayawada.\n*   A provisional member of Parliament from 1950–52; from 1952, a member of the Hyderabad Legislative Assembly, serving as Minister for Civil Supply, Agriculture, and Planning.\n*   Elected to the AP State Legislative Assembly from Vikarabad in 1957; became a Lok Sabha member in 1967 and served as Union Minister of Mines & Minerals.\n*   Elected **Chief Minister of Andhra Pradesh on 6 March, 1978**; later served as Governor of Uttar Pradesh, Punjab, Rajasthan, and Tamil Nadu.\n*   During his term as CM, **Ranga Reddy district was formed** (15 August, 1978), named after KV Ranga Reddy.\n*   Became Chief Minister for a second time in 1989, resigning amid religious conflicts in Hyderabad''s old city.\n\n### KV Ranga Reddy (1890–1970)\n\n*   Born in Pedda Mangalaram village, Chevella Taluk (now Rangareddy district).\n*   Served as secretary of Reddy Hostel.\n*   Established the _Vemanandhra Bhasha Nilayam_ at Nampally in 1923 with his own funds.\n*   Presided over Andhra Mahasabha meetings at Shadnagar (1936) and Hyderabad (1943).\n*   Founded AV College, Institute of Education, in 1944.\n*   Served as Revenue Minister in the Burgula government (1952–56) and as **Andhra Pradesh''s first Deputy Chief Minister** in Damodaram Sanjeevaiah''s cabinet.\n*   Authored _My Autobiography by KV Ranga Reddy_.\n*   A member of the Indira Sevasadan Society founded by Sangam Lakshmibai in 1952.\n\n### Makhdoom Mohiuddin (1908–1969)\n\n*   Born in Andol, Medak district; a prominent Urdu revolutionary poet, titled _Shayar-e-Inquilab_ (\"Poet of the Revolution\").\n\n**Literary works:**\n\n*   _Piladushala_ (_Pasupu Pachcha Utariya_) — his earliest work, written as a student in 1932.\n*   _Khoon ke Nakhoon_ — a play appreciated by Tagore.\n*   The song \"Jung hai Junge\" and the poem \"Bhagmathi,\" both popular nationwide.\n*   _Bisat-e-Raqs_ — a compilation of poems and ghazals.\n*   _Surk Savera_ (\"The Red Dawn\"), 1924.\n*   _Gul-e-Taar_ (\"The Dewdrenched Rose\"), 1961.\n\n**Famous ghazals**: _Ek chameli ki mandve taale_; _Aapki yaad aathi rahi raat bhar_; _Phir chide raat baat phoolonki_.\n\n**Other biographical details:**\n\n*   Awarded the **Sahitya Akademi Award in Urdu Literature in 1969**.\n*   Participated in the Quit India Movement against British rule, and was a key figure in the movement seeking the Nizam''s kingdom''s merger into the Indian Union.\n*   The seventh Nizam, believing his writings incited people against Hyderabad state, issued a _firman_ ordering his killing.\n*   A founder of the Communist Party of Andhra Pradesh, and founder of the Comrade Association.\n*   Met Yuri Gagarin, the first space traveller, during a visit to Russia, and wrote a poem about him.\n*   His son, Nusrat Mohiuddin, served as secretary for Insaf Tehriq.\n\n### JV Narsinga Rao\n\n*   Joginapalli Venkata Narsinga Rao, born in Laksettipeta, Adilabad district.\n*   Served as President of the Hyderabad PCC (1956) during the states'' reorganization; elected to the legislature from Laksettipeta.\n\n### Anantula Madan Mohan\n\n*   Instrumental in establishing the TPS during the 1969 movement.\n*   Won the Siddipet by-election on a TPS ticket in 1970, and was elected MLA from Siddipet in 1972, 1978, and 1983 (the seat was subsequently held by KCR, and, from 2004, by Harish Rao).\n*   Served as the opposition leader for Congress when the Telugu Desam Party came to power in 1983.\n*   JNTU was established during his tenure as Minister of Technical Education, Hyderabad.\n\n### Tanguturi Anjaiah\n\n*   Hometown: Banur village, Medak district.\n*   Worked at the Alvin Metal Company in Hyderabad, rising to become a labour union leader.\n*   Served as **Chief Minister of Andhra Pradesh, 1980–82**; his unusually large cabinet was nicknamed the \"Airbus cabinet.\"\n*   Served as Central Labour Minister in 1984.\n*   The first-ever World Telugu Conferences held abroad took place during his tenure as Chief Minister.\n\n### Vandemataram Ramachandra Rao\n\n*   Real name: Vavilala Ramachandra Rao.\n*   Given the title \"Vandemataram\" by Veer Savarkar at a Pandaripuram meeting.\n*   Authored the work _Hindu Sanghatan_.\n*   Imprisoned in Bhagalpur jail, Bihar, for participating in the Quit India Movement.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 17, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p17", "partName": "Telangana Martyrs'' Memorial — Foundation", "bodyMd": "*   Sridhar Reddy resolved to erect a monument commemorating the martyrs of the 1969 separate-Telangana movement.\n*   City Mayor S. Lakshminarayana laid the memorial''s foundation stone on **23 February, 1970**, at Gun Park (opposite the Assembly), despite several legal injunctions against doing so.\n*   The night before the foundation ceremony, Pratap Kishore and Williams Anthi gathered martyrs'' personal belongings — books, certificates, and similar items — and placed them at the memorial''s base.\n*   Leaders arrested for attending the foundation ceremony included Manik Rao, Chenna Reddy, E.V. Padmanabhan, T. Anjaiah, T. Govind Singh, and MM Hashim.\n*   The stupa itself was carved by **Ekka Yadagiri Rao**, and was completed in 1975.\n*   City Deputy Mayor M. Ramachandraiah inaugurated the memorial on **25 February, 1970**, at the Clock Tower area, Secunderabad.\n\n**Symbolism of the memorial''s design:**\n\n*   The stupa''s base is made of black stone, with nine holes on each of its four sides bearing inscriptions — the nine holes symbolizing the nine districts of the then-Telangana region.\n*   Above the base rises a stupa of red stone; red was chosen to symbolize sacrifice.\n*   An Ashoka Chakra sits atop the stupa; this Dharma Chakra signifies that the movement''s martyrs sacrificed their lives in defence of Dharma (truth).\n*   At the stupa''s centre is a _makara thoranam_, an architectural motif drawn from the Sanchi Stupa.\n*   The stupa''s vertical portion bears nine white stone flowers (nine-petalled jasmine blooms), symbolizing both sacrifice and peace.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 18, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter31-1969-telangana-movement-p18", "partName": "Additional Political and Structural Dimensions", "bodyMd": "### Fragmented Telangana Political Leadership\n\n*   Telangana lacked a single leader broadly acceptable across the whole state, a gap that persisted through the Brahmananda Reddy years.\n*   Under the Gentlemen''s Agreement, if the Chief Minister came from Andhra, the Deputy Chief Minister''s post was meant to go to Telangana — but the arrangement was applied unevenly: Sanjeevareddy, for instance, held the Deputy CM post only briefly before it changed hands.\n*   By 1967, when Brahmananda Reddy became Chief Minister, many shortcomings in implementing the Gentlemen''s Agreement had already surfaced, and the regional committee meant to safeguard Telangana had become largely nominal in practice.\n*   After Achyutha Reddy''s tenure as committee chairman, Telangana''s political representation splintered between rival camps — one under Achyutha Reddy, another under Hayagriva Chari — undermining a unified Telangana political voice even as the movement gathered pace.\n\n### The Unresolved Seniority List Issue\n\n*   Even 15 years after Andhra Pradesh''s formation, a joint seniority list combining employees of both regions had still not been finalized.\n*   Inequities and irregularities in the interim arrangements were regularly pointed out by Telangana employees, while a recurring core grievance was that Telangana''s funds were being diverted to Andhra rather than spent within the region — a complaint that ran throughout the movement''s employee-related demands.\n*   Telangana government employees in particular grew increasingly restive over this unresolved status, feeding into the broader employee unrest already detailed under \"Dissatisfaction Among Employees\" above.\n\n### The All-Party Committee — Central Government Oversight Mechanism\n\n*   As part of the All-Party Agreement''s implementation, two senior officials were designated to ensure the removal of non-natives from posts reserved for Telangana locals was carried out properly, and that no compensation was paid for such displacement beyond what the agreement specified.\n*   Under the arrangement, if suitable Telangana natives were unavailable for a reserved post, the post was to be kept vacant rather than filled by a non-native, and departments were instructed not to allow local reservation requirements to be circumvented through administrative loopholes.\n*   Non-native employees removed under this process were to be given employment opportunities in the Andhra region without loss of service benefits, with additional posts created there if necessary.\n\n### Seniority List Preparation Rules\n\n*   The rules specified that a single seniority list should be prepared for Andhra and Telangana employees together, based on their designated posts, applying uniformly wherever the Central Government had approved a list, or, where no such approved list existed, applying whatever provisional list the state government was currently following — while committing that no adverse action would be taken against employees on the basis of any list not yet approved by the Centre.\n\n### The Question of Telangana''s Surplus Funds — Broader Political Context\n\n*   The all-party agreement''s discussions on surplus funds proceeded alongside efforts to dispel misconceptions about how much of Telangana''s revenue had genuinely gone unspent within the region; this is the same subject examined in detail by the Kumar Lalith and Bhargava Committees (see the earlier section on these committees), which put the unspent surplus at **Rs 34.10 crore** and **Rs 28.34 crore** respectively.\n*   After the 1956 Gentlemen''s Agreement, a further arrangement more favourable to Telangana was reached as the movement intensified, though some of the officials responsible for implementing it were themselves implicated in irregularities as the agitation deepened.\n*   To address the risk of 4,500 identified non-mulki employees in Telangana being difficult to relocate to Andhra given Supreme Court constraints, the Chief Minister created **2,000 new posts specifically for Telangana** and empowered Collectors to fill them locally.\n\n### The Vanchu (Wanchoo) Committee — Additional Detail on Its Constitutional Reasoning\n\n*   A committee headed by former Supreme Court Chief Justice K.N. Wanchoo, with Central Government Attorney General M.C. Setalvad and former Attorney General Niren De as members, was tasked with finding constitutionally sound ways to protect Mulki-based employment safeguards.\n*   The committee''s central finding was that, while the Constitution empowers Parliament to legislate residency-based preferences for an entire state''s employment, it does not empower Parliament to restrict such preference to only one region within a state — meaning continued Mulki Rules of the kind Telangana sought were constitutionally unsustainable in their original form (this corroborates and adds constitutional reasoning to the Wanchoo Committee findings already detailed in the earlier section).\n*   The Committee accordingly held that no law could guarantee this kind of protection to any one district, taluka, or village''s residents in a way that fully insulated local job access from the wider state''s workforce — a structural limitation the Vanchu Committee itself flagged as the reason it recommended devolving administrative hiring authority (over Class III and IV posts) to the Telangana Regional Committee and local Collectors instead of relying on residency law.\n\n### Nagarjuna Sagar and Canal-Water Diversion Grievance\n\n*   When Hyderabad''s engineers originally designed the Nagarjuna Sagar project, the plan was for its left-canal waters to irrigate land beginning around Guntur district and extending across parts of coastal Andhra, while some portion of the canal was also meant to irrigate land in Telangana''s Nalgonda, Khammam, and Warangal districts along the Musi/Krishna–Godavari belt.\n*   Under the scheme, water was meant to reach the Nandigama area via Distributory 19, requiring canal construction to reach Nandigama; however, before that canal construction was completed, water from Distributory 16 was diverted directly to the Nandigama region — bypassing the planned sequence and effectively earmarking the area''s water supply ahead of schedule.\n*   This came to be seen, in Telangana public perception, as evidence that irrigation infrastructure nominally classified as a shared \"K.L. Project\" was, in practice, being used to prioritize Andhra-region needs over Telangana''s, deepening the region''s sense of economic marginalization already reflected in the Pochampad-funding grievance described earlier under \"9 January — Warangal Meeting.\"\n\n### Other Reasons for the Agitation\n\n*   Telangana and Andhra employees did not get along well at a basic working level: Andhra employees, considering themselves more knowledgeable, tended to look down on their Telangana counterparts.\n*   Telangana employees generally worked at a steady, unhurried pace, while Andhra employees worked with greater urgency and speed when performing tasks — a difference in working style that made it difficult for each side to understand the other and fostered mutual suspicion and envy.\n*   Telangana officials, regardless of whether they were Hindu or Muslim, tended to treat people evenhandedly in work-related matters, whereas the conduct of Andhra government employees was perceived as noticeably different in this respect — a perceived double standard that increased mistrust between the two communities'' officials.\n*   Andhra cultivators began purchasing Telangana farmers'' land at low prices (roughly three to five hundred rupees per acre) and then raising crop yields dramatically on that same land — since Telangana farmers who sold their land often ended up needing to work as labourers on their own former fields. \n\n*   Because migrant Andhra farmers demanded far less in wages than local Telangana labour required, this caused deep psychological distress in Telangana''s rural communities. This land-alienation dimension never received serious attention from the region''s political associations at the time, since — as the saying goes, \"whoever needs the sale has less bargaining power\" — those forced to sell were rarely in a position to hold out for a fair price.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p1", "partName": "Telangana Praja Samithi''s Early 1970 Activities", "bodyMd": "The Telangana Praja Samithi (T.P.S) began 1970 with a series of state-level mobilisation efforts aimed at pressing the Centre on the separate statehood demand.\n\n**First State-level meeting**\n- On **10th January, 1970**, the T.P.S State-level meeting was inaugurated by **Marri Chenna Reddy** at the Boys Scout Headquarters, Secunderabad.\n- **Nagari Krishna** was President of the invitation committee for this meeting.\n- **M.P. \"Gray\"** of the British Labour Party attended as a special guest.\n- At this meeting, Marri Chenna Reddy requested the Centre to hold a referendum on separate Telangana statehood.\n\n**Day of Protest and Martyr''s Day**\n- **15th January, 1970** was observed as a \"Day of Protest,\" marking one year since the launch of the Separate Telangana Movement.\n- A large open meeting was held at the Keshav Memorial ground, chaired by **M. Sridhar Reddy**.\n- On **21st January, 1970**, the two rival T.P.S factions — led by **Mrs. Sadalakshmi** and **M. Sridhar Reddy** — merged into a single entity.\n- Martyr **Shankar** had died during police firing at Sadashivpet on 24th January, 1969.\n- Mrs. Sadalakshmi called for **24th January, 1970** to be observed as **\"Martyr''s Day\"** in his memory.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p2", "partName": "Telangana United Front", "bodyMd": "Alongside the T.P.S, a parallel political formation emerged from within the state legislature.\n\n**Formation**\n- In January 1970, Telangana-seeking legislators met in Hyderabad under **Nukala Ram Chandra Reddy**''s chairmanship and decided to quit the State Government.\n- On **7th February, 1970**, **V.B. Raju** resigned from the State Cabinet, formed the **\"Telangana United Front\"**, and became its President, announcing that 35 MLAs and 12 MLCs backed the group.\n\n**Leadership and Recognition**\n- V.B. Raju — Chairman of the Front.\n- Nukala Ramachandra Reddy — leader of the Front in the State Assembly.\n- K. Rama Chandra Reddy — leader of the Front in the Council.\n- **Palvai Goverdhan Reddy**, the first signatory to the Front''s list, later withdrew; the remaining list was submitted to Assembly Speaker **B.V. Subba Reddy**.\n- On **9th February, 1970**, Speaker Subba Reddy recognised the Telangana United Front as the official opposition party.\n- The Front comprised 28 rebel Congressmen and 3 Independents (total 31 members).\n- **Rama Chandra Reddy** became Leader of Opposition, replacing **Gouthu Lachchanna**.\n- Key leaders who joined: Nukala Ram Chandra Reddy, Achyutha Reddy, Konda Laxman Bapuji, K. Ram Chandra Reddy.\n\n**Activities**\n- On **24th March, 1970**, during the budget speech by Finance Minister **Vijay Bhasker Reddy**, Front members walked out of the Assembly, demanding separate budget estimates for Andhra and Telangana regions.\n- At a public meeting at Tilak Park, Nizamabad, on **6th January, 1971**, V.B. Raju criticised the state government for neglecting Telangana''s economic and social development.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p3", "partName": "Centre''s Administrative Measures for Telangana (1969–70)", "bodyMd": "Parallel to political mobilisation, the Centre acted to strengthen the constitutional Regional Council mechanism and address grievances of Telangana employees.\n\n**Extended Powers to Telangana Regional Council**\n- On **7th March, 1970**, the President issued an order extending the powers of the Telangana Regional Council by amending the Andhra Pradesh Regional Committee Order, 1958.\n- These extended powers were to come into force from **9th March, 1970**.\n- New powers granted included: separate department-wise income–expenditure reporting for Andhra and Telangana regions; university education, jobs, and industry brought under the Committee''s ambit; recruitment rules for Telangana locals brought under the Committee''s ambit; the State Government required to report on implementation of the Centre''s decision on merger of services; and a report to be submitted every six months on action taken on the Committee''s recommendations.\n\n**High-Level Supervisory Committee on Employees'' Issues**\n- This Committee was constituted following a resolution passed by the Telangana Regional Committee in **August 1969**.\n- The State Government formed a high-level committee chaired by **Justice Nasirullah Baig** to examine issues concerning Telangana employees.\n- Other members: **Iqbal Chand** (member, Revenue Board), **C.R. Krishna Swamy** (Special Secretary, Board of Revenue), and **Rao Saheb**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p4", "partName": "V.B. Raju''s Sub-State Demand", "bodyMd": "- In the Rajya Sabha elections held on **28th March, 1970**, V.B. Raju was elected to the Rajya Sabha from the Telangana region by a margin of 31 votes in the third round of counting under the legislators'' quota.\n- On **13th May, 1970**, speaking in the Rajya Sabha, V.B. Raju requested the Centre to grant Telangana **\"Sub-State\" status**, similar to Meghalaya''s.\n- Congress member **Mrs. Yashoda Reddy** opposed this proposal.\n- Dr. Chenna Reddy, referring to V.B. Raju''s remarks, stated in Hyderabad that there was no change in the Telangana demand.\n- The **Students Action Committee** opposed V.B. Raju''s comments.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p5", "partName": "Agitation and Parliamentary Debate (April–May 1970)", "bodyMd": "**Praja Samithi Satyagraha and Related Arrests**\n- The Telangana Praja Samithi organised a four-day personal satyagraha programme for the achievement of Telangana statehood.\n- **Nukala Ramchandra Reddy** led the satyagraha on its first day, **27th April, 1970**.\n- On **28th April**, the government arrested Members of Parliament **R. Surender Reddy**, **Rameswara Rao**, and other volunteers involved in the satyagraha.\n- On **3rd May, 1970**, Hyderabad City Mayor **Lakshminarayana** was arrested for holding a satyagraha in front of All India Radio for a separate Telangana.\n\n**Loksabha Debate on Telangana**\n- On **18th May, 1970**, Jansangh MP **Chand Goyal** stated in the Lok Sabha that the Central and State governments were trying to keep Telangana permanently backward.\n- Responding, **Y.B. Chavan** said the government was trying its best to create a suitable environment on the Telangana issue.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p6", "partName": "K.V. Ranga Reddy''s Death and the Pochampadu Project", "bodyMd": "- **K.V. Ranga Reddy** (80) died at 10:30 pm on **24th July, 1970**.\n- His body was covered with the Telangana flag, and the funeral was held at Amberpet Cemetery under government mandate.\n- On the same day, **24th July, 1970**, Chief Minister **Kasu Brahmananda Reddy** inaugurated the Pochampadu Project by releasing its waters into the south canal.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p7", "partName": "Telangana Praja Parishad and Allied Bodies", "bodyMd": "- The **\"Telangana Praja Parishad\"** was formed to pursue separate Telangana, with **K.R. Amos**, **Jagan Mohan Reddy**, **G.P. Saxena**, and **Ansari** among its important members.\n- On **3rd September, 1970**, about 250 MPs presented a memorandum to the Prime Minister proposing a legislative mechanism to implement the Telangana Regional Committee''s decisions.\n- On **3rd January, 1971**, the **\"Telangana Student Council\"** was started in Kachiguda, with **G. Sudarshan Reddy** of Osmania University Arts College as convener.\n- On **28th January, 1971**, T.P.S President Marri Chenna Reddy launched a weekly magazine, **\"Telangana Sentinel.\"**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p8", "partName": "Evolution of Telangana Praja Samithi as a Political Party", "bodyMd": "- On **28th February, 1969**, students and lawyers formed the **\"Telangana People''s Convention,\"** which became the **\"Telangana Praja Samithi\"** on **25th March, 1969**, later transforming into a political party as political leaders joined it.\n- On **22nd–23rd July, 1970**, a State Council meeting at the T.P.S office in Hyderguda formally decided to convert the T.P.S into a political party, with a target of 10 lakh members; this proposal, approved by the working group on 22nd July, was ratified by the State Council on 23rd July.\n- **Konda Laxman Bapuji** opposed this decision.\n- On **4th January, 1971**, Marri Chenna Reddy formally announced the T.P.S as a political party.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p9", "partName": "Elections during the Movement (1970–71)", "bodyMd": "**Khairatabad By-election**\n- The by-election followed the death of Congress MLA **B.V. Guru Murthy**.\n- T.P.S nominated **Nagam Krishna Rao**, though **Konda Laxman** and **Sadalakshmi** opposed this and backed **Venkatesham** instead (who later withdrew).\n- Congress fielded **S. Yadagiri**.\n- On **15th June, 1970**, Nagam Krishna Rao won by a margin of about 14,000 votes; Chenna Reddy called it \"a victory of Telangana people.\"\n- Gram Panchayat elections were also held in 1970, in which the Praja Samithi won most Sarpanch posts, but its performance in indirect elections for Panchayat Samithi Presidents and Zilla Parishad Chairmen was nominal, attributed to the ruling party''s superior resources.\n\n**Legislative Council Elections**\n- On **24th June, 1970**, elections were held for six Legislative Council seats in Telangana; T.P.S contested 5 and won 3.\n- **Venkat Rami Reddy** (T.P.S, sitting MLC) won from one Hyderabad local constituency; Congress candidate **K. Prabhakar Reddy** won from another.\n- **P. Narsireddy** (Mahbubnagar) and **Souri Lakshma Reddy** (Medak) also won for T.P.S.\n- Seeing T.P.S''s growing strength, the government postponed the twin-cities'' municipal corporation elections, fearing defeat.\n\n**Siddipet By-election**\n- The Siddipet MLA seat fell vacant after V.B. Raju''s election to the Rajya Sabha.\n- On **17th November, 1970**, T.P.S candidate **Madan Mohan** defeated Congress''s **P.V. Rajeshwar Rao** by a margin of 19,800 votes.\n- Marri Chenna Reddy said the victory would ensure support for separate Telangana across all regions and communities.\n- Following this win, several parties reconsidered their stance: the **Jan Sangh** delegation at a Kakinada meeting said it would review its earlier opinion, and the **C.P.I** opined that a solution should be found for Telangana.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p10", "partName": "Split in the Praja Samithi and Negotiations with the Centre", "bodyMd": "- On **10th January, 1971**, at a T.P.S meeting in Sarojini Devi Hall, Hyderabad, Chenna Reddy''s pro-Prime Minister stance was condemned, resulting in the formation of a **\"New Praja Samithi.\"**\n- Alarmed by the Siddipet result, **Indira Gandhi** invited Marri Chenna Reddy to Delhi, proposing that T.P.S support Congress instead of contesting the upcoming Lok Sabha elections, with a possible merger.\n- Chenna Reddy conditioned any merger on Telangana statehood being formed.\n- Indira Gandhi proposed implementing an \"8-point formula,\" with Telangana to be formed after 5 years if two-thirds of Telangana legislators still found injustices persisting.\n- Chenna Reddy appointed a 14-member committee, convened by **Nukala Ramachandra Reddy**, to examine the proposal; the committee recommended a 2-year period instead of 5, tied to two-thirds support from legislators elected in the 1972 assembly elections — a condition Indira Gandhi did not accept.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p11", "partName": "Lok Sabha Elections, 1971", "bodyMd": "- In March 1971, Indira Gandhi dissolved the Lok Sabha and called general elections.\n- T.P.S, under Chenna Reddy, contested alone using the **\"Shovel\" (Salkepara)** symbol, with the slogan of eliminating economic disparities and achieving separate Telangana.\n- The Indira Congress contested with the **\"Cow-Calf\"** symbol.\n- T.P.S won **10 of 14** Lok Sabha seats in Telangana, securing **47.5%** of the vote — a sweep that gave the party its strongest bargaining position yet with the Centre.\n- Of the remaining 4 seats: Congress won 3 (Khammam, Nizamabad, Adilabad), and CPI(M)''s **Bhim Reddy Narsimha Reddy** won Miryalaguda.\n- The 10 T.P.S winners, spread across the region, were: **S.B. Giri** (Warangal), **Mallikarjun** (Medak), **G.S. Melkote** (Hyderabad), **G. Venkat Swamy** (Siddipet), **J. Rameshwar Rao** (Mahbubnagar), **M. Bhishma Dev** (Nagarkurnool), **M.M. Hashim** (Secunderabad), **V. Tulasi Ram** (Peddapalli), **K. Rama Krishna Reddy** (Nalgonda), and **M. Satyanarayana Rao** (Karimnagar).\n- Chenna Reddy himself did not contest, being under a six-year ban imposed by the Supreme Court.\n- Despite T.P.S''s regional sweep, Indira Gandhi, having won an absolute majority nationally, did not support separate Telangana statehood — but with the 1972 assembly elections approaching, she began efforts to merge the T.P.S into Congress.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p12", "partName": "Merger of T.P.S with Congress", "bodyMd": "- Marri Chenna Reddy reached an oral agreement with Indira Gandhi on **6 conditions** regarding protections for Telangana, and merged the T.P.S with Congress, holding secret talks in Delhi while setting aside the movement''s original aspirations.\n- On **18th September, 1971**, the T.P.S State Council resolved to merge with Congress, after the Centre approved the Six Principles proposed by T.P.S.\n- On **19th September, 1971**, student leaders and Telangana activists convened a meeting condemning the Praja Samithi''s decision as a \"sabotage\" and resolved to continue the movement; **Mrs. Eshwari Bai, Purushottama Reddy, Sridhar Reddy, Pratap Kishore,** and **Santapuri Raghuveera Rao**, among others, formed the **\"Sampurna Telangana Praja Samithi\"** to carry it forward.\n\n**Six Principles proposed by T.P.S:**\n1. Removal of Brahmananda Reddy as Chief Minister (change of state leadership).\n2. Statutory powers for the Telangana Regional Committee.\n3. Formation of a Special Pradesh Congress Committee for Telangana.\n4. Separate budget and estimates for Telangana.\n5. Implementation of Mulky rules.\n6. An opportunity for Telangana legislators to decide on separate statehood, based on the government''s performance, if required.\n\n- Of these six proposals, only the **first** was implemented.\n\n**Leadership change and merger completion**\n- On **15th September, 1971**, Chief Minister **Brahmananda Reddy** resigned.\n- On **18th September, 1971**, the T.P.S working group decided to merge with Congress.\n- On **24th September, 1971**, the **All India Congress Committee (AICC)** accepted the merger proposal.\n- On **25th September, 1971**, AICC observer **C. Subramanyam** presided over the Congress legislative session to elect a new leader; **P.V. Narsimha Rao** was elected.\n- On **30th September, 1971**, P.V. Narsimha Rao became Chief Minister of Andhra Pradesh — the **first person from the Telangana region** to hold this office.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p13", "partName": "P.V. Narasimha Rao", "bodyMd": "**Early Life**\n- Born **Pamulaparthi Venkat Narsimha Rao** on **28th June, 1921**, in Laknepally village, Warangal district.\n- Adopted by **Pamulaparthy Ranga Rao** and **Rukminamma** of Vangara village, Bheemadevarapalli mandal, Karimnagar district.\n- In 1938, he took part in the Vandemataram movement at Osmania University and was expelled.\n- He then joined Nagpur University, completed his LLB, and worked as a junior lawyer under **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao**.\n- He was a disciple of **Swami Ramananda Theertha** and was actively involved in State Congress politics.\n- He served as the founding President of the **Telugu Academy**, established in 1968.\n\n**Political Career in Andhra Pradesh**\n- First elected to the Andhra Pradesh Legislative Assembly in **1957** from **Manthani** constituency, on a Congress ticket; he represented Manthani for four consecutive terms (1957–1977).\n- Held ministerial portfolios: Justice and Information (1962–64), Justice and Endowments (1964–67), Health and Medical (1967), and later Education.\n- Served as Andhra Pradesh Chief Minister from **30th September, 1971** to **10th January, 1973**.\n\n**National Career**\n- First elected to the Lok Sabha in 1977 from **Hanumakonda**.\n- In the Union Government, he held the portfolios of Home Affairs, Defence, Planning Commission, and Foreign Affairs.\n- Became **Prime Minister of India** on **21st June, 1991**, serving until **16th May, 1996**, managing a minority government for five years — earning the title **\"Apara Chanukyudu.\"**\n- The **first South Indian** and the **only Telugu person** to become Prime Minister of India.\n- He assumed the Prime Ministership without being a sitting MP, later winning a by-election to the Lok Sabha from **Nandyal**.\n- Appointed economist **Man Mohan Singh** as Finance Minister and introduced India''s new liberalised economic policies.\n- Died on **23rd December, 2004**.\n\n**Literary Work**\n- Wrote an autobiography titled **\"Insider,\"** translated into Telugu as **\"Lopali Manishi\"** (Inner Person).\n- Could speak **17 languages**, including English, Hindi, and other southern languages.\n- Translated **Viswanatha Satyanarayana**''s Telugu classic **\"Veyipadagalu\"** into Hindi as **\"Sahasraphan,\"** for which he received the **Kendra Sahitya Akademi Award**.\n- Translated the Marathi novel **\"Pan Lakshat Kon Gheto\"** (But Who Pays Attention?) into Telugu as **\"Abala Jeevitham.\"**\n- Authored the well-known story **\"Golla Ramavva,\"** set against the backdrop of the Telangana armed struggle.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p14", "partName": "Presidents of Telangana Praja Samithi", "bodyMd": "Leadership of the T.P.S changed hands rapidly in its first months — a sign of the internal factional tussle between Chenna Reddy, Sadalakshmi, and Madan Mohan before Chenna Reddy''s tenure stabilised from August 1969:\n\n1. **Ananthula Madan Mohan** — 25 March to 6 April 1969\n2. **B.S. Giri** (temporary) — 6 to 9 April 1969\n3. **T.N. Sada Lakshmi** — 9 to 25 April 1969\n4. **Ananthula Madan Mohan** (second term) — 25 April to 22 May 1969\n5. **Marri Chenna Reddy** (first term) — 22 May to 25 June 1969\n6. **Madan Mohan** (second term) — 25 June to 2 July 1969\n7. **T.N. Sada Lakshmi** (second term) — 3 July to 29 August 1969\n8. **Marri Chenna Reddy** (second, long-running term) — 29 August 1969 until the party''s merger with Congress on 24 September 1971", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 15, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter32-telangana-movement-after-1970-p15", "partName": "Organisations of the Early Movement Phase and Their Founders", "bodyMd": "The movement drew support from several distinct strands — literary/cultural bodies, student groups, professional unions, and pro-integration counter-groups formed by the opposing side. Grouping them by strand, rather than by date alone, shows how broad-based the mobilisation was.\n\n**Literary and cultural organisations**\n- **Navya Sahithya Samithi** (1952) — Kuruganti Sitaramaiah\n- **Sahitya Vikasa Mandali** (1960) — Vemuganti Narsimha Charyulu\n- **Medak District Writers'' Association** (1964) — Mudumbai Ranga Krishnama Charyulu\n- **Freeverse Front** (1966) — Kundurti Anjaneyulu\n- **Srujana**, a modern Telugu literary platform (1966) — Pendyala Varavara Rao\n- **Revolutionary Writers'' Association (Virasam)** (1971) — Varavara Rao\n- **Bharathiya Sahithya Samithi** (1971) — Ande Venkata Rajyam\n- **Jana Natya Mandali** (1972) — Gaddar\n\n**Student and youth organisations**\n- **Students Working Committee** (28 July 1952) — Buchaiah\n- **Telangana Students Working Committee** (13 January 1969) — Mallikarjun\n- **City Students Integrated Working Committee** (1969) — Gottem Gopal Reddy\n\n**Regional/political mobilisation bodies**\n- **Hyderabad Hitha Rakshana Samithi** (1952) — G. Rama Chary\n- **Mitra Mandali** (1957) — Udayaraju Seshagiri Rao and Pendyala Raghava Rao\n- **Telangana Maha Sabha** (1958)\n- **Telangana Regional Council** (1968) — Kolishetty Ramdasu\n- **Telangana Protection Committee** (13 January 1969) — Katam Lakshmi Narayana\n- **Telangana Praja Samithi (T.P.S)** (25 March 1969) — Madan Mohan\n- **Sampurna Telangana Praja Samithi** (1969) — Satyanarayana Reddy and Keshav Rao Jadhav\n- **Telangana Udyama Samanvaya Sangham** (1969) — Achyuth Reddy\n- **Telangana Praja Parishad** (1969) — K.R. Amos\n- **Telangana Vimochana Samithi** (1969) — K. Narsimha Reddy\n- **Special Telangana Congress Forum** (4 July 1971) — Marri Chenna Reddy\n\n**Professional and employee unions**\n- **Telangana University College Teachers'' Convention** (20 May 1969) — Prof. Shah Manjur Alam\n- **Telangana NGOs'' Union** (1969) — Amos\n- **Telangana Advocates Union** (1969) — B.C. Jain\n- **Telangana Integration Union** (1969) — G. Venkata Swamy\n- **Labourers Joint Action Committee** (1969) — Govinda Singh\n\n**Pro-integration counter-groups (opposing separate Telangana)**\n- **Poti Telangana Praja Samithi**, a rival/competing T.P.S (May 1969) — Sridhar Reddy\n- **Telangana Congress Samithi** (1969) — Konda Laxman Bapuji\n- **Public Integration Committee** (Powrula Samaikyatha Sangam) (3 April 1969) — G. Venkata Swamy\n- **Bhava Samaikyatha Praja Sanghatana** (1969) — Swamy Ramananda Theertha\n- **Rashtra Samaikyatha Samrakshana Samithi** (1969) — P.V. Ranga Rao\n- **Poti T.N.G.O**, a rival/competing T.N.G.O (1969) — Harinath Rao\n\nThis split between separatist and integrationist bodies, especially visible in 1969, reflects how contested and organisationally fragmented the Telangana question had already become well before the T.P.S–Congress merger of 1971.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter33-post-1971-andhra-pradesh-political-history', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter33-post-1971-andhra-pradesh-political-history-p1", "partName": "P.V. Narasimha Rao''s Rule", "bodyMd": "After Brahmananda Reddy''s resignation, the Telangana leadership question resurfaced, with Chowkar Rao, Vengala Rao, and P.V. Narasimha Rao as contenders. When P.V. was chosen as leader, the Brahmananda Reddy faction cooperated with the ruling faction, and his government took the oath on **21 September 1971**.\n\nSanjeevaiah and Raghuramaiah, both national-level leaders, ensured Brahmananda Reddy''s supporters did not get ministerial berths, leaving that faction dissatisfied. Previously sidelined sections gained prominence in the ministry for the first time.\n\nGeneral elections to the Andhra Pradesh Assembly were held in **February 1972**. Backward classes, minorities, Harijans, and women together were allotted about **60% of the seats**, and for the first time forward castes and wealthier sections saw their ministry representation reduced. **B.V. Subba Reddy** became Deputy Chief Minister.\n\nTwo developments stood out from this period:\n\n*   Generous land grants made by earlier ministerial groups came under scrutiny.\n*   Two Panchayati Raj bodies, seen as fronts for landholders, faced Congress attempts at abolition.\n\nP.V. knew that although Indira Gandhi''s support — not forward-caste strength — had made him Chief Minister, retaining the post needed the cooperation of the Kamma, Reddy, and Velama castes, support he could not always count on. He therefore tactically drew Harijans, tribal groups, backward classes, and minorities toward himself.\n\nThis weakened the landholding classes'' grip and allowed the landless among these castes to drift away — a drift that contributed to the strengthening of the separate Andhra movement.\n\n### Land Reforms\n\nThe Andhra Pradesh government issued an ordinance banning acquisition of agricultural land beyond a ceiling:\n\n*   **18 acres** on wet lands\n*   **34 acres** on dry lands\n\nThis struck a hard blow at landholders, since political decentralisation was not possible without economic decentralisation. The ordinance issued on **3 May 1972** tried to block a coming land bill, but there was no courage to act on it openly.\n\nThe government announced in **December 1972** that it would pass the bill and implement it. Since the Panchayati Raj system could not cooperate with these reforms, attempts were made to change its structure.\n\nThe Land Reforms Bill was finalised in **September 1972**. A Select Committee report followed, and both houses of the legislature passed the bill by large majorities. Just as the ground seemed set for enforcement, the Supreme Court''s Mulki Rules verdict intervened (below).\n\n### The Mulki Rules Verdict and Its Fallout\n\nThree Andhra Pradesh High Court judges had, on **12 December 1970**, ruled the Mulki Rules invalid; six judges reversed this ruling on **4 February 1972**. The P.V. government appealed to the Supreme Court, hoping the High Court''s decision would be upheld in the state government''s favour.\n\n**Cause:** the Supreme Court, on **3 October 1972**, upheld the validity of the Mulki Rules → **Effect:** this created major problems for the state government. Chief Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao called it the final word on Telangana people''s rights, but it angered people in the Andhra region.\n\nLandholders used the verdict as a fresh pretext to avoid enforcing the land ceiling law. Agitators mobilised to resist it in several ways:\n\n*   Some convened meetings of landholders opposing the law.\n*   Others held meetings in support of landless labourers.\n*   Some declared minors in the family as adults, so land could be split across more individual shareholders.\n*   Some hastily married off minor daughters, for the same purpose of creating more individual landholders.\n*   Some declared their lands to be under mortgage to evade the ceiling.\n\nThese same groups both resisted the land law and joined the separate-Andhra agitation. The Chief Minister, Andhra ministers, and Telangana ministers began negotiating separately with each other as the Andhra region reached breaking point.\n\nTelangana and Andhra ministers, honouring earlier promises, together persuaded Indira Gandhi to announce the **Panchasutra (Five-Point) scheme**.\n\n### The Panchasutra (Five-Point) Scheme\n\n1.  Mulki-Rule reservations would apply to non-gazetted employees, Naib Tehsildars, Assistant Surgeons, and Junior Engineers in Telangana — in the Secretariat, one in three vacancies at departmental-head offices and two in three vacancies at district-level offices would fall under this reservation.\n2.  These protections would remain until **1980** in Telangana, and until **1987** in the twin cities.\n3.  Regularisation could extend to promotion-related services up to the first or second gazetted grade.\n4.  Technical education opportunities for Telangana people would expand, with no domicile-based restriction on new admissions.\n5.  A joint police cadre would be created for the twin cities.\n\nThis scheme, aimed specifically at Junior Engineers and Assistant Civil Servants, was only a compromise and not a permanent solution. The people did not accept it, and demand for a separate Andhra State grew louder.\n\n### Six-Point Formula and P.V.''s Exit\n\nAs demand for a separate Andhra State continued, several Andhra ministers resigned. On **11 January 1973**, Madduri Subbareddy, Anam Venkatareddy, G. Someshwar, C. Das, Vadde Nageshwara Rao, R. Rajagopala Reddy, and N. Amaranatha Reddy were appointed as new ministers.\n\nOn the same day, the state Congress working committee was dissolved and an emergency committee formed, signalling President''s Rule. On the Prime Minister''s directive, **P.V. Narasimha Rao resigned on 17 January 1973**.\n\n**President''s Rule** began on **18 January 1973**, with H.C. Sarin and V.K. Rao as Governor''s advisors. From 18 January to 13 December 1973 — **320 days** — the state remained under President''s Rule, with the Assembly suspended.\n\nAndhra-Telangana leaders formulated the **Six-Point Formula** to preserve state unity while addressing all regions'' needs.\n\n### The Six-Point Formula\n\nUnder this formula, Telangana no longer needed a regional council or the Mulki Rules; abolishing either would not harm Telangana''s people''s rights.\n\n*   For direct recruitment of non-gazetted employees, Tehsildars, Junior Engineers, and Assistant Surgeons, local candidates would get priority, with the first two promotions going by regional seniority.\n*   Three regional zones would be created, each with a regional development board.\n*   A Central University would be established with central funding to expand education in the state.\n*   A Tribunal would examine employees'' grievances, with its decisions treated as final.\n*   The capital city would grow in a planned manner, giving twin-city residents priority for employment.\n*   Backward regions would receive generous central financial assistance, prioritising mid-range irrigation and rural electrification.\n*   All backward regions would be brought to the general development level within a fixed period, with rapid agricultural development targeted for Telangana.\n\nDevelopment-board areas in Telangana, Rayalaseema, and Andhra received ₹100 crore in central funding, including ₹10 crore for Hyderabad city. These funds were distributed at a **5:3:2 ratio** among 162 backward taluks across the three regions, with the scheme due for completion by **31 March 1979**. Regional council chairmen would hold ministerial rank.\n\nP.V. played the main role in shaping this scheme, which received full approval. Andhra Congress leaders, tired of the agitation, sought power, and Congress legislators grew impatient with President''s Rule; a special session unanimously approved the Six-Point Formula in the legislature.\n\nSince Andhra leaders insisted P.V.''s leadership must go, he did not get the chance to prove his majority. The **1973 Six-Point Formula** brought a political settlement, and things proceeded peacefully for a while — though the separate Telangana movement kept simmering underground.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter33-post-1971-andhra-pradesh-political-history-p2", "partName": "Jalagam Vengala Rao''s Rule", "bodyMd": "**Jalagam Vengala Rao** was elected Chief Minister on **10 December 1973**, succeeding P.V. Narasimha Rao. Compared to most Chief Ministers, there was little doubt about his suitability.\n\nIn the favourable circumstances of the Emergency, he continued in power without facing party dissent, working hard both to strengthen himself and for state development. Political rivalry eased partly because Brahmananda Reddy was given a union ministerial berth, Chenna Reddy was appointed Governor of Uttar Pradesh, and P.V. left the state as AICC General Secretary. **B.V. Subba Reddy** became Deputy Chief Minister.\n\nVengala Rao understood well the trouble the land-ceiling law had created and its effect on the separate Andhra movement, so he thought through new schemes carefully before introducing them. Like Narasimha Rao, he lacked a distinct group of his own and needed the Kamma, Reddy, and Velama castes'' cooperation to continue.\n\nHe was not a natural reformer by temperament, nor capable of pushing through radical change. Some considered him radical only because his views aligned with weaker sections'' sentiments, but he was not fundamentally radical.\n\nAlthough he did not obstruct caste and political violence, and did not actively encourage it, rumours of ministry expansion spread. Quarrels broke out among competing-faction ministers in each district, and in districts with two ministers, factional rivalries became uncontrollable, with mutual accusations over whatever went wrong.\n\nVengala Rao organised the first-ever **World Telugu Conference in 1975**. Under Sanjay Gandhi''s push, police and official excesses during the Emergency reflected the distortions of his administration, and coercive sterilisation drives also caused him trouble.\n\nThe **Nagarjuna Sagar collapse on 19 November 1977**, with no advance-warning systems in place, killed about **10,000 people**.\n\n### Janata Party\n\nIn **March 1977**, the old Congress, Jana Sangh, Bharatiya Lok Dal, and Socialist Party united to form the **Janata Party**, which had little strength in the state at the time. All those opposed to Narasimha Rao''s and Vengala Rao''s actions joined it.\n\nJanata''s strength came from three sources:\n\n*   **Urban students**, who saw Congress as authoritarian and Janata as democracy''s protector.\n*   **Those hurt by land-ceiling enforcement and the loss of bonded-labour arrangements** — mainly wealthy agricultural Reddy families.\n*   Political and psychological factors that also drove support toward Janata.\n\nAfter Andhra Pradesh''s formation, the Reddy community had led politically, but this gradually diminished as Kamma, Kapu, and Velama dominance grew. Andhra-Rayalaseema Reddys got reduced representation under Vengala Rao, and his government''s abolition of Panchayati Raj posts — disproportionately Reddy-held — hurt the community further.\n\nFor these reasons, traditionally Congress-supporting landholding interests began strengthening the Janata Party, mainly through disaffected former Congress workers tired of Congress rule and looking for a new party.\n\n### Congress''s Severe Defeat\n\nIn the **1977 Lok Sabha elections**, Congress won only **153 seats**, while the Janata Party and its allied Democratic Congress won **298 seats**. The Janata wave swept the northern states but did not fully materialise in the south.\n\nStill, Janata did not fail entirely in Andhra Pradesh — its candidates placed second in nearly every constituency it lost, most receiving over a lakh votes. **Indira Gandhi, who lost the election, resigned as Prime Minister on 23 March 1977**.\n\nThis brought a non-Congress (Janata) coalition to power at the Centre for the first time, establishing a lasting trend in India''s political culture. Anti-establishment sentiment strengthened nationally, and regional parties in various states gained power by defeating Congress. This 1977 political legacy continues, and the roots of many current ruling parties, state and central, trace back to the Janata Party.\n\n**Morarji Desai** took the oath as India''s fourth Prime Minister on **24 March 1977**, but his stubbornness, outdated attitudes, and mistrust of colleagues quickly fractured Janata''s unity. He resigned on **15 July 1979**.\n\n**Charan Singh** then became Prime Minister with Indira Gandhi''s support; when she withdrew that support, his government lasted only **24 days**. President Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy dissolved the Lok Sabha on **27 August 1979** and ordered mid-term elections — there was no precedent, as in the states, for President''s Rule at the Centre.\n\n### Kasu Brahmananda Reddy as Congress President\n\n**Kasu Brahmananda Reddy** contested for the Congress presidency for the first time in 28 years, on **7 May 1977**, receiving **317 votes** against rival Siddhartha Shankar Ray''s **160**. Indira Gandhi, in political exile, set about splitting Congress for the second time — eight Congress presidents had already changed in the preceding eight years.\n\nKasu said Indira Gandhi began campaigning for reinstatement within just three months of becoming Congress president, in order to keep loyalists in power.\n\n### Formation of the Indira Congress\n\nOn **2 January 1978**, Indira Gandhi broke from the All India National Congress and formed the **Indira Congress**. Of 21 Congress Working Committee members, Kamalapati Tripathi, Syed Mir Qasim, P.V. Narasimha Rao, Ravindra Varma, Buta Singh, A.P. Sharma, and Maragatham Chandrasekhar stayed with her.\n\nThe AICC expelled Indira Gandhi from Congress on **8 January**. An Indira Congress convention was held in Hyderabad on **18 January**, and **Dr. Chenna Reddy** was appointed president of its state unit.\n\n### Assembly Elections — Unexpected Results\n\nIn **February 1978**, elections were held for the Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka Assemblies. The results surprised political observers, as no party won a majority in the state.\n\n| Party | Seats |\n|---|---|\n| Indira Congress | 175 |\n| Janata | 60 |\n| Congress (\"Ruling Congress\") | 30 |\n| CPI + CPM | 8 |\n| Independents | 15 |\n\nFollowing this, **Vengala Rao''s ministry resigned on 28 February 1978**.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter33-post-1971-andhra-pradesh-political-history-p3", "partName": "Five Chief Ministers in Five Years", "bodyMd": "### 1\\. Dr. M. Chenna Reddy\n\nSince Indira Congress lacked a full majority, its state unit president and legislature-party leader, **Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy, took the oath as Chief Minister on 6 March 1978**. In that cabinet, **19 people became ministers for the first time**, with posts split between Andhra and Telangana at a **3:2 ratio**.\n\n*   **Divi Kondaiah Chowdary** was elected unopposed as Speaker.\n*   **K. Prabhakar Reddy** became Deputy Speaker.\n*   Opposition leader **Gouthu Latchanna** was given ministerial rank.\n*   **M.A. Aziz** became PCC president.\n\nIn **April 1978**, when Chenna Reddy and Karnataka CM Devaraj Urs went to Delhi, they told the high command not to interfere in their states'' affairs. They believed internal party differences should be settled through mutual discussion, not through complaints to Delhi.\n\nEven though Indira Gandhi, as party president, still tried to direct state Chief Ministers as if she were Prime Minister, both understood such directives no longer held automatic force. So when meeting her in Delhi, they simply absorbed dissatisfied groups'' complaints and told her they would handle the issues themselves.\n\nWithin three months of taking office, dissatisfaction with Chenna Reddy began, and his fund-raising ventures came under sharp criticism. Indira Gandhi''s victory in the **1980 Lok Sabha elections** and her return to power emboldened dissatisfied groups within the state.\n\n**15 ministers signed an accusation letter** alleging he was misusing the administration for personal financial gain. Clashes between Chief Minister loyalists and dissidents reached a flashpoint, making central intervention unavoidable, yet Chenna Reddy did not go to Delhi and appeared favourable towards Indira Gandhi.\n\nOf Indira Congress''s 255 MLAs, **167 signed**, and of 40 MLC members, **29 signed**, demanding his removal. He called this a \"disgraceful\" state of affairs, and both sides stood their ground stubbornly.\n\nIndira Gandhi, though usually able to resolve political problems quickly, had to bide her time on this occasion — or deliberately delayed while inflicting mental anguish on Chenna Reddy for resisting high-command interference in state affairs. His loyalists believed that very resistance had encouraged dissidents to turn against him.\n\nThe Congress (I) high command never let Andhra Pradesh Chief Ministers retire with dignity:\n\n*   Dr. Chenna Reddy was kept in suspense for three months.\n*   Dissidents were encouraged to abuse and oppose him.\n*   Ministers refused to attend cabinet meetings.\n*   Permission for an Assembly session or Panchayati Raj elections was withheld.\n\nHe was finally asked to send his resignation to the Governor. **Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy resigned as Chief Minister on 10 October 1980**, ending a three-month crisis after a tenure of **30 months**.\n\nThe party turmoil left Chenna Reddy medically affected after leaving office. Despite his reputation for courage, enterprise, able administration, and hard work, his lack of patience and restraint meant he could not retain everyone''s affection — even his cabinet colleagues did not have much regard for him.\n\nCorruption allegations during his tenure grew louder, and his failure to carefully disprove them damaged his government''s public image. What mattered was not what he actually did but people''s perception of it, a lesson that emerges from the political history of the last two decades.\n\nAs Chief Minister, he acted independently, conducting himself as a leader with his own internal-party discipline. He never treated the high command as sacrosanct, never gave up his individuality, and conducted himself as befitted the Chief Minister of a six-crore-person state who valued regional self-respect — he never governed despotically, regardless of who opposed him.\n\nHis downfall was not the work of dissidents alone: Indira Gandhi, feeling he had not shown her the loyalty she expected, removed him from his post.\n\n### 2\\. \"Garibon Ka\" Symbol — T. Anjaiah\n\nFormer Union minister **Tanguturi Anjaiah**, who had once worked as a daily-wage worker in the Alwyn Factory, took the oath as the state''s eighth Chief Minister on **13 August 1980**, replacing Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy. Despite being seen as an ordinary, humble man, he became Chief Minister, trusted for his undisputed reputation, friendliness, and ability to unite everyone.\n\nThe Anjaiah ministry began with **22 members**. On **2 November 1980**, nine more were added, raising the total to **61** — the largest ministry in Andhra Pradesh''s history.\n\nSome who became ministers were not even Assembly members, and one who became a corporation chairman had not even won an election. All ministerial perks — official cars, bungalows, personal assistants — were made available to them. Of the Assembly''s 291 total members, about a hundred received posts.\n\nAnjaiah believed a large ministry made administration more effective, comparing it to an \"Air Bus\": the bigger the plane, the more comfortable the journey. He alone experienced full freedom in forming his ministry, and many were shocked at its size, though Indira Gandhi tolerated it out of personal regard at first.\n\nAs criticism mounted, she told him to reduce it, and within about ten weeks it was cut to **45**. Eleven months later, the same Anjaiah conducted the Panchayati Raj body elections.\n\nAnjaiah''s strength lay in Indira Gandhi''s backing, so there was no rebellion against him within the party — he used to say he was ready to step down whenever the high command felt he should. Even though he took some democratic decisions and implemented them, he too could not remain in power long.\n\nUndisciplined activities by ministers N. Janardhan Reddy and Kona Prabhakara Rao tarnished his ministry. N. Janardhan Reddy''s political manoeuvring in Delhi enraged Rajiv Gandhi, who had Anjaiah publicly insulted and removed from his post.\n\nDuring his tenure, government employees coordinated a unified stand. Anjaiah proved that a disciplined, law-abiding common man could rise to any heights, and he was also a strong encourager of the state''s industrial sector.\n\n### 3\\. Bhavanam Venkatram\n\n**Bhavanam Venkatram Reddy**, a member of the Legislative Council, took the oath as Chief Minister on **16 February 1982**, after Anjaiah. **C. Jagannatha Rao** of Telangana became Deputy Chief Minister.\n\nFilm actor **N.T. Rama Rao (NTR)** was by then preparing to launch a party, and since Bhavanam had no ability to counter him, the high command decided a more suitable person should be Chief Minister and asked Bhavanam to resign. He did so on **20 September 1982**, after a tenure of **6 months and 26 days**.\n\n### 4\\. Kotla Vijaya Bhaskara Reddy (first term)\n\nThe high command selected **Kotla Vijaya Bhaskara Reddy** after Bhavanam''s resignation; he took the oath as Chief Minister on **20 September 1982**. The NTR wave had already begun sweeping the state by then, and believing only Vijaya Bhaskara Reddy could confront NTR, Delhi assured he would continue even after the upcoming elections.\n\nDr. Y.S. Rajasekhara Reddy and Chandrababu Naidu served as ministers in his cabinet, while **G. Venkataswamy** became PCC president in this same period.\n\nCongress Chief Ministers not completing full terms did not reflect a lack of ability — most had competence and administrative skill. But because Congress was a national party, they had to bow to high-command directives, which prevented them from fully demonstrating their strength and brought them disrepute. This proved a boon to the Telugu Desam Party.\n\nThe Vijaya Bhaskara Reddy government lasted **3 months and 19 days**. With Congress''s defeat in the elections on **8 January 1983**, he resigned, and the Telugu Desam Party era began.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter33-post-1971-andhra-pradesh-political-history-p4", "partName": "The Emergence of the Telugu Desam Party", "bodyMd": "### NTR''s First Term\n\n**Nandamuri Taraka Rama Rao** announced his entry into politics on **2 March 1982**, founded the **Telugu Desam Party** on **29 March**, and became its first president. In just four years, the state had seen four Chief Ministers change, anti-Congress sentiment had grown, and people wanted change.\n\nThe TDP received tremendous support in the **1983 elections**. It is rare worldwide for someone unfamiliar with politics to seize power within a few months, yet even locally unfamiliar candidates fielded by the TDP won on the strength of Rama Rao''s personal appeal.\n\n| Party | Seats |\n|---|---|\n| Telugu Desam | 202 |\n| Congress | 60 |\n| CPI | 4 |\n| CPM | 5 |\n| Janata | 1 |\n| BJP | 3 |\n| Majlis | 5 |\n| Independents/others | 13 |\n\nThe NTR government took oath on **9 January 1983**. The first Mahanadu (party plenary) in Vijayawada in **May 1983** was a landmark in Indian political history, where leaders of 23 opposition parties — Left, centrist national parties, the BJP, and regional parties — discussed forming a unified opposition alliance.\n\nFrom that day, NTR gained recognition as a statesman, though excessive workload began affecting his health. He went to America on **24 July 1984** for bypass surgery, which succeeded in Houston. From this point, dissatisfaction and rebellion began within the Telugu Desam Party.\n\nWhile Rama Rao was in America, **Nadendla Bhaskara Rao**, calling himself \"co-pilot\" without either man''s knowledge, began campaigning against him. Many signed a memorandum publicly declaring distrust in NTR and stating he no longer had a legislative majority.\n\nRama Rao returned to Hyderabad promptly on **14 August** and wrote to the Governor without delay, asking for Bhaskara Rao''s removal. The Governor announced Bhaskara Rao''s dismissal on the Chief Minister''s advice.\n\nMinisters T. Jeevan Reddy, Sanga Reddy, Satyanarayana, and Rama Muni Reddy resigned, creating turmoil within the party — citing 17 months of humiliation under the Chief Minister''s autocratic style and resentment at being told they served only one party as a \"private company.\" Bhaskara Rao told the Governor he had a majority, and Governor Ramlal believed him and swore him in as Chief Minister.\n\n### Assessing the Removal of NTR\n\nA grave mistake occurred with NTR''s removal: it was neither the right time nor the right method. Rama Rao had, within his own party and among his own MLAs, been steadily losing respect, as corrupt practices, self-serving activities, alienating actions, and cancelled programmes eroded illusions about him.\n\nA nationwide anti-Congress wave was building while Indira Gandhi''s prestige was rising, so Rama Rao''s isolation stemmed largely from his own conduct. Yet the political attack on him brought vast, undeserved sympathy, briefly making him a political hero.\n\nOf 294 Assembly members, NTR had the support of **168**. Despite this majority, Governor Ramlal told him he had lost his majority and should resign. NTR was ready to bring his supporters to Raj Bhavan to prove his numbers, but the Governor refused to let him in — behaviour that drew widespread condemnation.\n\nThe Governor never tested the numbers of either Rama Rao''s or Bhaskara Rao''s supporters and refused to allow three days for verification, even though Rama Rao himself proved beyond doubt that he had a majority. The opposition used the opportunity to serve its own political interest.\n\nSince the Telugu Desam Party had no comparable strength outside NTR, it had to depend on friendly parties in the crisis. All opposition parties, with the BJP and Communists playing a small role, sided with NTR. Rama Rao said Congress had funded the opposition to profit by using the organisation for its own gain.\n\nSeeing nationwide agitation in NTR''s favour, Indira Gandhi backed down. Governor Ramlal was recalled and **Shankar Dayal Sharma** appointed in his place. Nadendla could not prove his majority.\n\n**NTR was reinstated on 16 September 1984**. The Assembly was dissolved on **27 September**, having lasted 22 months and 15 days from formation — even with an Assembly majority, the ministry felt a fresh public mandate was needed to restore political stability.\n\n### The 30-Day Chief Minister\n\nThe Governor ordered the new Nadendla Bhaskara Rao government to prove its majority within 30 days — this could not be achieved. Fearing Governor dismissal, the **Nadendla ministry resigned on 16 September**, and the suspended Telugu Desam Party, through the same political manoeuvring, continued in power for another six months.\n\n### NTR''s Second Term\n\nIn mid-term elections held in **January 1985**, the Telugu Desam Party won a majority of over two-thirds, and NTR again became Chief Minister. His early goal was breaking Communist dominance, then Congress''s strength — a goal not wrong in itself.\n\nBut the use of anti-social elements by political parties to achieve it had begun long before, and Vijayawada saw riots break out in this period, with murders occurring one after another, hidden behind unresolved crimes and dirty politics.\n\nNTR was again elected party president in **1986**. By rule, he alone had authority to appoint the general secretary, and he wanted **Chandrababu Naidu** for the role; though some party members suggested the post go elsewhere, Chandrababu Naidu received it.\n\n**P. Upendra** became the Telugu Desam Parliamentary Party president in Delhi. Around this time, Defence Minister V.P. Singh split from Rajiv Gandhi and founded \"Jan Morcha.\"\n\nFollowing NTR''s lead on the Bofors issue, opposition MPs resigned their Parliament memberships on **24 July 1989**, four months before the Lok Sabha elections.\n\n### The Rise and Fall of Upendra\n\nNo political party exists without satisfaction or dissatisfaction, and the Telugu Desam Party was no exception. As soon as Nadendla Bhaskara Rao''s chapter closed, the general-secretary position fell to P. Upendra.\n\nAs the first chief secretary to serve as a loyal aide during TDP election campaigns, Upendra received honour within the party. He built connections between the TDP and national opposition parties, gaining significant benefits from those ties by drawing on his past acquaintance with veteran advisor-level leaders.\n\nMany people whom Upendra had recommended, and to whom NTR gave Lok Sabha tickets, did not honour his recommendations for the Assembly elections. The seat Upendra sought was Tirupati; he was given Himayat Nagar instead, an area rife with Telangana and non-Telangana factional disputes — losing there was natural for him.\n\nSuspecting he might rebel after losing, NTR confined Upendra''s role to Delhi politics alone, sending him to the Rajya Sabha, where he carved out an important national position through his own skill. As his influence grew, NTR''s affection for him grew too.\n\nBut in a party functioning on one person''s charisma, no one would speak out against that person''s authoritarianism, however smoothly things went. Even a daring dissenter would face NTR''s wrath, as Upendra''s later fall exemplifies. Chandrababu Naidu became general secretary in his place.\n\nThe **National Front** was formed with great fanfare on **6, 7, and 8 August 1988**, with NTR unanimously chosen as its president and V.P. Singh as convenor. Before the 1989 elections, opposition parties under the National Front reached a seat-sharing agreement.\n\nThe Front, allied with the Left and the BJP, together won about **300 seats**. **V.P. Singh became Prime Minister**, and TDP Rajya Sabha member P. Upendra received the Union Information and Broadcasting portfolio.\n\nIn the **1989 Assembly elections**:\n\n| Party | Seats |\n|---|---|\n| Congress | 180 |\n| TDP | 74 |\n| CPI | 8 |\n| CPM | 6 |\n| BJP | 4 |\n| Majlis | 4 |\n\nIndependents and other candidates won the remaining seats, and Congress returned to power in the state.\n\nAfter the 1985 sweep, the TDP grew distant from influential classes. Government employees drifted away, trading communities became estranged over entry-tax issues, and backward classes who had placed hope in the TDP were left disappointed. Unemployment and rising education costs alienated the youth, and the party''s foundations in the districts began to crumble.\n\n\"Family rule\" became the excuse for many party pillars to crumble. The \"Okkokkare\" group — Nallapureddi Srinivasulu Reddy, Mudragada Padmanabham, K.E. Krishnamurthy, Janardhan Reddy, Vasantha Nageshwara Rao, Harirama Jogaiah, Penchalaiah, Ananda Gajapathi Raju, and Chintamohan — left the party. Their joining Congress benefited that side while the TDP lost out.\n\nIn a democracy, no party holds power permanently; changing fortunes by the people''s wishes is a parliamentary tradition, and these elections made this undeniably clear.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter33-post-1971-andhra-pradesh-political-history-p5", "partName": "Three Chief Ministers", "bodyMd": "### Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy (03-12-1989 to 07-12-1990)\n\nOnce Congress obtained a decisive majority, PCC president **Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy** became Chief Minister for the second time, taking oath in **December 1989**. This time, his rule began amid dissatisfaction, with two already-split camps making no effort to reconcile.\n\nRajasekhara Reddy''s faction and Janardhan Reddy''s faction were distinct dissatisfied groups within the cabinet. Rajasekhara Reddy''s confrontation with Janardhan Reddy was relentless, like a street fight with no pause — Rajasekhara Reddy declared he was not accustomed to fighting halfway and retreating.\n\nEven after returning from America, repeated trips to Delhi got Chenna Reddy no approval for reorganising his ministry, which only heightened dissatisfaction. Although the high command bore primary responsibility, only **Srinivasulu Reddy**, who had turned sharply against Chenna Reddy, escaped any loss of position — the Chief Minister lacked the will to treat him fairly even when he erred.\n\nSetting up unprincipled backward-class welfare schemes and reducing them to a joke provoked further anger against himself, and his dealings, limited to a loyal coterie, naturally hurt many colleagues. The high command''s own conduct — delaying corporation-chairman appointments and leadership decisions — contributed to his loss of prestige. Whenever he went to Delhi, however long he waited, he never got an interview with Rajiv Gandhi.\n\nAt the Centre, the V.P. Singh government collapsed after 11 months, failing to prove its majority on **7 November 1990**, and a Janata Dal (S) government formed under Chandrashekhar with Congress support.\n\nChief Minister Chenna Reddy failed severely in maintaining peace and order, especially in the capital. Communal riots in the first week of **December 1990** killed **71 people**, including 8 children — an unprecedented episode in Hyderabad''s history.\n\nCongress sent a five-member high-level delegation led by Balaram Jakhar to study conditions in the old city over three days. Rather than police or military handling the situation, the party delegation dealt directly with the Chief Minister, who had to follow its suggestions exactly.\n\nThe sudden communal riots, along with Minister Srinivasulu Reddy''s resignation threats, created a severe crisis within the party — replacing Chenna Reddy would have severely hurt both the party''s and Rajiv Gandhi''s prestige.\n\nDuring his tenure, Chenna Reddy scrapped many schemes introduced by the previous Telugu Desam government, dismantling and rebuilding systems NTR had scrapped. He was remembered for bringing World Bank loans to implement permanent schemes in cyclone-prone coastal districts.\n\nHis term ended amid celebrations, medical treatment, foreign travel, controversial statements and decisions, party dissatisfaction, refusal of high-command help, corruption scandals, Naxalite violence, and communal riots. Despite decades of political and administrative experience, his tenure ended this way. **Dr. Marri Chenna Reddy resigned as Chief Minister on 15 December 1990**.\n\n### N. Janardhan Reddy (17-12-1990 to 9-10-1992)\n\n**Nedurumalli Janardhan Reddy** became Chief Minister on **17 December 1990**, replacing Dr. Chenna Reddy. Since Dr. Bezawada Gopala Reddy became Chief Minister in 1955, leadership of the state had not returned to Nellore district until now, 35 years later.\n\nJanardhan Reddy had remained tied to Congress throughout his career despite humiliations: he was denied a ticket and humiliated in the **1985 elections**, appointed state Congress president in **1988** and removed within three or four months in favour of Chenna Reddy. His long loyalty to the party and the Indira Gandhi family finally resulted in his becoming Chief Minister — a decade of waiting that yielded good results.\n\nOn taking charge, his decisions to abolish regional boards, give police freedom on the Naxalite issue, and continue the mandal system pleased Congress party members. A change in the government office and school working-hours system also pleased people generally, and teachers and government employees did not oppose it.\n\nDifferences between PCC president V. Hanumantha Rao and the Chief Minister surfaced openly during candidate selection for the **1991 Lok Sabha elections**. Some Congress workers pushed the Chief Minister to appoint their preferred names to government posts, and Hanumantha Rao could not get him to act on this.\n\nHe resolved to reduce his differences with the Chief Minister through his closeness to Rajiv Gandhi.\n\n### Rajiv Gandhi''s Assassination\n\nOn the night of **21 May 1991**, Rajiv Gandhi was assassinated near Sriperumbudur, close to Madras, while going to speak at an election meeting. His assassination, occurring mid-election, created problems threatening the party''s very survival.\n\nIn the Lok Sabha elections held under these disturbed conditions, the Telugu Desam Party won the largest number of seats in the state, trying to reverse the 1989 result. But in constituencies voting after Rajiv Gandhi''s death, it could not withstand the sympathy wave — neither NTR''s campaign efforts nor his cinematic star power could overcome the sympathy vote.\n\n| Party | Seats |\n|---|---|\n| Congress | 24 |\n| TDP | 13 |\n| Majlis | 1 |\n| CPI | 1 |\n| CPM | 1 |\n| BJP | 1 |\n\n### P.V. as Prime Minister\n\nAfter Rajiv Gandhi''s assassination, **P.V. Narasimha Rao** was made Congress party president, then became Prime Minister following the elections. He was not a member of Parliament when chosen, and needed election within a fortnight.\n\nY.S. Rajasekhara Reddy had his own brother resign the Pulivendula seat so P.V. could contest and win it, avoiding any leverage Janardhan Reddy might otherwise gain by helping P.V. get elected. P.V., contesting a seat vacated for him, achieved a landslide victory.\n\nThe Chief Minister permitted 12 medical and 8 dental colleges in the state under the management quota, to collect capitation fees. The related Government Order, issued in **July 1992**, caused huge public outrage.\n\nStudent groups and political parties launched protests, and litigation proceeded in the High Court, which on **18 September 1992** struck down the G.O., observing that the permission had benefited relatives and associates of a \"Janapriya\" educational academy. Judges viewed this so severely that they denied the state government permission to appeal to the Supreme Court.\n\nEven before this, dissatisfied groups'' anger had been rising, and a high-command delegation from Delhi examined the state''s political conditions and reported to the Prime Minister. No further action was needed, since time had rendered the High Court''s decision moot.\n\nEven though N. Janardhan Reddy had hoped to serve a full term, one signature on the medical-college files brought his tenure down within just **21 months**.\n\n### Kotla Vijaya Bhaskara Reddy (second term)\n\n**Kotla Vijaya Bhaskara Reddy** took the oath as Andhra Pradesh''s fourteenth Chief Minister on **9 November 1992**, one of the most senior old-guard leaders aiming to build a new political edifice on moral, social, and economic reform. He earned women''s affection across the state for banning arrack (liquor).\n\nUnlike Chenna Reddy, he did not display autocratic tendencies, yet he could not earn a good reputation even among his own ministers and legislators. Corruption was less under his government than under Janardhan Reddy''s, and his political standing, though lower than Chenna Reddy''s or Janardhan Reddy''s, did not make him disliked.\n\nThe people simply showed no particular enthusiasm towards him, since old-style politics no longer attracted them. Despite his political experience, blemish-free character, and good governance, he kept failing to strengthen the party for elections.\n\nVijaya Bhaskara Reddy appeared to do everything properly and systematically, but that alone did not guarantee good outcomes. Banning arrack deprived the state of revenue, and the token tax imposed to compensate caused severe public backlash.\n\nRising token and liquor prices pushed all commodity prices up disproportionately, burdening the common man — this caused visible public resentment against his government. His cabinet''s large scale was not reduced either, and the discontent of those given generous ministerial posts across all sections was left largely unaddressed.\n\nKotla found it ironic that he had come to power a second time on NTR''s help. His distinguishing trait was self-confidence to the point of stubbornness; he prioritised urgent matters and believed competent administration alone was his guiding philosophy, a trait he was praised for.\n\nHis own history and long administrative experience kept public dissatisfaction less visible despite unruly ministers, and his own colleagues lacked the courage to turn against him. PCC president Majji Tulasidas could not control the party''s situation, and the Chief Minister made no attempt to fix it.\n\nFrequent leadership changes led to frequent changes in development programmes, and by the time Kotla accepted the post, the party''s state condition was already in severe decline. Yet in these two years he worked hard and enhanced the party''s prestige. Had there been no internal party conflicts, and had dissatisfied factions shown discipline, these two years could have been remembered as golden years for the state.\n\nAs Assembly elections approached, various Congress factions prepared rival candidates against each other, dividing votes, and the party high command''s efforts to resolve factional differences showed no result. The Chief Minister''s stated view up to the elections was that infighting should be kept in check, but Dr. Rajasekhara Reddy''s supporters carried out dissatisfaction activities blatantly, contributing most to the party''s pitiable state.\n\nRuling party and opposition are two wheels of the democratic chariot; if one is excessive and the other too weak, development becomes impossible — a problem the state now faced. Congress won only **25 seats**, owing to visible internal factionalism, with its vote share falling below 35 percent.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter33-post-1971-andhra-pradesh-political-history-p6", "partName": "The Fall of NTR", "bodyMd": "The Telugu Desam Party''s victory in **December 1994** proceeded like a triumphant march, in which many of Congress''s \"heavyweight\" candidates lost together. Both Communist parties, TDP''s allies, reaped seat-sharing rewards they had denied themselves for three decades. The TDP''s victory exceeded even its own leaders'' expectations.\n\n| Party | Seats |\n|---|---|\n| Congress | 26 |\n| Telugu Desam | 219 |\n| BJP | 3 |\n| CPI | 19 |\n| CPM | 15 |\n| Others | 10 |\n\n_(Total: 292 seats.)_\n\nCongress did not win a single seat in Adilabad, Karimnagar, Khammam, Mahbubnagar, Medak, Nalgonda, Rangareddy, Vizianagaram, and Visakhapatnam districts, winning only six seats across the whole of Telangana.\n\nBefore the elections, NTR fulfilled two promises: rice at two rupees a kilo, and total prohibition. Having clashed with the Congress government at the Centre before 1989, this time, as Chief Minister, he went to Delhi and asked P.V. Narasimha Rao to help build the Polavaram project and name it after him, trying to build cordial relations with, and secure assistance from, the Centre.\n\nIn **September 1993**, at a \"Major Chandrakanth\" centenary celebration in Tirupati, NTR announced on stage that he had married **Smt. Lakshmi Parvathi**, causing a great sensation — her entry into his life created a bigger stir than anything else.\n\nAfter the TDP''s landslide Assembly win, Lakshmi Parvathi, becoming a powerful figure, began playing her own role in the party. Those unwilling to accept her party control weakened within the party, and because of this **Chandrababu Naidu** took over the leadership.\n\nNTR had to pay a heavy price for failing to recognise the importance of people''s representatives in representative democracy. Party members who had campaigned tirelessly for months felt deeply hurt at being cast aside by him.\n\nHe continued to disregard, unhesitatingly, all opportunities available in politics, never giving MLAs their due respect — believing that if he snapped his fingers, they would come running wherever he was. Believing their own positions were at risk, MLAs coalesced around Chandrababu, forming a group and drawing closer to him.\n\nMixing up friendly words, unshakeable confidence that no one could confront him, and blind trust in his second wife together led to NTR''s great political downfall.\n\nOrdinarily, affection is weaker than power in politics. NTR''s own popularity did not fade, but the power flowing to Chandrababu, though initially less, carried more institutional weight than the appeal flowing to NTR. Well-disciplined MLAs, legislators, and workers who had trusted NTR alone lost trust in him because of just one woman.\n\nAt the TDP''s general body meeting on **30 August**, Chandrababu Naidu was unanimously elected party president, and after the resolution to remove NTR passed, Babu was announced as his replacement. **NTR resigned as Chief Minister on 30 August 1995.** With the slogan \"I myself am the Telugu Desam — Telugu Desam is me,\" the man who ruled the party as sole leader for 13 years was swept away from power politics.\n\nNTR''s permanent place in the hearts of the state''s people came not from politics but from cinema alone. He was an unmatched actor in the Telugu film industry, embodying virtues in every film — a widely known fact — and he portrayed villainy effortlessly on screen with ease.\n\nHe proved that same on-screen image could be changed into a clear-cut, straightforward Chief Minister, demonstrating this in 1983; but he was not an administrator, nor a political strategist. He won once. He lost once.\n\nAiming to become Chief Minister again, he promised rice at two rupees a kilo and declared prohibition to attract women — and won on these promises. These populist schemes cost the state up to **₹2,000 crore**, leaving the treasury empty.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter33-post-1971-andhra-pradesh-political-history-p7", "partName": "Chandrababu Naidu''s Second Term", "bodyMd": "### Friendship with the \"Lotus\" (BJP)\n\nIn the **1999 Assembly elections**, the Telugu Desam Party had to ally with the BJP. Of the state''s eight Lok Sabha seats and 25 Assembly seats, TDP ceded its own share to the BJP.\n\nChandrababu Naidu had been supporting the Vajpayee government on outside-support terms since 1998, and the CPI and CPM, who had backed the TDP from the start, drifted away over this, leaving Chandrababu no path but to ally with the BJP.\n\nThe BJP itself was not keen on being given many constituencies — doing so risked letting it later use that base to become powerful in the state, aiming for power down the road. To prevent this, Chandrababu allotted the BJP only weaker constituencies.\n\nCommunist parties realised that standing alone risked being wiped out in the Lok Sabha elections. Believing an alliance might not even guarantee the votes needed for national-party recognition, the CPI/CPM fielded candidates in 90 Assembly and 12 Lok Sabha seats to boost their vote count, using TDP leader Harikrishna as a foil to defeat Chandrababu Naidu.\n\nWhile the previous Assembly saw Congress win only 26 seats, in the new one it won **90 seats** — a confidence boost for that party. Nationally, the TDP emerged as the BJP''s biggest ally with **29 seats**.\n\nNTR''s would-be successors, party leaders Lakshmi Parvathi and Nandamuri Harikrishna, failed to get their candidates elected, but their failure was not the reason Congress lost — Congress, despite all its efforts, failed to come close to power on its own.\n\nNo one had estimated the TDP would win with such a huge majority. Combined with goodwill for the Vajpayee government, Chandrababu''s development programmes brought the TDP-BJP alliance **36 Lok Sabha seats** and **192 Assembly seats** — only slightly short of two-thirds in the Assembly. The Congress party was reduced to just **5 Lok Sabha seats**.\n\n### Harikrishna\n\nLegislator **Harikrishna**, who had joined Chandrababu against his own father in 1995, did not become a minister then, though he served as one for about six months later before growing estranged again, and did not get a ministry place when re-elected to the Assembly.\n\nHe began openly opposing party leader Chandrababu behind his back, and an indirect struggle developed between them. Harikrishna resigned his Assembly membership and founded the **\"Anna Telugu Desam\"** on **27 January 1999**.\n\n### Change in Governance\n\nChandrababu Naidu''s grand **1999** victory was important news not only for the state but for young India as a whole — he alone, at the time, dared to introduce populist schemes while also taking governance seriously.\n\nThe state had already fallen into significant debt by the time he took charge, since NTR''s populist schemes as father-in-law and predecessor had left it in severe financial ruin, and anyone continuing those policies would have benefited politically from them.\n\nPolitical leaders sheltering in such schemes — rice at two rupees a kilo, sarees at three rupees, free power, free housing, letting people roam Hyderabad''s streets without work — saw themselves as fortunate deities granting people''s wishes.\n\nChandrababu decided he had to overcome this situation, and even knowing this course would distance him from the people, he changed direction anyway.\n\nHe began telling people truthfully that appeasement schemes and outdated policies would not help, and that governance meant more than handouts, refusing to contribute to grand subsidy schemes like rice at two rupees a kilo. He made clear that good reasoning does not always guarantee political success, in the state or elsewhere.\n\nIn modernising government, he introduced computer methods, starting with land-record computerisation. A large share of court cases involved land disputes, and computerised records helped resolve them quickly and clearly, letting land ownership be checked with a mouse click from anywhere.\n\nHe launched Janmabhoomi programmes, and under the \"Ryotu Bazaar\" name, opened markets for farmers.\n\nHaving become one of the most influential figures in state politics after NTR, he had a personal vision to remain Chief Minister for another ten years. To achieve this, he introduced many schemes, going ahead of the World Bank to secure development funds, and undertook welfare schemes strictly within World Bank conditions rather than using irrigation and agriculture as tools of genuine economic empowerment.\n\nHe tried to please government officials with ad hoc daily allowances at short notice, implemented \"people''s rule\" and Janmabhoomi-type schemes, arranged gas connections for women affected by firewood-stove smoke, and extended loans to DWCRA groups.\n\nDespite all this, he lost the **2004 elections**. As soon as farmers felt left unattended, people began turning against him, and government employees, subjected to round-the-clock pressure of Janmabhoomi and constituent service without rest, grew increasingly alienated. As a result, he was defeated.\n\n### Relations with the Centre\n\nFrom 1995, Chandrababu Naidu began occupying a key role in national politics. In the \"hung\" Lok Sabha of **1996**, when the TDP had **16 Lok Sabha seats**, he played the leading role in shaping the United Front as an alternative to Congress and the BJP.\n\nThe BJP, as the largest party, formed a government under Vajpayee, which lasted only **13 days** before resigning for lack of majority. The United Front government then came to power with Congress support, with Chandrababu Naidu pulling the strings, and the Telugu Desam Party shared in that government''s power.\n\nIn the **1998 elections**, again no party won a majority. TDP won **12 Lok Sabha seats** and played a pivotal role; Chandrababu Naidu played a leading national role in forming the NDA coalition with the BJP, and from then shone in national politics.\n\nThe TDP accepted the Lok Sabha Speaker''s post but declared support from outside the government, and when ally DMK withdrew support, the NDA government fell within **13 months**.\n\nIn the **1999 elections**, the TDP again contested with the NDA and swept **29 Lok Sabha seats** in the state, again agreeing to support the government from outside and accepting only the Speaker''s post. That post went to Balayogi, who could not continue owing to an accident-related death.\n\nBJP leaders respected Chandrababu Naidu for behaving as a reliable ally, and whatever the state needed from the Centre, he obtained without delay. Despite the alliance''s frictions with other parties, only the TDP created no problems for the BJP, giving Chandrababu more prominence than other leaders.\n\nWhile United Front governments were in power, Chandrababu took his role as the front''s chief strategist seriously, spending an inordinate amount of time flying between Delhi and the state at the Chief Minister''s own cost to maintain his grip in Delhi. Ramakrishna Hegde, NTR, and Devi Lal had earlier carried the main responsibility of guiding national politics from the states; this role now passed to Chandrababu.\n\nWhile in power, he elevated information technology to the neglect of other sectors. In truth, his rule brought more harm than good to the state''s people — his policies suited countries with small populations, not a country with a large one, least of all his own state.\n\nIn healthcare, for example, thousands of jobs in developing government institutions were handed to VRS (voluntary retirement) and outsourced under World Bank terms, under the pretext of increasing efficiency, citing West Bengal CM Jyoti Basu''s record as precedent.\n\nTelugu people grew disillusioned with Chandrababu''s ambitions. With overweening self-confidence, believing no one else had his political-strategy skill, he underestimated Congress''s resurgence, trusting no fellow leader within the party and relying only on officials'' statistics and reports.\n\nWithout recognising ground-level realities, he formed opinions about every matter in advance, never changed his position regardless of circumstances, and hence could not build a group of leaders loyal to him beyond himself — nor did he allow leaders with grassroots support within the party to rise above a certain level.\n\nUsing some populist scheme or other, even in unfavourable circumstances, became Chandrababu''s habitual tactic; whether these schemes brought genuine benefit or reached the people, or merely served his publicity, was known to him alone. Whenever one scheme ended, he would announce a new one — this was his pattern, focused more on presenting a polished image and administrative reputation than on genuine implementation.\n\nFor example, one week after a successful public programme, in **October 1995**, he abruptly ended it and began the \"Shramadaan\" (voluntary labour) scheme, personally picking up a broom to sweep streets. Fearing his new schemes would lose their announcement-worthy shine, he would suddenly inspect government offices and hospitals, often without the officials responsible for those departments present.\n\nHe conducted several public-awareness tours, holding meetings at village, mandal, revenue-division, and town levels, explaining how far his government had resolved people''s problems — and the public accepted these new schemes with sudden interest each time, following this same pattern.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter34-movement-state-formation', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter34-movement-state-formation-p1", "partName": "Formation and Rise of the Telangana Rashtra Samithi", "bodyMd": "The separate-statehood demand, dormant for decades, resurfaced when the UPA coalition at the Centre decided to form three new states in the country. **Kalvakuntla Chandrashekar Rao (KCR)** resigned from the post of Deputy Speaker of the Assembly and from the primary membership of the Telugu Desam Party, and founded the **Telangana Rashtra Samithi (TRS)**.\n\nHe argued that, for decades in \"Vishalandhra,\" Telangana had been kept deliberately undeveloped. The settled Andhra population in the region, he said, had no genuine sympathy for Telangana — those who came to the region were interested only in electoral alliances, not in the region''s welfare.\n\nWith the formation of TRS, the separate-Telangana demand became a central plank in the region''s politics. However, realising he lacked the strength to achieve the goal alone, KCR allied with the Congress ahead of the 2004 elections; the CPI too was a willing supporter of a separate Telangana.\n\n### 2004 Elections and the Congress Alliance\n\n- **2004 Lok Sabha and Assembly elections:** TRS won **5 Lok Sabha seats** and **26 Assembly seats**, becoming part of Congress-led governments both at Delhi and in Hyderabad.\n- KCR and his senior colleague **A. Narendra** took up posts at the Centre; KCR''s nephew **T. Harish Rao**, along with six others, became members of Y.S. Rajasekhara Reddy''s state cabinet.\n- Of the 52 seats Congress contested in the Telangana region in the 2004 Assembly elections, it won 48; TRS won only 26. The Telugu Desam Party won only 11 seats in the region and secured just one Lok Sabha seat.\n\nCongress calculated that an alliance with TRS would prevent Chandrababu Naidu from returning to power a third time under a new political banner, so it extended support to the separate-Telangana sentiment mainly as an anti-TDP strategy, not purely on the merits of the demand.\n\n### Karimnagar By-election and TRS''s Standing\n\nState Sports Minister M. Satyanarayana Rao''s resignation triggered a Karimnagar Lok Sabha by-election.\n\n- Satyanarayana Rao had earlier won from Karimnagar with a majority of **1,56,328 votes** — the biggest record until then.\n- KCR won the by-election with a majority of **2,01,582 votes**, a spectacular victory.\n- This was the first time a regional party (TRS) had taken on the major state parties directly in a big electoral contest, proving that a regional outfit could stand on its own.\n- BJP had promised to introduce a bill in Parliament for separate statehood, but its candidate **C.H. Vidyasagar Rao** lost his deposit in humiliating fashion and blamed TRS for splitting his vote.\n\n### Resignations and the 2008 By-elections\n\nWhen the central coalition government failed to move on Telangana statehood, TRS members resigned in protest from **18 Assembly seats** and **4 Lok Sabha seats** in the state. In the by-elections held in **May 2008**, TRS won back most of the seats it had vacated, delivering unexpected and striking results at a critical juncture of the movement — KCR himself won with a majority of **15,765 votes**.\n\n- Alarmed by these results, Congress worked to dilute the Telangana sentiment and prevent its high command from being swayed.\n- In Nizamabad district, Congress managed to wrest two seats that had been firmly with TRS.\n- Distressed by this partial setback, KCR briefly resigned from the party presidency but was persuaded by senior leaders and workers to withdraw the resignation.\n- Despite the mixed by-election outcome, TRS drew national attention and managed to check defections of its leaders to Congress.\n\n### The 2009 Fast and Its Aftermath\n\nAfter suffering heavy political losses in the **2009 general elections**, KCR remained silent for a period while working internally to keep the movement alive. On **29 November 2009**, he began an **11-day fast** demanding Telangana statehood. Through this fast he drew distant Telangana supporters and movement organisations closer to him and built renewed pressure on the Centre.\n\nIn the by-elections for 8 seats held in **March 2012**, TRS won four of the five seats it contested in Telangana, including Adilabad, Kollapur, and Kamareddy — constituencies where TDP and Congress had traditionally prevailed and where TRS won for the first time. Over its first twelve years, TRS — built and steered single-handedly by KCR, founded in 2001 — overcame repeated setbacks through his political strategy, resilience after election losses, and his ability to connect with Telangana''s public through a native idiom of speech.\n\n---", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter34-movement-state-formation-p2", "partName": "Political Instability After Y.S. Rajasekhara Reddy (2009–2014)", "bodyMd": "### Y.S. Rajasekhara Reddy''s Second Term\n\n| Party | Seats (2004 Assembly) |\n|---|---|\n| Congress | 183 |\n| CPI | 9 |\n| CPM | 6 |\n| TRS | 26 |\n| TDP | 48 |\n| BJP | 2 |\n| Others | 20 |\n\n**Dr. Y.S. Rajasekhara Reddy (Y.S.R.)** became Chief Minister after this verdict.\n\nAhead of the 2009 elections, TDP chief and then-CM Chandrababu Naidu tried to consolidate coastal-district votes behind a \"Samaikyandhra\" (united Andhra Pradesh) plank, dissolving the Assembly in **November 2003** for snap elections — a gamble that ended with TDP relegated to the bottom of the contest.\n\nY.S.R.''s hold on the Chief Minister''s post was not simply handed down by the high command; it rested on genuine, overwhelming public support. He faced no real internal dissent and no threat of removal by the party leadership, and his government''s achievements were widely seen as his own rather than the party''s.\n\nAhead of the **2009 elections**, Chandrababu Naidu left no opportunity unexplored — including an unprecedented alliance with TRS, despite being long-standing political rivals of KCR — and even reversed the TDP''s stance to declare no objection to a separate Telangana. In this contest TRS won **10 seats**, and Congress returned to power.\n\n**Y.S.R.''s death:** After being re-elected, Rajasekhara Reddy did not remain in power for long — he died in a helicopter crash in the Nallamala forest area on **2 September 2009** while on tour.\n\n### K. Rosaiah''s Tenure\n\nAfter Y.S.R.''s death, Sonia Gandhi appointed the veteran **K. Rosaiah** as Chief Minister, expecting his experience to help navigate the state''s politically sensitive issues. The process of the Congress legislature party electing him was delayed by about three months.\n\nRosaiah — who had earlier won an MLC seat unexpectedly just before the elections — took charge just as the Telangana controversy flared up again, costing him his composure from the outset.\n\n- It would be inaccurate to say Rosaiah \"adjusted\" to the crisis; he largely endured it, often deflecting with remarks such as \"What is in my hands?\" or \"I am just an instrument.\"\n- From the moment **Y.S. Jaganmohan Reddy** (Y.S.R.''s son) took his oath as MLA, demands that he be made Chief Minister created difficulties for Rosaiah, with many ministers and MLAs openly campaigning for Jagan instead of supporting the government.\n- **KCR''s arrest:** As KCR left Karimnagar for Warangal to begin a fast-unto-death, he was intercepted near the Karimnagar district border and taken into custody before he could even begin the fast — an event described as possibly the first of its kind in recent history, where a person was forcibly arrested and jailed before starting an announced fast. His arrest triggered intense unrest across Hyderabad and other parts of Telangana.\n- KCR''s fast-unto-death shook the region; regardless of appeals, he refused to end it. The escalating situation forced the Congress high command to summon Rosaiah to Delhi. KCR ended the fast once the high command agreed to take a decision on Telangana, bringing Rosaiah temporary relief.\n- The subsequent launch of the **Samaikyandhra (united Andhra) movement** created a fresh crisis: MLAs and local-body representatives from Andhra and Rayalaseema began resigning in protest against separating Telangana, turning governance into a largely symbolic exercise.\n- With ministers and legislators divided along regional lines, any decision favouring one side angered the other, leaving Rosaiah unable to focus on governance.\n- Rosaiah''s roughly **14-month tenure** (14 months, 22 days) is remembered as a period defined by external crises rather than his own conduct — a contrast to his earlier, smoother tenure as Finance Minister. After stepping down as CM, he was appointed Governor, a post often regarded as a dignified retirement stage in political life; he has since lived in Chennai away from controversy.\n\nUnion Home Minister P. Chidambaram''s announcement on the night of **9 December 2009** that the process of forming a separate Telangana state would begin immediately jolted the Congress party in Andhra Pradesh, prompting the party to try to walk back the commitment.\n\n### Congress in Decline: N. Kiran Kumar Reddy''s Tenure\n\n**N. Kiran Kumar Reddy** took oath as the **16th Chief Minister of Andhra Pradesh** on **24 November 2010**. Revenue Minister **Botsa Satyanarayana** was made PCC president.\n\nWith the Chief Minister, Assembly Speaker, Legislative Council Chairman, and the new PCC president all drawn largely from Rayalaseema, the high command''s underlying strategy for these key appointments was unclear. Following Y.S.R.''s untimely death — after he had twice built Congress''s electoral fortunes, in 2004 and 2009 — his son Jaganmohan Reddy launched the YSR Congress Party.\n\n- The high command hoped that a young Kiran Kumar Reddy as CM would stabilise the party''s position, but within a short time concluded that his performance fell short of expectations, and dissatisfaction grew within the leadership.\n- To salvage the party''s image, PCC responsibilities were handed to Botsa Satyanarayana, who nonetheless considered himself still in contention for the CM''s post — sparking a rift between the two that the high command could never fully resolve.\n- Kiran Kumar Reddy could not build a loyal faction within the party; even senior cabinet colleagues who supported him did not enjoy his full trust, and key party leaders were largely opposed to him. He had to simultaneously manage the highly sensitive Telangana issue — opposed even within his own party — and the challenge posed by Jagan''s rise.\n- Despite widespread doubts about his position, Kiran Kumar Reddy showed striking self-confidence, believing no danger could touch his post. Within Congress''s political culture, a leader''s survival as CM depended more on the high command''s backing in Delhi than on MLA numbers — the high command traditionally favoured weaker leaders precisely because a leader who built independent strength within the party could become a leader the high command itself might fear crossing.\n- After resigning as CM, Kiran Kumar Reddy raised the \"Jai Samaikyandhra\" banner, but this found no support within Congress itself — having held every post the party could offer, the public was unwilling to forgive him for now condemning it. He remained the **last Chief Minister of undivided Andhra Pradesh**; three months after President''s Rule was imposed, the state was formally divided into Andhra Pradesh and Telangana.\n\n---", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter34-movement-state-formation-p3", "partName": "Formation of the New Telangana State (2014)", "bodyMd": "### Telangana''s Resources and Potential\n\nAmong India''s larger states, Telangana is not small — in geographical extent it exceeds states such as West Bengal and Kerala. With rich culture, natural resources, and river waters, the region is positioned for further growth.\n\nGiven its hardworking population alongside abundant natural resources — **water, land, and minerals** — planned development could make Telangana the country''s leading state: its river waters can supply irrigation and drinking water, while mineral deposits offer power-generation opportunities unmatched by other southern states.\n\n- The **Krishna** and **Godavari** rivers flow roughly parallel, west to east, across the state, carrying abundant flow for about six months of the year.\n- Other rivers include the **Tungabhadra, Manjeera, Praanahita, Sabari, Suddavagu, Peddavagu (Penganga)**, and **Paleru**.\n- Except for Hyderabad, in the remaining nine districts of Telangana about **90 percent** of the land is cultivable.\n\nTelangana''s freedom struggle was also marked by a flourishing poetic tradition — poetry recitations at library anniversaries, school functions, festivals, and political gatherings — with the region''s first Central Sahitya Akademi award in Telugu going to **Suravaram Pratapareddy**.\n\n### The TRS Victory and Formation of the Government\n\nHaving begun as a movement party under KCR''s leadership on the platform of separate statehood, TRS achieved its stated goal — not merely fulfilling a long-cherished popular aspiration, but also rising to the level of holding state power. Alongside the core Telangana sentiment, the party''s campaign on welfare programmes and development schemes helped it draw voters, while KCR skilfully channelled the region''s sense of neglect and its people''s emotional investment in statehood into a successful movement.\n\nThe Telangana Assembly and Lok Sabha elections were held together on **30 April 2014**, recording **78% statewide polling** — the highest in Nizamabad district and the lowest in Hyderabad. Telangana''s voters gave TRS an overwhelming majority.\n\n**2014 Election Results (Telangana region):**\n\n| Party | Assembly (MLA) Seats | Lok Sabha (MP) Seats |\n|---|---|---|\n| TRS | 63 | 11 |\n| Congress | 21 | 2 |\n| TDP | 15 | 1 |\n| MIM | 7 | 1 |\n| BJP | 5 | 1 |\n| YSR Congress | 3 | 1 |\n| Others | 5 | 0 |\n| **Total** | **119** | **17** |\n\nOn **17 May 2014**, KCR was elected leader of the TRS legislature party. On the morning of **2 June 2014**, Chief Justice **Kalyan Jyoti Sengupta** administered the oath of office to **E.S.L. Narsimhan** as Telangana''s first Governor; Narsimhan then administered the oath to party chief **K. Chandrashekar Rao** as the new state''s **first Chief Minister**, along with 11 other ministers.\n\n### First Cabinet of Telangana\n\n| Minister | Portfolio(s) |\n|---|---|\n| Kalvakuntla Chandrashekar Rao (Chief Minister) | General Administration, Electricity, Municipal Administration, Urban Development, SC, ST, BC and Minority Welfare, and other departments not allotted to other ministers |\n| M.D. Mahmood Ali (Deputy CM) | Revenue, Stamps, Registrations |\n| Dr. Thatikonda Rajaiah (Deputy CM) | Medical and Health |\n| Nayini Narsimha Reddy | Home, Jails, Fire Services, Ex-servicemen Welfare, Labour, Employment |\n| Etela Rajender | Finance, Planning, Small Savings Promotion, Civil Supplies |\n| Pocharam Srinivas Reddy | Agriculture and allied departments |\n| Thanneeru Harish Rao | Major Irrigation, Legislative Affairs, Marketing |\n| Kalvakuntla Taraka Rama Rao | Panchayati Raj, Information Technology |\n| P. Mahender Reddy | Transport |\n| Jogu Ramanna | Forests, Environment |\n| T. Jagadish Reddy | Education |\n| T. Padma Rao | Excise, Prohibition |\n\n### Cabinet After Reorganisation\n\n| Minister | Portfolio(s) |\n|---|---|\n| Kalvakuntla Chandrashekar Rao (Chief Minister) | SC and Minority Welfare, Municipal Administration, Urban Development, Electricity, General Administration, and other unallotted departments |\n| Mahmood Ali (Deputy CM) | Revenue, Rehabilitation, Urban Land Ceiling, Stamps and Registration |\n| Kadiyam Srihari (Deputy CM) | Education |\n| T. Harish Rao | Irrigation, Mines, Groundwater, Marketing, Legislative Affairs |\n| Kalvakuntla Taraka Rama Rao | Panchayati Raj, Information Technology, NRI Affairs, Employment |\n| Jupally Krishna Rao | Handlooms and Textiles, Sericulture, Sugar |\n| Nayini Narsimha Reddy | Home, Jails, Fire Services, Ex-servicemen Welfare, Labour, Employment |\n| P. Mahender Reddy | Transport |\n| Pocharam Srinivas Reddy | Agriculture, Horticulture, Sericulture, Animal Husbandry, Fisheries, Food Processing Industries Development Corporation, Seeds Corporation |\n| G. Jagadish Reddy | Electricity/Power |\n| Dr. C. Laxma Reddy | Medical and Health |\n| A. Indrakaran Reddy | Housing, Judiciary, Endowments |\n| Etela Rajender | Small Savings, State Lotteries, Excise Transactions, Legal Metrology, Finance |\n| Jogu Ramanna | Forests, Environment, BC Welfare |\n| T. Padma Rao | Excise, Prohibition, Sports, Youth Welfare |\n| Thummala Nageswara Rao | Roads, Transport, Women and Child Welfare |\n| Talasani Srinivas Yadav | Commercial Taxes, Cinematography |\n| Ajmeera Chandulal | Tribal Welfare, Tourism, Cultural Affairs |\n\nA cabinet extension on **16 December 2014** brought in six new ministers, including A. Indrakaran Reddy, C. Laxma Reddy, Talasani Srinivas Yadav, Thummala Nageswara Rao, Jupally Krishna Rao, and Ajmeera Chandulal — the appointments reflected in the reorganised cabinet table above. On **25 January 2017**, **Dr. Thatikonda Rajaiah** was removed as Deputy Chief Minister over corruption allegations; **Kadiyam Srihari** was appointed Deputy CM and Education Minister in his place, **G. Jagadish Reddy** was shifted from Education to Electricity, and **C. Laxma Reddy** was given the Medical and Health portfolio.\n\n---", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter34-movement-state-formation-p4", "partName": "Intensification of the Movement: TJAC and Forms of Protest", "bodyMd": "Following the Centre''s announcement on **9 December 2009** that the process of Telangana formation would begin, Seemandhra political leaders resigned en masse in protest, creating political and constitutional turmoil. Under this pressure, the Centre issued a second statement on **23 December 2009**, saying state formation would proceed \"only after consultations and consensus.\"\n\nTo transform the Telangana aspiration into a mass movement and unite the community''s various public unions and political parties, the **Telangana Political Joint Action Committee (TJAC)** was formed on **24 December 2009**.\n\nTJAC organised a wide range of protest programmes to sustain pressure on the Centre and keep the movement''s aspirations alive: the non-cooperation movement, the million march, Sakala Janula Samme, Sagaraharam, Samsad Yatra, and Chalo Assembly. Alongside these, the region''s cultural life — Bathukamma, Bonalu processions, and folk performance — was woven into peaceful protest through forms such as rasta roko (road blockades), hunger strikes, vanta-varpu (community cooking on roads), dhoom-dham (song-and-dance protest), manavaharam (human chains), funeral processions, candle rallies, and effigy burning.\n\n### Non-Cooperation Movement (17 February – 4 March 2011)\n\nThe non-cooperation movement — inspired by Gandhi''s non-cooperation movement against British rule — aimed to make the government helpless by having employees withhold cooperation and duties. It was TJAC''s **first major agitation programme**, demanding immediate introduction of a Telangana bill in Parliament, and lasted **16 days**.\n\n**Lead-up events:**\n\n| Date (Feb 2011) | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 13th | Dappu Chatimpu and tying of Kankanalu (yellow threads) |\n| 14th | Rallies held in constituencies |\n| 15th | \"Jail Bharo\" programme |\n| 16th | Dharna at houses of Congress and TDP MPs/MLAs |\n| 17th | Movement launched; ~3 lakh employees joined; Telangana MLAs disrupted the Governor''s Assembly address |\n\nThrough the following days, employees boycotted duty, withheld taxes, travelled without tickets, blockaded national highways, and held rallies. Key events included picketing of Central government offices (18th), \"Walk for Telangana\" and \"Prabathabheri\" protests with a blockade of NH-9 (19th), and \"Chalo Assembly\" and \"Chalo Raj Bhavan\" calls by student and lawyer JACs (21st).\n\nA 48-hour bandh followed across ten Telangana districts (22nd), Lok Sabha members chanted \"Jai Telangana\" and the House was adjourned three times (23rd), and a \"Rail Roko\" titled \"Palle Palle Pattala Paiki\" blocked North–South rail traffic (1 March). The movement ended on **4 March 2011** after TJAC and trade union leaders reached an agreement with state ministers.\n\n**Key points of the 4 March 2011 agreement:**\n1. A commission headed by a retired judge to oversee implementation of Presidential orders.\n2. The state government to take JAC leaders to Delhi to press for removal of paragraph 14(F) of the Presidential orders.\n3. A monitoring committee for implementation of Presidential orders, chaired by the Council of Ministers.\n4. A policy to ensure adequate representation for Telangana-district employees in state-level offices.\n5. Preference to local candidates in pending recruitments.\n\n### Million March (10 March 2011)\n\nThe Million March, held at Tank Bund, Hyderabad, was inspired by the Tahrir Square protests in Cairo, though condensed into a single day.\n\n- On **26 February 2011**, KCR — as chief guest at an employees'' hunger strike near the Inter Educational Directorate, Nampally — announced the million march without first consulting TJAC; public unions and the JAC nonetheless extended support.\n- Police denied permission and imposed **Section 144** in Hyderabad; JAC convenor **Prof. Kodandaram** and **Nayini Narsimha Reddy**, along with over a thousand activists, were arrested in advance to disrupt the programme.\n- Despite barricades and heavy policing, large numbers reached Tank Bund; Congress MP **Madhu Yashki Goud** and senior leader **K. Keshava Rao** were attacked by the crowd for not having resigned their posts.\n\n**Destruction of statues at Tank Bund:**\n- In 1986, then-CM **N.T. Rama Rao** had installed 33 statues of eminent Telugu personalities on Tank Bund; only 7 represented Telangana — Rudrama Devi, Pothana, Abul Hasan Tanisha, Ramadasu (Kancharla Gopanna), Mahbub Ali Khan, Makhdoom Mohiuddin, and Suravaram Pratap Reddy (an 8th, Komaram Bheem, was added later on **5 October 2012**).\n- When police did not respond to demands for the release of arrested activists, outraged protestors destroyed **16 statues** of predominantly Andhra figures, believing Telangana''s own history had been sidelined; some statues were thrown into Hussain Sagar, and 15 media personnel covering the incident were injured.\n- KCR, Prof. Kodandaram, Harish Rao, and KTR were among those booked under serious sections, including attempt to murder.\n- A cabinet sub-committee headed by **Vatti Vasanth Kumar** was formed to decide on re-installing the destroyed statues, but Telangana cultural organisations warned against installing any new statue on Tank Bund until statehood was achieved.\n\n### Sakala Janula Samme (13 September – 24 October 2011)\n\nSakala Janula Samme (\"strike of all the people\") was the movement''s most comprehensive mobilisation, spanning **42 days** and drawing in teachers, students, and public and private trade unions across Telangana.\n\n- On **15 July 2011**, TJAC-led trade unions issued a strike notice to CM Kiran Kumar Reddy, warning of a strike if statehood was not achieved by 1 August 2011; the government did not respond.\n- The strike, originally planned from 6 September, was postponed to **13 September** on account of Ganesh festival; a **Janagarjana Sabha** at Karimnagar on 12 September drew roughly 10 lakh people and confirmed the strike date. **Singareni coal workers** began the strike from midnight of 12/13 September.\n- The government invoked **G.O. No. 177** (salary deduction for absent employees) and threatened \"No Work – No Pay\"; KCR promised employees back pay with interest, plus a month''s bonus, once statehood was achieved.\n- The strike revealed that **80% of secretariat employees** were from the Seemandhra region, reinforcing Telangana''s sense of exclusion from job appointments.\n- **RTC workers** (about 67,000) joined from 19 September, blocking national highways; clashes with police at Nizam College and Osmania University left students **Kishore Goud** and **Shiva Goud** wounded by rubber bullets.\n- On **1 October 2011**, Congress MLA and Minister **Komati Reddy Venkat Reddy** resigned in protest; he began a fast-unto-death on 1 November 2011 (withdrawn on 9 November after police intervention).\n- Strikes wound down in stages through October: RTC workers ended their 27-day strike (16th), the Teachers'' Action Committee called off its strike (17th), Singareni workers ended their 35-day strike (18th), and finally **TNGO** called off the strike on **24 October 2011**, ending Sakala Janula Samme.\n\n**Key points of the agreement ending the strike:**\n1. Reaffirmed the 4 March 2011 commitment on a retired-judge commission for Presidential order implementation.\n2. Withdrawal of criminal cases against employees without direct involvement.\n3. Government to consult the Advocate General on cancelling G.O. No. 177.\n4. Sympathetic review of deputations and transfers made during the strike.\n5. Review of ESMA notifications issued during the strike in certain departments.\n6. Consideration of treating the strike period as paid or other leave.\n7. Re-hiring of outstanding contract employees.\n8. A special advance to be paid to employees despite \"No Work – No Pay,\" in view of festival-season hardship.\n\n### Sagaraharam / Telangana March (30 September 2012)\n\nAnnounced by JAC chairman Prof. Kodandaram on 7 July 2012 as \"Chalo Hyderabad\" under the slogan \"Intiko Manishi, Chethilo Janda,\" the march was renamed **Sagaraharam** on the suggestion of Mallepalli Laxmaiah. The government initially refused permission, citing potential damage to state assets and clashing events (Ganesh immersion, a UN biodiversity conference), before permitting the march from Sanjeevaiah Park to Necklace Road, 3 pm–7 pm.\n\nOn the day, all trains through the region were cancelled and Osmania University was sealed, yet nearly **2 lakh activists** reached Necklace Road. The crowd stayed past the government deadline, leading police to use tear gas, before the march was called off around 11:30 pm due to rain.\n\n### Chalo Assembly (14 June 2013) and Samsad Yatra – Chalo Delhi (April 2013)\n\n- For **Samsad Yatra**, nearly 2,000 activists travelled to Delhi by a special train named \"Telangana Express\" (27 April 2013); a **Satyagraha Deeksha** began at Jantar Mantar on 29 April 2013, drawing support from national leaders including CPI''s Suravaram Sudhakar Reddy and BJP''s Prakash Javadekar, Sushma Swaraj, Uma Bharati, and Smriti Irani. Telangana Congress MPs staged a 48-hour sit-in near the Mahatma Gandhi statue in Parliament.\n- For **Chalo Assembly** (14 June 2013), the government denied permission and pre-emptively arrested activists and raided university hostels; the Assembly session was cancelled that day, but thousands of activists still gathered, and TRS MLAs **Dasyam Vinay Bhaskar** and **Vidya Sagar Rao** climbed onto the Assembly building to protest with black flags.\n\n### Other Forms of Protest\n\n- **Vanta-Varpu** (\"community cooking\"): first held on **3 February 2010** (the day the Sri Krishna Committee was appointed), with community cooking blocking the Hyderabad–Karimnagar and Hyderabad–Warangal roads; repeated on 19 June 2011 as \"Rajadhanilo Vanta, Delhilo Manta\" across 150 centres in Hyderabad.\n- **Manavaharam (Human Chain):** on **4 February 2010**, a human chain of **500 kilometres** across six districts (Adilabad to Alampur, via Mahbubnagar, Nizamabad, Medak, Ranga Reddy, Hyderabad) was formed — the longest human chain in the country''s history; a second chain followed on 5 February 2010 along NH-9 between Hyderabad and Kodad.\n- **TJAC Bus Yatra:** conducted in two phases — Gun Park (Hyderabad) to Hanumakonda (21–23 March 2010) and Komuravelli to Mancherial (9–12 April 2010).\n- **Palle Palle Pattala Paiki** (\"villages onto the railway tracks\"): a Rail Roko programme on 1 March 2011.\n- **TJAC Singareni Yatra** (9 November 2011): three teams under Prof. Kodandaram, Mallepalli Lakshmaiah, and Pittala Ravinder covered the Khammam, Karimnagar, and Adilabad coal-belt areas; the first two teams were arrested en route, while Pittala Ravinder''s team reached Kothagudem.\n- **Samara Deeksha** (27–28 January 2013): a 36-hour protest at Indira Park, where Prof. Kodandaram gave the slogan \"Congress ko Khatam Karo – Telangana Hasil Karo.\"\n- **Sadak Bandh** (21 March 2013): a road blockade on NH-44 between Shamshabad and Alampur; non-bailable cases were registered against Prof. Kodandaram and TRS MLAs Etela Rajender and Jupally Krishna Rao.\n\n### Hunger Strikes for Separate Telangana\n\n| Leader | Duration |\n|---|---|\n| Ravindranath | 8–22 January 1969 |\n| Potu Krishnamurthy | 10–23 January 1969 |\n| Konda Laxman | 5–8 April 1969 |\n| Ravindra Babu | 20 July – 1 August 1969 |\n| Mallikarjun | 10 October – 3 November 1969 |\n| Ramadevi | 18–24 October 1969 |\n| Vishnuvardhan, Jaleel | 21–26 October 1969 |\n| Konda Laxman | 1–12 November 1969 |\n| KCR | 29 November – 9 December 2009 |\n| Konda Laxman | 1–7 November 2011 |\n| Komati Reddy Venkat Reddy | 1–9 November 2011 |\n| Nalini (former DSP) | 9–16 December 2011 |\n\n---", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter34-movement-state-formation-p5", "partName": "Parliamentary Process for the Formation of Telangana State", "bodyMd": "### Article 3 of the Constitution\n\n**Article 3** governs the formation of new states and the alteration of existing state boundaries or names. Under it, Parliament holds absolute power over the formation and modification of states, while the affected state legislatures have only an **advisory role**:\n\n- A new state may be formed by separating territory from an existing state, by merging two or more states or territories, or by merging any territory with part of a state.\n- Parliament may increase or decrease a state''s area, alter its boundaries, or alter its name.\n- The Union Cabinet forwards the bill to the President, who sends it to the concerned state legislature for its opinion within a set (extendable) time period — in the Andhra Pradesh case, the deadline was extended.\n- The state legislature may approve or disapprove the bill, but Parliament is **not bound** to accept its views (the Andhra Pradesh legislature in fact rejected the bill, which nonetheless proceeded).\n- A **simple majority** — not a two-thirds special majority — is sufficient to pass a state-partition bill in Parliament, and even the debate quorum requires only 10% of members.\n\n### Key Case Law on State Partition\n\n- **Babulal Parate vs. State of Bombay (1959):** The Supreme Court held that once a draft bill has been sent to the state legislature and its views received, the Centre may make key changes before introducing the bill in Parliament without needing to again consult the legislature; Parliament is not bound by the state legislature''s views. The Court held that sovereignty rests with the people of India, constituted by the union of states.\n- **Mangal Singh vs. Union of India (1987):** Parliament has the power to create new states or alter existing ones, provided this does not violate the spirit of the Constitution.\n- **Mullaperiyar & Environmental Protection Forum vs. Union of India (2006):** Parliament''s power in matters of state division under Articles 3 and 4 is supreme and absolute.\n- **Pradeep Chaudhary vs. Union of India (2009):** Parliament is not bound to accept the state legislature''s views — \"consultation\" means seeking an opinion, not obtaining assent; Parliament''s supreme authority in this regard cannot be challenged under Article 246 or the Seventh Schedule. This judgment is treated as the standard reference on state-partition procedure, and its reasoning was earlier upheld in the **Berubari case** (via *V.B. Raju vs. State of Gujarat*, 1980).\n\n### The UPA Government''s Shifting Stance\n\nFollowing KCR''s fast-unto-death, Union Home Minister Chidambaram announced on **9 December 2009** that Telangana formation would begin, but Seemandhra resignations forced a reversal on **23 December 2009**. An all-party meeting called by the Home Ministry on **5 January 2010** failed to satisfy any side, intensifying the movement.\n\nThis led the Centre to appoint the **Sri Krishna Committee** on **3 February 2010** \"citing the need for detailed consultation.\" The Committee submitted its report on **30 December 2010**, but the Centre did not act on it for over two years, prompting TJAC''s escalating protest calendar (non-cooperation, million march, Sakala Janula Samme).\n\n**Committees and turning points, 2012–2013:**\n\n- **All-Party Meeting (28 December 2012):** Chaired by Home Minister Sushil Kumar Shinde in Delhi, with two representatives each (one from Telangana, one from Andhra/Rayalaseema) from Congress, TRS, TDP, BJP, YSRCP, CPI, CPM, and MIM; CM Kiran Kumar Reddy attended as special invitee. Shinde promised a solution within a month, though it was delayed — this meeting is regarded as the most significant of all such meetings, since the CWC''s later decision built directly on it.\n- **CWC Resolution (30 July 2013):** After senior Congress leader K. Keshava Rao and two other MPs defected to TRS, and a BJP public meeting in Hyderabad (3 June 2013) signalled political shifts, the Congress Working Committee met under Sonia Gandhi, PM Manmohan Singh, and other senior leaders. It decided to form Telangana with **10 districts including Hyderabad**, with Hyderabad as joint capital for 10 years, national status for the Polavaram project, and a new Andhra Pradesh capital to be built within a decade.\n- **Antony Committee (6 August 2013):** Formed under **A.K. Antony** (with Digvijay Singh, Veerappa Moily, and Ahmed Patel) to coordinate the partition process; also called the \"Partition Committee.\" Its report (4 October 2013) recommended **GHMC** (rather than the wider HMDA) as the joint-capital boundary, equal educational opportunities for Seemandhra students in Hyderabad for 10 years, and national-project status with rehabilitation measures for Polavaram.\n- **Group of Ministers – GoM (8 October 2013):** Chaired by A.K. Antony, with Sushil Kumar Shinde, Chidambaram, Veerappa Moily, Jairam Ramesh, and Ghulam Nabi Azad as members, given six weeks to resolve post-partition issues. Party positions before the GoM diverged sharply. **TRS** wanted no administrative control over Telangana and joint-capital status limited to five years; **TDP** boycotted the process, calling it unilateral. **AIMIM** opposed any Central rule over Hyderabad and sought an anti-communal-violence bill; **YSRCP** favoured a united Andhra Pradesh. **CPM** favoured continued linguistic-state unity, while **BJP** sought a national-parties meeting before Parliament approval. A demand for a separate **\"Rayala Telangana\"** by Rayalaseema leaders J.C. Divakar Reddy and T.G. Venkatesh was ultimately dropped after TRS-led protests (4–5 December 2013).\n\n### Passage of the Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Bill\n\n| Date | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 5 December 2013 | GoM finalised its report; Union Cabinet approved the Telangana Draft Bill 2013 |\n| 11 December 2013 | President Pranab Mukherjee sent the bill to the State Assembly, allowing 45 days (later extended by a week) for its opinion |\n| 16 December 2013 | Bill introduced in the Assembly and Legislative Council by Speaker Nadendla Manohar; Seemandhra MLAs tore up copies in protest |\n| 30 January 2014 | Assembly passed a Rule 77 resolution asking the President not to introduce the bill; the bill was returned to the Centre as rejected (9,072 amendments had been proposed; 87 members spoke) |\n| 7 February 2014 | Union Cabinet approved the bill despite the Assembly''s rejection (Parliament not bound to comply, per the Home Ministry) |\n| 13 February 2014 | \"Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Bill, 2013\" introduced in the Lok Sabha by Sushil Kumar Shinde; MP Lagadapati Rajgopal used pepper spray during the debate, causing the House to adjourn; 16 MPs (14 Seemandhra, 2 Telangana) were suspended |\n| 18 February 2014 | Lok Sabha passed the bill by voice vote |\n| 19 February 2014 | CM Kiran Kumar Reddy resigned as CM, MLA, and from Congress; bill introduced in the Rajya Sabha |\n| 20 February 2014 | Rajya Sabha passed the bill by voice vote after PM Manmohan Singh offered Andhra Pradesh six guarantees, including special category status for five years and central completion of the Polavaram project |\n| 1 March 2014 | President''s assent given; President''s Rule imposed on united Andhra Pradesh |\n| 2 March 2014 | Bill notified as the **Andhra Pradesh Re-organisation Act, 2014** in the Gazette of India (a 71-page Act) |\n| 4 March 2014 | **2 June 2014** declared as Telangana''s \"Appointed Day\" |\n| 2 June 2014 | Telangana became India''s **29th state** |\n\nSeparately, six Congress MPs from Seemandhra (Lagadapati Rajgopal, Sabbam Hari, Undavalli Arun Kumar, Rayapati Sambasiva Rao, Sai Pratap, and Harsha Kumar) moved a no-confidence motion against the Centre and were suspended from the party on 11 February 2014. On 1 March 2014, before the Act took effect, the Cabinet decided to merge **seven Polavaram-affected mandals** of Khammam district into Andhra Pradesh — an amendment formalised by ordinance and approved by the President on 28 May 2014.\n\nThe government also set up **14 department-wise committees** (covering Finance, Irrigation, Planning, Roads and Buildings, Energy, Industries, Medical and Health, Revenue, and General Administration, among others) on 1 March 2014 to oversee the partition, alongside a high-level committee headed by a Special Chief Secretary and a State Reorganization Cell in the Secretariat.\n\n### The Andhra Pradesh Re-organisation Act, 2014\n\nThe Act comprises **12 parts, 108 sections, and 13 schedules**. Key provisions include:\n\n- **Section 3:** Formed the State of Telangana from ten districts — Adilabad, Nizamabad, Karimnagar, Medak, Warangal, Nalgonda, Ranga Reddy, Hyderabad, Khammam, and Mahbubnagar (later modified by the Polavaram-related ordinance transferring parts of Khammam to Andhra Pradesh).\n- **Section 5:** Made Hyderabad the **common capital** of both states for a period not exceeding 10 years.\n- **Section 6:** Provided for an expert committee to recommend a new capital for Andhra Pradesh within six months.\n- **Sections 7–9:** The existing Governor to serve both states; the Governor responsible for security of life and property in the common capital, assisted by two Central-government-appointed advisors; Central assistance for additional police forces.\n- **Section 12–14:** Allocated **7 Rajya Sabha seats to Telangana and 11 to Andhra Pradesh**; allocated **17 Lok Sabha seats** to Telangana.\n- **Section 17:** Fixed Assembly strength at **119 seats for Telangana and 175 for Andhra Pradesh** (later increased under Section 26 to 153 and 225 respectively, subject to delimitation).\n- **Section 18:** Provided for one nominated Anglo-Indian member each in both Assemblies.\n- **Section 22:** Provided Legislative Councils of up to 40 members (Telangana) and 50 members (Andhra Pradesh).\n- **Section 30–33:** The Hyderabad High Court to remain the common High Court until a separate Andhra Pradesh High Court is constituted.\n- **Section 46–67:** Governed distribution of revenue, assets, liabilities, debts, pensions, and provident funds between the successor states, generally on a population-ratio basis.\n- **Section 84:** Provided for a **Godavari River Management Board** (Telangana) and a **Krishna River Management Board** (Andhra Pradesh), supervised by a Central **Apex Council** chaired by the Union Water Resources Minister.\n- **Section 90:** Declared the **Polavaram Irrigation Project** a national project, with the Centre responsible for construction.\n- **Section 95:** Continued the Article 371D local-quota protections for higher education in both states for **10 years**.\n- **Schedule 13:** Listed Central development measures for 10 years, including a tribal university and a horticultural university in Telangana, a 4,000 MW NTPC power facility, and a rail coach factory.\n\n---", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter34-movement-state-formation-p6", "partName": "Notable \"Firsts\" of Telangana State", "bodyMd": "| Position | First Holder |\n|---|---|\n| Chief Minister | Kalvakuntla Chandrashekar Rao |\n| Governor | E.S.L. Narsimhan |\n| Speaker, Legislative Assembly | E. Madhusudhana Chary |\n| Deputy Speaker | Padma Devender Reddy |\n| Chairman, Legislative Council | K. Swamy Goud |\n| Deputy Chairman, Legislative Council | Nethi Vidya Sagar |\n| Leader of Opposition, Assembly | K. Jana Reddy (Congress) |\n| Pro-tem Speaker | K. Jana Reddy (Congress) |\n| Anglo-Indian nominated MLA | Stephenson |\n| Leader of Opposition, Legislative Council | D. Srinivas |\n| Advocate General | K. Rama Krishna Reddy |\n| Chief Secretary | Rajeev Sharma |\n| Director General of Police | Anurag Sharma |\n| Chief Information Commissioner | Prabhakar Reddy |\n| Chief Justice, High Court | T.B. Radhakrishnan |\n| Chairman, Public Service Commission | Ganta Chakrapani |\n| Chairman, Women''s Commission | Sunitha Lakshma Reddy |\n| Chairman, SC/ST Commission | Errolla Srinivas |\n| Chairman, Minority Commission | Mohammad Kamruddin |\n| Mayor, GHMC | Bonthu Rammohan |\n| Deputy Mayor, GHMC | Baba Fasiuddin |\n| Ambassador (from Telangana) | Sania Mirza |", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p1", "partName": "Overview", "bodyMd": "The Vishnukundis (Vishnukundins) hold a primary place among the important royal dynasties of the Andhra-Telangana region, not merely for spreading its name and fame but for introducing new practices in religion, language, and culture. Starting as feudatories in the Krishna-Godavari region, they eventually extended their empire up to the Narmada river and, for a time, ruled the territory between the three seas — the Arabian Sea, the Bay of Bengal, and the Indian Ocean. For nearly 210 years, between 358 CE and 569 CE, they ruled primarily over Telangana and northern Andhra lying north of the Krishna river.\n\nThey were originally feudatories of the Ikshvakus, and their kings called themselves “Amarpuriswaras” or “Lords of Amarapuram.” Their political stint began on the northern bank of the Krishna river, in the districts of present-day Nalgonda and Mahabubnagar in Telangana. The second king, Madhava Varma, married a Vakataka princess and thereby secured his kingdom. Seventeen Vishnukund kings ruled Andhra Pradesh for about 260 years; the lion springing forward (a pouncing lion) was the royal emblem of the dynasty.\n\nThe first few Vishnukund rulers were Buddhists; later rulers adopted the Vedic (Brahmanical) faith. During their time, Sanskrit and spoken Telugu were both in use as administrative/literary languages. In Andhra political history, it is their cultural history — even more than their political history — that stands out as their most glorious legacy. Politically, they established a strong independent kingdom in Andhra for roughly a century and a half; on one side they gained prominence through marriage alliance with the powerful Vakataka emperors, and on the other, through warfare, they subdued the Kalinga chieftains. Their reign also holds a distinct place in the religious history of Andhra.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p2", "partName": "Capitals", "bodyMd": "The Vishnukunds had their capitals at **Amarapuram, Indrapalanagaram, and Dendaluru**.\n\n*   **Amarapuram** is identified with Amrabad, the present-day mandal headquarters in Mahabubnagar district.\n*   **Indrapalanagaram**, also called **Sukrapuram**, is located on the outskirts of Tummalagudem village in Valigonda mandal of Nalgonda district.\n*   **Dendluru** is located near Pedavegi in West Godavari district.\n*   There is also some indication that a place between Bezawada (Vijayawada) in Andhra Pradesh may have served as a Vishnukund capital for some time.\n*   Another capital, known as **Puranisangamam**, could be either Sanganigundala in Amrabad mandal or Sangameswaram near Alampuram; the ruins of an erstwhile town lie on the outskirts of Medimankal village near Sanganigundala.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p3", "partName": "Sources for Vishnukund History", "bodyMd": "The main sources for the study of Vishnukund history are **inscriptions**. The dynasty''s history is known not only from **13 epigraphs issued during their own period**, but also from **8 further inscriptions issued in the later period** — 21 inscriptions in all, of which **16 are copper plate inscriptions and 5 are stone inscriptions**.\n\n**Inscriptions issued during the Vishnukund period:**\n\n| Name of the Inscription | Region |\n|---|---|\n| Tummalagudem Copper plates (2) | Valigonda Mandal, Nalgonda district |\n| Chaitanyapuri stone inscription | Hyderabad, Rangareddy district |\n| Keesaragutta stone inscription | Amrabad Mandal, Mahabubnagar district |\n| Saleswaram stone inscription | Sattenapalli, Guntur district |\n| Velpuru Stone inscription | Tenali, Guntur district |\n| Ipur Copper plate inscriptions (2) | Visakhapatnam district |\n| Ramatirtham Copper plate inscription | Tuni, East Godavari district |\n| Chikkulla Copper plate inscription | Tuni, East Godavari district |\n| Tundi Copper plate inscription | Ramachandrapuram, East Godavari district |\n| Polamuru Copper plate inscription | Satara district, Maharashtra |\n| Khanapur Copper plate inscription | Satara district, Maharashtra |\n\nBesides inscriptions, the **coins** issued by the Vishnukunds, fort walls, cities, and cave temples they built, and objects, tools, and sculptures unearthed in excavations all aid in reconstructing their history. Writings of the period, such as **Janasraya Chhando Vichchitti** and **Sethubandha**, also help in understanding the historical developments of those times.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p4", "partName": "Political Developments", "bodyMd": "The decline of the Ikshvakus and the subsequent Pallava expansion paved the way for the rise of Vishnukund independence.\n\n*   Till **355 CE**, the Ikshvakus ruled districts in the middle of Andhra Pradesh, with Vijayapuri (Nagarjunakonda) as capital, lying on both sides of the Krishna river.\n*   The Ikshvakus were defeated by the **Pallavas of Kanchi**, who brought the Krishna river region up to Srisailam under their control.\n*   In **358 CE**, the Gupta king **Samudragupta**, ruling from Pataliputra, defeated the Pallavas in his victorious southern expedition and returned north.\n*   Taking advantage of this, the **Anandagotrins (Kandara kings)** — earlier feudatories of the Ikshvakus in the Krishna-Guntur region — and the **Salankayanas** in the Vengi region (Eluru) declared independence. In Telangana, the **Vishnukundins** became independent.\n\nFrom then, the Vishnukunds ruled the Telangana region for some time (first half of the 5th century CE) and the Maharashtra area for nearly 200 years. \n\n**Vishnukund Rulers (widely accepted chronology):**\n\n| No. | Ruler | Reign |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Indra Varma | 358 – 370 CE |\n| 2 | Madhava Varma I | 370 – 398 CE |\n| 3 | Govinda Varma | 398 – 440 CE |\n| 4 | Madhava Varma II | 440 – 495 CE |\n| 5 | Deva Varma | 495 – 496 CE |\n| 6 | Madhava Varma III | 496 – 510 CE |\n| 7 | Vikramendra Varma I | 510 – 525 CE |\n| 8 | Indrabhattaraka Varma II | 525 – 555 CE |\n| 9 | Vikramendra Varma II | 555 – 569 CE |\n\nMadhava Varma II is noted to have \"taken Vakataka\" — i.e. absorbed Vakataka territory — from Prithivishena. **Manchanna Bhattaraka** is also said to have ruled for some time around 570 CE in the Amrabad region.\n\nThe first two kings began their political career in south Telangana, in the Mahabubnagar and Nalgonda districts, which made it easier for later kings to expand the territory.\n\n### Govinda Varma (398–440 CE)\n\n*   **Expansion:** Expanded the Vishnukund kingdom on both sides of the Sriparvata (Srisailam–Nagarjunakonda) region through his physical prowess; occupied territory up to the Gundlakamma river by defeating the Pallavas.\n*   **Alliance:** Married the daughter of a powerful local chief, Mularaja of Gunapasapuram in coastal Andhra. With his help, Govinda Varma defeated the **Salankayanas of Vengi** and annexed their kingdom.\n*   **Northern gains:** Areas on the other side of the Musi river also came under his kingdom.\n*   **Evidence:** A stone inscription of his time was found at Chaitanyapuri in Hyderabad; Vishnukund fort and temple remains survive at Keesaragutta on the outskirts of Hyderabad.\n*   The **Kesari vagu** (stream) below Keesaragutta gave the surrounding area the name **Gattukesari**, now called Ghatkesar — \"Kesari\" means lion, the royal Vishnukund emblem. A royal seal bearing this emblem was recently found at Keesaragutta.\n\n### Madhava Varma II (440–495 CE) — the greatest of the Vishnukund kings\n\n**Military career**\n\n*   Fought and won more than **100 battles**; as a mark of victory in each battle, he installed a Sivalinga at Keesaragutta.\n*   Built a **Ramalingeswaraswamy temple** at every place he won a battle — these temples at Velpuru (Sattenapalli taluk, Guntur district), Ipur, Indrapalanagaram, and Keesaragutta still receive worship today.\n*   With the help of his relative **Prabhakara**, ruler of Gunapasapuram, occupied Kalinga (northern coastal Andhra and south Odisha).\n*   In his **33rd regnal year**, attacked the Pallavas again and expanded the kingdom up to the Gundlakamma river.\n*   In the west, defeated **Prithvisena**, the last ruler of the main Vakataka line (460–480 CE), and married his daughter — a marriage that secured Vakataka territory for the Vishnukunds.\n\n**Extent of empire:** With these victories, Madhava Varma II''s empire extended from the Bay of Bengal in the east to the Arabian Sea in the west, and from the southern ocean (perhaps Pulicat Lake) in the south to the Reva (Narmada river) in the north. Many feudatories submitted to him; one of his inscriptions was found in Maharashtra, and Vishnukund coins have been found across Maharashtra.\n\n**Epithet and capital shift:** He bore the epithet _''**trivara nagara bhavnagata sundari hridaya nandana**''_ (\"the one who brought happiness to the beautiful maidens living in the buildings of the city of Trivara\"). The identification of Trivaranagaram remains debated — among the possibilities suggested are Tripuri/Tevar near Jabalpur on the Narmada, and a capital in the South Kosala or northern Kalinga region — but it is clear his kingdom extended to the Narmada and Mahanadi rivers in the north.\n\nBecause the Vishnukunds and Pallavas were perpetually at war, Madhava Varma II shifted his capital to **Denduluru**, near Vengi, to better guard against Pallava invasions. He also strengthened the walls of **Amarapuri**, his original capital, and appointed his eldest son, **Deva Varma**, as royal representative there.\n\n**Sacrifices:** To announce his victories, Madhava Varma II performed **11 Ashvamedha sacrifices, 16 Rajasuya sacrifices**, and a thousand other sacrifices such as Vajapeya and Purushamedha. It is said he authored a book on metre (Chandas) titled _**Janasraya Chandovichhitti**_, beginning with the title \"Janasraya.\" \n\n### Vikramendra Varma I (510–525 CE)\n\n*   Succeeded his father Madhava Varma II.\n*   Known as _''**Vishnukundi Vakataka Vamsadwayalankara Janma**''_ — one born of both the Vishnukund and Vakataka lines, since his father was Madhava Varma II and his mother Vakatadevi was of the Vakataka family.\n*   Held the title **''Mahakavi''** (great poet).\n*   Taking advantage of Vikramendra Varma''s literary preoccupations, **Madhava Varma III**, son of Deva Varma, declared independence at Amarapura. This Madhava Varma completely exterminated the **Kandara (Anandagotrika) dynasty** and assumed their title, _''Trikuta Malayadhipa''_.\n*   Vikramendra Varma — whether driven from the Vakataka throne or arriving from his capital Denduluru — suppressed Madhava Varma III and ruled the Vishnukund kingdom for about **15 years (510–525 CE)**.\n\n### Indra Varma II (525–555 CE)\n\n*   Succeeded Vikramendra Varma I; was attacked by Madhava Varma III with the help of the Chalukyan king **Pulakesi** (the title _''Ranavikrama''_ of Pulakesi found at Yeleswaram supports this). Because of this association, the Chalukyas — like the Vishnukunds — began using titles containing the word _''asraya''_.\n*   Defeated Madhava Varma II''s other relatives (Palivandlu) and may have merged the Amarapuri kingdom into his own territory, called _''Rajakshetram''_.\n*   **Kalinga alliance:** The Kalinga feudatories formed an alliance against Indra Varma II in the northeast; Indra Varma II emerged victorious and saved his kingdom.\n*   **Setback:** The Maukhari king **Esana Varma** of North India claimed to have conquered the Salika and Andhra kings, indicating that in his southern campaign Esana Varma defeated Indra Varma II. To restore friendly relations, Indra Varma II gave his daughter **Indrabhattarikadevi** in marriage to **Sarva Varma**, son of Esana Varma, and maintained friendship with them thereafter.\n\n### Vikramendra Bhattaraka Varma (555–569 CE)\n\n*   After Indra Varma II''s death, his minor son Vikramendra Bhattaraka Varma was placed on the throne by the council of ministers. His epithet _''**Sakala Bhuvana Rakshabharanaikasraya**''_ suggests the vast extent of his kingdom.\n*   In his **11th regnal year**, the Pallava king **Simhavarama** attacked the Vishnukund kingdom; the battle took place around **566 CE** near Dhanyakataka in Guntur district. Vikramendra Varma II won this battle with the help of **Prithivimula**, and as a mark of the victory made donations to the _Vihara_ of Paramabhattarika Devi at Indrapalanagaram.\n*   At the same time, **Prithvimaharaja** declared independence at **Pishtapuram**; the districts of Godavari, Khammam, and Nalgonda were then under his kingdom.\n*   Vikramendra Varma II later attacked Prithvimaharaja after gathering his army, but **died in this battle**. With his death, the Vishnukund dynasty disappeared within a short span.\n\n### Decline\n\n*   With Vikramendra Varma II''s death, **Prithvimula** also became independent and ruled the Godavari district areas.\n*   From the decline of the Vishnukund kingdom (**569–590 CE**) for about half a century, up to **617 CE**, **Prithvimaharaja** ruled south-eastern Andhra and **Prithvimularaja** ruled the Guntur-Krishna and Godavari districts.\n*   Both were defeated in **617 CE** by **Pulakesi II**, the Badami Chalukya ruler, who thereby extended his kingdom into the Telangana and Andhra areas.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p5", "partName": "Administrative System", "bodyMd": "*   Though the Vishnukunds acquired their kingdom through their own prowess and strength, their inscriptions state that they gained the authority to rule by serving at the feet of **Sriparvataswamy**, and they carved the word \"Sriparvata\" on their coins — reflecting a stated humility and loyalty.\n*   They earned fame for proper and popular rule: monks, orphans, beggars, the diseased, and the distressed could earn a living justly under them. They extended protection to Brahmins, giving them lands, villages, and posts, and donated servants, cots, chariots, travel equipment, food, water, buildings, jewellery — and girls — to establish name and fame among the poor.\n*   **Extent of rule:** After the Satavahanas, the Vishnukunds are credited as the next dynasty to rule an extensive empire in the Deccan. For nearly 50 years, Maharashtra, Telangana, and Andhra Pradesh were under their rule, though Telangana and coastal Andhra north of the Krishna remained under them for a longer period.\n*   **Titles:** Because of ruling such a vast empire, they assumed the title **''Maharaja''**; because they protected all types of people they also adopted titles such as _''Sakala Bhuvanaikasraya, Janasraya, Satyasraya, Uttamasraya,_ and _Vikramasraya''_. This set an example for later dynasties — the later Chalukyas wore _''asraya''_ titles, and the Kakatiyas referred to Madhava Varma as their dynastic founder; the kings of Pithapuram and the Velamas made the same claim.\n*   **Legal system — ''Divyalu'':** Madhava Varma is said to have discovered the legal duties known as _''**Divyalu''**_. When a prince''s chariot accidentally killed the son of a tamarind-selling woman, Madhava Varma imposed the death sentence on the prince. Tradition holds that Malliswaraswamy, pleased by this justice, made gold rain at Bezawada and restored life to both the prince and the boy — a story also found in later literary works such as _Nachiketopakhyanam, Prabhodha Chandrodayam,_ and _Panchatantram_.\n*   **Territorial divisions:** The kingdom was divided into Provinces (**Rashtra**), Provinces into **Vishayas**, and Vishayas into villages. A Rashtra was governed by a **Rashtrika**. Known Rashtras include Paraki Rashtra, Plekki Rashtra, Karma Rashtra, and Kalinga Rashtra. A **Vishaydhipati** was the governing head of a Vishaya; known Vishayas include Grudhranadi Vishaya and Netrapati (Natavadi) Vishaya.\n*   The practice of appointing the king''s son as **''Yuvaraja''** for part of the kingdom existed. Some feudatory states remained subordinate to the Vishnukund kingdom, and matrimonial relations were formed with some of them, who in turn helped protect and extend the kingdom.\n*   The king was assisted by the **Yuvaraja, Mahamatya, Amatya**, an Intelligence Officer, a Secret Adviser, and other officials.\n*   **Forts:** Vishnukunds built several forts, often named for meanings similar to Amarapuram, their first capital — e.g. Indrapalanagarm (Ramannapet taluk, Nalgonda district), Indrakeeladri at Bezwada, and Indur in Nizamabad. To guard their capital from Pallava invasion, they built the **biggest fort in the Andhra-Telangana region** in the Amrabad area, whose wall stretched around **200 kilometres**; construction of this wall was continuous, giving rise to a local saying comparing any long-running house-wall construction to \"the construction of Patalabhadruni fort.\"", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p6", "partName": "Economic Development", "bodyMd": "*   Villages of the Vishnukund period had a **self-sufficient economy**, with farmers and other occupational groups depending on one another.\n*   Besides agraharas, Brahmins were also donated farmers, and servants and maid servants, to cultivate land; Brahmins with knowledge of the seasons developed agriculture with the help of these servants, bringing new lands under cultivation.\n*   Inscriptions record that the Vishnukunds dug many wells and tanks for agriculture, through which wet crops were cultivated. Land was measured in terms of **nivartanas**.\n*   **Domestic trade:** Vishnukund coins are found at Yeleswaram, Bhuvanagiri, and Sultanabad (Karimnagar district) in Telangana; at Bojjannakonda and Elamanchili (Visakhapatnam district) in Andhra Pradesh; and at Nasik, Khanapur, Nagpur in Maharashtra, and Brahmagiri in Madhya Pradesh — indicating a network of internal trade.\n*   **Foreign trade** grew after the occupation of Vengi from the Salankayanas and of the Kalinga kingdom. More Vishnukund coins found in coastal Andhra, ship marks on the coins, and Madhava Varma II''s title _''trisamudradhipati''_ (\"lord of three seas\") all point to the importance of foreign trade.\n*   Foreign trade was carried on from eastern coastal ports such as **Motupalli and Kodur** with eastern countries — Burma, Siam, Cambodia, China, Japan, Ceylon, Sumatra, Java, Borneo, Malaya, Arakan — and western countries — Egypt, Rome, Greece.\n*   Vadapalli, Bezawada, Amaravati, Motupalli, Ghantasala, Koduru, and Alampuram-Kudalisangamam on the banks of the Krishna mainly carried on international trade, occasionally taking up foreign trade as well.\n*   In excavations at **Kudali Sangameswaram near Alampur**, a **gold coin of the Roman Emperor Constantine (306–337 CE)** was discovered along with other coins, showing that the Krishna-Tungabhadra doab was a centre for foreign trade even before the Vishnukund period, continuing through it.\n*   The Chinese traveller **Fa-Hien** recorded that shells were also used as currency in the Vishnukund kingdom in the early 5th century CE. Another Chinese traveller, **Huien-Tsang**, wrote of full-size golden Buddha statues at Sriparvata (not found), though copper Buddha statues (4th–5th century CE) measuring one to five inches have been found at Buddham and Amaravati, indicating skilled carpentry/craftsmanship. A cast-iron snuff box shaped like a mango bud, measuring 7 cm, found at Keesaragutta, is further testimony to this craftsmanship.\n*   Inscriptions state that sufficient wealth existed for monks, Brahmins, orphans, beggars, the diseased, and the poor to lead a justly-earned life — indicating a strong economy for the period.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p7", "partName": "Social Conditions", "bodyMd": "*   Vishnukund inscriptions state that \"they adhered to the _varnasramadharma_, which is associated with giving gifts and donations.\" **Govinda Varma** in particular is said to have respected varnasramadharma, and the Vishnukunds tried to ensure that all four varnas — brahmana, kshatriya, vaisya, and sudra — followed their professional duties, though the first two castes seem to have held greater respect.\n*   The importance of the brahmana and kshatriya castes is reflected in the title **''parama brahmanya''** borne by some Vishnukund rulers, and in their assumption of \"brahma-kshatra\" brilliance through performing the **hiranyagarbha** sacrifice. This indicates that the Vishnukunds, who had originally been Sudras, rose in status through performing many sacrifices, and gave many agraharas to Brahmins while also honouring higher-caste members with official posts.\n*   **Vaisyas** were engaged in internal and external trade. Among the Sudras, **carpenters, sculptors, and handloom weavers** held a rightful place.\n*   **Kanyadanas** (offering girls in marriage) made the families of both bride and groom happy; the Vishnukunds also donated items needed for daily life — sleeping, sitting, drinking, eating — and buildings and jewellery. That Madhava Varma is said to have always served at the feet of his parents suggests that reverence for parents and elders was valued in the society of the time.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p8", "partName": "Cultural Development", "bodyMd": "The Vishnukunds were followers of the **Vedic religion**, with **Sriparvataswamy** as their family deity; their names reflect Vedic affiliation, and titles such as **''Paramamaheswara''** and **''Parama Brahmanya''** indicate devotion to Siva and to Brahmanical religion.\n\n*   Before the Vishnukunds became independent rulers, the Allahabad inscription issued by Samudragupta used the phrase _''Paramabhattaraka Padanudjyatasya''_, which closely resembles _''Sriparvataswami Padanudhyatasya''_ in the Vishnukund inscriptions.\n*   The name **''Paramabhattarikadevi''**, wife of Govinda Varma (the first-generation Vishnukund king), also resembles the name of a Gupta goddess. Tradition connects Sriparvataswamy/Srisaila Mallikarjuna Swamy with **Chandravati (Sandravati)**, said to be the daughter or sister of King Chandragupta. The ruins of the Guptamaheswara temple, said to have been installed by her, stand even now to the north of the Umamaheswara temple and below the Prataparudra fort.\n*   It appears that after Madhava Varma II married Vakatakadevi, the Vishnukunds did not patronize religions other than Vedic-Brahmana religion — the Vakatakas being relatives of the Guptas. On the whole, the influence of the Guptas and Vakatakas led the Vishnukunds to become sanskritised followers of Vedic-Brahmana religion.\n\n### Vedic-Puranic Religion\n\n*   Though the Vishnukunds patronized Buddhism (very popular in the 4th–5th centuries CE), it appears they made greater donations to Brahmins, remaining in reality followers of Vedic and Puranic religion. All their names relate to the Vedic and Vaishnava faith.\n*   **Indrabhattaraka Varma** and his son **Vikramendra Varma** assumed the title **''Parama Maheswara''**. The Vishnukunds acknowledged that they gained authority to rule through _''Sriparvataswamy Padanudhyanam''_ (worshipping at the feet of Sriparvataswamy), and carved the word \"Sriparvata\" on their coins.\n*   At Sriparvata _padam_ (foot), in Amrabad mandal, is the (Uma)Maheswara temple, which became famous as the northern gateway of Srisaila Devasthanam; **Srisaila Mallikarjuna** also became a popular name during Vishnukund times.\n*   In his **33rd regnal year (489 CE)**, Madhava Varma II ordered the installation of a war-drum idol and of **Dantamukhaswamy (Ganapati)**; a Venkaiah (Ganapati) idol outside Amrabad village, along with the Venkaiah _bavi_ (well) and the ruins of a Siva-Parvati temple, and Ganeswara temple in the nearby hills, are connected to this. The war-drum is now kept in the Umamaheswara temple compound.\n*   Inscriptions record that **Govinda Varma constructed several ''divyatanas''** (shrines). Vishnukund temples were located in their early capital in Amrabad mandal, in the valleys of the Nallamala forests, under waterfalls, and in caves.\n*   The **Anandagotris (Kandara kings)**, contemporaries of the Vishnukunds who ruled areas east of the kingdom, worshipped ''Sambhu'' at Vanakeswaram (in Amrabad mandal).\n*   **Akkanna-Madanna caves and temples**, though popularly attributed to the historical figures Akkanna and Madanna (17th century CE), were in reality built by **Madhava Varma II** — including the Akkanna-Madanna caves in the Kanaka Durga group of temples on Indrakeeladri at Bezwada, the temples at Keesaragutta, and the Maheswara temple on the outskirts of Hyderabad. Because of similarities in promoting Hinduism, the name of Madhava Varma appears to have been mistakenly attributed to Akkanna-Madanna.\n*   Madhava Varma II built many **Ramalingeswara temples**, one for each battle won — at Velpuru (Sattenapalli taluk, Guntur district), Ipur, and Keesaragutta, still worshipped today. To the southeast of the Umamaheswara temple at Amrabad, footprints of Rama are found; to its north is the **Pallaveswara temple**, built by the Pallavas — who attacked the Vishnukunds two or three times — as a mark of their own victory. As the Vishnukunds were Vaishnavas, their Sivalingas are called **Ramalingas**; the same practice of dedicating temples to victories was later followed by the Eastern Chalukya king **Vijayaditya II**, who built 108 Bhimeswara temples to mark his 108 victories.\n*   Madhava Varma II was described as _''**Snanapunyodaka pavitrikrita sirshah**''_ (\"the one whose head was purified by the sacred waters\"), showing the growing importance of pilgrimage and ritual bathing during his time.\n*   **Vikramendra Varma** donated Regonta village as _devabhoga_ to the Triyambakanatha temple (God Somagiriswaranatha) in Netrapati vishaya. The Vishnukunds donated villages, lands, old chariots, horses, cows, cots, arts, water, and food along with these grants.\n*   Madhava Varma II performed numerous sacrifices and rituals — _Ekadasaswamedha, Avabritha, Avodhauta, Jagatkalmasha, Agnistoma, Bahuswarna, Purushamedha, Vajpeya Shodarajasuya, Prajapatyaneka Paundarika Sata Sahasrayojino_. The practice of donating thousands of servants and girls, and of sacrificing men in the yajnas, reflects prevailing social inequalities of the time. The **avabritha bathing ghat** at Munnanoor fort remains a memory of these sacrifices.\n\n### Buddhism\n\nBy the first phase of Vishnukund rule, Buddhism had spread widely in society; early Vishnukund rulers, while themselves Vedic followers, patronized Buddhism to respect majority opinion.\n\n*   **Govinda Varma**, in his 37th regnal year, on the full moon day of Vaisakha month, donated resources for light, sandal flowers, dhwaja water, food, sleep, _arama_, _grasa_, and medicine to the _mahavihara_ built by his queen **Paramabhattarika** at Indrapalanagara, addressed to **Dasabalabali**, well-versed in the 18 branches of Buddhist _Dharma_, for the 14th Arya Sangha at Penkaparra village. He also donated the village of Enmadala to the same sangha.\n*   **Vikramendra Varma** donated Irrundero village in 566 CE for the enjoyment of monk sanghas at the same vihara.\n*   Govinda Varma''s uncle **Prithivimula** built a _mahavihara_ named Tokhalikhata at the capital Gunapasapuram (East Godavari district). His son **Harivarma** built another _mahavihara_ at Gunapasapuram, donating Kattacheruvu village to it, and also built a **Sekya bhikshu vihara** near Tatikonda.\n*   Inscriptions indicate that Vishnukund kings likely built or extended Buddhist _arama-viharas_ on all sides of their kingdom. Surviving proof includes the **Govindaraja vihara** at Chaitanyapuri (Hyderabad); Buddhist ruins at Keesaragutta and Kondapuram; _arama_, _stupa_, _viharas_ at **Phanigiri** and Gajulabanda in Nalgonda district; and _stupas, aramas_ at Nelakondapalli in Khammam district.\n*   At **Phanigiri**, excavations in 2011 revealed six _aramas_, each with several rooms. Buddhist sites also existed at Jaggayyapeta, Salihundam, Ramatirtham, Thotlakonda, Bojjanakonda, Buddham, and elsewhere in Andhra Pradesh, though most predate the Vishnukunds — the dynasty is credited with major extension activity at these sites during its period. **Saleswara vihara**, in Amrabad mandal, Mahabubnagar district, built originally during Ikshvaku times, shows brick structures and Brahmi-script inscriptions of the Ikshvaku period alongside later Telugu-Kannada script inscriptions.\n*   The **Hinayana** sect was present at Chaitanyapuri, Hyderabad, where a Prakrit inscription in six rows records that **Bhadanta Sanghadeva** built a rock house for those offering sandal and clothes to the Govindaraja vihara. This Chaitanyapuri inscription, dated to the beginning of the 5th century CE, is the most ancient inscription found in the Hyderabad region.\n*   At **Keesaragutta**, traces of **Mahayana** Buddhism survive, including a metal statue of Mahayana founder **Acharya Nagarjuna**, collected by historian Sri Kurra Jitendrababu; the hood of a snake over the sitting figure''s head recalls the iconography also associated with the 23rd Jain Tirthankara, suggesting that Jainism may also have flourished in the Keesaragutta region.\n*   The inscription mentions **Pindapatikula**, a special branch of Hinayana Buddhism, and states that there were **18 branches of Buddhism**, with Dasabalabali being learned in all of them. There were several _sanghas_ within these branches; the **14th Arya Sangham** was one. For **Asanapura Arya Sangham**, Vikramendra Varma had the **Trilokesraya Rajamahavihara** constructed, and for its representative Sanghadasa, donated two villages with their gardens. Vikramendra Varma bore Buddhism-related titles of **Mahabodhisatva** and **Paramasaugata**; Sanghadasa belonged to the Kshemacharya branch, associated with Tamraparni (Sri Lanka).\n*   The Vishnukunds were the **last Telugu kings to patronize Buddhism**. By the end of the 5th century CE, south of the Krishna river in the Amaravati/Nagarjunakonda region, the **Vajrayana** branch had emerged, into which women, wine, non-veg, and deceitful _mantras_ entered, gradually losing purity — such places (Bomkur, Chinna Bomkur, Pedda Bomkur, Lanjaladugu near Jaggayyapeta) became associated with decline.\n*   Another development was growing **Brahmin influence within Buddhist sanghas and arama-viharas**, reflected in the phrase _''Anantha brahmana sambharasya''_ in Govinda Varma''s inscription. Brahmins gradually depicted Buddha as the ninth _avatara_ of Vishnu, and some Buddhist centres turned into Vaishnava (Narasimha) centres — Dharmapuri (Karimnagar district), originally Buddhist, came to have temples of Ramalingeswara Swamy and Narasimha Swamy.\n*   The last great Buddhist philosophers lived in the Vishnukundin kingdom: **Dingnaga**, a 5th-century logician, debated with **Isvarakrishna** (author of _Sankhya Karika_) at Vengi. Kalidasa''s _Meghasandesam_ indicates Dingnaga lived for a time at Ramagiri (Ramagundam) and Munulagutta (near Kotilingala) in Karimnagar district; he authored over a hundred books, the famous _Pramana Samuchchayam_ among them, written in Sanskrit, and taught the Yogachara school — he was the last of the great Buddhist scholars of Telugu country. A stupa and small inscriptions at Basara also indicate his presence there for some time.\n\n### Jainism\n\n*   A Kannada literary work, **Dharamarutam** (1115 CE) by the Jain poet **Nayasena**, explains that the name \"Sriparvata\" derives from **Sridharamuni**, brother of the Ikshvaku king Priyabala who ruled Vengi country from Pratipalapuram. According to this text, gods worshipping him gathered at a place thereafter called Amaravati; sky-gods showered jasmine flowers on him while he meditated under an _Arjuna_ (Maddi) tree, giving rise to the name **Mallikarjunam**.\n*   Corresponding places are found in Amrabad mandal, Mahabubnagar district — Vataverlapalli near **Mallela Tirtham**, identified with Mallikarjunam; the **Pulumavi inscription** (Nasik, first half of 2nd century CE) mentions a place \"Siridhan,\" possibly named after Sridharan.\n*   Jain _rushis_ are called \"munulu\"; the place where Sridhara and other Jain _munis_ meditated may have become \"**munuluru**\" — now Munnenoor (Munnanoor), also in Amrabad mandal. The text further states that minister **Indraprabhudu** brought Sridhar to Rishinivasa area and made him administrator there for a time as the king of Vengi; since Indra Varma was the first Vishnukund king, he may have been appointed by Sridharudu as administrator of the Amrabad area.\n*   **Prithvimula**, father-in-law of the first-generation Vishnukund king Govinda Varma, is recorded making donations to the **Jain vihara at Vardhamanu**, further indicating Jain influence of the period. To the east of Umamaheswaram stood the temple of **Mukmiridani**, and **Mukmirapet** near Munulagutta in Vellatur mandal, Karimnagar district — Mukmiri appears to have been a Jain-related goddess.\n*   Jainism disappeared during the Vishnukund period itself. The **Kapalika sect**, which arose during those days — practising magic, mantras, illicit relations with women, drinking, and vulgar dancing — lost popular favour and became responsible for the decline of Jainism; Jain temples were converted into Saiva temples, Srisailam itself being cited by Nayasena as a prime example.\n*   It is recorded that **Kumarilabhatta**, an Andhra born at Jayamangala village on the bank of the Mahanadi in Kalinga (Odisha), received Jain education in his youth but later condemned Jainism, attracting many Jains toward Vedic religion. Among those who shifted from Jainism to Vedic religion, **Indra Varma**, the first Vishnukund king, may also have been one.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p9", "partName": "Literary Development", "bodyMd": "*   The Vishnukunds established **ghatikasthanas** (centres of learning) and patronized Vedic studies; study of the **Vedas** and reverence for Brahmins are recorded in the inscriptions. Brahmins who studied the Vedas were given agraharas — inscriptions record names such as **Agnisarma** and **Easwarasarma** among the recipients.\n*   **Ghatikesari**, the suburb of Hyderabad, is understood to have been the Ghatikasthana of the Vishnukunds, supported by structures of the Vishnukund period unearthed near Keesaragutta. Those who received agraharas were well-versed in education and literature; **Dasabalabali**, the Buddhist guru, is recorded as learned in the four Vedas, adept in all sciences, and versed in all 18 Buddhist _dharmas_.\n*   Several kings were themselves scholars, as reflected in their titles:\n    *   **Govinda Varma** — _''Shadabhigna''_\n    *   **Vikramendra Varma** — _''mahakavi''_ and _''parama sogatasya''_ (as enlightened as Buddha)\n    *   **Indrabhattaraka Varma** — _''ghatikavapta punyasancharya''_\n    *   **Madhava Varma II** — _''vidyadvijaguru vipravruddh tapasvi janasraya''_These titles show the Vishnukund kings were themselves writers and scholars as well as patrons of poets and scholars.\n*   **Sanskrit replaced Prakrit as the state language** during the Vishnukund period. As Buddhism declined, Prakrit — its associated language — also disappeared; notably, the Chaitanyapuri inscription of Govinda Varma, connected to Buddhism, is in Prakrit, while all later inscriptions were issued in Sanskrit.\n*   The language of the common people, however, remained **Telugu**. In the Chikkulla inscription of Vikramendra Varma (in Sanskrit), the phrase _''vijaya rajya samvatsarambul''_ appears, in which \"ambul\" is a Telugu word — showing the influence of Telugu (with \"l\" used for \"lu,\" and \"ambu/bu\" for \"mu,\" as an earlier form of \"samvatsaralu\"). Village names recorded in Vishnukund inscriptions are entirely Telugu.\n*   Telugu terms surviving from Vishnukund inscriptions include Kudavada, Velimbali, Marokaki, Kalika, Puranisangama, Peruvatika, Dendulur, Ravareva, Regosam, Penkapara, Irundero, Tundi, Paraki, Vishayam, Netrapati, Vishaya, and _''toluchuvandhu''_ — a pure Telugu word carved on a boulder at Keesaragutta.\n*   The Sanskrit poetic work _''Janasrya chhando vichhitti''_ shows references to the Telugu language and is felt to have been authored by **Madhava Varma II**, based on his title _''janasraya''_ — though some scholars attribute it instead to a person named **Ganaswamy**, since the book''s actual preface is not available. Its fifth chapter deals with Telugu _Kavitvam_ — _ganas, yatulu, praralu, jati padyalu_, and _dvipada/tripada_ verses. The word _''janasraya''_ also appears in _''kavijanasrayam''_, a Telugu metre work written by **Malliya Rechana** in the early 10th century CE, whose roots lie in this earlier work.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p10", "partName": "Development of Architecture and Sculpture", "bodyMd": "*   Structures of the Vishnukund period demonstrated a new architectural style. Their capital cities, caves, temples, and _arama-viharas_ became key sources for the study of this architecture.\n*   In Telangana, Vishnukund forts survive in their capitals of Amaravati (Amrabad), Indrapalanagaram, and Keesaragutta. Below Keesaragutta and adjoining the tank, spacious brick buildings, palaces, Siva temples, and Shakti temples — around 1500 years old — still show their brilliance, containing _garbhagriha, arthamandapam,_ and _mukhamandapam_ like present-day temples. On the walls of **Bhuvanagiri fort**, sculptures of the royal Vishnukund emblem — the pouncing lion — are found, suggesting the fort was originally built by the Vishnukunds.\n*   Buddhist _viharas_ and _aramas_ built by Vishnukund kings, royal relatives, and wealthy persons are found mainly at Chaitanyapuri (Hyderabad), Indrapalanagaram, Phanigiri, Tirumalgiri, and Gajualabanda in Nalgonda district, and Nelakondapalli in Khammam district.\n*   **Govindaraju** built a Raja _vihara_ at Chaitanyapuri; his queen **Paramabhattaraka Mahadevi** built a _vihara_ at Indrapalanagaram known as the Paramabhattarika Mahadevi vihara — recent excavations near this vihara have identified sculptures of Buddhist _acharyas_ carved on caves at two locations.\n*   Excavations at **Phanigiri** revealed sculptures depicting Buddhist Jataka stories, Buddha''s footprints, chaityas, stupas, and monks'' _aramas-viharas_ and _mantapas_. The Buddhist _stupa_ at **Tirumalagiri**, viewed from above Phanigiri, appears in the seeming distance; the Buddhist _stupa_ at **Nelakondapalli** was so large it was called **Virat stupa**, later corrupted to **Virata Raju Gadde**. Marble sculptures of Buddha and metal statues have been recovered from these excavations.\n*   **Chaitya decorations** of the Vishnukund period appear on _mantapa_ pillars found on caves at L. Madugu (near Manthani town, Karimnagar district) and on caves at Gaurigundam waterfall — considered Buddhist _Aramas_. The **Komati caves**, below L. Madugu, are believed to have served as _Aramas_ for contemporary Jains.\n*   **Umamaheswaram** (near Amaravati/Amrabad, the birthplace of the Vishnukunds), **Yeleswaram caves**, and **Alampuram** shone as prominent Saiva-Shakti temple sites, reflecting the Vishnukunds'' devotion to Siva as indicated by titles like _''parama maheswaras''_ and by the word \"Sriparvata\" on their coins. At Umamaheswaram, a Sivalinga carved by the Pallavas and a war-drum (_nagara bheri_) arranged by the Vishnukunds survive even today. Among the two cave temples at **Saleswaram**, an inscription in Brahmi script reads _''viswesa kakkalesa''_. Alampuram, in existence from Ikshvaku times, became famous for **Bala Brahmeswara** and the temple of **Jogulamba**; the **Nava Brahma** temples there were built by kings who came after the Vishnukunds. Cave temples of Vishnukund times are also found at **Bhairava Kona**, on the border of Prakasam and Nellore districts.\n*   In the surroundings of Vijayawada — another Vishnukund capital area — multi-storeyed Buddhist _arama-viharas_ were carved and built at **Undavalli, Mogalrajapuram**, and **Indrakeeladri** hills. The inscription _''Sri Utpatti Pidugu''_ is found on their walls or beneath their pillars, and similar writings appear at the cave temples of Saleswaram and Pandavulagutta in Warangal district. Some historians believe these writings, made after Saivites drove out Buddhists and Jains, name the society of architects and sculptors; the existence of such a society of architects and sculptors is further evidenced by the Telugu word _''Toluchuvandlu''_ found at Keesaragutta.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter4-vishnukundis-p11", "partName": "Summary", "bodyMd": "The Vishnukunds ruled the entire Telugu country north of the Krishna river for more than two hundred years. During their long rule they introduced Hindu-based culture in place of the earlier composite (Buddhist-influenced) culture, and set an example for the dynasties that followed — their styles of architecture and sculpture were adopted by the Pallavas in the south and the Chalukyas in the west. \n\nThat the most popular later kings, the Kakatiyas, took Madhava Varma II, the Vishnukund king, as their dynastic founder speaks to the splendour of Vishnukund administration. Even as they followed Vedic religion and performed sacrifices and rituals, they also patronized Buddhism and Jainism and made donations freely to their organisations and monks, reflecting a policy of religious toleration. Through their administrative splendour, literary patronage, and establishment of _ghatikas_ at important centres, the Vishnukunds — the first royal dynasty to begin their political career in Telangana — secured eminence for their region and peace and prosperity for their people.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p1", "partName": "Chalukyan Age — Overview", "bodyMd": "After the fall of the Satavahanas, several parts of South India came under the rule of the Chalukyas, whose age lasted for around six centuries. The branch that ruled from Badami (Vatapi) in Bijapur district formed the parental line — the Badami (Western) Chalukyas. The Eastern Chalukyas branched off from them and ruled Vengi uninterruptedly for four and a half centuries; towards the end, they became the descendants of the Tanjavoor Cholas and ruled the Tamil region for another two and a half centuries.\n\nUnder Chalukyan patronage, South Indian culture flourished both politically and culturally. Hindu religious culture, which had taken birth under the Ikshvakus and Vishnukundins, reached its blossoming under the Chalukyas. Vedanta literature, architecture and sculpture attained their final, highest form during this period, and the temples the Chalukyas built in Karnataka, Gujarat, Maharashtra and Andhradesa remain storehouses of Chalukyan-style art. Regional languages — Telugu, Kannada, Marathi and Gujarati — all attained new literary form during Chalukyan times.\n\n**The Chalukyan branches and their periods:**\n\n| Branch | Period | Core Territory |\n|---|---|---|\n| Badami (Western) Chalukyas | 543 – 752 A.D. | Maharashtra and Karnataka |\n| Eastern Chalukyas (Vengi Chalukyas) | 624 – 1075 A.D. | Vengi (Andhra) |\n| Vemulavada Chalukyas | 750 – 973 A.D. | Northwestern Telangana, as feudatories of the Rashtrakutas |\n| Mudigonda Chalukyas | 850 – 1200 A.D. | Areas around Khammam |\n| Kalyani Chalukyas | 973 – 1157 A.D. | Belonged to the Badami Chalukya line; ruled from Karnataka |\n\nEach of these branches contributed to the development of its respective region as a regional administrative centre, with its own specialities in art, literature and governance.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p2", "partName": "Sources of Chalukyan History", "bodyMd": "Like the history of other South Indian ruling dynasties, Chalukyan history depends mainly on stone and copper-plate inscriptions. Most information on the Eastern Chalukyas comes from copper-plate grants. In Chalukyan inscriptions, historians notice three distinct phases:\n\n1.  **Early phase (Kubja Vishnuvardhana to Vishnuvardhana V):** Inscriptions of this phase name only three generations — the donor, his father and grandfather — with no historically valuable information about victories or the sequence of rulers beyond who succeeded whom.\n2.  **Change after Gunaga Vijayaditya (Vijayaditya III):** From his time, inscriptions begin recording genealogy and events in more detail.\n3.  **Later detailed inscriptions**, which supply administrative and military information.\n\n**Key inscriptions and what they reveal:**\n\n| Inscription | Date | Significance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sataluru grant of Gunaga Vijayaditya | — | Gives the chronology and rule periods of earlier Eastern Chalukya rulers |\n| Neelgund inscription | 866 A.D. | Records Gunaga Vijayaditya as a feudatory of Amoghavarsha I (Rashtrakuta), paying tribute to him |\n| Sanjan copper plate | 872 A.D. | Records that, unlike other feudatories, Gunaga Vijayaditya personally cleaned Amoghavarsha''s walking path |\n| Kopparam inscription | 631 A.D. | Pulakesi II refers to Vishnuvardhana as heir-apparent (Yuvaraja) |\n| Cheepurupalli inscription | 641 A.D. | Shows Vishnuvardhana had assumed the title of maharaja and ruled independently |\n| Aihole (Ayyavolu) prasasti | — | Composed in Sanskrit by Ravikeerthi, Pulakesi II''s War Minister; the main source for Pulakesi II''s reign |\n| Kollipara copper plate | — | Records Arikesari I''s victories and administration (Vemulavada Chalukyas); its authenticity has been questioned by some scholars |\n| Kuruvagattu stone inscription | — | Issued by Beeragruha (Mahaboobnagar), informs on Vemulavada administrative details |\n| Vemulavada copper plate of Arikesari II & Chennur copper plate | 941 A.D. | Record how Arikesari II sheltered Yuddhamalla I and made him ruler of Chennur |\n| Karimnagar copper plate | 946 A.D. | Traces Vemulavada developments from Yuddhamalla to Arikesari |\n| Kurkyala inscription | 940 A.D. | Written by Jayavallabha (brother of the poet Pampa); records that Pampa wrote _Adipurana_ in 941 A.D. |\n| Parbhani copper plate (Arikesari III) | 966 A.D. | Records that his Subahdar built Jain temples |\n| Repaka inscription (Arikesari) | 968 A.D. | Records construction of a Jain temple and land grants at Repaka |\n\n**Literary sources:** _Vikramarjuna Vijayam_, written in Kannada by the poet Pampa, is a prominent work of the Vemulavada Chalukya period; it contains a eulogy (prasasti) of Arikesari II and explains his relations with the Rashtrakutas. Somadevasuri, a Jain scholar, wrote _Yasastilaka Champukavyam_ (also called _Yasodhara Charitra_) during the time of the later Vemulavada kings — a key source for the religious doctrines of Jainism as well as social and political details of the age. Somadevasuri''s _Neetikavyamruta_ is also regarded as a leading literary source of the period.\n\nOnly two donors of copper-plate inscriptions are known from Mudigonda Chalukya times: the **Mogala Cheruvu inscription** of Kusumayudha IV, and the **Krivvaka inscription** of Kusumayudha VI (informs on chronology). The **Narayanagiri copper plate** (1004 A.D.) describes the administration of Chalukya Gonagarasa, who ruled the Warangal region. The **Bezawada copper plate** (892 A.D.) records that Kusumayudha donated Kukiparru village to a brahmin, Potamaiah. The **Koravi inscription** (935 A.D.) is another important source.\n\n**Coins as archaeological sources:** Gold coins bearing the name \"Chalukya Chandra,\" issued during the time of Eastern Chalukya Saktivarma (999–1011 A.D.), have been found in Arakan and Siam. Gold, silver and copper coins circulated during the Chalukyan age. The gold coin was called _gadya_ (weighing 88 grains), with variants such as _Rajanarayana gadya_, _Surabhi gadya_ and _Tyaga gadya_. The silver coin was called _mada_, and _kasu_ was the name used for the copper coin.\n\n**Monuments as sources:** Architectural and sculptural monuments testify to Chalukyan religious patronage across Jainism, Saivism and Vaishnavism, treated with equal favour. Since no temples existed anywhere in India for Brahma, the first of the trimurthis, the Western Chalukyas met this gap by constructing the Nava Brahma temples at Alampur. The Raja Rajeswara temple built by the Vemulavada Chalukyas at Vemulavada became famous as a Saiva temple of the age. Chalukyan architecture attained special significance at Alampur, and the Western Chalukyas also built famous structures at Badami and Pattadakal.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p3", "partName": "Origin of the Word \"Chalukya\"", "bodyMd": "No certain sources establish the origin of the Chalukya dynastic name or their place of origin; different inscriptions offer differing, partly legendary, accounts.\n\n**1\\. Chandravamsa/Ayodhya** **Traditions**\n\n*   Some inscriptions state Chalukyas belonged to the Chandravamsa that ruled Ayodhya and came south seeking victory, settling there over time. Modern historians give little weight to this, since Ayodhya was never the capital of Chandravamsa kings in later-attested history.\n*   In the **Solanki inscriptions** of Gujarat, it is stated that the dynasty''s founder was born from the _chalukya_ (palm) of Brahma — hence the name Chalukya.\n*   Bilhana''s _Vikramankadeva Charitra_ narrates a variant version of this same \"born from Brahma''s palm\" story: amid sufferings caused by rakshasas, Brahma, while offering oblation to the Sun after hearing Indra''s prayer, saw a great warrior born from his palm (_chuluka_) — armoured and strongly built — who came to be called Chalukya, with his descendants named Chalukyas.\n*   The **Kautham inscription** of Kalyani Chalukya ruler Vikramaditya V gives yet another version: Chalukyas belonged to Chandravamsa kings of Ayodhya. After 59 kings ruled Ayodhya, they went into exile in South India; after 16 more kings ruled there, that kingdom too declined. Some generations later, Jayasimhavallabha defeated the Rashtrakutas and fully re-established Chalukya authority.\n\n**2\\. The \"Chalika\" tribal-name theory**\n\n*   The word \"Chalika\" first appears in a third-century C.E. inscription of the Ikshvakus at Nagarjunakonda, describing Khandachelika Remmanaka, a feudatory of the Ikshvakus, as king of Hiranya rashtra (Cuddapah district). If \"Chalika\" is an old form of \"Chalukya,\" this would read as \"Chalukya Remmanaka.\"\n*   Chalukya inscriptions show variant forms of the word — Saliki, Saluki, Chelka, Chelalya, Chalukya — suggesting it was originally the name of a Dravidian-language tribe that later became the dynastic name.\n*   Dr. Rice felt Chalukyas may have belonged to the **family of Seleucus in Central Asia**, based on the similarity of the words \"Chalukya\" and \"Seleucus\" — an opinion not considered trustworthy, as similarity of sound is its only basis.\n*   D.C. Sircar held that the Chalukyas belonged to a **Kannada family** who declared themselves Kshatriyas and had a relationship with the Chulikas of Northern India, though no firm sources support this.\n*   The **Ranastapudi inscription** states the dynasty''s founder stayed in the ashram of Vishnubhatta Somayaji at Mudivemu agraharam (still existing in Cuddapah district — the Hiranya Rashtra of those times). The **Tummeyanoor inscription** of Pulakesi II similarly calls parts of Kurnool and Mahaboobnagar districts \"Chalukya Vishaya,\" on the model of how tribal/community names (Mulaka, Asaka, Andhra, Mahishaka) came to name their areas of habitation. Historians believe the native place itself became known as Chalukya Vishaya.\n*   In Maharashtra, a \"Chaliki\" caste exists near Bidar, where a village called Chalkapuri and a river called \"Chalki\" are also found.\n*   Chinese traveller Hiuen Tsang referred to Pulakesi II as \"**Maharastradhipati**.\" Based on this and other clues, Durga Prasad Dikshit proposed that the Bidar–Osmanabad region was the Chalukyas'' native land.\n*   Like the Kadambas, the Chalukyas referred to themselves as belonging to _manvya gothrikas_ and as _haratiputras_. The Chalukya royal names Pulakesi and Bittarasa are also held to belong to Karnataka usage — \"Pulakesi\" derives from _Polikesi_, meaning \"hair at the centre of the head\" (a hairstyle seen on ancient South Indian warrior sculptures, called _pulakesi_/_ashtavira_). The Kannada word _arasa_ (meaning king) appears in the names of Vengi and Vemulavada Chalukya kings and in some inscriptions — leading B.C. Sircar and other scholars to conclude the Chalukyas were of Karnataka origin.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p4", "partName": "Badami (Western) Chalukyas (543 – 752 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "### Background and Founding\n\nThe ancestors of the Chalukyas were feudatories of the Ikshvakus (who ruled Andhradesa from Vijayapuri) and ruled Hiranya rashtra in Cuddapah district — their native land. In the first half of the fourth century C.E., the Pallavas overthrew the Ikshvakus and occupied Andhradesa, and the Chalukyas were reduced to Pallava subordination. Resenting this, they tried to become independent; Trilochana Pallava attacked and defeated them.\n\nMayurasarma, the Kadamba ruler, was hostile to the Pallavas, so he allied with the Chalukyas, and together they fought the Pallavas before migrating into Karnataka and establishing a kingdom at Badami; their royal emblem was the **varaha (boar)**.\n\nThe founder of the Chalukya dynasty was **Jayasimha (Jayasimha Vallabha)**, who defeated the Rashtrakutas of Manyakheta. The Undikavatika copper plate of the Rashtrakuta ruler Abhimanyudu names a commander-in-chief called Jayasimha; it is opined this was the same Jayasimha Vallabha whom the Aihole inscription of Pulakesi II names as founder of the Chalukya kingdom. After Jayasimha, his son **Ranaraga** ascended the Chalukyan throne.\n\n### Rulers of Badami Chalukyas\n\n| Ruler | Reign |\n|---|---|\n| Pulakesi I | 535 – 566 A.D. |\n| Keertivarma | 566 – 597 A.D. |\n| Mangalesa | 598 – 609 A.D. |\n| Pulakesi II | 609 – 642 A.D. |\n| Vikramaditya | 642 – 680 A.D. |\n| Vinayaditya | 680 – 696 A.D. |\n| Vijayaditya | 696 – 733 A.D. |\n| Vikramaditya II | 733 – 744 A.D. |\n| Keertivarma II | 745 – 752 A.D. |\n\n### Pulakesi I (535 – 566 A.D.)\n\nSon of Ranaraga and founder of the independent Chalukyan dynasty. He raised Chalukyan authority from feudatory status to that of an independent kingdom, defied the Kadambas, occupied Vatapi, and built a strong fort there in 543 A.D., making it his capital. His victories are recorded in an inscription found at Badami (543 A.D.). To proclaim his triumphs, he performed an _asvamedha_ sacrifice. An inscription found at Yeleswaram suggests his kingdom extended up to Nalgonda district. He was the first Chalukyan ruler to assume the title \"maharaja.\"\n\n### Keertivarma (566 – 597 A.D.)\n\nSon of Pulakesi I, as capable and courageous as his father. His wife belonged to the Sendraka royal family. He defeated the Mauryas ruling the Konkan region and the Kadambas of Banavasi, extending his kingdom up to Konkan. He built several palaces and temples at Vatapi (Badami), earning praise from historians as the first ruler to beautify Vatapi as a city.\n\n### Mangalesa (598 – 609 A.D.)\n\nAs Keertivarma''s eldest son Pulakesi II was a minor at his father''s death, Keertivarma''s brother Mangalesa came to the throne. He defeated the Kadambas and conquered Revati island (Goa), expanding the kingdom, and held the title \"paramabhagavata.\"\n\nMangalesa tried to ensure the throne would pass to his own heirs after him, and this triggered a war of succession between Pulakesi II and Mangalesa, recorded in the **Hyderabad inscription** (612 A.D.), in which Mangalesa was killed and Pulakesi II ascended the throne in Saka era 581 / C.E. 609.\n\n### Pulakesi II (609 – 642 A.D.)\n\nThe greatest ruler among the Western Chalukya kings, comparable to North Indian emperors such as Chandragupta Maurya and Chandragupta I of the Gupta dynasty. With his rise to power, the \"Chalukyan age\" is said to have properly begun in South Indian history.\n\n**Conquests and expansion**\n\n*   Conducted several military expeditions and converted the Chalukyan kingdom — until then modest in extent — into a great empire spanning Maharashtra, Malwa, Kalinga, Andhra and Karnataka.\n*   His minister for war, Ravikeerthi, described his campaigns in the **Aihole (Ayyavolu) prasasti**, composed in Sanskrit. According to it, Pulakesi II first defeated the Kadamba, Ganga, Alupa, Maurya, Lata and Ghurjara kings to secure supremacy over South India.\n\n**Defeat of Harsha**\n\n*   On the banks of the river Narmada, Pulakesi II fought and defeated **Harshavardhana**, the \"Lord of Northern India,\" becoming the first Chalukyan ruler to check a North Indian emperor.\n*   The **Ayyavolu inscription** states Pulakesi II arranged his army most effectively and personally defeated Harshavardhana in this famous battle; afterward, the Narmada river became the recognized boundary between the two kingdoms.\n*   After this war, Pulakesi II assumed the title \"**Parameswara**.\"\n\n**Eastern campaigns and Vengi**\n\n*   After settling conquered northern areas under the control of his brothers, Pulakesi II began an eastward military expedition, conquering Kosala and Kalinga and raiding Vengi.\n*   Disordered political conditions then prevailing in Andhra land aided his campaign greatly; most areas of Telangana and Rayalaseema had already come under his control, though the lower Krishna river areas were under Pallava occupation and Vengi kingdom was under the Durjayas.\n*   Entering via Kalinga, Pulakesi first fought and defeated the Durjayas in the Pishtapuram and Kunala battles and occupied Vengi. His donations in Andhra are known from his Kopparam and Maratoor inscriptions.\n\n**Conflicts with the Pallavas**\n\n*   Pulakesi II advanced to Kanchipuram, the Pallava capital, and in a fierce battle at Pullalur defeated Pallava **Mahendravarma** (C.E. 600–630).\n*   When Mahendravarma I died, Pulakesi II again invaded, aiming to bring the Pallava kingdom fully under his control; the Banas of Rayalaseema, in this connection, came under Chalukyan control.\n*   Battles at Periyala, Manimangalam and Suramara followed between Pallava and Chalukya forces, with many Pallava areas coming under Pulakesi II.\n\n**Death**\n\n*   During the reign of Pallava Narasimhavarma I (630–668 A.D.), the battles took an extreme turn, and Pulakesi II died at Pallava hands; Narasimhavarma advanced to Badami, diminished its importance, and assumed the title \"**Vatapikonda**,\" in a battle fought in C.E. 642 at Manimangalam.\n    \n*   Though his royal authority thus ended, Pulakesi II is still regarded as the most warlike ruler in South Indian history.\n    \n\n**Other notable facts**\n\n*   The Persian emperor sent **Khusru as an ambassador** to Pulakesi II''s court; a painting in the Ajanta caves depicts this Persian embassy.\n*   Chinese traveller Hiuen-tsang visited his court in 641 A.D. and recorded that the Chalukyan kingdom was extensive, wealthy and prosperous, and that Pulakesi was a kshatriya commanding a great army.\n*   With Pulakesi II''s death and Narasimhavarma''s aggression, Badami''s prestige declined; Pallava armies looted the kingdom and created chaos. His sons — **Adityavarma, Chandraditya, Vikramaditya and Jayasimha** — failed to remain united in defending the kingdom from enemy attacks.\n\n### Vikramaditya (642 – 680 A.D.)\n\nThird son of Pulakesi II. He steadied and saved the kingdom from Pallava attacks, and conquered the Pandya, Chola and Kalabhra rulers who had earlier assisted the Pallavas. During his reign, Chalukya–Pallava conflict continued, and Chalukyan authority was extended up to \"three seas.\" He appointed his brother Jaysimhavarma, who had cooperated in his campaigns, as viceroy of the Lata (South Gujarat) kingdom.\n\n### Vinayaditya (680 – 696 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vikramaditya; ruled the inherited kingdom effectively and peacefully, developing it further. His inscriptions record an invasion of Northern India; he fought many battles and received gifts from the Kalabhra, Kerala, Haihaya, Malwa, Chola and Pandya kings. He was finally killed in the \"**Vilande**\" battle at the hands of the armies of Prameswaravarman, the Ganga king.\n\n### Vijayaditya (696 – 733 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vinayaditya; invaded with a large army and won against several North Indian royal dynasties. He adopted the Ganga-Yamuna torana and the **Pala flag** as his official emblem. As Vinayaditya had died at Badami, Vijayaditya took up kingly duties in C.E. 696. He built temples at Pattadakal (Kesuval).\n\n### Vikramaditya II (733 – 744 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vijayaditya. During his reign, he confronted the rising power of the Arabs and their attacks in the Sindh region. His contemporary, Pallava **Nandivarma II** (C.E. 695–722), assumed the title \"**Pallavamalla**\"; the Badami Chalukyas defeated the Pallavas in several battles fought between the two. He defeated the Pandya, Chola, Kerala and Kalabhra kingdoms, and as a mark of victory erected a pillar on the coast of the Southern Ocean. His wives, Lokamahadevi (of the Kalachuri dynasty) and Rajaneetrailokyadevi, had the temples \"Lokeswaralayam\" and \"Tralokeswaralayam\" built at Pattadakal in their names.\n\n### Keertivarma II (745 – 752 A.D.) — End of the Badami Chalukyas\n\nSon of Vikramaditya II; the last ruler of the Western Chalukya (Badami/Vatapi) dynasty. During his rule, Chalukyan authority began to decline as he was made to confront many critical conditions:\n\n*   Rashtrakuta **Dantidurga** not only occupied the Ellora region but also turned the Chalukya feudatory kings of Kosala, Kalinga and the Telugu Cholas toward himself.\n*   As Dantidurga''s power rose and Chalukya feudatories defected to him, in C.E. 752 he overthrew the Badami Chalukya dynasty and established the Rashtrakuta kingdom in Karnataka.\n\nInscriptions explaining the decline of Badami Chalukya authority — the \"**Aviyar rock slab**\" and \"**the Meraj plates**\" — are found in the surrounding areas of Maharashtra.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p5", "partName": "Eastern Chalukyas (Vengi Chalukyas) (624 – 1075 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "Eastern Chalukyas stand among the dynasties that rendered the most valuable service to Telugu culture and literature. Because the Vengi region — lying between the Krishna and Godavari rivers — became their capital area, they are also called Vengi Chalukyas. Among the kingdoms that sprang from the mother line of the Badami Chalukyas, Eastern Chalukyas became the leading one, ruling Andhra land for four and a half centuries (C.E. 624 to 1075) with a highly praiseworthy history. Though considered \"non-Andhras\" by origin, by settling in Vengi and administering in the local language and with local feeling, they safeguarded Telugu culture — even while dynasties such as the Cholas and Chalukyas, fighting each other for supremacy, turned Andhra land into a battlefield, the Vengi Chalukyas tried to uphold the independence, honour and prestige of Andhra land.\n\n### Establishment and Three Phases of Rule\n\nThere were different opinions regarding the exact date of establishment, but as per Vengi Chalukya inscriptions, their rule at Vengi commenced in 624 A.D. Their rule (C.E. 624–1075) is divided into three phases:\n\n| Phase | Period | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| First | C.E. 624 – 753 | Peace and tranquility |\n| Second | C.E. 753 – 973 | Deadly enmity between the Vengi Chalukyas and the Rashtrakutas |\n| Third | C.E. 973 – 1075 | Decline of Rashtrakuta power; emergence of the Kalyani Chalukyas |\n\nThough aggression on Vengi occurred at different times, effective Chalukya authority over Vengi began only from C.E. 624 — when the Badami Chalukya king **Pulakesi II**, after conquering the Vengi region, appointed his brother **Kubja Vishnuvardhana** as viceroy there. Kubja Vishnuvardhana came to be regarded as the founder of the Vengi Chalukya kingdom.\n\n### Kubja Vishnuvardhana (624 – 641 A.D.)\n\nEastern Chalukyan inscriptions record that he ruled Vengi for 18 years. After becoming viceroy, the Vengi kingdom became independent and expanded. He bore titles such as \"**Vishamasiddhi**,\" \"**Kamadheva**\" and \"**Makaradhwaja**\" — the \"Vishamasiddhi\" title reflecting his fame for subjugating impregnable enemy fortresses.\n\n*   Up to C.E. 631, he ruled Vengi as representative of his brother, Pulakesi II. The **Kopparam inscription of C.E. 631** records that Pulakesi II permitted him to rule independently; his own inscriptions (found at Cheepurupalli and Timmapuram) confirm his kingdom extended from Visakhapatnam in the north to Kammanadu in the south, and that he had declared himself maharaja.\n*   From his time, feudatory kingdoms began to form within his territory; he honoured servants who helped him in war by granting them small kingdoms. Among the feudatories, he defeated **Indravarma III**, the Ganga dynasty ruler of Kalinga, and made him his feudatory.\n*   The **Timmapuram inscription** praises him as a devotee of Vishnu and as \"Paramabhagavata.\" Even so, he also came to be known as \"Kartikeya Kaki\" (devotee of goddess Durga). His wife, **Ayyanamahadevi**, favoured Jainism, and built a Jain monastery at Bezawada (Vijayawada) for Jain monks, donating a village for it.\n*   Chinese traveller **Hiuen-tsang** (C.E. 600–644) visited and recorded conditions of the time.\n*   Pulakesi II made two attacks on the Pallava kingdom during C.E. 631–641; in the last expedition, Pallava Narasimhavarma attacked Vatapi, and historians opine that Kubja Vishnuvardhana took part in support of his brother and died along with him.\n*   Kubja Vishnuvardhana was a skilful troop leader, an unrivalled warrior, and capable of subjugating any impregnable fortress — plain, forest or hillock. After him, his eldest son, **Jayasimha I**, ascended the throne in C.E. 642.\n\n### Jayasimha I (642 – 673 A.D.)\n\nKnown as Jayasimhavallabha I — the first Eastern Chalukyan king to bear the name \"Jayasimha.\" Chalukya inscriptions record he reigned 33 years. His titles were \"Sakala Lokasraya\" and \"Sarvasiddhi.\" Though his rule was largely peaceful, the Pulamboor inscription records some battles; he stabilised his supremacy over the Boya kings in the Nellore region.\n\n**Significance for Telugu:** During his reign, an inscription in the Telugu language was issued at Vipparla village (Narasaraopet mandal, Guntur district) — the **first Telugu inscription of the Eastern Chalukyas**, showing that Telugu was already gaining prominence from the time of Jayasimhavallabha I. He ruled with great splendour and died in C.E. 673.\n\n### Indrabhattaraka (673 A.D.)\n\nAs Jayasimhavallabha I had no children, his brother Indrabhattaraka came to the throne and ruled for only seven days. In this brief week, he issued the **Kondanagoor inscription**. His titles were \"tyagadhanudu\" and \"makaradhwaja.\"\n\n### Vishnuvardhana II (673 – 682 A.D.)\n\nSon of Indrabhattaraka. People praised him as \"**dharmasastravetta.**\" His titles included \"vishamasiddhi,\" \"makaradhwaja,\" \"sarvalokesaraya\" and \"pralayaditya\" — the \"vishamasiddhi\" title showing he had assumed the titles of Kubja Vishnuvardhana.\n\n### Mangi Yuvaraju (682 – 706 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vishnuvardhana II; ruled for 25 years with the title \"**vijayasiddhi.**\" His inscriptions show he expanded the kingdom from Visakhapatnam to Ongole. He had many wives, and from them three sons and a daughter (named Prithvipori).\n\n### Jayasimha II (706 – 718 A.D.)\n\nEldest son of Mangi Yuvaraju. From his time onward, **wars of succession began**, weakening the Chalukyan kingdom. As he had no children, after him his brothers **Kokkili Maharaju** (C.E. 718–19) and then **Vishnuvardhana III** ascended the throne.\n\n### Vishnuvardhana III (719 – 755 A.D.)\n\nBrother of Kokkili Maharaju. A fight took place between the two brothers for authority over the kingdom; after ruling six months, when Vishnuvardhana III rebelled, Kokkili fled to the central Kalinga region of Elemanchili. During these disturbances, Boya Kottalu in the Nellore region was taken back under Pallava control. He bore the title \"tribhuvanamkusa\"; his wife was named Vijayamahadevi. He issued many copper-plate inscriptions.\n\n### Vijayaditya I (755 – 772 A.D.)\n\nHis reign is important as marking the entry of the Eastern Chalukyas into their **second phase**. Around C.E. 755, the Rashtrakutas under **Dantidurga** ended the Badami Chalukya dynasty and established a new empire with Manyakheta as its capital (their first place of habitation was Latur village in Hyderabad province).\n\nWith the rise of the Rashtrakutas, the Chalukya–Rashtrakuta wars began, continuing for around 200 years and leaving history \"tainted with blood\"; though the Chalukyas made many efforts to confront the Rashtrakutas — even taking advantage of internal conflicts in the enemy camp — they could not escape defeat, and Rashtrakutas under Arikesari I, who ruled Vemulavada in Telangana, attacked Vengi-trikalinga, so that Vengi was at last made to accept the suzerainty of Manyakheta. Vijayaditya I bore the title \"tribhuvanamkusa.\"\n\n### Vishnuvardhana IV (772 – 808 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vijayaditya I; ruled Vengi, starting his rule as a feudatory of Rashtrakuta king **Krishna I** (as his father had been defeated). When Krishna I died in C.E. 722 \\[as per source\\], a war of succession broke out among his sons, and **Dhruva** ascended the throne in C.E. 780.\n\n*   In the succession war, Dhruva''s brother **Govinda** was helped by Vishnuvardhana IV. Dhruva sent **Arikesari I** (Vemulavada Chalukya, ruling Vemulavada in Telangana) against Vishnuvardhana IV, who had helped Govinda. Arikesari defeated Vishnuvardhana. The **Parbhani inscription (C.E. 966)** informs that Arikesari conquered Vengi and Kalinga.\n*   Vishnuvardhana accepted Rashtrakuta sovereignty and gave his daughter **Seelamahadevi** in marriage to Dhruva. But even such political marriage alliances could not end the deep-seated enmity between the two dynasties.\n\n### Vijayaditya II (808 – 847 A.D.)\n\nAlso known as Narendra Mrigaraju. He fought endless battles with Rashtrakuta feudatories — the Western Ganga and Vemulavada Chalukyas — staying in the battlefield day and night, sword in hand.\n\n*   When the Rashtrakutas interfered in the war of succession between him and Bhimasaluki, the prestige of the Vengi kingdom suffered; despite this, with the help of Nruparudra of the Haihaya dynasty, he fought 108 battles over more than twelve years against the supporters of Bhimasaluki, and at last occupied Vengi.\n    \n*   For these victories he earned the **titles** \"Narendreswara,\" \"Narendramrugaraju,\" \"Chalukyaraya\" and \"Vikramadavali.\"\n    \n*   Rashtrakuta king **Amoghavarsha**, who ascended the throne while still young, could not resist Vijayaditya II''s strength. **Karkaraju**, a Gujarat feudatory safeguarding the Rashtrakuta kingdom, accepted Vijayaditya''s supremacy and gave his daughter Seelamahadevi in marriage to Kalivishnuvardhana, Vijayaditya''s son. The Ghurjara-Prathiharas were defeated in Central India, and his remaining rule went on peacefully.\n    \n*   He is credited with building Siva temples at the 108 places where he fought his battles — installing a Sivalinga and constructing a Siva temple at each battlefield. It is said Bezawada (Vijayawada) got its name because of him.\n\n### Kalivishnuvardhana (847 – 848 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vijayaditya II (also called Vishnuvardhana V). Had four sons: Vijayaditya III, Vikramaditya, Nripakamudu and Yuddhamalla.\n\n### Gunaga Vijayaditya (Vijayaditya III) (849 – 892 A.D.)\n\nThe greatest among the Eastern Chalukyas — also called Gunaka Vijayaditya for his many good qualities. He was the ruler who could make the victory-flag of the Chalukyas march \"from the Ganges in the North to the Cauvery in the South.\" Around four decades were spent in wars and crisis.\n\n**Consolidation and administration**\n\n*   To suppress feudatories and establish peace, he appointed brahmin commanders-in-chief of highest loyalty, such as **Pandurangadu**, **Vinayadisarma** and **Rajaditya**.\n*   Pandurangadu conquered Boya Kottas, held Nellore and established peace there; a strong fort was built with Kandukoor as the central point, equivalent in importance to Bezawada.\n*   Though his mother **Seelamahadevi** was a Rashtrakuta princess, stable peace did not follow between the two dynasties for various reasons. As long as Amoghavarsha lived, the Chalukyas (under Gunaga) remained subordinate to Rashtrakuta authority.\n\n**Military campaigns**\n\n*   Conquered Gangavadi and Nolambavadi in C.E. 870, defeating Ranavikrama, the Gangavadi ruler, at Sivaganga. Rashtrakutas and their allies who supported them fell into trouble at the hands of Gunaga''s commander-in-chief. Forts like Achalapuram, Kiranapuram and Chakrakutam got destroyed. The Dahala kingdom (between the Ganga and Narmada rivers), South Kosala and Kalinga kingdoms were subjugated by Chalukyan armies.\n*   Achieving great victory and revenge, Rashtrakuta king Krishna had no alternative but to accept Gunaga Vijayaditya''s authority. Gunaga took charge of the Pali dhvaja and the Krishna–Yamuna torana, which remained symbols of Eastern Chalukya supreme authority.\n*   During his reign, Eastern Chalukyan authority extended from Mahanandi to Pulicat lake, remaining unrivalled in South Indian politics.\n*   After the death of Amoghavarsha (C.E. 880), his son **Krishna II** assumed authority but was defeated by Gunaga Vijayaditya; Krishna II had no alternative but to conclude peace with Gunaga and accept his sovereign authority (details recorded in the Dharmavaram, Attili and Paderu inscriptions).\n*   The **Sataluru inscription** of Gunaga Vijayaditya records victories against the Pallavas and Pandyas. Defeating the Pallavas and Pandyas, Vijayaditya may have saved a Chola king (possibly Aditya Chola) who had sought Pandurangadu''s shelter. Some inscriptions suggest Baddega, the Vemulavada king, was also defeated by him.\n*   Making the Rashtrakutas — themselves once conquerors of the entire South India — his feudatories, Gunaga Vijayaditya became the absolute and supreme sovereign of the entire South India. He assumed **titles** such as \"tripuramartamaheswara,\" \"parachakrarama\" and \"ranarangasudraka.\"\n\n**Decline after his death:** After him, quarrels started in the Vengi Chalukya dynasty over the throne. Taking advantage of these internal disturbances, enemies captured many occupied areas: the Ganga king **Vajrahasta** annexed the Trikalinga kingdom into his own. This brought down the prestige of the Vengi kingdom; the fact that divisions arose within the core strength of the Vengi Chalukyas showed how terrible conditions in the kingdom had become.\n\n### Chalukya Bhima I (892 – 921 A.D.)\n\nHis accession was opposed by his uncle **Yuddhamalla I** (his father Gunaga Vijayaditya''s brother), who sought help from Rashtrakuta Krishna. His rule did not go on peacefully.\n\n*   Vemulavada king **Baddega**, insulted during Gunaga Vijayaditya''s time, came with Rashtrakuta armies and captured Chalukya Bhima in the war fought at Kolleru — possibly when Bhima took shelter in the Kolleru water fort.\n*   The **Koravi copper plate inscription (C.E. 935)** informs that, to defeat the Rashtrakuta army, Bhima made **Mahakala**, son of his stepmother, associate with him as Dandanadha (commander-in-chief). Bhima defeated the Rashtrakutas and drove them away.\n*   In a fierce battle, **Irimartiganda**, the son of Bhima, killed a Rashtrakuta in the Nidadavolu and Peravanguru battles and sacrificed himself on the battlefield; as a memorial to his heroic son, Bhima made Vedatalur village an agrahara and donated it.\n*   Chalukya Bhima installed the Bheemeswaraswamy (temple) at Draksharamam.\n*   Around C.E. 900, during his time, the Kanchipura Pallava empire was conquered by the Cholas.\n*   His inscriptions record that during his 30-year rule he waged 360 battles.\n\n### Vijayaditya IV / Kollabhiganda (921 A.D.)\n\nSon of Chalukya Bhima I; ruled for only six months in C.E. 921. When **Vajrahasta III** of the Kalinga Ganga dynasty rebelled, the **Viraja battle** was fought against him, in which Vijayaditya IV was defeated and, it appears, died.\n\n### Ammaraju I (921 – 927 A.D.)\n\nAscended after Vijayaditya IV. It is said Rajamahendrapuram was constructed during his reign and became the Chalukyan capital — as Vengi, being in a flat plain, was easily subjected to enemy attacks, Ammaraju established his capital on the northern coast of the Godavari, bearing the title \"rajamahendra.\"\n\n**Wars of succession:** After Ammaraju''s death, succession wars started among the brothers of Gunaga Vijayaditya — Vikramaditya and Yuddhamalla. Up to Ammaraju I, only the descendants of Vikramaditya had ascended the Vengi throne, but Yuddhamalla''s line refused to tolerate this and declared war. **Talaraju** (C.E. 927), son of Yuddhamalla I, and then his son **Yuddhamalla II** (C.E. 928–934), occupied the kingdom. Yuddhamalla II is said to have shifted his capital to Bezawada out of insecurity, built a Kartikeya temple there, and issued the famous **Bezawada stone inscription (C.E. 930)**.\n\n### Chalukya Bhima II (934 – 945 A.D.)\n\nHalf-brother of Ammaraju I; drove away Yuddhamalla II and occupied the throne. He killed his competitors Tatabikki, Rajamarthanda, Lovabikki, Ayyapa and others who had occupied the throne. He could to some extent limit Rashtrakuta influence in Vengi politics. His wife Ankidevi, daughter of the Kalinga king, may have provided him Rashtrakuta-side help. During his time, the rebellion of the feudatories (Vemulavada Chalukyas) within the Rashtrakuta kingdom against Govinda IV worked to his advantage. He had two sons — Ammaraju II and Danarnava.\n\n### Ammaraju II (945 – 970 A.D.)\n\nRuled Vengi for 25 years, though dethroned twice. His mother was Lokamahadevi.\n\n*   After his coronation, the grandsons of Yuddhamalla I — **Badaparaju** (C.E. 945) and **Talapa II** — drove him away and occupied the kingdom for some time.\n*   Rashtrakuta **Krishna III** then sent an army against Ammaraju; Ammaraju''s kingdom was in turn occupied by Krishna III. After Krishna III turned back, Ammaraju returned to Vengi, occupied the kingdom, and reigned up to C.E. 970.\n*   He gave donation at Bezawada to a Saiva temple called \"samastabhuvanasraya,\" built by Narendramrigaraju. During his time, the Kalamukha sect developed.\n*   At the end, in C.E. 970, his brother **Danarnava** raided him; first defeated, Danarnava sought the help of the Mudigonda Chalukyas and, along with Rashtrakuta Krishna III, killed Ammaraju II and ascended the Vengi throne.\n\n### Danarnava (970 – 973 A.D.)\n\nHis mother, Urjapaya, was a princess from Kalinga. Though Krishna III had helped him gain the throne, Danarnava was able to conquer Southern Andhra, which had been lost by the Vengi Chalukyas. Taking advantage of the decline of Rashtrakuta power, he invaded Renadu in C.E. 972, took Velanadu, Konidesa and Pottapinadu under his control, and gifted away Pottapinadu to the Mudigonda Chalukyas who had helped him — thereby gaining rule over 300 villages.\n\nWhile Danarnava was ruling ably, **Jatachoda Bhima**, a relative of Ammaraju II, attacked him, killed him, and declared himself king.\n\n### Jatachoda Bhima (973 – 999 A.D.)\n\nWith his reign, Vengi political history entered its **third stage**. He dashed into Vengi politics; the inscription at the Kailasanatha temple gives information about him. He ruled with **Peddakallu** (Kurnool district) as his capital. Jatachoda was his father, and Vallabhiganda (daughter of Vijayaditya, granddaughter of Chalukya Bhima I, of the Chola dynasty) his mother.\n\n*   The killing of Ammaraju II and Danarnava''s seizure of the Vengi kingdom angered Jatachoda Bhima, who attacked Vengi, killed Danarnava, and occupied it. He is not mentioned in Vengi Chalukyan inscriptions.\n*   Danarnava''s sons, **Saktivarma and Vimaladitya**, fled to Kalinga and, with the help of Ganga Kamarnava, invaded Vengi; to confront them Jatachoda Bhima fought unending battles on all sides and defeated the Kalinga forces.\n*   As his authority strengthened in Vengi and Kalinga, Saktivarma and Vimaladitya, not knowing what to do, ran away to the Chola kingdom for shelter. Chola king **Rajaraja Chola** (C.E. 985–1013) then ruled, and clashes had begun between the Cholas and the Western Chalukyas of Karnataka for South Indian supremacy.\n*   Realizing that resisting Western Chalukya aggression required Chola predominance in Vengi, Rajaraja strengthened political ties by giving his daughter Kundavva in marriage to Vimaladitya; with Chola help, Saktivarma re-entered Vengi and warred with Jatachoda Bhima, and these wars are recorded in the Prabhuparru and Penneru inscriptions.\n*   Jatachoda Bhima drove away Saktivarma and the Chola army up to Kanchipuram, but died near Kanchipuram itself in the battle fought there, in C.E. 999. Vengi and Kalinga then came under Saktivarma''s control.\n\n### Saktivarma (1000 – 1011 A.D.)\n\nCame to the throne in C.E. 1000 after defeating Jatachoda Bhima; Vengi and Kalinga came under his control. His brother **Vimaladitya** (C.E. 1011–1018) succeeded him.\n\n*   Both brothers were anxious to bring Vengi fully under their own control, and Chalukyan prestige began to decline as the balance of power in South Indian politics was disrupted by growing Chola dominance in Vengi (unacceptable to the Western Chalukyas).\n*   Clashes began between Chola Rajaraja (C.E. 985–1013) and the Western Chalukya ruler; Vengi became the centre for these clashes. Law and order declined completely, exposing the weakness of the Vengi Chalukyas and causing much political damage to Vengi.\n*   Saktivarma bore the title \"**Chalukya Chandrudu**\"; coins with this title have been found on the Arakan coast, indicating traders of the day travelled to eastern islands for trade.\n\n### Vimaladitya (1011 – 1018 A.D.)\n\nBecame king after Saktivarma''s death; his coronation took place in May, 1011 A.D., with titles \"rajamarthanda\" and \"mummidibhima.\" Details of his eight-year rule are sparse. Some historians hold that Karnataka forces attacked Vengi during his time; unable to resist, he fled to Cholamandalam and returned with his uncle''s support to regain the kingdom.\n\nThough a Saiva in the beginning, he later accepted **Jainism**; Desiganacharya, \"trikalayogi\" and \"siddhantadeva\" were his gurus at the time of his initiation into Jainism. He built a cave temple called Ramakonda at Ramathirtham (Visakhapatnam mandalam).\n\n### Rajaraja Narendra (1019 – 1061 A.D.)\n\nVimaladitya had two wives: **Kundavva**, daughter of Chola emperor Rajaraja, and **Melima**, princess of the Telugu Choda dynasty. Kundavva gave birth to **Rajaraja Narendra**; Melima gave birth to **Vijayaditya**.\n\n**Succession dispute and Chola intervention**\n\n*   After Vimaladitya''s death, his son Rajaraja Narendra was to assume kingship, but as the kingdom did not come under his control, his coronation was held only after four years.\n*   His step-brother **Vijayaditya** disliked Rajaraja Narendra''s accession and allied with **Jayasimha II** (the Karnataka Chalukya emperor) and **Madhukamarnava** (the Kalinga Ganga king), occupying Vengi.\n*   Rajaraja Narendra, unable to oppose them, went to Cholamandalam as his ancestors had done and sought Chola help. By then his maternal grandfather Rajaraja Chola had died, and his uncle, **Rajendrachola**, was ruling the empire. Feeling pity for his nephew''s distress and troubled by Vengi''s fall into other hands, emperor **Rajendra Chola** invaded and defeated Vijayaditya and his allies, driving them from Vengi and celebrating Rajaraja Narendra''s coronation on **16th August, C.E. 1022**.\n*   Rajendra Chola''s daughter, Ammangadevi, was Rajaraja Narendra''s wife.\n\n**Ongoing conflict**\n\n*   Many matters relating to his reign are unclear, but when the Cholas helped him, the Western Chalukyas and Kalinga (who did not tolerate Chola presence in Vengi) helped Vijayaditya instead.\n*   With Western Chalukya king **Aahavamalla Someswara** on one side and **Rajadhiraja** (who became the Chola ruler) on the other, mutual clashes intensified. Someswara conquered Vengi and Kalinga; his feudatories, such as Sobhanarasu, assumed the title \"Vengipuravareswara.\"\n*   Rajadhiraja made great, sustained efforts over more than ten years — more than ten battles were fought to destroy Chalukyan domination at Vengi. In the **Dharanikota battle**, Western Chalukya commanders-in-chief Gangadhara and Gandapayya were killed; Chola armies occupied territory up to Kollipaka.\n*   Details of the Chola–Western Chalukya wars in this period are not fully clear. When Rajadhiraja died in the **Kopparam war (C.E. 1053)**, this meant the loss of Chola protection for Rajaraja Narendra — leaving no option except to accept the supremacy of Ahavamalla Someswara.\n\n**Cultural patronage:** Just as his contemporary Bhojamaharaja patronised Sanskrit poets, Rajaraja Narendra patronised Telugu poets and won fame; his contribution to Telugu language was more remarkable even than Bhoja''s contribution to Sanskrit. **Nannaya**, the poet titled \"sabdasasana,\" adorned his court. The first two-and-a-half parvas of the _Andhra Mahabharata_ were written by Nannaya, assisted by **Narayanabhattu**.\n\n### Rajendra (Kulottunga Chola) and the End of Vengi Chalukyas (to 1075 A.D.)\n\nAfter Rajaraja Narendra''s death, his son **Rajendra** was to ascend the throne, but the details of the events at this time are unclear. His uncle (father''s step-brother) **Vijayaditya** (C.E. 1061–1075) occupied the throne, preventing Rajendra''s accession.\n\n*   Rajendra''s uncle (mother''s brother), **Rajendra Chola II**, tried to drive Vijayaditya VII from Vengi and restore Rajendra to his father''s kingdom. After Rajendra Chola II, **Virarajendra** raided several times and, at last in C.E. 1067, conquered Vengi, Kalinga and Chakrakuta.\n*   It is not clear why Rajendra was not given his father''s kingdom; instead his uncle-rival Vijayaditya was preferred and kept in Chakrakuta mandal. Afterward, in a short period when Virarajendra died, Rajendra came from Chakrakutam to Vengi, defeated Vijayaditya, and — in order to join Vengi to the Chola empire — came to war with Vijayaditya; but the latter received help from **Rajraja Devendra Varma**, the Eastern Ganga king, and **Yasahkarnadeva**, the Chedi king, and as a result, Rajendra''s army was defeated.\n*   Rajendra, in order to defeat Vijayaditya, made a plan of marrying his daughter, Rajasundari, to Devendravarma. As **Vijayaditya died in C.E. 1075**, the Vengi Chalukya dynasty came to an end. Rajendra, who had ascended the Chola throne with the title **Kulottunga Chola**, finally joined Vengi into the Chola empire.\n\n### Chola – Western Chalukya Relations in Vengi\n\nChola Rajaraja, keeping the interest of his empire in view and aiming to bring Vengi under his rule, gave shelter to Saktivarma and Vimaladitya. He gave Vimaladitya his daughter Kundavva in marriage, and made Saktivarma the king of Vengi — turning the Vengi kingdom into a Chola-protected kingdom.\n\nChola influence in Vengi became an eyesore to the Kalyani Chalukyas, who had their eye on sovereign authority over South India. With Vengi remaining the battleground between the Chola and Kalyani Chalukyas, many battles took place. Eastern Chalukya king Saktivarma turned back a Kalyani Chalukya attack with Chola help; his brother Vimaladitya married Chola princess Kundavva, and also Melima, daughter of Jatachoda Bhima.\n\nThe coronation of Rajaraja Narendra was delayed due to the conflicting efforts of the Kalinga king Madhukamarnava (desiring to free himself from Vengi Chalukya hegemony), the Kalyani Chalukya king Jayasimha (aiming to end Chola dominance), and — as a climax — Vijayaditya VII planning to occupy the Vengi throne with the help of both these rulers. Vijayaditya''s efforts to occupy Vengi with Chola help were foiled by Rajara Narendra.\n\nAs Jayasimha, the Kalyani Chalukya king, died, his son **Ahavamalla Someswara** ascended the throne. Later, when Rajendra Chola died, **Rajadhiraja** came to the Chola throne. During the time of these two rulers, Chalukya–Chola conflict reached a high pitch, and Vengi witnessed chaos. Ahavamalla foiled Chola efforts and established Kalyani Chalukyan dominance at Vengi.\n\nWith the death of Ahavamalla (C.E. 1068), internal struggle started among his sons. Afterward, Virarajendra died within two years, and in a few weeks his son Adhirajendra also died. Eastern Chalukyan king Rajendra occupied Gangaikonda Cholapuram and, with the name Kulottunga Chola, ascended the throne. After the death of **Vijayaditya VII (C.E. 1075)**, Vengi finally became joined to the Chola empire.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p6", "partName": "Vemulavada Chalukyas (750 – 973 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "Vemulavada Chalukyas were feudatories of the Rashtrakutas, ruling the north-western Telangana region with **Vemulavada** (Karimnagar district) as their capital. As feudatories of the Rashtrakutas, they ruled from C.E. 750 to 975 — around 225 years. Their rule, which started with the kindness of the Rashtrakutas, also ended with the Rashtrakutas.\n\n**Sources:** Six stone inscriptions and two copper-plate inscriptions are the main sources for their history — the Kuruvagattu stone inscription (Mahaboobnagar), the Karimnagar inscription (C.E. 946), and the Vemulavada stone inscriptions issued by Arikesari II. The Chennur stone inscription (C.E. 941) of Arikesari II records that Yuddhamalla I was opposed by the Mudigonda Chalukya king, Vijayaditya; when the latter invaded him, he sought the help of Vemulavada king Arikesari II, who not only sheltered Vijayaditya but also made him administrator of the Chennur region. The Kurkyala inscriptions (C.E. 940) of Jinavallabha and the stone inscriptions in the Karimnagar museum reveal the genealogy of the Vemulavada Chalukyas; the Kollipara copper-plate inscription of Arikesari I and the Parbhani copper plate (C.E. 966) of Arikesari III help reconstruct their history. Literary works — **Vikramarjuna Vijayam** by Pampa, and **Yasastilaka Champukavyam** by the Jain scholar Somadevasuri (also useful for religious and social conditions and political details) — are additional sources. Hindu and Jain temples built at Repaka, Korutla and Bommalagutta serve as archaeological sources. Nelatoori Venkataramanaiah undertook integrated research on the Vemulavada kings and, after gathering the available information, wrote works in English and Telugu.\n\n### Early Rulers\n\nAs per their inscriptions, the founder of the dynasty was **Satyasraya Ranavikrama (C.E. 650–675)**. His sons **Prithvipathi (C.E. 675–700)** and **Maharaju (C.E. 700–725)**, and the latter''s son **Prithvivikrama (C.E. 725–750)**, all ruled, but details of their reigns, where they ruled, and the extent of the kingdom are not clearly known.\n\n### Vinayaditya Yuddhamalla (750 – 775 A.D.)\n\nFounder of the Vemulavada Chalukyan kingdom (as an established, better-documented line). The political history of the first four rulers of this branch, up to Vinayaditya Yuddhamalla, is not clear. He is described as a great conqueror. Historians opine he was the son of Dharasraya Jayasimhavarma; some argue he was the brother of Pulakesi only.\n\n*   He joined as commander-in-chief of **Dantidurga**, founder of the Rashtrakuta empire, and participated in his wars.\n*   He is said to have ruled **Sapadalaksha** (a kingdom of one lakh twenty-five thousand villages) — no one else would have ruled such a kingdom — had all his elephants bathed in an oil tank at Podana, conquered the impregnable Chitrakuta fort, and won Dantidurga''s appreciation.\n*   Impressed by his services, Dantidurga appointed him feudatory for the areas of Bodhan (Nizamabad) and Karimnagar; he ruled with **Bodhan** as his capital.\n*   Pampa''s _Vikramarjuna Vijayam_ mentions he had elephant forces in greater numbers, and that he conquered Turkish, Yavana, Barbara, Kashmira, Kamboja, Magadha, Kalinga, Ganga, Pallava, Pandya and Kerala areas. The Kollipara copper-plate inscriptions state that the rulers of these kingdoms worshipped at his feet.\n\n### Arikesari I (775 – 800 A.D.)\n\nAssumed authority after his father Vinayaditya Yuddhamalla''s death. He was a feudatory of Rashtrakuta **Dhruva** (C.E. 780–793).\n\n*   In the war of succession between Dhruva and his brother Govinda II, Arikesari I supported Dhruva; the Kollipara copper-plate inscription informs that when Eastern Chalukya ruler Vishnuvardhana IV helped Govinda, the wars fought as a reaction to this were led by Arikesari I, and in these wars, Vishnuvardhana gave his daughter Seelamahadevi in marriage to Rashtrakuta Dhruva.\n    \n*   As a mark of victory, the Kuruvagattu inscription records that Dhruva gifted Arikesari I the areas of Nagarkurnool in Mahaboobnagar district and Ramadugu in Nalgonda district. With this eastward extension, the capital shifted from Bodhan to **Vemulavada**.\n    \n*   Arikesari I made an educational donation of Belmoga village to Mugdhasivacharya, disciple of Kalamukha Saivacharya Sadyasivacharya. His kingdom extended to the regions of Nalgonda, Karimnagar and Mahaboobnagar.\n    \n*   The Kollipara inscription records he was a great scholar with remarkable prowess in Gajatantra, archery and Ayurveda, and held titles such as \"samastalokasraya,\" \"tribhuvanamalla,\" \"rajatrinetra\" and \"sahasrarayadi.\"\n    \n*   After Arikesari I, his sons in order — **Narasimha I (C.E. 800–825)** and the latter''s son **Yuddhamalla II (C.E. 825–850)** — ruled. During their fifty-year rule, no victories or developments of note occurred.\n\n### Baddega (850 – 895 A.D.)\n\nSon of Yuddhamalla II. Held the title \"**soladaganda**,\" meaning \"a warrior who knew no defeat\" — earned through victory in **forty-two battles**.\n\n*   The Parbhani inscription (C.E. 966) records he invaded Vengi, defeated Chalukya Bhima, and took him into custody \"just like catching a crocodile in a pond.\"\n*   Eastern Chalukyan inscriptions also state that Baddega defeated Gunaga Vijayaditya. After Gunaga''s death, in the revengeful war waged by Rashtrakuta **Krishna II**, Baddega played a leading role.\n*   But Kusumayudha, of the Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty, defeated Baddega, drove him away, got Chalukya Bhima released from captivity, and enabled him to ascend the throne — so despite his title \"soladaganda,\" Baddega could not avoid this defeat.\n    \n*   He built a temple in his own name at Vemulavada, known as the Baddegeswara temple — now famous as the Vemulavada **Bheemeswara temple**.\n*   After Baddega, his son **Yuddhamalla III** ruled from C.E. 895 to 915, though details of his reign are not available. After him, his son **Narasimha II (C.E. 915–930)** brought successes back to the dynasty.\n\n### Narasimha II (915 – 930 A.D.)\n\nCame to the throne in C.E. 915 as successor to Yuddhamalla III. A warrior of great strength who won many battles; through a military expedition up to the Ganges river, he secured a place of importance in the history of the age.\n\n*   He was the feudatory of Rashtrakuta **Indra III** (C.E. 915–922), participating in Indra III''s northern Indian military expedition. On Indra III''s orders, he invaded the northern region, conquered Lata and Saptamalava, and collected tribute. He later invaded Kanyakubjam, defeated **Mahipala** (C.E. 912–944), the Gurjara Prathihara ruler, and drove him away — an attack which, made with Indra III''s help, dealt a serious blow to the Prathihara kingdom from which it did not recover.\n*   The **Vemulavada inscription** records that as Mahipala fled, Narasimha II''s chasing horses bathed in the Ganga river; on the return march, as a mark of victory, he erected a victory pillar at Kalapriyam (opined by Altekar to be on the coast of the Yamuna river).\n*   Narasimha II and Indra III were friends, relatives and contemporaries — the wife of Narasimha II happened to be the sister of Indra III; her name was **Jakavva**, and she became the mother of **Arikesari II**. Appreciating Narasimha II''s victories, Indra III is thought to have married his sister to him.\n*   During his time, Jaina \"choumukhas\" (four-faced Jain images) were carved at Vemulavada. His military abilities and leadership were responsible for his extraordinary rise — \"an ordinary leader with limited authority could never dream about such elevation.\"\n\n### Arikesari II (930 – 955 A.D.)\n\nAmong the Vemulavada Chalukya rulers, Arikesari II earned the most name — admired as a scholar, warrior, good ruler, great organiser and patron of poets.\n\n**Political role**\n\n*   War of succession erupted in the Rashtrakuta kingdom during his time: when **Amoghavarsha II** (son of Indra III) ascended the throne, he was dethroned by his younger brother **Govinda IV**, who led a life without peace due to his cruelty and immoral behaviour, causing many enemies to spring up.\n*   His uncle Baddega and Krishna (son of Baddega) prepared plans for dethroning Govinda IV; parallel wars of succession also took place in Vengi after Chalukya Bhima I.\n*   **Vijayaditya** of Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty joined Arikesari II and fought the Rashtrakutas. Under these circumstances, Baddega was invited by Arikesari II to Vemulavada; he was declared Rashtrakuta emperor, defeated Govinda IV''s armies in many battles and drove him away, and Baddega (under the name **Amoghavarsha III**) was installed on the Rashtrakuta throne.\n\n**Patronage and achievements**\n\n*   Under his patronage, the **first great work in Kannada literature, Pampa''s \"Vikramarjuna Vijayam,\"** was written during his rule. **Aditya Griham** was built at Vemulavada.\n*   He married **Revakanirmadi**, daughter of Indra III and Lokambika, another Rashtrakuta princess.\n*   His titles: pambaramkusa, ammaanagandhavarana, udattanarayana, gunanidhi, gunarnava, saranagata, vajrapanjara, priyagala, tribhuvanamalla, samantachudamani.\n*   Of his four surviving inscriptions, the **Vemulavada inscription** (in Sanskrit) is the most important, giving the genealogy of the Vemulavada Chalukyas from Vinayaditya Yuddhamalla to Arikesari II. This same inscription states that **Peddanaryudu**, his war minister, built Adityalaya at Vemulavada, coming to the temple on Sankranti day to donate 100 nivaratnas of land.\n*   **Pampa** was his court poet, and in _Vikramarjuna Vijayam_ compared Arikesari II to Arjuna. Delighted with the work, Arikesari II gave Pampa Dharmapuri (Jagityal Taluka) as an agrahara.\n*   The Baddega inscription found at Chennur (Adilabad district, C.E. 941) states his kingdom extended from Bodhan to Chennur.\n*   He had two sons: **Vegaraju** (born to Revakanirmadi), the eldest, and **Baddega II** (born to Lokambika).\n\n### Vegaraju (955 – 960 A.D.)\n\nEldest son of Arikesari II, assumed royal authority — also a feudatory of Krishna III. He ruled the kingdom with **Gangadhara** town as his capital; this was located at Yelpadi (Chittoor district). Somadevasuri, in his chempukavya, states he completed writing his work in Saka era 881 (C.E. 959) during Vegaraju''s time (during the time of Arikesari II, per the source).\n\n### Baddega II / Bhadradeva (960 – 965 A.D.)\n\nAs Vegaraju had no children, his younger brother Baddega II (Bhadradeva) came to the throne. He issued an inscription at Vemulavada, and, for the sake of Somadevasuri, built a Jaina monastery of Gauda Sangha named **subhadamajenalaya**. During his time, Bommala gattu developed into a great Jaina centre.\n\n### Arikesari III (965 – 973 A.D.) — Last of the Vemulavada Chalukyas\n\nSon of Baddega II; the **last king of the Vemulavada Chalukya dynasty**. He issued the Parbhani copper-plate inscription (966 A.D.) and the Karimnagar copper-plate inscription (946 A.D.). Vemulavada was his capital.\n\n*   He granted Repaka village (in present-day Sircilla taluka) to \"subhadamajenalayam,\" which had been built by his father for Somadevasuri''s sake. The Repaka inscription (968 A.D.) confirms a Jaina temple was built there and granted land.\n*   His titles: pambaramkusa, vidyadhara, vikramarjuna, samantachudamani.\n\n**End of the dynasty:** In **973 A.D., he destroyed the Rashtrakuta dynasty and established the Kalyani Chalukya kingdom with Tailapa II as king.** Along with the Rashtrakutas, Vemulavada Chalukya rule also came to an end in 973 A.D.\n\nVemulavada Chalukyas ruled Karimnagar and Nizamabad districts as though it was a golden age; many Hindu and Jain temples were built in these areas, and the Rajarajeswara and Baddigeswara (Bhimeswara) temples became famous. Their role in Telangana history was remarkable.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p7", "partName": "Mudigonda Chalukyas (850 – 1200 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "Mudigonda Chalukyas ruled over **Manchikondanadu in Koraviseema** (present Telangana), with **Mudigonda** (Khammam district) as their capital. The Khammam and Warangal areas came to be known as \"Koraviseema\" — the eastern part of which was called Manchikondanadu. Like the Vemulavada Chalukya kingdom, the Mudigonda kingdom lay on the borders of the Vengi and Malkhed empires and controlled all the routes entering Vengi. Mudigonda Chalukyas guarded the north-western borders of the Vengi Chalukyas and carried on their rule as trusted feudatories of the Vemulavada Chalukyas and their neighbours — the Eastern Chalukyas and Rashtrakutas.\n\n**Sources:** The Koravi copper-plate inscription (C.E. 935) and the Mogalcheruvu inscription cover the period up to the Eastern Chalukyan age; for later history, the Viriyalavari Gudoor inscription (C.E. 1124) and the Krivvaka inscription of Kusumayudha are the main sources. As these inscriptions have no dates and their information is inadequate, there was limited opportunity to know their full history.\n\n### Ranamardhudu — Founder\n\nFounder of the Mudigonda Chalukyan dynasty, possibly belonging to the Western Chalukyan family line. He acquired Koravi mandalam with the help of the Eastern Chalukyas and ruled from Mudigonda.\n\n### Kusumayudhudu / Kusumayudha I (C.E. 870 – 895)\n\nSon of Ranamardhudu (per the Koravi inscription). He was contemporary with Gunaga Vijayaditya and Chalukya Bhima, and played a leading role in the Vengi Chalukya–Rashtrakuta conflict of his time.\n\n*   After the death of Gunaga Vijayaditya, Rashtrakuta Krishna II, in a revengeful attitude, first occupied the Koravi region and then led an expedition against the Vengi Chalukyas; in response, Kusumayudha attacked the Rashtrakutas, freed Chalukya Bhima from Krishna II''s captivity, installed him on the throne, drove the Rashtrakutas from Koraviseema, and skilfully got the empire protected, and with the empire being handed back to Chalukya Bhima, Kusumayudha earned respect in several ways.\n    \n\n### Gonagaiah (895 – 910 A.D.)\n\nKusumayudha had two sons, Gonagaiah and Niravadya; out of regard for Gunaga Vijayaditya, one son was named \"Gonagaiah\" (the short form of Gunaga Vijayaditya). Gonagaiah was also called Karigonaga (\"black Gonaga\"). After Kusumayudha, Gonagaiah ascended the throne. Because of internal conflicts that arose in Vengi after Chalukya Bhima, Gonagaiah lost the affection of the Vengi Chalukyas.\n\nTaking advantage of these developments, his brother Niravadya (C.E. 910–935) removed Gonagaiah and himself ascended the Mudigonda Chalukyan throne, as recorded by the Koravi inscription; having lost the kingdom, Gonagaiah sought the help of Arikesari II (C.E. 930–955), and with his help, dethroned Niravadya and got the Mudigonda kingdom back. In the later period, only the descendants of Gonagaiah ruled over the Mudigonda kingdom.\n\n### The Emergence of the Kakatiyas and Mudigonda–Kakatiya Conflict\n\nIn this period, the **Kakatiya kingdom emerged**. Perhaps with the idea of keeping the Mudigonda Chalukyas in check, Rashtrakuta king Krishna II appointed his commander-in-chief **Yeriya** as administrator over **Kurravadi** (only part of the Koravi kingdom). Gundaya, the father of Yeriya, was a Rashtrakuta, referred to in the Machilipatnam inscription of Chalukya Bhima as Dandeva Gundana. Perhaps Yeriya made **Orugallu (present Warangal)** his capital — with this, the Kakatiya dynasty was installed.\n\n*   From this point, **Kakatiya–Mudigonda Chalukya conflict** began; the incidents of the first stage are not clearly known, but because of the rise of the Kakatiyas, the Chalukyas appear to have been forced temporarily to leave Koravi.\n*   Yeriya''s son was **Betiya**, and Betiya''s son was **Kakartiya Gundana**. It appears Betiya was driven away and the Mudigonda Chalukyas reoccupied Koraviseema. But, during their period, in the conflict that occurred for the Chalukyan throne (Vengi), **Danarnava** killed his younger brother Ammarajamahendra and ascended the Vengi throne. For helping in this, Danarnava made Kakartya Gundana ruler of **Natavadi**, and tried to send the Mudigonda Chalukyas to the Renati region.\n*   In the meantime, political conditions changed in both the Vengi and Rashtrakuta kingdoms: in Vengi, Danarnava was removed by **Jatachoda Bhima**; and in the Rashtrakuta kingdom, **Tailapa II** (Kalyani Chalukyas) removed the Rashtrakuta dynasty. In this situation, Kakartya Gundana invaded Koraviseema, defeated the Mudigonda Chalukyas, and reoccupied **Kurravadi kingdom**, which had been lost by his father Betiya.\n*   The Mudigonda Chalukyas, having lost their kingdom, sought Tailapa II''s help. As both were Chalukyan families, and the Kakatiyas had been their enemy commanders-in-chief, Tailapa II decided to remove the Kakatiyas and restore Koraviseema to the Mudigonda Chalukyas. Tailapa II entrusted this task to his commander, **Viriyala Yerrabhupati**, who defeated Kakartya Gundana, killed him, and — as stated in the Gudoor inscription (C.E. 1124) — installed **Bottu Betadu** as king in Koraviseema. According to the Krivvaka inscription, **Bottu Bethadu (C.E. 1125–50)**, installed by Yerrabhupati, is learnt to be of Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty. Either way, based on these two inscriptions, Bottu Bethadu of the Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty again became king of Koraviseema.\n\n**Later succession:** Due to Kakartya Gundana''s death, his son **Garuda Betaraju**, being a minor, lost the kingdom and became helpless. Seeing the minor child, **Kamasani**, wife of Yerrabhupati, took pity on him, sought Tailapa''s help, and could get the kingdom back to Betaraju. Tailapa gave the **Hanumakonda** area to Garuda Betaraju. As a result of this arrangement, the Kakatiyas in Warangal and the Mudigonda Chalukyas in Koraviseema got stabilised, and both became feudatories of Tailapa II.\n\n*   The **Palampeta inscription** informs that **Kakati Rudra** invaded the Mudigonda Chalukyas (feudatories of the Kalyani Chalukyas) and drove them away. In the later period, after the death of **Rudradeva**, the Mudigonda Chalukyas reoccupied Koraviseema.\n\n### Kusumayudha VI (1150 – 1175 A.D.) — Last of the Mudigonda Chalukyas\n\nLearnt from the Krivvaka inscription to be the **last ruler among the Mudigonda Chalukyas**. During the time of his younger brother, **Nagatiraju (C.E. 1175–1200)**, **Ganapatideva** (Kakatiya kingdom) invaded the Mudigonda Chalukyan kingdom, defeated him and drove him away.\n\nWith this, the Mudigonda Chalukyan kingdom became part of the Kakatiya empire; it is learnt that the last Mudigonda Chalukyas, having lost the kingdom, went to Vengi, stayed there for some time, and died — and with their death, the Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty, it is learnt, has ended.\n\n### Mudigonda Chalukya Genealogy\n\n| Ruler | Period |\n|---|---|\n| Ranamardha | 850 – 870 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha I | 870 – 895 A.D. |\n| Gonagaiah | 895 – 910 A.D. |\n| Niravadya | 910 – 935 A.D. |\n| Baddega | 935 – 941 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha II | 935 – 960 A.D. |\n| Vijayaditya II | 960 – 980 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha III | 980 – 1000 A.D. |\n| Gonaga | 1000 – 1025 A.D. |\n| Nijjayaraja Mallappa | 1025 – 1050 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha IV | 1050 – 1075 A.D. |\n| Betaraju | 1075 – 1100 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha V | 1100 – 1125 A.D. |\n| Bottu Beta | 1125 – 1150 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha VI | 1150 – 1175 A.D. |\n| Nagatiraju | 1175 – 1200 A.D. |\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p8", "partName": "Kalyani Chalukyas (973 – 1157 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "Among the great dynasties that ruled South India and exercised influence over Andhradesa, the Kalyani Chalukyas deserve mention. Though the language of their inscriptions is Kannada and the area they mostly ruled was Karnataka, the works _Prabhanda Chintamani_ and _Rasamalaprabandham_, written by **Meruthunga Charyulu**, state that they belong to Telangana. Kalyani Chalukyas are also called **Western Chalukyas**, and belong to the Badami Chalukya dynastic line.\n\nThe Rashtrakuta empire, established after overthrowing the Badami Chalukya dynasty, continued for two and a half centuries and suffered decline at the hands of **Tailapa II**, who belonged to the Kalyani Chalukyan dynasty. The first capital of the Kalyani Chalukyas was **Manyakheta**; later they made **Kalyani** their capital and ruled from there for around 200 years. Their rule was established by Tailapa II.\n\n### Tailapa II (973 – 997 A.D.)\n\nBy 957 A.D. he was a feudatory of the Rashtrakutas, belonging to the dynasty of **Satyasraya Bhimarasu**, who ruled the Hanumakonda area as a Rashtrakuta feudatory. He was the son of Vikramaditya IV and Bontadevi. As Bontadevi was a Chedi princess, Tailapa may have secured Chedi help in overthrowing the Rashtrakutas. Some opine he belonged to the dynasty of Bhimarasu, uncle (father''s brother) of the last Badami Chalukya ruler Keertivarma II — from the title \"mahamandaleswara\" of Tailapa II, it appears he was a feudatory of the Rashtrakutas.\n\n*   By defeating **Karka II**, the last Rashtrakuta king, in **C.E. 973**, the sovereignty of the Kalyani Chalukyas was established. He had no titles until C.E. 957; by C.E. 965, he assumed the titles \"mahasamantadhipa,\" \"chalukyarama,\" \"satyasrayakula tilaka,\" \"ahavamalla,\" \"prithvi vallabha.\"\n*   Chola emperor Rajaraja was his contemporary. With Hanumakonda as capital and ruling over Western Telangana as the ancestral area — and as a feudatory under the Rashtrakutas, as known from inscriptions — the works _Prabhanda Chintamani_ and _Rasamala Prabhandam_ claim he was the head of Telangana. **Jakavva**, wife of Tailapa II, was the daughter of Karka II, the Rashtrakuta king.\n\n**Wars**\n\n*   In the north, **Munjaraja**, the Paramara king, was his enemy and invaded him sixteen times as a rival. Once Tailapa imprisoned Munjaraja, who escaped and fled; the last time, in C.E. 995, when Munjaraju crossed the Godavari, **Yadava Bhillama**, Tailapa''s feudatory, attacked and killed him in battle. Though Tailapa had imprisoned Munjaraja earlier, Mrunalavati, the widowed sister of Tailapa, loved Munjaraja and helped him escape; he was caught and killed, \"as ''prabhanda chintamani'' informs in a terrible manner.\"\n*   In the south, Tailapa II could not escape trouble from the Cholas — described in inscriptions as a \"devastating fire\" in respect of the Cholas, suggesting he fought several battles with them; enmity developed between Tailapa and the Cholas. When Rajaraja Chola occupied Mysore and began acting aggressively, **Tailapa in C.E. 992 defeated them and took away their elephant force as a gift.** He also defeated the Paramaras and Cholas, and conquered Lata, Ghurjara and Pallikota areas; the Banavasi area was joined to his kingdom.\n*   **Badaparaja**, of the Chalukya dynasty, was his commander-in-chief. **Ranna**, one of the \"kaviratnatrayam\" poets in Kannada literature, flourished in Tailapa''s court.\n\n### Satyasraya (997 – 1008 A.D.)\n\nEldest son of Tailapa II; bore the titles \"irivabendaga\" and \"akalamka charita.\" He ascended the Kalyani Chalukya throne in 997 A.D. As crown prince, remaining as his father''s right hand, he helped him in many battles — his title \"irivabendaga\" suggests that, in battle, he used to pierce himself toward the enemy army.\n\n*   During his reign, enmity with the Cholas became serious: Satyasraya did not tolerate the Cholas coming closer to Vengi, giving protection to **Saktivarma and Vimaladitya**, the two sons of Danarnava. Chola Rajaraja invaded Karnataka and conquered Gangavadi and Nolambavadi; as a result, his entire time was spent fighting continuous wars against the Cholas.\n*   His daughter Pampadevi was given in marriage to Pallava king Irivanolambadhi. To protect Vengi from Chalukyan armies, Rajaraja sent one army to Vengi to help Saktivarma, and another under his son Rajendra Chola on an expedition against Rattapadi.\n*   The **Mattur inscription (C.E. 1007)** informs that with a huge, fully-armed force stationed near Donapoor, people were butchered without discriminating women, children, the aged and brahmins, and the kingdom was looted. Chola inscriptions record that Banavasi and Kollipaka were conquered; the Chalukyan armies from Vengi were attacked again at Kollipaka by the Chola armies and were defeated — Satyasraya got subjected to some humiliation.\n*   **Vimalachandra**, a Jain scholar, was guru to Satyasraya.\n\n### Vikramaditya V (1008 – 1018 A.D.)\n\nAs Satyasraya had no sons, and his brother Dasavarna had died earlier, the younger brother''s son, Vikramaditya V, assumed royal authority in C.E. 1008 and ruled up to C.E. 1018, with the title \"tribhuvanamalla.\"\n\n*   Though **Nolambhadiraju**, the son-in-law of Satyasraya, accepted his supremacy, **Kundamarasu**, who was looking after Banavasi affairs, opposed him. As Kundamarasu described himself as \"death to devour the Cholas,\" it is assumed he also fought against the Cholas.\n*   Vikramaditya V was liberal and made donations; he had a sister named Akkadevi.\n\n### Jayasimha II (1018 – 1042 A.D.)\n\nAfter Vikramaditya V, his younger brother Ayyana became king but died before coronation could take place; his younger brother **Jayasimha II** ascended the throne. By the time his rule commenced, the kingdom was in difficulties.\n\n*   To avenge the humiliation caused to Munjaraja, Bhojaraja invaded the Chalukyan kingdom and occupied parts of it; inscriptions record that Bhoja conquered Karnataka, Lata and Konkan areas and held victory celebrations. But, contrary to Paramara inscriptions, Chalukyan inscriptions state that Jayasimha defeated the Paramaras and occupied the Northern areas of Kolhapur.\n    \n*   His commander-in-chief, **Chavanarasu**, also held the title \"Konkana dhoomaketa.\" Around C.E. 1020–24, conflicts between the Chalukya and Paramara dynasties reached an intensive form; Jayasimha personally led an expedition against the Paramara king. Chalukyan inscriptions state Saptamalava was conquered.\n    \n*   Jayasimha also faced war from the Cholas; Nolambavadi and Bellari areas were subjected to Chola attack, but inscriptions inform that all these areas were reoccupied. **Rajendra Chola defeated Jayasimha in the battle at Maski** and by C.E. 1026, occupied Rattapadi areas. As a result, the Chola war became serious; some say he began construction of the Kalyana fort in C.E. 1028.\n*   Jayasimha II had two wives — **Devalamahadevi** and **Suggaladevi** — who together, in C.E. 1029, donated Puligera area to Brahmarasi Pandita, a Pasupathacharya. Though he followed Jain religious principles in the beginning, he accepted **Kalamukha Saivism** due to the encouragement of these queens.\n*   His kingdom extended over the districts of Cuddapah and Anantapur in Rayalaseema, and the entire Telangana region. His titles: \"jagadekamalla,\" \"trailokamalla,\" \"vikramasimha.\"\n\n### Someswara I (1042 – 1068 A.D.)\n\nAfter Jayasimha II, his son Someswara I assumed power in C.E. 1042 and ruled up to C.E. 1068, with the titles \"aahavamalla,\" \"rajanarayana\" and \"trailokamalla.\" He was the greatest among the Kalyani Chalukyas.\n\n*   **Bilhana**, in his _Vikramankadeva Charita_, mentions that Someswara built the **Kalyani city**, made it his capital and ruled from it. His chief queen, **Mylaladevi**, visited Srisailam in C.E. 1059 and donated gifts to Sureswara, a Jain scholar who was a Kalamukha Saiva guru.\n*   Nearly twelve fierce battles were fought with the Cholas even during his reign. By the time he assumed authority, he used to look after the affairs of the Potlakere region.\n*   After Someswara I ascended the throne, as Vijayaditya made one more effort to occupy Vengi, his help was sought; thinking Chola authority at Vengi could be removed, his army was sent. With this army, Vijayaditya defeated Rajaraja Narendra, drove him out, and occupied Vengi.\n*   His successful expedition went through Vengi, Kalinga, Panchala, Magadha, Malwa and Kerala kingdoms. Wars with Paramara rulers that had begun earlier continued during his time; the rulers of Konkan, Sevuna and Vindhya regions supported the Paramaras. His commander-in-chief **Nagavarma** participated in many wars, fought ably and achieved victories.\n*   **Kakatiyas came into prominence during the time of Someswara I.** In the expedition Someswara made on Konkan and Chakrakuta, **Prolaraju I** of the Kakatiya dynasty participated, and appreciating his services, was given control over Hanumakonda and honoured. Prola''s son, **Betaraju**, along with Someswara, participated in the wars fought with the Paramaras and Cholas.\n*   Because of Rajaraja''s statecraft, Chola influence in Vengi politics increased, leading to battles with the Western Chalukyas. After Rajaraja Narendra (C.E. 1022–61) came to the Vengi throne, Chola dominance in Vengi increased; conditions worsened, and internal conflicts took the stage. Vijayaditya, the half-brother of Rajaraja Narendra training to capture kingship, provided an opportunity for Western Chalukya entry into Vengi politics.\n\n**Continued conflict and death**\n\n*   Someswara I fought many battles with the Cholas while helping Vijayaditya, who resisted Rajarajanarendra and carried on his rule with Chola help and per Rajarajanarendra''s whims. The **Kalidindi inscription of Rajaraja Narendra** informs that three leading Chola commanders-in-chief were killed at the hands of Western Chalukyan armies. Even during Someswara''s time, battles were fought fiercely; his commanders-in-chief Gandapaiah and Gangadhara got killed in war at the hands of the Cholas, and Vijayaditya, Vikramaditya, Madhusudana, Ganapati and Angamaiah (commander-in-chief) all had to flee. With the Cholas'' rise, the Western Chalukyas suffered huge losses and several cities were destroyed.\n*   In the **Kopparam battle of C.E. 1053–54**, the Western Chalukyas killed Chola **Rajadhiraja**; Rajaraja Narendra, receiving no help from the Cholas under these circumstances, had no alternative except to accept the sovereignty of the Western Chalukyas.\n*   Someswara, who fought many battles — the Kanchi invasion (C.E. 1049), the Kopparam war (C.E. 1053–54), a raid on Vengi (C.E. 1061), and the Mudakkara battle (C.E. 1062) — had parts of his kingdom apportioned: his wife Mylaladevi was appointed supervisor over Banavasi region, son **Someswara II** was appointed over Belvala and Puligere areas, and another son **Vikramaditya VI** was appointed supervisor over Gangavadi region.\n*   The life of Someswara, who achieved many victories, ended tragically. In his last days, he was defeated in two wars fought at Kudali Sangamam. He asked the Cholas to come for battle the next day, but when the Chola armies arrived at the appointed time, Someswara did not come to the field. As he failed to appear, **Virarajendra occupied the kingdom**; unable to bear this final humiliation, and overtaken by a serious illness, **Someswara I, on 28th March C.E. 1068, went with a musical instrument to the Tungabhadra river and committed suicide by jumping into it.** Many inscriptions record his death, but Someswara I is remembered as the one ruler who got involved in incessant fighting with four emperors.\n\n### Someswara II (1068 – 1076 A.D.)\n\nAfter Someswara I, his son Someswara II carried on the rule from C.E. 1068 to 1076, with the title \"**bhuvanaikamalla**.\"\n\n*   Soon after his accession, Chola–Chalukya conflict started again. His brothers **Jayasimha** and **Vikramaditya VI** occupied Nolambavadi and Banavasi areas and rebelled against him. Chola **Virarajendra** invaded the Chalukyan kingdom; the Chalukyan cavalry force defeated the Chola armies and saved the Gutti fort.\n*   **Cause → Effect:** When Someswara kept his younger brother Vikramaditya VI in charge of protecting Gangavadi, the latter — having an eye on the Chalukyan throne — rebelled against his elder brother, defeated him, and, as stated in Bilhana''s _Vikramankadeva Charita_, occupied the kingdom.\n\n### Vikramaditya VI (1076 – 1126 A.D.)\n\nYounger brother of Someswara II and son of Someswara I; got coronated at Kalyani and ruled from C.E. 1076 to 1126 — considered a great king among the Western Chalukyas. His court poet **Bilhana''s** _Vikramankadeva Charita_ gives details of his reign.\n\n*   In the very first year of his reign, the **Salivahana era in vogue until then was changed and the Vikrama era was started in its place** — thus the **Chalukya Vikrama era** commenced from C.E. 1076.\n*   Though Kalyani remained his capital, to improve the popular system of administration, different towns such as Vijayapuram and Vikramapuram were developed as important sub-category towns. Frequently moving about in these towns, he maintained peace and had high popular respect; administration attracted popular favour.\n*   When his younger brother Jayasimha tried to rebel in C.E. 1080, it was suppressed. Aiming to occupy areas of the kingdom, the administrator of Vengi was defeated.\n*   During his time, **Vishnuvardhana** of the Hoyasala dynasty occupied some parts of the kingdom, passing through Uchchangi and Belvala areas; the Hoyasalas occupied territory up to the river Krishna. Taking advantage of this, administrators of other feudatory kingdoms tried to rebel.\n*   During his time, poets **Bilhana and Vijnaneswara** were patronised. Historians state that during his reign there was progress in all fields. He held the title \"tribhuvanamalla.\"\n\n### Someswara III (1126 – 1138 A.D.)\n\nAfter Vikramaditya VI, his son Someswara III ruled the kingdom from C.E. 1126 to 1138. He wrote the work _Abhilashitardhachintamani_, described as \"vijnanasarvasvam\" (encyclopedia), also called \"manasollasam.\"\n\nSoon after becoming king, he took Banavasi and Gangavadi regions from the feudatories into his own control. But during his time only, the Vengi kingdom defied the supremacy of the Kalyani Chalukyas.\n\n### Jagadekamalla (1138 – 1151 A.D.)\n\nDuring his time, the Hoyasalas rose in power. He was the son of Someswara III. After Jagadekamalla, his younger brother **Tailapa III (C.E. 1151–57)** assumed authority.\n\n### Decline and End of the Kalyani Chalukyas\n\nTailapa III''s reign period was filled with wars against feudatory kings. By that time, **Kalachuri Bijjala**, discharging the duties of a minister, had grown stronger and secured the cooperation of feudatory kingdoms.\n\n*   The minister of Bijjala was **Basaveswara**, the famous religious reformer; Bijjala favoured Jainism.\n*   **In C.E. 1157, Kalchuri Bijjala drove away Tailapa III**, who left the capital and fled.\n*   When the son of Tailapa III, **Someswara IV**, came to the capital Kalyani in C.E. 1183, **Ballala III**, the Hoyasala ruler, defeated him and drove him away. Someswara, for some time, lived in exile, and died at last in **C.E. 1200**.\n\n**With this, the Kalyani Chalukyan dynasty has ended.**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p9", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Administrative System", "bodyMd": "In the Chalukyan age, Coastal Andhra remained under Vengi, and integrated Western Andhra feudatory dynasties used to rule. These feudatories, while cooperating with their sovereigns in waging wars, began to rule their kingdoms independently; their courts resembled those of the emperor, and they maintained councils of ministers.\n\nRejecting considerations of country, caste and family, and becoming selfish, feudatories fought among themselves for the throne; among them, Bhimasaluki and Yuddhamalla did not hesitate even to mortgage the country''s independence. At that time armies roamed about freely; Vengi, Dharanikota, Nellore and Khammam got destroyed many times, and many villages were burnt — \"there is no exaggeration in stating that the entire country got turned into a big war camp.\"\n\nFrom another angle, some kingdoms flourished in a golden age (the Vemulavada Chalukyas). The administrative, economic and religious systems, social-life policy, architecture and sculpture, language and literature, and patronage of fine arts that developed during Chalukyan rule (sixth to twelfth century) in South India remained as **models for later dynasties** — the impact of these varied developments spread deeply into people''s lives and progress.\n\n### Emperor, Power, Status\n\nChalukyan government was a **hereditary monarchy**; the king was all in all. Kingly authority consisted of seven items (_saptanga_): king, minister, kingdom, treasury, army, friend and fort. In running the administration, the king was assisted by the **yuvaraja** (crown prince), also called **Uparaja**; kings without sons used to appoint their brothers or their brothers'' sons as yuvaraja.\n\nChalukyan kings used titles such as \"parameswara,\" \"paramabhattaraka,\" \"parama maheswara,\" \"prithvivallabha,\" \"Bhuvanasraya,\" \"Rajadhiraja\" and \"rajaparameswara.\" Though the emperor was all-powerful, custom required him to rule according to the **dharmasastras**. Though a gap existed in this age between the king and the people, the duties of the emperor consisted of:\n\n*   Protecting the people\n*   Solving disputes among the people and delivering justice\n*   Maintaining law and order in the country\n*   Saving the kingdom from external invasions\n\nFor discharging these duties, the emperor arranged the **mantrimandali** (council of ministers).\n\n### Council of Ministers\n\nFor helping the emperor in administration, the council of ministers was formed. It included those who knew the dharmasastras, the commanders-in-chief of the army, and important relatives of the king. The **minister, commander-in-chief, treasurer, purohit and yuvaraja** used to advise the king in administration.\n\n*   These posts were generally held by men who observed dharma, could be trusted, were of good character, and were brahmins well versed in the knowledge of dharmasastras. They held these posts **hereditarily**.\n*   During the Chalukyan times, these posts were gradually also held by Sudras. It is also learnt that these posts were held, as per ancient moral codes, by the \"eighteen tirthas\" of Sapthangas.\n*   In the Vemulavadi inscription of Arikesari II, employees known as \"mahasandhivigraha,\" \"tantrapala\" and \"Satradipala\" are mentioned. Employees referred to as \"niyogadikrutha\" and \"niyogavallabha\" perhaps served as supervisors of departments. It could be said that the **bahattara (72) niyogadhipaties** found in Kakatiya inscriptions might have been the descendants of these earlier officials. These employees received land grants as honorarium for their services.\n\n### Local Government\n\nChalukyan administration to some extent resembled that of the Cholas.\n\n**Administrative divisions:**\n\n| Unit | Head |\n|---|---|\n| Rashtra (province) | Rashtradhipati |\n| Vishaya (also called Nadu) | Vishyadhipati |\n| Village | Gramani |\n\n*   For administrative convenience, the empire was divided into provinces, and provinces were divided into **vishayas** (also called _nadus_): Kammanadu, Prolanadu, Attilinadu and Venginadu were among the names of local government units. In Rayalaseema, names like Seemanadu are found; in Southern Andhra, rashtras were called \"Kottalu\" — mentioned in the inscriptions of Pulinadu, located as Perumbagapadi.\n*   It is learnt from inscriptions that there were **thirty vishayas in Venginadu**.\n*   Local government was, to a great extent, in the hands of feudatory kings. In **Yellamanchilli, Pithapuram and Mudigonda**, only kings of Chalukyan dynasties assumed authority; besides them, the **Pattavardhani, Durjaya and Haihayadi** feudatory dynasties also rose.\n*   **Village was the foundation of the administrative system.** A Grama sabha used to be there in every village; the village system attained a stable shape during the time of the Eastern Chalukyas. **Gramikas** were the important village officers during the time of the Rashtrakutas.\n*   The Machilipatnam inscription of Chalukya Bhima II informs that village administration was carried out through committees; important decisions in village affairs used to be taken in the presence of village officers. Even emperors, when making land grants in villages, used to take their permission. Grama sabhas existed in Renadu; during their time, **Karanams** used to maintain village accounts. It is learnt through inscriptions that in Kunganoor, a person named Adavallam was appointed Karanam by the Grama sabha.\n\n### Kingdom''s Income and Expenditure\n\n**Sources of income**\n\n*   Tribute paid by feudatories to the kings was the main source of income for the Chalukyan government.\n*   Some villages decided a yearly tax for the entire village rather than individual taxes — a method known as **\"gramvar.\"**\n*   Along with land tax, several other kinds of taxes existed; taxes could be paid either in cash or in kind. **Land tax amounted to 1/6th of the produce.** The word \"sarvakarapariharam,\" used at the time of granting agraharas, indicates the wider tax net.\n*   During the reign of **Trinetrudu**, king of Vaidumba, in Renadu, it is recorded that taxes on farmers such as _degaracha_ tax, _padevalu pannu_, _padiyeri pannu_ and _Sandhivigraha_ tax were removed.\n*   Taxes such as _Kallavakkam, Vidanali Vishaya Sunkam, biradayam_ and _chittavatam_ are seen in Rayalaseema inscriptions.\n*   The **Koravi inscription** issued by Niravadya, the Mudigonda Chalukyan king (C.E. 935), records that in Telangana, taxes were collected by kings from village leaders as a fixed or contractual amount; fines levied on criminals and other miscellaneous taxes could be collected by the village leader.\n*   The **Vaidumba \"Irugaya maharaju\" inscription** (Vayalpadu Taluka, Chittoor district) records that village leaders such as _rattodi_ and _rattagudi_ could pay the king taxes such as _dogaraja_ tax, _padevali_ tax, _padiyeri_ tax and _sandhivigraha_ tax, while other miscellaneous taxes could be taken by the leaders themselves — _dogaraja_ tax was for the maintenance of the yuvaraja; _padevali_ tax was collected for maintaining the army; _sandhivigraha_ tax was for protecting the kingdom from enemy attack.\n*   The Chevuru copper inscription of Eastern Chalukyan ruler Ammaraja I (C.E. 935) informs that, while appointing a village official for Mumikili village, it was laid down that eight _gadyanas_ paid for the endowment of the _gramani_ had to be paid to the king only, and the remaining miscellaneous taxes need not be paid by the gramani.\n*   Employees decided the additional tax to be paid during the crop season, depending on the produce; **Rachakaranas** and **Rachasettis** collected this. Professional taxes were also levied — potters, washermen, barbers, goldsmiths and blacksmiths had to pay professional taxes. Chalukyas also received revenue from taxes on water-drawing machines, house tax, oil-mill tax, cattle tax, sheep tax, and market tax.\n\n**Expenditure**\n\n*   Chalukyan kings spent income from taxes on the army, royal family maintenance, salaries and allowances of employees, welfare activities, and charities and philanthropy.\n*   It is learnt from Sukra''s _Neetisaram_ that a king always had to keep **1/6th** of income in the treasury; of the remaining income, **half was to be spent on the army**, **1/12th on royal family expenses**, **another 1/12th on salaries and allowances of employees**, **another 1/12th on people''s welfare**, and the **last 1/12th on charities and philanthropy**.\n*   Public welfare activities of the time included: construction and maintenance of temples, patronage of poets/scholars/artists, development of educational activities, construction and maintenance of irrigation tanks, donations and charities, performance of sacrifices and rituals, and donations to religious institutions and mattahas.\n\n### Judicial Administration\n\nClear information is not known about the judicial organisation followed by the Chalukyan rulers, though some inscriptions give references.\n\n*   **The king remained the chief justice of the kingdom.** Judgments were given by \"rashtradhipati\" in provinces and \"vishayapati\" in vishayas; in villages, Grama sabhas gave judgments. Ordinary disputes were decided entirely in gramasabhas.\n*   There were special judges called **\"pradvivakas.\"** When disputes occurred between villages over land borders or use of water from a river, kings had them resolved through the pradvivakas, their ministers, or caste elders. These courts followed principles recorded in ancient Smriti works such as the **Yajnavalkya Smriti**.\n*   Judgments delivered by these courts were issued under the king''s emblem and in the name of \"jayapatras.\" Special officers called **\"mudravartulas\"** were responsible for stamping the emblem.\n*   The **Koravi copper-plate inscription (C.E. 935)**, considered the first Telugu inscription, gives details of the criminal investigation procedure followed under the Chalukyas. **\"Mitakshara\" of Vijnaneswara** and **\"Neetikavyamruta\" of Somadevasuri** are referred to as the dharmasastra works of the age. **\"Smritisangraha,\"** otherwise known as \"Saraswathi Vilasam,\" written by \"Prataparudragajapati\" (a contemporary of the Yelamanchili Chalukya family), also informs about the justice and judicial codes of the Chalukyan times.\n*   Only kings had the right to protect dharma and punish criminals; though the king was at the apex of the judicial system, in many parts of the kingdom he established courts and appointed judges. According to the Kollipara copper-plate inscription, **Arikesari I**, as a judge, established a court named **\"dharma sabha.\"** According to \"Saraswati Vilasam,\" members of the dharmasabha, numbering 3, 5 or 7, knowing the Vedas and judicial codes, were ordained to be truthful and impartial. Membership was denied to those with no knowledge of the kingdom''s traditions and sastras, and to those known for cruelty.\n*   Even guilds had special courts. As stated in \"Vaisya purana,\" opposing the wrong decisions of king \"Rajamahendravara,\" the vaisyas of 18 towns got organised into a **\"vaisya sangha.\"**\n*   Responsibility for preventing village thefts belonged to a village official called **Talari** — the leading servant of the village, also called village Police, pattilaka, pattera and Patel. His duty was to catch the thief and hand him over to the concerned legal authority; if he failed to catch the thief, he had to pay the cost of the stolen articles himself. This method was known as **\"atchukavali.\"**\n*   During Chalukyan times, **death sentence was the highest punishment**. At some places, though the intensity of punishment to brahmins was lessened somewhat, they never escaped punishment entirely. Depending on the degree of offence, punishments included: cutting of hands and legs, cutting of ears and nose, cutting the fore-part of the fingers of hands and legs, and burning the body in flames.\n*   If a court employee committed a wrong, he was removed from post and his sources of income lessened — such an incident occurred in Pabubarru village, donated by Saktivarma, when a gramani who failed to obey the king''s orders was removed from post. Basing on this, the Mudigonda and Vengi Chalukyas followed the codes of Saktivarma: if a person killed another, **120 drammas** was levied as penalty; if a person caused physical harm to another, **60 drammas** was imposed. If the modesty of a lady was attacked, a fine of **25 drammas** was imposed. As per the Srimadhavigeya maharaja inscription of Vaidumba king Bhuvanatrinetra, in cases of sexual atrocities, punishment was imposed on both men and women — males were fined **64 gadyanas**, and for women, **the nose used to be cut**. On the whole, punishments remained hard.\n\n### Military Organisation\n\nChalukyan emperors used to personally lead their armies during wars, even while maintaining well-trained commanders-in-chief. **Kshatriyas** became famous as soldiers of courage and bravery. Because of constant Chalukya–Pallava–Chola–Rashtrakuta–Kalyani Chalukya conflicts, the need for soldiers increased, and persons of courage and bravery from **all castes** were taken and appointed as soldiers and commanders-in-chief.\n\n*   Because of wars, a greater portion of the income received (from taxes and war loot) was spent on army maintenance. Chalukyan feudatory chiefs used to come, as and when needed, with their own armies to wage wars for the protection of the Chalukyan kingdom — hence it was said that the **main strength of the Eastern Chalukyan kingdom lay in the forces of their feudatory chiefs**.\n*   Foot soldiers, elephant force and naval force were the main organs of the army. The **Aihole inscription of Pulakesi II** gives details of **six types of army organisations**:\n    1.  **Mula** — the hereditary force\n    2.  **Bhritya** — those who accompanied soldiers\n    3.  **Sreni** — guild-based feudatory forces\n    4.  **Mitra** — armies of friendly kingdoms\n    5.  **Atavika** — forces of forest-based tribes\n    6.  **Amitra** — captured forces\n*   Army employees were called **\"mahabaladhikruta\"** (Great Commander-in-Chief). Forts were under the control of the **\"durgadhipathi,\"** who bore full responsibility for the protection of the fort — some army units were under his control. For army maintenance, either the wealth of the emperor or the income from villages was allotted.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p10", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Economic Development", "bodyMd": "### Agriculture, Irrigation, Crops, Land Revenue, Other Taxes\n\nDuring the Chalukyan period, people''s dependence on land and agriculture increased — a development that had begun after the Satavahanas. Brahmins, associated with temple service, were given lands and agraharas and turned into landlords; Sudras cultivated their lands and remained loyal to them. As pressure on land grew, expansion of agriculture became inevitable, and uncultivated and forest lands were converted into crop lands.\n\n*   For tax purposes, agricultural lands were divided into different types: **low-lying/wet lands, dry lands, sandy lands (garuvu) and garden lands.** Land tax was fixed depending on water facility, land fertility and crop yield.\n*   Chalukyan-era inscriptions reference commercial crops such as coconut, betel leaf, betel nut, mango, sugarcane, cotton, grams and pulses.\n\n**Irrigation**\n\n*   Emperors took steps to construct tanks and wells, and dig canals to pool water resources for agriculture. Rich people, feudatories and wealthy farmers helped construct and maintain tanks.\n*   The **Chaita tank** and **Bhimasamudram** tank, mentioned in the Tadikonda inscription of Ammaraju II, still supply water to cultivated lands today.\n*   A Western Chalukya inscription mentions a big tank called **Ananta Sarovaram** in Chebrolu (Guntur district). A Mahaboobnagar district inscription mentions a tank called **Bhimasamudram**. A big tank existed in Nalgonda district, under which 12 nivartanas of land were donated to brahmins. In Medak district, a tank known as **\"madivojanakare\"** existed. Feudatories of Kullottunga Rajendra II dug the **Polinayaka** and **Potta** tanks.\n*   For those who brought new lands under cultivation, tax exemption was given in the beginning for some years, encouraging agriculture. Garden lands and light-soil lands were donated to temples — e.g., for _akhanda deepa_ maintenance at the Bheemeswara temple, Draksharamam, a garden of 2000 betel-nut trees was donated; a mango garden for the deity in Penta village (Nizamabad district) and one more mango garden for the temple at Bapatla were also donated.\n\n**Land rights and taxes**\n\n*   Every landlord had full authority over his land for donation, buying and selling, though acceptance from concerned village elders had to be obtained before taking these up. Nobody could hold personal rights over **banjar land**.\n*   When land was donated, the extent of tax exemption given was known through **\"sarvakara Pariharam\"** — meaning exempted from all taxes.\n*   On occasion, when kings and feudatories made donations depending on need, there was a custom of buying private individuals'' lands and giving them to the donee.\n*   Land tax related terms: **Ari** — tax paid after change of crop; **Siddhayam** — tax that had already been levied whether or not the crop came up; **taggu, tere** — other agriculture-related taxes.\n*   Grain measurements in use: nivartana, martu, khandika, putti; even today in Telangana, measures such as putted manika and sola exist. For land measurement, there were \"gedalu\" (possibly wooden sticks) known as rajamana, mummidibhimakola, Janakola and rajarajakola.\n*   Crop conditions, types and rain relationships were explained in a work known as **\"sasyananda.\"** Writers such as **Somadevasuri** opined that taxes should not be collected before crops ripened and armies should not be quartered on crop lands — failing this would destroy the kingdom.\n*   **King Vijayaditya''s tax schedule** for lands in Malliswaradeva town (Vengi mandalam):\n\n| Land Type | Tax (per putti) |\n|---|---|\n| Oodpu paddy field | 2 chinnas |\n| Oodpu field | 6 chinnas |\n| Dry land | 4 chinnas |\n| Jowar land | (damaged in inscription — unknown) |\n| New land | 2 chinnas |\n| Gingelly land | (not preserved in inscription) |\n\nBasing on such schedules and land productivity, tax used to be fixed; concessions were given for newly-cultivated lands, and animal husbandry was also encouraged.\n\n### Industries\n\nChalukyan-era inscriptions show industries, trade and commerce were in good condition — industries allied to agriculture, jewellery-making, vehicle- and object-manufacturing, and smaller craft-based industries all existed.\n\n*   Most agricultural-era wealth was spent on wars, luxuries and pomp; only a smaller portion went to production and welfare programmes. The administration settled craftsmen in villages by giving craft land grants and tax exemptions — villagers manufactured land-tilling implements (arakalu), cots, benches and low-seat armless single-seaters (peetalu). Iron ploughs and earthenware pottery were also made.\n*   An inscription from the Draksharamam area shows **Teliki** (oil-extractor), **potterer, blacksmith, carpenter, maker of brass utensils (kanchari), and basket maker (medari)** were given tax-free lands.\n*   Cloth industry continued through the period; thin, delicate fabrics were made for emperors and queens, while thicker woven cloth served common people. Implements and vessels were made with great skill in all metals, from gold to iron. Most villages had oil presses (ganugulu) and weaving looms.\n\n### Trade and Commerce\n\nTrade and commerce flourished during the Chalukyan age, though industry remained largely confined to rural needs. Powerful trade guilds carried on brisk trade, mostly in weapons for emperors and armies, luxury articles for the wealthy, spices, perfumes, diamonds, pearls, crystals, precious stones, and finely textured cloth.\n\n*   Barter continued at a high level in villages, while coins circulated in towns. **Powerful merchant guilds** emerged; inscriptions record that vaisyas, telula (oil-pressers), perikas and balejas had their own guilds. Huge wealth came to the government through these guilds in the form of taxes and levies.\n*   Fairs and pilgrimage places developed into trade centres — the fair at **Konidesa** used to run every Thursday.\n*   Business was mainly in the hands of the **vaisyas**, called _nakarams_; every nakara had a _sreshti_. **Penugonda** became a leading trade city with 18 leading cities in the Vengi kingdom as central points. **Vengi, Ghantasala, Visakhanagaram and Nidadavolu** were trade centres. The **Ahadanakara inscription** informs about trade-guild activities. **Prithvivallabhapatnam** was considered a leading port town of the time.\n*   The presence of Eastern Chalukyan coins in **Burma and Siam** testifies to continental trade. Many Andhra port towns were given Tamil names during this period: **Visakhapatnam** was named **Kulottunga Cholapattanam**, **Ghantasala** was named **Chola Pandyapuram**, and **Motupalli** was named **Deseeyakonda pattanam**.\n\n**Coinage**\n\n*   Currency in usage: **gadyana, mada, panam, kalamju, dramas** — denoting gold, silver and copper coins.\n*   As per **Pavuluri Mallanna''s** work, the relations between different coins were:\n\n| Conversion |\n|---|\n| 4 gokara kanulu = 1 sorakani |\n| 4 sora kanulu = 1 pittha |\n| 4 pitthalu = 1 kani |\n| 4 kanulu = 1 visam |\n| 4 visalu = 1 pattika |\n| 4 pattikalu = 1 chinnam |\n| 4 chinnalu = 1 madha |\n\n*   Besides coins, liquid measures (sola, manika, adde, kuncham) and solid measures (tulam, seru, veese, manugu, putti) were also in use.\n*   **Trade guilds never sold all goods** — each guild sold only one commodity; those selling a particular commodity formed themselves into a guild, and only they could sell that commodity. On goods brought for sale, name-duty and place-duty were imposed; tax existed on salt and on cattle sold, collected only by officials. In the name of \"Kolpuveesalu,\" a **Kolagaram tax** existed; when grain and other goods were measured, a duty was taken out of the total measured.\n*   **Vedic religion did not allow sea voyage**, so Chalukyas did not carry on much foreign trade directly. Goods came in Chinese ships from Eastern countries and East Indies, and goods of the time were carried back in the same ships. Eastern Chalukyas sent ambassadors to Burma and China and maintained friendly relations with them, though foreign trade remained limited. Some locally-grown commodities and manufactured cloth were exported; horses and spices needed for emperors were imported from foreign countries. **Pavuluri Mallanna**, of the 12th century, gave the prices of commodities in his work \"Sarasangrahaganitam.\"\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p11", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Social Development", "bodyMd": "To know about social conditions of the Chalukyan period, their inscriptions prove useful. **Vedic Brahman religion gained prominence and the varna system took stable shape.** Protection of _varnasrama dharma_ went on well during their rule.\n\n*   Migrations as per occupation, regional bases, and the influence of different religions caused divisions to crop up in many castes. At this stage, there was **social uplift** in the lifestyle of people at the lowest rungs of the social ladder. From this age onward, people had freedom to make charities as per their desire. **Sanskrit education**, unlike earlier times, was made open to all from this age. Each caste''s people had freedom to make necessary alternatives or additions to their caste regulations. Social customs and festivals varied from caste to caste — inscriptions giving information regarding the honour and status of different castes are seen in this period, though details on marriage relations and inter-dining between different castes and varnas are not clearly known.\n\n### Brahmins\n\nIn the Chalukyan four-fold varna system, **brahmins held first place**, remaining at the top of society because of their Sanskrit education, study of Vedas and Vedangas, and service as teachers.\n\n*   Reference to them is found in all royal-dynasty inscriptions; many emperors gave them agraharas — a system personally started by **Pulakesi II**. In Chalukyan age, all the ministers and dandanayakas who stood by the ruling dynasties belonged to the brahmin community only. Depending on gotra and pravara branch, there were divisions even among brahmins, identified as per the areas or centres in which brahmin families resided.\n*   Due to discontent established in religious and political fields, royal patronage, and personal or family difficulties, brahmin families since the **9th century A.D.** started migrating; rulers provided arrangements for their stay. During Chalukyan times, in the Ghantasala area, house sites were given to **2000 brahmin families**.\n*   More than their supposed eminence, their place in society came to be recognised because of their respected profession; countless agraharas are referenced in inscriptions given for maintaining items related to Vedic education and their livelihood.\n\n### Kshatriyas\n\nMany battles took place during the Vengi Chalukyan period, needing many soldiers for wars. **These kshatriyas went as soldiers at the time of wars and fought.** Those kshatriyas who considered administrative duties their responsibility came to be described as of \"warrior varna.\"\n\n*   From the Chalukyan time onward, **all categories of people began to participate in all kinds of activities**. To claim equal status with the old kshatriyas, every caste''s people, from Chalukyan times, began to derive their origin from some pristine Purana Personality, and following this, as stated in inscriptions, members began to claim themselves of **lunar dynasty (Chandravamsa)** and **Surya vamsa (solar dynasty)**. The idea was prevalent that whoever performed kshatriya dharma or duty was kshatriya alone.\n\n### Vysyas\n\nIn the Four-fold varna organisation, vaisyas formed the third group; their occupation was trade. They were called **sreshti, setti and komati**. Being devotees of Gomateswara (Jainism), they were also called _gomatlu_ or _komatlu_.\n\n*   During Chalukyan times, trade and business were their occupation; organising into guilds and carrying on business both within and outside the country, they became rich. The fact that vaisyas could have their own armies for protecting their properties and providing security during trade journeys, and even giving loans to the administration itself, indicates their economic growth.\n*   Vaisyas also had **born gotra names**. For their rights, they formed \"**nakaram**\" (nagaram); guilds worked to solve caste-related problems. Their caste goddess was **Vasavikanyaka** at Penugonda — there is no truth in the story mentioned in their \"Kula puranam\" that when a Chalukyan ruler tried to force her, she jumped into fire and died.\n*   More than following administrators'' orders, observing caste dharma and taking permission from caste elders for social activities was the prevailing norm.\n\n### Sudras\n\nThose belonging to the fourth group in the four-fold varna system came to be regarded as sudras. Just like other castes, many divisions emerged in their caste too, depending on occupation adopted for living, birth and regionalism — some took to agriculture, others adopted handicrafts.\n\n*   During the Medieval age, many of those who took to military service happened to be sudra varna; their involvement was there in industries, handicrafts, and temple activities.\n*   During Chalukyan times, opportunity came for some sudras to assume authority over the kingdom — Chalukya feudatories such as **Velanati kings, Kondapadamatlu, Tyagi and Parichchedi** all belonged to sudra caste only.\n*   Contemporary historians state that people of craft-based occupations craved for social status; with the expansion of agriculture, their economic condition also gradually improved. The divisions among the sudras — **Velama and Kamma** — came as per regional divisions (Velanadu, Kammanadu); **Reddy**, in the first stage, was either a Rashtrakuta chief or Rattodi (an official of village administration) and gradually remained as the name of a division among the sudras.\n*   As per Chalukyan-age inscriptions, in Edurupalli village near Draksharama, land grants were given separately to **teliki (oil-pressers), kammari (blacksmith), kamsali (carpenter), kanchari (makers of bronze articles)**, coconut and betel-nut gardeners, barbers, bronze workers, washermen, basket makers, and leather workers.\n*   It is learnt from the organisation \"sanimunnurvuru\" that, just like people of other castes and occupations, **prostitutes also got organised into guilds** and led a movement for the protection of their rights, referred to in many inscriptions.\n\n### Position of Women\n\nIn the patriarchal form of society prevalent during Chalukyan times, there was restriction on women, but they were \"no impediment for the honour and status of women.\"\n\n*   Though Smriti works and Jain works imposed restrictions and limitations on the freedoms of women, this need not be taken as reflecting contemporary realities — from politics to fine arts, there are many sources mentioning women''s interference and participation.\n*   Though marriage alliances did not generally occur between the Chalukya–Rashtrakuta dynasties, on other occasions, as **Chola princesses in succession married Chalukyan kings**, significant changes occurred in history. Because of queens such as **Kundavva, Ammangadevi and Madhurantaki**, it could be thought that Chola influence got established over the Vengi monarchy.\n*   **Upper caste ladies and prostitutes used to earn skill in fine arts and other needed types of education.**\n\n### Educational Organisation\n\nIn the Chalukyan age, religion-related Vedic studies received a lot of patronage. **Puranas, itihasas, dharmasastras, agamasastras, grammar, Vedanta, Astronomy, Diplomacy, Mathematics, Ayurveda and Logic** were studied.\n\n*   In land-grant inscriptions, the rich knowledge of brahmin grant-receivers has been greatly described — in the Peddamaddali inscription, it is said that **Gupta Sarma** studied seventy commentaries on Logic alone. The Nidaparru inscription states that **Katte Sarma** thoroughly studied Vedas, itihasas, Puranas, dharmasastras and Upanishads; his grandfather, **Mandasarma**, was a scholar in Kalpa, purana, itihasa and dharmasastras at **Asanapura Ghatikasthana**.\n*   Asanapuram became famous as a prominent **Ghatikasthanam** — the most eminent educational centre was called Ghatikasthanam. During Chalukyan times, **Ayurvedam, Dharmavedam, Gandharva and Arthasastras** used to be taught. Those who graduated from such Ghatikas were called **Ghatikasikas**.\n*   **Rajaraja Narendra** stated he had studied Puranas, dharmasastras, kavyas and dramas, and had taken interest in Eashwaragamala. Educational learning mainly took place at **Agraharas, Brahmapurillu, Ghatikas, temples and matthas.** For traditional Sanskrit educational progress, agraharas remained as abodes given to scholars for patronising education and culture; sources state education was taught freely in agraharas, temples and matthas, and boarding/lodging facilities were provided.\n*   **Nolambeswara temple in Hemavati** was a Ghatikasthanam; the **Hiresamudra inscription** mentions boarding arrangements provided for more than ninety students. Some educational centres had libraries — at **Nagai**, a big library was run, attached to an educational centre.\n*   The educational system received financial help from the government, as well as cooperation and support from individuals, institutions and guilds. Some temples ran educational choultries.\n*   Great effort was made in Mathematics during this period — **Pavuluri Mallanna**, who lived during Rajaraja Narendra''s time, translated **\"Ganitasastram\"** written by **Mahaviracharya** (a Jain scholar), with the name **\"Sarasangraha ganitam.\"** This work mentions the coins, tables and measurements in use at that time.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p12", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Cultural Development", "bodyMd": "### Religious Developments\n\nAmong those who received Agraharas from the Chalukyan kings, brahmins — the basic pillars of Vedic religion — were greatest in number. During Chalukyan times, **Vedic religion was in the highest position**; **Kumarilabhattu**, who propagated _purvamimamsa Vedanta_ method, belonged to this age.\n\n*   Because of self-reforms within Vedic religion, people''s interest towards rituals and charities, idol worship, and construction of major temples increased. **Temple construction was taken up by the government as a movement and a social necessity.**\n*   It became a tradition that the number of wars fought by Chalukyan kingdoms had to be equalled by the construction of an equal number of temples: **Vijayaditya II**, who waged **108 wars**, built **108 temples**; **Chalukya Bhima**, who waged **360 wars**, built **360 temples**.\n*   The temple group at **Bikkavolu** was constructed during the time of Gunaga Vijayaditya; **Pancharamas**, prominent Saiva temples, were constructed during the time of the Chalukyas. A huge number of temples were built during the time of **Rajaraja Narendra**.\n*   **Jataras** used to be held in temples — the _pradigam_ (procession) of idols was an important aspect. The Bezawada inscription (C.E. 930) of Yuddhamalla records that in Chebrolu, Mahasena''s jatara procession was taken from Chebrolu to Bezawada.\n\n### Saivism\n\nSaivism had popular following during this period. In Saiva deeksha, **rudraksha, vibhuti and gurupadakam** (water from the feet of the teacher) formed main aspects; differences existed among Saiva branches regarding the manner of deeksha. A 7th-century inscription refers to \"sivamandala deeksha.\"\n\n*   At places like Vijayawada, propaganda known as \"simha parishats\" was established. **Srisailam, Alampuram and Draksharamam** had been exclusively Saiva centres; **Srisailam has been described by Bhavabhuti as a Kapalika centre.**\n*   The temples built in the almost early stages of Chalukyan rule were all related to Saivism only. Siva was described as \"mahavrati\" by Nannechoda; Kapalika _vratam_ was only mahavratam. **Srisailam, Alampuram, Bezawada (Vijayawada) and Pithapuram** were the centres of Sakti worship of the day.\n*   During this age, **Basaveswara (C.E. 1100–70)** in Karnataka started the **Vira Saiva movement**. Already before him, **Ekanta Ramaiah** and **Sripati Pandita** had propagated Virasaiva doctrines. It is said that, to prove the superiority of Siva, Ekanta Ramaiah got his head cut and received it back; Sripati Pandita (Vijayawada) kept fire in his upper garment (\"uttariyam\") to exhibit the superiority of Siva.\n*   The idea that Siva was supreme deity, the wearing of _linga_ as an instrument of devotion, and the integration of _linga_ (idol) and _anga_ (body) had been chief aspects of Virasaivism, which was called \"agamantam\" (end of agama). After Basaveswara, **Mallikarjuna Pandita** propagated Saivism further — while going around the country, he exterminated Jains and Buddhists and propagated Saivism.\n*   **Ammaraju II** donated Tadikonda village to Bhuvanasraya Siva temple in Vijayawada — on behalf of the Siva temple, this land grant was received by **Prabhutarasi**, a Saiva saint. Vemulavada Chalukyas also gave donations to Saiva saints; among them, **Arikesari I** donated Belmoga village to Sadyosivacharya as an educational grant.\n\n### Vaishnavism\n\nWith the new vigour that came from the propagation of **Ramanuja**, Vaishnavism began to compete with Saivism. In the Chalukya–Chola age, there were many Dravida Vaishnava families. \"**Keyurabahu Charithra**\" informs that **Kommana** minister carried out Vishnu installation in **32 temple centres**. In Vaishnava deeksha, \"Chakrankitalu,\" \"Pundradharana,\" \"Uchishista Bhojana,\" \"dasanamadharana\" and \"ekadasivratam\" had been main aspects.\n\n*   During Chalukyan age, extremist movements sprang up for the sake of Saiva and Vaishnava faiths. **Ramanujacharya** imparted Vaishnava deeksha to lower-varna people but did not condemn the varna system. **Palanati Brahmanayudu** propagated Vira Vaishnavism.\n*   Besides Virasaiva and Viravaishnava religions, over and above sectarian differences, the traditional **Smarta faith** also received encouragement. Vaishnava temples such as **Bapatla Bhavanaryana Swami temple, Sarpavarapu Bhavanarayanaswami Temple and Mandvya Narayanaswamy temple at Samarlakota** attained prominence during the Chalukyan time.\n\n### Buddhism\n\nIt is learnt through the biography of Panditaradhya that Buddhists lived in Chandole area. From the sentence \"Chaityamatynatam yatra nana chitrasuchitatvam,\" found in Amaravati inscriptions, it is understood that the **Dhanyakataka Chaitya did not suffer loss**, but Buddha, as an incarnation of Vishnu only, was getting worshipped — this is also stated in inscriptions at Amaravati, Karimnagar and Bikkavolu.\n\nThe name-sake Buddha worship, as a result of extremist Saiva propaganda by Panditaradhya, got ended; after the 5th and 6th centuries C.E., Buddhism began declining in the region.\n\n### Jainism\n\nInscriptional sources record that **Jainism was patronised** during the time of the Badami, Vengi and Kalyani Chalukyas.\n\n*   **Ayyanamahadevi**, queen of Kubjavishnuvardhana, established **Nedumba Vasadi** at Bezawada and donated Mushinikonda village to it.\n*   **Ammaraju II**, at the request of his wife \"Chamakamba,\" donated \"Kalachumbarru\" village to the Jains; at the request of **Durgaraju**, head of Karmarashtra vishaya, he gave many donations for constructing Katakabharana Jina temples. Further, Ammaraju II, at the request of his commanders-in-chief **Bhima and Naravahana**, gave Pedagadelaparru village for constructing Jina temples at Bezawada.\n*   **Ramathirtham, Bikkavolu, Hanumakonda, Potlacheruvu, Kolanupaka (Nalgonda) and Vardhamanapuram** were prominent Jain centres. **Vemulavada**, the capital of the Vemulavada Chalukyas (feudatories of the Rashtrakutas ruling in present Telangana), and their early capital, **Podanapuram (Bodhan)**, were also leading Jain centres — at **Potlacheruvu (Patan Cheruvu)** alone, there were around **500 Jain Vasadis** (places of stay for Jain monks).\n*   **Vimaladitya**, the Vengi Chalukya king, also patronised Jainism, adopting it toward the end of his rule. During the time of **Anantavarma Rajarajadeva**, who ruled Kalinga in C.E. 1178, a merchant named Kannamanayaka built the Rajaraja Jina temple at Bhogaram village (Visakhapatnam district) and made land grants to it. **Danavulapadu** in Jammalamadugu Taluk developed as a great Jain temple centre.\n*   During Vemulavada Chalukya times, **Yuddhamalla Jina temple at Sanigaram, Baddega Jina temple at Vemulavada (Subhadama jina temple), and Arikesari Jina temple at Repaka** were constructed. At Bodhan, it seems there was earlier a statue of Gomattheswara; seeing this, **Chamundaraya**, minister of Rachamalla Gangaraju, installed the idol of Gomattheswara at **Sravanabelagola** in Karnataka around C.E. 970–990.\n*   **Somadevasuri**, a Jainacharya with the Vemulavada kings, was a great scholar who wrote **\"Yasastilaka\"** — a champu kavya, also called **Yasodhara Charitra** — and **\"Neetikavyamrutam.\"** For him, king Baddega built a famous Jain temple, the **Subhadama Jina temple**, at Vemulavada; for its maintenance, **Arikesari III** donated the village **Vanikatupalli**.\n*   **Pampa**, the poet, in his \"Vikramarjuna Vijayam,\" also gave an account of Jain Tirthankaras. **Jinavallabha**, brother of Pampa, was himself a Jain devotee — he issued an inscription stating he had the statue of the Jain goddess **Chakreswari** carved on a hillock near Kurkyala village (about 20 km from Karimnagar) and held celebrations there.\n*   **Hanumakonda** is one of the ancient Jain temple centres in Telangana. On the present-day **Padmavathi hillock**, images of the Jain Tirthankaras Parsvanatha and Sasanadevi are found. In Jain Sangha, both _yathulu_ (monks) and _upasakulu_ (laity) existed; among Jains, divisions such as **Sangha, Gana, Gachha, Bali and Anya** existed. Educational learning of Jains started with the principle \"Om namah Siddhamnamah\" — it is the opinion of some that, by adding \"Sivayanamah,\" this was later turned into a Jain principle.\n\n### Literary Development\n\n**Telugu language**, which till then remained \"like a forest tree in unknown state,\" started from the time of the Chalukyas to develop into a tree blossoming with leaves, flowers and fruits. The great literary movement that started in this age went on progressing; most Telugu inscriptions of the Chalukya–Chola age are in verse form, and every verse inscription turned out to be a masterpiece.\n\n*   As per the **Chebrolu inscription**, the nature of literary industry and the fact that in those days _marga_ and _desi_ literary styles were prevalent in Telugu are known. **Nannechoda** was the greatest prevalent poet of the age; as far as known in Telugu, **he was the first poet honoured by the royal dynasty**, holding the title \"Kavirajasekhara\" for his \"Kumarasambhavam.\"\n*   The influence of Kannada language and literary tradition was high; **Prakrit** remained the state language during Satavahana and Ikshvaku times, and from the time of the Vishnukundins, **Sanskrit** became the state language, with Telugu remaining only the medium of the common people''s matters. **For the first time, it was the Eastern Chalukyas who gave the honour of official language to Telugu.**\n*   Though Sanskrit inscriptions were more numerous during their period, the Chalukyas also issued inscriptions in Telugu. The **Telugu Chola** rulers, ruling the Renadu area, were the first to introduce the practice of getting royal inscriptions written in Telugu; the **Vipparla inscription of Jayasimhavallabha I** was the **first Eastern Chalukyan inscription** in Telugu. **Mallikarjuna Panditaradhya** wrote \"Sivatatvasaram\" — more than 400 kanda verses — recognised by scholars as a **Sataka**; hence, the credit for starting the \"Sataka Prakriya\" (style) in Telugu goes to Panditaradhya.\n*   Among those who used the Telugu language were the **Renati Cholas, Western Chalukyas and their feudatories, and Bana kings**. In inscriptional Telugu of the first stage, all inscriptions were written in prose, and Prakrit words got mixed into Telugu extensively — e.g. the word \"reddi\" appeared as \"rattagudi/rattadi/rattodi\"; \"purugulu\" for \"nasanam\" (destroyer) appeared as \"pruvullu\"; \"cheyuvadu\" (rachuvasu) used to appear; plural indicators used \"lu\"; \"gudulu\" and \"madulu\" were called \"gudlu\" and \"madlu\"; \"padamata\" was uttered as \"paruta\"; and \"Chola/Choda\" was called \"Chora.\"\n*   There is something significant in the method of sentence formation: in inscriptions of our present-day sentence structure, subject comes first with some adjectives, then object and other words. A different sentence-writing style existed in the **Macherla inscription of Jayasimha Vallabha I** and the **Potladurthi–Malepadu inscription of Renati Cholas**.\n*   By the time of **Gunaga Vijayaditya**, Telugu had come under Sanskrit''s influence; poetry writing started with local metre, guided by matra and gana. **Taruvoja** in the inscription of Panduranga; \"seesa verses\" in the Kandukuru inscription; and \"**madhyakkara**\" in the Bezawada inscription (C.E. 930) are examples.\n*   Gradually, Telugu language attained \"**kavya**\" shape; **Pampa**, who wrote \"Vikramarjuna Vijayam\" — the first \"kavya\" in Kannada — belonged to the age of the Chalukyas. Scholars opine **Tikkana Somayaji** was the greatest not only among the poets of the Chalukyan age but among all Telugu poets — his was a scholarly family. **Bhaskara mantri** wrote \"Bhaskara Ramayananm.\" **Ketana**, who wrote \"Kadambari\" as a Telugu padya kavya, might have been Tikkana''s elder father.\n*   **Tikkana** was not only a great minister and poet — \"he was a great cultural movement.\" He wrote \"**Nirvachanottara Ramayana**,\" dedicated to Manumasiddhi. The works of **Palkuriki Somanatha** — \"Panditaradhya Charitra\" and \"Basava Puranam\" — won fame in Telugu. **Ketana**, who wrote \"Dasakumar Charitra\" and \"Vijnaneswaram,\" and **Marana**, who wrote \"Markandeya Purana,\" belonged to this age. **Narayanabhattu** helped Nannaya in writing \"**Andhra Mahabharata**.\" In this way, the Chalukyan age contributed greatly to Telugu literary development.\n\n### Art Development\n\nRoyal courts and temples of Chalukyan times were the centres for the arts of music, dance, architecture and sculpture. The early Chalukyas, at their capital Badami and its surrounding places, constructed many cave temples.\n\n*   Prominent temples came up at **Aihole and Pattadakal** in Karnataka — as many as around **70 temples at Aihole** and **10 temples at Pattadakal** were constructed, exhibiting Chalukyan architectural and sculptural styles. These temples were built by erecting a rectangular platform over which a row of pillars was arranged to bear the roof load — walls were absent on the four sides, and this structure, called \"**sandhagaram**,\" might have been used as a meeting place for village elders and people.\n*   In Mahaboobnagar district (present-day Telangana), at **Alampur** on the bank of the sacred Tungabhadra river, there are **nine temples**, otherwise called the **Navabrahma temples**. Among them, six had compound walls; the remaining stay outside this compound wall. All nine temple names end with the word \"brahma,\" but all these are actually **Siva temples**, with Siva lingas installed in them. Among these nine, \"**Viswabrahma**\" temple was the biggest; these temples still receive worship today.\n*   Temples built at **Vemulavada** belonged to the ninth and tenth centuries. Among these, **Bhimeswara temple** is important — built by **Baddega**; it was earlier called \"Baddegeswara\" temple. At its sanctum-sanctorum entrance, a **Gajalakshmi** carving was placed; the walls of the sanctum sanctorum have four pillars — a tradition of the early Chalukyas.\n*   At the **Bhimalingeswara temple** in Vemulavada, on the northern wall of the sanctum sanctorium, a sculpture of **\"Mahishasura mardini\"** was carved. **Nageswara temple** is another temple at Vemulavada, built during the time of Arikesari II — the god worshipped by merchants is called \"Nagareswara,\" with a Siva linga installed in the inner temple. A Jain temple was built at Repaka by Arikesari III (per inscriptions). In Karimnagar district, 22 km from Manthena, beside \"Adavisomnapalli\" village, **four cave temples** exist.\n*   In coastal Andhra, Eastern Chalukyas built many temples, displaying love for architecture and sculpture, showing the influence of Pallava-Chalukya traditions. **Vijayaditya II stated he built 108 temples.** Inscriptional evidence states **Gunaga Vijayaditya built the Vijayeswara temple at Bikkavolu**. **Chalukya Bhima** constructed **Bhimeswara temples at Draksharama**; the temples at **Bhimeswaram, Palakollu and Amaravati** (in Pancharamas) were also Chalukyan constructions. **Yuddhamalla** built the **Kumaraswamy temple** at Bezawada; **Mahadevi**, queen of Vikramaditya II, built a magnificent Saiva temple at Pattadakal to mark her husband''s victories. At **Yeleswaram** in Nalgonda district, many old Siva temples emerged.\n*   **Gunaga Vijayaditya** built **six temples at Bikkavolu**; among these, excepting the **Golingeswara temple**, all others are in ruined condition. **Golingeswara temple, Chandrasekhara temple and Rajarajeswara temple** were all built on a square plan, two to three storeys, with art and nagara-style vimanas — these were rebuilt in later times. **Rajarajeswara temple was built by Rajaraja Narendra.**\n*   Music and dance were patronised in royal courts and temples. **Ammaraju** held the title \"kavigayaka kalpataruvu.\" **Chalukya Bhima I** honoured **Challavva**, an adept in Gandharva Vidya, with a land donation; her father **Mallappa** was described as like Tumbura in the art of music. Sculptures of the age show musical instruments such as violin, flute, mrudangam (maddela) and talas. Names of dancers, musical-instrument players and dance teachers are found in inscriptions; several types of dance songs and different kinds of ragas (tunes) are recorded respectively in \"Panditaradhya Charitra\" and \"Kumara Sambhavam.\"\n*   Painting had great importance in Chalukyan age. Painting at **Badami** is called a beautiful specimen of Deccan painting; paintings in **cave temples two and three at Badami** (C.E. 578) are wonderful testimony to early brahmin painting. In sculptures at Vijayawada, Jammidoddi, and one found at Chebrolu, dance and instrumental \"operas\" are depicted. Among the pastimes of the period, **kolatam** — an admixture of dance and singing — was popular.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p13", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Concluding Assessment", "bodyMd": "The period of Chalukyan rule could be considered a **glorious age in South Indian history**. Over their long period of rule — sixth to twelfth century — the Chalukyas achieved political unity and successfully resisted foreign invasions of South India; regarding the Arab invasion of Northern India during their time, they were able, to some extent, to prevent its spread to the western areas. Even though Chalukyan kings emerged from continuous wars, they developed South India in all fields and thereby could bring back memories of Satavahana rule.\n\n*   The rule of **Chalukya Bhima**, who ruled from Vengi as capital, deserves special mention — during his time, the Bhimeswara temple at Draksharama and one more Bhimeswara temple at Chalukya Bhimavaram were built, and the fame of the Chalukyas was taken to greater heights. During their time, though Buddhism slid back, **Jainism flourished greatly**.\n*   During the time of the **Badami Chalukyas**, Pulakesi II, as part of his military conquests, occupied most parts of Telangana and Rayalaseema and proclaimed the eminence of the Chalukyas. **Vemulavada Chalukyas**, who ruled over Telangana areas, gave the fruits of great administration to the people of Telangana — during the time of **Baddega**, who fought 42 battles without tasting defeat in any one of them, war heroes like him brought glory to the age. During the time of Vemulavada Chalukyas, **Arikesari II**, taking advantage of relations with the Rashtrakutas, was able to expand his empire. **Mudigonda Chalukyas** also, with the confidence of the Eastern Chalukyas, ruled over the Telangana region.\n*   Though local government resembled that of the Cholas, the Chalukyas divided their empire into administrative units. A large percentage of state income was spent on people''s welfare programmes, earning the people''s admiration. In army maintenance too, new changes could take place — **Pulakesi II**, in the Aihole inscription, referred to the six types of army organisations. Keeping responsibility for catching criminals with local officials, and giving importance to village assemblies in judicial trials, remained useful and praiseworthy measures.\n*   Giving importance to tank construction contributed to improving crop yields — the **Chaita tank** and **Bhima Samudram**, mentioned in the Tadikonda inscription of Ammaraju II, still supply water to cultivated lands today. Along with agriculture, industries, trade and commerce proceeded on a progressive path; weaving of different kinds of cloth gained importance among the handicrafts.\n*   Though the four-fold varna system was prevalent, **sudras and women could freely take up their activities and enjoy their rights**. Saiva and Jain faiths enjoyed good patronage and progress — reflected in temples constructed at **Alampuram, Vemulavada, Kolanupaka, Aihole and Pattadakal**, which stand even today as specimens of Chalukyan architectural and sculptural style.\n*   Next to Saivism and Jainism, Vaishnavism also gained importance and enjoyed patronage. In literary field, inscriptions and writings began in Prakrit and Sanskrit languages, and gradually — during the time of the Eastern Chalukyas and Vemulavada Chalukyas — **Telugu, the language of the local people, started becoming the medium for inscriptions and writings**; with the preparation of \"Mahabharata\" in Telugu by **Nannaya**, court poet of Rajaraja Narendra, the way became clear for the development of Telugu language and literature.\n\nIt could be realised that the Chalukyas, during their long rule of six centuries, could introduce **stable, praiseworthy, popular, ideal and trend-setting systems and changes** in political, administrative, economic, social and cultural fields. In that way, their rule period could be considered as an **age by itself** in Telangana as well as South Indian history, without any doubt.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter6-rashtrakutas', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter6-rashtrakutas-p1", "partName": "Rashtrakutas — Overview and Origin", "bodyMd": "Rashtrakutas occupied the most prominent place in the history of South India. Remaining as feudatories of the Chalukyas of Badami, they established an independent and vast empire after the decline of the Badami Chalukyas. Many districts of Telangana used to be under Rashtrakuta rule, and **Manyakheta** in the Karnataka region remained their capital.\n\n### Theories on Origin\n\nThere are different opinions among historians regarding the origin of the Rashtrakutas — while some scholars held that their political life started in Maharashtra, others argue that their original homeland was Karnataka.\n\n1.  **Dr. Fleet** felt that Rashtrakutas belonged to the Rathor descendants of Northern India.\n2.  **R.G. Bhandarkar** opined that the Rajjakas (provincial governors) of the Mauryan days only might have become Rashtrakutas.\n3.  **Dr. Barnal** held that Rashtrakutas were Telugu people who, after some time, came to be called Reddis.\n4.  **Mallampalli Somasekhara Sarma** similarly held the opinion that, remaining as Rattadi or charioteers, the Rashtrakutas were those who later became Reddis of Telugu land.\n5.  **A.S. Altekar** stated that the term \"Rashtrakuta\" remained the name of an employer post, and later, as they established an independent kingdom, that name became established as their dynastic name.\n\n**The employer-name theory:** The word Rashtrakuta was not the name of a race — just as \"gramakuta\" means a village official in ancient days, \"Rashtrakuta\" too indicates an employer name. During the Mauryan period, a person known as \"Rashtriya\" used to be the viceroy of bigger regions such as Gujarat and Kathiawar. In the first centuries A.D., Rathikas and Maharathikas governed small regions in Maharashtra and Berar, and when the central government became weak, they established independent kingdoms.\n\nIt is learnt that the word \"Rashtrakuta\" was adopted as their dynastic name. Among the Rashtrakutas who became independent, the important ones were those who became the chiefs of **Lattaluru** — the present-day Kannada-speaking Latur in Bidar district. Hence, their mother tongue must have been Kannada only.\n\nDuring the time of the Badami Chalukyas, the Rashtrakutas remained their feudatories. They might have belonged to the Rathika family in Maharashtra areas, or to the Reddi family in Andhra and Karnataka regions. In their dynastic anecdotes, the Rashtrakutas claimed to have originated from **Satyaki**, the brother of Sri Krishna. Ruling Bidar and Maharashtra areas, these Rashtrakutas cooperated in maintaining the prestige of the Badami Chalukya kingdom; when that kingdom disintegrated, they established their own independent kingdom and, within a short time, became the emperors of Dakshinapatha in South India.\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter6-rashtrakutas-p2", "partName": "Rashtrakutas: Political Developments", "bodyMd": "Rashtrakutas ruled for nearly 200 years. The brother of Nannaraju, who ruled the Ellichpur area in 631 A.D., is considered the ancestor in their dynasty.\n\n**Indra Raja I (696 – 710 A.D.)** was the founder of the Rashtrakuta dynasty. He and his successors ruled Manyakheta and Western Telangana areas as feudatories of the Badami Chalukyas. After him, **Govindaraja or Varma I (710 – 725 A.D.)**, **Kanakaraja I (725 – 735 A.D.)** and **Indraraja III (735 – 748 A.D.)** continued as feudatories of the Badami Chalukyas.\n\n### Dantidurga (748 – 758 A.D.)\n\nThe independent Rashtrakuta kingdom was established by Dantidurga, son of Indraraja II. He was highly courageous and of keen temperament.\n\n**Establishing independence**\n\n*   Dantidurga defeated **Keertivarma II** (753 A.D.), the Badami Chalukyan king, and established the independent Rashtrakuta kingdom. The greater part of Telangana came under their empire.\n\n**As a feudatory — early campaigns**\n\n*   Earlier, as feudatory of the Western Chalukyan ruler **Vikramaditya II**, Dantidurga participated in several expeditions and conquered the Pallava, Malwa and Gujarat areas.\n*   In a battle fought in 738 A.D., he severely defeated the Arabs.\n*   Pleased with this great victory, Vikramaditya II gave Dantidurga the titles \"Prithvivallabha\" and \"Khadgavaloka.\"\n\n**Consolidation as independent ruler**\n\n*   Dantidurga occupied the Nasik, Poona, Satara and Kolhapur districts. Becoming overlord of the entire Maharashtra, he assumed the titles \"Maharajadhiraja,\" \"Paramamaheswara\" and \"Paramabhattaraka.\"\n*   By 757 A.D., he had conquered Kanchi, Kalinga, Kosala, Srisailam, Malwa, Lata and Sindhu areas.\n*   The Vayamgadh inscriptions and the inscriptions at the Dasavatara temple in Ellora (also built by Dantidurga) describe his great war victories.\n*   He invaded Malwa, conquered it, and in its capital **Ujjaini** made a \"Hiranyagarbha\" donation to declare his victory.\n*   He gave his daughter in marriage to Pallava king **Nandivarma II**.\n\nIn his short period of rule, Dantidurga established an extensive Rashtrakuta empire and died in 758 A.D.\n\n### Krishna I (758 – 772 A.D.)\n\nAfter Dantidurga, Krishna I ruled the Rashtrakuta kingdom, carrying forward the conquests started by Dantidurga.\n\n*   He occupied Southern Konkan and sent crown-prince **Govinda II** to Vengi; Govinda II defeated Vengi Chalukyan ruler **Vishnuvardhana IV** and occupied some territory.\n*   During Krishna I''s time, rivalry started between the Vengi Chalukyas and the Rashtrakutas — a conflict that would continue for generations.\n*   Krishna I bore the titles \"Subhattunga\" and \"Akalavarshudu.\"\n*   He was also a great patron of art, building the **Kailasanadha temple (rock-cut) at Ellora** — its construction went on for nearly a hundred years, and it has since received recognition as a world heritage site.\n\n### Govinda II (772 – 780 A.D.)\n\nAfter Krishna I, Govinda II ruled between 772–780 A.D. He won victory in the **battle of Gangavadi**, and was called \"Suvarna Govindaraja\" and \"Prabhuta Varsha.\"\n\n### Dhruva Raja or Dhruva (780 – 792 A.D.)\n\nDhruva killed his brother Govinda II, ascended the throne, and then defeated the kings of Kanchi, Gangavadi, Vengi and Malwa.\n\n**Conquests and the Tripartite Struggle**\n\n*   Dhruva defeated the Eastern Chalukyan ruler **Vishnuvardhana IV** and married his daughter **Seelamahadevi**.\n*   He had great courage and capacity — not confining himself to Dakshinapatha or South India, he conquered North India too, making its rulers his feudatories.\n*   Dhruva was the **first Rashtrakuta ruler to enter the Tripartite wars** — the struggle for control of Kanauj fought between the Pala, Pratihara and Rashtrakuta dynasties.\n*   He defeated Pratihara-Kanauj ruler **Vatsaraja**, and as a mark of this victory adopted the \"Ganga-Yamuna torana\" as his kingdom''s emblem.\n\n**Significance of his reign**\n\n*   The Rashtrakuta kingdom, until then a local power, was extended to North India — creating a special place for the Rashtrakutas in the history of India.\n*   Dhruva held the titles \"Srivallabha,\" \"Nirupamavallabha\" and \"Dhanarnava.\" As a result of the victories in this expedition, the military strength of the Rashtrakutas and the prestige of the Rashtrakuta kingdom rose immensely.\n*   During his thirteen-year rule, Dhruva expanded the Rashtrakuta kingdom into a vast empire. In all these wars, his sons **Stambha, Karka, Govinda and Indra** helped him.\n\n### Govinda III (793 – 814 A.D.)\n\nAfter Dhruva, his son Govinda III assumed power — the greatest among the Rashtrakutas. His titles included \"Prabhutavarsha,\" \"Rajadhiraja,\" \"Rajaparameswara,\" \"Tribhuvanadhavali,\" \"Sri Vallabha,\" \"Janavallabha\" and \"Kirthinarayana.\"\n\n**Wars and conquests**\n\n*   The **Sanjan inscription** records his greatness and victories. He fought wars with the Pallava, Western Ganga, Ghurjara-Pratihara and Pala kings, achieving success in all.\n*   He defeated Pratihara king **Nagabhata II**.\n*   He ruled for 22 years, spending his entire lifetime waging wars. His conquests extended to Chitrakuta, Ujjaini, Bengal, Kalapriya and the Ganga-Yamuna coastal plain.\n*   The ruler of Simhala country (Sri Lanka) became his friend. Broach ruler Sribhavana also maintained good relations with him.\n*   When Govinda III was on his Northern expedition, the Pallava, Pandya, Kerala and Ganga kings formed themselves into a confederacy against him; learning of this, he made lightning attacks on them, and by 802 A.D. this confederacy was broken.\n\nThrough these achievements, Govinda III became famous as a great warrior, commander-in-chief and statesman among the Rashtrakuta emperors. During his reign, the name and fame of the Rashtrakutas reached its highest level.\n\n### Amoghavarsha (814 – 880 A.D.)\n\nAfter Govinda III, his only son Amoghavarsha ruled — his original name was **Saru**. As he was a minor when he came to the throne, his uncle Indra''s son **Karka** took up the administrative responsibility of the kingdom as his protector.\n\n**Wars and diplomacy**\n\n*   Amoghavarsha''s reign was filled with rebellions, internal conflicts and wars. He defeated the Vengi king, **Gunaga Vijayaditya**.\n*   In South Indian politics, he strengthened his relations with some royal dynasties through marriage alliances.\n*   In the South, Gangavadi king Nitidurga declared his independence; Amoghavarsha defeated him but, with political foresight, gave back his kingdom to him, and gave his daughter Chandrabbalachhen in marriage to the Ganga king''s son, **Butuga**.\n*   Another daughter, Renuka (Revaka) Nirmadi (Sankha), was given in marriage to the Pallava king, **Nandi Varma**.\n*   He gave his sister in marriage to the Vengi crown-prince **Vishnuvardhana VII**. Eastern Chalukyan ruler **Gunaga Vijayaditya** became his feudatory.\n\n**Literary and religious patronage**\n\n*   Not only in political and military fields, but also in religious and literary fields, Amoghavarsha''s contribution was great.\n*   He was a great poet — he wrote \"**Kavirajamarga**,\" the first \"alankara\" (rhetoric) work, and also \"**Prasnottara Ratnamalika**.\" He held the title \"Kaviraja.\"\n*   Jain poets of his period, **Mahavira Acharya** and **Saktayana**, wrote \"**Ganitasara Sangraha**\" and \"**Amoghavritti**\" respectively.\n*   Amoghavarsha built **Manyakheta (Malkhed)** city and made it his capital.\n*   The Arab traveller **Suleman**, describing Amoghavarsha''s activities, praised him as one of the four great emperors in the world.\n*   In order to stop a famine and pestilence in his kingdom, Amoghavarsha offered his left-hand finger as a sacrifice to the Mahalakshmi goddess at Kolhapur.\n*   Amoghavarsha adopted **Jainism**, observed \"Sallekhanavrata,\" and died.\n\n### Krishna II (880 – 914 A.D.)\n\nAfter Amoghavarsha, his son Krishna II became king. **From his time onward, the decline of the Rashtrakutas began.**\n\n*   Continuous wars were fought with the Pratihara, Kalinga, Ganga and Magadha kingdoms.\n*   Ghurjara-Pratihara king **Mihira Bhoja** swooped on the Rashtrakuta kingdom.\n*   Eastern Chalukya king **Gunaga Vijayaditya** invaded the Rashtrakuta kingdom and destroyed the cities of Kiranapura, Achalapura and Chakrakuta.\n*   Krishna II clashed with Chola king **Parantaka** and was defeated.\n*   Krishna II gave his daughter in marriage to Chola king **Aditya I**.\n\nThe reign of Krishna II, who faced defeats in many wars, shook the very foundations of the Rashtrakuta Empire.\n\n### Later Rashtrakutas\n\n*   After Krishna II, **Indra III (914 – 928 A.D.)** ruled. During his time, the Arab traveller **Al-Masudi** visited the Rashtrakuta kingdom.\n*   **Amoghavarsha II (928 – 929 A.D.)** and **Govinda IV (930 – 936 A.D.)** ruled next. Govinda IV, disgusted with the rebellions of the Mudigonda Chalukyas, gave up the kingdom and ran away.\n*   As a result, **Amoghavarsha III (936 – 939 A.D.)**, Govinda IV, and **Krishna III (939 – 966 A.D.)** ruled the Rashtrakuta kingdom in succession.\n\n**Krishna III — restoration of prestige**\n\n*   During the time of Krishna III, Rashtrakuta prestige was restored. He invaded the Chola kingdom; crown-prince **Rajaditya** was killed, and Krishna III assumed the title \"Tanjavoor Konda.\"\n*   In the **Takkolam battle**, Parantaka Chola I was defeated, and a victory pillar was erected at Rameswaram.\n*   A Northern Indian expedition was also made, and the Ujjain ruler **Mahipala** was defeated.\n\n**Khottiga (967 – 972 A.D.)**\n\n*   After Krishna III, his brother Khottiga became ruler.\n*   Malwa king **Harshaseyaka** defeated Khottiga and destroyed Manyakheta, and Khottiga, highly grief-struck over this insult, died.\n\n### Karkaraja II (972 – 973 A.D.) — End of the Rashtrakuta Dynasty\n\nAmong the Rashtrakutas, the last ruler was Karkaraja II. During his time, feudatories became independent.\n\nUnder these circumstances, **Chalukyan Tailapa**, the ruler of Tondavadi and a feudatory, defeated Karkaraja, killed him, and occupied Manyakheta; with this, the rule of the Rashtrakutas disappeared permanently, and Tailapa II established the rule of the Kalyani Chalukyan dynasty.\n\n### Rulers of the Rashtrakutas at a Glance\n\n| Ruler | Reign |\n|---|---|\n| Indra Raja I (founder, as feudatory) | 696 – 710 A.D. |\n| Govindaraja / Varma I | 710 – 725 A.D. |\n| Kanakaraja I | 725 – 735 A.D. |\n| Indraraja III | 735 – 748 A.D. |\n| Dantidurga (independence established) | 748 – 758 A.D. |\n| Krishna I | 758 – 772 A.D. |\n| Govinda II | 772 – 780 A.D. |\n| Dhruva Raja | 780 – 792 A.D. |\n| Govinda III | 793 – 814 A.D. |\n| Amoghavarsha | 814 – 880 A.D. |\n| Krishna II | 880 – 914 A.D. |\n| Indra III | 914 – 928 A.D. |\n| Amoghavarsha II | 928 – 929 A.D. |\n| Govinda IV | 930 – 936 A.D. |\n| Amoghavarsha III | 936 – 939 A.D. |\n| Krishna III | 939 – 966 A.D. |\n| Khottiga | 967 – 972 A.D. |\n| Karkaraja II (last ruler) | 972 – 973 A.D. |\n\n* * *", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter6-rashtrakutas-p3", "partName": "The Glory of the Rashtrakuta Empire", "bodyMd": "In the history of India itself, the Rashtrakuta rule period attained a distinction. The Rashtrakuta emperors, unrivalled, daring and war-like, not only established a vast empire that extended from Kanyakubja to Kanyakumari, but also introduced an effective system of administration for the welfare of the people, becoming popular rulers. These Rashtrakutas, who created a heroic age in the history of India, ruled in the interests of the people. In the beginning, **Ellichpur, Ellora and Paithan** cities remained their capitals; later, during Amoghavarsha''s reign, **Manyakheta** became the capital.\n\n### Administrative System\n\nIn the Rashtrakuta Empire, the king or emperor enjoyed absolute power and remained independent in statecraft. Authority over the kingdom was hereditary; Rashtrakuta kings assumed the titles \"Maharajadhiraja,\" \"Dharavarsha\" and \"Vikramavaloka.\"\n\n*   Though kingship was generally hereditary, there was a practice in those times that, on occasions when a son other than the eldest was found able and efficient, in the interests of the kingdom, he was made the ruler.\n*   The king used to be the commander-in-chief, and used to personally lead the armies during wars.\n*   The king''s court, full of ostentation and splendour, remained the symbol of his power and supremacy. Feudatories, foreign ambassadors, poets, doctors, astrologers, higher civil, military and government officials, and representatives of the Srenis (guilds) used to attend the king''s court.\n\n### Ministry\n\nMinisters used to assist the king in the administration of the kingdom. The important among them were called \"**Mahamantri**.\"\n\n*   The ministry included the Prime Minister, Finance Minister, Head of the Treasury, Law Minister, Commander-in-Chief and Purohita.\n*   Inscriptions have mentioned the posts of \"**Mahamatya**\" and \"**Mahasandhivigrahaka**.\"\n*   Kings and officials who were appointed at different places in the kingdom used to be called \"**Rajasthaneeya**.\"\n*   In the Rashtrakuta Empire, some parts of the empire were under the direct rule of the emperor, while others were under the rule of feudatories.\n*   Feudatories used to obey the orders of the emperor, pay tribute, and, as per agreement, supply armies to the king. Rashtrakuta kings took effective measures to ensure the feudatories did not rebel.\n\n### Local Administrative System\n\nThe kings divided the kingdom into **Rashtra, Vishaya and Villages**.\n\n| Unit | Head |\n|---|---|\n| Rashtra | Mahasamantha / Mahamandaleshwara (belonging to the status of feudatories) |\n| District (Vishaya) | Vishayapati / Bhogapati |\n| Town | Nagarapati |\n| Village | Gramapati |\n\n*   The Village Assembly, known as \"**Mahajanam**,\" belonged to the Agraharas. Combined village assemblies generally had a farmer assembly.\n\n### Army Organisation\n\nRashtrakutas maintained big armies — according to Altekar''s opinion, their army numbered less than five lakhs.\n\n*   Generally, the major part of the army was kept at the capital. Infantry had greater importance within the army.\n*   Soldiers belonging to all Varnas are seen in their army organisation. Members of Jainism, such as **Bankaya and Sri Vijaya**, served as commanders-in-chief.\n*   For soldiers who died in war, the government used to give relief or a pension. Commanders, just like feudatories, enjoyed control over elephants, cavalry and chariots.\n*   Merchants, during the time of expeditions, used to sell supplies in large quantities for army needs.\n*   The police staff of those days consisted of \"**Chorodharinukulu**\" and \"**Dandaposikulu**.\"\n\n### Revenue Administration\n\n**Land tax** was the main source of income to the kingdom.\n\n*   Besides this, a tax known as \"**Padenala tax**\" was levied for army maintenance. \"**Sandhi Vigraha**\" tax was imposed for the defence of the kingdom.\n*   Heavy duties, in the form of transit duties and market duties, were collected from native and foreign merchants.\n*   As the monarchical form of government took root, the tax burden on the people began to increase. Taxes were, in many instances, collected in installments.\n*   Though taxes to a greater extent were remitted in the form of grain, payments in cash were also made.\n*   There used to be a tax on the wheels that drew water for the fields. Among the property taxes — besides land tax — were house tax, oil-mill tax, cattle tax, sheep-herd tax and shop tax.\n\n**Distribution of income:** Out of the income received, it is learnt that **three parts** went to army maintenance, **one part** was kept as reserve fund, and **1/12th share** was spent on charities, the palace, and maintenance of the kingdom.\n\n*   On average, **15%** was left to the **Kudivaram** (local village officers) for the use of the people. Besides this, **1/3%** from the **Melavaram** was given by the central government for people''s welfare activities.\n\n### Judicial Administration\n\nThe king remained the main judicial head. Judicial administration was carried through the \"**Dharmasana**\" that belonged to the Grama Sabha. Activities against the king and the monarchy were personally examined by the king, and punishments were awarded.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter6-rashtrakutas-p4", "partName": "Rashtrakuta Age: Economic Conditions", "bodyMd": "For the economic development of the Rashtrakuta times, agriculture, industries, village crafts and commerce all contributed.\n\n### Agriculture\n\nAgriculture was the primary basis for the economic organisation.\n\n*   Because of the land grants made for the maintenance of the employer, extensive lands were brought under cultivation. Arab historians wrote that in Western India, land was fertile and paddy and fruits were in plenty; jowar and bajra were cultivated.\n*   Mango and toddy gardens were kept in an extensive manner; in Karnataka, betel-nut gardens were grown extensively.\n*   In South India, agricultural land was specially referred to as \"**Yeripatti**\" or \"**Cheruvukattu**\" land. Agriculture that depended on tanks was very much prevalent across the entire peninsula.\n*   The agriculturist used to pay one-sixth or one-tenth of the crop from the land as tax. Besides this, for the maintenance of tanks and other irrigation sources, temples, and other expenses, agriculturists used to pay several local taxes.\n\n### Industries\n\nThe **textile industry** became famous among all the industries. Gujarat, Berar and Telangana remained important centres for these industries; cloth manufactured in greater quantities used to be exported to foreign countries.\n\n*   Persons belonging to different industries got organised into guilds.\n*   Besides the cloth and spinning industries, spices, sandalwood, timber, teakwood and leather industries also existed.\n\n### Varied Crafts\n\nIt is learnt that in the Rashtrakuta kingdom''s village administration, not only administrative officials but persons belonging to twelve crafts contributed, and with their cooperation village administration went on properly.\n\n*   **Twelve crafts:** Grasti, talari, astrologer, blacksmith, potter, carpenter, washerman, barber, village teacher, leather worker and weaver craftsmen were part of it.\n*   There used to be **eighteen crafts people** in total: farmers, sheep and cattle breeders, handloom weavers, clerks, shop owners, soldiers, artists, scholars and traders. Crafts thus found a place in the economic life of those times.\n\n### Trade and Commerce\n\nTrading did not actively develop very much.\n\n*   The work \"**Cosmos Indicopleustes**\" mentioned the trade that went on between the Eastern and Western coastal areas of South India and Southwest Asian countries. **Pepper, cardamom and pearls** had been the main articles of export.\n*   It was clearly evident that during this period there was an increase in the importing of horses from the northwestern border. **Textiles**, their related industries, metal industry and carpentry developed during this period.\n*   For the most part, all this trade got confined to the respective local areas only, but trade in the 11th and 12th centuries A.D. received new vigour.\n*   **Craft guilds** such as \"**Manigaram**,\" \"**Nanadeseeyulu**\" and \"**Tissaiva Aayutta Aainnurvar**\" systematised trade in the South. Among these, \"**Aainnurvar**\" or \"**Ayyavolepura**\" (\"500 Swamies\") was a trade guild that attained much prominence — its members visited all regions in India, as well as Persia and Southeast Asian countries, and were the judicial protectors of leading merchants.\n*   \"**Manigaram**\" was the local trade guild; \"**Nagaram**\" meant an organised sales centre in South India.\n*   Trade with China and Sri Vijaya countries was an event that deserved recognition during this age.\n*   **Port towns:** Mahabalipuram, Nagapattanam, Kaveripattanam, Motupalli and Krishnapatnam on the Eastern coast, and Chaul, Sopara and Calicut on the Western coast, were famous port towns.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter6-rashtrakutas-p5", "partName": "Rashtrakuta Age: Social Organisation", "bodyMd": "In South India and the Deccan, the social structure essentially remained as per the \"**Varnasrama Dharma**\" and the four-fold division of the varnas. Inscriptions of all periods state that rulers continued to uphold Varnasramadharma.\n\n### Brahmins\n\nAs Brahmins had prominence in religious and ritual rites, they occupied a unique and higher place in society.\n\n*   Just like commanders and ministers, Brahmins took up military and administrative professions, increasing their economic potential and playing a significant role.\n*   As Vedic literature was studied with tax exemption, they received Agraharas and \"**Brahmadeyas**\" from the kings as donations.\n*   From the 10th and 11th centuries A.D. onward, as temples developed as organisations, the Brahmadeyas declined; besides this, as the importance of the craftsmen and merchant classes began to increase, the economic condition of the Brahmins went down, with only theoretical religious significance remaining.\n*   On account of invasions, Brahmins resorted to migration; the Malkhed Rashtrakutas brought with them many Purohit families. Depending on the geographical divisions in which the Brahmins resided, sects developed among them.\n*   The ruling class desired that the Kshatriya position should belong to them.\n\n### Kshatriyas\n\nRashtrakutas have been considered \"**Sat-Kshatriyas**\" — a special sub-sect among the Kshatriyas.\n\n*   Ordinary Kshatriyas used to follow the rites and rituals of the \"Dvijas.\" In order to increase their status, they used to claim or announce that their dynasties belonged to either the Chandra or Surya royal lineages.\n\n### Vaisyas\n\nVaisyas belonged to the trading and agricultural classes, and were called \"**Komaties**\" and \"**Seths**.\" Right from ancient times, they had an integrated organisation for carrying on their trading activities.\n\n### Sudras\n\nSudras mainly took up the occupations of agriculture, agricultural labour and military service.\n\n*   Because of the increased difference between Sudras and Chandalas, Sudras gained higher status; \"Rattadi\" or \"Reddi,\" \"Vellala\" and \"Kapu\" were the major Sudra classes.\n\n### Craftsmen, Labourers and Lower Status Groups\n\nUntil the 10th and 11th centuries A.D., craftsmen, labourers and Chandalas remained in lower status only. \"**Vetti Chakiri**\" (forced/unpaid labour) was condemned in \"**Yasastilaka**,\" a literary work of the 10th century A.D.\n\n*   Another development worth noting is that, alongside the emergence of the landlord-farmer class, in Karnataka and Tamil Nadu areas in the 9th and 10th centuries A.D., \"**Gurava**\" community persons were able to obtain the position of Purohit in Siva temples.\n*   The emergence of the crafts class \"**Panchanamvaru**\" in Andhra and \"**Edangai**\" in Tamil Nadu, in the 11th century A.D., deserves to be noted significantly. New religious movements such as **Virasaivism, Aaradhya Saivam and Sri Vaishnavam** were patronised by these craft classes and landlord farmers.\n\n### Progress of Education and Literature\n\nThe Rashtrakuta emperors showed a lot of interest in the development of education and literature, giving importance to the standards of higher education in the kingdom.\n\n**Educational patronage**\n\n*   The Rashtrakuta emperors and rich people used to donate Agraharas and lands to Brahmins. Agraharas used to be centres for Sanskrit education.\n*   Temple areas also developed as educational institutions — for instance, in the \"**Trayipurusha**\" temple at Selorgi in the Karnataka region, where a big college was located, there were **27 hostels**.\n*   Jain and Buddhist religions also imparted their own religious education. Students came from distant places for the sake of receiving education. Important cities and holy places developed as educational centres — **Malkhed, Nasik and Paithan** remained important among them.\n*   Subjects of higher education included Vedas, Grammar, Astrology, Literature, Philosophy, Dharmashastra, Puranas, Jurisprudence and Sanskrit education. Rashtrakuta kings patronised Sanskrit and Kannada literature.\n*   The **Dhulia inscription**, issued by Dhruva, records that grant-recipients were scholars in Vedas, Vedangas, Itihasas, Puranas, Grammar, Philosophy and Logic — Sanskrit grammar was stated to be the base for all the Sastras or sciences.\n*   In 975 A.D., land admeasuring 50 Mattaras (possibly around 200 acres) was donated to a Mutt located in the **Bhujabheswara temple** in Dharwad Mandal — students there had free education and food. Grants made to temples also appear to have included books for educational institutions and students.\n*   Temples and Agraharas of those days became, respectively, educational institutions and colleges. There was a Sanskrit educational institution at **Kaulas Agrahara** in Dharwad Mandal, where 200 Brahmin families remained well-versed in Grammar, Moral Science, Literature and Puranas.\n\n**Literature**\n\nSanskrit was the official language of the Rashtrakuta kingdom, and Jaina literature flourished during their rule.\n\n*   During the time of King Krishna III, **Halayudha** wrote \"**Kavirahasyam**,\" explaining the use of \"Sanskrit Dhatuvulu,\" along with a prasasti (praise) of emperor Krishna.\n*   **Jinasena**, the revered teacher of Amoghavarsha, wrote \"**Adipurana**\" — the biography of the Jain Tirthankaras — which was completed by his disciple **Gunachandra**.\n*   Jinasena, in his \"**Parsvabhyudayam**,\" applied every sloka line of \"Meghasandesam\" to the life of Jain Parsva, achieving a wonderful coordination of meaning.\n*   During Amoghavarsha''s time, a work on grammar titled \"**Amoghavritti**\" was written by **Sakatayana**, and another work titled \"**Ganitasarasangraha**\" was written by **Ganitayana Viracharya**.\n*   \"**Kavirajamarga**,\" the first work on poetics in the Kannada language, was authored by **Amoghavarsha** himself, who also wrote \"**Ratnamalika**\" and \"**Neetikavyam**.\"\n*   **Ponna**, second in the \"triumvirate\" of Kannada writers, was the court poet of Krishna III; he wrote \"**Shanti Puranam**.\"\n*   **Pampa**, the poet who was first in the Kannada triumvirate and a court poet of the Vemulavada Chalukyas (feudatories of the Rashtrakutas), wrote \"**Adipurana**\" and \"**Vikramarjuna Vijayam**.\"\n\n### Architecture\n\nIn place of earlier brick-and-timber structures, stone structures and cave temples were witnessed from the **6th and 7th centuries A.D.**\n\n*   The present-day Tamil Nadu and Kerala areas have been referred to as \"lower Dravidadesa,\" and Andhra and Karnataka areas as \"upper Dravidadesa\" — structures in lower Dravidadesa adopted the **Dravidian style**, while those in upper Dravidadesa stood as symbols for the spread of the **Nagara style**. These are seen in the 7th and 8th centuries A.D.\n\n**Ellora cave temples**\n\n*   During the time of the Rashtrakutas, cave temples were carved at Ellora. In the first cave, **Dantidurga built the Dasavatara temple**; the Nandi mandapam in this temple stands as an ancient monument of Rashtrakuta art.\n*   The **Kailasanatha temple at Ellora** was the artistic creation of **Krishna I**.\n    *   This rock-cut temple has a sanctum sanctorum, one mukhamandapam, and one roofed verandah or hall — the lower storey was not put to use.\n    *   The temple''s surface shows decorative foundation and pillar-like walls with shelves, in the same model as the storey in use.\n    *   The front has a projected mandapam and entrances on the north, south and west; at the centre of the roof of the mandapam, a lotus with huge wings has been carved.\n    *   The superstructure was in four storeys. The main temple of worship, seven small temples around it, and one Nandi mandapam are all there — following South Indian convention, the temple plan consisted of eight temples of worship in total.\n    *   The Gopuram (tower) had two storeys, with the upper part roofed in the shape of a hut-cart.\n    *   This temple holds all the forms of Siva found in sculptural science, standing as a repository for wonderful masterpieces of sculpture depicting episodes from the Hindu Puranas.\n*   Other famous structures include the \"**Chhota Kailasa**\" temple among the Ellora cave temples, the mandapa in front of the 15th cave, and the **Jain temple at Pattadakal**.\n\n### Sculpture\n\nThe Dasavatara temple was built with wonderful sculpture — its walls are decorated with images of Saiva and Vaishnava gods and goddesses. Among all its sculptures, the statue of **Narasimha killing Hiranyakasipa** became the most famous.\n\n*   In the Kailasanatha temple at the same Ellora, Siva''s images have been carved in great style of art. The cave temples on **Elephanta Island** near Bombay also have highly imposing images carved in stone.\n*   Among these sculptures, the statue of **Maheswara**, carved with the faces of the Trimurtis, not only stands as testimony to the great sculptural art of the Rashtrakutas but also became world-famous. The carving of Maheswara as the personification of creation, stability and destruction is highly commendable.\n\n### Painting\n\nThe paintings made on the walls and under the roofs of the Kailasanadha temple at Ellora stand as the best model for the art of painting of Rashtrakuta times. The paintings of **Nataraja, Lingodbhava and Vidhyadharulu** have been rendered in attractive colours and striking descriptive style.\n\n*   In painting, for the most part, the sculpture style was closely followed. The paintings in the Jaina cave, called \"**Indraprabha**,\" at Ellora, are characterised by beautiful style and broad, wide-open eyes.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter6-rashtrakutas-p6", "partName": "Rashtrakuta Age: Religious Developments", "bodyMd": "### Saivism\n\nLiterary and inscriptional sources prove that Saivism and its sects were in greater prevalence.\n\n*   In the Deccan, during the time of the Rashtrakutas, the \"**Kalamukha**\" and \"**Kapalika**\" sects came into vogue.\n*   Inscriptions of the 9th century A.D. show a new non-Brahmin devotee group known as \"**Gurava**\" came into existence — they had to take an oath of brahmacharya and then serve as Purohits in Siva temples.\n*   **Srisailam** came to be recognised as a great \"Kapalika\" centre.\n\n### Vaishnavism\n\nThe title \"**Sree Prithvi Vallabha**,\" borne by the Rashtrakuta kings, may have been intended to declare themselves as an avatara or incarnation of Vishnu.\n\n*   The worshipping of the avataras became an important characteristic of Vaishnavism. In the Deccan and South India, the \"**Narayana**\" concept became famous; Narayana, and Vishnu in sitting and standing postures, received much propagation. The Alvars praised Vishnu in these forms.\n*   In all, Vishnu in \"**Sayana**\" or lying posture was regarded as the highest stage, symbolising other-worldliness. The worship of Goddess **Lakshmi** was another important characteristic of Vaishnavism.\n*   During this time, **Srirangam, Kanchi, Ahobilam, Tirumala and Sreekurmam** were Vaishnava centres that attained eminence.\n\n### Buddhism\n\n**Huien-Tsang**, the famous Chinese traveller, mentioned that in Andhra, all permanent Buddhist residences were desolate, though there were many temples with a Brahmin base.\n\n*   **Sriparvatam** attained the status of an important centre for the worship of \"**Vajrapani Dharani**.\"\n*   **Vajrayana Buddhism** gained ground from the 7th century onward. This formed as a result of changes that occurred within Mahayana Buddhism, and is also called \"**Tantric Buddhism**.\" It implies three practices — **Vajrayana, Sahajayana and Kala Chakrayana** — an understandable practice.\n*   The teachers or professors in this movement were called \"**Siddhus**\"; their total number was **84**. Kalpanika Vedakas such as \"Mantra,\" \"Mudra\" and \"Mandala\" are implied in Vajrayana worship.\n*   By the 12th and 13th centuries A.D., **Amaravati, Guntupalli, Sriparvatam or Nagarjuna Konda, and Salihundam** in Andhra, along with **Nagapatnam and Kanchi**, remained as famous Buddhist centres.\n\n### Jainism\n\nDuring the time of the Rashtrakutas, Jainism had greater prominence than Buddhism and enjoyed patronage and influence.\n\n*   Officials and chiefs who were Jains were appointed in the Andhra area, contributing to the spread of Jainism. **Akalanka, Pampa and Ponna** were great persons associated with Jainism.\n*   Famous Jain sects — **Mulasangha, Yavaneeya and Dravidasangha** — were in circulation in Andhra. **Perur, Danuvulapadu, Vemulapadu, Ramathirtham, Hanumakonda, Patancheru and Kollipaka** had been famous Jain centres in Andhra.\n*   In the Karnataka region, the Rashtrakutas were the chief patrons of Jainism. Rashtrakuta king **Amoghavarsha I** and the figure **Chamundaraya** were famous patrons of Jainism. Among the several Jain centres, **Sravanabelagola** was the most famous. In Karnataka, the **Digambara** Jain sect was in practice.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter6-rashtrakutas-p7", "partName": "Summary", "bodyMd": "Rashtrakutas played a prominent role in the history of South India and Telangana. It is a matter of pride that **Amoghavarsha I**, the Rashtrakuta emperor, was regarded by the contemporary Arab traveller **Suleiman** as one of the world''s renowned great emperors. The extension of the boundaries of their empire from Kanauj to Kanyakumari stands as testimony to their military victories, prowess and courage.\n\n*   Their system of administration achieved a fine balance between the strength of the central government and the freedom of local government. Through inscriptions and writings, the Rashtrakuta emperors contributed to the progress of the Kannada and Sanskrit languages.\n*   Though they patronised Jainism heavily, they showed a tolerant attitude not only towards Hinduism and Buddhism but also Islam, taking steps that contributed to their freedom and development.\n*   The fact that the Rashtrakutas, besides religion and literature, also contributed immensely to the development of architecture and sculpture indicates their great interest and love for cultural development.\n*   The **Dasavatara temple** at Ellora, the **Kailasanadha temple** at the same place, and the statue of **Maheswara** on Elephanta Island stand as great specimens of their rock-cut architecture; the knitting (lesu) method and feat of decoration make viewers spell-bound.\n\nNot only in Telangana, but even in the history of Dakshinapatha or South India as a whole, the period of Rashtrakuta rule glows in a striking and complete manner.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p1", "partName": "Sources of Kakatiya History", "bodyMd": "Two broad categories of sources help reconstruct Kakatiya history: **Archaeological** and **Literary**.\n\n**Archaeological sources** — Inscriptions, coins, and monuments.\n\n*   Inscriptions give information on socio-economic conditions, wars, conquests, and military affairs of the period.\n*   Gold, silver, and copper coins were minted; **Kesari Gaddyana, Nishka, Madas, and Kesaris and Pati-Varas** are the important coin types.\n*   Kakatiya monuments include the **Orugallu fort**, the famous **Kakatiya Toranas** (entrance arches), the **thousand-pillar temple at Anumakonda**, the **Ramappa temple at Palampet**, and other temples at **Pillalamarri, Nagulapadu, and Ghanapuram**. Sculptures on the walls, pillars, and doors of these temples reflect the drapery and ornament styles of the period, and some important events are carved as panels on temple walls.\n\n**Key inscriptions and what they reveal:**\n\n| Inscription | Issued by | Significance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Magallu inscription (A.D. 956) | Eastern Chalukyan king Danarnava | First mention of Kakartya Gundyana and his preceding generations |\n| Anumakonda 1000-pillar temple inscription (A.D. 1163) | Rudradeva | Declaration of independence |\n| Draksharamam temple inscription (A.D. 1158) | Prola II''s minister Inangala Brahmireddy | Records Prola II''s son Rudradeva''s accession |\n| Bayyaram inscription (A.D. 1219) | Ganapatideva''s sister Mailamba | Digging of Bayyaram tank (Khammam district) |\n| Motupalli inscription (A.D. 1244) | Ganapatideva | Remissions granted to foreign merchants |\n| Malkapuram inscription (A.D. 1261) | Ganapatideva | References to Vidyamandapas (educational institutions) |\n| Chandupatla inscription (A.D. 1289) | Rudramadevi''s servant Puvvulamummadi | Rudramadevi''s death while suppressing Ambadeva''s revolt |\n| Tripurantakam inscription (Nellore) (A.D. 1291) | Ambadeva, Rudrama''s official | Records Ambadeva''s victories |\n| Salakalaveedu inscription (A.D. 1317) | Pratapa Rudra II | Donation marking victory over the Pandyas |\n| Kolunupalli inscription (Mahaboobnagar) (A.D. 1321) | Pratapa Rudra II | Donations to Chenna Keshavaswamy and Narasimhaswamy temples |\n\n**Literary sources** are equally important, written in Telugu, Sanskrit, and other languages:\n\n| Book | Author | Language | Details |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pandiradya Charitam, Basavapuranam | Palkuriki Somanatha | Telugu | Conditions of Saivism and other religions |\n| Neetisaram / Neeti Sastramuktavali | Baddena | Telugu | Explains Raja neeti and Raja dharma |\n| Shivayogasaram | Kolanu Ganapatideva | Telugu | History of Indaluri Nayakas |\n| Kridhabhiramam | Venukonda Vallabharaya | Telugu | Life and conditions of people in Warangal fort |\n| Pratapa­rudriyam (Pratapa­rudra Yashobhushanam) | Vidyanatha | Sanskrit | Written by Pratapa Rudra II''s court poet |\n| Nrutyarathnavali | Jayapa Senani | Sanskrit | Characteristics of Nrutya and Natya |\n| Andhra Mahabharatam | Tikkana Somayaji | Telugu | Written at Ganapatideva''s court, at friend Manumasiddi''s request |\n| Nirvachanottara Ramayanam | Tikkana Somayaji | Telugu | Viraata Parvam |\n\nContemporaries writing in other languages also inform Kakatiya history: **Tariq-i-Farishta** (Farishta), **Futuh-us-Salatin** (Isami), **Tariq-i-Ferozshahi** (Barani), **Rehalla** (Ibn Battuta), and **\"The Travels\"** by Marco Polo, who recorded details of foreign trade at Motupalli during Rudramadevi''s period.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p2", "partName": "Kakatiyas – Identity and Early History", "bodyMd": "The identity and early history of the Kakatiyas is drawn mainly from inscriptions and literary works.\n\nThe Eastern Chalukyan king Danarnava''s **Magallu inscription (A.D. 956)** is the earliest to mention Kakartya Gundyana along with his preceding three generations. Some historians regard him as the founder of the dynasty.\n\nThere is no unanimity on the origin of the name \"Kakatiya\":\n\n*   One group holds that **Kakatipuram** village was their early home, and the family took its name from this place.\n*   A second group holds that the Kakatiyas worshipped **''Kakati,''** a local mother goddess (originally a Jain goddess), and derived their name from her.\n    *   Kumaraswamy Somapeethi, a 15th-century commentator on Vidyanatha''s Pratapa­rudriya, states that the family took the name Kakatiya because they worshipped Kakati, considered one of the forms of goddess Durga.\n    *   Vinukonda Vallabharaya''s Kridhabhiramam also mentions the idol of the Kakati goddess.\n    *   Sitabkhan''s 16th-century inscription records that Sitabkhan reinstated the Kakati idol in Warangal fort after it was destroyed by the Delhi Sultanate''s army.\n*   Kakati is not named among Durga''s forms in the Hindu puranas, but many village goddesses were locally accepted as forms of Durga — so Kakati came to be worshipped this way, alongside the village goddess Ekaveera.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p3", "partName": "Kakatiyas as Feudatories (A.D. 956–1162)", "bodyMd": "The early members of the family are described as **feudatory Kakatiyas**. From Kakartya Gundyana to Rudra I (Rudradeva) — that is, from A.D. 956 to 1162 — the rulers of present-day Karimnagar, Warangal, and Khammam were feudatories, first serving the Rashtrakutas and later the Kalyana Chalukyas.\n\nKakartya Gundyana played a crucial role in the conquest of Bezavada fort under the **Rashtrakuta king Krishna II** but lost his life in the Peruvangooru battle against the Vengi Chalukyas. Impressed by his feudatory''s faithfulness, the Rashtrakuta emperor appointed Gundyana''s son **Eriya** as administrator of Kurivadi (Warangal) seema, which was earlier under the Mudigonda Chalukyas. This appointment marked the beginning of Kakatiya rule and relations with the people of Telangana.\n\n### Betaraja I\n\nWhen Kakartya Gundyana died, Beta I was a small child, and protection of the kingdom fell to his maternal aunt Kamasani and her husband, **Veeriyala Errabhupati**. Errabhupati reinstated Beta I at Kakatipuram after defeating his enemies and expanded his rule to Koraviseema.\n\n*   After Errabhupati''s death, Beta faced renewed danger from the Vengi Chalukyas.\n*   On Kamasani''s advice, Beta accepted the **Kalyana Chalukya king as his overlord**, and with Chalukya support was able to face the Vengi threat.\n*   Beta I held the title **\"Kakatipuradhinatha.\"**\n\n### Prolaraja I (A.D. 1050–1075)\n\nSon of Betaraja I. His achievements are recorded in the Khajipeta, Pillalamarri, and Palampeta inscriptions.\n\n**Conquests:**\n\n*   Set right the chakrakuta; conquered the Konkana Mandala.\n*   Killed Gonaaraja of Purukuta.\n*   Defeated the Bhadranagapuradeeshvara (ruler near the Indravati River) and killed the Purukuta king in battle.\n*   Defeated Kodavarti Duggaraju (ruling near Warangal) and occupied his territory.\n\n**Status:** Kalyana Chalukya king Someshvara I, appreciating Prolaraja I''s achievements, appointed him feudatory ruler of Anumakonda Vishaya with hereditary rights — a recognition that enhanced Prola I''s status even though Anumakonda was already under his rule.\n\nMost of Prolaraja I''s victories are tied to his overlord''s campaigns. According to Bilhana''s Vikramankadeva Charitra, Someshvara I sent an army under his son Vikramaditya to conquer the Chola kingdom; this army moved through Konkan, Kerala, and the Pandya kingdoms to Gangaikondapuram and Kanchipuram, and then to Vengi and Chakrakuta. Prola I fought in the Konkana, Gangaikonda, and Kanchi campaigns and, after the overlord''s army moved to Chakrakuta, took charge there himself.\n\n### Betaraja II (A.D. 1075–1108)\n\nSon and successor of Prolaraja I.\n\n*   His **Anumakonda inscription** (A.D. 1070–80) does not mention the title \"Tribhuvanamalla,\" but his **Khajipeta inscription** of A.D. 1090 mentions this title along with another — \"Vikramachakriya.\"\n*   During this period there was fierce enmity between the Chola Chalukyas and Kalyana Chalukyas; to occupy Vengi, Vikramaditya likely sought Betaraja''s help. Betaraja''s minister Vaizadandanatha played an important role in this.\n*   Pleased with the help in conquering Vengi, Vikramaditya VI handed over Sabbi Mandala to Betaraja, enhancing his status.\n*   Betaraja II took titles including **Mahamandaleshvara, Tribhubanamalla, and Vikramachakri.**\n*   His spiritual master (guru) was **Rameshwara Deekshitha**.\n*   On Beta II''s death in A.D. 1108, his son **Durgarupati** ascended the throne.\n\n### Prolaraja II (A.D. 1115–1157)\n\nThe strongest ruler among the feudatory Kakatiyas, ascending the throne after his brother Durgaraju.\n\n**Military achievements:**\n\n*   Immediately after ascending, defeated the feudatory rulers of Kalyana Chalukyas on his kingdom''s borders — a fact supported by his son Rudradeva''s Thousand Pillar Temple inscription at Anumakonda.\n*   Defeated **Paramara Jaggadeva**, ruler of Kolanupaka.\n*   In **A.D. 1137**, together with his friend, Kalyana Chalukya king **Jagadekamalla II**, attacked and defeated the army of the Kanduri Chodas, killing their king Tailapa (who had earlier defeated Bheema Choda).\n*   Marched to Srisailam and erected a pillar of victory there.\n*   Made war with Rajendra Chola.\n\n**Relations with the Polavasa lords:** Friction developed between the Polavasa lords and the Kakatiyas — though both were feudatories of the Kalyana Chalukyas, family feuds over inheritance repeatedly put them on opposite (and sometimes the same) sides.\n\n**Significance:** Prolaraja II successfully checked the declining influence of the Kalyana Chalukyas in Telangana and made the Kakatiyas a strong regional force.\n\n**End:** Prolaraja II was killed by enemy forces. He was succeeded by his son **Rudradeva**, who ascended the throne in **A.D. 1158**, remained a feudatory until **A.D. 1162**, and then declared independence.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p4", "partName": "Sovereign / Independent Kakatiyas (A.D. 1163–1323)", "bodyMd": "Rudradeva, who ascended the throne in A.D. 1158 as a feudatory of the Kalyana Chalukyas, **declared independence in 1163**, as recorded in the Thousand Pillar Temple inscription of Anumakonda. From this point the Kakatiyas ruled as sovereigns.\n\nThe independent Kakatiya rulers, in order, were:\n\n| Ruler | Reign |\n|---|---|\n| Rudradeva | A.D. 1163–1195 |\n| Mahadeva | A.D. 1195–1199 |\n| Ganapatideva | A.D. 1199–1262 |\n| Rudramadevi | A.D. 1262–1289 |\n| Pratapa Rudra II | A.D. 1289–1323 |\n\nThey ruled with Warangal as their capital for about **160 years**, uniting the Telugu-speaking people under one banner and doing unique service to Telugu culture — earning permanent fame in the medieval history of Telangana and Dakshinapatha. The Sovereign Kakatiyas fought against contemporary Hindu kingdoms — the Yadavas, Hoyasalas, and Pandyas — and their rule finally ended with the attacks of the Delhi Sultans.\n\n### Rudradeva (A.D. 1158–1195)\n\nRudradeva''s reign falls into two phases: **feudatory (A.D. 1158–1163)** and **independent (A.D. 1163–1195)**. His mother was Muppamamba and father was Prola II. His minister Inangala Brahmireddy''s Draksharamam inscription describes him as a great warrior; Chalukya-Chola king Raja Raja II was his contemporary, and in this inscription he is called \"Anumakonda Rudra.\"\n\n**Conquests and expansion:**\n\n*   Defeated the rulers of Nagar (Karimnagar/Naganuru) — Dommaraja, Polavasa ruler Medaraju II, Myligadeva, and Chodo Udaya — establishing dominance over Telangana.\n*   Rudradeva first defeated **Mylagideva** and then attacked the Kalachuris; after occupying the Kalachuri kingdom, his territory extended north to the banks of the Godavari, and Sabbinadu also came under Kakatiya control.\n*   **Marched against the Kanduri Chodas**: after Tailapdeva''s death, his successors Kanduri Choda Bheema (capital at Vardhamanapura) and Kanduri Udayachoda contested the throne at Panugallu. Rudradeva took the opportunity, conquered the Kanduri kingdom, and merged it into his own — appointing Udayachoda as his feudatory and marrying his daughter Padmavati.\n*   The A.D. 1202 **Jammaluru inscription** of Cheruku Bollayya records that Rudradeva handed over the vast forest area of Srisailam to his friend Cheruku Bollayya. Through these victories, his influence extended to today''s Nalgonda and Mahaboobnagar areas, and his kingdom''s boundary reached the Godavari in the north, Bidar in the west, and Srisailam in the south.\n*   Issued a **Vijaya Sasana** (victory inscription) at Anumakonda and built a temple there consecrating the idols of Rudreshvara, Vasudeva, and Suryadeva.\n*   Declared war (\"Palanati war\") on those responsible for his father''s death, aiding Nalagamaraju; around the same time the Chalukyas of Pithapuram sought his friendship. He also planned to occupy the Velanati Choda kingdom, attacking their feudatory Kondapadamatis (supported by the Tripuranthakam inscription).\n*   Fought the Yadavas while trying to expand westward; in the **A.D. 1195 war with Yadava king Jaitugi (Jaitrapala)**, the Kakatiya army was defeated and **Rudradeva lost his life**.\n\n**Rudradeva''s greatness and administration:**\n\n*   Established sovereign Kakatiya power with **Warangal as capital.**\n*   His most able administrator was **Gangadhara of the Vellanki family** (inscription from Karimnagar, A.D. 1170), who joined as army chief under Prola II, administered Anumakonda, and later, under Rudradeva, excavated a water tank and built the Prasanna Keshava swamy temple at Anumakonda.\n*   Built the **Thousand Pillar Temple**; built, near Anumakonda, a new city for civil and military convenience — **Warangal (Orugallu) fort**, which became the new Kakatiya capital.\n*   A great patron of arts, poets, and literature — called \"**Kalpa taruvu**\" (wish-fulfilling tree) to scholars, per the Nayareddy Pillalamarri inscription of A.D. 1196.\n*   The Draksharamam inscription calls him \"**Vinayabhusana**.\" In a poem in Baddena''s Neetisaramuktavali, Rudradeva is named the author of Neetisastra.\n*   Rudradeva was **childless** and was succeeded by his brother **Mahadeva in A.D. 1195** (supported by the Khandavalli copper plates).\n\n### Mahadeva (A.D. 1155–1199, reign ~1195–1199)\n\nSon of Prolaraja II and elder among Rudradeva''s brothers. Very little inscriptional or literary information survives about him — two inscriptions: one from Sundela village, Peddapalli taluqa of Karimnagar (A.D. 1197), and a damaged, undated one from Warangal fort.\n\n*   Fought only one war in his life — against the Yadavas — and was defeated and killed in it.\n*   In the war with the Yadavas, Yadava king Jaitrapala defeated the \"Trilingadhipati\" (Mahadeva), based on the Paithan copper plates of Yadava Ramachandradeva, and then released young Ganapatideva from prison and placed him on the Warangal throne. This is also supported by literary works like Pratapa Charitra and Somadeva rajiyam.\n*   During his period a feudatory ruler rebelled against him.\n*   His queen was **Bayyamba**; they had a son, Ganapatideva, and two daughters, Mailamba and Kundamamba.\n*   Mahadeva was a follower of Saivism; his teacher was Dhruveshwara Pandita.\n*   His ablest army chief, **Recherla Rudra**, acted cleverly to protect Kakatiya rule when the kingdom was in crisis after the defeat by the Yadavas.\n\n### Ganapatideva (A.D. 1199–1262)\n\nRegarded as the greatest of the Kakatiya rulers, Ganapatideva''s father died in the war with Jaitugi, and young Ganapatideva became a prisoner. With efforts made by Recherla Rudra, Jaitugi released Ganapatideva and had him anointed to the throne (A.D. 1199), and Ganapatideva then worked to suppress internal rebellions and enemies such as Mallyala Chounda.\n\nGanapatideva united all Telugu-speaking people and ruled efficiently for **63 years (A.D. 1199–1262)**, administering both Telangana and Andhra regions from his capital, Warangal. His first surviving inscription belonging to his reign is the **Manthena inscription** from Karimnagar, issued 26 December 1199.\n\n**Why was Ganapatideva released?** Historians hold differing opinions:\n\n1.  Many historians believe Hoyasala king Ballala II intended to take advantage of the situation at Warangal to expand his own kingdom; since this would raise Hoyasala prestige, the Yadavas — their strong rivals — did not want this, and so Jaitugi released young Ganapatideva to occupy the Warangal throne, checking Hoyasala expansion. This is viewed by these historians as a political move by the Yadavas.\n2.  Moreover, Jaitugi gave his daughter **Somaladevi** in marriage to Ganapatideva.\n\n**Kakatiya-Yadava contemporary rulers table:**\n\n| King | Dynasty | Reign | Relation to Kakatiyas |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jaitugi (Jaitrapala) | Yadavas (Devagiri) | A.D. 1193–1199 | Ganapatideva''s father died fighting him |\n| Singhana | Yadavas | A.D. 1200–1247 | War with Hoyasalas, victory |\n| Krishana | Yadavas | A.D. 1246–1261 | — |\n| Mahadeva | Yadavas | A.D. 1261–1271 | War with Hoyasalas |\n| Ramachandradeva | Yadavas | A.D. 1271–1311 | War with Hoyasalas and defeat; later defeated by Delhi Sultans, accepted subordination |\n| Shankera deva | Yadavas | A.D. 1311–1313 | Attacked and killed by Mallik Kafur |\n| Harapala | Yadava | A.D. 1313–1317 | Defeated and killed by Alauddin Khilji''s son Mubarak Shah; downfall of Yadava kingdom |\n\n**Kakatiya-Hoyasala contemporary rulers table:**\n\n| King | Reign | Relation to Kakatiyas |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bhallala II | A.D. 1170–1220 | Defeat of Yadava king Billalmadeva |\n| Narasimha II | A.D. 1220–1254 | — |\n| Narasimha III | A.D. 1254–1295 | War with Yadava Ramachandradeva (victory); defeated by Pandya king Kulashekara |\n| Bhallala III | A.D. 1295–1342 | Attacked Yadava kingdom (A.D. 1305), defeated Ramachandra''s army |\n\n**Coastal conquests and expansion (from A.D. 1201):** By the time Ganapatideva ascended the throne, the older ruling families of the coastal region — Eastern Chalukyas, Chola-Chalukyas, Kalyana Chalukyas — had grown weak. The **Velanati kingdom** was powerful; its ruler **Prudhvishwara** (grandson of Chola Rajendra II) had made neighbouring rulers his feudatories, including the Kolanu kings (Kolleru), Kotas of Guntur, Prakasham Konidena Telugu Cholas (Nellore, Kadapa, Chengalpattu), Gudimetta Chagis (Krishna bank), and Natavadi kings (Khammam district), with some coastal areas under the Kalinga Gangas.\n\n*   Ganapatideva invaded the coastal region in A.D. 1201; the **Bezavada inscription** (A.D. 1201, issued by Nattavadi Rudra, Ganapatideva''s brother-in-law) records his army''s victory over Prudhvishwara''s forces, and the Kota Nayakas of Dharani Kota were made feudatories.\n*   He then invaded **Divi Seema**, ruled by the Ayyavamsha rulers. Their ruler **Pinna Choda** fought fiercely but was defeated; Ganapatideva''s army chief **Malyala Chounda** took the titles \"Dwipiluntaka\" and \"Divichoorakaara\" for this conquest.\n*   As a political strategy, Ganapatideva handed Divi Seema **back** to Pinna Choda, securing support for further expansion; this led to matrimonial ties — Ayyavamsha Pinnachoda gave his two daughters, **Naramba and Peramba**, in marriage to Ganapatideva, and Pinnachoda''s son **Jaayapa** was appointed **Gajashahini** in the Kakatiya army.\n*   With the Ayyavamshas as feudatories, Prudhvishwara''s strength weakened further; Nellore''s Tikkaraju and others also joined Ganapatideva''s side. **Prudhvishwara lost his life in the war** — Ganapatideva''s inscriptions describe him as having \"played with Prudhvishwara''s head as a ball\" (_prudhvishwara shirah kanduka kreeda vinodah_). The last Ayyavamsha inscription (from Anantha, treasurer of Prudhvishwara, A.D. 1206) suggests Prudhvishwara died in defeat around this time. Ganapatideva appointed **Jayapa Senani as viceroy** of this region.\n\n**Southern conquests (A.D. 1207–1209):** Ganapatideva waged two southern expeditions, driven by the contemporary political situation of the **Telugu Chodas of Nellore**.\n\n*   Telugu Choda king **Nallasiddi** had declared Nellore''s independence taking advantage of a weakening Chola emperor Rajadhiraja II. His successors — Betaraja I, Errasiddi (with sons Manumasiddi I and Tammasiddi) — followed.\n*   Chola emperor Kulathonga III interfered in Telugu Choda politics: when Manumasiddi I ascended, Kulothunga III invaded Nellore and defeated him, then handed the Nellore throne back to Nallasiddi as his feudatory.\n*   Later, Manumasiddi I''s son **Tikka Bhupala** sought Ganapatideva''s help against Tammasiddi. Ganapatideva''s army invaded the Nellore Choda kingdom (**A.D. 1207–1208**), defeated Tammasiddi, and installed Tikkabhupala as ruler of Nellore — supported by the Chebrolu inscription (A.D. 1228) and Jayapasenani''s Ganpeshwaram inscription (A.D. 1231). This was Ganapatideva''s first southern victory; his army also attacked **Kanchi**, the Chola capital.\n\n**Kalinga conquest (A.D. 1211–1212):** After the Velanadu and Nellore victories, Ganapatideva sent his armies to Kalinga (earlier under Prudhvishwara). Army chief **Induluri Somaya mantri** led the campaign; Kalinga king **Aniyanka Bheema III** faced the Kakatiya army, but neither side was decisively victorious. Ganapatideva made matrimonial alliances instead: he married his elder daughter **Ganapaamba** to Kota Rudra''s son Myna Beta, and his second daughter **Rudramadevi** to Needadavolu Chalukya king Veerabhadra. Later, under Aniyanka Bheema''s son Narasimhadeva, the Kakatiya army attacked and conquered Vengi and Kalinga again (A.D. 1238).\n\n**Later career and Muthukuru defeat:**\n\n*   In A.D. 1248, Ganapatideva''s friend Choda Tikkaraju died; his son Manumasidde II (Veera Ganda Gopala) lost his kingdom to rival kinsmen and sent his minister-poet Tikkana Somayaji to Ganapatideva''s court for help. Ganapatideva sent an army under **Samantha Bhoja**, defeated Manumasiddi''s enemies Bayyana and Akkana, occupied Kanchi (capital of Kulothunga/Rajendra III Chola), and later defeated a combined Dravida-Karnataka army at Palleyuru in Tanjore district. By A.D. 1250, Manumasiddi II was reinstalled at Nellore.\n*   Kakatiya army also defeated Vaidumba king Rakkasi Gangadi, with Kayastha **Gangaya Sayani** (Manumasiddi''s army chief) participating. Ganapatideva returned the occupied kingdom and recognized Gangaya Sayani''s capability, appointing him **Bahattar Niyogadhipati** (supervisor over the seventy-two Niyogas) — supported by the Tripurantakam inscription of A.D. 1250.\n*   **Kakatiya–Yadava relations** remained friendly under contemporary Yadava king Singhana; no war is recorded between them. Yadava king Singhana''s son Permadideva reportedly lived at Ganapatideva''s court for a period.\n*   **War with Pandya king Jataverman Sundar Pandya (A.D. 1257–1263):** By A.D. 1257, Sundar Pandya invaded the Nellore Telugu Choda kingdom, which had earlier accepted his overlordship; Manumasiddi II sought help from the Kakatiya, Yadava, and Bana kings.\n\n*    The Pandya king split his army into two divisions — one against Draksharamam (defeated by the Kakatiya army, with its commander Koppera Zingana accepting Ganapatideva''s overlordship), and a second against Manumasiddi II at Nellore. By **A.D. 1263**, the Pandya army reached Muthukuru near Nellore; in the fierce battle, the Kakatiya army and its allies were **defeated** — Veera Ganda Gopala lost his life and Manumasiddi died. Nellore became part of the Pandya kingdom. \n*   This was the **only defeat** in Ganapatideva''s 62-year career, and it led him to hand over administration to his daughter **Rudramadevi**.\n\n**Importance of Ganapatideva:** A great emperor and political strategist, he saved the Kakatiya kingdom from internal disturbances, rebellions, and external attacks; strengthened his power through matrimonial alliances and diplomacy; transferred the capital from Anumakonda to Warangal; and completed the construction of the new capital city Orugallu, begun by his grandfather Prola and uncle Rudradeva. \n\n*   His feudatories and army chiefs included Recherla Rudra, the Malyala Nayakas (Malyala Choda and his son Katama), Jayapa of Divi Seema (Gajasahini), Nellore''s Manumasiddi (a good friend), Induluri Somaya mantri, Prolabhima Nayaka, and Kayastha Gangaya Sahini.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p5", "partName": "Rudramadevi (A.D. 1262–1289)", "bodyMd": "Rudramadevi is considered one of the most meritorious administrators in the history of medieval India, especially South India. Daughter of Somamba and Ganapatideva, she was trained under her father''s supervision in political administration and army affairs. Since Ganapatideva had no sons, he declared her his successor and handed over administration to her in **A.D. 1262**, after his defeat at Muthukuru.\n\n**Marco Polo''s account:** The Venetian traveller Marco Polo visited India during Rudramadevi''s period, landing at the **Motupalli port**. He praised her administrative capabilities highly, along with the trade and commerce, export and import activity at the port, the conduct of port officials, and the care taken of foreign traders under her arrangements.\n\n### Administration and Rebellions\n\nWhen Rudramadevi took over administration, Kakatiya power was already under strain. Main causes: rebellion of feudatories and administrators, the defeat by the Pandyas at Muthukuru, and the threat of the Yadava king Mahadeva (A.D. 1262–1271), who planned to occupy Kakatiya territory.\n\n*   During this critical period, **Recherla Prasadaditya** took responsibility for protecting the kingdom; the Velugoti Vanshavali records him with titles like \"Kakatiya vamsha sthapanacharya\" and \"Rayapithamahanka.\"\n*   Other loyal officials who stood guard for Rudramadevi included Kayastha Jennigadeva, Kayastha Tripurari, Malyala Gundiya Nayaka, and Madaya Nayaka, and Mahapradhana Kandara Nayaka.\n*   According to Ekamranatha''s Pratapa Charitra, her step-brothers **Harihara and Murari deva rebelled** against her; she suppressed these rebellions with the support of her able army chiefs.\n*   Eastern Ganga king **Narasimha deva** invaded and occupied the Vengi kingdom. \n\n*   In **A.D. 1274**, his son **Bhanudeva I** invaded the Kakatiya kingdom; Rudramadevi''s army chiefs **Potinayaka and Proli nayaka** defeated this invasion — a fact supported by the Draksharamam temple inscription.\n*   The **Godavari River became the border** between the Kakatiya and Ganga kingdoms afterward.\n\n### Yadava Invasion on the Kakatiya Kingdom\n\nWhile Rudramadevi was suppressing internal rebellions, Yadava king Mahadeva invaded. She personally faced the Yadava army and won a decisive victory, driving the enemy back to the **Devagiri fort**. Her army chief **Sindhu Bhairava** played an important role in this war (recorded in an inscription found at Bidar fort).\n\n*   A recently found inscription from **Allapadu** records that Yadava king Ellanadeva married Rudramadevi''s daughter.\n*   Hemadri, in his work \"Vratakhandam,\" deliberately did not mention that his lord (the Yadava king) was defeated at the hands of a woman.\n*   As a symbol of this historic victory, Rudramadevi took the title **\"RayaGaja kesari\"** and constructed the Ranga mandapam at the Swayambhu temple in Warangal fort. The title Rayagaja Kesari is also mentioned in her Bidar fort inscription.\n\n### Rebellion of the Kayastha Ambadeva\n\nThe most serious challenge of her reign came in its final years from Kayastha **Ambadeva**, recorded in the Tripuranthakam and Nandaluru inscriptions.\n\n*   Kayastha **Jennigadeva** was Rudramadevi''s most faithful subordinate, ruling the Nandluru region (Kadapa district); on her orders, in A.D. 1264 he ended Pandya administration there.\n*   After Jennigadeva, his younger brother **Tripuranthaka** ruled the region (A.D. 1270–1272), remaining faithful to Rudramadevi.\n*   After Tripuranthaka, his younger brother **Ambadeva** became ruler of the region — but he **rejected Rudramadevi''s subordination and rebelled**, defeated her feudatories, and made them his own subordinates. He allied with her enemies, the Pandyas and Yadavas.\n*   According to the Tripuranthakam inscription (A.D. 1290), Ambadeva took titles including \"Pandya rajanya-priya-preshita-chandavedanda-Turugasthaara-viraajmaana-samshoshita-souhaardra\" and \"Devagiri-rajaprasarpita-praabhrutamani-kanakabhushana.\"\n*   Ambadeva defeated Rudramadevi''s regent in the Guntur region, **Sripati Ganapati**, in A.D. 1273, taking the title **\"Raya Sahasra malla,\"** and occupied Valluru (under Pandya rule).\n*   By A.D. 1287, Ambadeva occupied **Gandikota, Mulikinadu, Renadu, Perakallu, Sakilinadu, and Pottapinadu**, ruling from Vallura as capital (recorded in the Attirala inscription).\n\n### Death of Rudramadevi\n\nAmbadeva''s expansion in all directions posed a grave danger to Rudramadevi''s power. In **A.D. 1286, Ambadeva defeated the Pandyas**; the defeated Pandya king Maraverman Kulashekara fled the battlefield, and Ambadeva grew stronger.\n\nAmbadeva''s own Tripuranthakam inscription describes his victories. According to the recently found inscription at **Chandupatla village (Nalgonda district)**, **Rudramadevi and her chief Mallikarjuna rayalu lost their lives while suppressing Ambadeva''s rebellion**. Mallikarjuna''s servant Puvvalamummadi donated lands to the Somanatha temple in Chandupatla village to commemorate this.\n\nThe Chandupatla inscription does not give details of when or where Rudramadevi actually died — even today there is no complete historical source establishing the exact circumstances of her death. The majority view among historians is that she died before **27 November 1289**, while suppressing Ambadeva''s rebellion, since no inscription of hers is found later than that date.\n\n### Greatness of Rudramadevi\n\nRudramadevi ruled very efficiently from Orugallu as capital. Though a woman, she received thorough training in administrative affairs under her father''s supervision and conducted governance with the welfare of the people as her aim. She faced enemy attacks daringly and had faithful, able army chiefs and feudatories who stood with her. Her only failure was the Ambadeva rebellion.\n\n*   Among her army chiefs and feudatories: **Malyala, Recherla, Gona family, Reddy Nayakulu, and Velama families.** Among the Velama families, **Recherla Prasaditya** earned particular fame during her period. The **Kayastha kings**, who ruled prior to Ambadeva''s rebellion, protected the south-western region of the kingdom from enemy attacks.\n*   Her husband was **Chalukya Veerabhadra** of the Nidadavolu branch. Among her employees, Devagiri king Singhana''s son **Sarangapani** was important; **Ranaka Gopadevaraju** of the ''Are'' community was also in her service.\n*   Rudramadevi had **three daughters**: **Mummadamma, Rudrama, and Ruyyamma**. Since she had no son, her daughter Mummadamma''s son **Pratapa Rudra II** was declared her successor. Mummadamma''s husband was Mahadeva, a Kakatiya prince. Her second daughter married the Yadava prince Ellanadeva. Her youngest daughter''s husband was Induluri nayaka.\n*   In the medieval history of the Deccan, Rudramadevi ruled prior to Chanda Bibi and Hayat Bakshi Begum, and earned lasting fame among them.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p6", "partName": "Pratapa Rudra II (A.D. 1290–1323)", "bodyMd": "The last ruler of the Kakatiya dynasty. Prior to Ambadeva''s rebellion, Rudramadevi had already declared Pratapa Rudra II as her successor. He took over the administration in **A.D. 1290**, ruling with two main aims: **1)** to finish off the danger arising from Ambadeva''s rebellion, and **2)** to protect the Kakatiya kingdom from enemy attacks.\n\n**Army reorganization:** To fulfil these aims, Pratapa Rudra II overhauled the army, handing control to the **Nayakas** — he appointed **77 Nayakas**, explained to them how many soldiers each needed to maintain, and how much land and how many villages would be under their control, arranging for special army training.\n\n**Campaigns against Ambadeva and the Yadavas/Pandyas:**\n\n*   Prepared a three-phase strategy against Ambadeva and his allies, the Yadavas and Pandyas.\n*   In **A.D. 1291**, sent a huge army against Tripuranthakam, Ambadeva''s centre, under **Manumagannayya** (son of Kolani Somana mantri) and **Ayyadeva** (son of Induluri Pedda Gannayya mantri). The Kakatiya army won a great victory here — recorded in the Tripuranthakam inscription issued by Induluri Annayya (A.D. 1291) and supported by Parabrahma Sastry''s account. No further inscription of Ambadeva is found at Tripuranthakam after A.D. 1291.\n*   Ambadeva''s son and successor **Murari II** ruled independently from Mulkinadu. Pratapa Rudra II sent a huge army under **Maharaya Patta Sahini Somaya** and **Induluri Annaya** against him; this war (before A.D. 1304) ended in a great Kakatiya victory, and the **Kayastha kingdom was merged into the Pratapa Rudra II''s kingdom**, with **Soma Nayaka** appointed as administrator.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p7", "partName": "Attacks of the Delhi Sultanate on Orugallu", "bodyMd": "During Pratapa Rudra II''s period, the Kakatiya kingdom — like the other contemporary kingdoms of the Yadavas, Hoyasalas, and Pandyas — faced attacks from the Delhi Sultanate.\n\n*   In **A.D. 1295**, Delhi Sultan **Jalaluddin Khalji** sent a huge army under his son-in-law **Garshasp Malik (Garshans-i-Malik)** against Yadava Ramachandra''s capital, **Devagiri**. The Yadava army was defeated; Ramachandradeva accepted Khalji overlordship, paid huge tribute in money and gold, and made peace. This defeat of the Yadavas endangered the standing of other Hindu kings in the region.\n*   Pratapa Rudra II, in response, strengthened his army and defenses — building up infantry, war horses, elephants, and archers.\n\n### First Muslim Attack on Telangana (A.D. 1303)\n\nIn **A.D. 1296**, **Garshasp Malik treacherously killed Jalaluddin Khalji**, his uncle and the Sultan of Delhi, and ascended the throne as **Allauddin Khalji**, ruling until A.D. 1316.\n\n*   For the first time, Allauddin Khalji sent his army against **Trilinga/Telangana/Orugallu in A.D. 1303**. Delhi Sultanate army chiefs **Malik Fakruddin Jana and Zhujas of Kara** entered Telangana.\n*   The historic battle took place at **Upperpalli village, Karimnagar district**; Kakatiya army chiefs **Potuganti Myli** and **Venna, son of Recherla Prasadaditya**, showed courage and talent, and repelled the Sultan''s army — recorded in the Telugu writing _Velogotivari Vamshavali_.\n\n### Second Attack on Orugallu by the Delhi Sultanate (A.D. 1309)\n\nAllauddin Khalji, driven by ambition for a larger empire and by the sting of his earlier failure in Telangana, sent a huge army in **A.D. 1309** under the leadership of **Malik Kafur and Khwaja Haji**.\n\n*   The Sultan''s contingent entered Warangal via Devagiri, starting in **October 1309**.\n*   In **February 1310**, a ferocious battle took place between the two sides; ultimately the Kakatiya emperor **accepted defeat and made a peace settlement**. Till Allauddin Khalji''s death, Pratapa Rudra **paid tribute every year** and remained faithful to the Delhi Sultan.\n*   Pratapa Rudra''s feudatories rebelled against him because of this defeat and subordination: Nellore Telugu Choda king **Ranganatha** rebelled in the south, and Vaidumba chief **Mallideva**, based at Gandikota, also rebelled. Pratapa Rudra sent an army under **Juttayalenka Gonkayareddy** to suppress these rebels; after defeating Mallideva, Gonkayareddy was appointed the king''s regent over Gandikota and nearby areas.\n*   In **A.D. 1311**, the Delhi Sultan attacked the Pandya kingdom; Pratapa Rudra participated with his army at the Sultan''s request. Sultan''s chief **Malik Kafur** marched up to Madurai. Pratapa Rudra''s chief **Muppidi Nayaka** played an important role in these campaigns.\n*   In **A.D. 1316, Allauddin Khalji died**, and his army returned to Delhi. This allowed Pratapa Rudra''s army to fight the Nellore ruler, **Ballala III of the Hoyasalas**, and the Pandya king **Veera Pandya**.\n*   Pratapa Rudra''s army chief defeated Hoyasala Ballala III and his ally, the Shambhuvaraya king of Padaiveedu, and the Chandragiri ruler Yadavaraya, in a fierce battle; Kanchi city was occupied — recorded in the Draksharamam temple inscription, preserved in the Mackenzie manuscripts. \n\n*   Kakatiya chiefs Muppidi Nayaka, Recherla Eradacha, and Manaveera and Deveri nayaka supported him in this campaign against the Pandya army; Sundara Pandya was placed on the Pandya throne as a result. \n*   As a mark of victory, Deveri nayaka made donations to the Ranganatha temple of Salakalaveedu village on the Kaveri Island.\n\n*   Pratapa Rudra extended his dominion in the Dakshinapatha during **A.D. 1312–1316**.\n\n### Pratapa Rudra II and the Politics of the Delhi Sultans (A.D. 1316–1317)\n\nAfter Allauddin Khalji''s death in A.D. 1316, his army chief **Malik Kafur** put the Sultan''s son **Shahboddin** on the throne and continued administration — but was murdered soon after. **Taking this as an opportunity, Pratapa Rudra stopped paying yearly tribute.** Similarly, in the Yadava kingdom, Ramachandradeva''s son-in-law **Harapaladeva** rebelled against the Sultan.\n\n*   In this changed political scenario, Allauddin Khalji''s other son **Qutubuddin Mubarak Shah** dethroned Shahboddin and ascended the Delhi throne. He sent an army against those Dakshinapatha kings who had earlier been his father''s feudatories.\n*   In **A.D. 1318**, he invaded Devagiri first, defeating and killing Harapala; Devagiri came under Delhi''s control. Mubarak Shah then sent his army chief **Khusru Khan** against the Kakatiya kingdom. Historians such as Amir Khusru and Isami give differing accounts of this campaign; on the whole, a mutually agreed settlement was reached, and Pratapa Rudra accepted feudatory status under Mubarak Shah, paying tribute.\n\n**War with Kampili (A.D. 1318):** Pratapa Rudra fought a war with Kampili ruler **Kumara Rama** in A.D. 1318. The main reason was that Kumara Rama had insulted Pratapa Rudra and disapproved of his helping Ballala III of the Hoyasala dynasty. \\[Discrepancy between sources: the Kannada work _Kumara Ramana Charitra_ and the Telugu work _Bheemeshwara Puranam_ give different versions of the outcome — it seems that both sides may have claimed victory in this war.\\]\n\n### Ghiyasuddin Tughluq Ascends the Delhi Sultanate (A.D. 1320)\n\nIn A.D. 1318, Delhi court politics reached a peak of factionalism. Allauddin Khalji''s son Qutubuddin ordered his army chief **Khusru Khan** to invade Mathura; Khusru Khan was ultimately murdered by the Turkey nobles, who then appointed **Ghiyasuddin Tughluq** as Delhi Sultan (A.D. 1320), starting the **Tughluq dynasty**.\n\n*   Ghiyasuddin Tughluq''s son **Ulug Khan** was sent to invade Warangal — first in **A.D. 1323**. Ulugh Khan failed in this first attempt, but on his father''s orders made a second attack on Orugallu the same year.\n*   Pratapa Rudra II and his army fought ferociously, but this time **Ulug Khan won**. The wealth and fort of Warangal became the target of the Sultanate army. While being taken captive to Delhi, **Pratapa Rudra escaped and committed suicide by jumping into the Narmada River** — recorded in the **Vilasa copper plate inscription of Masunuri Prolaya Nayaka**.\n*   With Pratapa Rudra II''s death, the **Kakatiya dynasty came to an end**. Telangana and Orugallu were merged into the Delhi Sultanate empire, and Orugallu''s name was changed to **Sultanpur**.\n\n**Pratapa Rudra II''s feudatories, army chiefs, and ministers:** Kayastha Mallideva, Recherla Mummadi Nayaka, Recherla Venna (son of Recherla Prasadaditya), and his grandchildren Eradacha and Nalladacha were his key supporters.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p8", "partName": "Delhi Sultanate Attacks on Dakshinapatha — Downfall of the Kakatiya Kingdom", "bodyMd": "Under the Kakatiyas, today''s Telangana and Andhra regions were united, and language and culture progressed. But the contemporary Deccan Hindu kingdoms — Hoyasala, Pandya, Yadava, and Kakatiya — all ended in the attacks of the Delhi Sultans, laying the foundation for the spread of Islam and Turkish political authority in the Dakshinapatha.\n\n*   Muslim attacks on North India began around **A.D. 950**, whereas in South India they began only in **A.D. 1303**. The Slave dynasty ruled North India between A.D. 1206–1294; occupied with Mongol attacks and consolidating the north, and not cutting off relations with Afghanistan, the Delhi Sultans did not attempt southern conquests during this period. \n\n*   But by the time of the Khalji dynasty (beginning with Jalaluddin, continued by his son-in-law Allauddin Khalji), the Mongols were brought under control and the Rajputs were completely defeated.\n\n*   Camped at Kara, Allauddin Khalji concluded that conquering the south was the way to meet the expenses of his army. By that time Devagiri was already occupied, so the \"second step\" fell invariably on Andhra. The south Indian kingdoms during the Muslim attacks had no strategy to work together or treat the threat as a common disaster — this lack of unity was a pathetic feature of the era''s politics.\n\n**Number of Turkish attacks:** Ekamranatha''s Pratapa Charitra, Masunuri Prolaya Nayaka''s Vilasa copper plate, the Anitally and Kaluvacheruvu copper plates, and other local histories record **eight Turkish attacks** during Pratapa Rudra II''s period, though Muslim historians record only five.\n\n1.  **A.D. 1303** — Malik Fakruddin Juna and Zaju attacked Telangana via Bengal for the first time; the two armies fought near Upperapalli in Karimnagar district, where Kakatiya chiefs Recherla Venna and Potuganti Myli led the Kakatiya army. **The Khalji army was defeated.**\n2.  **A.D. 1309** — Second invasion, led by Malik Naib Kafur and Khwajahaji (described by contemporary historians Amir Khusru and Barani). The Khalji army reached Devagiri, then crossed Basirghar and Kunarbal to reach Anumakonda; after a month-long siege they occupied the mud fort, then attacked the stone fort, burning nearby villages. In this helpless situation, Pratapa Rudra II agreed to a treaty, paying a huge yearly tribute to Malik Kafur.\n3.  **Third attack** — After Allauddin Khalji''s death, Pratapa Rudra stopped paying tribute; Qutubuddin Mubarak (the Delhi Sultan at that time) sent Khusru Khan against Orugallu. Pratapa Rudra was defeated and again accepted to pay tribute via a peace treaty.\n4.  **A.D. 1321–22 — Fourth attack**\n    *   In 1320 Khalji rule ended at Delhi and Tughluq rule began; Ghiyasuddin Tughluq, wanting to build a Muslim rule in the south instead of merely collecting tribute, sent his son **Juna Khan**, who reached Orugallu via Devagiri and attacked for six months.\n    *   When the Tughluq army was about to win, poet **Ubaid spread a rumour of the Delhi Sultan''s death**, and two army chiefs, **Taimur and Tigrin, rebelled** and began discussions with Pratapa Rudra; Juna Khan stopped the attack and returned to Delhi, and while retreating, the Kakatiya army caused them great loss and trouble.\n5.  **A.D. 1323 — Fifth attack**\n    *   Juna Khan reached Devagiri, and with additional reinforcements from Delhi occupied Bidar and Bodhan, then attacked Orugallu; the siege continued for five months, emptying Pratapa Rudra''s treasury and creating fear and panic among the people through the wanton destruction of villages.\n    *   **Jealousy between the Velamas and Reddys reached its peak** — Pratapa Rudra''s respect for the Velamas was resented by the Reddys, and army chief **Bobba Reddy left the war with his army**; the protection of the Kakatiya kingdom was fractured by these **caste differences**, and **Pratapa Rudra was defeated by Juna Khan and taken captive**. While being taken to Delhi, he escaped and committed suicide by jumping into the Narmada River. The Kakatiya kingdom became part of the Delhi Sultanate; Orugallu, popularly known as \"Andhra nagari,\" was changed to **Sultanpur**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p9", "partName": "Kakatiya Administration", "bodyMd": "The Kakatiyas ruled from **A.D. 1163 to 1323**, with Orugallu as their capital city. Historians hold differing opinions on the exact form and nature of their administration; the Kakatiyas broadly followed the traditional system of administration already existing in India and South India — as in all Hindu kingdoms, the son held the hereditary right to succeed the throne. Though of **Sudra** origin, the Kakatiyas followed ancient Raja Dharma and Raja Neeti as laid down by the ancient law-writers.\n\n*   Since Ganapatideva had no sons, he trained his daughter Rudramadevi in political administration and declared her his successor, thus **starting a new tradition**.\n*   The **Manadala system** was a new system introduced by the Kakatiyas in the central government; they encouraged powerful **Mandalikas** and cultivated friendship with them.\n*   Rudramadevi declared her grandson Pratapa Rudra as her successor, and he ascended the throne after her.\n\n**Nature of the administration — historian debate:**\n\n*   One opinion holds that the Kakatiyas followed a **hereditary despotic political system**.\n*   According to **P.V. Parabrahma Sastry**, their administration was built on **decentralization of power** — proven by the strengthening of feudatories/the feudal system, though there are differing opinions on whether this feudatory system strengthened or weakened Kakatiya administration.\n*   Historian **C.V. Ramachandra Rao** held that Kakatiya administration was very close to a **\"segmentary state\"**, as proposed by American historian Burton Stein.\n*   Among all these opinions, the **most widely accepted** view is that the Kakatiya administration was a **decentralized system**.\n\n**Administrative lineage:** The decentralized, seventy-two Niyoga administrative system is understood to follow the pattern of the Western Chalukya administration. The Kakatiya **Nayankara system** is considered, per Parabrahma Sastry, the **precursor to the Vijayanagara period''s Amara Nayaka system**. Administration moved step by step from village, to district, to state, to central government, with powers clearly defined at each level, though feudatories also played an important role alongside these formal levels.\n\n**Titles and governance style:** Though the emperor held all power, the Kakatiya rulers did not adopt grand titles like \"Maharajadhiraja\" or \"Parameshwara\" — instead they used simple titles similar to their own feudatories, such as \"Samadigata Panch mahasabda\" and \"Mahamandaleshwara.\" They did not rule as autocrats regarding themselves as sovereign monarchs. The Kakatiyas introduced a new type of administration in which they dominated the internal administration of their feudatories while ensuring feudatory power never grew too strong, and made appointments of administrative officials throughout the empire.\n\n*   During the Kakatiya period, feudatories rebelled only once — the **A.D. 1290 rebellion of Kayastha Ambadeva** — because feudatories were generally treated as **co-rulers**. The Kakatiyas respected local traditions and continued them in conquered regions; for example, they took no action against their feudatory Cheruku family, who never even mentioned the Kakatiyas as their sovereigns.\n\n**Council of Ministers:** Inscriptions and literature show that the king administered through a council of ministers. The Kakatiyas followed the concept that the state consists of **seven limbs (saptanga samanvita)**. \n\nContemporary works like _Neetisaram_, _Neeti sastra muktavalli_, and _Pratapa Rudra Yashobhushanam_ discuss the duties and role of the king in political affairs, his authority over ministers, officials, and forts, and matters of safety. The council consisted of ministers, Amatyas, Preggadas, feudatories, and Dandanayakas, though their precise portfolios and authorities are not clearly known.\n\n*   **Malyala Hemadri reddy** was the **Mahapradhani** during Ganapatideva''s period, while **Muppidi Nayaka** held this position under Pratapa Rudra.\n*   The contemporary work \"Purushartha saram,\" written by Siva Devayya during Ganapatideva''s reign, records that the king met daily with all his ministers to discuss administrative matters.\n*   All administrative affairs (civil and military) were divided into **72 Niyogas (classes)**; the official who controlled all these Niyogas was the **Niyogadhipati**. During Ganapatideva''s period, Kayastha **Gangayya sahini** held this post; during Rudramamba''s period, **Tripuri and Ponka Malllaya Pragada** acted as Bahattar Niyogadhipati.\n\n### Regional Administration\n\nThe Kakatiyas divided their empire into **Rastra, Sthala, Seema, Nadu Bhumi, and Garma** for administrative convenience — names found in inscriptions of Ganapatideva''s and Pratapa Rudra''s periods. The Srisailam inscription (of Pratapa Rudra II) mentions divisions such as Naravadi nadu, Motavadi, Pakanadu, Renadu, Mulikinadu, Are Bhumi, Kandurunadu, Venginadu, Velanadu, Natavadi, Eruvanadu, Sakalinadu, and Prolinadu.\n\n*   A **Sthalam** is an area comprising **twenty-four villages**; Sthalams were numerous in number. In the Warangal inscription, Anumkonda, Mattewada, and Orugallu together form one Sthalam.\n*   What control the central government exercised over the officials of the Rastra is not clearly known from any source.\n\n### Village Administration\n\nDuring the Kakatiya period, the village remained the lowest level of administration, as in earlier periods. The village''s officers, collectively, were known as **Ayyagars** — an already-established system before the Kakatiyas, with **twelve** officers.\n\nNamed village officials found in inscriptions include: **Karanam, Reddy, Talari, Purohita, Kammari, Kamsali, Vadrangi, Kummari, Chakali, Mangali, Vetti, Charmakara, and Niruduvadu.** All were given tax-free land for performing their services, and among them, **Karanam, Reddy, and Talari** were the government officials, who also received a share of the crop.\n\n### Army Administration\n\nThe Kakatiyas maintained a large, well-trained army — their many victories against the Yadavas, Hoyasalas, Pandyas, and Muslim invaders support this. Contemporary political works (Baddena''s _Neetishastra muktavalli_, Madiki Singhana''s _Sakala Neeti Sammatam_) also help in understanding army administration during this period, though these cannot be treated as complete or unbiased, since the authors held important administrative positions themselves.\n\n**Forts:** The Kakatiyas gave importance to protecting the empire through forts; political thinkers of the period considered a ruler with strongly fortified defenses very difficult to defeat. As mentioned in the _Neetisaram_ of Pratapa Rudra, there were **four types of forts**: **Sthala** (land), **Jala** (water), **Vana** (forest), and **Giri** (hill). \n\n*   Forts built by the Kakatiyas — **Orugallu, Raichuru, Golakonda, Bhuvanagiri, Rachakonda, Devarakonda, Nalgonda, and Panugallu** — were considered invincible.\n\n**Nayankara system:** The administrative/military system distributed lands to military officials called **Nayakas**. A village or villages given to a Nayaka is known as **Nayakasthala** or Nayakasthala vritti; there was no clear, fixed definition of the Nayakas'' characteristics or responsibilities, and the _Neetisaram_ of Pratapa Rudra offers only limited guidance here.\n\n*   To maintain the fourfold army (**Chaturanga balas**) and fill the treasury, the king was to keep the larger, higher-income villages for himself and give smaller villages to subordinates, per Pratapa Rudra''s own guidance. \n\n*   Here \"Nayakas\" effectively means \"subordinates.\" Villages given to Nayakas were of two types: small villages given for their maintenance (no separate salary paid), and income from bigger villages, part of which was paid to the king as tribute while the rest was spent maintaining troops for the king''s service. \n\n**Forces:** Kakatiya kings maintained, with discipline, **Ashwa (horse), Gaja (elephant), Ratha (charioteers), and Kalbala (foot soldiers)** forces — mentioned in contemporary works. \n\nThe army of Pratapa Rudradeva consisted of **100 elephants, 20,000 horses, and nine lakh foot soldiers**. Officials in charge were known as **Gaja sahini and Ashwa sahini**. The Nalgonda inscription of Ganapatideva records that Kayastha **Gangaya sahini** was brought from North India to maintain the horse force. **Induluri Annaya** led the five lakh foot soldiers; **Jayapa senani** was Gaja sahini during Ganapatideva''s period, while **Bendapudi Annayya** was Gajabaladhyaksha during Pratapa Rudra''s period.\n\n*   The army had two types of military organisation: the **Moolabalam (main force)** or **Sthirabalam (standing army)**, under the direct control of the king or his appointed officials (such as Gaja sahini and Ashwa sahini), and a **subordinate army**.\n*   Along with army strength, the Kakatiyas gave importance to forts (Vana, Giri, Sthala, and Jala forts existed); _Neetisaram_ and _Prushartha saram_ both explain the importance of fort protection. The Kakatiya armies'' fierce, heroic resistance against the Delhi Sultan''s forces stands as ample proof of their military strength and valour.\n\n### Judicial System\n\nThe Kakatiya rulers followed the judicial code that had existed in South India for ages; several inscriptions record different types of legal disputes.\n\n**Example 1 — Village boundary dispute (A.D. 1214, Duggirala, Guntur district):** After conquering Velanadu, while camping in the region, Ganapatideva was approached by the people of Poondi, Mormapoondi, and Eevani villages to resolve a boundary dispute. \n\n*   He asked his ministers **Mallavaraju and Rudraraju** to solve it; they heard arguments from the people of all three villages and consulted the opinion of the **Mahajanas**. **Sooravaraju of Eevani village** (likely its Karanam) was chosen for his clear understanding of the boundaries, and the dispute was resolved using his experience.\n*   This inscription shows that **Mahajanas and Karanams** were recognized as judicious persons; such a judicial meeting held outside the capital, in a village, in the presence of ministers, is termed an \"**Aprathisthista sabha.**\"\n\n**Example 2 — Water canal dispute (A.D. 1246, Karimnagar):** A dispute arose between two villages over rights to a village tank''s water canal in \"Nedhapur\" village. Local officials Ravidattu, Nathadeva, and Hingadeva explained the dispute to Ganapatideva, who assigned the matter to **Manchiraju**, minister of Jayapa senani. \n\n*   Manchiraju visited the village, discussed the matter with the Mahajanas and elders of Chamanapally, Kummarikunta, Devanapally, and Katlakolapally, and submitted a report to Ganapatideva. \n*   The king discussed the matter with the local representative **Akshayadeva** and finally ordered his judgment communicated to the people and local officials — though the people were initially unhappy, they accepted Akshayadeva''s judgment. \n*   Ganapatideva then had the judgment engraved on copper plates and executed: the Mahajanas of Chamanapally were granted overall rights over the canal, and other villages had no rights over its water.\n\nThese two judgments — termed a \"**Shashita Aprathisthita Sabha**\" — reveal how righteous Kakatiya-period judgments were; the opinion of villagers was given significant importance in delivering judgments.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p10", "partName": "Social Conditions", "bodyMd": "The Kakatiyas belong to the early medieval period, during which religion had considerable influence on society — the relations and associations of different classes of people, their place in society, and the social distance among various classes were all shaped by religious change and upheaval. \n\nAlong with the four Varnas, many castes and sub-castes emerged during this period, formed on the basis of occupation; hereditary occupations became castes, each with its own rules and restrictions, regulated by caste associations known as **\"Kula Samayas.\"** These Kula Samayas had tremendous influence on the life of their members. Friction between the Saiva, Vaishnava, and Jaina religious sects created disunity in society and obstructed the unity of various groups.\n\n### Caste System\n\nThe Kakatiyas, after achieving political power, declared themselves **Kshetriyas**. While the Cholas and Chalukyas also declared themselves Kshetriyas, many ruling families in the Andhra region instead proudly declared themselves belonging to the **\"fourth caste\" (Chaturtha kulajas)**, without claiming Kshetriya lineage.\n\n*   The Kakatiyas, too, did not come forward to claim Kshetriya status until Ganapatideva''s period. After Ganapatideva became a great conqueror, uniting the whole of Andhradesa politically as emperor, he declared himself **Kshetrya** — though historians and sociologists note there is no true Kshetriya caste in South India.\n*   The Kakatiyas did not clearly state their caste in their own inscriptions; Ganapatideva''s inscriptions mention them as **Suryavamsha (Solar) Kshetriyas**, while Vidyanatha wrote that Pratapa Rudra''s dynasty excelled that of both Surya (Solar) and Chandra (Lunar) vamsha Kshetriyas.\n\n**Religious/caste conflicts:** Kakatiya-period society was in a transition phase, marked by severe religious conflicts, class wars, and socio-religious jealousy.\n\n*   As per Pratapa Rudra Charitra, when Pratapa Rudra donated golden cows to learned Brahmins, some Brahmins who did not receive cows instigated some **Chandalas** to agitate for the dead cows. Similarly, the Visva Brahmins, Saiva Brahmins, and Vaidiki Brahmins fought among themselves before the king, each claiming sole authority over astrology, trying to prove their own superiority.\n*   **Poetess Molla**, who belonged to the fourth caste, wrote a Ramayana and tried to dedicate her work to King Pratapa Rudra — this was obstructed by Brahmins, who came forward to have the _Bhaskara Ramayana_ read instead of the _Molla Ramayanam_. In this helpless situation, Pratapa Rudra simply honoured Molla without formally accepting the dedication of her work.\n*   In this period Bhakti movement reached an extreme state: **Chakrapani Ranganatha**, a Vaishnava preacher, got himself blinded to avoid seeing the Tripuranthakam (Saiva) temple gopuram. **Veera Saiva preacher Palkuriki Somanatha** abused the Vaidiki Brahmins as \"karma chandalas,\" \"vrata bhrastas,\" and \"pashukaras,\" and called Vaishnavas \"Namala Kukkalu\" (dogs of Namas); he held that Jaina and Buddhism should be destroyed. **Pratapa Charitram** and **Siddheshwara Charitram** record clashes between Veerasaiva scholars and Jaina/Buddhist scholars.\n*   Against this backdrop of intolerance, some figures — like **Swamy Dandanayaka and Bekkallu Mallireddy**, who called themselves **\"Chatussamaya summuddaranalu\"** — showed extreme tolerance. Against the extreme Saiva-Vaishnava rivalry, **Poet Tikkana Somayaji** preached **Harihara tatvamu** (the combined philosophy of Vishnu and Shiva).\n\nThis religious intolerance obstructed social unity and influenced socio-political systems of the time. Kakatiya rulers, however, appointed people of all castes to government service; in both the Moolabalam (main army) and Nayankara (subordinate) army, importance was given to all communities as much as possible.\n\n*   Pratapa Rudra had given more importance to the **Velamas**, which upset the Reddys; to make Pratapa Rudra realize this, in one of the wars, **Terala Bhoj Reddy** and his followers **left the war** — their aim was to make him recognize their importance in protecting the king and kingdom, but the plan backfired: they lost both the king and the kingdom. \n\n*   This lack of unity and the resulting unrest contributed to the **downfall of the Kakatiya kingdom**; this social unrest due to caste conflicts led to the establishment of the **Bahamani kingdom** after the Kakatiya downfall.\n\n**Caste associations (\"Samayas\"):** A unique feature of Kakatiya-period society was caste associations called **\"Samayas.\"** The Brahmin Samaya was known as **\"Mahajanas\"** and the Vaishya Samayas as **\"Nakara.\"** \"Sanimunnuru,\" \"Telikivepuru,\" and \"Panchanam\" were some other samayas. \n\n*   Inscriptions mention ''Uru'' (village) samayas and ''Sthala'' (local) samayas at different levels; these Samayas held special powers — safeguarding and collecting taxes, resolving disputes, etc., for the people of that caste. \n*   10th–11th century inscriptions mention **Ashtadash (eighteen) samayas** in society. \n*   Among Brahmins, the **Vaidiki (ritualistic)** section was also seen in government service.\n\n**Social trends:** During this period, the political importance of Brahmins declined while the prominence of the fourth caste increased; the fourth caste enhanced its social respect and hierarchy, and the ruling class made matrimonial alliances with influential people from it. The caste system was not strong or rigid. \n\n*   Negative social habits also increased across all castes: **child marriages, Varasulka (bride-price), forced widowhood, and Sati sahagamanam** were seen frequently. \n*   Common people remained largely far from education. **Drinking, gambling, cockfights, and bull fights** were common.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p11", "partName": "Economic System", "bodyMd": "In the early days of Kakatiya rule in the Deccan plateau, they had scanty water resources and less fertile land in the Telangana region. Ganapatideva, through his military victories, brought the fertile coastal Andhra region under Kakatiya authority. \n\nThrough agricultural taxes and taxes on trade and commerce, considerable income reached the treasury. Contemporary historians and travellers — **Barani, Isami, Ferishtha, and Marco Polo** — mentioned the Kakatiyas'' wealth and richness in their writings. \n\nKakatiya rulers and their feudatories showed special interest in agricultural development, constructing tanks and canals, and in some places clearing forests to convert them into agricultural land. Along with agriculture, internal and external trade, and industries developed. There are no surviving sources giving exact figures for Kakatiya income and expenditure.\n\n### Agriculture\n\nAgricultural land was divided into two types: **Magani (wet land)** and **Metta (dry land)**.\n\n*   Within Magani: **Neeru nela (water land)** and **Thota (garden land)**.\n*   In Metta (dry) land, rain-dependent crops such as **Maize, Mustard seeds, Castor seeds, and Sessamam** were grown.\n*   Irrigated lands were known as **\"Acchu Kattu\"** lands, and the government treasury collected land tax on these lands. When land was leased, the agreement between owner and lessee was formalized by the exchange of **Tambulam (betel leaves)**.\n\nVillages were numerous during the Kakatiya period, and the majority of people''s occupation was agriculture. Inscriptions record that the Kakatiyas and their feudatories built many new villages — **Manthena, Chennuru, Kaleshwaram, Narasampeta, Achampeta, Khammammetta, and Kothagudem**, among others.\n\n*   Kakatiya rulers and feudatories also built new tanks. For example, during Ganapatideva''s period, his feudatory **Prolaraju**, ruling over Chennuru rajya, built a new village and a tank, collecting for it **one-sixteenth of a ruka per marutur land** (one visa as the god''s portion) — recorded in the A.D. 1303 **Katakuru inscription** of Pratapa Rudra.\n*   Important agricultural crops of the period: **paddy, cotton, ulavalu (horsegram), oilseeds, millets (jonnalu), vegetables, chilies, black gram, green gram, ginger, sugarcane, wheat, onions, and blue (neeli) dye**. Flowers and fruits were grown in special gardens.\n\n**Taxes on agriculture:** Named taxes found in inscriptions include **Siddayam, Oir Pangamu, Pannu, Kanika, Darishanamu, Vennu Pannu, Neeruveedi, Ardhayam, and Pullari (tax on grassland)**. Tax was collected either in cash or kind; **tax was exempted** on lands given to Brahmins, temples, and village servants.\n\n*   The **Manthena inscription** records that Brahmin Manchibhattu, a Rajapurohita, donated a wide tract of land with the king''s permission.\n*   Ganapatideva''s **Kaleshwaram inscription** records that Lord Budhadeva, son of Rudra, donated four nivartanas of land near the village tank.\n*   Many villages were named after the Kakatiya kings and queens who built them: **Ganapavaram** (by Ganapatideva), **Mahadevapuram** (by Mahadeva), **Rudravaram** (by Rudra), **Bayyaram** (by Bayyaladevi), and **Muppavaram** (by Muppamamba).\n\n**Land ownership debate:** The **A.D. 1311 inscription from Mellacheruvu (Nalgonda district)** records that the king held all rights over **Rachapolamu** (regadi or veligadu/sandy soil) lands, and could distribute these lands to Brahmins or farmers as he wished.\n\n*   Building villages and excavating tanks was considered a **meritorious deed (Poonya)** in Kakatiya-period society. Ganapatideva''s sister **Kundamamba** built \"Kundavaram\" village in the Chennuru taluqa of Adilabad district and excavated a tank called \"Kunda Samudram\" after herself. Ganapatideva''s teacher (Guru), **Visweshvara Sivacharya**, purchased forest land for 850 gadyanas, cleared it, built a village named \"Visvanathapura\" after himself, and excavated a tank there.\n*   The king gave special tax exemptions on land around newly built villages to encourage cultivation. Nayankara ministers, army chiefs, Karanams, village servants, and Ayyagars were given tax-free land in the villages where they lived.\n*   Ganapatideva''s daughter **Ganapamba''s Mogalutla inscription** records that village servants such as **Kammarlu (blacksmiths), Kummarlu (potters), and Chakali (washermen)** had to donate half the income from their cultivated land to the donee. **Rachapolam/Racha bhumulu** (king''s lands) are mentioned in the Mellacheruvu inscription. Newly cultivated land was known as **\"Podu,\"** and inscriptional evidence shows tax exemptions were granted for such newly cultivated lands.\n\n### Irrigation System\n\nThe Kakatiyas took special care to provide water to agricultural lands, and tank excavation proceeded briskly during their rule. Big tanks were called **\"samudram\"** and were constructed as a chain system — when upper tanks filled, extra water flowed into lower-level tanks, a system called **\"Golusukattu cheruvulu\"** (tanks constructed in a chain system). Motupalli and Bayyaram inscriptions give details of the period''s tanks.\n\n**Important tanks table:**\n\n| Tank/Samudram | Builder | Place | Details |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kesamudram / Kesari samudram | Prolaraju I (Arigajakesari) | Mahaboobabad taluqa, Warangal district | Named after his title |\n| Settikeriya cheruvu / Kesari samudram | Betaraju II | Warangal district | Erected a statue of Varuna deva (per Hanmakonda inscription) |\n| Anumakonda cheruvu | Rudradeva | Hanumakonda | — |\n| Pakala cheruvu | Ganapatideva''s minister Bayyana Nayaka''s son Jagadalumummadi | Narasampet tq., Maneru basin | Irrigates 9,037 acres even today; forest-covered on four sides |\n| Ramappa Cheruvu | Ganapatideva''s army chief Recherla Rudra (A.D. 1213) | Palampeta village, Mulugu taluka | The Ramappa temple is believed to have been built along with the tank; irrigates 4,350 acres today |\n| Ghanpur tank | Ganapatideva''s period | Warangal district | Irrigates 350 acres in Ghanpur village today |\n| Lakhnavaram | — | — | — |\n| Bayyaram cheruvu | Mailamba (Ganapatideva''s sister, wife of Natavadi Rudra) | Bayyaram village, Khammam district | — |\n| Kunda samudram | Kundamamba (another sister of Ganapatideva) | Kundavaram, Adilabad district | — |\n| Chounda samudram | Ganapatideva''s general Mallyala Chounda | Kondaparti village, Warangal district | — |\n\nAlong with tanks, water springs and canals played an important role in irrigation — inscriptions mention ''Gosugu canal,'' ''Ravipati canal,'' Moosati canal, Aleti canal, Kuchineni canal, and Bommakanti canal. Water was also drawn from wells using **Etams and pulleys**; the A.D. 1254 Dosapadu inscription records donations of bulls and wood for making pulleys/etams.\n\n*   Tanks and canals were **repaired every year**; persons appointed to maintain them were paid **one kuncha of grain for every putti of grain** produced under these canals — a system called **\"Dashbandha\" or \"Dashavandha manyam.\"** Some land was also given as manyam to those carrying out this maintenance work.\n*   This level of irrigation care was not continued after the Kakatiyas. Farmers of the period worshipped bulls, ploughs, and agricultural implements before beginning agricultural activities, celebrating them as festivals — **''Eruvaka'' and Sankranti** remain major farmers'' festivals even today.\n\n### Village Economy\n\nThe Kakatiyas ruled for over two and a half centuries with Anumakonda and Warangal as capitals; the village remained the base of administration and economy. Agricultural land was divided into ''Magani'' (wetland, sub-divided into **Neerunela** — crop-growing land — and **Tontanela** — garden land) and ''Metta'' (dryland, growing sesamam, castor seeds, blue dye/neelimandu, and ragulu). Lands tilled with ploughs were called **''Achukattu nela''** — \"Achukattu\" referring to a tax called ''Ari'' collected on such lands.\n\nMany varieties of paddy were grown; in the **Orugallu region**, a scented paddy variety was cultivated. Coconut, guava, mango, banana, and betel-leaf gardens were common. Jaggery, sugar, and oil-pressing industries were found in nearly every village. The number of cows donated to temples, per inscriptional data, indicates the cattle wealth of the period.\n\n### Industries – Inland and Foreign Trade\n\nAlong with agriculture, many industries developed. Palkuriki Somanatha, in his Basavapuranam, mentions **20 varieties of cloth**. Marco Polo wrote that high-quality cloth appreciated even by kings was woven in **Mysolia (Machilipatanam)**. In Warangal, **Ratnakamballu (quality woollen blankets)** and **Makhmal (fine cloth)** were woven.\n\n*   Many handicrafts made of different metal alloys (**pancha lohas**) were produced; inscriptions mention metal lamps, bronze hand lamps, plates, and hand bells. The **Palanati Veera Charitra** records that Brahmanaidu erected a panch loha flag post (pillar) at the Tripuranthakam temple.\n*   **Guttikonda and Palanatiseema** were famous for iron industries; weapons used in wars were locally manufactured. **Swords made at Nirmal (Adilabad district)** became popular even in foreign countries. Marco Polo also mentioned diamond mines in the **Golconda** region (diamond mines existed in Rayalaseema too). Carpenters made colourful **Ratnas (water pulleys)** and ivory boxes; **wax dolls (Lakka Bommalu)** and other sport items were made in Andhra Pradesh. Coloring of puppets used in puppet shows became a major industry.\n*   Important commercial centres of the period included **Orugallu, Panagallu, Jedcherla, Alampur, Maktal, Manthena, Peruru, Velpuru, Tripuranthakam, Penugonda, Kocherlakota, Pedaganjam, Nelluru, Ghantasala, Gudivada, Durgi, Macherla, Tangeda, Enamadala, Lebaka, Nandaluru, Upperapalle, Mopuru, and Dornala.** \n\n*   The Warangal fort inscription of Ganapatideva records the various businesses conducted by traders in market yards; these traders were organized into **Shrenis (guilds)**.\n\n**Trade guilds:** Shrenis/guilds, which flourished in Andhra during the Satavahana period (both occupational/craft guilds and trade guilds), continued to play an important role in Kakatiya-period trade and commerce. \n\nThe A.D. 1282 Tripuranthakam inscription records that \"**Nanadesi Pekkandru**,\" a trading guild, received permission for trading from the king. Both occupational and trading guilds carried on business jointly. Each guild elected a head, called **''Shresthi'' or Setti**, and members'' problems were solved within the guild itself; each guild had its own official insignia and maintained its own army for protection. The businessman was called **''Behari''** and the business itself **''Vyavaharam.''**\n\n*   Two types of trade were carried out by guilds: **Swadeshi (inland)** — called \"Nakaram\" — and **Paradeshi (foreign)** — called \"Nanadeshi Pekkandru\" / \"Ubhaya nanadeshi pekkandru.\" \n\n*   The **Aiyyavole-500 guild** did not have a good reputation in the Telugu area during the Kakatiya period. Guilds donated a share of their profits to temples/gods. \n*   **Vaisyas** were superior in business; some inscriptions link Vaisya guilds with Reddy, Naidu, Boya, and Dasari communities.\n\n*   The **Veerabananja Samaya (shreni)** received permission to trade in Pakanadu, Vengi, and Anumakonda cities.\n*   Guild names found in inscriptions: **Swadesi, Paradesi, Vaadalavaru, Saasar booru, Tagarambur, Ubhaya Nanadessi**, etc. Trade and commerce were centred on the Vaisya community, known as **Chettis, Settis, or Shresttis**. Baddena''s Neetisastramuktavalli likens the importance of the Vaisya community to a city as breath is to life, water to a crop, and a trunk to an elephant. Kings encouraged Vaisyas in their trade and commerce.\n*   The A.D. 1268 Mahaboobabad inscription of Rudramadevi mentions one **Preggada Daram Govindasetti**; the Draksharamam inscription of Kulothunga Gonkaraja''s mentions **Setti Surapa setti**; another inscription mentions **Ayyapareddy, son of Reddy Sunkana** — suggesting Reddys were also appointed as **\"Raja Settis.\"** Trade could not be conducted without the king''s permission.\n\n**Commercial routes and foreign trade:** With the growth of inland trade, foreign trade also prospered. Commercial centres were well connected to ports by highways — for example, Motupalli, Machilipatanam, Andhra, and Karnataka were well connected by highways from Motupalli to Bellary via Tripuranthakam. A trade route from Palanadu connecting Nalagonda, via Nakarekall, to Orugallu is mentioned in the _Kridhabhirammam_.\n\n*   Based on inscriptional references to taxes levied on ports, it is understood that the **Krishna and Godavari rivers** were used as waterways. **Motupalli was the centre of foreign trade**; other important foreign trade ports included **Krishnapatanam, Hamsaladeevi, and Machilipatanam**.\n*   Ganapatideva issued the **\"Abhaya sasana\"** at Motupalli, giving assurances to foreign traders. The Motupalli inscription details imports and exports from the port: elephants, horses, gold, jewels, sandalwood, camphor, camphor oil, rose water, ivory, copper, tin, lead, mercury, silk, ruby, and other \"Sughandha dravvalu\" (fragrant goods). **Trade was conducted with China, Persia, Arabia, Ceylon, and the East India islands.**\n*   The rate of tax was fixed based on the quality of goods — for example, if 30 elephants were traded, one elephant had to be paid as tax. Ganapatideva assured that **taxes would be lesser** for traders, and that government officials would not exploit traders, ensuring trade was carried out ethically without cheating people.\n\n### Different Types of Taxes and Tolls\n\nTaxes during the Kakatiya period fell into four broad types: **1) Land tax, 2) Industrial tax, 3) Professional tax, 4) Commercial tax.**\n\n**Taxes table:**\n\n| Name of Tax | Collected from | Rate | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pannu, Pangamu, Kanika, Darishanamu, Siddayam (cash) | Land-related, all types of lands | — | Warangal district Matedu inscription, A.D. 1310 |\n| Ari, sankamu, Pannu, Kanika, Puttimanika, Netamaggaluvaru, Nuneganugalavaru | Taxes on industries | Based on production and sale, right of god''s share | Rudramadevi''s period, Durgi inscription A.D. 1269 |\n| Srimangali, Netavaru, Gollavaru | Professional tax, per weaver and per house | 1 chinnam | P.V. Parabrahma Sastry |\n| Kummari, ayyagandru, Karanalu, Soldiers | Per house, per head | 1 chinam, 2 chinnams, 1 chinnam, 1 addaga | — |\n| Addagattu / Angadi sunkam, Madiga sunkam, Kolaga sunkam | Trade-related, shops; levied on oil | — | — |\n| Mudar | Tax on salt bags | — | — |\n| Ellari (house) | House tax | 2 rukas | A.D. 1314, Pratapa Rudra II |\n| Wealth tax (Pullari) | On cattle (50), cows (25) | 2 gadyas 5 rukas yearly | — |\n\nSpecial officers, government servants, and market officers were appointed for tax collection. Among market officials, **Sunkamanyagadu, Teerpari, Kolagadu, and Karanam** were important — the first purchased the right to collect tax from shops (Angadi/Penta/Adda); the government''s share was remitted to the Karanam, who systematically collected tax dues from traders. **Teerpari** assessed the exact value of goods sold and bought, and the **Kolagadu**''s job was to measure market goods without fraud. **Karanam and Teerpari were government employees; Sunkamanyagadu and Kolagadu were private employees** — per the Tripuranthakam inscription of A.D. 1296.\n\nOther named taxes: **Burra sunkam, Madiga sunkam, Pelli pannu (marriage tax), Gandi sunkam (on the pulley well), Puttuperu sunkam (naming-ceremony tax), Revu sunkam (port tax), Alamu (tax on vegetables), Antharayam (tax on arecanut gardens), and Kilaramu (tax on sheep hoard)**.\n\n**Guild/market goods table (Warangal fort inscription):**\n\n| Angadi/Santha/Penta | Samaya | Goods Sold |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cheeremareya | Swadeshi-Paradeshi | All types of clothes |\n| Pokamathiya | Swadeshi-Paradeshi | Arecanuts |\n| Sugandh Dravyalu (spices) | Ubhaya nanadeshi | Sandalwood, camphor, kasturi, spice items |\n| Ratnalu, Dantam (jewel and ivory) | Ubhaya nanadeshi | Pearls, bangles, beads |\n| Noolu malega | Ubhaya nanadeshi | Cotton and wool |\n| Uppu penta | Swadeshi-Paradeshi | Salt |\n| Aakupenta | Saasirbaru | Betel leaves |\n| Metal implements | Tagaramvaru | Tin, copper, lead, other metals |\n| Noovula penta | Swadeshi-Paradeshi | Sesamam, wheat, pulses, millet, oils, grains |\n\nTrade and commerce, though concentrated in Vaisya hands, also involved people of other castes: the **Salevaru (weavers), Perikalu (goods transporters on bulls), Kummarlu (potters), and Vadrangulu (carpenters)** provided services to the Vaisyas.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p12", "partName": "Religion under the Kakatiya Period", "bodyMd": "Prior to the rise of Kakatiya power in Telangana, the people had shown some inclination toward **Jainism and Buddhism**.\n\n### Jainism\n\nDuring the Western Chalukya period, Jainism enjoyed special patronage among the people of Telangana. **Nizamabad (Bodhan), Karimnagar (Vemulawada), Medak (Kolcharam, Patancheruvu), Nalgonda (Kollipaka/Kolanupaka), and Warangal (Anumakonda)** were important Jaina centres. \n\nKakatiya rulers of the first generation followed and patronized Jainism, even though the Eastern Chalukyas were patrons of Vedic religion. The _Orugallu Kaifiyat_ records that Eastern Chalukya king Rajaraja Narendra indulged in violence against a Jain ascetic, Rushbhanatha, who then went to Anumakonda and got shelter there. **Prolaraja''s Anumakonda inscription begins with a prayer to Jinendra.** \n\nSlowly, Jainism and Buddhism lost the patronage of the local people and kings, and Saivism and Vaishnavism became prominent instead.\n\n*   The **Vemulawada Chalukyas** patronized Jainism; their court poet, the Jaina scholar **Somadevasuri**, wrote \"Yashastilaka,\" and poet **Pampa** wrote \"Adipuranam.\" The **Sanigaram inscription** records a donation made to Yudhamalla Jinalaya by Betaraju I. **Betaraju II** made donations to Jaalna Jaina basadi.\n*   **Prolaraju''s A.D. 1117 Padmakshi temple inscription** (Warangal) records that his wife **Mailamma** made donations for the construction of the Kadalaraya Jaina basadi.\n*   Jaina poet **Appayyarya** wrote _Jinendra Kalyanabhyudayam_ during Pratapa Rudradeva''s period.\n\n**Jaina sculpture earlier than the Kakatiya period:** Before the expansion of Kakatiya power, Jainism was popular in Telangana during the Rashtrakuta and Chalukya periods, and many Jaina basadis were built. **Chilkuru, Kulcharam (Medak), Patancheruvu, Kolanupaka, Hanumakonda, and Polavasa** had sculptures of Jaina Thirthankaras. \n\nThe **Parshvanatha sculpture found at Kulcharam** (Medak district) is particularly notable — nine feet tall, seated under a seven-headed Nagaraju (serpent) canopy, with a serenely-faced idol carved delicately, hands reaching to the knees. \n\nThe inscription at its base reads \"Sri Chalukya kula tilakam Tailavarasan,\" dating it to the period of **Tailapa II (A.D. 973–995)**. The sculptures found at Chilkuru and Patancheruvu are in a dilapidated state and preserved in the State Archaeology Museum, Hyderabad.\n\n### Saivism\n\nSaivism received the fullest patronage of the Kakatiyas. During their period, **Kalamukha, Pashupata, Aradhya, and Veerasaiva** were the sub-sects of Saivism practiced. Early Kakatiyas followed and patronized the **Kalamukha** sect specifically.\n\n*   The Anumakonda inscription records that **Betaraju II** was a disciple of **Rameshwara pandita**, head of Seelamatham in Srisailam — a great scholar of \"Lakuleshwara Agama Mahasiddhantam\" (Lakuleshwara/Sakuleshwara being the founder of Kalamukha philosophy). Rameshwara pandita gave Siva diksha to Betaraju''s sons Durgaraju and Prolaraju II. \n\n*   The Kalamukha saivas of Karnataka regarded **Srisailam** as their centre and Rameshwara Pandita as their teacher (guru), underlining Srisailam''s importance. Rameshwara Pandita was also Sthanapati of the Draksharamam temple; the Kakatiya kings maintained friendly relations with Kalamukha teachers.\n\n*   **Alampur** (on the banks of the Tungabhadra, present Mahaboobnagar district) was a famous Saiva kshetra; its Sthanapatis at the Brahmeshwara temple were pious scholars. **Agastheshwaram** near Alampur was another Kalamukha centre; notable Sthanapatis of the Brahmeshwara Matham included Lokabharanam pandita, Someshwara bhattaraka, Brahmarishi bhattaraka, and Dharindrasi pandita.\n*   **Vemulawada** (Karimnagar district) was once a great Saiva kshetra patronized by Kalyana Chalukya kings — Tailapa II donated to Maheshvara Shakti pandita of the Kalamukha matham, and Mahamandaleshvara Rajaditya donated to the presiding deity of the Rajarajeshwara Swamy matham.\n*   **Prolaraja II''s teacher, Rameshwara pandita, settled at Kaleshwaram** (Warangal district) and consecrated the Siva linga there.\n\n*   Important Kalamukha Saiva kshetras in Kakatiya-period Telangana included **Dharmapuri (Karimnagar), Anumakonda (Warangal), Ainavolu (Warangal), Panagallu (Nalgonda), Nizamabad, Nandikandi (Medak), and Sanigaram (Karimnagar)**. \n*   In Rayalaseema, **Pushpagiri** near Kadapa was a great Kalamukha kshetra; in the coastal region, **Amaravati, Samarlkota, Bheemavaram, Palkollu, and Draksharama Bheemeshwara temple** were the great Kalamukha kshetras (per M. Krishnakumari''s _Pancharamas in Medieval Andhradesa_, 1989).\n\n**Pashupatas / Saiva sidhantas — second important sect:** Agamas were the main sources for this sect, which followed the \"Yoganisthas\" and remained attached to Siva temples. **Mattamayura**, a branch of the Golakhi matha, organized itself and sought royal patronage alongside the Vedic Brahmins. \n\nThe Kalachuri kingdom, spreading around Jabalpur (Madhya Pradesh), became the prominent centre of Saiva sidhanta; **Rannad and Kadvaha mathas** were the early Saiva mathas there. Kalachuri kings Yashakarma, Gajagarna, Narasimha, Jayasimha, and Vijayasimha had close relations with Pashupata Siva yogis; **Sadasivayogi, Purandhara yogi, and Prabhasiva yogi** acted as teachers to Kalachuri kings.\n\n*   In the **13th century**, when political disturbance and instability struck the Kalachuri kingdom, its Saiva teachers migrated to Andhradesa. **Dharmasiva** came to Draksharama before A.D. 1234 and settled there, becoming the teacher of **Ganapatideva**. \n*   During Ganapatideva''s time, at temples under Kakatiya control — Tripuranthakam, Bhattiprolu, Eleshwaram (Nalgonda), Manthena, Mandadam, Kaleshwaram (Karimnagar), Malkapuram (Guntur), Somasila (Mahaboobnagar), Visveshwara — Sivadeshika **established Saiva Golaki mathas** and was elevated to the position of **Rajaguru** — Rajaguru to both Ganapatideva and his daughter Rudramadevi.\n*   Per Rudramadevi''s **Malkapuram inscription (A.D. 1261)**, on the wish of her father Rudramadevi donated Mandam village on the banks of the Krishna in Velanati vishaya to **Visveshvara shambu**, who constructed a Sudha Saiva matham, food-feeding house, and Sivalaya there — this became popular as the **Golaki matham**.\n\n*   The Mandama village settlement had three teachers for Vedas, five for Logic/Literature, and Agamas, with free boarding and lodging provided to students (recorded across multiple inscriptions in _Medieval Andhra Desh Religious Institutions_, translated by G. Anjaia). \n*   This Golaki matham also had a **hospital with a delivery room**.\n\n*   **Visveshvara shambhu** also established a Sivalaya and Upala matha on the banks of the Godavari at Kaleshwaram. Branches of the Golaki mathas were established at Pushpagiri, Sriparvatam, Tripuranthakam, Alampur, Draksharamam, Bhattiprolu, etc. — all headed by Sivacharyas. \n\n*   The Golaki mathas enjoyed prominence up to Pratapa Rudra''s period before declining. **Srisailam** had an **Abhinava Golaki matham** in this period, maintained partly through donations by Kayastha Jannigadeva.\n\n*   **Umamaheshvaram, Alampur, Tripuranthakam, and Pushpagiri** (Srisailam''s entry kshetras) received strong royal and popular patronage. Temple priests called **Maheshvarlu** became very powerful.\n\n**Religious tolerance:** The Kakatiyas'' policy of religious tolerance helped sow tolerance among all religious sects of the period. In contemporary Karnataka, conflicts between Veerasaiva and Saiva sects were common — this was not seen in Andhra desa, owing to the Kakatiyas'' tolerant policy. Kakatiya rulers and their royal teachers preached religious harmony and peaceful living, seen by later generations as an ideal model.\n\n### Vaishnavism\n\nKakatiya rulers gave their people complete religious freedom. The **Varaha insignia** (boar symbol) on their signs and coins, and Vaishnava idols erected in temples they built, show their religious tolerance.\n\n*   **Rudradeva** built the **Trikuta (Rudreshwara) temple** at Anumakonda, erecting the idol of Vasudeva there. His minister **Vellanki Gangadhara** erected the Prasanna Keshava swamy idol and built a large temple at Anumakonda.\n*   **Ganapatideva''s sister, Rani Mylamba**, built the temple for Gopalakrishna at **Inugurti** and made several donations for its maintenance.\n*   **Pratapa Rudra II''s** army general **Devari Nayaka**, on his king''s orders, donated Sakalveedu village to the Ranganatha Swamy temple on the banks of the Kaveri River.\n*   **Pratapa Rudra II**, in A.D. 1321, gave donations to the Chenna Keshava swamy temple; his queen **Lakshmidevi** donated gifts to the Ramanatha temple at Elleda.\n*   Many **Vishnumurthy temples** were constructed during the Kakatiya period in the Telugu region — at Simhachalam, Sarpavaram, Macherla, Ahobilam, Dharmapuri, Tirupati, Srikurmam, Mangalagiri, Ponnuru, Nelluru, Nandaluru, Kopparam, Karempudi, Kalvakulaku (Mahaboobnagar), Munigadapa, Godaliparti, and Kondapaka (Medak).\n*   The **Palanati Veera Charitra** (Battle of Palanadu) also belongs to this period — in reality a war between the followers of the Veervaisnava sect leader **Brahmanaidu** and the Saivite leader **Nayakuralu Nagamma**. **Macherla Chenna Keshavaswamy temple** was a great Vaishnava kshetra of the time; here Brahmanaidu preached casteless, \"Jati-less,\" universal Vaishnavism and organized common dining, known as **\"chapakudu.\"**\n\nLike this, Kakatiya kings respected all religious sects and understood the essence of each, encouraging religious tolerance across their empire (Telangana, Andhra, and Rayalaseema).", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p13", "partName": "Kakatiyas'' Service to Language", "bodyMd": "Kakatiya kings, ministers, and officials put in great effort for the development of **Sanskrit and Telugu**. Educational institutions and scholars are referenced in period inscriptions.\n\n*   The **A.D. 1261 Malakapuram inscription** describes the conditions of educational buildings and their activities. \n\n*   **Visveshvara Sivacharya**, Rajaguru to both Ganapatideva and Rudramadevi, received the village Mandaram as an agrahara from Rudramadevi, built a new village named Golaki there, established a Saiva matha, and constructed a **Vidya mandapa** (educational building) where the **Rig Veda, Yajur Veda, Sama Veda, Grammar, Logic, and Literature** were taught. Srisailam and Pushpagiri were other centres with Vidya mandapas. Teaching largely took place in Sanskrit.\n\n*   **Vidyanatha**, Pratapa Rudra II''s court poet, was a great Sanskrit poet; his famous work is **\"Pratapa­rudriyam\"** or **\"Pratapa­rudra Yashobhushanam.\"** **Shalakyamalla kavi** was another great poet of the period, author of \"Udaatha raghavam\" and \"Neeroshte Ramayanam.\" Rudradeva''s Uttareshvara inscription mentions **Viddanacharya**, author of \"Prameyacharchamrutam\" — a work not available today.\n*   Vidyanatha, in his work, praised his emperor Pratapa Rudra II in Alankara shastra, describing his conquests over Kalinga, Pandya, and Seuna (Yadava). \n\n*   Another popular Sanskrit scholar at Pratapa Rudra II''s court was **Gundayabhattu**, who wrote a commentary on Sri Harsha''s advaita work _Khandana khanda khadyam_. \n*   **Jayapa**, of the Ayyavamsha, was appointed by Ganapatideva as Gajasahini and wrote **\"Nrityaratnavalli\"**, describing all the dance and drama forms existing in \"Anadhradesa\" during the period — it has eight prakarnas (chapters) and remains a standard textbook for dance and drama teachers today; on the walls of the Palampet Ramappa temple, the postures described in this book are carved in detail. \n*   Jayapa senani is also said to have written \"Geetaratnavalli\" (on music) and \"Vaadyaratnavalli\" (on musical instruments), though these are not available today.\n\n### Telugu Literature\n\nTelugu''s evolution began with words in the _Gaathasaptashati_ of the Satavahana period, and it evolved fully by the Eastern Chalukya period. In the Kakatiya period, as the people of Telangana, Andhra, and Rayalaseema were united, Telugu became popular as **the people''s language**; Saiva and Vaishnava poets wrote their works in the local dialect (**Jana Telugu**), and Telugu was used in inscriptions of the period.\n\nImportant Telugu poets of this period include **Tikkanna Somayaji, Palkuriki Somanatha, Baddena, Kolanu Ganapatideva, Rudradeva, Ekamaranatha, and Kase Sarvappa**. \n\nImportant Telugu works of the period include **Andhra Mahabharatam, Nirvochanottara Ramayanam, Andhra Dashakumara Charitam, Purushartha saram, Jainendra Kalyanabhyudayam** (Appayyarya was a Jaina poet), **Panditaradya Charitam, Basavapuranam, Sivayoga saram, Neetisaram, Neetisarmuktavalli, Pratapa charitra, Siddeshwara charitra,** and **Kreedhabhi ramam**.\n\n*   **Tikkana Somayaji**, born at Nellore, went to Orugallu and completed his Mahabharata composition at Ganapatideva''s court; he is regarded as the figure who culturally unified Telangana-Andhra regions through his literary work, and can be called a foundational figure of the tradition.\n*   **Palkuriki Somanatha** wrote works showing that spoken Telugu (\"jana Telugu\") had no shortage of dignity for poetry; his _Basavapuranam_ develops the life of Veerashaiva reformer Basaveshwara and is not a translation of any Sanskrit work.\n*   The dvipada (couplet) literary tradition''s most famous work of the era is the **Ranganatha Ramayanam** — its Purva Ramayana portion composed by Buddharaju, and Uttara Ramayana by Vithaladevudu and Buddharaju''s sons.\n*   The **Bhaskara Ramayanam** was composed jointly by five poets — **Mallikarjuna Bhattu, Kumara Rudrudu, Ayyalaryudu, Bhaskarudu, and Hulakki Bhaskarudu.**\n*   Other notable works of the era: **Marana''s Markandeya Puranam**, and **Manchana''s Keyura Bahu Charitra**.\n\n**Poet Molla** episode (from Social Conditions section above) reflects the caste tensions of the era around literary patronage. Poet **Palkuriki Somanatha** is particularly noted for using vernacular Telugu, freed from excessive Sanskritized vocabulary, while still drawing on earlier Sanskrit source material for many of his ideas.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter7-kakatiyas-p14", "partName": "Architecture and Monuments of the Kakatiya Period", "bodyMd": "As successors of the Rashtrakutas and Kalyana Chalukyas, the Kakatiyas built wonderful temples, forts, and entrance doors (Toranas) across Telangana and Andhra. Their feudatories, ministers, army chiefs, and regional rulers, inspired by their overlords, also built many beautiful temples.\n\n**Forts:** Forts built during the Kakatiya period include **Golkonda, Orugallu, Deverakonda, Rachakonda, and Bhuvanagiri**, among others.\n\n*   The **Orugallu fort** was particularly strongly built for the protection of the capital, with **three walls**: the **first wall** was constructed of mud, about ten feet high, surrounding Kazipeta and Anumakonda; the **second wall**, also a mud fort, lay outside the Orugallu fort and originally had four entrances on four sides at important points (today only the old city remains within this mud fort). \n*   Passing through this, at a distance of about a furlong, is the **masonry fort**, called **Kanchu Kota** (Bronze fort). Inside this fort stands the temple of **Swayambhunatha**, in front of which four wonderful Toranas (entrance arches) were constructed. The sculptural art of this temple was largely destroyed during the Muslim attacks.\n\n### Temples of the Kakatiya Period and Their Characteristics\n\nImportant art and architectural characteristics of Kakatiya temples: (1) high Adhisthana (plinth), (2) pillars with many sculptures, (3) wall parapits of mandapas with Jalies (lattice/checker screens), (4) entrance (Torana) pillars, (5) high vimanas, (6) Ranga mandapas, (7) Mukha mandapas.\n\n**Temple table:**\n\n| Temple | Location | Key details |\n|---|---|---|\n| Anumakonda thousand-pillar temple (Rudreshwaralayam) | Rudradeva built, A.D. 1163 | A Trikutalaya, with Rudra, Vasudeva, and Surya as main deities; the temple''s A.D. 1163 inscription supports the Kakatiyas'' declaration of sovereignty |\n| Ramappa temple (Rudreshwaralayam) | Palampet village, Mulugu tq., Warangal district, built A.D. 1213 by Ganapatideva''s general Recherla Rudradeva | Built on a 6.5 ft upa pitha with a four-storeyed vimana and doors on three sides; features an interior Nandi, decorated tiles, and bold sculptures — including \"Madanika\" female figures in tri-bhangima poses (per Jayapasenani''s Nrityaratnavalli), musician and bhakti-singer carvings, and Rangamandapa pillar scenes of Gopika vasthrapaharanam and rasa kreeda |\n| Ghanapuram Fort temple | Warangal | Beautiful Nayika (heroine) sculptures in the Rangamandapa; Nayikas carved in various poses playing musical instruments; Nandi sculpture decorated with a beaded garland |\n| Nagulapadu Trikutalayam (1234), Kameshwaralayam (A.D. 1256) | Nagulapadu | Rangamandapam has beautifully carved pillars depicting scenes of the Ravana sabha |\n| Swayambhulinga temple (A.D. 1254) | Warangal fort | Destroyed during Muslim attacks; currently in a dilapidated condition |\n| Pillalamarri Erekeshwaralayam (A.D. 1208) | Nalgonda | Mukkanteeshwaralayam (A.D. 1195), Nameshwaralayam (A.D. 1203) |\n| Soumyanathalayam | Nandaluru | Sculpture of Visvaksena carved in a wonderful way |\n| Pachala Someshwaralayam | Panugallu, Nalgonda | Built A.D. 1015–1042, Kalyana Chalukya period |\n| Ramlingeshwaralayam | Nandikandi, Medak district | Built A.D. 1015–1042 |\n\n**Significance of temples in medieval society:** Temples in medieval India functioned as landlord, owner, consumer of goods and services, banker, school, museum, and theatre — a centre of excellence in arts and civilization, and a model and instrument of righteous human values.\n\n*   Under Kakatiya patronage, temples in Andhradesa flourished with abundant income and wealth. Ekamranatha''s _Pratapa Charitra_ records that in Pratapa Rudra II''s kingdom there were **5,500 Saiva temples, 1,300 Vaishnava temples**, 2,400 temples of Mallaradeve, and **4,400 temples** together dedicated to Bhairava, Durga, and Ganapati.\n*   The **Srisailam Mallikarjuna Swamy temple** had **fifty-two villages** assigned to it by A.D. 1313 (Pratapa Rudradeva''s time), whose income supported daily poojas and services by temple employees and priests. Different castes and traders donated shares of their income to temples; temple lands received special tax exemption. \n*   The **Perikas** (transporters), who carried daily-needed fruits and goods on bulls and bullock carts to various temples, were exempted from tax on these goods — recorded in the Tripuranthakam inscription.\n\n**Sculptural themes:** In Kakatiya sculpture, four broad categories predominate: deity forms, dance forms (Nartaki), dancer groups (Nartakibrundam), and general/secular forms.\n\nAmong decorative motifs unconnected to deity forms, six types were prominent: **makara toranas (crocodile-arch motifs), hamsas (swans), padmas (lotuses), latas (creepers/vines), elephants, and lion friezes.** Kakatiya hamsa (swan) motifs are depicted in graceful rows, with wings turned and elaborate feather-patterns forming beautiful borders. \n\nKakatiya sculptors gave special importance to elephants and makaras (crocodile-motifs), but — unlike other South Indian temple traditions — rarely carved lions or horses. Sculptors of the period developed a distinctive individual style, producing innumerable variations on traditional themes rather than repeating a single standard pattern.\n\nThe **Ramappa temple at Palampet**, Warangal district, is considered the most artistically accomplished of all Kakatiya temples — an example of the Kakatiyas'' devotion to the art of dance, built by Recherla Rudradeva (general and minister under Ganapatideva) in A.D. 1213. Every part of the temple is an exquisite work of sculptural art.\n\n*   It is believed that Jayapa Senani himself assisted in perfecting these dance sculptures. The **Nandi at Ramappagudi** is considered an unmatched divine sculptural masterpiece, and the beauty and grace of the carved serpent figures (Nagini) and dance postures there are especially notable.\n\nDevalaya (temple) construction proceeded vigorously throughout the Kakatiya period, and the architectural design skill of Telugu craftsmen flowed generously onto the fine-grained black stone of the region. Kakatiya temples were generally **Trikuta** (three-shrine) constructions — a common feature was that alongside the main temple there would typically be two smaller subsidiary temples. The main shrine was generally dedicated to Shiva, while the subsidiary ones were dedicated to other deities.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p1", "partName": "Background: The Fall of Warangal and the Post-Kakatiya Liberation Movement", "bodyMd": "The fall of Warangal in **1323 A.D.** is a landmark event in Telugu history, ending Kakatiya rule and opening space for a liberation movement against Tughlaq authority.\n\n*   This movement was led by a confederation of erstwhile Kakatiya nobles, chief among them:\n    *   The **Musunuri Nayakas**\n    *   The **Recherla Velamas** of Rachakonda and Devarakonda\n    *   The **Reddys** of Kondavidu\n    *   The **Koppula chiefs**\n*   **Musunuri Kapaya Nayaka** recaptured Warangal in **1336 A.D.**\n*   After this recapture, all the above groups established their own independent kingdoms across present-day Andhra and Telangana.\n*   Because the territory of each of these kingdoms was small and their duration short, the polity/statecraft they practiced could not make a lasting mark of its own impact.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p2", "partName": "The Musunuri Nayakas (1325–1335 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "### Sources for Musunuri Nayaka History\n\nThe primary sources for reconstructing Musunuri Nayaka history are:\n\n*   Musunuri **Prolaya Nayaka''s** fourteenth-century **Vilasa Copperplate grant** (undated precisely)\n*   Musunuri **Kapaya Nayaka''s Prolavaram (Guraja) record**, dated **1345 A.D.**\n*   Reddy Queen **Anitalli''s Kaluvacheru grant**, dated **1423 A.D.**, which also refers to Prolaya and Kapaya\n*   Inscriptions of the Recherla Padmanayakas at **Pillalamarri, Ainavolu, and Ganapeshwaram**\n*   The literary work **Velugotivari Vamsavali**, and other contemporary accounts\n\n### Musunuri Prolaya Nayaka (1324/1325–1333 A.D.)\n\nProlaya Nayaka was the grandson of **Pota** and son of **Pocha**. Pocha and his three brothers may have served the last Kakatiya ruler, **Prataparudra**.\n\n*   After Warangal''s fall in 1323 A.D., Prolaya took advantage of the discontent under the tyrannical Tughlaq administration to win popular confidence and become a leader in north coastal Andhra.\n*   He received support from all his relations, especially his cousin **Kapaya Nayaka**.\n*   By the time Prolaya assumed leadership in **1324 A.D.**, all of Andhradesa was under the Delhi empire; **Malik Maqbul** was in charge at Warangal with Muslim garrisons stationed at other forts.\n\n**Rebellion (1325 A.D.):**\n\n*   The death of Sultan Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq, and the new sultan Muhammad bin Tughlaq''s preoccupation with family affairs, gave **Prolaya Nayaka** the opening to become among the first rebel leaders to strike against local administrators, in 1325 A.D.\n*   He chose **Rekapalle** on the banks of the Godavari, amidst thick forests and hilly terrain, as his base — a location that made it hard for the enemy to retaliate.\n*   This let him drive away Tughlaq-appointed administrators and occupy the Telangana region as well.\n\n**Supporters and allies:**\n\n*   Surviving veteran generals/administrators from the earlier regime — **Induluri (Ariyeti) Annamantri** and **Kolani Rudradeva** — supported his rebellion.\n*   Younger nobles/chieftains who revolted simultaneously in different parts of Andhradesa:\n    *   **Prolaya Vema Reddy** of Addanki\n    *   **Prolaya Nayaka** of the Koppula family\n    *   **Anapati Nayaka** of the Manchikonda family\n    *   **Recherla Singama Nayaka**\n    *   **Vengabhupala** of Undi\n*   His brother **Musunuri Porlaya Nayaka**, cousins, and other followers also stood by him.\n\n**Achievements and honours:**\n\n*   Prolaya Nayaka inflicted a series of defeats on Tughlaq representatives and established himself as independent ruler of the Godavari region.\n*   The _Bhimesvara Purana_ (Canto I, verse 49) narrates that he granted **Aredu village** (West Godavari district) to **Annamantri** as an _agrahara_, in appreciation of his services.\n*   Prolayavema Reddy independently ruled Punginadu (Guntur–Nellore tract) with Addanki as capital; in his **1325 A.D. Mallavaram epigraph**, he uses the epithet _''mlechchhabdhi kumbhodbjhava''_ (\"a veritable Agastya to the ocean,\" i.e., the Muslim army).\n*   **Recherla Singama Nayaka** established his authority in the Mahbubnagar area.\n*   In Raichur, Kadapa, and Kurnool regions, the **Aravitis** worked to drive away the outsiders.\n\n**Record of reign:**\n\n*   Prolaya Nayaka''s reign was short (**1324–1333 A.D.**).\n*   The fourteenth-century **Vilasa grant**, issued on the occasion of a lunar eclipse (either 1328 or 1333 A.D.), records the grant of Vilasa village in Konasima as an _agrahara_ to the Brahman scholar **Vennaya**, who in turn distributed agrahara lands to other Brahmans. The grant enumerates the achievements of Prolaya''s short rule.\n\n### Musunuri Kapaya Nayaka (1333–1368 A.D.)\n\nProlaya Nayaka had no children. The next heir, his younger brother **Erapota Nayaka**, died in battle, so his cousin **Kapaya Nayaka** ascended the throne at Rekapalle.\n\n**Accession context — the Kampili episode:**\n\n*   In pursuit of the rebel governor of Sagar, Malik Bahauddin Gurshasp, Sultan Muhammad bin Tughlaq''s forces attacked and destroyed **Kampili** in 1327–28 A.D.\n*   Ferishta''s account: Kapaya approached **Ballala III**, the powerful king of Carnatic, and convinced him of the need to avert extermination of Hindus from the Deccan (though no independent evidence confirms this).\n*   Ballala III raised an army and placed part of it under Kapaya Nayaka''s command.\n\n**Recapture of Warangal (1336 A.D.):**\n\n*   Kapaya Nayaka drove away **Malik Maqbul**, the Bain Wazir of Telangana, and occupied Warangal; Maqbul fled to Delhi. Warangal was thus totally lost to the Sultan.\n*   Muhammad bin Tughlaq, occupied with revolts in the north, could not respond in the south.\n*   Kapaya Nayaka assumed rulership of Andhradesa, taking the titles **Andhra Suratrana** and **Andhradesadhisavara**.\n*   Around the same time, per Barani, a relative of Kapaya who had converted to Islam and been loyal to the Tughlaqs raised the banner of revolt at **Kampili**, and Kampili too was lost to the Tughlaqs. Only Daulatabad and Gujarat remained of the sultan''s southern domain.\n\n**Consolidation of power:** To strengthen his position in Telangana and coastal areas north of the Krishna, Kapaya Nayaka conferred high positions on close associates/relatives and entered matrimonial alliances with some of them.\n\n| Associate | Position / Territory | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Anavota Nayaka (cousin) | Governor of coastal area, headquartered first at Toyyeru, later Rajahmahendravaram (Rajahmundry) | Popularly known as **Toyyeti Anavota Nayaka** |\n| Muppabhupa | Ruled Sabbinadu (north-western region), capital Ramagiri (modern Adilabad district) | Patron of Telugu poet **Madiki Singana** |\n| Manchikonda Kunya Nayaka | Built the fort at Korukonda | Son-in-law of Kapaya Nayaka''s brother; guarded against forest-folk raids and aided the coastal governor |\n| Mummadi Nayaka (Kunya''s son) | Married Kapaya Nayaka''s sister''s daughter | — |\n| Namaya Nayaka (Koppula family) | Placed at Pithapuram | — |\n\n*   **Kunavaram**, at the confluence of the Godavari and the Sabari, is named after Kunya Nayaka, indicating the extent of his principality.\n*   The **Ganapesvaram record** indicates Kapaya Nayaka''s authority over the Recherla Velamas of Amanagallu and Pillalamarri — though there is no clear evidence Prolayavema Reddy of Punginadu was ever his subordinate.\n*   Also in existence at this time: the ancient kingdom of the **eastern Gangas of Kalinga**, flourishing for over a thousand years in the area of modern Visakhapatnam, Vizianagaram, Srikakulam and Ganjam districts.\n\n**Downfall and death:**\n\n*   **Recherla Singama Nayaka** (of the Padmanayakas/Velamas), who had cooperated with the Musunuris earlier, began asserting his own power in Telangana, moving his capital to Rajukonda/Rachakonda — a more secure place.\n*   This bid for power caused Kapaya Nayaka to suffer a revolt that forced him to flee. He later recovered, extending his authority up to Pillalamarri and beyond, as shown by the **Pillalamarri inscription of 1357 A.D.**\n*   While Kapaya Nayaka was engrossed fighting the **Bahmanis** (1359–61 A.D.), Singama Nayaka declared independence, extending his kingdom to the river Krishna and capturing forts in the Krishna–Tungabhadra doab.\n*   **1361 A.D.:** Singama Nayaka''s elder son **Anapota Nayaka**, with his brother, stormed **Jallipalli**, massacred its inmates and allies, and assumed the title **Somakulaparasurama**. He then fortified Rachakonda, making it impregnable.\n*   Kapaya Nayaka''s subsequent efforts to assert authority over Anapota Nayaka enraged him. Anapota marched on Warangal in fury.\n*   **1368 A.D.:** In the ensuing battle at **Bhimavaram** near Warangal, **Kapaya Nayaka lost his life**. Anapota Nayaka went on to conquer Bhongir, Warangal, and Singavaram.\n*   Kapaya Nayaka''s death at Anapota''s hands marked the end of the Musunuri Nayakas; no successors of Kapaya Nayaka are known to history, and Warangal lost its prominence.\n*   Anapota Nayaka, having killed Kapaya Nayaka and conquered his lands, found himself stuck with Kapaya''s old enemy — the **Bahmani Sultan** — and carried on a life-and-death struggle against the Bahmanis until the total annexation of Telangana to the Bahmani Kingdom.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p3", "partName": "Velamas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda (1324–1475 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "### Origins as Kakatiya Subordinates: The Recherla Velama Family\n\nThe **Recherla Velama** (or **Padmanayaka**) rulers rose to prominence in the 14th and 15th centuries; they belonged to the **Velama caste**.\n\n*   They were loyal subordinates of the Kakatiyas until that dynasty ceased to rule following defeat at the hands of Tughlaq prince **Ulugh Khan** in 1323 A.D.\n*   The new southern Tughlaq province (comprising the sultan''s latest conquests) was weak from the start and soon broke into revolts. Foremost among the rebels were the Musunuri Nayakas, **Harihara and Bukka** (from Bellary), and the Rachakonda and Devarakonda Velamas.\n\n**Founder — Betalanayaka:**\n\n*   The founder of the Recherla Velama family was **Betalanayaka**, of the dominant Velama peasant caste.\n*   The _Velugotivari Vamsavali_ describes his rise in the Amanagallu (Nalgonda district) area.\n*   Kakatiya ruler **Ganapatideva (1199–1262 A.D.)** assigned the administration of Amanagallu to him; in return Betalanayaka extended support to the ruler — a tradition continued by his sons **Dama, Prasaditya, and Rudra**, all of whom held important positions under Ganapatideva and later under his daughter **Rudramadevi (1262–1290 A.D.)**.\n*   The Recherla Velamas filled the gap left when the Malyala and Recherla Velama chiefs (prominent under Rudradeva and Ganapatideva) disappeared from active participation; Betalanayaka''s sons filled this gap.\n*   Historian **P.V. Parabrahma Sastry** establishes the role of loyal subordinates like the Recherla Velamas in Kakatiya affairs, referring to \"Rudra, the head of the Recherla Velamas family,\" indicating Betalanayaka had passed the baton to his sons.\n\n**Recherla Rudra under Ganapatideva:**\n\n*   Ganapatideva, after consolidating his throne with the help of loyal commanders **Recherla Rudra** and **Malyala Chaunda**, prepared an expedition to the coastal region.\n*   The Recherla and Malyala chiefs were the oldest of the Kakatiya feudatories.\n*   Following the death of Kakatiya king **Rudradeva** and his brother **Mahadeva** in wars against the Yadavas, Ganapatideva was imprisoned at Devagiri. Nobles revolted, but **Recherla Rudra stayed loyal**, put down the revolt, drove away foreign invaders, and governed the kingdom until Ganapatideva''s return from captivity.\n\n**Under Rudramadevi:**\n\n*   Ganapatideva, having no male heir, nominated his elder daughter **Rudramadevi** as heir; she began ruling as co-regent from **1259–60 A.D.** under the name **Rudradeva Maharaja**.\n*   Some royal family members revolted against the queen. She managed to overcome this with the help of loyal supporters, including the Kayastha chiefs **Jannigadeva** and his younger brother **Tripurari**, and the Velama chief **Prasaditya**.\n*   As per the _Velugotivarivamsavali_, Prasaditya assumed the titles **Kakatiya Rajya Sthapanacharaya** and **Raya Pitamahamka**, as reward for pinning down a \"formidable ring of enemies\" who opposed a woman ruler.\n*   His brother **Rudra** also held a high position in the Kakatiya queen''s court.\n*   The third Recherla brother, **Dama Nayudu**, looked after Amanagallu''s affairs. The _Velugotivari Vamsavali_ says he acquired the titles **Khadganarayana, Rayagaya Govala, Bhujabala Bhima**, and **Pratiganda Bhairava** — though no other literary/epigraphic source confirms this.\n\n**Prasaditya and the Nayamkara system:**\n\n*   Prasaditya was appointed a **Nayaka** under the _Nayamkara_ system that came to fruition under Ganapatideva; he is said to have been entrusted defence of the kingdom as one of a group of **77 Nayakas**.\n*   The author of _Pratapacharitra_ credits **Prataparudra** (Rudramadevi''s successor) with introducing the Nayamkara system, implying it did not exist before — but inscriptions refer to _Nayamkara_/the office of _Nayaka_ as early as **1279 A.D.**, contradicting this.\n*   Therefore, Prasaditya seems to be the first Recherla to have received the title, rights, and duties of a _Nayaka_ under the Nayamkara system, during Rudramadevi''s reign. He remained loyal both to her and to her grandson/successor **Prataparudra**, whom he helped strengthen the kingdom in his early years.\n\n**From Dama''s line to independence:**\n\n*   No direct descendants of Recherla Rudra or Prasaditya are known; **Vennama Nayaka** and **Sabbi Nayaka** succeeded their father Dama.\n*   Vennama Nayaka appears in the **Ainavolu inscription**. The _Velugotivarivamsavali_ records a battle he fought against the Delhi Sultanate — likely during **Alauddin Khilji''s first invasion of Telangana in 1303 A.D.**, when he sent an army under Malik Fakruddin Ulugh and Malik Jajju of Karra. The Kakatiyas lost a large army in this battle, in which **Sabbi Nayaka** also fought alongside his brother.\n*   Vennama Nayaka and Sabbi Nayaka each had a son — **Erra Dacha** and **Nalla Dacha** — who were also loyal to the Kakatiyas and distinguished themselves for Prataparudra in a war against the **Pandyas of Madura**. As a mark of appreciation, Prataparudra conferred on Erra Dacha the titles **Pandyadalavibhala** and **Pandyaraya Gajakesari**.\n*   From among the progeny of Erra Dacha — **Singama Nayaka, Venna Nayaka, and Echa Nayaka** — emerged the first independent Recherla Velamas ruler, **Singama Nayaka (I)**.\n\n**Context of independence — post-1323 unrest:**\n\n*   By 1323 A.D., Warangal fort had fallen to Tughlaq prince Ulugh Khan, who conquered much of Telangana and beyond before returning to Delhi.\n*   The repressive new administrative setup left behind by Ulugh Khan gave rise to deep-seated unrest, giving rise to local rebel leaders, among whom the Recherla Velamas emerged as a successful regional power group in the Rachakonda and Amanagallu areas under Singama Nayaka I.\n\n### Singama Nayaka I (1325–1361 A.D.)\n\nThe capital from which Singama Nayaka I ruled is a matter of some controversy.\n\n*   The _Velugotivari Vamsavali_ states that Singama Nayaka I, son of Erra Dacha, lived in Rachakonda and Devarakonda during the time of Prataparudra — but this lacks confirmation, and this area lies 60 miles from Amanagallu (the hereditary village given to founder Betalanayaka by Ganapatideva).\n*   The _Velugotivarivamsavali_ (the primary authority for Recherla Velama history) does not mention Rachakonda at all until the time of Singama Nayaka I''s son and successor, **Anapota Nayaka**. It states that Anapota Nayaka and **Mada Nayaka** (Singama Nayaka I''s second son), after returning from a victorious campaign, ruled Amangallu and divided Rachakonda and Devarakonda between themselves — evidence that Rachakonda was already in Recherla Velama possession even before Anapota''s time.\n*   Another statement, from the _Rasarnava Sudhakaram_ (a work on rhetoric written by Anapota Nayaka''s son, **Singama Nayaka II**), states that **Rajachala/Rachakonda** was the hereditary capital of the Recherla Velama family.\n*   These references indicate Rachakonda came into Recherla possession much earlier, probably during the time of Singama Nayaka I, who must have built a fort there. It probably became the primary capital only from Anapota Nayaka''s time, later becoming a stronghold of the family.\n\n**Reign — territorial expansion:**\n\n*   Singama Nayaka I''s rule began in turbulent times, with the overthrow of Tughlaq authority.\n*   While Musunuri Kapaya Nayaka was engaged in battle with the Bahmanis (1359–61 A.D.), Singama Nayaka I used the opportunity to extend his kingdom as far as Eleshwaram on the banks of the Krishna, seizing forts in the Krishna–Tungabhadra doab.\n*   He came into conflict with the **Chalukya princes**, who sought help from the **Reddys of Kondavidu** to stop his aggressive attacks.\n*   Undaunted, Singama Nayaka I seized the fort of **Jallipalli** (near Khammam), then under Chalukya control, where his brother-in-law **Chintapalli Singama Nayaka** had been imprisoned by the Chalukya princes during the ongoing battles.\n*   To free Chintapalli, Singama Nayaka carried out a vigorous attack on Jallipalli fort. The Chalukya princes, unable to hold the fort, resorted to treachery and had **Singama Nayaka I assassinated** by one **Tambalajiyya**.\n*   Following the assassination of Singama Nayaka I, his enraged descendants seized the fort of Jallipalli and massacred all the kshatriyas and their allies lodged there, in 1361 A.D.\n\n### Anapota I (1361–1383 A.D.) and Mada I (1361–1384 A.D.)\n\nSingama Nayaka I''s sons **Anapota I** and **Mada I** first avenged their father''s death and assumed the title **Somakula Parasurama**.\n\n*   **Anapota I** ruled from **Rachakonda** (also known as Rajukonda); he placed his brother **Mada I** on the throne of **Devarakonda** to secure that region.\n*   After avenging his father, Anapota I modified the fort of Rachakonda to make it impregnable. Three inscriptions of Anapota I, all dated **1365 A.D.**, record construction of a stone fort, a reservoir called **\"Anapota Samudra,\"** and defences to protect the fort.\n\n**War with Kapaya Nayaka and expansion:**\n\n*   Anapota I and Mada I headed north and attacked Musunuri Kapaya Nayaka for control of Warangal fort, **defeating and killing him at the Battle of Bhimavaram (1368 A.D.)** near Warangal.\n*   Anapota I then conquered the forts of **Tribhuvanagiri (Bhuvanagiri)** and **Sanigaram**, among other minor Telangana forts — campaigns recorded in his inscription at **Ainavolu** near Warangal, inscribed on the occasion of his victory over the entire Telangana region.\n*   By **1369 A.D.**, the kingdom of Rachakonda extended to the **Krishna and Tungabhadra in the south** and **Sanigaram** (in modern Karimnagar district) in the north.\n*   After this series of victories, Anapota I placed his brother **Mada Nayaka I** at Devarakonda to protect the south-eastern parts of the kingdom; Mada I and his successors ruled the territory around Devarakonda, which became the **second capital** of the Recherla Velamas.\n\n**Conquest of Kondavidu territory south of the Krishna:**\n\n*   Anapota I and Mada I, along with their cousin **Naga** (son of Yachama Nayaka), invaded the Reddy kingdom of **Kondavidu**.\n*   The _Velugotivari Vamsavali_ states that Mada Nayaka and Anapota Nayaka defeated **Anavema Reddy** of Kondavidu at **Dharanikota**.\n*   Following this victory, Anapota I and Mada I crossed the Krishna and conquered the region to its south (areas in modern Kurnool and Guntur districts); parts of the Reddy kingdom on the southern banks of the Krishna, particularly around Srisailam (now Kurnool district), were annexed — corroborated by epigraphic evidence.\n*   The _Rasarnava Sudhakaram_ states Mada I constructed a flight of steps to the sacred mountain of **Sriparvatam**, making it accessible to pilgrims from Telangana — indicating Sriparvatam was part of their kingdom. This is supported by an inscription of Mada I at **Jatararevu** near Srisailam, stating the steps were built for the convenience of pilgrims from Telangana.\n*   The joint rule of the two brothers marked the **climax of Recherla Velama power** in Telugu country. By **1384 A.D.**, the kingdom extended south of Devarakonda:\n    *   The inscription of Mada I at **Umamaheshwaram** states his rule extended between Srisailam and the Vindhya mountains.\n    *   The inscription of Anapota at **Simhachalam** indicates the coastal campaigns of the Recherla Velamas went up to Simhachalam — though they could not annex any part of coastal Andhra, which remained under Recherla Velama control only prior to the extension of Bahmani rule into Telangana.\n\n### Singama Nayaka II (1383–1399 A.D.) and Vedagiri I (1384–1410 A.D.)\n\nAnapota I and Mada I were succeeded by their sons — **Singama Nayaka II** (at Rachakonda) and **Vedagiri I** (at Devarakonda) respectively.\n\n*   **Singama Nayaka II**, also known as **Sarvajna Singa Bhupala**, was a great scholar and poet; he composed two important Telugu-literature-related Sanskrit works, _Rasarnava Sudhakaram_ and _Ratnapanchalika_.\n*   His cousin **Vedagiri I** maintained close relations with the Rachakonda chiefs and ruled Devarakonda from **1384 to 1410 A.D.**\n\n**Conflicts with Vijayanagara and the Reddys:**\n\n*   In the conflicts between the Reddys and Velamas, the Vijayanagara rulers supported the Reddys, which pushed the Recherla Velamas toward closer ties with the Bahmani kingdom.\n    \n*   This alliance proved useful when the Vijayanagara ruler attacked Singama Nayaka II but was successfully repelled with Bahmani help — this battle was called the **Battle of Kottakota**; the Vijayanagara army chief **Saluvaraya died** in this battle.\n    \n*   An epigraph dated **1387 A.D.** at Simhachalam suggests the Recherla Velamas invaded the Reddy dominion during the early years of the reign of **Kumaragiri Reddy**.\n    \n*   Singama Nayaka II and his cousin Vedagiri I decided to conquer the northern districts of the Reddy kingdom by taking advantage of the succession tussle between **Peda Komativema Reddy** and Kumaragiri Reddy.\n*   While the Recherla Velama chiefs were engaged in coastal Andhra with the Reddys of Rajahmahendravaram, **Bukka II of Vijayanagara** attacked and occupied the fort of **Panugal** (Mahbubnagar district) and **Warangal fort**, a strategic Recherla Velama possession, in **1397 A.D.**\n*   With the help of the Bahmani ruler **Firoz Shah**, Singama Nayaka II and Vedagiri I put up strong resistance, but this time **lost to Vijayanagara**.\n*   Singama Nayaka II and Vedagiri I also came into conflict with the **Gajapatis of Orissa**, as indicated by the latter''s inscriptions at **Srikurmam** (Srikakulam district) and at **Simhachalam**.\n\n### Anapota II (1399–1421 A.D.), Rao Madanedu, and Mada Nayaka II (1421–1430 A.D.)\n\nIn **1399 A.D.**, Singama Nayaka II was succeeded by his sons **Anapota II** and **Rao Madanedu**. At the same time, **Mada Nayaka II** succeeded his father Vedagiri I at Devarakonda.\n\n*   Their reigns saw a series of wars with the Reddys, the Vijayanagara Rayas, and finally the Bahmanis.\n*   They provoked conflict with the **Reddys of Rajahmahendravaram (Rajahmundry)** by offering asylum to **Annadeva Choda**, a lower-Godavari chief driven out of his dominions by these Reddys.\n*   Madanedu of Devarakonda defeated the Reddys and annexed Annadeva Choda''s dominion — but **Pedakomati Vema Reddy**, with Bahmani help, soon restored the lost territory to Annadeva Choda.\n*   The **Bahmani army led by Sultan Firoz Shah attacked Panugal fort**; this time **Singama Nayaka \\[II''s successors\\] and Vijayanagara joined together**, marched a combined army against the Bahmanis, and defeated them.\n    *   In this battle (**1419 A.D.**), **Macha Reddy**, brother of Pedakomati Vema Reddy, was killed.\n*   Following this defeat, the Recherla Velamas, allied with Pedakomati Vema Reddy, attacked Devarakonda and killed Vedagiri. Soon after, in 1420 A.D., the Velamas killed Pedakomati Vema Reddy and hanged him at the entrance of Devarakonda fort. After this war, the Reddy kingdom of Kondavidu virtually disappeared from the political scenario of Andhra.\n    \n*   After Anapota II, his brother **Rao Madanedu** ascended the throne as regent at Rachakonda, since Anapota''s son **Singama Nayaka III** was too young to be king.\n*   Rao Madanedu and his brothers — **Dacha, Vallabhanedu, Vedagiri, and Damanedu** — helped the young king in wartime and daily administration.\n*   **Rao Madanayaka** (younger brother of Anapota II), also called **Kumara Anapota**, ascended the throne at Rachakonda in **1423 A.D.**\n\n### Singama Nayaka III/Sarvagna Rao and Lingama Nayaka (1430–1475 A.D.)\n\nIn **1425 A.D.**, Anapota II was succeeded by his son **Singama Nayaka III**, and Mada II was succeeded by his son **Vedagiri II** and **Lingama Nayaka** at Devarakonda.\n\n*   **Decline of the kingdom:** during this period, Rachakonda witnessed decline.\n    *   The **Bahmanis of Bidar** occupied the forts of Medak, Warangal, and Bhuvanagiri during **1433–38 A.D.**\n    *   What happened to Singama Nayaka III subsequently is not known; he probably retired to **Bellamkonda** in Guntur district, where he appears to have led an eventful life till **1455 A.D.**\n    *   Except for Devarakonda, the rest of the Recherla Velama kingdom was subjugated by the Bahmanis.\n\n**Lingama Nayaka''s revival via Orissa alliance:**\n\n*   **Lingama Nayaka** (known as Lingamanedu), unable to resist Bahmani attacks, sought the help of the Orissa rulers.\n*   Because of the Velamas'' weakness, Kapileshwara Gajapati of Orissa readily accepted Lingama Nayaka''s request and sent a large force to Devarakonda against the Bahmanis.\n*   Lingama Nayaka **reoccupied his lost territories such as Orugallu and Bhuvanagiri in 1461 A.D.**, becoming a vassal of the Gajapatis of Orissa on account of this help — corroborated by inscriptions of Kapileshwara Gajapati.\n*   **Rao Dharma Rao**, son of Lingama Nayaka, was appointed to rule over Orugallu.\n\n**End of the kingdom:**\n\n*   The integrity of the kingdom of Rachakonda and Devarakonda came to an end in Lingama Nayaka''s last years.\n*   At the death of **Kapileshwara Gajapati in 1470 A.D.**, conflicts arose between his sons **Purushotama Gajapati** and **Kumara Hamvira Gajapati** for the throne.\n*   Taking advantage of this Orissa succession conflict, the Bahmani Sultan Muhammad Shah II defeated Lingama Nayaka and occupied Rachakonda and Devarakonda.\n*   After Lingama Nayaka, his brother **Suranedu**, son **Parvata Rao**, and grandson **Lingama Nayaka II** ruled over Devarakonda for some time as subordinates of the Bahmanis, paying annual tribute; later they came under the yoke of the **Vijayanagaras**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p4", "partName": "Inter-State Relations of the Velamas", "bodyMd": "The Velamas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda ruled the entire Telangana as a successful \"Regional Power\" for **one and a half centuries**, playing an active role in contemporary politics.\n\n*   Since the kingdom was situated amidst the **Bahmanis of Gulbarga** (west), **Rayas of Vijayanagara** (south), **Reddys of Kondavidu** (east), and **Gajapatis of Orissa** (north), the Velamas had to contend with these neighbours to stabilise and extend their power — hence they often shifted alliances in the troubled waters.\n\n### Relations with Bahmanis of Gulbarga\n\nDuring the early rule of the Velamas, relations with the Bahmanis were cordial — **Bahaman Shah I** helped **Anapota Nayaka I** against Kapaya Nayaka. Cordial relations continued through Singama Nayaka II and Vedagiri I.\n\n*   During the reigns of Anapota II and Mada II, when they were at war with the Reddys, Feroz Shah II attacked and besieged Panugal fort (1417 A.D.), which ended the friendly relationship; Anapota II cut off the traditional policy of loyal friendship with the Bahmanis and joined hands with the Vijayanagara.\n*   The unfriendly attitude continued till the end of the Velamas.\n*   During the reigns of **Sarvagna Rao Singama Nayaka III and Lingama Nayaka**, the Bahmani Sultan **Ahmad Shah II** attacked the forts of Orugallu, Bhongir, Rachakonda, and Devarakonda and seized wealth. This ultimately resulted in the **subjugation of the Velamas** to vassalage under the Bahmanis and, finally, the Vijayanagaras.\n\n### Relations with Reddys of Kondavidu\n\nRelations between the Velamas and Reddys were never cordial from the outset.\n\n*   Since the Reddy kingdom was a doab with rich, fertile rice-yielding soils on the coast, while the Velama kingdom was an arid zone, the Velamas'' eyes were fixed on the Reddys'' coastal territory, making conflict inevitable.\n*   The _Velugotivari Vamshavali_ refers to Velama–Reddy conflicts that ended with no material benefits to either party, except giving scope for **Bahmani and Vijayanagara intervention** in their political affairs.\n\n### Relations with Rayas of Vijayanagara\n\nThe political alliances between the Velamas and Vijayanagara kings were fluid and temporary.\n\n*   When Singama Nayaka I and Vedagiri were engaged in war with the Reddys in coastal Andhra, Bukkaraya II attacked Panugal fort, taking advantage of their absence in Telangana — resulting in the outbreak of rivalry between the two parties.\n*   Vijayanagara''s intervention into Reddy–Kondavidu affairs prevented the Velamas'' coastal campaigns. The last powerful ruler, **Lingama Nayaka**, who attempted to win Vijayanagara territories, ended his career in failure.\n\n### Relations with Gajapatis of Orissa\n\nRelations between the Velamas and Gajapatis of Orissa were friendly and cordial.\n\n*   The early reign of **Anapota I** and his brother''s son **Vedagiri** extended as far as Srikurmam and Simhachalam, in the process of kingdom extension.\n*   During the last phase, under **Lingama Nayaka**, **Kapileshwara Gajapati of Orissa** came to assist the Velama chief of Devarakonda to overcome the invasion of **Humayun Shah** of the Bahmani dynasty; the Orissa rulers came forward as far as Orugallu, supported by epigraphs laid down by the Orissa rulers at that fort.\n*   Thereafter, the Velamas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda acted as subordinates to the Gajapati rulers, owing to their assistance sought in their wars with the Bahmanis.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p5", "partName": "Administrative System of the Velamas", "bodyMd": "Main sources for the polity of the Velamas period in Telangana include contemporary Telugu works: _Sakala Niti Sammatamu_ (Madiki Singana), _Rukmangada Charitra_ (Praudha Kavi Mallana), _Kridabhiramamu_ (Srinatha), _Bhaskara Ramayanamu_, _Harivamsamu_ (Erra­preggada), _Pratapa Charitra_ (Ekambranatha), and _Siddhesvara Charitra_ (Kase Sarvappa). These furnish material on polity, administration, offices (civil and military), taxation, war and peace, forts, and espionage.\n\n*   The _Velugotivari Vamsavali_ also deals with the history of the Recherla Velamas.\n*   Between **1323–1475 A.D.**, all the kingdoms were guided by the theory of _**saptanga**_ — the seven constituent elements of sovereignty enjoined by **Kautilya**: king, minister, land, fort, treasury, army, and friend.\n*   Contemporary Telugu works on polity also emphasized this theory:\n    *   _Andhra Kamandakamu_ (cited by Singana) also emphasizes _saptanga_.\n    *   _Rukmangada Charitra_ lists: _pati_ (king), _amatya_ (minister), _durgamu_ (fort), _kshiti_ (land), _mitra_ (friend), _dhanamu_ (treasury), and _sena_ (army).\n\n### Saptanga in Practice\n\n**The King:**\n\n*   Monarchy was the order of the day; the king was the supreme head of state, expected to govern without fear or favour per the _dharma sastras and smritis_.\n*   Post-Kakatiya kings did not assume regal titles signifying kingship (unlike their Kakatiya masters) — the Musunuri and Velama kings omitted the epithet _mahamandaleshwara_ from their _prasasti_.\n\n*   Because they belonged to the _chaturthakula_ (fourth caste), the Hindu dharma sastras did not permit them regal titles, as they did not belong to the kshatriya caste claiming descent from the Sun god or Moon god.\n\n*   According to contemporary literature, the king was expected to be well-educated, and to maintain cordial relations with other ruling chiefs.\n*   **Madiki Singana** draws a verse from _Kumara Sambhavam_ enjoining the king to be thorough in the _sapta upayas_ (seven strategies):\n    *   _Samamu_ (conciliation)\n    *   _Bhedamu_ (creating a division)\n    *   _Danamu_ (charity)\n    *   _Dandamu_ (punishment)\n    *   _Maya_ (deception)\n    *   _Upeksha_ (ignoring)\n    *   _Indrajala_ (magic)\n*   The king was also expected to possess sharp intelligence, strong memory, foresight, knowledge of warfare, and to strictly maintain _varnasrama dharma_.\n*   _Rukmangada charitra_ prescribes the art of riding elephants, horses, and chariots, and wielding different weapons, besides studying _natya_ (dance), _kavya_ (literature), _alankara sastra_ (rhetoric), and histories as laid down in the _Puranas_ and _Itihasas_.\n*   An examination of inscriptions reveals **Musunuri Kapaya Nayaka** was an expert in statecraft, had a _Vimala charita_ (spotless character), and was authoritative in the observance of _dharma karmas_. Among the Recherla Velamas, **Sarvagna Singa Bhupala** was a great scholar who wrote _Rasarnava Sudhakaram_.\n*   As prescribed by law, the king was to rule with justice and morality:\n    *   The _Velugotivari Vamsavali_ states a king must, like Lord Rama of the _Ramayana_, evoke the faith and confidence of his subjects.\n    *   The _Rukmangada charitra_ states a king who rules without morality and justice would be deprived of his wealth and become destitute.\n*   **Succession** was normally hereditary, based on the law of **primogeniture** (succession by the eldest); however there were a few instances of succession by brothers or cousins, as with **Musunuri Prolaya Nayaka**, succeeded by his brother Kapaya Nayaka, and **Peda Komati Vema**, succeeding his uncle Komaragiri after the death of his son Anavota II.\n*   There appears to have existed a kind of joint rule or _**dvairajya**_ by the king and the crown prince (_yuvaraja_), probably meant to impart practical administrative training to the crown prince. _Sakala Niti Sammatamu_ states the _yuvaraja_ and the minister (_amatya_) were like the two shoulders to the king.\n\n**The Minister:**\n\n*   The minister (_mantri_) stood next in importance to the king. Matters of importance prescribed that a king hold consultations with a minister or council of ministers.\n*   The council was generally to comprise:\n    *   **12 members** according to Manu\n    *   **16** according to Brihaspati\n    *   **20** according to _Sukranitisara_\n*   **Mallana** warns the king not to rely entirely on the minister''s advice — if the king entrusts the kingdom to the minister''s care without personally looking after affairs, the minister will become the master in due course.\n*   The head of the council of ministers was called _**pradhani**_; other members were called _**mantrins**_.\n*   Mallana prescribes qualities required of a minister: _**suddhi**_ (righteousness), _**buddhi**_ (wisdom), _**santi**_ (composure), _**vriddhacharamu**_ (tradition), _**vivekamu**_ (discrimination), _**sraddha**_ (devotion), _**dhairyamu**_ (courage), and _**prabhu buddhi**_ (loyalty).\n*   Almost all works on polity state that only **Brahmans** should be appointed as ministers.\n\n**The Fort:**\n\n*   The fort held the next place of importance. **Madiki Singana** devotes a chapter titled _Durga Samrakshanamu_ in his _Sakala Niti Sammatamu_, discussing kinds of forts, methods of attack and defence, and garrison.\n*   He cites a verse from _Kamandakamu_: a king should take refuge in a fort like a tiger in the bushes, an elephant on the hilltop, and a lion in the cave; a king requires a strong fort on high mountain cliffs or in a dense forest, like Lord Vishnu whose abode is the deep ocean, or Siva on the high Himalayas.\n*   _Purushartha Sara_ compares a king without a fort to an elephant without rut and a serpent without poison.\n*   Singana prescribes several artificial contrivances to strengthen forts: a high rampart (_prakara_), towers (_gopura_), and bastions (_attalaka_ and _kottala_).\n*   The garrison should contain plenty of grains, wealth, water, men, material, wood, machines, weapons of war, and strong entrance/exit gateways.\n*   **Mallana** emphasizes forts and prescribes as essential: _kota_ (_prakara_), _agadta_ (moat), _attada_ (tower), _gavani_ (gateway), and _kavata_ (doorframe).\n*   Forts of the post-Kakatiya period belonged to all four kinds mentioned:\n    *   **Land forts** (_sthala durgas_): **Warangal**\n    *   **Hill forts** (_giri durgas_): **Devarakonda and Bhuvanagiri**\n*   Forts enjoyed a prominent place in the Recherla and Devarakonda Velamas'' kingdom, evident from titles borne by various kings — e.g., the **Draksharama inscription of Anapota** calls him _Dvipijeta_.\n*   Rachakonda, Devarakonda, and Bhuvanagiri were the main **hill forts** of the Recherla Velamas; Orugallu, Amanagallu, Anathagiri, Aruvapally, Podichedy, Anumula, Pangal, and Jallipalli were their **land forts**.\n\n**Land or Territory:**\n\n*   A king is not worth the name without territory. Kautilya accords it third place in order of importance. Contemporary Telugu works call it _rashtra_. **Mallana** assigns it fourth place and calls it _Kshiti_, advising a king to protect his land effectively from enemy attacks and not turn his back.\n\n**Mitra (Friend):**\n\n*   Kautilya assigns suitable friends the seventh place; contemporary Telugu works place it fifth in priority. \n*   In practice, the Rachakonda and Devarakonda Velamas, considering the size and strength of their kingdoms, entered into matrimonial alliances and friendship with one superpower or another — either the Rayas of Vijayanagara or the Bahmanis of Gulbarga — to save themselves.\n\n**Treasury:**\n\n*   Kautilya places the royal treasury fifth in importance, but Telugu works stress the king should not extract more money than needed — likened to \"eating the fish after excavating the tanks.\"\n*   While information specific to the Musunuri and Rachakonda/Devarakonda Velamas is not directly available, **Madiki Singana** and **Mallana** provide information on state income sources:\n    *   _**Kshiti pancham**_ (produce of the land paid by the farmer to the king), termed _**arambamu**_\n    *   Taxes: _**karamu, kappamu, ari**_ and _**appanamu**_\n    *   _**Illari**_ — taxes on houses\n    *   _**Vritti katnalu**_ — taxes on looms, oil mills, etc.\n    *   _**Pullari**_ — taxes on pasture, lands\n    *   _**Sunka**_ — taxes/duties on import of trade articles\n\n**The Army:**\n\n*   This occupies **sixth** place in Kautilya''s _saptanga_ theory, but Telugu works place it in **seventh** position.\n*   The army was based on the traditional _**chaturangabala**_: infantry, cavalry, elephant, and chariots (chariot design/draught animal is not known).\n*   An inscription of the Velamas reveals the infantry consisted of **four divisions**:\n    1.  _**Selagola**_ — javelin/spear-bearers\n    2.  _**Vilukandru**_ — archers/bowmen\n    3.  _**Kaijitagandru**_ — warriors/soldiers who drew salaries daily or weekly\n    4.  _**Ekkati**_ — a soldier kept in reserve, capable of handling heavy weapons like maces\n*   Weapons in vogue: sabres, daggers, maces, javelins, spears, battle axes, discs, and _pashana yantras_ (catapults). The shield was known as _arige_ or _dalu_.\n*   Military generals were known by different names: _padinayaka, padalu, dalavayi,_ and _dandanayaka_. The _Velugotivari Vamsavali_ mentions _padinayakas, biruda rahuttas,_ and _lenkas_.\n\n### Administrative Divisions\n\nThe Rachakonda and Devarakonda Velama kingdoms were divided into administrative units: _**bhumi, nadu, sthala,**_ and _**grama**_ (village).\n\n*   The village formed the basic unit of administration.\n    *   _**Talari**_ — head man of the village\n    *   _**Karanam**_ — village accountant, maintained land records and tax rates for arable, waste, cultivated, fallow, wet, and dry lands\n    *   _**Bari kapu**_ — village policeman; maintained boundaries and guarded fields from wild beasts and thieves\n*   Other officials:\n    *   _**Kavile sampratis**_ — record keepers who maintained registers of royal orders on revenue and expenditure\n    *   _**Rayasams**_ (or _vrayasa_) — scribes/private secretaries to the king; required to be quick in hand, write many scripts, and be proficient in various languages", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p6", "partName": "Social Conditions", "bodyMd": "Social mobility generally occurred in two phases: first, the lowest castes and tribes were **sanskritized**; second, the sanskritized people were drawn into caste society as **Sudras**.\n\n*   The service of **Brahmans** was essential for this mobility — they brahmanised Desi culture on one hand, and on the other provided lineages to rulers, legitimizing them as Kshatriyas and stabilizing their ritual hegemony.\n*   The **Varna structure** in the period is tripartite: Brahmans, Vaisyas, and Sudras (no Kshatriya institutions existed in medieval South India).\n\n### The Brahmans\n\nThe Brahmans, per _Purusasukata_, were said to be born from the head of _Viratpurusa_, and were theoretically the _agra varna_ in contemporary Dharma Sastras and inscriptions.\n\n*   Their ritual purity, determined by birth, elevated their position as pace-makers and legitimizers of ruling power.\n*   Brahmins were expected to perform:\n    *   **Panchayajnas**: _Pitru Yajna_ (worship of ancestors), _Guru Yajna_ (worship of priests), _Evan Yajna_ (worship of God), _Brahma Yajna_ (worship of Brahmins), _Go Yajna_ (worship of cows)\n    *   **Shatkarmas**: _Yajna_ (sacrifice), _Yajana_ (priest to the sacrifice), _Svadhyaya_ (self-study), _Adhyapana_ (teaching), _Danam_ (donation), _Pratigrahana_ (accepting donation)\n    *   Austerities: _yama, niyama, dhyana, dharana, mouna, anushta,_ etc.\n*   Whenever forests were cleared, new villages were formed and donated to Brahmins as _agraharas_ — a practice extensively done in Kakatiyan times as part of economic policy.\n*   Meanwhile, pastoral communities (**Gollas, Kurumas**) and tribes (**Pulindas, Koyas, Boyas**) were drawn into caste society as Sudras; the Brahmins brahmanized their deities and identified them with the Hindu pantheon.\n*   Kings gave _agraharas_ to Brahmins in newly conquered provinces so they could act as pace-makers with the people and legitimize royal rule — an exchange of material resources for royal honours.\n*   Notable Brahmin families in Kakatiya strategic posts: **Gangadhara, Tikkana, Sivadevaiah, Induluri, Bendapudi**.\n*   Under **Mada Nayaka I (1361–1384 A.D.)**, the Brahmin minister **Potanamatya** was regarded as _Rajyangaika durandhrah_ (expert in statecraft), _Sarvasastra visarada_ (scholar in different branches of learning), _Kavivaa_ (best among poets), and _Sthotraika patrikrutam_ (only person eligible for flattering \\[the king\\]).\n*   There was a shift in the Brahmins'' conception of role in society, reflected in professional mobility; sectarianism among Brahmins was not yet highly deep-rooted.\n*   With the reduction of _agraharas_ and the extensive appointment of Brahmins in royal courts, a gulf broadened between the **Vaidika Brahmins** (teaching/priestly professions) and the **Niyogis** (secular duties).\n\n### Kshatriyas\n\nThere was a total absence of formal Kshatriya institutions in medieval South India; the dharma/duty of Kshatriyas was to protect and rule the people.\n\n*   Peasant warrior families that dominated South India in the medieval period gradually became rulers of the land, called **sat-Sudras** because of their pertinence to domestic/ritual engagements — among such Sudras, the **Reddys, Velamas, Kammas** deserve mention.\n*   Dominant peasant warrior groups of the early medieval times — the **Rastakutas, Chalukyas, Kakatiyas** — claimed Kshatriyahood after attaining sovereignty; the Rastrakutas, Chalukyas, and Kakatiyas were claimed to be Kshatriyas of the **lunar race**.\n*   Regional/local powers that arose after the fall of the Kakatiya kingdom followed the same practice — the **Chodas of Kandur, Nellore, Konidena, and Pottapi** were said to be Kshatriyas of the **solar race**.\n*   This transmission of political domination associated with the symbol of Kshatriyahood, from central to supra-local/local powers, was common in medieval times.\n*   **Herman Kulke** terms this process **\"Kshatriyaization\"** — the sat-Sudra families/dominant peasant warrior groups, in the process of agrarian expansion, attained political power and claimed Kshatriyahood to stabilize their newly acquired authority, tracing their lineage to mythical races.\n*   It is interesting that the **Velamas**, who emerged as a regional power in post-Kakatiya Telangana, did **not** claim lineage of the solar race — instead they claimed equality with the **Raghu Vamsa**. Their subordinate rulers of **Jallipalli** were called **Soma Vamsa Kshatriyas**.\n\n### The Vaisyas\n\nThe Vaisyas, locally called **Komatis**, were the traditional traders in Telugu country.\n\n*   By the time of the Chalukya-Cholas, the Vaisyas of coastal Andhra were organized into a caste-cum-professional guild known as **''Nakaram''** at eighteen different places, with headquarters at **Penugonda** in West Godavari district.\n*   During Kakatiyan times, the Vaisyas were organized into _Desi, Nanadesi, ubhaya nanadesi, Nakaras_ dealing with different items ranging from grain to elephants, as trade span increased.\n*   The Vaisyas had to compete with **Balijas**, mentioned in records as _Balanjasettikandru_; they claimed themselves as _Virabalanjara dharma Pratipalitulu_ and were organized into another guild called **Pekkandru** (itself indicating an itinerant guild).\n*   The Vaisyas'' profession was proliferated by these Balijas; by the 15th century the Vaisyas had become part and parcel of the Pekkandru guild — indicating decline in the itinerant nature of Vaisya trade, and that their profession was proliferated by Sudra-varna.\n\n### The Sudras\n\nThe Sudras, constituting the bulk of the population, ranged from **sat-Sudras to Panchamas**, divided into right-hand and left-hand communities:\n    *   **Right-hand community / sat-Sudras**: engaged in clean/agricultural operations — included the Velamas, Kammas, and Reddy communities, segregated on lineage and territorial basis\n    *   **Left-hand community**: the others\n*   Among **Kapu**, there were **14 branches** organized territorially:\n\n| # | Branch | # | Branch |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Pakanati | 8 | Nereti |\n| 2 | Velanati | 9 | Bhumanci |\n| 3 | Motati | 10 | Desati |\n| 4 | Morasa | 11 | Ayodhya |\n| 5 | Pongalinati | 12 | Oruganti |\n| 6 | Munnuti | 13 | Kuriceti |\n| 7 | Panta | 14 | Gandikota |\n\n*   They claimed themselves as the **settis**. The **Panchnamvaru**, who dealt with carpentry, pottery, blacksmithing, masonry, and goldsmithing, claimed their origin from the five faces of Brahma and claimed equality with the Brahmins, as revealed by their elaborate _prasastis_.\n*   **Tambalas** were another community, most of whom were saints who followed austerities such as purity and pollution, and performed the duties of priests, also claiming equality with the Brahmins. In the famous **Ayyanavolu temple**, the priests are Tambalas.\n    *   In the left-hand communities, social mobility is \"vertical and group-wise,\" unlike among the sat-Sudras, where mobility was family-wise or individual.\n\n### The Panchamas\n\nThe Panchamas constitute **Malas** and **Madigas**.\n    *   **Malas** — constituted agricultural labour\n    *   **Madigas** — prepared accessories for agriculture\n*   Both lived in the outskirts of villages, but sometimes actively participated in fairs and festivals of village gods/goddesses. In the **Ekavira temple**, during festival times, Mala ladies played a very prominent role. The Madigas were employed for executing animal sacrifices. Ritually they were incorporated into society through temples and festivals.\n\n### The Tribals\n\nTribals included **boyas, pulinda, koyas,** and **lambadis**; among pastoral communities, **gollas** and **kurumas** deserve mention.\n\n*   The **Gollas and Kurumas** were dominant communities in this region; their gods **Birappa** and **Mallanna** were equalized with Lord **Mallikarjuna of Sriparvata**.\n\n### Women''s Position\n\nThe Vedic style of family was based on the docility and submissiveness of women; unquestioning obedience on the wife''s part was held up as the supreme virtue of womanhood.\n\n*   The _Rukmangada Charitra_ states that a woman should not be angry even if her husband desires the company of another woman — she should please both by her behaviour. If the husband dies, she should jump into the funeral pyre.\n*   **The widow** was perhaps the most miserable of all — an object of ridicule, a victim of insults and injuries. The law ignored her; society spurned her; per the law, her blessings were considered poison and none should accept them.\n*   Contemporary works praised to the skies wives who committed **Sati** and condemned those who preferred to live. Examples:\n    *   **Peradevi**, wife of Alaraju, committed Sati when her husband was poisoned to death by Nayakuralu of Palanadu.\n    *   **Rati Devi**, wife of Manmatha, asked for a funeral pyre to be prepared for herself and her husband, when the latter was turned to ashes by Siva.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p7", "partName": "Religious Conditions", "bodyMd": "During the Velama regions of Rachakonda and Devarakonda, the era witnessed the growth of **Saivism and Vaishnavism** in Telangana.\n\n*   During Kakatiya times, **Maktal, Borugadda, and Lingagiri** were centres of Vaishnavism.\n*   The growth of the Vijayanagaras, who patronaged the Chennakeshava cult, would have necessitated the Velama kings to also extend patronage to the Vaishnava cult in this region.\n*   The Velama kings of Rachakonda and Devarakonda had links with the **Vaishnavate acharyas of Srirangam Desika**; perhaps **Vedanta Desika** of Srirangam was preceptor of **Venna**, son of Mada Nayaka I.\n*   Through the efforts of these acharyas, the two opposite schools of Vaishnavism soon crystallized into two well-defined sects: the **Vadagalai** (northern school) and the **Tengalai** (southern school). **Vedanta Desika** was leader of the Vadagalai school, and **Pillai Lokacharya** the leader of the Tengalai school.\n*   The Velama kings gifted in cash and kind to temples of both Saivite and Vaishnavate cults:\n\n**Gifts to Temples**\n\n| King | Inscription | Gift |\n|---|---|---|\n| Anapota I | Simhachalam temple (1380 AD) | Five Tanks |\n| Singa II | Simhachalam temple (1387 AD) | Tiruvalika kola |\n| Vedagiri I | Simhachalam temple (1404 AD) | Tankalu 80 |\n| Vedagiri I | Srikurmam (1405 AD) | 100 madas |\n| Venna | Kandikonda (1375 AD) | Lands |\n| Mada I | Domalapalli (1367 AD) | Lands |\n| Anapota I | Ayanavolu (1369 AD) | Ayanavolu village |\n| Vedagiri I | Murupunutala (1399 AD) | Murupunutala village |\n| Rao Dharma Nayaka | Sayampet (1469 AD) | 10 vrittis of village Macherla |\n\n*   This shows the Velama kings tried to exchange their \"honours\" with sectarian leaders through temples; the latter became an important component of royal policy. The most important aspect was the construction of **royal temples** or popular cult centres — Velama kings not only constructed new temples but also renovated existing ones:\n\n**Saivate Temples**\n\n| King | Inscription | Temple/Mandapa |\n|---|---|---|\n| Anapota I | Rachakonda (1365 AD) | Bhairava Prastishta |\n| Mada I | Umamaheswaram (1376 AD) | Mandapa |\n| Venna | Kandikonda (1375 AD) | Temple |\n| Anapota | Ayanavolu (1369 AD) | Walls construction |\n\n**Vaishnavate Temples**\n\n| King | Inscription | Temple/Mandapa |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mada I | Domalapally (1367 A.D.) | Chennakesava Pratista |\n| Venna | Kandikonda (1375 A.D.) | Temple |\n\n*   Gifting and renovating established cult centres such as the **Mailara temple of Ayanavolu** and the **Umamahesvaram temple** at Umamaheshwaram reflect the kings'' efforts to stabilize power through ritual policy.\n*   Traditional scholars held that the Velamas were staunch Vaishnavites, extending patronage to Vedanta Desika and constructing new Vaishnavite temples. \n\n*   But the **Ayanavolu inscription** states that **Mailaradeva** was their _Kuladevata_ (tutelary deity) — a contradictory piece of evidence, showing that like other medieval power groups, the Velama kings tried to stabilize their hegemony through royal temple policy, extending patronage to popular cults and centres broadly.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p8", "partName": "Economic Conditions", "bodyMd": "Economic factors play a dominant role in characterizing any given polity.\n\n*   After 1300 A.D., the trend in the economy changed — a shift known in economic nomenclature as ''revival'' — attested by increased money circulation and growth of trade and commerce.\n*   Some inscriptions of the Rachakonda and Devarakonda Velamas refer to monetary grants given to the temples of Simhachalam and Srikakulam:\n\n**Monetary Grants**\n\n| King | Inscription | Grant |\n|---|---|---|\n| Anapota I | Simhachalam temple (1380 AD) | 5 Tankas |\n| Vedagiri I | Simhachalam temple (1404 AD) | 80 Tankas |\n| Vedagiri I | Srikurumam (1405 AD) | 100 Madas |\n\n*   The Simhachalam and Srikakulam inscriptions indicate the clue to economic revival. Prior to Kakatiya rule, most inscriptions issued by feudatories and local chiefs record land donations to temples instead of monetary donations. Post-Kakatiya-period inscriptions issued by feudal lords refer to monetary grants alongside land grants — indirectly reflecting the growth of a monetary economy, negligible in the pre-Kakatiya period, showing signs of revival in the post-Kakatiya age.\n*   The money endowments to temples also indicate enhanced temple investment capability due to the cautious royal temple policy. **Temple economy** under regional power started emerging as an institution for resource mobilization and re-distribution. Temples also received gifts and donations from guilds.\n    *   No inscriptions belonging to the provinces of Rachakonda and Devarakonda Velama kings mention guilds directly, but contemporary inscriptions of regional powers like the Reddys refer to guilds. Guild activity seems to have undergone change, reflecting the revival in the economy owing to increased money circulation.\n*   The growth of trade and commerce resulted in the expansion of agriculture. The Velamas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda, being subordinates of Kakatiyas, followed their footsteps — the Kakatiya rulers had formulated a policy of deforestation and land reclamation for agricultural expansion.\n\n### Geography and Irrigation\n\nThe Telangana region is a **rain shadow area** with a limited river system; even rivers flowing across the region are hardly beneficial for cultivation. There is no big river flowing across Telangana; only the **Krishna** touches its peripheral zones. Rivers **Dindi, Musi, Aleru**, etc., are tributaries of the Krishna.\n\n*   The region ruled by Rachakonda and Devarakonda Velama kings lies mostly in the present-day districts of **Mahaboobnagar and Nalgonda** — districts covered with red soils hardly drained by any river system, remaining waste and producing only wild bushes/shrubs.\n*   Many epigraphs issued by Rachakonda and Devarakonda kings speak of construction of tanks and wells:\n\n**Irrigation Facilities**\n\n| King | Inscription | Irrigation Work |\n|---|---|---|\n| Anapota I | Rachakonda (1365 AD) | Anapota Samudram (tank); 2 Wells |\n| Anapota I | Rachakonda (1380 AD) | Raya Samudram (tank) |\n| Nagambhika Queen of Rao Mada Nayaka | Devulanagaram (1429 AD) | Naga Samudram (tank) |\n| Vedagiri | Sannad (1429 AD) | Vedagiri Tatakamu (tank) |\n| Madhava Rao | Sannad (1429 AD) | Parvata Rao Tatakamu (tank) |\n| Madahava Rao | Sannad (1429 AD) | Madha Rao Cheruvu (tank) |\n\n*   About **20 inscriptions** belonging to Velama kings were found in this region; of these at least four are fragmented. Of the remaining 16, **6 inscriptions** (three of them _sannads_) mention either construction or reconstruction of tanks.\n*   Contemporary epigraphs and literature mention the founding of new villages alongside tank construction; every village was benefitted by a major tank in the region — the tank irrigation system generally resulted in the establishment of nuclear villages.\n\n### Land Tenure and Village Categories\n\n**First category**: villages held directly by the state (central power), where the state collected revenues directly through its own administrative machinery — generally known as _**Bhandaaravada gramas**_ or _**kara**_ villages. The Mohammedan rulers called them **Khalsa** or **Khas** lands/villages, meaning \"crown lands.\"\n\n*   **Second category**: **Nayaka villages**, held by state officials/feudatories rendering military services to the state — situated around the forts of Rachakonda, Devarakonda, Bhuvanagiri, Golconda, Sirikonda, Panugallu, Kolanupaka, Pillalamarri, Hanamkonda, Orugallau, etc.\n    *   These areas were not hereditary, and the emperor could confiscate holdings at any time.\n    *   These feudatories also leased out lands to revenue farmers or _**guttadars**_, though the villagers themselves held most of the land; therefore \"they claimed no property right, nor did they regard the cultivators as their serfs.\"\n*   **Third category**: villages held by groups of people or institutions like temples, paying nothing or a nominal amount as revenue — called _**brahmadeyas**_ or _**devadana**_ lands.\n\n**Brahmadeya and Devadana Land Tenures**\n\n| King | Inscription | Devadana | Brahmadeya |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Anapota I | Aynavolu (1369 AD) | Aynavolu village | — |\n| Vedagiri | Murupunutala (1399 AD) | Murupunutala village | Land grants |\n| Mada I | Domalapally (1367 AD) | Land grants | Land grants |\n| Venna | Kandikonda (1375 AD) | – | Land grants |\n| Rao Dharma Nayaka | Sayampeta (1464 AD) | Maccerla village and Land grants | Maccerla village and Land grants |\n\n*   It is interesting to note from the above table that the _brahmadeya_ grants, which dominated in Pre-Kakatiyan times, showed a decrease in the post-Kakatiya period, and their place was largely taken over by _devadana_ grants.\n\n### Soil Types and Land Measurement\n\n**Soil Types**\n\n| King | Inscription | Wet Land | Dry Land |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Mada I | Domalapally (1367 A.D.) | Kulyakshetram (wet land under canal, _niru nela_); Kedara (wet land) | Velibhumi (dry land) |\n| Rao Dharama Nayaka | Sayampet (1464 A.D.) | Kedara (wet land) | Krishna (black soil), Pandura |\n\n*   The above table indicates these chiefs followed systematic land administration — land revenue assessment was based on soil types. Not only were soils divided, but land under cultivation was divided into various measurements.\n\n**Land Measurements**\n\n| King | Inscription | Measurements |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mada I | Domalapally (1367 A.D.) | _Nivart_ (a land measure equal to modern acre); _Maruthuu_ (a certain/uncertain land measure generally used as equivalent to modern acre) |\n| Venna | Kandikonda (1375 A.D.) | _Tumu_ (a measure of land) |\n\n*   The breakup of cultivable lands into various sizes, and their donation to temples or individuals, reflects that **private property in land** was becoming apparent in medieval times.\n*   The ownership of the soil is a debatable question for medieval times: one school of thought believed land belonged to the entire village, shared among them equally; another group believed land belonged to the king. However, neither notion is completely correct — land grants by kings, chiefs, guilds, or commoners reveal that private proprietary rights over land existed during medieval times.\n*   Thus, increasing trade and commerce indicated economic revival and resulted in the expansion of cultivation. The founding of new villages and construction/reconstruction of tanks brought forth legal issues regarding land ownership — **private property in land became apparent** during this period under the regional powers.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p9", "partName": "Sanskrit and Telugu Literature", "bodyMd": "### Sanskrit Literature\n\n**Sarvajna Singama II**, son of Anapota I, was an eminent poet who composed _Rasarnava Sudhakaram_, _Ratna Panchalika_, and _Sangeeta Sudhakaram_. His son **Rao Madha Naya** also wrote a Sanskrit book, _Raghaviyam_, a commentary on the Sanskrit Ramayana.\n\n*   **Naganatha**, a well-known Sanskrit poet of Anapota I, wrote _Madanavilasa bhanamu_.\n*   **Visveshwara Kavi** and **Appayaruyudu** flourished during the reign of **Singhamabhupala II**. **Visveshwara**, the court poet of Singamabhupala, wrote a book on _Alankara Sastra_ titled _Chamatkara Chandrika_, in which he described Sarvagna II as _sahitya silpavadhi_ (i.e., \"sculptor in literature\").\n*   **Sakalya Mallubattu**, another eminent Sanskrit scholar, composed _Nirdhyoshta Ramayanam_, _Udara Raghavamu_, and _Avyaya Sangraha nighantuvu_. He is also called **Chaturbhasha Kavita Pitamahanka** (\"poet-grandfather in four languages\"). He was court poet of **Singama Nayaka III**. His grandson **Sakalya Ayyalarya** was the laureate writer of the court of **Vedagiri I**, of the _Bhaskara Ramayanam_.\n\n### Telugu Literature\n\n**Pusapati Naganathudu** worked on _Vishnupuranam_, describing the victories of Velama kings.\n\n*   **Gaurana** wrote _Navanatha Charitra_ and _Harichandropakhyanam_ in Telugu.\n*   **Koravi Goparaju** wrote _Simhasana Dvatrimsika_ during the Velama kingdom period.\n*   **Bammara Pothana** is one of the great poets; his finest writings include _Bhogini Dandakam_, _Andhramaha Bharatham_, and _Veerabhadra Vijayam_.\n*   The Velamas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda, like other medieval rulers, tried to legitimize their power through entitlement, foliation (descent claims), and patronage to arts and letters.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p10", "partName": "Art and Architecture", "bodyMd": "\n\n### Painting\n\n**Machaldevi**, the courtesan of the last Kakatiya ruler Prataparudra, is said to have maintained _Chitrasalas_ (painting museums) in Orugallu, described in _Kridabhiramam_. It appears the painting tradition continued into the Rachakonda and Devarakonda Velamas age.\n\n*   Poet **Potana**, in his greatest work _Maha Bhagavatam_, vividly described the process of mixing five important colors used for paintings.\n*   Historical paintings surviving to date are meagre in Telangana as in other regions, but the archaeological survey of the region revealed paintings of the **Padmanayaka period** at:\n    1.  **Rachakonda**, Nalgonda District\n    2.  **Devarakonda**, Nalgonda District\n    3.  **Pandavulagutta**, Warangal District\n\n**Ramanavami Paintings in Rachakonda:**\n\n*   Recently discovered paintings in a temple in Rachakonda closely match descriptions of Potana''s work; the concept appears drawn from the contemporary work _Ranganatha Ramayanam_.\n*   Found in the temple located inside the south-east entrance of the fort — the walls and roofs were once full of paintings, but Bahamani Sultans who defeated the Rachakonda Velama kings had almost all of them chiseled out, as decorations were treated against Shariat law.\n*   Only one stone slab over the roof displays a painting depicting episodes of _Putrakameshthi_ and _Asvamedha Yagas_ conducted by **Dasaratha**, as described in _Ranganath Ramayana_ (Balakanda):\n    *   One painting shows Dasaratha conducting _Putrakameshthi Yajnam_ on a platform over the bank of river Sarayu, under the guidance of Sage Vasishta and Santa-Rishyashrunga, with his three wives Kausalya, Sumitra, and Kaikeyi (the last with a wicked face).\n    *   Another displays the _Asvamedha Yagam_, showing noblemen receiving the horse that returned victorious after roaming the surrounding countries of Ayodhya, with people of the four Varnas, divine persons, sages, divine ladies, and female servants attending the sacrificial ceremonies — depicted with black outlines on white paste over the roof.\n*   The paintings are dated to roughly 650 years ago, based on an inscription of **1365 A.D.** by Rachakonda ruler **Anapota Nayaka**, found in front of the temple of Rama at Rachakonda.\n\n**Pre-Historic Paintings Continuing in Rachakonda:**\n\n*   There are pre-historic-style paintings under a rock shelter adjacent to a temple constructed by the Padanayakas. The huge cave shelter was inhabited by devotees during the period under review, attested by a medieval engraving of Lord Ganesh on the wall opposite the paintings.\n*   The propounding feature: a series of lines about a foot in length and 3 cm in width, in a row of 7 metres, depicted in ochre red colour. Adjacent to the lines is a diametric design (hoped to represent a deity), a metre in length and half a metre in width. A group of devotees follows the design, dancing, headed by a priest.\n*   The whole group painting appears to represent a deity invoked by devotees in the **Megalithic age**, some **3,000 years ago**, since several megalithic burials are noticed a short distance from the cave.\n\n**Hanuman Paintings:**\n\n*   The Padmanayakas held a unique title, _**Astadika raya**_ — indicating the eight cardinal points of the kingdom were conquered by the Padmanayakas.\n*   One can attest the eight cardinal points of the forts of the Padmanayakas bounded by three sculptures on boulders — usually near the entrance gates of the forts — including **Hanuman, Ganesh, and Bhairava**.\n*   The Hanuman sculpture is the most popular; one cannot find a village in Telangana without a Hanuman temple or sculpture — because **Vaishnavism was patronized by the Padmanayakas**, and the Vaishnavite god Hanuman was promoted throughout the kingdom.\n*   This tradition is found in:\n    1.  **Devarakonda** — the top table land of the Devarakonda fort has a huge lake surrounded by huge boulders; one has a brushed surface displaying a painting of Lord Hanuman in regular idol size of 3 feet, in red ochre with black lines where necessary, bordered (6 inches) all around.\n    2.  **Pandavulagutta** — considered the most varied site of pre-historic and historic cave paintings in Telangana. The cave complex has several natural compartments; the ending point in the complex is called **Gonthemma Kollu** — a cave with pre-historic and historic paintings under its roof. The roof-front was painted during the regnal years of the Recharla Padmanayakas. The predominant picture is Lord Hanuman, painted in red ochre in a jumping posture with a huge rod/weapon in his right hand. The name of the cave — Gonthemma — is the corrupt form of **Kunti**, mother of the Pandavas of Vaishnava faith.\n\n### Gravitational Water Supply Technology in Rachakonda\n\nThe gravitation technology used in pumping water from the **Secret Lake (Durgam Cheruvu)** to Golconda fort during the 16th century made Golconda one of the earliest kingdoms in the world to use such technology before electricity and motors were invented.\n\n*   As per an inscription found engraved on a boulder beside a lake in Rachakonda, the two lakes — **Anapota Samudram** and **Raya Samudram** — were constructed by king **Anapota Nayaka in 1368 A.D.**; a corbelled canal extends up to the palaces, in-laid in the outer walls of the palaces.\n*   By observing the structures, one can understand that drinking water was drawn from lakes at higher altitudes to palaces at lower altitudes, using **gravitational technology** to facilitate free flow of water from high to low. Royal family and nobility living in the harem/palaces obtained water in individual rooms and toilets through taps attached to the corbelled canal running through the palace walls.\n*   The **corbelled drainage system** had already existed in India from the **Indus Valley Civilization** (c. 2700 BC) onwards, but was confined to draining used water only, not drinking water.\n*   The technology of **gravitational suction of drinking water** was first used by **Raja Bhoja** inside the tank bund of Bhopal Lake and on a stream in Bhojpur, Madhya Pradesh, during the **11th century**, utilizing architectural features from Bhoja''s work _Samaraanagana Sutradhaara_.\n*   Telangana''s Kakatiyas of Warangal and Velamas of Rachakonda had already utilized these architectural features — for instance, _Jhaaraavali_, the outer roofs of temples designed to look like dropping water drops, was employed by these Telangana rulers. \n\n*   Similarly, the Velama kings of Rachakonda may have followed the gravitation technology coined by Bhoja, which found its further way to Golconda two centuries later.\n\n### Sculpture and Architecture\n\nBy and large, the architecture and sculpture of Telangana after the Vishnukundis is characterized by **Chalukyan style**, continued and improved by the Kakatiya dynasty, and the same tradition was continued by the Velamas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda.\n    \n*   Though the quality, quantity, and diversity of Padmanayaka sculpture/architecture was inferior to their rich predecessors the Kakatiyas, the Padmanayakas had some significant architectural features to their credit:\n    \n    1.  Construction of **temples on top of hills**\n    2.  Construction of **temples for Lord Narasimha in caves** on top of hills\n    3.  **Sculptures of Bhairava, Hanuman, and Ganesha** on the natural borders near the fortifications of temples at eight cardinal points\n*   Almost all these features are found in the forts/temples at the following places: Rachakonda, Devarakonda, Koulasa, Bheemgal, Hastalapur, Ramagiri Khilla, Koilkonda, Thirumalanatha Konda, Pedda Revalla, Bhuvanagiri, Charigonda, Urlugonda, Anantagiri, Umamaheshvaram, Jatararevu, Mannanur, Domalapalli, Mattapalli, Vellatur, Kandikonda, Inugurti, and Badigetpalli. \n    \n\n### Rachakonda — Architectural Beauty\n\nAmongst all places developed by the Velamas of Rachakonda, Rachakonda itself stands first in architectural beauty, with:\n    1.  **Religious architecture**: Temples — lost sculptures\n    2.  **Secular architecture**: Fortifications, tanks, canals, wells\n*   Rachakonda and Devarakonda Velamas initially patronized **Saivism** and subsequently embraced the faith of **Vaishnavism**. Therefore, two types of temples are found at their capital Rachakonda:\n    *   **Saivite temples** — constructed on land surface\n    *   **Vaishnavite temples** — placed on high platforms or in caves\n*   Numerous temples are found in Rachakonda fort, especially in its south-west stretch.\n\n**The Ramalayam (Temple of Lord Rama):**\n\n*   The most important and earliest temple in Rachakonda fort, located in the south-western portion. It was built by the first independent ruler of the Padmanayaka dynasty, **Anapota Nayaka**, in **1365 A.D.** (per the site inscription).\n*   The temple was constructed over a natural cave formed under a boulder — since the roof of the cave is still invoked today, it can be said the cave was earlier a cave temple of pre-historic people.\n*   Local tradition holds the present idols of Lord Rama, Mother Seetha, Laxmana, and Hanuman were brought here from over the fort, since royal harem members could not climb the fort for _darshan_ and _puja_.\n*   The temple is architecturally simple, with a **Rangamantapa, Dhvajastambham,** and **Prahari wall**.\n*   A pair of foot prints were engraved on the hillock north of the Ramalayam, but a single-hall temple nearby, by its architectural style, reveals it was built for **Jains** during the reign of the **Rashtrakutas** or their successors the **Western Chalukyas of Kalyani**, when Jainism was in vogue in Telangana.\n\n**Naganayakonda Vaishnavite Temple:**\n\n*   From the small temple, three routes lead eastward: two into the **Naganayani Fort**, and one into the valley between the Naganayani Fort and the Kacheri Fort.\n*   The northernmost route leads to the **Naganayakonda Fort**, home to a masterpiece Vaishnavite temple.\n*   The temple was deserted and its sacred idols stolen long ago, so it cannot now be said with certainty which deity it was constructed for.\n*   However, it can be confirmed as a **Vaishnavite temple**, since the _Dvarapala_ sculptures are of Lord Hanuman on one side and Lord Garuda on the other — a unique architectural feature that came into being during the Padmanayaka period. Similar sculptures are found in front of two types of temples built by the Padmanayakas: temples of Lord Rama, and temples of Lord Laxmi Narasimha.\n*   Since almost all Narasimha temples were built in caves/rock caverns, the temple under review may safely be ascribed to Lord Rama, as it was constructed on land surface, though on hilly space.\n*   The temple complex has **five structures**: the main temple, **Kalyana Mandapam**, **Prakaara** (enclosing wall), cloisters, and an open well-like pond of large parameters.\n*   The main temple has a sanctum sanctorum (_garbhagriha_), _ardhagriha_ or _shukanashi_, and _Mukha Mandapam_ or _Navaranga Mandapam_. The _ardha mandapa_ has sculptures of Garuda and Hanuman on either side of its door jambs. The _Garbhagriha_ is pinnacled by a _Sikhara_ in pyramidal steps.\n*   With these features, the temple appears built in the **Chalukyan style of architecture**. Though Chalukyan style is represented, it seems to have been constructed by the Recharla Padmanayakas only, for two reasons:\n    1.  The door jambs of the _ardhamandapa_ carry sculptures of Garuda and Hanuma, deities invoked by the Padmanayakas.\n    2.  There is no material evidence or inscription tracing it to the Chalukyan period in or around Rachakonda or elsewhere.\n*   **Kalyana Mandapa** stands to the left (north) of the main temple. A long rock basement lies in front of the temple — it seems to represent a long cloister of cells for devotees to rest/stay during festivals. A long well-like pond is constructed at a distance of 200 metres from the temple.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter8-rachakonda-rulers-and-musunuri-nayakas-p11", "partName": "Summary", "bodyMd": "The Velamas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda ruled over this region for about **152 years**, holding sway over the area lying between the rivers **Godavari and Krishna**.\n\n*   The reign of the Velamas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda is considered a **glorious epoch** in Telangana history, witnessing remarkable progress in art and architecture, language and literature, religion, and philosophy.\n*   After the Kakatiyas, the Recherla Velamas left their impress as native rulers of Telangana for more than a century. When Warangal fort was lost to the Bahmanis, the Velamas shifted to surrounding areas, and remained **Jagirdars, Maqtedars,** and **Deshmukhs** until recent times under **Nizam rule**.\n*   The regional power of the Recharla Padmanayakas of Rachakonda and Devarakonda — so far often considered a \"forgotten period\" or \"dark phase\" in the annals of medieval Andhra history — is, in fact, a **brilliant epoch**, witnessing significant transitions in the socio-economic, political, and cultural spheres of the state.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas', 'telangana-history-en', '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p1", "partName": "Fall of Warangal and Advent of Muslim Rule", "bodyMd": "In **1323 CE**, the Kakatiya capital fort of **Orugallu (Warangal)** fell before the Muslim army sent by the Delhi Sultanate. The campaign was led by **Ulugh Khan**, son of Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq (later known as **Muhammad bin Tughlaq**), under whose leadership the expansion continued further south. Their march advanced through Tamil Nadu without resistance until Madurai. The Andhra and Tamil regions conquered by the Muhammadans were merged together and made part of the **Ma''bar province** of the Delhi Sultanate. This marked the first time the Telugu land came under Muslim rule.\n\n**Excesses under early Muslim rule:**\n\n*   The victorious Muslim army committed widespread atrocities.\n*   Temples were destroyed and mosques built in their place.\n*   Ripened crops were forcibly seized from cultivators.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p2", "partName": "Musunuri Nayakas and the Revolt Against Muslim Rule", "bodyMd": "Faced with oppressive Muslim administration, the old royal houses, former Kakatiya armies, great chieftains (Mahasamanthas), and local dynastic families came together to resist. To lead the effort to liberate the collapsing Telugu land, they chose **Musunuri Prolaya Nayaka**, a warrior from the Vengi region, as their chief.\n\n*   In **1325**, the ancient republican political tradition of Andhra was revived — a significant development in the region''s political history.\n*   Under Prolaya Nayaka''s leadership, the Hindu kings of Andhra first organised a government-in-exile against Muslim rule, and subsequently established an independent kingdom. This is regarded as a golden chapter in Andhra history.\n*   Coastal Andhra came under Prolaya Nayaka''s control, while **Kapaya Nayaka**, his cousin, held Warangal at the time.\n*   Since Prolaya Nayaka had no children, on his death Kapaya Nayaka also became ruler of coastal Andhra.\n\n### Formation of the Recherla Padma Nayaka Kingdom\n\nAround the same period, the **Recherla dynasty**, belonging to the Velama community, established the Padma Nayaka kingdom in Telangana with its capital at **Rachakonda**. The rulers of **Devarakonda** were also Velamas. Both lines were originally Shaivites, and later became Vaishnavites.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p3", "partName": "The Six Kingdoms of the Deccan (1335–36)", "bodyMd": "By 1335–36, six major independent political powers had emerged in the Deccan, each rising from the confusion following the Kakatiya collapse:\n\n| # | Kingdom | Founder / Rulers |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Coastal Andhra territories | Musunuri rulers |\n| 2 | Warangal | Kapaya Nayaka |\n| 3 | Reddy kingdom | Prolaya Vema Reddy |\n| 4 | Rachakonda and Devarakonda | Padma Nayakas |\n| 5 | Vijayanagara empire | Harihara and Bukkaraya |\n| 6 | Bahmani kingdom | A rebelling local governor of the Deccan |", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p4", "partName": "The Coalition of 77 Nayakas and Recovery of Warangal", "bodyMd": "Before launching their campaign against the Turks, **77 nayakas** united through a mutual agreement to fight together for their homeland. Their coming together under the leadership of Musunuri Prolaya Nayaka to re-establish Hindu rule is regarded as a great chapter in regional history.\n\n**Terms of the agreement among the 77 nayakas:**\n\n*   Muhammadan rule would be jointly driven out.\n*   Once achieved, each nayaka would take control of his own respective territory.\n*   No nayaka would interfere in the territory belonging to another — a term all of them accepted.\n\n**Sequence of events:**\n\n1.  Prolaya Nayaka shifted his capital from the Vengi region to **Rekapalli**, near Bhadrachalam on the banks of the Godavari.\n2.  Prolaya Nayaka died around **1330 CE**.\n3.  After his death, his cousin''s son, **Kapaya Nayaka**, took up the leadership.\n4.  In **1336**, Kapaya Nayaka attacked the Warangal fort, drove out the Muhammadans, and took control of it.\n5.  The capital was shifted from Rekapalli to Warangal.\n6.  Kapaya Nayaka assumed the titles **Andhra Suratrana**, **Andhra Deshadhishwara**, and **Kakatiya Sthapanacharya**.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p5", "partName": "Decline of Kapaya Nayaka''s Power", "bodyMd": "Kapaya Nayaka aspired to establish himself as the sovereign successor (chakravarti) of the Kakatiya empire, having made Warangal his own capital rather than sharing power under the earlier agreement. He steadily expanded his territory by strengthening his following.\n\n*   Recherla Singama Nayaka, who controlled a larger share of Telangana, grew envious of Kapaya Nayaka''s ambitions, and as a result encroached upon and seized some of his territories.\n    \n*   Constant warfare that followed steadily weakened Kapaya Nayaka''s strength.\n    \n*   Kapaya Nayaka died in **1367–68**.\n    \n*   Thereafter, **Anavota Reddy** gradually brought the forts of Telangana under his own control.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p6", "partName": "Padma Nayakas", "bodyMd": "### Origins and Rise\n\nThe Padma Nayakas were originally feudatories (samanthas) of the Kakatiya kings. Their rise began during the reign of **Ganapatideva**, when they took an enthusiastic part in politics and contributed to the expansion of the Kakatiya empire. Their native place was **Amanagallu**, in present-day Nalgonda district.\n\n### Capitals and Forts\n\nAfter becoming independent, the Padma Nayaka rulers first held Amanagallu, and later established their capitals at **Rachakonda** and **Devarakonda**, where they built strong forts. Rachakonda town is located in the Narayanapuram mandal of Nalgonda district.\n\n**Rachakonda and the protection of dharma:**\n\n*   For the kingdom to remain stable, the Padma Nayakas of Rachakonda committed themselves to the protection of dharma.\n*   After the fall of the Kakatiyas, Warangal and the surrounding Andhra regions had passed under Muslim control, and in these circumstances, protecting one''s own religion (swadharma), one''s own community (swamata), and promoting general development became essential duties of the rulers.\n*   All religious communities and sects had a place at Rachakonda.\n\n### Cultural and Literary Patronage\n\nRachakonda gained renown for its contributions to literature, music, and dramatic arts, and Sanskrit received great patronage under its rulers.\n\n*   **Court poet:** Salakya Ayyalarjudu, court poet of the Padma Nayakas, completed the **Bhaskara Ramayanam**.\n*   **Telugu poets patronised:** Gaurana and Pothana were the most celebrated Telugu poets who received the patronage of the Rachakonda rulers.\n\n### Sarvagna Singabhupaludu\n\n**Sarvagna Singabhupaludu**, a Rachakonda ruler, was himself a connoisseur of music and literature, a discerning aesthete, a prolific author, and the foremost among poets of his time. He extended his patronage to **Sreenatha**, who adorned the Rachakonda court.\n\n### State of Society and the Legacy of Pothana\n\nThe rulers of the age had grown degenerate, and ministers pursued selfish interests. Corrupted by the times, the people themselves turned to bribery. Continued contact and conflict with Muslim rulers steadily weakened Hindu society, which lacked genuine spiritual or selfless direction for the nation''s welfare.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, **Sri Rama** was upheld as the ideal ruler and **Ramarajyam** as the ideal state, a message spread to the world through the **Bhagavatam**. In Pothana''s hands, the Telugu language flourished; his writing flowed with natural sweetness, achieving a rare fulfilment of style and substance.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p7", "partName": "Decline and End of the Padma Nayaka Kingdom", "bodyMd": "After the fall of the Kakatiyas, the Musunuri and Recherla Padma Nayaka rulers governed Telangana for close to **150 years**. However, they were unable to provide capable and stable governance throughout this period, as their rule was spent almost entirely in continuous warfare — against the Bahmani Sultans, in hostility with the Delhi Sultans, and amid the rising influence of Vijayanagara and the Gajapatis, among other reasons. \n\nBy **1475 CE**, weakened by these prolonged conflicts, the Padma Nayaka kingdom came to an end. Even so, the Padma Nayakas played a major role in the history of Telangana.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p8", "partName": "Agriculture and Trade Under the Nayaka Period", "bodyMd": "The Kakatiyas had already developed Telangana as a region rich in irrigation tanks even before this period. Continuing this tradition, the Velama rulers too built tanks — **Anasomasamudram**, **Rayasamudram**, and **Nagasamudram** being chief among them — and promoted agriculture.\n\n*   Agricultural conditions remained generally satisfactory in parts of the region, though continuous military campaigns disrupted regular crop cultivation elsewhere.\n*   Trade and commerce received little protection during this turbulent period.\n*   Although kingdoms directed repeated military campaigns toward capturing the fertile Godavari coastal region, even when captured, they were unable to hold this territory for even ten days at a stretch.\n*   Foreign trade suffered as a result, but domestic trade continued — petty traders and cottage-industry workers carried on their livelihoods largely independent of the political turmoil.\n*   Agriculture remained the chief occupation, and even those engaged in other trades and crafts held lands of their own.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter9-padmanayakas-p9", "partName": "Recherla Padma Nayakas as Scholars", "bodyMd": "The Recherla Padma Nayakas were not merely patrons of literature but were themselves accomplished authors, composing several authoritative works in both Sanskrit and Telugu.\n\n**Sanskrit works:**\n\n*   Rasarnava Sudhakaram — a treatise on dramaturgy (Natyashastra)\n*   Ratnapanchalika — a Sanskrit drama\n*   Chamatkara Chandrika — a treatise on rhetoric (Alankarashastra)\n*   Amara Pada Parijatam — a commentary on the Amara Kosha\n*   Lakshanadeepika\n*   Harishchandropakhyanamu\n\n**Telugu works:**\n\n*   Simhasana Dvatrimshika\n*   Navanatha Charitra\n*   Bhagavatam\n*   Bhogini Dandakam\n\nThrough these contributions, literature came to occupy a distinguished place under the patronage of the Padma Nayakas.", "free": false}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = excluded.concepts_json;
